/* THE STUDY OF EVERY BEDTIME STORY. Built from the picture-book pages in
   www/stories by tools/build-story-study.js: the story itself, the Traveler of
   the night, the Feeling Moment and its Five Stones, Remember This, the
   questions for the child and the prompts for the parent. The timeline's Study
   Library loads this only when someone opens an In-Depth Study. Keyed by the
   library's story id (b8-w3-n1). Rebuild it when a story page changes. */
window.LST_STUDY={"b4-w1-n1":{"story":["Long ago, Zechariah told the people what Heavenly Father said. He spoke of a King who would come.","Zechariah said, “Shout for joy! Your King is coming to you, riding on a donkey.” This King would not come proud and grand. He would come gently and humbly.","Zechariah was talking about Jesus! Many years later, Jesus rode a young donkey into the city of Jerusalem.","A big, happy crowd spread their coats on the road. Others cut branches from trees and laid them down too. They did this to honor their King. They shouted, “Hosanna!” to praise Him.","Jesus is your gentle King too. Tonight, wave your arms like the happy crowd, and whisper, “Welcome, Jesus!”"],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout loves to look for something special, like the people who watched the road for their King. In Scout’s world today: Scout waited at the window for Grandpa’s car. When he saw it coming, Scout ran outside, waving both arms. Just like the happy crowd, Scout learned that it feels wonderful to welcome someone we love.","feel":"Hold your arms up high like tree branches. Wave them slowly side to side. Now whisper a happy “Hosanna!” Can you show me your happy welcome face?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus rode into the city on a donkey, and the people felt happy and excited."],["NAME","That bouncy, smiling, can’t-wait feeling is called joy. Your mind might think, ‘Jesus is here!’ Your body might feel light and bubbly in your chest."],["FIND","When have you felt joy when someone you love came?"],["FORK","The people could stay home, or go out to welcome Jesus. They took the road of welcoming their King."],["CALM","Fold your hands on your tummy. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for sending Jesus, my King.”"]],"remember":"Jesus is my gentle King, and I can welcome Him with joy!","ask":["“What animal did Jesus ride?”","“What did the people put on the road?”","“Who was Scout waiting for at the window?”"],"share":["When have you felt joy in welcoming Jesus into your life?","How can our family make Jesus welcome in our home this week?","Which grandparent or ancestor loved Easter? Share a story about them."]},"b4-w1-n2":{"story":["Long ago, Isaiah wrote that Someone would carry our sorrows, our sad feelings. Isaiah was talking about Jesus.","One night, Jesus went to a garden called Gethsemane. He asked Peter, James, and John to wait and watch.","Jesus went a little farther and bowed down low to pray. He felt very sad and heavy.","Prophets teach that Jesus felt all our sadness there. He felt our wrong choices too. It was very hard. But He kept praying, “Father, not what I want, but what You want.”","When you feel sad, Jesus understands. He carried your sad feelings too. Tonight, tell Him how you feel. He knows just how to help."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow has a tender heart, and tonight Jesus showed the most tender love of all. In Willow’s world today: Willow’s friend dropped her ice cream and started to cry. Willow sat beside her and held her hand until she smiled again. Just like Jesus, Willow learned that loving hearts help carry someone’s sadness.","feel":"Kneel up in your bed and bow your head, like Jesus praying. Now give yourself a gentle, warm hug. Can you show me your quiet, praying hands?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus prayed in the garden and felt very sad, but He kept going because He loves us."],["NAME","That heavy, droopy, teary feeling is called sorrow. Your mind might think, ‘I feel so sad.’ Your body might feel heavy in your chest and wet in your eyes."],["FIND","When have you felt sad and needed someone to help?"],["FORK","Jesus could stop, or He could keep praying for us. Jesus took the road of love and kept going."],["CALM","Put your hand on your heart. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that Jesus understands me.”"]],"remember":"Jesus carried my sad feelings, so He can always help me!","ask":["“Where did Jesus go to pray?”","“What did Jesus carry for us?”","“How did Willow help her sad friend?”"],"share":["When have you felt Jesus help you through sorrow?","How can we help carry each other’s sad days?","Which grandparent or ancestor found comfort in Jesus during a hard time? Share their story."]},"b4-w1-n3":{"story":["Isaiah wrote that Jesus would be quiet, like a lamb. When a farmer clips a sheep’s wool, it stands still and makes no sound.","Isaiah said, “He opened not His mouth.” Isaiah was talking about Jesus. Jesus would not say angry words back.","After Jesus prayed in the garden, men took Him to the high priest.","Many people told untrue things about Jesus, but their stories did not match. The high priest stood up and asked, “Will You answer nothing?” Jesus stayed calm and quiet, just as Isaiah said. He was strong inside.","When someone says unkind words, you can stay calm too. Tonight, lie still like a gentle lamb. Take a slow, quiet breath."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau is brave, and tonight Jesus showed a quiet, strong kind of brave. In Beau’s world today: At the park, a boy said Beau’s block tower was silly. Beau took a slow breath and did not say mean words back. He kept building. Just like Jesus, Beau learned that staying calm and kind can be very brave.","feel":"Curl up like a soft little lamb. Hold very still and very quiet. Now take one slow, calm breath. Can you show me your calm, strong face?","stones":[["SPOT","People said untrue things about Jesus, and He stayed calm, quiet, and strong."],["NAME","That still, steady, peaceful feeling is called calm. Your mind might think, ‘I can stay kind.’ Your body might feel slow breaths and soft, easy shoulders."],["FIND","When have you stayed calm when someone was unkind?"],["FORK","Jesus could say angry words back, or He could stay quiet. Jesus took the calm, gentle road."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly, then out slowly. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me be calm like Jesus.”"]],"remember":"Jesus was quiet and strong like a gentle lamb, and I can be calm too!","ask":["“What animal did Isaiah say Jesus was like?”","“What did Jesus do when people said untrue things?”","“What did Beau keep doing at the park?”"],"share":["When has staying calm helped you more than talking back?","How can our family answer unkind words with kindness?","Which grandparent or ancestor was known for a calm, gentle heart? Share about them."]},"b4-w1-n4":{"story":["Long ago, someone wrote a song of praise to the Lord.","It said, “The stone the builders did not want became the corner stone.”","A corner stone is the big stone at the corner, where two walls meet. It holds the walls straight and strong.","This song was about Jesus! Some leaders came while Jesus taught at the temple. They asked, “Who gave You the right to do these things?” They did not want Him. So Jesus told them the words of the song. He was that stone.","Some pushed Jesus away, but He is the most important of all. When you follow Him, He holds you strong. Tonight, make a corner with your two hands, and say, “Jesus holds me strong.”"],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren loves to wonder about big ideas, like how a stone no one wanted became the most important. In Wren’s world today: Wren found a lumpy rock that nobody picked. She put it at the bottom of her rock tower, and it held up every other rock. Just like Jesus, Wren learned that the one others pass by can hold everything up.","feel":"Make your hands into two strong fists, like stones. Stack one on top of the other. Hold them steady and still. Can you show me your strong, steady stones?","stones":[["SPOT","Some leaders did not want Jesus, but He became the most important of all."],["NAME","That quiet, thinking, amazed feeling is called wonder. Your mind might think, ‘How can that be?’ Your body might feel still, with wide eyes and a slow smile."],["FIND","When have you wondered about something big and amazing?"],["FORK","The leaders could listen to Jesus, or push Him away. They pushed Him away, but Jesus kept teaching the truth."],["CALM","Rest your hands on your bed like strong stones. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me build my life on Jesus.”"]],"remember":"Jesus is the most important stone, and He holds me strong!","ask":["“What is a corner stone?”","“Who is the most important stone?”","“Where did Wren put the lumpy rock?”"],"share":["How has building on Jesus held your life together?","What can our family do to keep Jesus at the center?","Which grandparent or ancestor built their life on Jesus? Share a story about them."]},"b4-w1-n5":{"story":["Isaiah wrote that Jesus would be hurt for us. Then we could be healed, made well and whole. Isaiah was talking about Jesus.","Isaiah also said Jesus would carry the wrong things that many people do.","One day, Jesus carried His cross to a place called Golgotha. There, men put Him on the cross.","Jesus’s mother and some friends stood near Him. Jesus said, “It is finished.” Then He bowed His head and died. It was a very sad day. But Jesus had finished what He came to do. He did it because He loves us. And Sunday was coming!","Jesus loves you that much. Tonight, cross your arms over your heart like a hug. Feel His love for you."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow has a warm, loving heart, and tonight we learn about the greatest love of all. In Willow’s world today: Willow’s grandma was sick. Willow wanted to play outside, but she stayed in and made Grandma a bright card instead. Just like Jesus, Willow learned that real love gives something for others.","feel":"Wrap your arms around yourself in a big, warm hug. Squeeze gently. Now open your arms wide, like Jesus’s love. Can you show me how big His love is?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus gave His life, and His friends felt sad, but His love was bigger."],["NAME","That warm, full, safe feeling is called love. Your mind might think, ‘Jesus loves me so much.’ Your body might feel warm and cozy in your heart."],["FIND","When have you felt very loved by someone?"],["FORK","Jesus could stop, or He could finish what He came to do. Jesus took the road of love for us."],["CALM","Lay your hands on your heart. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for Jesus and His love.”"]],"remember":"Jesus gave His life because He loves me, and Sunday was coming!","ask":["“Who stayed near Jesus?”","“Why did Jesus give His life?”","“What did Willow make for her grandma?”"],"share":["When have you felt Jesus’s love most deeply?","How can our family show love by giving to others this week?","Which grandparent or ancestor showed great love by giving to others? Share their story."]},"b4-w1-n6":{"story":["Isaiah wrote that when Jesus died, His grave would be with the rich. Isaiah was talking about Jesus.","After Jesus died, a rich man named Joseph came. He was a follower of Jesus. He asked Pilate, the ruler, to let him care for Jesus’s body.","Pilate said yes. So Joseph gently wrapped Jesus’s body in a clean cloth.","He laid Jesus in his own new tomb, a room cut into rock. He rolled a big stone over the door. It was just as Isaiah said. Everything was quiet. But this was not the end!","Joseph gave Jesus his very best. Tonight, think of one kind thing you can do for Jesus tomorrow."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren loves to think quietly, and tonight Jesus’s friends waited quietly too. In Wren’s world today: Wren and her mom planted seeds in a pot. Nothing showed the next day, so Wren waited and watered them. Soon tiny green sprouts came up! Just like Jesus’s friends, Wren learned that quiet waiting can come before something wonderful.","feel":"Pretend to roll a big, heavy stone. Push, push, push! Now rest your arms and lie very still. Can you show me your quiet, waiting face?","stones":[["SPOT","Joseph gently cared for Jesus’s body, and Jesus’s friends felt quiet and sad."],["NAME","That soft, waiting, looking-up feeling is called hope. Your mind might think, ‘Something good is coming.’ Your body might feel quiet and slow, like a deep breath."],["FIND","When have you waited and hoped for something good?"],["FORK","Joseph could stay away, or he could help. Joseph took the road of giving Jesus his very best."],["CALM","Lie still and breathe slowly. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me give Jesus my best.”"]],"remember":"I can give Jesus my best, and something wonderful is coming!","ask":["“Whose new tomb did Jesus rest in?”","“What did Joseph roll over the door?”","“What came up from Wren’s seeds?”"],"share":["When has waiting on the Lord brought you something wonderful?","What is one best gift our family can give Jesus?","Which grandparent or ancestor gave generously to others? Share a story about them."]},"b4-w1-n7":{"story":["Early on Sunday, as the sun came up, three women walked to Jesus’s tomb. They brought sweet spices for His body, to show their love.","On the way they wondered, “Who will roll away the big stone?” But when they looked, the stone was already rolled away!","Inside sat a young man in a long white robe. He said, “Do not be afraid. He is risen; He is not here.” Jesus was alive again!","Long before, Isaiah wrote, “He will swallow up death in victory.” Death would not win! Isaiah was talking about Jesus. Prophets teach that because Jesus rose, everyone will live again.","Jesus is alive, and He loves you! Tonight, smile as you think of the people you love."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout loves to discover things, and tonight the women discovered the happiest news ever. In Scout’s world today: On Easter morning, Scout found a little bird’s nest with an empty, cracked eggshell. The baby bird was singing nearby! Just like the women at the tomb, Scout learned that an empty place can bring happy news.","feel":"Cover your eyes with your hands. Now open them wide. Surprise! Throw your arms up high. Can you show me your happy “He is risen” face?","stones":[["SPOT","The women found the tomb empty, and they felt surprised and amazed."],["NAME","That bright, bursting, happy feeling is called gladness. Your mind might think, ‘Jesus is alive!’ Your body might feel bouncy, with a great big smile."],["FIND","When have you heard happy news that made you glad?"],["FORK","The women could stay home, or go to Jesus’s tomb. They went early and found the happiest news ever."],["CALM","Smile and take a big, happy breath. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that Jesus lives!”"]],"remember":"Jesus is risen, so everyone will live again!","ask":["“Was Jesus still in the tomb?”","“What did the man in white say?”","“What did Scout find in the nest?”"],"share":["When did you first know that Jesus lives?","How can our family share the happy news of Easter?","Which grandparent or ancestor are you excited to see again someday? Share a story about them."]},"b4-w2-n1":{"story":["Moses and his big brother Aaron went to see Pharaoh, the king of Egypt. The Lord wanted His people to go free.","The Lord said Pharaoh would ask them to show a wonder.","So Aaron threw down his tall walking stick, called a staff. It became a snake!","Pharaoh’s magicians threw down their sticks too, and theirs became snakes. But Aaron’s staff swallowed up all their sticks! The Lord’s power is greater than any other. Still, Pharaoh’s heart was hard, like a shut door. He would not listen.","Moses and Aaron were brave, because they obeyed the Lord. When you feel shy, you can stand tall and do what is right too."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau is brave, like Moses and Aaron, who stood before the king and did just what the Lord said. In Beau’s world today: Beau felt shy when it was his turn to sing in front of his church class. He stood up tall and sang anyway. Just like Aaron, Beau learned that Heavenly Father helps us be brave when we do what is right.","feel":"Hold your arm out straight, like Aaron holding his staff. Now lay it down softly on your blanket. Can you show me your brave, steady arm?","stones":[["SPOT","Moses and Aaron stood before the king. They felt brave, because they did what the Lord said."],["NAME","That tall, strong, ready feeling is called courage. Your mind might think, ‘I can do what is right.’ Your body might feel strong in your back and steady in your feet."],["FIND","When have you done something brave?"],["FORK","Aaron could be afraid of the king, or obey the Lord. Aaron obeyed and threw down his staff."],["CALM","Stretch your arms out, then let them rest. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me be brave.”"]],"remember":"The Lord’s power is greatest, and He helps me be brave!","ask":["“What did Aaron throw down?”","“What did Aaron’s staff swallow up?”","“What did Beau do in front of his class?”"],"share":["When has the Lord given you courage to do something hard?","Where have you seen the Lord’s power in your life?","Which grandparent or ancestor stood up bravely for what was right? Share about them."]},"b4-w2-n2":{"story":["The Lord told Pharaoh, “Let My people go.” But Pharaoh said no. So the Lord sent signs to show His power.","Aaron held his staff over the river. The water turned to blood, and no one could drink it.","Next, frogs came hopping out of the river! They got into houses, into beds, and even into ovens.","Pharaoh said, “Pray for the frogs to go away. Then I will let the people go.” Moses said, “You will know there is no one like the Lord.” Moses prayed, and the frogs were gone from the houses. But Pharaoh did not keep his word.","The Lord made rivers and frogs, and they obey Him. He made you too. Tonight, pray, and He will hear you."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout loves to find out new things, and tonight he finds out that no one is as strong as the Lord. In Scout’s world today: Scout found a little green frog by the pond. He watched it hop and asked, “Who made frogs?” His dad said, “Jesus made them for Heavenly Father.” Just like Moses, Scout learned that there is no one like the Lord.","feel":"Make your hand into a little frog. Hop it softly across your blanket, one, two, three. Now let it rest. Can you show me your sleepy frog?","stones":[["SPOT","Pharaoh saw the river turn to blood and frogs everywhere. Moses felt sure the Lord was strong."],["NAME","That sure, steady, He-is-strongest feeling is called trust. Your mind might think, ‘The Lord is stronger than anything.’ Your body might feel calm and steady in your tummy."],["FIND","When have you felt sure that Heavenly Father could help you?"],["FORK","Pharaoh could keep his word, or break it. Pharaoh broke it, but Moses kept praying and trusting the Lord."],["CALM","Rest your hands on your tummy and breathe slowly. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, You are strong. I trust You.”"]],"remember":"There is no one like the Lord!","ask":["“What came hopping out of the river?”","“What did Moses do so the frogs would go away?”","“What did Scout find by the pond?”"],"share":["When have you felt that there is no one like the Lord?","How has prayer helped our family in a hard time?","Which grandparent or ancestor trusted the Lord through hard times? Share their story."]},"b4-w2-n3":{"story":["Pharaoh still would not let the Lord’s people go. Aaron hit the dust with his staff, and it became tiny bugs called lice. Pharaoh’s magicians could not do it. They said only Heavenly Father’s power could.","Next, the Lord sent swarms of flies. But no flies came to Goshen, where the Lord’s people lived.","Then the Egyptians’ animals got sick. But the Lord kept His people’s animals safe.","Pharaoh even said, “You may go,” then changed his mind. His heart grew hard, like dry clay that will not bend.","Soft clay can bend and change. Heavenly Father can help your heart stay soft. Tonight, think of a time you turned a “no” into a kind “yes.”"],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren likes to stop and think, and tonight she thinks about hard hearts and soft hearts. In Wren’s world today: Wren’s little brother asked to use her blue crayon. At first Wren said, “No!” Then she stopped and thought. She smiled and gave it to him. Just like Moses, who listened to the Lord, Wren learned that a soft heart listens and changes.","feel":"Make two tight fists, hard like Pharaoh’s heart. Now open your hands, soft and wide. Can you show me your soft, listening hands?","stones":[["SPOT","Pharaoh kept saying no. The Lord’s people felt safe, because the Lord protected them."],["NAME","That soft, open, I-will-listen feeling is called being humble. Your mind might think, ‘I want to do what Heavenly Father says.’ Your body might feel soft and open, with gentle hands."],["FIND","When have you changed your mind to do the right thing?"],["FORK","Pharaoh could soften his heart, or keep it hard. Pharaoh kept it hard, but Moses kept listening to the Lord."],["CALM","Open your hands softly on your blanket. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me listen to You.”"]],"remember":"A soft heart listens to Heavenly Father!","ask":["“What did the dust turn into?”","“Where did the flies not go?”","“What did Wren give her little brother?”"],"share":["When has the Lord softened your heart?","How do you keep your heart soft when you feel upset?","Which grandparent or ancestor had a soft, listening heart? Share how they showed it."]},"b4-w2-n4":{"story":["Pharaoh still would not let the Lord’s people go. So the Lord told Moses, “Stretch out your hand toward heaven.”","Moses lifted his hand up to the sky. Then a thick darkness covered all of Egypt for three days.","It was so dark the Egyptians could not see one another. No one got up from their place for three whole days.","But all of the Lord’s people had light in their homes! The Lord was taking care of them. Light helps us see the way. Jesus said, “I am the light of the world.”","Even on a dark night, Jesus is your light. Tonight, look at your night-light, and remember that He is with you."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau is brave, and tonight’s story helps him when the dark at night feels big. In Beau’s world today: One night the lights went out in Beau’s house. Beau felt a little scared. He held his dad’s hand, and together they turned on a flashlight. Just like the Lord’s people in Egypt, Beau learned that the Lord gives us light, so we can be brave.","feel":"Cup your hands together, like you are holding a little light. Now lift it up gently. Can you show me your glowing light hands?","stones":[["SPOT","Egypt was very dark, but the Lord’s people had light. They felt safe at home."],["NAME","That cozy, glowing, safe-at-home feeling is called comfort. Your mind might think, ‘Jesus is with me, even in the dark.’ Your body might feel warm and snug, with soft shoulders."],["FIND","When have you felt safe, even in the dark?"],["FORK","Moses could give up, or keep obeying the Lord. Moses obeyed and lifted his hand, and the Lord gave His people light."],["CALM","Cup your hands over your heart and breathe slowly. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for Jesus, my light.”"]],"remember":"Jesus is my light, even in the dark!","ask":["“How many days was Egypt dark?”","“Where did the Lord’s people have light?”","“What did Beau and his dad turn on?”"],"share":["When has Jesus been a light to you in a dark time?","How can we fill our home with Jesus’s light?","Which grandparent or ancestor was a light to others? Share about them."]},"b4-w2-n5":{"story":["The Lord told Moses how families should get ready. Each family chose a perfect little lamb for supper.","They put some of its blood on the sides and top of the door. The Lord said, “When I see the blood, I will pass over you.”","It would be a very sad night in Egypt. But the Lord would keep each marked home safe.","Every family did just what the Lord said. They ate with their shoes on, ready to go. This supper is called the Passover, because the Lord passed over. The lamb helps us remember Jesus, who gave His life for us.","The Lord kept those families safe. Tonight, look at your door. Jesus loves you and watches over you too."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow loves her family, and tonight’s story is about families together, safe inside their homes. In Willow’s world today: Willow helped set the table for family supper. She put a napkin by every plate so no one was left out. Then everyone sat close together. Just like the families at the first Passover, Willow learned that families can follow the Lord together.","feel":"Reach your hands up high, like the top of a door. Now slide them down each side. Can you show me your safe, cozy door?","stones":[["SPOT","Each family marked their door and ate together. They felt safe, because the Lord would pass over."],["NAME","That snug, together, all-safe-inside feeling is called safety. Your mind might think, ‘My family is here, and the Lord is near.’ Your body might feel warm and relaxed, all snuggled in."],["FIND","When have you felt safe with your family?"],["FORK","The families could ignore the Lord, or do just what He said. The families did just what the Lord said."],["CALM","Snuggle under your blanket and breathe slowly. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for keeping my family safe.”"]],"remember":"The Passover lamb helps us remember Jesus, who keeps us safe!","ask":["“What did each family put on their door?”","“What did the Lord do when He saw the mark?”","“What did Willow put by every plate?”"],"share":["What does it mean to you that the Passover lamb points to Jesus?","How can our home be a place where the Lord feels welcome?","Which grandparent or ancestor made their home a safe, faithful place? Share about them."]},"b4-w2-n6":{"story":["That night, Pharaoh called for Moses and Aaron. He said, “Go! Take your sheep and cows, and serve the Lord.”","The people hurried. There was no time to let their bread dough rise. They wrapped the dough bowls in clothes and carried them on their shoulders.","Families walked out together with all their animals. About six hundred thousand men went on foot, plus all the children!","The Lord’s people had lived in Egypt for 430 years. On that very day, the Lord brought them out, just as He promised. They were free!","When you go somewhere new, the Lord goes with you too. Tonight, hug your favorite toy and say, “The Lord is with me.”"],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout loves new adventures, and tonight the Lord’s people start a brand-new journey. In Scout’s world today: Scout’s family moved to a new house. Scout packed his favorite toys in a box and carried his own little backpack. Just like the Lord’s people leaving Egypt, Scout learned that the Lord is with us when we go to a new place.","feel":"Pretend to carry a bundle of dough on your shoulder. March your feet under the blanket, left, right, left! Can you show me your happy marching feet?","stones":[["SPOT","The Lord’s people hurried out of Egypt. After so many years, they felt free and happy."],["NAME","That light, happy, bursting-out feeling is called joy. Your mind might think, ‘We are free!’ Your body might feel bouncy and light, like your heart is smiling."],["FIND","When have you felt so happy you wanted to jump?"],["FORK","The people could stay in Egypt, or go when the Lord said. They packed up quickly and walked out together."],["CALM","Rest your feet and breathe out slowly. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for keeping Your promises.”"]],"remember":"The Lord brought His people out of Egypt, just as He promised!","ask":["“What did the people carry on their shoulders?”","“Who did Pharaoh call for in the night?”","“What did Scout carry to his new house?”"],"share":["When have you waited a long time for the Lord to answer?","How has Jesus helped you feel free?","Which grandparent or ancestor moved to a new place by faith? Share their story."]},"b4-w2-n7":{"story":["Moses told the people, “Remember this day, when you came out of Egypt.” The Lord’s strong hand had set them free.","Every year, moms and dads would tell their children the story. They said, “The Lord brought me out of Egypt.”","Then the Lord led His people. By day, He went ahead in a pillar of cloud, tall like a tower.","At night, the cloud became a pillar of fire to give them light. It never went away, day or night. It showed the Lord was with them, leading the way.","Your family can remember what the Lord has done too. Tonight, tell someone one good thing Heavenly Father gave you today."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren loves to think and remember, and tonight Moses says, “Remember this day.” In Wren’s world today: Wren’s mom told her the story of the day Wren was born. “Heavenly Father sent you to us,” Mom said. Wren smiled and hugged her. Just like the families of Israel, Wren learned that remembering helps us thank Heavenly Father.","feel":"Reach your arms up tall, like the pillar of cloud. Now wiggle your fingers, like warm, glowing fire. Can you show me your tall, glowing pillar?","stones":[["SPOT","Families remembered what the Lord did. The cloud and the fire helped them feel safe."],["NAME","That warm, thankful, I-remember feeling is called gratitude. Your mind might think, ‘Look what the Lord did for us!’ Your body might feel full and warm in your chest."],["FIND","What is something good the Lord has done for you?"],["FORK","Moses could just lead the people, or also teach them to remember. Moses taught families to remember the Lord every year."],["CALM","Fold your hands and think of one good thing. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, I remember what You did for me.”"]],"remember":"I will remember what the Lord has done for me!","ask":["“What did the Lord use to lead His people in the day?”","“What did the cloud become at night?”","“What story did Wren’s mom tell?”"],"share":["What has the Lord done for you that you want our children to remember?","How can our family remember the Lord’s help each year?","Which grandparent or ancestor’s story of the Lord’s help should our family always remember?"]},"b4-w3-n1":{"story":["Heavenly Father’s people had just left Egypt. They set up their tents beside the sea.","Then the king of Egypt chased after them with soldiers and horses.","Now the sea was in front of them, and the soldiers were behind. The people were very afraid.","Moses trusted the Lord. He said, “Do not be afraid. Stand still, and see how the Lord will save you today.” The people could not save themselves, but the Lord could. Moses said, “The Lord will fight for you. You only need to be still.” The Lord who saved them was Jesus.","When you feel scared, you can be still and trust Jesus too. Tonight, lie very still and take a slow breath. Jesus is with you."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau is brave, and tonight Moses was brave and calm when everyone around him felt afraid. In Beau’s world today: Loud thunder made Beau feel scared in his bed. Beau’s dad sat beside him and said, “Let’s be still and pray.” Beau held still, and soon he felt calm. Just like Moses, Beau learned that being still and trusting the Lord helps us feel brave.","feel":"Lie very still, like a little statue. Breathe in slowly through your nose. Breathe out slowly. Can you show me how still and calm you can be?","stones":[["SPOT","The people saw the soldiers coming and felt very afraid, but Moses trusted the Lord."],["NAME","That shaky, want-to-run feeling is called fear. Your mind might think, ‘What will happen to me?’ Your body might feel a fast heart and wobbly legs."],["FIND","When have you felt scared and needed someone to help you?"],["FORK","The people could run and cry, or stand still and trust the Lord. Moses stood still and trusted."],["CALM","Hold very still and breathe slowly. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me be still and trust You.”"]],"remember":"When I am afraid, I can be still and trust the Lord!","ask":["“What was in front of the people?”","“What did Moses tell the people to do?”","“What helped Beau feel calm in the storm?”"],"share":["When have you had to stand still and wait for the Lord’s help?","How has the Lord fought for you in a hard time?","Which grandparent or ancestor trusted the Lord when they were afraid? Share their story."]},"b4-w3-n2":{"story":["The Lord’s tall cloud had always led His people. Now it moved behind them, between them and the soldiers. It gave them light all night.","The Lord told Moses, “Lift up your walking stick. Stretch your hand over the sea.” Moses did. All night, the Lord sent a strong wind.","The water moved back, and the bottom of the sea became dry ground!","The people walked right through the middle of the sea. The water stood like a wall on each side. Then the water came back, and the soldiers never chased them again. The people believed the Lord and Moses. The Lord was Jesus!","Jesus can make a way for you too. Tonight, tell Him about something hard. He can help you through."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout loves to discover new paths, and tonight the Lord made a brand-new path right through the sea. In Scout’s world today: A big puddle was in the way to the park. Scout’s mom showed him stepping-stones across it. Scout stepped on each stone and got safely to the other side. Just like the people of Israel, Scout learned that the Lord can show us a way through hard things.","feel":"Make one hand a tall wall of water. Walk the fingers of your other hand right beside it, on the dry path. Can you show me how you cross the sea?","stones":[["SPOT","The people walked through the sea on dry ground and felt amazed and safe."],["NAME","That wow, wide-open feeling is called amazement. Your mind might think, ‘Look what the Lord did!’ Your body might feel tingly, with big eyes and an open mouth."],["FIND","When have you seen something that made you say, “Wow”?"],["FORK","The people could stay on the shore, or walk the path the Lord made. They walked, and they were safe."],["CALM","Stretch your arms wide, then rest them down. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for making a way.”"]],"remember":"The Lord can make a way for me, even through the sea!","ask":["“What did Moses stretch over the sea?”","“What did the water look like on each side?”","“How did Scout get across the puddle?”"],"share":["When has the Lord made a way for you when you could not see one?","How can we help our children remember what the Lord has done for us?","Which grandparent or ancestor traveled a hard road with the Lord’s help? Share their story."]},"b4-w3-n3":{"story":["Heavenly Father’s people were safe on the other side of the sea. They felt so happy that they wanted to sing!","Moses and the people sang, “The Lord is my strength and my song.” The Lord had made them strong. He had given them a happy reason to sing.","Then Miriam, Aaron’s sister, picked up a timbrel. It was a little hand drum that jingled, like a tambourine.","All the women followed Miriam, playing their drums and dancing. Miriam sang, “Sing to the Lord!” They were thanking Him for keeping them safe.","You can thank the Lord with a happy song too. Tonight, think of one thing you are thankful for. Then whisper, “Thank you, Heavenly Father.”"],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow has a warm, happy heart, and tonight Miriam helped everyone share their thankful hearts with the Lord. In Willow’s world today: Willow’s grandma had been sick, and now she was better. Willow felt so glad that she made up a little thank-you song and sang it for her family. Just like Miriam, Willow learned that we can thank Heavenly Father with a happy song.","feel":"Pretend you have a little drum in your hand. Tap it softly on your blanket: tap, tap, tap. Can you show me your happy, thank-you face?","stones":[["SPOT","The people were safe, and they felt so happy and thankful that they sang and danced."],["NAME","That bright, bubbly, thank-you feeling is called joy. Your mind might think, ‘The Lord helped us!’ Your body might feel light, with a big smile and dancing feet."],["FIND","When have you felt so happy that you wanted to sing?"],["FORK","The people could forget to say thank you, or sing their thanks to the Lord. Miriam led them in singing."],["CALM","Put your hands over your happy heart. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for keeping me safe.”"]],"remember":"I can thank the Lord with a happy song!","ask":["“What did Miriam play?”","“Why did the people sing to the Lord?”","“Who did Willow sing her thank-you song for?”"],"share":["Which song helps you feel close to the Lord, and why?","When has your heart felt so thankful that you wanted to sing?","Which grandparent or ancestor loved to sing or make music? Share a memory of them."]},"b4-w3-n4":{"story":["Moses led the people away from the sea into a wide, dry land. They walked for three days and found no water.","At last they found water, but it tasted bitter and yucky. The people grumbled at Moses, “What shall we drink?”","Moses did not grumble. He prayed to the Lord. The Lord showed Moses a tree. Moses threw it into the water, and the water turned sweet!","The Lord was teaching His people to trust Him. He said, “Listen to Me. I am the Lord who heals you.” Then He led them to Elim, with twelve wells and seventy palm trees.","When something feels bitter or hard, you can pray like Moses. Heavenly Father can help hard things turn sweet."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren likes to think things through, and tonight Moses stopped to pray instead of grumbling. In Wren’s world today: One puzzle piece would not fit, and Wren wanted to give up. Instead, she took a slow breath and asked her grandpa for help. He showed her where it went. Just like Moses, Wren learned that asking for help works better than grumbling.","feel":"Make a sour, bitter face, all scrunched up. Now let it melt into a sweet, happy smile. Can you show me your sweet-water smile?","stones":[["SPOT","The water tasted bitter and the people grumbled, but Moses prayed and the Lord helped."],["NAME","That grumpy, nothing-is-right feeling is called frustration. Your mind might think, ‘This is not fair!’ Your body might feel hot cheeks and a tight, frowning face."],["FIND","When have you felt grumpy because something did not go your way?"],["FORK","The people could grumble, or pray and trust. Moses prayed, and the Lord made the water sweet."],["CALM","Unscrunch your face and breathe slowly. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me pray instead of grumble.”"]],"remember":"When things are hard, I can pray, and the Lord will help me!","ask":["“How did the water taste at first?”","“What did the Lord show Moses?”","“Who helped Wren with her puzzle?”"],"share":["When has the Lord turned something bitter in your life into something sweet?","What helps you choose to pray instead of complain?","Which grandparent or ancestor found the Lord’s help in a hard place? Share their story."]},"b4-w3-n5":{"story":["The Lord said, “I will rain bread from heaven for you.” He wanted to see if they would trust Him.","In the morning, tiny round flakes lay on the ground, like frost. The people asked, “What is it?” They called it manna. It tasted like thin crackers with honey.","Moses said, “Gather just what you need. Do not save any for morning.” Some saved extra anyway. By morning it was wormy and smelly!","On the sixth day, they gathered twice as much. The seventh day was the Sabbath, the Lord’s day to rest. That extra manna stayed fresh, and the people rested.","The Lord gave them enough, one day at a time. Tonight, thank Him for today’s food. He will care for you tomorrow too."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau has strong faith, and tonight the people needed faith to trust the Lord one morning at a time. In Beau’s world today: Beau wanted to hide all the crackers in his room, just in case. Beau’s mom smiled and said, “There will be more tomorrow.” Beau ate just a few. Just like the people of Israel, Beau learned that we can trust the Lord to give us enough each day.","feel":"Pretend to pick tiny manna flakes off your blanket: one, two, three. Put them in your basket hands. Can you show me how you gather just enough?","stones":[["SPOT","The people gathered manna every morning and learned to trust the Lord for each new day."],["NAME","That calm, safe, I-have-enough feeling is called trust. Your mind might think, ‘The Lord will help me tomorrow too.’ Your body might feel relaxed, with soft, easy breaths."],["FIND","When have you trusted someone to take care of you?"],["FORK","The people could hide extra manna, or trust the Lord for tomorrow. The ones who trusted had fresh bread every day."],["CALM","Open your hands like you are holding manna. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, I trust You for tomorrow.”"]],"remember":"The Lord gives me what I need, one day at a time!","ask":["“What did the manna look like on the ground?”","“What happened to manna saved for the next day?”","“What did Beau’s mom say about the crackers?”"],"share":["How has the Lord given you just enough, one day at a time?","What helps our family make the Sabbath a day to rest and remember the Lord?","Which grandparent or ancestor trusted the Lord when food or money was scarce? Share their story."]},"b4-w3-n6":{"story":["The people came to a dry place with no water. They were thirsty, and so were their children. They argued with Moses, “Give us water!”","Moses prayed, “What shall I do?” The Lord said, “Take your walking stick. I will stand before you on the rock. Hit the rock, and water will come out.”","Moses hit the rock. Out came the water, and the people drank!","The people had asked, “Is the Lord with us, or not?” He was right there! Long after, Paul, who taught about Jesus, wrote, “That Rock was Christ.” He meant Jesus was with them all along.","Jesus is strong like a rock, and He is with you too. Tonight, think of something you need, and ask Him for help."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout loves to find hidden things, and tonight he found that the rock was a picture of Jesus. In Scout’s world today: On a hot hike, Scout felt tired and thirsty. He and his dad sat on a big, strong rock to rest, and Dad shared cool water. Just like the people of Israel, Scout learned that the Lord gives us what we need in dry places.","feel":"Make a fist like a strong rock. Now slowly open your hand and wiggle your fingers like water coming out. Can you show me the water flowing?","stones":[["SPOT","The people were thirsty and worried, then water came from the rock, and they felt glad."],["NAME","That ahh, all-better feeling is called relief. Your mind might think, ‘The Lord did help us!’ Your body might feel loose and soft, like a long, happy sigh."],["FIND","When have you felt all better after someone helped you?"],["FORK","The people could argue with Moses, or ask the Lord for help. Moses asked the Lord, and water came."],["CALM","Breathe in a big breath, then sigh it out. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for giving me what I need.”"]],"remember":"Jesus is my strong rock, and He gives me what I need!","ask":["“What did Moses hit with his staff?”","“What came out of the rock?”","“Where did Scout sit to rest?”"],"share":["When have you wondered, “Is the Lord with me?” and then felt Him near?","How has Jesus been like a strong rock in your life?","Which grandparent or ancestor stood strong in their faith? Share a story about them."]},"b4-w3-n7":{"story":["Jethro, the father of Moses’s wife, came to visit Moses. Moses told him all the Lord had done to save the people. Jethro was so glad!","The next day, the people came to Moses with all their problems. They waited from morning until night. Moses was helping them all by himself.","Jethro said, “This is too heavy for you. You cannot do it alone.”","Jethro said, “Choose good, honest men who honor the Lord to help you.” Moses listened. The helpers took care of the small problems. Moses helped with the hard ones, and the work felt lighter.","Big jobs feel lighter when we share them. You can be a helper too. Tonight, think of one way you can help tomorrow."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow has a warm, caring heart, and tonight Jethro cared about Moses and helped him not be so tired. In Willow’s world today: Willow’s mom had a big pile of laundry to fold. Willow sat beside her and folded all the little socks. The work went faster, and Mom smiled. Just like Moses’s helpers, Willow learned that sharing the work makes a big job lighter.","feel":"Hug a big pretend pile of work. Oof, it is heavy! Now hand some to a helper beside you. Can you show me how light your arms feel?","stones":[["SPOT","Moses was tired from doing all the work alone, and he felt thankful when helpers came."],["NAME","That warm, light, not-alone feeling is called thankfulness. Your mind might think, ‘I am glad you are here.’ Your body might feel light shoulders and a soft smile."],["FIND","When has someone helped you with a big job?"],["FORK","Moses could keep doing everything alone, or listen to Jethro and share the work. Moses listened."],["CALM","Let your shoulders drop and relax. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for my helpers.”"]],"remember":"When we help each other, the work feels lighter and happier!","ask":["“Who came to visit Moses?”","“What did Jethro tell Moses to do?”","“What did Willow fold for her mom?”"],"share":["Who has helped you carry a load that was too heavy?","How could our family share the work at home more kindly?","Which grandparent or ancestor was a wise helper like Jethro? Share a story about them."]},"b4-w4-n1":{"story":["Heavenly Father’s people walked out of Egypt. They came to a tall mountain called Sinai.","Moses went up the mountain, and the Lord spoke to him. The Lord was Jesus. He said, “I carried you on eagles’ wings and brought you to Me.”","Big, strong eagles fly high on wide wings. The Lord meant He had carried His people safely.","Then the Lord asked them to make a covenant with Him. That is a promise that goes both ways. If they kept it, they would be His special treasure. All the people said, “All that the Lord has said, we will do!”","The Lord carries you too. Tonight, hug your pillow like soft wings. Heavenly Father holds you close and keeps you safe."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow has a warm, loving heart, and tonight the Lord tells how lovingly He carried His people. In Willow’s world today: Willow’s little brother got tired on a long walk. Willow held his hand and helped him all the way home. Just like the Lord, who carried His people, Willow learned that love helps carry others when they are tired.","feel":"Stretch your arms out wide like eagle wings. Now fold them in and hug yourself softly. Can you show me your safe, cozy wings?","stones":[["SPOT","The Lord told His people He had carried them safely. They felt loved and safe."],["NAME","That warm, held-close, safe feeling is called being loved. Your mind might think, ‘Someone takes care of me.’ Your body might feel warm and soft in your chest."],["FIND","When have you felt safe and held close by someone who loves you?"],["FORK","The people could say no to the Lord, or make a promise with Him. They promised, “We will do it!”"],["CALM","Wrap your arms around yourself. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for carrying me.”"]],"remember":"The Lord carries me with love, and I will follow Him!","ask":["“What big bird did the Lord talk about?”","“What did all the people promise?”","“How did Willow help her little brother?”"],"share":["When have you felt the Lord carry you through a hard time?","What promises has our family made with Heavenly Father?","Which grandparent or ancestor kept their promises to the Lord? Share a story about them."]},"b4-w4-n2":{"story":["The Lord told Moses, “I will come to you in a thick cloud. Then the people can hear Me talk with you.”","On the third morning, thunder boomed and lightning flashed. A trumpet blew, louder and louder.","The whole mountain shook and smoked. The Lord had come down on it in fire. The people at the bottom trembled. It was so big and so loud!","Moses spoke, and the Lord answered him. Then the Lord called Moses up to the very top. Moses told the people, “Do not be afraid.” The Lord had come to help them choose the right.","The Lord is mighty and strong, and He loves you. Tonight, listen to the quiet, and feel how great and good He is."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout loves to discover big, amazing things, and tonight the whole mountain shook and smoked! In Scout’s world today: One night a storm rolled in. Scout sat close to Dad by the window. Flash went the lightning! Boom went the thunder! Just like the people at the mountain, Scout learned that Heavenly Father’s world is big and amazing.","feel":"Pat your legs fast like rumbling thunder. Now slower, slower… and stop. Can you show me your big, wondering eyes?","stones":[["SPOT","The mountain thundered and smoked when the Lord came down. The people felt the Lord’s great power."],["NAME","That big, wow, hold-your-breath feeling is called awe. Your mind might think, ‘Heavenly Father is so great!’ Your body might feel very still, with wide eyes."],["FIND","When have you seen something so big it made you say, “Wow”?"],["FORK","Moses could stay far away, or go close to the Lord. Moses went up to the top to listen."],["CALM","Take one slow, quiet breath. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, You are great, and You love me.”"]],"remember":"The Lord is mighty and strong, and He loves me!","ask":["“What did the mountain do when the Lord came down?”","“What did Moses tell the people?”","“What did Scout watch from the window?”"],"share":["When have you felt awe for the Lord’s power?","How can we show reverence when we feel the Lord is near?","Which grandparent or ancestor told you about a time the Lord helped them? Share it."]},"b4-w4-n3":{"story":["On the mountain, the Lord gave His people ten commandments. They are rules from Heavenly Father that keep us safe and happy.","The Lord said, “I am the Lord, who brought you out of Egypt.” First, let nothing be more important than the Lord.","Second, do not make statues to bow down to. A statue cannot hear us or love us. Third, say the Lord’s name only with love and respect.","Fourth, remember the Sabbath day and keep it holy. The Lord made the world in six days and rested on the seventh. It is a day to rest and think about Heavenly Father.","You can put Heavenly Father first too. Tonight, fold your arms and tell Him you love Him."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau is brave, and it takes a brave heart to put Heavenly Father first. In Beau’s world today: Beau was building a tall block tower when Mom called, “Time for family prayer!” Beau wanted to keep building, but he hurried in to pray. Just like the people at the mountain, Beau learned that putting Heavenly Father first makes hearts glad.","feel":"Hold up one finger, then two, three, four, for tonight’s four commandments. Now wiggle them softly. Can you show me your four fingers?","stones":[["SPOT","The Lord taught His people to love Him first, and they felt His love."],["NAME","That warm, full, first-place feeling is called love. Your mind might think, ‘I love Heavenly Father most.’ Your body might feel warm and happy in your heart."],["FIND","When have you felt your love for Heavenly Father?"],["FORK","The people could love other things more, or put the Lord first. They promised to put Him first."],["CALM","Fold your arms gently. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, I love You most of all.”"]],"remember":"I can love Heavenly Father first, say His name kindly, and keep His day special!","ask":["“How many commandments did the Lord give?”","“What is the Sabbath day for?”","“What did Beau do when it was time to pray?”"],"share":["How has keeping the Sabbath day blessed your life?","What helps you put Heavenly Father first each day?","Which grandparent or ancestor showed great reverence for the Lord’s name? Share a story about them."]},"b4-w4-n4":{"story":["The Lord gave six more commandments. These teach us how to treat the people around us.","Fifth, honor your father and mother. To honor them is to love them, listen to them, and show respect. Sixth, do not kill. Seventh, keep the promises of marriage.","Eighth, do not steal. Ninth, do not tell lies about others. Tenth, do not wish for things that belong to others.","Many years later, Jesus told the two greatest commandments. First, love Heavenly Father with all your heart. Second, love others as you love yourself. All ten commandments help us do those two things!","Tonight, think of one kind thing you can do tomorrow. Being kind is a way to love others, just as Jesus taught."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow has a warm, kind heart, and tonight’s commandments are all about loving others. In Willow’s world today: Willow’s friend had a shiny new toy. Willow wished it were hers, but she smiled and said, “That is so fun! Can we play together?” Just like the Lord taught His people, Willow learned that kind hearts are glad for others.","feel":"Give yourself a gentle hug for Mom and Dad. Now hold out one open, sharing hand. Can you show me your kind, gentle hands?","stones":[["SPOT","The Lord taught His people to be kind to each other, so everyone could feel safe and loved."],["NAME","That soft, giving, caring feeling is called kindness. Your mind might think, ‘I want to help.’ Your body might feel warm, with gentle, open hands."],["FIND","When have you been kind to someone in your family?"],["FORK","The people could be unkind to each other, or follow the Lord’s rules. They promised to follow the Lord."],["CALM","Open your hands softly on your blanket. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me love my family.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father’s commandments teach me to love Him and to love others!","ask":["“What does it mean to honor your mom and dad?”","“What did Jesus say are the two greatest commandments?”","“What did Willow say about her friend’s toy?”"],"share":["How have the commandments protected our family?","When has being honest blessed your life?","Which grandparent or ancestor honored their parents in a special way? Share a story about them."]},"b4-w4-n5":{"story":["The Lord said, “Moses, come up to Me on the mountain. I will give you flat stones with My commandments written on them.” Then Moses could teach the people.","Moses climbed up with his helper Joshua. A cloud covered the mountain for six days. On the seventh day, the Lord called Moses into the cloud.","Far below, the people watched. The Lord’s glory shone on the mountaintop, bright like a fire.","Moses stayed with the Lord for forty days and forty nights. That is more than a whole month! Then the Lord gave Moses two flat stones, written with the Lord’s own finger.","Moses waited and listened a long time. Tonight, lie very still and listen. Heavenly Father is near you too."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren likes quiet thinking times, and tonight Moses spent many quiet days with the Lord. In Wren’s world today: Wren and Grandma baked cookies. Wren wanted to open the oven right away, but she waited and read a book with Grandma. Soon the cookies were warm and ready! Just like Moses, Wren learned that good things come when we wait patiently.","feel":"Walk your fingers up your blanket like Moses climbing the mountain. Now cup your hands around you like a soft cloud. Can you show me your quiet, waiting face?","stones":[["SPOT","Moses waited forty days on the mountain with the Lord. He was patient and listened."],["NAME","That quiet, still, waiting feeling is called patience. Your mind might think, ‘I can wait.’ Your body might feel calm and slow, with soft breathing."],["FIND","When have you waited a long time for something good?"],["FORK","Moses could hurry back down, or stay and listen. Moses stayed forty days with the Lord."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and out slowly. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me wait and listen.”"]],"remember":"When I wait and listen, I can learn from Heavenly Father!","ask":["“What covered the mountain?”","“How long did Moses stay with the Lord?”","“What did Wren and Grandma bake?”"],"share":["When have you waited on the Lord, and what did you learn?","Where is a quiet place in our home to listen to Heavenly Father?","Which grandparent or ancestor was patient through a long, hard time? Share a story about them."]},"b4-w4-n6":{"story":["Moses was up on the mountain a long time. The people asked Aaron to make them something to follow.","Aaron melted their gold earrings and made a gold calf, a baby cow. The people bowed down to it. They forgot the Lord, who brought them out of Egypt.","Moses came down and saw the calf. He was very upset. He threw down the Lord’s stones, and they broke. He burned up the calf.","The next day, Moses said, “You have done a very wrong thing.” But he still loved them. He went back and asked the Lord to forgive them.","When we are sorry, Jesus helps us start again. Tonight, tell Him if you are sorry for anything. His love is always there for you."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau is brave, and it takes courage to remember what is right when others forget. In Beau’s world today: At a friend’s house, some kids grabbed cookies they were told not to take. Beau said, “No thanks. Let’s ask first.” Just like Moses, Beau learned that brave hearts remember what is right, even when others forget.","feel":"Pretend to hold heavy stone tablets. Set them down very gently. Now put both hands on your heart. Can you show me your remembering hands?","stones":[["SPOT","The people forgot the Lord and made a gold idol. Moses felt upset, but he still loved them."],["NAME","That hot, tight, this-is-wrong feeling is called upset. Your mind might think, ‘This is not right!’ Your body might feel hot in your face and tight in your hands."],["FIND","When have you felt upset because something was wrong?"],["FORK","Moses could give up on the people, or ask the Lord to forgive them. Moses asked the Lord to forgive them."],["CALM","Squeeze your hands tight, then let them go soft. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me remember You.”"]],"remember":"When I forget, I can say sorry, and Jesus helps me start again!","ask":["“What did Aaron make out of gold?”","“What did Moses ask the Lord to do?”","“What did Beau say about the cookies?”"],"share":["When have you felt the Lord forgive you and help you start again?","What helps our family remember the Lord every day?","Which grandparent or ancestor stood up for what was right? Share a story about them."]},"b4-w4-n7":{"story":["The Lord talked with Moses face to face, like a friend. He said, “Cut two new flat stones. I will write My words on them again.”","Early in the morning, Moses climbed the mountain all alone. He carried the two stones.","Moses stayed with the Lord forty days and forty nights. The ten commandments were written on the new stones.","When Moses came down, his face shone with light! It shone because he had been talking with the Lord. Moses did not even know. The people were afraid to come near. But Moses called them, and they came, and he taught them.","You can be close to Heavenly Father too, when you pray. Tonight, smile a soft, bright smile. His light can shine in you."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout loves to notice things, and tonight everyone noticed that Moses’s face was shining. In Scout’s world today: Scout said his morning prayer, then skipped to breakfast. “You look so happy and bright today!” said Mom. Just like Moses, Scout learned that time with Heavenly Father can fill us with light.","feel":"Rub your hands together until they feel warm. Now hold them softly on your cheeks like warm light. Can you show me your shining smile?","stones":[["SPOT","Moses’s face shone after talking with the Lord. The people were amazed to see it."],["NAME","That bright, glowing, full-of-light feeling is called joy. Your mind might think, ‘Heavenly Father is near me.’ Your body might feel warm, with a big, soft smile."],["FIND","When have you felt bright and happy after praying?"],["FORK","The people could stay away from Moses, or come close and listen. They came close, and Moses taught them."],["CALM","Smile softly and close your eyes. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me stay close to You.”"]],"remember":"When I stay close to Heavenly Father, His light can shine in me!","ask":["“How many new stone tablets did Moses carry?”","“Why did Moses’s face shine?”","“What did Mom say when she saw Scout?”"],"share":["When have you seen the Lord’s light in someone’s face?","How do our family prayers bring light into our home?","Which grandparent or ancestor had a face that shone with goodness? Share a story about them."]},"b8-w1-n1":{"story":["Long ago, Heavenly Father’s people were taken far away to Babylon. Their real home was Jerusalem. The Lord’s house there had been knocked down.","Years later, a new king named Cyrus ruled the land. The Lord put a good idea into his heart.","King Cyrus wrote to all his people. He said, “Go up to Jerusalem and build the Lord’s house!”","Many people got ready to go. Their neighbors helped with silver, gold and animals. King Cyrus even gave back the Lord’s own gold and silver dishes!","Heavenly Father never forgot His people far from home. He never forgets you either. Tonight, hug your pillow and thank Him for your home."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout fits tonight because going home took trust that Heavenly Father would lead the way. In Scout’s world today: Scout’s family just moved to a brand-new house, and the new room felt strange and a little scary. But Scout took a big breath, remembered Heavenly Father leads His children, and went exploring — finding a friendly neighbor’s dog and the coziest reading corner. “Heavenly Father helped me find good things here!” Just like the people trusted Him on the road home, Scout trusted Him in a brand-new place.","feel":"Let’s pack a pretend bag! Reach out and “grab” your favorite things and tuck them inside. Now sling it over your shoulder and take three big marching steps — we’re going home! Can you feel how happy a big journey feels?","stones":[["SPOT","Cyrus and the people felt hopeful and excited to go home."],["NAME","That happy, can’t-wait feeling is called excited — and a little bit nervous, too."],["FIND","When have you felt excited and nervous at the very same time — like a first day somewhere new?"],["FORK","A nervous feeling can make us want to hide — or we can hold Heavenly Father’s hand and go bravely. The people chose to trust Him and go."],["CALM","Take one slow breath together and let it out slow. Say, “Heavenly Father is with me.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father keeps His promises to YOU, just like He did for His people going home. When He asks you to do something good, you can trust Him with your whole heart.","ask":["“Who let Heavenly Father’s people go home?”","“What did the people go home to build?”"],"share":["When has Heavenly Father kept a promise to you, even after a long wait? Tell your child how it felt to finally see it come true.","Describe a time He softened someone’s heart to help your family in a way you didn’t expect.","Did a grandparent or ancestor make a long journey or a big move because they trusted Heavenly Father? Share where they were going and why."]},"b8-w1-n2":{"story":["Heavenly Father’s people were home in Jerusalem at last. They began to build the Lord’s house again. First they laid the foundation, the strong bottom part that holds it up.","Then the priests, who served in the Lord’s house, blew trumpets. Other helpers clanged cymbals.","They all sang together, “The Lord is good! His kindness lasts forever!”","The people gave a great shout of joy! But some old men cried out loud. They remembered the first house of the Lord from long ago. The happy shouts and sad cries were so loud, people far away heard them.","Happy and sad can come together, and Heavenly Father understands both. Tonight, tell Him one happy thing and one sad thing from your day."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow fits tonight because she notices every kind of feeling. In Willow’s world today: at Grandma’s birthday, Willow saw Grandma cry — but they were happy tears! Instead of feeling worried, Willow climbed up for a hug and said, “Your happy tears are okay, Grandma.” Just like the people who laughed and cried while they praised Heavenly Father, Willow learned that joy and tears can live in the very same heart.","feel":"Let’s make a joyful noise! Clap your hands like clanging cymbals — crash, crash! Now hum a happy little “la-la-la.” Some feelings are so big they bring happy tears. Can you show me a happy face… and a teary face?","stones":[["SPOT","The people felt joyful, and some felt tender and teary too."],["NAME","That full-up, tender-happy feeling is called joy."],["FIND","When did something make you SO happy you almost cried, or felt full inside?"],["FORK","A big feeling can bubble over into a fuss — or we can let it out gently in a song or a hug. The people let theirs out in praise to Heavenly Father."],["CALM","Put a hand on your heart and hum one slow, happy note together."]],"remember":"Your feelings are a gift from Heavenly Father — even the big ones! It’s okay to feel happy and cry at the very same time. He loves your whole heart.","ask":["“What did the people build first?”","“Why did some of the older people cry?”"],"share":["When has worship or a sacred moment brought happy tears to your eyes? Share what you were feeling.","Tell your child about a “foundation” moment in your family — a small beginning that grew into something you treasure.","Did a grandparent or ancestor help build or begin something for Heavenly Father — a chapel, a home, a family tradition? Describe it."]},"b8-w1-n3":{"story":["Heavenly Father’s people were building the Lord’s house. But some people nearby did not want it built. They made trouble until the work stopped.","For years and years, the Lord’s house sat unfinished.","Then the Lord sent two prophets, Haggai and Zechariah, to share His words. They cheered the builders on and helped them start again.","The builders worked and worked. At last, the Lord’s house was finished! All the people came together to give it to the Lord. Their hearts were full of joy.","When something is hard, you can start again too. Heavenly Father will help you, one little step at a time. Tonight, think of one hard thing you will try again tomorrow."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau fits tonight because he keeps going when things get hard. In Beau’s world today: Beau was building a tall block tower and it kept toppling — crash! Beau wanted to quit and cry. But he took a breath, said a little prayer, and tried again, stacking slower and steadier, until the tower stood tall. “I didn’t give up!” Just like the people who finished Heavenly Father’s temple, Beau finished the hard thing with Heavenly Father’s help.","feel":"Let’s build together! Stack your hands one on top of the other, higher and higher — tap, tap, tap. Now throw your arms up high: “It’s finished!” Doesn’t it feel good to finish something you worked hard on?","stones":[["SPOT","At first the builders felt discouraged when others tried to stop them."],["NAME","That heavy, want-to-quit feeling is called discouraged."],["FIND","When have you felt like giving up because something was too hard?"],["FORK","Feeling discouraged can make us stop — or we can take a breath, ask for help, and try again. The builders kept going and trusted Heavenly Father."],["CALM","Breathe in slow, then say together, “I can try again.”"]],"remember":"When something good is hard, Heavenly Father helps you keep going! Maybe you’re learning to tie your shoes or clean your whole room. Don’t give up — He is right there with you.","ask":["“Who told the people to keep building?”","“How did everyone feel when the temple was finished?”"],"share":["When did you finish something hard because you trusted Heavenly Father to help? Tell the story.","Who were the “Haggai and Zechariah” voices that cheered you on when you wanted to quit?","Did a grandparent or ancestor persevere through hard times to finish something important? Share their grit and faith."]},"b8-w1-n4":{"story":["A long, long time ago, Heavenly Father's people had to live far away from home, in a land called Babylon. Their own city, Jerusalem, was empty. After many years, the king said, \"You may go home!\" So the people packed up and started the long walk back.","One of those people was a man named Ezra. Ezra loved Heavenly Father very much. And do you know what Ezra loved to read? The scriptures. The scriptures are the books that hold Heavenly Father's words, the true stories He wants us to know.","Ezra did not read them fast and forget them. He read a little, and stopped, and thought about it. He wanted to know the words so well that he could do them, and then tell them to everybody else. That is why people said Ezra was a teacher of Heavenly Father's words.","The king trusted Ezra. He said, \"Go back home to Jerusalem, and teach the people Heavenly Father's ways.\" So Ezra walked a very long way, and he carried the scriptures with him. He held them like a treasure. A treasure is something so precious you keep it close and never lose it — and Heavenly Father's words were Ezra's treasure. Heavenly Father watched over Ezra the whole, long way.","When Ezra got home, he read the words out loud to everyone, and the people listened and listened. Tonight you are doing the very same thing Ezra did. You are listening to Heavenly Father's words at bedtime. Every story you hear goes into your heart and stays there, like a little treasure you get to keep."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren fits tonight because she slows down to let good things sink in. In Wren’s world today: at story time, everyone was rushing to the next page, but Wren wanted to stop and think. “Wait — what did that part mean?” she wondered. She read it again and found something wonderful. Just like Ezra, who set his heart to study Heavenly Father’s words, Wren learned that pondering slowly helps the good things stay in your heart.","feel":"Let’s open a pretend book! Hold out both hands like pages and “read” with your finger, left to right. Now hug the book close to your heart. Heavenly Father’s words are a treasure. Can you show me how much you love them with a great big squeeze?","stones":[["SPOT","Ezra loved Heavenly Father's words so much that he carried them all the way home and read them out loud to everyone."],["NAME","That warm, happy, want-more feeling is called eager. Your mind might think, \"Tell it again!\" Your body might feel wiggly and awake, with your eyes open wide."],["FIND","When did you feel eager today? Maybe you wanted the same story one more time, or you ran to the door when someone you love came home."],["FORK","When we feel eager, there are two roads. One road rushes ahead and misses the best part. The other road slows down and holds it close. Ezra took the slow road — he stopped, and thought, and kept it."],["CALM","Snuggle down and take one slow breath in and out. Then whisper, \"Heavenly Father, thank you for your words. Help me keep them in my heart.\""]],"remember":"Heavenly Father's words can be my treasure too, just like Ezra's!","ask":["“Where had Ezra and his people been living before they walked home?”","“What did Ezra carry all the way, like a treasure?”","“What is one scripture story you would love to hear again?”"],"share":["When did a scripture settle into your heart and change how you lived? Share that verse and moment.","How have you tried to “set your heart” to study Heavenly Father’s word? Tell your child about a habit you love.","Did a grandparent or ancestor treasure the scriptures? Describe how their love of Heavenly Father’s word touched your family."]},"b8-w1-n5":{"story":["Nehemiah worked for a great king far away. His job was to bring the king his wine.","One day, Nehemiah’s face looked sad. The king asked, “Why is your face so sad?” Nehemiah felt very afraid.","Nehemiah said, “Jerusalem, my family’s city, is broken. Its gates were burned.” Its strong walls, which kept the people safe, were broken down too.","The king asked, “What do you want?” First, Nehemiah prayed to Heavenly Father. Then he said, “Please send me to build it again.” The king said yes! He gave Nehemiah letters and wood to help.","Heavenly Father hears you anytime, even when you pray in your heart. Tonight, tell Him one thing you need help with."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau fits tonight because a prayer helped him feel brave. In Beau’s world today: Beau had a big pile of toys to clean up and it felt too big to start. His tummy felt worried. So Beau said a quick prayer — “Please help me” — took a deep breath, and picked up just one toy, then another. Soon the whole room was tidy! Just like Nehemiah prayed and then began the big work, Beau prayed and started, one little step at a time.","feel":"Let’s be brave like Nehemiah! First, fold your arms and bow your head to pray — soft and quiet. Now stand up tall, put your hands on your hips, and say, “Let us rise up and build!” Can you feel your prayer turning into brave?","stones":[["SPOT","Nehemiah felt sad and worried when he heard the bad news."],["NAME","That heavy, worried feeling is called worried."],["FIND","When has your heart felt heavy or worried about something?"],["FORK","A worried feeling can freeze us — or we can pray, then begin one small step. Nehemiah prayed first, and then he started to build."],["CALM","Fold your arms, bow your head, and take one slow breath with Heavenly Father."]],"remember":"When your heart feels heavy or scared, you can pray — and Heavenly Father will help you be brave! Then you can start your job one little step at a time, just like Nehemiah.","ask":["“What did Nehemiah do first when his heart felt heavy?”","“What did he want to rebuild?”"],"share":["When did prayer turn your heavy heart into a brave one? Share the before and the after.","Tell your child about a big, scary task you began “one step at a time” with Heavenly Father’s help.","Did a grandparent or ancestor pray for courage before a hard task, then step forward in faith? Describe their bravery."]},"b8-w1-n6":{"story":["The wall around Jerusalem was almost done. Only the doors for the gates were left.","Some men did not want the wall finished. They sent Nehemiah a message: “Come down and meet with us.” But they wanted to make trouble for him.","Nehemiah sent back his answer. “I am doing a great work, so I cannot come down. Why should the work stop?”","Four times they asked, and four times Nehemiah said the same thing. The wall was finished in just 52 days! Even the people all around knew Heavenly Father had helped.","Your good work is great too, like being kind and helping at home. Tonight, think of one good job you want to finish tomorrow."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren fits tonight because she stops to think about what matters most. In Wren’s world today: Wren was cleaning up like Mom asked when a friend called, “Come play right now!” Wren paused and wondered, “What’s most important first?” She finished her job, then went to play with a happy heart. Just like Nehemiah wouldn’t come down from his great work, Wren knew the difference between a silly distraction and an important job.","feel":"Let’s stay strong on the wall! Plant your feet, stand up tall, and shake your head “no, no, no” to the silly distractions. Now say it big: “I am doing a great work!” Can you keep building with your hands while you say it?","stones":[["SPOT","The grumpy men’s teasing might have made Nehemiah feel annoyed or pulled-away."],["NAME","That tugged-in-two-directions feeling is called distracted."],["FIND","When have you felt pulled away from something important by something silly?"],["FORK","A distraction can pull us off our good work — or we can say, “Not now,” and stay on task. Nehemiah stayed on the wall and finished."],["CALM","Stand tall, take one strong breath, and say, “I can finish my job.”"]],"remember":"You can keep doing good, even when others try to pull you away! Like Nehemiah, you can say, “I have important work to do.” Finishing your good jobs makes Heavenly Father happy.","ask":["“What did the grumpy men want Nehemiah to do?”","“What did Nehemiah say back to them?”"],"share":["When did you stay focused on something good while others tried to pull you away? Share how you kept going.","How do you decide what matters most when life gets noisy and distracting? Tell your child your secret.","Did a grandparent or ancestor stay true to an important work despite discouragement? Describe their steadiness."]},"b8-w1-n7":{"story":["Ezra loved the Lord’s words. He got his heart ready to learn them, live them and teach them.","All the people gathered in Jerusalem. Men, women and all who could understand came to listen.","Ezra stood up high on a wooden stand. He opened the book of the Lord’s commandments, and all the people stood up! He read from morning until noon.","Helpers explained the words so everyone understood. The people cried when they heard the Lord’s words. But Nehemiah and Ezra said, “Do not be sad. The joy of the Lord is your strength!” So the people shared food and were very glad.","The Lord’s words can make you glad and strong too. Tonight, ask a grown-up to read you one verse."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout fits tonight because Scout loves to discover treasure and share it. In Scout’s world today: at preschool, Scout learned a happy new song about Heavenly Father and could hardly wait to share it. Scout ran home, calling, “Listen! I learned something wonderful!” and taught it to the whole family. Just like the people who found joy in Heavenly Father’s words, Scout discovered that the best things are even better when we share them.","feel":"Let’s celebrate the scriptures! Cup your hands around your mouth and cheer, “Hooray for Heavenly Father’s word!” Now rub your tummy and share a pretend treat with someone you love. Can you turn a sad face into a great big joyful smile?","stones":[["SPOT","The people felt sorry at first, then Heavenly Father’s words made them glad."],["NAME","That turned-around, better-now feeling is called comforted."],["FIND","When has a hug or a kind word turned your sad feeling into a happy one?"],["FORK","A sad feeling can stay stuck — or Heavenly Father’s words and love can turn it to joy. The people let His word cheer their hearts."],["CALM","Take a breath, then trade a great big smile with someone you love."]],"remember":"Heavenly Father’s words can make your heart glad and strong, even on a hard day! When you listen to scripture stories, joy sneaks right into your heart.","ask":["“What did Ezra read to all the people?”","“What did the leaders say would be the people’s strength?”"],"share":["When have Heavenly Father’s words turned your sorrow into joy or strength? Share that tender experience.","How does “the joy of the Lord” strengthen you on a hard day? Tell your child what that joy feels like.","Did a grandparent or ancestor find strength and joy in the scriptures during a difficult season? Describe their example."]},"b8-w2-n1":{"story":["Long ago, King Ahasuerus ruled a very big land. He lived in a palace in the city of Shushan.","In Shushan lived a man named Mordecai. He was a Jew, one of Heavenly Father’s people of Israel.","Mordecai’s young cousin Esther had no mother or father. So Mordecai took her home and raised her as his own daughter.","Esther grew up. The king loved her and put a royal crown on her head. Now Esther was the queen! Mordecai told her not to tell anyone yet that she was a Jew.","Mordecai loved Esther like a dad. Heavenly Father loves you like that too. Tonight, give a big hug to someone who cares for you."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow fits tonight because a kind heart is her superpower. In Willow’s world today: a new child stood alone at the playground, looking shy. Willow could have kept playing, but instead she walked over and said, “Want to play with me?” The new friend beamed. Just like Esther’s kindness shone in the palace, Willow’s kindness helped someone feel welcome and loved.","feel":"Let’s put on a pretend crown! Place your hands gently on your head like a sparkly crown, then take a little bow. A crown is nice — but a kind heart is even better. Can you show me your very kindest smile?","stones":[["SPOT","Esther may have felt shy and unsure in the big new palace."],["NAME","That small, unsure feeling is called shy."],["FIND","When have you felt shy somewhere new, like a new class or a big party?"],["FORK","A shy feeling can make us hide — or we can be brave and share a kind smile. Esther stayed kind and gentle even when everything was new."],["CALM","Take a slow breath and picture Heavenly Father right beside you."]],"remember":"Your kind heart is beautiful to Heavenly Father — more than any crown! You can be gentle and kind wherever you go, just like Esther.","ask":["“Who took care of Esther and loved her?”","“What did the king choose Esther to be?”"],"share":["When has a quiet act of kindness mattered more than anything fancy? Share that memory.","Tell your child about a time Heavenly Father placed you somewhere for a reason you only understood later.","Did a grandparent or ancestor become known for gentle kindness? Describe how their warmth blessed the family."]},"b8-w2-n2":{"story":["The king made a man named Haman his most important leader. Everyone had to bow low to Haman to show him honor.","But Mordecai would not bow. Haman was full of anger and pride. He thought he was better than everyone else.","Haman knew Mordecai was a Jew. So he made a mean plan to hurt all of Mordecai’s people.","Haman told the king, “These people do not keep your laws.” So the king gave Haman his ring, which stamped the king’s rules.","Don’t worry, this story has a happy ending! Haman chose pride, but Jesus teaches us to be kind. Tonight, think of one kind thing to do tomorrow."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau fits tonight because he does what’s right even when it’s hard. In Beau’s world today: some kids were teasing another child, and they wanted Beau to join in. Beau felt his tummy flip, but he shook his head and said, “That’s not kind.” Then he went and sat by the child who was sad. Just like Mordecai stood tall for Heavenly Father, Beau stood tall for what was right, even when it wasn’t easy.","feel":"Let’s stand tall like Mordecai! Put your feet strong on the floor, straighten your back, and stand as tall as you can. Now put your hand on your heart. Can you stand tall and say, “I love Heavenly Father”?","stones":[["SPOT","Standing alone, Mordecai might have felt nervous but also sure inside."],["NAME","That firm, do-the-right-thing feeling is called brave."],["FIND","When have you had to do the right thing even though it felt a little scary?"],["FORK","A nervous feeling can make us follow the crowd — or we can stand tall and choose right. Mordecai chose to be faithful to Heavenly Father."],["CALM","Plant your feet, take one strong breath, and stand tall together."]],"remember":"You can stand tall for Heavenly Father, even when others don’t! You can be kind and respectful and still choose what’s right.","ask":["“Who did Mordecai bow down to worship?”","“Why did Haman get angry?”"],"share":["When did doing right require quiet courage while others went a different way? Share it.","How do you teach your child to be respectful and firm at the same time? Tell about a moment it mattered.","Did a grandparent or ancestor stand firm in their faith when it was unpopular? Describe their courage."]},"b8-w2-n3":{"story":["When Mordecai heard about Haman’s plan, he cried a loud, sad cry. Jews in every land cried too.","Mordecai sent a message to Queen Esther. “Please go to the king. Ask him to help your people.”","But there was a strict rule. No one could go to the king unless he called them. If the king held out his golden scepter, a gold stick, they were safe.","Esther felt worried. But Mordecai said, “Who knows? Maybe you became queen for just a time like this.” Esther was in just the right place to help her people.","Heavenly Father has good things for you to do too, right where you are. Tomorrow, look for one person you can help."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"","feel":"Let’s think quietly, like Esther. Put your finger to your chin and make a big “hmm, I wonder” face. Now nod your head like you’ve decided something brave. What do you think Esther will do?","stones":[["SPOT","Esther felt scared, and also thoughtful about what to do."],["NAME","That mixed-up, thinking-hard feeling is called uncertain."],["FIND","When have you felt unsure about doing something big and important?"],["FORK","An uncertain feeling can make us freeze — or we can pause, pray, and find our brave. Esther took time to think and chose to help."],["CALM","Sit still, put a hand on your chin, and take one slow, thinking breath."]],"remember":"Heavenly Father gives YOU a purpose, right where you are — in your family, your class, your home! You can look for ways to help, just like Esther.","ask":["“What wise thing did Mordecai tell Esther?”","“What did “for such a time as this”"],"share":["When did you sense you were placed somewhere “for such a time as this”? Share the experience.","How do you help your child see they have a purpose from Heavenly Father? Tell about a time you felt yours.","Did a grandparent or ancestor step up at just the right moment to help others? Describe their courage of timing."]},"b8-w2-n4":{"story":["Esther sent a message to Mordecai. “Gather all the Jews here, and fast for me for three days. My helpers and I will fast too.”","When people fast, they go without food and drink for a little while. It shows Heavenly Father how much they need His help.","Esther said, “Then I will go to the king, even against the rule.”","On the third day, Esther put on her royal clothes. She stood in the king’s courtyard. The king was pleased to see her! He held out his golden scepter, a gold stick that meant welcome. Esther touched the top.","Esther was brave, and her people fasted with her. Tonight, think of someone who needs help, and ask Heavenly Father to bless them."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau fits tonight because he asks Heavenly Father for help before doing hard things. In Beau’s world today: Beau had to get a shot at the doctor, and he felt scared. He squeezed Mom’s hand, took a big brave breath, and whispered a little prayer. It still pinched, but Beau did it! Just like Esther prayed and then bravely went to the king, Beau prayed and then did the hard, brave thing.","feel":"","stones":[["SPOT","Esther felt afraid before going to the king."],["NAME","That heart-pounding feeling is called afraid."],["FIND","When have you felt afraid to do something brave, like a shot or a new activity?"],["FORK","Being afraid can make us run away — or we can pray, breathe, and go bravely anyway. Esther prayed and then walked forward."],["CALM","Take one big “brave breath” in and out with Heavenly Father."]],"remember":"When something feels scary, you can pray and ask Heavenly Father for a brave heart — then do the hard thing anyway, like Esther!","ask":["“What did Esther ask everyone to do for three days?”","“How did the king welcome Esther?”"],"share":["When did prayer and fasting give you courage to face something hard? Share it simply.","Tell your child about a time you felt afraid but did the right thing anyway with Heavenly Father’s help.","Did a grandparent or ancestor show great courage in a frightening moment? Describe how faith carried them."]},"b8-w2-n5":{"story":["Long, long ago, a girl named Esther lived in a land called Persia. It was far from the land her family came from. Her people, the Jews, had been taken away from home, so they lived there too, and they loved Heavenly Father. Esther was chosen to be the queen. A queen is the king's wife. Now she lived in a palace, which is a very big house where a king lives.","A man named Haman worked for the king. Haman made a bad plan against Esther's people. Esther wanted to go tell the king and ask him to help them. But there was a rule back then: nobody could walk in and talk to the king, not even the queen. She had to wait until the king said yes.","So Esther did a brave thing first. She asked all her people to fast with her. A fast means going without food for a little while so you can pray with your whole heart. For three days they fasted and prayed to Heavenly Father. Then Esther went to the king, and the king was glad to see her, and he said yes.","Esther did not tell the king right away. She asked the king and Haman to come to a feast. A feast is a big, happy dinner with lots of good food. Everyone ate, and the king said, \"Esther, what would you like? I will give it to you.\" Esther could have said it all right then. Instead she said kindly, \"Please come to one more dinner tomorrow.\" Waiting one more day was hard. But Esther waited.","The next night, when the time was just right, Esther told the king the truth, and the king helped her people. Esther had prayed first, and Heavenly Father helped her be brave and know when to speak. Doing the right thing at the right time is called being wise. Tonight, if you have something big to say, you can pray in your heart and wait for a good moment too."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren fits tonight because she knows the wisdom of waiting for the right moment. In Wren’s world today: Wren wanted to tell Dad her big news, but he was on an important phone call. She really, really wanted to interrupt! Instead, she waited patiently, and when he finished, she shared her news — and he could truly listen. Just like Esther waited for the right time, Wren learned that patience makes our words land better.","feel":"Let’s practice waiting wisely. Fold your hands in your lap and sit as still as a statue... wait... wait... now gently smile. Waiting can be hard! Can you show me your calmest, most patient face?","stones":[["SPOT","Esther really, really wanted to speak to the king, but she prayed first and waited one whole day for the right moment."],["NAME","That hard-to-wait feeling has a name: impatient. Impatient means wanting something RIGHT NOW. Your mind might think, \"I have to say it now or I will burst!\" Your body might feel wiggly, with a jumpy tummy and words pushing at your lips."],["FIND","When did you feel impatient today? Maybe waiting for your turn, or waiting while a grown-up was talking, or waiting for a snack."],["FORK","Impatient takes us to two roads. One road is blurting it out and fussing. The other road is breathing, saying a quiet prayer, and waiting for a good moment. Esther took the waiting road, and it helped her whole family."],["CALM","Put your hands on your tummy and take three slow, patient breaths. Then whisper, \"Heavenly Father, please help me be brave and wait for the right time.\""]],"remember":"Heavenly Father helps me be brave and wait for the right time, just like wise Esther!","ask":["“Where did Esther live, and who did she ask to come to her feast?”","“What did Esther and her people do for three days before she went to the king?”","“What is something you had to wait for today, and how did your tummy feel?”"],"share":["When has patience — waiting for the right moment — made all the difference? Share that story.","How do you model wise timing and self-control for your child? Tell about a time it paid off.","Did a grandparent or ancestor show wisdom in knowing when to wait and when to act? Describe their good judgment."]},"b8-w2-n6":{"story":["Esther made a big, special dinner for the king and Haman. The king asked, “Queen Esther, what do you want? I will give it to you.”","Esther said, “Please save my life and my people. Someone has a plan to hurt us all.”","The king asked, “Who would do this?” Esther said, “It is this bad man, Haman.” And Haman’s plan was stopped.","Then Esther’s people had light and joy! Their sad days turned into happy days. They had feasts and sent gifts to each other and to the poor. They called these days Purim.","Esther helped save her people. Jesus came to save us all. His love can turn sad days into happy ones. Tonight, think of something you could share."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout fits tonight because Scout is honest and speaks up. In Scout’s world today: Scout saw a smaller child’s tower get knocked down by accident, and the child was blamed. Scout spoke up: “It was a bump — nobody meant to.” Telling the truth made everything fair again. Just like brave Esther told the truth to save her people, Scout learned that honest words can protect and help others.","feel":"Let’s use a brave, truthful voice! Stand tall, put your hand on your heart, and say clearly, “I will tell the truth.” A truthful voice is a strong voice. Can you say it big and brave?","stones":[["SPOT","Esther felt nervous but determined as she told the truth."],["NAME","That strong, do-it-anyway feeling is called courageous."],["FIND","When have you needed courage to tell the truth, even though it was hard?"],["FORK","Fear can make us stay quiet — or we can speak the truth kindly and bravely. Esther bravely told the king the truth."],["CALM","Hand on your heart, take one strong breath, and say, “Truth is brave.”"]],"remember":"Telling the truth, even when it’s hard, is brave and good — and it can help the people you love! You can be honest like Esther.","ask":["“What truth did Esther bravely tell the king?”","“What did the king do to keep the people safe?”"],"share":["When did telling the truth — even at a cost — protect or bless your family? Share it.","How do you nurture honest courage in your child? Tell about a time truth mattered most.","Did a grandparent or ancestor speak a hard truth that helped others? Describe their integrity."]},"b8-w2-n7":{"story":["Long ago, Heavenly Father's people lived far, far away from their old home, in a land called Persia. A girl named Esther lived there too. The king picked her to be his queen. Nobody knew that Esther was one of Heavenly Father's people. It was her quiet secret.","Then her people were in big trouble. A man had made a plan to hurt them. Esther was scared, but she was brave. She walked right into the king's big room and asked him to help her people. And he did! Everyone was safe.","Esther's people were so glad they cried happy tears. They wanted to remember that day forever and ever. So they made a special day that comes back every year, just like your birthday comes back every year. They called it Purim. Purim means \"the lot.\" A lot is like drawing a stick to pick a day. Someone drew a day to hurt them — and Heavenly Father turned it into the day they were saved.","On Purim day, families ate yummy treats and gave little presents to each other. They gave presents to people who did not have much, too, so nobody would be left out of the happy. Sharing the glad made the glad bigger.","Tonight you can do a tiny Purim. Think of one good thing Heavenly Father gave you today — a snuggle, a snack, a friend. Then pick one person to share a bit of your happy with tomorrow: half your treat, a picture you drew, or a big smile at the door. Happy grows when you hand it to somebody else."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow fits tonight because she loves to share joy and give to others. In Willow’s world today: Willow had two cookies and saw a friend who had none. She thought about keeping both… but instead she said, “Here — let’s share!” Both girls giggled with happy, full hearts. Just like the people shared gifts at their celebration, Willow learned that joy grows bigger the moment we share it.","feel":"Let’s celebrate! Clap your hands three times — clap, clap, clap! Now pretend to hand a gift to someone you love: “This is for you!” Sharing makes joy grow. Can you show me your happiest celebration dance?","stones":[["SPOT","Esther's people were safe, and their scared day turned into a glad day — so they shared treats and gifts with everyone."],["NAME","That warm feeling is called grateful. It means glad for something good someone gave you. Your mind might think, \"I love this — I'm so glad!\" Your body might feel warm in your tummy, a smile that comes by itself, and a wiggly wanting to jump."],["FIND","Think of a time today when you felt that warm glad — maybe at dinner, or when someone hugged you, or when you got to play. That was grateful."],["FORK","When we feel glad, there are two roads: keep it all to ourselves, or hand some of it to someone else. Esther's people took the sharing road — they gave gifts so everyone felt glad too."],["CALM","Put one hand on your heart and feel it go thump, thump. Then whisper, \"Thank you, Heavenly Father, for my good things. Help me share them.\""]],"remember":"Happy grows bigger when you share it!","ask":["“Why were Esther's people so glad and so thankful?”","“What did they share with each other on that special day?”","“What is one glad thing you can share with somebody tomorrow?”"],"share":["When has remembering Heavenly Father’s help filled you with gratitude? Share that feeling.","How does your family turn blessings into generosity? Tell about a meaningful time of giving.","Did a grandparent or ancestor celebrate Heavenly Father’s goodness by giving to others? Describe their generous joy."]},"b8-w3-n1":{"story":["Long, long ago, before Jesus was born on the earth, there lived a good man named Job. Job loved Heavenly Father with his whole heart. He was kind to everybody — to his children, to his helpers, and to people who had no food.","Job had a big family and lots of sheep and camels to take care of. Every single morning Job prayed for his children. He asked Heavenly Father to watch over them and keep them safe. Praying for someone means asking Heavenly Father to help them.","Then some very hard, sad days came to Job. He lost his animals, and he got sore and sick, and he felt so sad that he sat down on the ground in the ashes. Ashes are the soft gray dust left after a fire. Long ago, when people were very sad, they would sit in ashes so everyone could see their sad heart. Even sitting there, Job said, \"I know that my Redeemer lives.\" A Redeemer is someone who saves you and brings you home. Job was talking about Jesus.","Job's story was written down for us a long time ago so we would know something important: Job did not love Heavenly Father only when everything was easy and happy. Loving Heavenly Father was just who Job was, deep down inside, on the good days and the hard days too.","Tonight, in your own bed, you can be like Job. On a happy day you can whisper \"thank you\" to Heavenly Father. And on a day when something goes wrong — a scraped knee, a friend who said something unkind, a toy that broke — you can still talk to Him. He is listening on both kinds of days."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"","feel":"Let’s thank Heavenly Father like Job! Put your hands together and count three blessings on your fingers — one, two, three. Now give a happy sigh: “Thank you, Heavenly Father.” What are you thankful for tonight?","stones":[["SPOT","Something hard happened to Job, and he still loved Heavenly Father and talked to Him."],["NAME","The sad, heavy feeling Job had is called sad. Your mind might think, \"This is not fair,\" or \"Nothing good will happen now.\" Your body might feel tight in your tummy, or hot behind your eyes, or like your shoulders want to droop down."],["FIND","Think of one time today or this week when something went wrong for you. Where were you? What happened right before you felt that heavy feeling?"],["FORK","When a hard thing comes, there are two roads. One road says, \"I will stop talking to Heavenly Father.\" The other road says, \"I will tell Heavenly Father how I feel.\" Job took the second road, even sitting in the ashes."],["CALM","Put one hand flat on your tummy and take three slow breaths, in through your nose and out through your mouth. Then whisper, \"Heavenly Father, thank you for today. Please help me on the hard days too.\""]],"remember":"Heavenly Father is close on happy days and hard days, and you can talk to Him on both!","ask":["“What did Job do every morning for his children?”","“Why did people long ago sit in ashes when they were sad?”","“What is one hard thing you could tell Heavenly Father about tonight?”"],"share":["How do you cultivate gratitude in the good seasons, so it’s ready in the hard ones? Share your practice.","Tell your child about a daily habit of thanks or prayer that steadies your family.","Did a grandparent or ancestor live with deep gratitude to Heavenly Father? Describe their thankful heart."]},"b8-w3-n2":{"story":["In the land of Uz lived a good man named Job. He loved the Lord. Job had seven sons and three daughters.","Job had many sheep, camels and oxen. Early each morning, he gave gifts to the Lord for his children.","Then a very hard day came. Job’s animals were taken, and his children died. Job’s heart hurt so much.","Job bowed down to the ground and worshipped the Lord. He said, “The Lord gave, and the Lord has taken away. Blessed be the name of the Lord.” Job knew every good thing came from the Lord. So he kept loving Him.","Every good thing we have is a gift from Heavenly Father. Tonight, hug your family and say, “Thank you, Heavenly Father.”"],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau fits tonight because he trusts Heavenly Father even when things are hard. In Beau’s world today: Beau’s favorite toy broke, and he felt so sad — he even cried a little. But after his tears, Beau took a breath and remembered that Heavenly Father still loved him and would help him feel better. Just like Job kept trusting through his hard day, Beau learned that we can be sad AND still trust Heavenly Father at the same time.","feel":"When things are hard, we can hold on tight. Give yourself a big squeezy hug and hold on… hold on… now take a slow breath out. Heavenly Father holds us close. Can you give a strong “holding-on” hug?","stones":[["SPOT","Job felt very sad when hard things happened."],["NAME","That deep, heavy feeling is called sad."],["FIND","When have you felt really sad about something you lost or that didn’t go your way?"],["FORK","A sad feeling can make us stop trusting — or we can hold onto Heavenly Father right through it. Job stayed sad but kept trusting Him."],["CALM","Give yourself a hug and breathe out slowly, saying, “Heavenly Father is with me.”"]],"remember":"Even when something makes you sad, you can still trust Heavenly Father — He never leaves you! It’s okay to feel sad and hold onto Him at the same time.","ask":["“What happened to Job that made him sad?”","“What did Job say he would still do?”"],"share":["When did you choose to trust Heavenly Father in the middle of loss or pain? Share it gently.","How do you show your child that sadness and faith can live together? Tell about a hard season He carried you through.","Did a grandparent or ancestor keep their faith through great hardship? Describe how they held on."]},"b8-w3-n3":{"story":["Then Job got very sick, with sore spots from his feet to his head. He sat in the ashes, sad and hurting.","Three friends heard about Job’s troubles: Eliphaz, Bildad and Zophar. They came to comfort Job and help him feel better.","When they saw Job, they cried out loud. They tossed dust on their heads, the way people then showed sadness.","Then they sat with Job on the ground for seven days and seven nights. No one said a word, because they saw how sad he was. After that, Job told them how very sad he felt.","When someone you love is sad, you can sit close. Tonight, think of one person you can sit by tomorrow."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow fits tonight because she notices who is hurting and stays close. In Willow’s world today: Willow’s friend fell down and scraped her knee and started to cry. Willow didn’t know how to fix it, so she just sat beside her friend and held her hand until she felt better. Just like Job’s friends sat quietly with him, Willow learned that being near someone who is sad is a gift of love.","feel":"Let’s show quiet kindness. Sit close to someone you love and give a gentle pat on the back — pat, pat, pat. No words needed! Can you be a kind, quiet friend right now?","stones":[["SPOT","Job felt comforted when his friends came and stayed with him."],["NAME","That not — so-alone feeling is called comforted."],["FIND","When has someone sitting close made you feel better when you were sad?"],["FORK","When a friend is sad, we can walk away — or we can stay close and comfort them. Job’s friends chose to sit right beside him."],["CALM","Sit close to someone you love and take one calm breath together."]],"remember":"When someone is sad, you can be a kind friend just by being near them — a hug or a hand to hold means so much!","ask":["“What did Job’s friends do when they first came?”","“How can we help a friend who feels sad?”"],"share":["When did someone’s quiet presence comfort you more than any words? Share that memory.","How do you teach your child the ministry of simply “being with” someone? Tell about a time you offered it.","Did a grandparent or ancestor have a gift for comforting others? Describe their tender presence."]},"b8-w3-n4":{"story":["Job said that when a tree is cut down, it can sprout again. New green branches can grow!","Then Job wondered, “If a person dies, will he live again?” That was his big question.","Job wished his words could be carved in rock forever. He said, “I know that my Redeemer lives!”","Job’s Redeemer is Jesus, who saves us and brings us back to Heavenly Father. Job said that one day He will stand on the earth. Job knew he would live again in his own body. Jesus died and came back to life, so everyone will live again.","Jesus lives! Because of Him, your family will live again too. Tonight, put your hand on your heart and feel His love."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren fits tonight because she thinks deeply and holds onto hope. In Wren’s world today: Wren had a hard day and felt grumpy inside. At bedtime, instead of keeping it in, she told Heavenly Father all about it in her prayer. Afterward, she felt lighter, and she remembered that tomorrow was a fresh new day. Just like Job shared his honest feelings and kept his hope, Wren learned that telling Heavenly Father everything brings peace.","feel":"Let’s tell Heavenly Father our feelings. Put your hand on your heart and take a deep breath. Now whisper, “Heavenly Father, this is how I feel.” He always listens. Can you take a big feelings-breath with me?","stones":[["SPOT","Job felt sad and confused, but also hopeful deep down."],["NAME","That wondering, mixed-up feeling is called confused."],["FIND","When have you felt confused or full of questions about something hard?"],["FORK","A confused feeling can bottle up inside — or we can pour it out to Heavenly Father in prayer. Job told Him everything, and hope stayed alive."],["CALM","Hand on your heart, breathe in and out, and tell Heavenly Father one true feeling."]],"remember":"You can tell Heavenly Father exactly how you feel — even the sad or confused feelings! He always listens and always gives hope.","ask":["“Who did Job talk to about his big feelings?”","“What hopeful thing did Job remember?”"],"share":["When did honest prayer — even complaint — bring you closer to Heavenly Father? Share it.","How do you invite your child to bring real feelings to Heavenly Father? Tell about a prayer that helped you.","Did a grandparent or ancestor cling to hope through sorrow? Describe the hope that held them."]},"b8-w3-n5":{"story":["At last the Lord spoke to Job out of a whirlwind, a spinning wind. The Lord was Jesus, speaking for Heavenly Father.","He asked Job, “Where were you when I made the earth?” The Lord had measured it, the way a builder measures a house.","He asked about the day the morning stars sang together. Heavenly Father’s children shouted for joy!","Job was not there, so he put his hand over his mouth and listened. But the Lord was there. Jesus made the earth for Heavenly Father. Before you were born, you lived with Heavenly Father as His spirit child. You may have shouted for joy too!","Tonight, think of the stars. Jesus made this beautiful world for Heavenly Father’s children, and that means you!"],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout fits tonight because Scout loves to notice the wonders of Heavenly Father’s world. In Scout’s world today: on a walk, Scout stopped to watch a tiny ant carry a crumb ten times its size. “Look and see — how did Heavenly Father make something so little so strong?” Scout wondered. Just like Job’s eyes grew big at Heavenly Father’s creations, Scout discovered that the whole world is full of Heavenly Father’s amazing work.","feel":"Let’s look at the wonders! Point up high like you’re touching the stars, then sweep your arms wide like the big ocean. Wow — Heavenly Father made it all! Can you show me your most amazed “wow” face?","stones":[["SPOT","Looking at creation, Job felt small — and full of wonder."],["NAME","That big-eyed, whoa feeling is called amazed."],["FIND","What is something in nature — stars, bugs, oceans — that fills you with wonder?"],["FORK","Feeling small can make us feel scared — or it can help us trust Heavenly Father, who is so big and wise. Job’s wonder helped him trust."],["CALM","Look up, take a slow breath, and whisper, “Heavenly Father, You are so great.”"]],"remember":"When you look at the stars, the animals, and the tiny bugs, you can see how great and wise Heavenly Father is — big enough to care for you!","ask":["“What did Heavenly Father show Job?”","“What can we learn about Heavenly Father from the stars and the sea?”"],"share":["When did the wonder of creation restore your trust in Heavenly Father’s wisdom? Share that moment.","How do you use nature to teach your child about Heavenly Father’s greatness? Tell about a favorite spot.","Did a grandparent or ancestor find Heavenly Father in the natural world? Describe their sense of wonder."]},"b8-w3-n6":{"story":["A long, long time ago there was a good man named Job. Job loved Heavenly Father. Then Job had many sad, hard days, and he did not know why. His friends came and said, \"You must have done something bad.\" But that was not true. Job kept praying and asking, \"Why?\"","Then Heavenly Father talked with Job. He did not scold him. He asked Job gentle questions: Do you know where the stars go? Do you know who sends the rain? Do you know how the baby deer grow? Job thought and thought. He said, \"No. I do not know those things. But You do.\" Heavenly Father was showing Job something kind: I take care of every star and every raindrop, so I am taking care of you too.","Job also said a very happy thing, even on his sad days. He said, \"I know that my Redeemer lives.\" A Redeemer is someone who comes to save you and bring you home. Job was talking about Jesus. Job knew Jesus was real and would come and would help him, even before Jesus was born on the earth.","\"I trust You,\" Job said. Trust means I know You love me, so I do not need to know everything. And a warm, quiet, safe feeling came into Job's heart. He still did not have all the answers. He had something better. He had Heavenly Father.","Tonight, if there is a why inside you — why did my friend say that, why was today hard, why do I feel sad — you can do just what Job did. You can tell Heavenly Father about it in your prayers, and then let your shoulders go soft and let Him hold the answer for you while you sleep. He is awake all night, and He loves you."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren fits tonight because she’s learning that we don’t have to understand everything. In Wren’s world today: Wren asked why she couldn’t have a treat before dinner, and Mom said, “Trust me — it’s so you’ll feel good later.” Wren didn’t fully understand, but she trusted Mom’s love. Just like Job trusted Heavenly Father’s wisdom without knowing everything, Wren learned that trust means believing in someone’s love and wisdom.","feel":"Let’s show trusting hearts. Cross your arms gently over your heart and give a little squeeze. Now nod your head “yes” to Heavenly Father. Can you whisper, “I trust You, Heavenly Father”?","stones":[["SPOT","Job did not know why his days were hard. He told Heavenly Father about it, and a warm, safe feeling came into his heart."],["NAME","That warm, safe feeling is called peace. When you do not understand something, your mind might think, \"This isn't fair\" or \"Why is this happening to me?\" Your body might feel a tight tummy, hot cheeks, or a heavy chest."],["FIND","When did you not understand something today? Maybe you had to stop playing, or someone said no, or a friend walked away. Can you remember how your tummy felt right then?"],["FORK","Job could have stayed cross and sad and stopped talking to Heavenly Father. Or he could tell Heavenly Father all about it and trust Him. Job chose to trust Him, and peace came."],["CALM","Cross your arms over your heart and take three slow breaths. Then whisper, \"Heavenly Father, I don't understand, but I know You love me. Please help my heart feel calm.\""]],"remember":"I don't have to know everything, because Heavenly Father does — and He loves me!","ask":["“What questions did Heavenly Father ask Job about the stars and the rain?”","“Who was Job talking about when he said, \"I know that my Redeemer lives\"?”","“What is one thing you did not understand today that you can tell Heavenly Father about?”"],"share":["When did you find peace by trusting Heavenly Father’s wisdom over your own understanding? Share it.","How do you help your child rest in trust when answers don’t come? Tell about a time you had to.","Did a grandparent or ancestor trust Heavenly Father through mystery and find peace? Describe their quiet faith."]},"b8-w3-n7":{"story":["Job told the Lord, “I had heard about You. But now I see You with my own eyes.”","The Lord told Job’s friends, “You have not said what is right about Me.” He said Job would pray for them. So Job prayed for his friends.","Then the Lord gave Job twice as much as he had before. His brothers, sisters and friends came to eat with him and comfort him.","Job had seven more sons and three more daughters. He named his girls Jemima, Kezia and Keren-happuch. Job lived 140 more years and saw his grandchildren, and their children too.","Tonight, say a little prayer for a friend by name. Heavenly Father hears you, and He loves your friend too."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau fits tonight because his faithfulness carried him all the way through to joy. In Beau’s world today: Beau was sad when it rained all day and playtime outside was canceled. But he trusted it would pass — and the next morning, the sun came out and the puddles were perfect for splashing! Just like Job trusted Heavenly Father through the hard time and joy came back, Beau learned that after the rain, the sunshine always returns.","feel":"Let’s celebrate the sunshine after the rain! Wiggle your fingers down like falling rain, then throw your arms up like a bright, warm sun. Joy comes back! Can you show me rain… and then a big, happy sun?","stones":[["SPOT","After his hard time, Job felt joyful and thankful again."],["NAME","That bright, happy-again feeling is called joyful."],["FIND","When has a sad time gotten better and turned into a happy one?"],["FORK","When we’re in a hard time, we can believe joy will never come — or we can trust that Heavenly Father brings the morning. Job trusted, and joy returned."],["CALM","Throw your arms up like sunshine and take one big, happy breath."]],"remember":"Even on your hardest days, morning always comes again — Heavenly Father brings joy back around! He loves you through the sad parts.","ask":["“What did Heavenly Father do for Job after his hard time?”","“What did Job learn about Heavenly Father?”"],"share":["When did joy return after a long, hard season, reminding you of Heavenly Father’s faithfulness? Share it.","How do you help your child hold onto hope that morning comes again? Tell about your own “sunrise” after sorrow.","Did a grandparent or ancestor see Heavenly Father restore joy after great trial? Describe their story of hope renewed."]},"b8-w4-n1":{"story":["The first song in Psalms tells about a happy person.","That person loves the Lord’s words and thinks about them “day and night.” He stays away from people who choose wrong.","The song says he is “like a tree planted by the rivers of water.” Its leaves stay green, and it gives good fruit.","Why a tree by a river? In that land, summers were hot and dry, and many plants dried up. But a tree by a river drinks every day with its roots. The Lord’s words are like that water. The Lord is Jesus, and His words help us grow strong.","Jesus’s words are like water for your heart. Tonight, listen to one of His words, and grow strong like a tree."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren fits tonight because she loves to soak in Heavenly Father’s words. In Wren’s world today: every night, Wren asks for one more scripture story before bed. Little by little, those stories are growing in her heart like water helps a tree. When a friend was sad, Wren remembered a story about kindness and knew just what to do. Just like the happy tree by the river, Wren is growing strong and good from Heavenly Father’s words.","feel":"Let’s be a strong little tree! Stand tall, stretch your arms up like branches, and wiggle your fingers like leaves in the breeze. Now stand firm and strong. Can you be a happy tree, rooted deep?","stones":[["SPOT","The happy tree feels strong, steady, and full of life."],["NAME","That rooted, sturdy feeling is called strong."],["FIND","What helps you feel strong and steady inside?"],["FORK","We can skip the things that help us grow — or drink them in like the tree drinks water. Loving Heavenly Father’s words each day keeps our hearts strong."],["CALM","Stand tall, breathe in slow, and imagine your roots growing deep and strong."]],"remember":"When you listen to Heavenly Father’s words each day, you grow strong and good, just like the happy little tree by the river!","ask":["“What is the person who loves Heavenly Father’s words like?”","“What helps the little tree grow strong?”"],"share":["How has daily time in Heavenly Father’s word kept you rooted through storms? Share it.","What small, daily spiritual habit do you hope takes root in your child? Tell why it matters to you.","Did a grandparent or ancestor stay “planted by the water” through steady devotion? Describe their rootedness."]},"b8-w4-n2":{"story":["Long ago there were no city lights. At night, the whole sky sparkled with stars.","David looked up and sang to the Lord. “I see Your heavens, the work of Your fingers, the moon and the stars!”","Then David asked, “What is man, that You think about him?” The sky is so big. Why would the Lord think about one person?","But He does! David sang that the Lord made us a little lower than the angels. The Lord who made the stars was Jesus. He made them for Heavenly Father.","Heavenly Father is thinking of you right now. Tonight, peek at the sky or picture the stars. The One who made them knows you!"],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"","feel":"Let’s reach for the stars! Open and close your hands high in the sky like twinkling stars — twinkle, twinkle. Now point to yourself: Heavenly Father loves ME! Can you make your hands twinkle like little stars?","stones":[["SPOT","Looking at the stars, the person felt wonder and loved."],["NAME","That small-but-special feeling is called loved."],["FIND","When do you feel most loved and special?"],["FORK","A big sky could make us feel tiny and forgotten — or remind us that Heavenly Father made it all and still loves us. The person felt cherished."],["CALM","Look up, breathe slow, and whisper, “Heavenly Father knows my name.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father made the whole sky full of stars — and He still knows and loves YOU! You are precious to Him.","ask":["“What did the person see in the night sky?”","“Even with all the stars, who does Heavenly Father still love?”"],"share":["When did creation’s vastness make Heavenly Father’s personal love feel more real, not less? Share it.","How do you help your child feel seen and treasured by Heavenly Father? Tell about a moment you felt His notice.","Did a grandparent or ancestor carry a deep sense of being known by Heavenly Father? Describe their assurance of His love."]},"b8-w4-n3":{"story":["Before David was king, he took care of his father’s sheep. Later he wrote a song: “The Lord is my shepherd.”","David sang, “He makes me lie down in green pastures.” Those are the soft grassy fields where sheep eat and rest.","He sang, “He leads me beside the still waters.” Fast water can sweep a little sheep away. So a good shepherd finds calm water for them to drink.","David sang, “I will not be afraid, for You are with me.” A shepherd’s strong sticks kept the sheep safe from wild animals. Many years later, Jesus said, “I am the good shepherd.”","You are one of Jesus’s little lambs. Tonight, snuggle down like a sheep in soft grass, safe with your Shepherd."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"","feel":"Let’s be cozy little lambs! Curl up small and soft, then give a gentle “baa.” Now feel safe, because the Good Shepherd is near. Can you snuggle up like a sleepy little lamb?","stones":[["SPOT","With the Good Shepherd near, David felt safe, even in the dark."],["NAME","That warm, safe-and-sound feeling is called safe."],["FIND","When do you feel most safe and cozy?"],["FORK","The dark can make us feel afraid — or we can remember the Good Shepherd is right beside us. David chose to feel safe with Heavenly Father."],["CALM","Snuggle in, breathe slow, and whisper, “Heavenly Father is my shepherd.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father is your Good Shepherd — He leads you, keeps you safe, and is with you even in the dark!","ask":["“What did David call Heavenly Father in his song?”","“Why didn’t David need to be afraid, even in the dark?”"],"share":["When did the image of Heavenly Father as your Shepherd carry you through a “dark valley”? Share it.","How do you comfort your child’s nighttime fears with Heavenly Father’s nearness? Tell about your own comfort.","Did a grandparent or ancestor lean on the Shepherd’s care in frightening times? Describe their trust."]},"b8-w4-n4":{"story":["David had some hard, scary days, but he trusted the Lord.","He sang, “The Lord is my light. Whom shall I fear?” David meant, “Who should I be afraid of? No one!”","When it is dark, we cannot see, and that can feel scary. Light helps us see that we are safe. That is why David called the Lord his light.","David also sang, “The Lord is the strength of my life.” In hard times, the Lord would keep him safe, high up on a rock. Many years later, Jesus said, “I am the light of the world.” The Lord is Jesus!","If the dark feels big tonight, you are not alone. Say, “The Lord is my light,” and remember Jesus is near."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau fits tonight because Heavenly Father’s light makes him brave. In Beau’s world today: the power went out and the whole house went dark, and Beau felt his heart jump. Then he remembered that Heavenly Father was with him, bright as any light. He took a brave breath, helped find the flashlight, and even comforted his little sister. Just like the psalm-writer said Heavenly Father is his light, Beau’s fear turned to brave.","feel":"Let’s be a little light! Make a fist, then pop your fingers open wide like a bright light turning on — shine! Now hold your light up high. Heavenly Father lights our hearts. Can you shine your bright light?","stones":[["SPOT","In the dark, the person chose not to be afraid because of Heavenly Father."],["NAME","That no-need-to-fear feeling is called brave."],["FIND","When has a little light helped you feel brave in the dark?"],["FORK","The dark can grow our fear — or we can let Heavenly Father’s light make us brave. The person chose to shine with Heavenly Father’s light."],["CALM","Take a brave breath and picture Heavenly Father’s warm light inside your heart."]],"remember":"Heavenly Father is your light! When you feel worried or scared, He shines warm and bright inside you and helps you be brave.","ask":["“What did the song say Heavenly Father is?”","“How does a light help us when it’s dark?”"],"share":["When did Heavenly Father become “light” to you in a frightening or uncertain time? Share it.","How do you point your child to Heavenly Father when fear comes? Tell about a fear His light dispelled.","Did a grandparent or ancestor walk by faith’s light through dark days? Describe how it guided them."]},"b8-w4-n5":{"story":["This song was for the sons of Korah, a singing family. It begins, “Heavenly Father is our safe place and our strength.”","He is “a very present help in trouble,” always near. So the song says, “We will not fear.”","Then the Lord says, “Be still, and know that I am God.” That means get quiet and calm inside.","Why be still? When we rush and worry, it is hard to feel Him near. When we are quiet, we feel His peace. Later, Jesus was in a boat in a big storm. He told the wind and sea, “Peace, be still.” And all was calm.","Tonight, lie very still and breathe slowly. Heavenly Father is your safe place, and He is near."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren fits tonight because she knows the gift of being still. In Wren’s world today: the day was noisy and Wren felt wiggly and grumpy all over. Mom said, “Let’s be still for a minute.” Wren sat quietly, took a few slow breaths, and felt the grumpies melt away. Just like the psalm says to be still and know Heavenly Father is near, Wren discovered that quiet moments bring His peace.","feel":"Let’s be still together. Sit very quietly and put your hands softly in your lap. Take one slow breath in... and out. Shhh — can you feel how peaceful still can be? Can you be as still as a sleeping kitten?","stones":[["SPOT","When things felt loud and busy, the psalm invites a still, calm feeling."],["NAME","That slowed-down, quiet feeling is called calm."],["FIND","When do you feel wiggly or busy inside, and need to get calm?"],["FORK","Busy feelings can make us fussy and fast — or we can be still and find Heavenly Father’s peace. The psalm shows us to be still and know He is near."],["CALM","Sit still, hands in your lap, and take three slow, quiet breaths together."]],"remember":"When everything feels too fast or too big, you can be still, take a breath, and remember Heavenly Father is right here with you!","ask":["“What does the song say to do when life feels busy?”","“What can we feel when we get very still?”"],"share":["How do you create stillness so you can sense Heavenly Father in a noisy life? Share your practice.","How do you teach your child to self — soothe and find calm with Heavenly Father? Tell about a still moment together.","Did a grandparent or ancestor have a quiet, grounded peace in Heavenly Father? Describe their stillness."]},"b8-w4-n6":{"story":["A long, long time ago there was a boy named David. He watched sheep out in the fields, and while he watched he sang songs to Heavenly Father. When David grew up he became the king of his people. Kings have a lot to take care of, and David still sang. He made up his songs and wrote them down. A song written down like that is called a psalm, and we can still read David's psalms today.","Being a king was not always easy. Some people were unkind to David, and some people even tried to hurt him, so he had to hide and wait a long time. On those days his heart felt very heavy. In one song David said he felt stuck down in a deep, muddy hole, and he could not climb out. David did not really fall in a hole. That was his way of showing us how heavy and sad and stuck his heart felt inside, because a muddy hole is hard to get out of all by yourself.","So David called out to Heavenly Father for help. And Heavenly Father heard him! He lifted David up and set his feet on a big strong rock. A rock does not wobble and it does not slip. Standing on a rock means feeling safe and steady again. David sang that Heavenly Father was his rock. In another song, David sang about a special Son who would come to help everybody. He was singing about Jesus. Jesus is our strong rock too.","David was so thankful that a brand-new song came right into his heart, a happy thank-you song for Heavenly Father. That is what a new song means: a heart so glad that it wants to sing. David wrote his song down so that other people who felt stuck could read it and remember to call out too. That is why we still have it - David wanted us to know what to do on heavy days.","Tonight, if something feels heavy or stuck inside you, you can do just what David did. You can call out to Heavenly Father. He hears you, and He and Jesus can help your heart feel steady and glad again."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow fits tonight because she knows the joy of being lifted up. In Willow’s world today: Willow felt stuck and grumpy because she couldn’t build her puzzle. She was about to cry when Grandpa gently helped her find the right piece. Suddenly her heart felt light and happy, and she hummed a little tune. Just like Heavenly Father lifted the psalm-writer from the pit and gave him a new song, kind help turned Willow’s stuck feeling into joy.","feel":"Let’s climb up out of the pit! Reach your hands down low, then slowly lift up, up, up onto strong rock — you made it! Now sing a happy “la-la- la.” Can you climb up and sing your new song?","stones":[["SPOT","David felt sad and stuck, he called out to Heavenly Father, and Heavenly Father lifted him up and put a happy new song in his heart."],["NAME","That heavy, stuck feeling is called discouraged. Your mind might think, \"I can't do it, and nothing is working.\" Your body might feel hot in your face, tight fists, or heavy, droopy shoulders."],["FIND","When did you feel stuck today? Maybe a zipper would not zip, or your block tower kept falling down, or someone would not play with you."],["FORK","One road is to give up and stay sad all by yourself. The other road is to ask for help. David took the asking road. He called out to Heavenly Father, who lifted him up and gave him a new song."],["CALM","Take one slow breath and reach your arms way, way up high, like you are being lifted onto a big strong rock. Then whisper, \"Heavenly Father, please help me feel steady. Thank you.\""]],"remember":"When you feel stuck or sad, you can call out to Heavenly Father, and He lifts you up and puts a happy song in your heart!","ask":["“How did David feel before Heavenly Father helped him?”","“What did Heavenly Father put in David's heart?”","“What is something you could sing thank you to Heavenly Father for tonight?”"],"share":["When did Heavenly Father lift you from a “pit” and give you a new song? Share that rescue.","How do you help your child ask for help instead of staying stuck? Tell about a time He lifted you.","Did a grandparent or ancestor testify of being rescued by Heavenly Father? Describe their “new song.”"]},"b8-w4-n7":{"story":["A long time ago, a shepherd boy named David grew up to be a king. David loved Heavenly Father so much that he made up songs for Him. Those songs are in our scriptures, and we call them Psalms. A psalm is a song that is also a prayer.","The people sang David's songs together when they came to worship Heavenly Father. They played harps, which are wooden instruments with strings you pluck with your fingers, and they blew horns that made a big, happy sound. One psalm says, \"Sing unto him a new song.\" That means: don't just sing the same old way - make up a brand new happy song just for Him!","Another psalm says the earth is full of Heavenly Father's goodness. That is a picture-word. It means everywhere you look - trees, puppies, snow, your own warm bed - there is something good He made for you. That is why their hearts felt so full they had to sing it out loud.","In one of these songs, Heavenly Father says, \"Thou art my Son.\" David was talking about Jesus. Even way back then, before Jesus was born, the songs were already telling people that Jesus was coming, and that He is Heavenly Father's Son.","Tonight you can sing to Heavenly Father too. You do not need a harp or a horn. You can hum, clap your hands, or just whisper \"thank You\" for one good thing He made. He hears every little song, even the quiet ones."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout fits tonight because Scout loves to share joyful praise. In Scout’s world today: Scout woke up to a bright, sunny morning and felt so happy that a little song just bubbled right out. Scout marched around singing, “Thank you, Heavenly Father, for this beautiful day!” and soon the whole family was singing along. Just like the psalm invites us to sing joyfully, Scout discovered that praising Heavenly Father makes joy spread everywhere.","feel":"Let’s make joyful music! Clap a happy beat — clap, clap, clap — then pretend to strum a harp or toot a horn: “toot-toot!” Now sing “I love Heavenly Father!” Can you make a joyful, praising sound?","stones":[["SPOT","The people's hearts felt so full of love for Heavenly Father that they sang and clapped and played music."],["NAME","That bursting, bubbly feeling is called joyful. Your mind might think, \"I love this so much I can't hold it in!\" Your body might feel wiggly legs, a big smile, and a fast, happy heart."],["FIND","When did you feel that bubbly way today? Maybe when someone came home, or you saw something beautiful outside?"],["FORK","When joy bubbles up, one road is to keep it all inside. The other road is to let it out and give it to Heavenly Father. David and his people took the singing road."],["CALM","Put your hand on your chest, take one slow happy breath, and hum one soft note. Then whisper, \"Heavenly Father, thank You for the good things You made.\""]],"remember":"Heavenly Father loves your song, whether it is loud or just a little hum!","ask":["“What is a psalm?”","“How did David's people make music for Heavenly Father?”","“What is one good thing Heavenly Father made that you want to thank Him for tonight?”"],"share":["When has worshipful music lifted your heart to Heavenly Father? Share a song that does it for you.","How does your family praise Heavenly Father with joy? Tell about a meaningful time of singing together.","Did a grandparent or ancestor love to praise Heavenly Father in song? Describe the music of their faith."]},"b8-w5-n1":{"story":["Sometimes we make mistakes or do things we know are wrong, and our hearts feel yucky and sad inside. One of the Psalms is a gentle song about that.","The person sang, “Heavenly Father, please make my heart clean and new again.” He knew Heavenly Father could help him feel better.","When we say we’re sorry and try to do better, Heavenly Father washes our hearts clean — just like warm water washes away the mud.","Heavenly Father always forgives us when we come to Him. He gives us a fresh, clean heart and a happy new start. Isn’t that a wonderful gift?"],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"","feel":"Let’s wash our hearts clean! Rub your hands together like you’re washing with warm, soapy water — scrub, scrub. Now shake them out and feel fresh and new! Can you show me your clean, happy heart?","stones":[["SPOT","Before saying sorry, the person felt sorry and heavy-hearted."],["NAME","That yucky, did — something-wrong feeling is called guilty."],["FIND","When have you felt yucky inside because you did something you knew was wrong?"],["FORK","A guilty feeling can make us hide — or we can say sorry and let Heavenly Father wash our hearts clean. The psalm-writer chose to come to Him."],["CALM","Rub your hands, breathe out, and whisper, “Please make my heart clean, Heavenly Father.”"]],"remember":"When you make a mistake, you can say sorry, and Heavenly Father will make your heart clean and new again! He always forgives you.","ask":["“What did the person ask Heavenly Father to make new?”","“What does Heavenly Father do when we say we’re sorry?”"],"share":["When did Heavenly Father’s forgiveness give you a genuine fresh start? Share that relief.","How do you teach your child that repentance is gentle and hopeful, not scary? Tell about His mercy to you.","Did a grandparent or ancestor find peace through Heavenly Father’s forgiveness? Describe their clean-heart story."]},"b8-w5-n2":{"story":["When we feel small or shaky, it helps to stand on something big and strong. One of the Psalms calls Heavenly Father a great big Rock.","“Heavenly Father is my Rock,” the person sang. “He is my safe, high place. I will trust in Him.”","A rock doesn’t wobble or tip over. It stays strong and steady, no matter what. That’s just like Heavenly Father.","When we feel weak or worried, we can lean on Heavenly Father, our strong and steady Rock. He will always hold us up. We are safe when we trust in Him."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau fits tonight because he leans on Heavenly Father when he feels weak. In Beau’s world today: it was Beau’s first day at a new preschool, and his legs felt wobbly and unsure. He took a deep breath and remembered Heavenly Father was his strong Rock, holding him up. He felt steadier, walked in, and even made a new friend. Just like the psalm-writer trusted Heavenly Father as his Rock, Beau leaned on Him and found his strong.","feel":"Let’s be strong and steady like a rock! Stand with your feet apart, make your body big and firm, and don’t wobble. Now say, “Heavenly Father is my Rock!” Can you stand as steady as a strong rock?","stones":[["SPOT","Feeling small, the person chose to feel steady by trusting his Rock."],["NAME","That shaky, unsure feeling is called wobbly."],["FIND","When have you felt wobbly or unsure, like on a first day somewhere new?"],["FORK","A wobbly feeling can topple us over — or we can stand on Heavenly Father, our strong Rock. The person trusted Him and stood steady."],["CALM","Plant your feet firm, breathe in, and stand steady like a strong rock."]],"remember":"When you feel weak or wobbly, you can lean on Heavenly Father — your strong, safe Rock! He will always hold you up.","ask":["“What did the person call Heavenly Father?”","“How is Heavenly Father like a strong rock?”"],"share":["When did Heavenly Father prove to be your steady “Rock” while everything else shook? Share it.","How do you help your child find footing on Heavenly Father in unsteady moments? Tell about a time you did.","Did a grandparent or ancestor stand firm on Heavenly Father as their rock? Describe their steadiness."]},"b8-w5-n3":{"story":["King David wrote this song in the wilderness of Judah. It was a wide, dry land with very little water.","David was thirsty for the Lord, the way dry land needs water.","At night he sang, “I remember You on my bed. I think about You in the night.”","Because the Lord had helped him, David sang a happy song. He sang, “In the shadow of Your wings I will be happy.” A mother bird spreads her wings over her babies to keep them safe. Jesus used this picture too. He wanted to gather His people like a hen gathers her chicks.","Tonight, lie still like a chick under soft wings. Tell Heavenly Father thank you for one good thing today."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"","feel":"Let’s get cozy and think of Heavenly Father. Rest your head on your hands like a little pillow and close your eyes softly. Now think one happy thought about Heavenly Father. Can you show me your sleepy, peaceful face?","stones":[["SPOT","At bedtime, the person felt peaceful and close to Heavenly Father."],["NAME","That cozy, near-to-Him feeling is called peaceful."],["FIND","What good things could you think about as you fall asleep tonight?"],["FORK","At night our minds can fill with worries — or we can think of Heavenly Father and feel peaceful. The psalm-writer chose to think of Him."],["CALM","Rest your head, breathe slow, and think one happy thought about Heavenly Father."]],"remember":"Bedtime is a wonderful time to think about Heavenly Father and feel Him close! As you fall asleep, you can remember His love.","ask":["“When did the person think about Heavenly Father?”","“What did he remember that made his heart full?”"],"share":["How does turning your mind to Heavenly Father at night bring you peace? Share your bedtime practice.","How do you help your child end the day with thoughts of Heavenly Father? Tell about a peaceful nighttime habit.","Did a grandparent or ancestor keep Heavenly Father close in quiet, nightly devotion? Describe their nearness to Him."]},"b8-w5-n4":{"story":["This happy song does not say who wrote it. It calls the whole world to shout and sing for joy.","Then the singer called, “Come and hear! I will tell what the Lord has done for me.”","He told how he prayed out loud. Then he said, “Heavenly Father has heard my prayer!”","In another song, Asaph sang, “We will not hide these stories from the children.” Families told their children the wonderful things the Lord had done. That way the children would trust Him too.","Your family has stories of how Heavenly Father helped them. Tonight, ask a grown-up to tell you one. Someday you can say, “Come and hear!” too."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout fits tonight because Scout loves sharing good news. In Scout’s world today: Scout prayed to find a lost stuffed bunny, and later that day, there it was under the bed! Scout was so happy and grateful that he ran to tell the whole family, “Heavenly Father helped me find it!” Just like the psalm-writer said, “Come and hear,” Scout shared how Heavenly Father had helped, and everyone’s hearts felt glad.","feel":"Let’s share the good news! Cup your hands around your mouth and call out, “Come and hear! Heavenly Father is so good!” Now wave everyone over to listen. Can you share the happy news nice and loud?","stones":[["SPOT","The person felt so grateful and excited he had to share it."],["NAME","That bursting-to-tell feeling is called excited."],["FIND","When have you been so excited about good news you couldn’t wait to tell someone?"],["FORK","Good news can be kept to ourselves — or shared to grow the joy. The psalm-writer shared all that Heavenly Father had done."],["CALM","Take a happy breath and think of one good thing Heavenly Father has done for you."]],"remember":"When Heavenly Father does good things for you, you can share the happy news with everyone you love — it makes your joy grow!","ask":["“What did the person want everyone to come and do?”","“What was he so excited to tell about?”"],"share":["When did sharing what Heavenly Father did for you multiply your joy? Share that experience.","How do you help your child “declare” Heavenly Father’s goodness naturally? Tell about a time you testified.","Did a grandparent or ancestor love to tell of Heavenly Father’s goodness? Describe how their testimony blessed others."]},"b8-w5-n5":{"story":["A long, long time ago, in a land called Israel, people who loved Heavenly Father sang their prayers to Him. They sang them out loud together at the temple - the temple was Heavenly Father's special house. Those song-prayers were written down, and we still have them in our scriptures. A song-prayer like that is called a psalm. Tonight's psalm was sung by a man who was very old.","His hair had turned gray and white, the way hair does when someone has lived a great many years. He was old and slow now, and some things made him feel a little worried. So he sat and thought back over his whole long life - when he was a little boy, when he was a big strong man, and now. And he saw something that made his worry grow small. Heavenly Father had been with him the whole time. Not one single part was left out.","That made him want to sing, so he did: \"You have been my hope since I was very young. You have taken care of me all my life.\" Hope means knowing something good is coming and holding on to it. Heavenly Father had been his good thing to hold on to since he was small, and He still was, even now with gray hair. Remembering made him brave again.","Heavenly Father has been taking care of you too. He loved you before you were even born. He loved you when you were a tiny baby who could not talk or walk. He loves you right now, in your bed, in your pajamas - and He loves you so much that He sent Jesus to us. And when you are big, and even when your hair turns gray someday, He will still be loving you. His love does not run out and it does not stop.","So tonight, before you close your eyes, you can do what the old man did. Think back - all the way back to when you were little - and know He was there. He was there then. He is here now. Tomorrow when you wake up, He will still be there."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow fits tonight because she feels Heavenly Father’s gentle, lifelong love. In Willow’s world today: Willow looked at baby pictures and giggled at how tiny she used to be. Mom said, “Heavenly Father has loved you and watched over you this whole time.” Willow’s heart felt warm and safe, knowing she was never once alone. Just like the psalm-writer trusted Heavenly Father his whole life, Willow felt His love from the very beginning until now.","feel":"Let’s show how we grow with Heavenly Father! Crouch down tiny like a little baby, then s-l-o-w-l-y grow up, up, up, standing tall and big. Heavenly Father is with us the whole time! Can you grow from tiny to tall?","stones":[["SPOT","An old man with gray hair felt a little worried, so he looked back at his whole life - and saw that Heavenly Father had been taking care of him the entire time, from when he was tiny until now."],["NAME","The feeling he felt is called safe - like someone strong has been holding you the whole time and you did not even know. Your mind might think, \"I was never, ever alone.\" Your body might feel warm and soft in your chest, and your shoulders might drop down and rest."],["FIND","When did you feel safe like that today? Maybe when someone held your hand, or fed you, or tucked you in tonight. Heavenly Father was there in that moment too."],["FORK","There are two roads. One road is forgetting, and feeling like nobody is watching over you. The other road is remembering that Heavenly Father has been with you all along. The old man took the remembering road, and it made him want to sing."],["CALM","Wrap your arms around yourself and give a slow, gentle squeeze. Take one big breath out. Then whisper, \"Heavenly Father, thank You for loving me since I was little.\""]],"remember":"Heavenly Father loved you when you were tiny, He loves you tonight, and He will love you when you are big!","ask":["“What is a psalm, and where did people sing them?”","“What did the old man remember that helped his worry grow small?”","“When did Heavenly Father start loving you?”"],"share":["When you look back over your life, where do you see Heavenly Father’s hand the whole way? Share it.","How do you help your child sense they’ve always been held by Heavenly Father? Tell of His lifelong care for you.","Did a grandparent or ancestor testify of Heavenly Father’s faithfulness across their whole life? Describe their long trust."]},"b8-w5-n6":{"story":["One of the Psalms has a very special job for grown-ups and children alike. It says to remember the wonderful things Heavenly Father has done — and to tell them to the children.","“We will not hide these things from our little ones,” the song says. “We will tell the children about Heavenly Father’s love and His mighty works.”","That way, children grow up knowing Heavenly Father. And when they grow up, they tell THEIR children too. The happy stories get passed down, on and on.","Every time you hear a story about Heavenly Father, you become part of that beautiful chain of love. And one day, you can tell these stories to others too!"],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"","feel":"Let’s pass the story along! Cup your hand to your ear and “listen” to a story, then turn and pretend to tell the happy story to someone else — “Let me tell YOU!” Can you listen, then pass it on?","stones":[["SPOT","Sharing the stories, everyone feels connected and thankful."],["NAME","That part-of — something-special feeling is called connected."],["FIND","Who tells YOU stories about Heavenly Father — and who could you tell one day?"],["FORK","Stories can be forgotten — or remembered and passed on. The psalm reminds us to tell the children, so the love keeps going."],["CALM","Take a cozy breath and think of your favorite story about Heavenly Father."]],"remember":"Every time you hear a story about Heavenly Father, you become part of a beautiful chain of love — and one day you can tell these stories too!","ask":["“What does the song say to tell the children about?”","“What happens when children grow up and have their own little ones?”"],"share":["How are you intentionally passing your testimony to the next generation? Share what you hope endures.","Which family faith story do you most want your child to carry forward? Tell it tonight.","Did a grandparent or ancestor faithfully pass down their testimony to you? Describe the gift they handed on."]},"b8-w5-n7":{"story":["The very last song this month is full of love and praise. The person sang about how kind and good Heavenly Father is.","“Heavenly Father, You are so good and forgiving,” he sang. “You are full of love for everyone who calls on You. Please teach me Your way.”","He wanted to learn to be more like Heavenly Father — kind, patient, and full of love. So he asked Heavenly Father to help him.","Heavenly Father is good, and He helps us become good too. When we ask Him to teach us His ways, He fills our hearts with love and helps us grow a little more like Him each day."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"","feel":"Let’s praise good Heavenly Father! Put both hands over your heart, then open them wide like you’re giving all your love up to Him. Now say, “Heavenly Father, You are so good!” Can you show Him your biggest love?","stones":[["SPOT","Praising Heavenly Father’s goodness, the person felt loving and hopeful."],["NAME","That warm, want-to-be-good feeling is called loving."],["FIND","What is one good, kind thing you’d like Heavenly Father to help you learn?"],["FORK","We can try to be good all alone — or ask Heavenly Father to teach us His way. The person asked Him for help to grow good."],["CALM","Hands on your heart, breathe in, and whisper, “Teach me Your way, Heavenly Father.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father is so good, and when you ask Him to teach you His ways, He helps you grow good and loving too — a little more each day!","ask":["“What did the person say Heavenly Father is full of?”","“What did he ask Heavenly Father to teach him?”"],"share":["How has asking Heavenly Father to “teach you His way” changed you over time? Share it.","What Christlike quality are you asking His help to grow right now? Tell your child why it matters.","Did a grandparent or ancestor grow remarkably Christlike over a lifetime? Describe the goodness He built in them."]},"b12-w1-n1":{"story":["Micah told the people what Heavenly Father said. He lived long ago, in the same years as Isaiah.","In his day there was fighting, and the people felt worried. So Micah told them about a happy day to come. Nobody would fight anymore!","Each family would sit under its own grapevine and fig tree. Fig trees have big, wide leaves that make cool shade.","Sitting under your own tree meant being home and safe. Micah said, “None shall make them afraid.” Micah chose to follow the Lord. He said, “We will walk in the name of the Lord.”","Your bed can be your cozy fig tree tonight. Snuggle in and feel safe. Heavenly Father is watching over you."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren loves quiet, peaceful thinking, and tonight Micah tells of a calm, peaceful time. In Wren’s world today: Wren sat in the shade of the big tree in her backyard, eating apple slices. The breeze was soft, and she felt happy and safe. Just like Micah taught, Wren learned that the Lord wants His children to feel safe and at peace.","feel":"Stretch your arms up wide like a big fig tree. Now bring them down softly and hug yourself like cool shade. Can you show me your safe, cozy face?","stones":[["SPOT","Micah told of families resting under their own fig trees, feeling safe and happy."],["NAME","That cozy, nothing-to-fear feeling is called feeling safe. Your mind might think, ‘I am home, and all is well.’ Your body might feel soft, warm, and still."],["FIND","When have you felt cozy and safe at home?"],["FORK","The people could live in worry, or walk in the name of the Lord. Micah chose to walk with the Lord."],["CALM","Snuggle under your blanket like cool shade. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for my safe home.”"]],"remember":"The Lord wants me to feel safe and at peace!","ask":["“What trees did the families sit under?”","“Did anyone make the families afraid?”","“Where did Wren sit in the shade?”"],"share":["When have you felt real peace in our home?","What can we do to make our home feel safe and calm?","Which grandparent or ancestor made a peaceful home? Share a story about them."]},"b12-w1-n2":{"story":["Long before Jesus was born, Micah told the people what Heavenly Father said. He told them a great King would come one day.","Micah did not name a big, busy city. He said, “Bethlehem, though thou be little.” Bethlehem was a small town on the hills, with sheep fields nearby.","Out of little Bethlehem would come a ruler to lead Heavenly Father’s people. Micah was talking about Jesus!","Many years later, wise men came to Jerusalem looking for the new King. The teachers there remembered Micah’s words. They said, “In Bethlehem!” And that is just where Jesus was born.","Little things can be great to Heavenly Father, and so can little you! Tonight, hold up one little finger and think of baby Jesus."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout loves to look closely at little things, and tonight Micah tells how little Bethlehem would be great. In Scout’s world today: Scout caught a tiny snowflake on his dark mitten. He looked very close. It had six little points, sparkly and beautiful! Just like Micah taught about little Bethlehem, Scout learned that little things can be great to Heavenly Father.","feel":"Curl up small like a little town on a hill. Now slowly stretch up tall, like a bright star over Bethlehem. Can you show me how big you can grow?","stones":[["SPOT","Micah said a great King would come from little Bethlehem, and the people felt hope."],["NAME","That glad, something-good-is-coming feeling is called hope. Your mind might think, ‘Jesus is coming!’ Your body might feel a warm smile and a fluttery tummy."],["FIND","When have you waited for something wonderful?"],["FORK","The teachers could forget Micah’s words, or remember them. They remembered and said, “In Bethlehem!”"],["CALM","Hold up one little finger. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for sending baby Jesus.”"]],"remember":"Jesus was born in little Bethlehem, just as Micah said!","ask":["“What little town did Micah talk about?”","“Who was born in Bethlehem?”","“What tiny thing landed on Scout’s mitten?”"],"share":["What does Jesus’s birth in little Bethlehem teach you?","How can our family keep Jesus at the center of Christmas?","Which grandparent or ancestor had a special Christmas tradition? Share it together."]},"b12-w1-n3":{"story":["Micah asked a big question: “What can I bring to the Lord?” In those days, people gave animals to the Lord as gifts.","Should he bring young calves? Thousands of sheep? Some people brought big gifts but were unkind to others.","So Micah said the Lord has already shown us what is good. He wants kind hearts even more than gifts.","The Lord wants us “to do justly,” being fair and doing what is right. He wants us “to love mercy,” being kind and forgiving. And He wants us “to walk humbly” with Heavenly Father. That is like holding His hand, knowing we need His help.","Tonight, think of one fair, kind thing to do tomorrow. Maybe say “It’s okay” when someone is sorry."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow has a warm, kind heart, and tonight Micah teaches that the Lord wants us to love mercy. In Willow’s world today: Willow’s little brother knocked over her block tower. She felt mad, but she took a breath and said, “It’s okay. Let’s build it together.” Just like Micah taught, Willow learned that being kind and forgiving makes Heavenly Father glad.","feel":"Make two fingers walk slowly across your blanket. Walk softly, like you are walking right beside Heavenly Father. Can you show me your gentle, humble walk?","stones":[["SPOT","Micah taught that the Lord wants fair, kind hearts, and the people could feel glad."],["NAME","That soft, kind-to-others feeling is called mercy. Your mind might think, ‘I will be kind, even when it’s hard.’ Your body might feel a warm heart and gentle hands."],["FIND","When have you been kind to someone who needed it?"],["FORK","Micah could think gifts were enough, or listen to the Lord. Micah listened and taught fair, kind hearts."],["CALM","Fold your gentle hands. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me be fair, kind, and humble.”"]],"remember":"Be fair, love being kind, and walk with Heavenly Father!","ask":["“What animals did Micah wonder about bringing?”","“What does the Lord want more than gifts?”","“What did Willow say to her little brother?”"],"share":["When has someone shown you mercy?","How can our family be more fair and kind this week?","Which grandparent or ancestor walked humbly with Heavenly Father? Share a story about them."]},"b12-w1-n4":{"story":["Micah told the people what Heavenly Father said. The people had made many wrong choices, called sins.","But Micah knew something wonderful. He said the Lord “delighteth in mercy.” The Lord is glad to forgive!","Micah said the Lord “will have compassion upon us.” He loves us and wants to help, like a mom hugging her crying child.","Micah said the Lord will throw our sins “into the depths of the sea.” The sea was the deepest place people knew. Things thrown there were never found again! When we are sorry, Jesus forgives us, and our sins are gone.","Tonight, pretend to toss a wrong choice far, far away. When you are sorry, Jesus forgives you too."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau is brave, and it takes courage to say sorry and start again. In Beau’s world today: Beau grabbed a toy truck from his friend, and his friend cried. Beau’s tummy felt heavy. He gave it back and said, “I’m sorry.” Just like Micah taught, Beau learned that the Lord loves to forgive us when we are sorry.","feel":"Pretend to hold a heavy rock in your hands. Now throw it far, far away into the deep sea. Splash! Can you show me how light your hands feel now?","stones":[["SPOT","Micah told how the Lord forgives sins, and the people could feel light and happy again."],["NAME","That light, clean, fresh-start feeling is called relief. Your mind might think, ‘I am forgiven!’ Your body might feel loose shoulders and a big, happy breath."],["FIND","When have you felt better after saying sorry?"],["FORK","The people could hide their wrong choices, or turn to the Lord. Micah turned to the Lord, who delights in mercy."],["CALM","Take a deep breath and let it out slowly. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for forgiving me.”"]],"remember":"The Lord loves to forgive, and He throws my sins into the deep sea!","ask":["“Where did Micah say our sins would go?”","“What does the Lord love to do?”","“What did Beau give back to his friend?”"],"share":["When have you felt the Lord’s forgiveness?","How can our family forgive each other more quickly?","Which grandparent or ancestor was quick to forgive? Share a story about them."]},"b12-w1-n5":{"story":["Nahum told the people of Judah what Heavenly Father said. A big, proud city called Nineveh had made trouble for them.","Nahum wrote, “The Lord is good, a strong hold in the day of trouble.”","Long ago, when trouble came, families hurried into a strong hold. It was a tall tower with thick stone walls. The walls kept them safe until the trouble was over.","Nahum said the Lord is like that safe place. He also said, “He knoweth them that trust in him.” The Lord knows each person who trusts Him to help. He knows you!","When you feel worried, run to the Lord, like running into a hug. Tonight, wrap your blanket around you like strong walls. You can trust Him."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren likes to think about big words, and tonight Nahum calls the Lord a strong hold. In Wren’s world today: Thunder boomed outside, and Wren felt scared. She ran to Mom, who wrapped her in a big hug. Soon she felt safe. Just like Nahum taught, Wren learned that the Lord is a safe place when trouble comes.","feel":"Press your palms together and stretch them up tall, like a strong tower. Hold very still and steady. Can you show me your strong, safe tower?","stones":[["SPOT","Nahum said the Lord is a strong, safe place, and the people could feel safe and trusting."],["NAME","That steady, I-can-lean-on-Him feeling is called trust. Your mind might think, ‘The Lord knows me and will help.’ Your body might feel slow breaths and a relaxed tummy."],["FIND","When have you run to someone who kept you safe?"],["FORK","In trouble, the people could feel all alone, or run to the Lord. Nahum taught them to trust the Lord."],["CALM","Wrap your blanket snugly around you. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, You are my safe place.”"]],"remember":"The Lord is good, and He keeps me safe like a strong tower!","ask":["“What did Nahum call the Lord?”","“Who does the Lord know?”","“Where did Wren run when thunder boomed?”"],"share":["When has the Lord been your strong hold on a hard day?","How can our home feel like a safe strong hold?","Which grandparent or ancestor trusted the Lord in hard times? Share their story."]},"b12-w1-n6":{"story":["Habakkuk told the people what Heavenly Father said. He knew hard times were coming to his land.","So Habakkuk sang a prayer. Maybe the fig trees would not bloom. Maybe no grapes would grow, and the sheep would be gone.","“Yet I will rejoice in the Lord,” Habakkuk sang. Even with empty fields, he would still be glad in the Lord.","He sang, “The Lord is my strength.” He said the Lord would make his feet like a deer’s feet. Deer climb steep, rocky hills and do not slip. The Lord would help Habakkuk walk strong in hard, high places.","When something feels hard, the Lord can make you strong too. Tonight, wiggle your feet, then let them rest. He will help you climb."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau is brave, and Habakkuk was brave enough to be glad even in hard times. In Beau’s world today: Beau climbed the big hill at the park. His legs felt tired. He whispered, “Help me, Heavenly Father,” and kept stepping until he reached the top! Just like Habakkuk, Beau learned that the Lord gives us strength for hard climbs.","feel":"Lie back and make your feet climb the air: step, step, step, like a deer up a rocky hill. Can you show me your strong, sure feet?","stones":[["SPOT","Habakkuk faced hard times, but he felt glad and strong in the Lord."],["NAME","That brave, glad-anyway feeling is called joy. Your mind might think, ‘Even when it’s hard, the Lord helps me.’ Your body might feel a strong back and a warm smile."],["FIND","When have you kept going when something was hard?"],["FORK","Habakkuk could give up and stay sad, or rejoice in the Lord. Habakkuk chose to rejoice."],["CALM","Wiggle your toes, then let them rest. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for making me strong.”"]],"remember":"Even in hard times I can be glad, because the Lord makes me strong!","ask":["“What animal did Habakkuk sing about?”","“What did Habakkuk say he would still do?”","“What did Beau climb at the park?”"],"share":["When has the Lord given you strength for something hard?","What helps our family find joy in hard times?","Which grandparent or ancestor stayed joyful in hard times? Share their story."]},"b12-w1-n7":{"story":["Zephaniah told the people what Heavenly Father said. Many people had turned away from the Lord, and hard days were coming.","But Zephaniah told of a happy day to come. He said, “Be glad and rejoice with all the heart.” The Lord would be “in the midst of thee,” right there with them.","Zephaniah was talking about Jesus! He said, “Fear thou not.” No one needs to be afraid when Jesus is near.","Zephaniah said the Lord “will rejoice over thee with joy.” He said the Lord “will joy over thee with singing”! He is so happy with His children, like a mom singing over her baby.","The Lord loves you that much! Tonight, as you snuggle down, picture Him smiling over you with joy."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow is warm and tender, and tonight Zephaniah tells how tenderly the Lord loves His children. In Willow’s world today: Willow sang a soft lullaby to her baby cousin. The baby smiled and fell asleep. Willow felt so much love! Just like Zephaniah taught, Willow learned that the Lord loves us so much, it is like He sings over us.","feel":"Hug your arms around yourself like a soft, warm blanket. Now rock gently side to side, like a song. Can you show me your happy, loved smile?","stones":[["SPOT","Zephaniah said the Lord would rejoice and sing over His people, and they felt deeply loved."],["NAME","That warm, wrapped-in-love feeling is called feeling loved. Your mind might think, ‘The Lord is happy with me.’ Your body might feel warm all over, like a hug."],["FIND","When has someone sung you a sweet song?"],["FORK","The people could stay afraid, or hear Zephaniah’s words, “Fear thou not.” Zephaniah taught them to be glad."],["CALM","Hum one soft note. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for loving me so much.”"]],"remember":"The Lord is close to me, and He is so happy to love me!","ask":["“What did Zephaniah say the Lord would do over us?”","“Is the Lord near or far away?”","“Who did Willow sing to?”"],"share":["When have you felt the Lord’s joy over you?","What songs help our family feel the Lord’s love?","Which grandparent or ancestor sang to their children? Share a song or story about them."]},"b12-w2-n1":{"story":["The people had come home to Jerusalem from far away. They built nice houses for themselves. But the Lord’s house was still not built.","“It is not time yet,” they said. So Heavenly Father sent Haggai. “Is it time for nice houses,” the Lord asked, “while My house lies broken?”","The people worked hard, but they never had enough. “Consider your ways,” said the Lord. Stop and think: what should come first?","“Go up to the mountain, bring wood, and build the house,” said the Lord. The people obeyed. “I am with you,” the Lord said through Haggai. Everyone went to work!","You can put Heavenly Father first too, like praying before you play. Tonight, think of one way to put Him first tomorrow."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren likes to stop and think, just as the Lord asked the people to “consider your ways.” In Wren’s world today: Wren ran to her toys one morning. Then she stopped and thought, “What should come first?” She knelt and said her prayer, then played happily. Just like the people of Jerusalem, Wren learned that putting the Lord first feels right inside.","feel":"Pretend to carry wood from the mountain. Stack it, one, two, three. Can you show me how you build the Lord’s house?","stones":[["SPOT","Haggai asked the people to stop and think, and they felt ready to put the Lord first."],["NAME","That quiet, thinking-it-over feeling is called thoughtfulness. Your mind might think, ‘What should come first?’ Your body might feel still, with a calm, slow breath."],["FIND","When have you stopped to think about what to do first?"],["FORK","The people could keep fixing their own houses, or build the Lord’s house. They obeyed and built the Lord’s house."],["CALM","Fold your hands and be very still. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me put You first.”"]],"remember":"When I put the Lord first, He is with me!","ask":["“What did the Lord ask the people to build?”","“What did the Lord say to the people who listened?”","“What did Wren do before she played?”"],"share":["How has putting the Lord first blessed your life?","What is one way our family can put the Lord first this week?","Which grandparent or ancestor put the Lord first? Share a story about them."]},"b12-w2-n2":{"story":["The people were building the Lord’s house again. Some old people remembered the first one, so big and beautiful.","“How does this house look to you now?” the Lord asked. “Like nothing?” Next to the first one, it looked so small.","So Heavenly Father sent Haggai with a message. “Be strong, all you people, and work,” said the Lord. “For I am with you.”","The Lord said, “My Spirit stays with you. Fear not.” He promised this house would be even greater than the first. “In this place I will give peace.”","When a job feels too big, the Lord is with you too. Tonight, make strong arms and say, “I can keep going!”"],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau is brave, and tonight the Lord told the builders, “Be strong, and work.” In Beau’s world today: Beau’s puzzle had so many pieces that he wanted to quit. He took a deep breath and found one piece, then another. Just like the builders, Beau learned that we can be strong and keep working, because the Lord is with us.","feel":"Sit up tall and make strong arms. Now hug yourself, like the Lord saying, “I am with you.” Can you show me your brave, happy face?","stones":[["SPOT","The new temple looked small, but the Lord said, “I am with you,” and the people felt strong."],["NAME","That strong, keep-going feeling is called courage. Your mind might think, ‘I can do it.’ Your body might feel tall, with strong arms and a steady heart."],["FIND","When have you kept working even when a job felt too big?"],["FORK","The people could stop because the house looked small, or keep working. They were strong and kept building."],["CALM","Hug your pillow and breathe slowly. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for being with me.”"]],"remember":"Be strong and keep going, for the Lord is with you!","ask":["“What did the Lord tell the people to be?”","“Who did the Lord say was with them?”","“What did Beau keep working on?”"],"share":["When did you feel the Lord with you during a hard job?","What hard thing can our family keep working on together?","Which grandparent or ancestor kept working through hard times? Share their story."]},"b12-w2-n3":{"story":["Zechariah shared Heavenly Father’s words. The people were home in Jerusalem again. But the walls that kept them safe were still broken.","Zechariah saw a man with a long rope for measuring. “Where are you going?” he asked. “To measure Jerusalem,” the man said.","So many people would live there, they would spread past any wall. “I will be a wall of fire all around her,” said the Lord.","A wall of fire glows bright and keeps danger away. “Sing and be happy!” the Lord said. “For I come, and I will live with you.” The Lord who said this is Jesus.","When you wonder, you can ask, just like Zechariah. Tonight, hum a happy song. Jesus loves to be near you."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout loves to ask questions, just like Zechariah asked, “Where are you going?” In Scout’s world today: Scout saw his dad with a measuring tape. “What are you measuring?” Scout asked. “Your room, for a new shelf,” Dad said. Scout held the end. Just like Zechariah, Scout learned that asking questions helps us learn wonderful things.","feel":"Stretch your arms way out wide, like a measuring line. Now make a big circle around you, like the Lord keeping you safe. Can you show me your big safe circle?","stones":[["SPOT","Zechariah learned the Lord would come and live with His people, and they felt happy enough to sing."],["NAME","That bright, singing feeling is called joy. Your mind might think, ‘Jesus wants to be with me!’ Your body might feel bouncy, with a big smile."],["FIND","When have you felt so happy that you wanted to sing?"],["FORK","Zechariah could stay quiet, or ask about what he saw. He asked, and he learned wonderful news."],["CALM","Hug yourself softly and smile. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that Jesus will come again.”"]],"remember":"Sing and be happy, for Jesus will come and live with His people!","ask":["“What did the man have in his hand?”","“What did the Lord tell the people to do?”","“What did Scout help his dad measure?”"],"share":["What helps you feel happy when you think of Jesus coming again?","What song about Jesus could our family sing together?","Which grandparent or ancestor loved to sing about Jesus? Share a story about them."]},"b12-w2-n4":{"story":["The Lord showed Zechariah a picture. Joshua, the leader of the priests at the Lord’s house, stood before an angel.","Joshua’s clothes were dirty, a picture of the wrong things he had done.","“Take away the dirty clothes,” the angel said. He told Joshua, “I have taken your wrong things away.” They dressed him in clean clothes and a clean hat!","Then the Lord promised to send “the Branch.” A branch is new green growth from an old tree. Jesus would come from King David’s family, like a new branch. The Branch is Jesus!","When we are sorry, Jesus makes us clean and new inside. Tonight, tell Heavenly Father one thing you are sorry for. Then feel brand new!"],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow has a tender heart, and tonight the Lord tenderly gave Joshua clean new clothes. In Willow’s world today: Willow played outside and came in muddy from head to toe. Mom helped her wash up and put on soft, clean pajamas. Willow twirled. “I feel brand new!” she said. Just like Joshua, Willow learned that being made clean and new feels wonderful.","feel":"Pretend to take off a dirty, heavy coat and drop it away. Now slip on a soft, clean shirt. Can you show me how clean and new you feel?","stones":[["SPOT","The angel took away Joshua’s dirty clothes and gave him clean ones, so Joshua could feel clean."],["NAME","That light, clean-and-new feeling is called peace. Your mind might think, ‘I can start fresh.’ Your body might feel light, like a heavy bag was put down."],["FIND","When have you said sorry and felt better inside?"],["FORK","We could hide our wrong choices, or bring them to the Lord. Joshua stood before the Lord’s angel, and he was made clean."],["CALM","Rub your hands softly, like washing. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, please help me feel clean and new.”"]],"remember":"Jesus can make us clean and new inside!","ask":["“What kind of clothes was Joshua wearing at first?”","“Who is the Branch?”","“What did Willow put on after she washed up?”"],"share":["When have you felt clean and new after saying sorry?","How can our family forgive each other quickly?","Which grandparent or ancestor was quick to forgive? Share a story about them."]},"b12-w2-n5":{"story":["An angel woke Zechariah. “What do you see?” he asked. Zechariah saw a golden lampstand with seven little lamps.","Two olive trees stood beside it. Back then, lamps burned olive oil to make light. “What are these?” Zechariah asked.","The angel gave a message for Zerubbabel, who was building the Lord’s house. “Not by might, nor by power, but by my spirit,” said the Lord.","Zerubbabel did not have to be big and strong. The Lord’s Spirit would help him finish the Lord’s house!","You do not have to be big and strong either. Tonight, think of a big job for tomorrow. Say a prayer, and the Lord’s Spirit will help you."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau is brave, and tonight he learns that real strength comes from the Lord’s Spirit. In Beau’s world today: On his first day in a new class, Beau’s tummy felt wobbly. He said a little prayer and felt calm inside. Then he waved hello to a new friend. Just like Zerubbabel, Beau learned that the Lord’s Spirit helps us when we feel small.","feel":"Make big muscle arms. Now let them go soft, and hold your hands open like a little lamp. Can you show me your quiet, glowing light?","stones":[["SPOT","Zerubbabel had a big job, but the Lord’s Spirit would help, and he could feel sure."],["NAME","That calm, safe-inside feeling is called trust. Your mind might think, ‘The Lord will help me.’ Your body might feel calm, with soft shoulders and a steady heart."],["FIND","When have you asked Heavenly Father for help with something big?"],["FORK","Zerubbabel could try in his own strength alone, or trust the Lord’s Spirit. With the Lord’s help, he finished the Lord’s house."],["CALM","Open your hands softly on your blanket. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, please help me by Your Spirit.”"]],"remember":"The Lord’s Spirit helps me do big things!","ask":["“What did Zechariah see beside the golden lampstand?”","“Whose Spirit helps us do big things?”","“What helped Beau feel calm on his first day?”"],"share":["When has the Spirit helped you do something too big for you?","What big job can our family ask the Lord to help with?","Which grandparent or ancestor trusted the Lord with a big job? Share their story."]},"b12-w2-n6":{"story":["Many people had lived far away from Jerusalem. Now some had come home. Zechariah told them about a happy day to come.","“I will come back and live in Jerusalem,” said the Lord. It would be a city of truth. His mountain there would be holy, very special to Heavenly Father.","There would be old men and old women in the streets again. Each would hold a walking stick, because they were so old.","And the streets would be full of boys and girls playing! Young and old would be safe and happy together.","Your home can feel like that city too. Tonight, think of a kind thing to do for a grandma, grandpa or friend."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow has a warm heart for young and old, and tonight the Lord tells of a city full of both. In Willow’s world today: Willow walked with her grandpa. He walked slowly with his cane, so she walked slowly too and held his hand. They laughed at the ducks together. Just like the people in Zechariah’s happy city, Willow learned that being kind to young and old makes a happy place.","feel":"Wiggle your toes like boys and girls skipping and playing in the street. Now lie still like a grandpa resting. Can you show me your happy playing face?","stones":[["SPOT","The Lord told of a city with old people and children playing, and they felt happy and safe."],["NAME","That warm, all-together feeling is called belonging. Your mind might think, ‘I am safe here.’ Your body might feel warm and snuggly, with a happy smile."],["FIND","When have you felt happy and safe with your family?"],["FORK","The people could be unkind to each other, or show love and kindness. The Lord asked them to be kind to everyone."],["CALM","Hug your pillow like a family hug. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me make my home happy and kind.”"]],"remember":"The Lord’s holy city is happy for grandparents and children too!","ask":["“Who will play in the streets of the happy city?”","“What did the old men and old women hold?”","“How did Willow help her grandpa?”"],"share":["What makes our home feel safe and happy to you?","How can our family show kindness to older people this week?","What do you remember about being with your grandparents as a child? Share a story about them."]},"b12-w2-n7":{"story":["Zechariah told about the day Jesus comes again as King. “The Lord shall be king over all the earth,” he said.","Even the horses’ bells will say, “Holiness unto the Lord.” Something holy belongs to Heavenly Father and is special to Him.","Even the cooking pots will be holy, like the bowls at the Lord’s house.","Why bells and pots? People used them every day. Zechariah was teaching that everyday things can be holy too.","A spoon stirring soup for a sick friend is for the Lord too. Tonight, pick one thing in your room to use for Him tomorrow."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren loves to wonder, and tonight she wonders how even a cooking pot can be holy. In Wren’s world today: Wren helped her mom stir soup for a sick neighbor. “Is cooking holy?” she asked. “When we do it with love for Jesus, it can be,” Mom said. Just like the pots in Zechariah’s words, Wren learned that everyday things can be for the Lord.","feel":"Pretend to ring a tiny bell, ding, ding, ding. Now stir a little pot slowly. Can you show me how you do everyday things with love?","stones":[["SPOT","Zechariah told how even bells and pots will be holy, and the people felt wonder and joy."],["NAME","That soft, wide-eyed feeling is called wonder. Your mind might think, ‘Everyday things can be for Jesus!’ Your body might feel tingly and still, with big shining eyes."],["FIND","When have you done an everyday job with love?"],["FORK","We could think everyday things do not matter, or do them for the Lord. Zechariah taught that everyday things can be holy."],["CALM","Hold your hands like a tiny bell, very still. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help my whole day be for You.”"]],"remember":"Everyday things can be holy when we do them for the Lord!","ask":["“What words will be on the horses’ bells?”","“What everyday kitchen things will be holy?”","“What did Wren help her mom cook?”"],"share":["What everyday thing helps you feel close to the Lord?","How can our family make our home holy every single day?","Which grandparent or ancestor made everyday work holy? Share a story about them."]},"b12-w3-n1":{"story":["Long before Jesus was born, Heavenly Father’s people came home to Jerusalem. But many had stopped thinking about the Lord.","So the Lord gave Malachi a message to share. It began with four warm words: “I have loved you.”","The people asked, “How have You loved us?” So the Lord reminded them. He had loved their family since long ago, all the way back to Jacob.","The Lord said His name would be great everywhere. From “the rising of the sun” to its “going down,” all would know Him. The Lord who spoke to Malachi was Jesus, speaking for Heavenly Father.","Jesus loves you too, every morning and every night. Tonight, say “I love you” to someone you love, and feel His love too."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow has a warm, loving heart, and tonight the Lord told His people, “I have loved you.” In Willow’s world today: Willow’s little brother felt grumpy and alone. Willow sat by him and said, “I love you, every single day.” He gave her a big smile. Just like the Lord did for His people, Willow learned that saying “I love you” helps hearts feel warm.","feel":"Stretch your arms up high like the morning sun. Slowly bring them down like the sun setting. Then wrap them around you. Can you show me a big, loved hug?","stones":[["SPOT","The Lord told His people, “I have loved you,” and He wanted them to feel loved."],["NAME","That warm, safe, cared-for feeling is called feeling loved. Your mind might think, ‘Someone loves me.’ Your body might feel warm and soft, right in your heart."],["FIND","When have you felt really, really loved?"],["FORK","The people could forget the Lord’s love, or remember it. Malachi took the road of sharing the Lord’s loving message."],["CALM","Put your hand on your heart. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for loving me.”"]],"remember":"The Lord loves me, from sunrise to sunset!","ask":["“What did the Lord tell Malachi’s people?”","“Where would people know the Lord’s name?”","“What did Willow tell her little brother?”"],"share":["When have you felt the Lord’s love for you?","How can our family show love from morning to night?","Which grandparent or ancestor made you feel loved? Share a story about them."]},"b12-w3-n2":{"story":["Long before Jesus was born, the Lord gave Malachi a happy promise. He said, “I will send my messenger.”","This helper would be sent ahead to “prepare the way.” He would help people get ready, like tidying up before a special guest comes.","Then the Lord Himself would come to His temple. Malachi was talking about Jesus.","Many years later, John the Baptist came. He helped people get ready for Jesus and baptized them in the Jordan River. Jesus said John was the messenger Malachi wrote about.","You can get ready for Jesus too, by being kind like Him. Tonight, think of one kind thing you will do tomorrow."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau is brave and ready to help, like John the Baptist, who boldly helped people get ready for Jesus. In Beau’s world today: Grandma was coming to visit! Beau picked up his toys and swept the front step with his little broom. When she came, he opened the door wide. Just like John the Baptist, Beau learned that getting ready shows how much we love the one who is coming.","feel":"Pretend to sweep a path with a little broom, swish, swish. Now open your arms wide to welcome a special guest. Can you show me your ready, happy face?","stones":[["SPOT","Malachi promised a messenger, and John helped people get ready for Jesus, full of hope."],["NAME","That eager, can’t-wait, getting-ready feeling is called excitement. Your mind might think, ‘Someone special is coming!’ Your body might feel bouncy, with a big smile."],["FIND","When have you gotten ready for someone special to come?"],["FORK","John could stay quiet, or tell everyone to get ready. John bravely took the road of helping people get ready for Jesus."],["CALM","Fold your arms and take a slow breath. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help my heart be ready for Jesus.”"]],"remember":"John helped people get ready for Jesus, and I can get ready too!","ask":["“Who did the Lord promise to send?”","“Who did John help people get ready for?”","“How did Beau get ready for Grandma?”"],"share":["How do you get your heart ready for Jesus at Christmas?","Who helped you get ready to follow Jesus?","Which grandparent or ancestor helped others get ready for Jesus? Share a story about them."]},"b12-w3-n3":{"story":["Malachi’s people had stopped following the Lord’s ways. So the Lord called to them, “Return unto me.”","Malachi said the Lord would be “like a refiner’s fire.” The refiner heated silver in his fire until it melted.","Then the dirty bits came up, and he took them away. The silver was left clean and shiny. Malachi was talking about Jesus, who makes us clean.","The Lord would also be like “fullers’ soap.” The fuller scrubbed cloth with strong soap until it was white.","When we are sorry and try again, Jesus helps us start fresh. His love can make your heart clean and shining too. Tonight, tell Heavenly Father you will try again."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren loves to wonder what picture-words mean, like Malachi’s refiner’s fire and washing soap. In Wren’s world today: Wren helped Grandma polish an old silver spoon. It looked dark and dull, but they rubbed and rubbed until it shone. Wren could see her smile in it! Just like Malachi taught, Wren learned that Jesus helps us become clean and shining inside.","feel":"Rub your hands together like you are washing with soap. Scrub, scrub, scrub! Now hold them up, clean and shining. Can you show me your sparkly clean hands?","stones":[["SPOT","Malachi taught that Jesus makes people clean and shining, like silver, and that gave them hope."],["NAME","That fresh, clean, start-again feeling is called feeling forgiven. Your mind might think, ‘I can try again.’ Your body might feel light, like after a warm bath."],["FIND","When have you felt fresh and new after saying sorry?"],["FORK","The people could keep their wrong ways, or come back to the Lord. Malachi taught them the Lord said, “Return unto me.”"],["CALM","Rub your hands slowly, then rest them still. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me be clean and shining.”"]],"remember":"Jesus helps me be clean and shining, like silver!","ask":["“What did the refiner make clean and shiny?”","“What did the fuller wash with soap?”","“What did Wren help Grandma polish?”"],"share":["When have you felt Jesus help you start fresh?","How can our family help each other say sorry and try again?","Which grandparent or ancestor worked hard with their hands? Share a story about them."]},"b12-w3-n4":{"story":["In Malachi’s day, people grew their own food. The Lord asked them to pay tithing: one part of every ten, given back.","They brought it to the storehouse, a room for food at the Lord’s house. But many people had stopped bringing it.","So the Lord said, “Prove me.” He meant, “Try it, and see what I will do.”","He promised to open “the windows of heaven” and “pour you out a blessing.” Rain falls from the sky and makes food grow. The Lord would pour down blessings like rain, more than they had room for.","Children can pay tithing too, one coin out of every ten. Tonight, count three blessings on your fingers and say thank you."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau has faith, and it takes faith to give back to the Lord and trust Him. In Beau’s world today: Beau got ten shiny coins for his birthday. He bravely chose one coin and put it in the tithing envelope with his dad. Just like the Lord promised, Beau learned that giving back to the Lord brings blessings.","feel":"Hold your hands up high like open windows. Now wiggle your fingers down slowly, like gentle rain falling on you. Can you show me how blessings pour down?","stones":[["SPOT","The Lord promised to pour down blessings, and His people could feel trust and thanks."],["NAME","That full, warm, thank-you feeling is called gratitude. Your mind might think, ‘I have so many blessings!’ Your body might feel full and happy in your chest."],["FIND","When have you felt thankful for a blessing?"],["FORK","The people could keep it all, or give back one part. Malachi asked them to give, and see the Lord’s blessings pour down."],["CALM","Rest your open hands on your blanket. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for pouring down blessings.”"]],"remember":"When I pay my tithing, the Lord pours down blessings!","ask":["“What did people bring to the storehouse?”","“What did the Lord promise to open?”","“What did Beau put in the tithing envelope?”"],"share":["What blessings have come to our family from paying tithing?","How can we help each other give back to the Lord?","Which grandparent or ancestor paid tithing faithfully? Share a story about them."]},"b12-w3-n5":{"story":["In Malachi’s day, many people forgot the Lord. But some loved Him. They talked together often, and they thought about Him.","The Lord listened, and He heard every word.","So a book was written for them. It was called a book of remembrance, so they would always be remembered.","The Lord said, “They shall be mine... my jewels.” Jewels are bright, precious stones that people keep safe. He would keep His people safe too, like a dad keeps his son.","You are precious to the Lord too. Tonight, talk with your family about something you love about Jesus."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow has a tender heart, and tonight the Lord tenderly called His people “my jewels.” In Willow’s world today: Willow and Dad hung ornaments on the Christmas tree. Willow held the little glass one very gently. Dad smiled and said, “You keep it safe because it is precious. You are precious to me, and to the Lord.” Just like the Lord’s people, Willow learned that she is precious and loved.","feel":"Cup your hands together like you are holding a tiny jewel. Hold it close to your heart, very gently. Can you show me how you keep something precious safe?","stones":[["SPOT","The people talked about the Lord, and He called them His jewels, so they felt precious."],["NAME","That treasured, cared-for, special feeling is called feeling precious. Your mind might think, ‘I matter to Heavenly Father.’ Your body might feel warm and glowing, like a hug."],["FIND","When have you felt special and treasured?"],["FORK","The people could forget the Lord, or talk together about Him. Those good people took the road of remembering Him."],["CALM","Hold your cupped hands close to your heart. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that I am precious to You.”"]],"remember":"I am one of the Lord’s precious jewels!","ask":["“What did the Lord call His people?”","“What kind of book was written for them?”","“What did Willow hold very gently?”"],"share":["What would you write in our family’s book of remembrance?","When have you felt precious to the Lord?","Which grandparent or ancestor kept a journal or family record? Share a story about them."]},"b12-w3-n6":{"story":["Malachi had a happy promise for the people who loved the Lord.","The “Sun of righteousness” would rise, the One who is always good and right. Malachi was talking about Jesus.","Like the morning sun, Jesus brings light and warmth to the whole world. He would come “with healing in his wings,” like warm sunbeams spreading wide.","Then the people would “grow up as calves of the stall.” Baby cows wait inside a barn stall. When they are let out, they run and jump in the sunshine!","Jesus helps us feel better and glad inside. Tonight, wiggle your toes like a happy calf. Then rest in His warm love."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout loves to discover things outside, like the warm sun and the happy calves in Malachi’s picture. In Scout’s world today: Scout had a cold and stayed inside for days. When he felt better, he ran out into the sunny yard, jumping and laughing. Just like Malachi said, Scout learned that Heavenly Father helps us get better and feel glad.","feel":"Spread your arms wide like warm sunshine. Now kick your feet under the blanket like a happy calf running out to play. Can you show me your sunny, happy feet?","stones":[["SPOT","Malachi said Jesus would bring healing, and people would feel glad, like calves let out to play."],["NAME","That bright, bouncy, free feeling is called joy. Your mind might think, ‘I feel so much better!’ Your body might feel light and springy, like you could jump."],["FIND","When have you felt bouncy and glad after feeling better?"],["FORK","The people could turn away from the Lord, or love and honor Him. Malachi said those who honor Him would feel His healing light."],["CALM","Feel your warm blanket like sunshine on you. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for Jesus, my healing light.”"]],"remember":"Jesus is like the warm sun, and He helps me feel better!","ask":["“What did Malachi say would rise?”","“What do calves do when they are let out?”","“What did Scout do when he felt better?”"],"share":["When has Jesus helped you heal, in body or in heart?","How can our family bring sunshine to someone who is sad?","Which grandparent or ancestor helped others feel better? Share a story about them."]},"b12-w3-n7":{"story":["At the end of his book, Malachi wrote a promise. The Lord said, “I will send you Elijah the prophet.”","Elijah had taught Heavenly Father’s words long before. Then he was taken up to heaven.","Elijah would “turn the heart of the fathers to the children.” Children’s hearts would turn to their fathers too. Families now would love their families from long ago.","In 1836, Elijah came! He stood before Joseph Smith and Oliver Cowdery in the Kirtland Temple. He gave them Heavenly Father’s power to join families together forever in temples.","Your family can be together forever too. Tonight, as you snuggle in, think of your family, even great-grandparents long ago."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren loves to wonder about long ago, and tonight Elijah came to turn hearts to families of long ago. In Wren’s world today: Wren looked at an old photo of her great-grandma as a little girl. “She looks like me!” Wren said, and she felt a warm love for her. Just like Elijah promised, Wren learned that our hearts can turn to our family from long ago.","feel":"Put both hands on your heart. Now turn slowly to one side, then the other, like turning your heart toward your family. Can you show me your turning heart?","stones":[["SPOT","Elijah came as Malachi promised, so families could be together forever and feel full of love."],["NAME","That close, heart-to-heart, together feeling is called belonging. Your mind might think, ‘We belong together.’ Your body might feel warm and snug, all over."],["FIND","When have you felt that you belong with your family?"],["FORK","Joseph Smith could keep this to himself, or teach it. Joseph taught it, so families everywhere could be together forever."],["CALM","Hug your pillow like a family hug. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that families can be forever.”"]],"remember":"Families can be together forever, because Elijah came!","ask":["“Who did the Lord promise to send?”","“Where did Elijah come to Joseph Smith?”","“Whose old photo did Wren look at?”"],"share":["What do you feel when you think of the temple?","How can our family learn about our families of long ago?","Which grandparent or ancestor would you love to meet someday? Share what you know about them."]},"b12-w4-n1":{"story":["Long ago, Heavenly Father helped a man named Balaam see far ahead. Balaam said, “There shall come a Star out of Jacob.”","Jacob’s family was Israel, Heavenly Father’s people. A star shows travelers the way. Balaam was talking about Jesus!","When Jesus was born, wise men in the east saw His star. They came a long way to find the new King.","The star went ahead and stopped over the house where young Jesus was. The wise men were so full of joy! They knelt and worshiped Him. They gave Him gold and sweet-smelling frankincense and myrrh.","They kept following, and they found Jesus. Tonight, picture that bright star. You can keep looking for Jesus too, by learning about Him."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout loves to seek and find, just like the wise men who followed the star to Jesus. In Scout’s world today: At a Christmas party, Scout played a star hunt. He followed little paper stars down the hall, one by one, until he found the last one. Just like the wise men, Scout learned that if we keep looking, we find what we are seeking.","feel":"Hold one hand up high like a shining star. Now walk your other fingers across your blanket, following it. Can you show me how the wise men followed the star?","stones":[["SPOT","The wise men followed the star and found Jesus, and they felt great joy."],["NAME","That bright, bouncy, I-found-it feeling is called joy. Your mind might think, ‘We found Him!’ Your body might feel light and sparkly, with a big smile."],["FIND","When have you felt joy after finding something special?"],["FORK","The wise men could stay home, or follow the star a long way. They followed it and found Jesus."],["CALM","Hold your hands over your heart like a gift. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for Jesus.”"]],"remember":"Jesus is the bright Star, and wise people still seek Him!","ask":["“What did the wise men follow?”","“What gifts did the wise men give Jesus?”","“What did Scout follow down the hall?”"],"share":["When did you first truly seek Jesus for yourself?","What gift could our family give Jesus this Christmas?","Which grandparent or ancestor gave up a lot to follow Jesus? Share their story."]},"b12-w4-n2":{"story":["Long before Jesus was born, Isaiah told what Heavenly Father showed him. He talked about an old tree stump.","A stump looks all done. But Isaiah said a little new branch would grow out of its roots!","The stump was a picture of Jesse’s family. Jesse was King David’s father. Someone special would grow from their family, like that new branch.","Isaiah said the Spirit of the Lord would rest on Him, making Him wise. Years later, an angel told Mary, “You will have a son. Call Him Jesus.” The angel said He would be King, like David. Isaiah was talking about Jesus!","Jesus is that little Branch. Tonight, think of a tiny green shoot. With Jesus, good things can always grow again."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren loves to wonder, and tonight brings a big wonder: how can a new branch grow from an old stump? In Wren’s world today: On a walk in the park, Wren found an old tree stump. A tiny green shoot was poking up beside it! Wren knelt down and smiled. Just like Isaiah, Wren learned that new life can grow where things look finished.","feel":"Curl up small, like a quiet root under the ground. Now slowly stretch one arm up, like a little green branch growing. Can you show me your growing branch?","stones":[["SPOT","Isaiah told of a new branch from an old stump, and the people felt hope."],["NAME","That warm, looking-forward feeling is called hope. Your mind might think, ‘Something good is coming.’ Your body might feel light, with a soft, happy chest."],["FIND","When have you felt hope that something good was coming?"],["FORK","The people could think Jesse’s family was all done, or trust the Lord’s promise. Isaiah trusted, and Jesus came."],["CALM","Stretch your fingers up like a tiny branch. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for sending Jesus.”"]],"remember":"Jesus is the little Branch from David’s family, sent with love!","ask":["“What grew from the old tree stump?”","“Who was the Branch Isaiah talked about?”","“What did Wren find by the stump?”"],"share":["When has Heavenly Father brought new hope after a hard time?","How have you seen Jesus’s wisdom help our family?","Which grandparent or ancestor helped our family tree grow strong in faith? Share their story."]},"b12-w4-n3":{"story":["Long ago, Micah told the people what Heavenly Father said. Bethlehem was a little town, but Micah said it would be special.","A great Ruler would come from Bethlehem, King David’s town. Micah was talking about Jesus!","Years later, a ruler named Caesar said everyone must be counted. Joseph was from David’s family, so he took Mary to Bethlehem.","While they were there, baby Jesus was born! Mary wrapped Him snugly in soft cloths. There was no room for them at the inn, where travelers stay. So Mary laid Him in a manger, a little feed box for animals.","Mary and Joseph made a cozy place for Jesus with love. Tonight, you can make room for Jesus in your heart too."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow has a tender heart, and tonight tiny baby Jesus was wrapped up warm and laid gently in a manger. In Willow’s world today: At Grandma’s house for Christmas, every bed was full. Willow scooted over and shared her blanket with her little cousin. Just like Mary and Joseph, Willow learned that love finds a cozy place, even when there is no room.","feel":"Hold your arms like you are cradling a tiny baby. Sway slowly, soft and gentle. Can you show me how softly you would hold baby Jesus?","stones":[["SPOT","Baby Jesus was born in little Bethlehem, and Mary wrapped Him with gentle love."],["NAME","That soft, warm, snuggly feeling is called love. Your mind might think, ‘I want to keep you safe.’ Your body might feel warm all over, with gentle arms."],["FIND","When have you felt love for someone small?"],["FORK","Mary and Joseph could give up when the inn was full, or make a cozy place. They laid Jesus in a manger."],["CALM","Wrap your blanket snug, like soft baby cloths. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for baby Jesus.”"]],"remember":"Jesus was born in little Bethlehem, and there is room for Him in my heart!","ask":["“In what little town was Jesus born?”","“Where did Mary lay baby Jesus?”","“How did Willow make room for her cousin?”"],"share":["How do you make room for Jesus in the busy Christmas season?","What helps our home feel peaceful at Christmas?","Which grandparent or ancestor always made room for others in their home? Share a story about them."]},"b12-w4-n4":{"story":["Long ago, Abraham told Isaac that Heavenly Father would give a lamb. Later, Heavenly Father asked His people for perfect lambs.","Those lambs were a picture. They pointed to Jesus, Heavenly Father’s perfect Son.","The night Jesus was born, shepherds near Bethlehem were keeping their sheep safe.","Suddenly an angel came, and bright light shone all around. The shepherds were afraid. The angel said, “Fear not! I bring you good news of great joy. A Savior is born!” Years later, John the Baptist said of Jesus, “Look, the Lamb of God!”","Jesus is Heavenly Father’s Lamb, sent to save us. When you feel afraid, remember: “Fear not!” Rest snug and safe, like a little lamb."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau is brave, and tonight the angel told the frightened shepherds, “Fear not!” In Beau’s world today: At the neighbor’s house, a big fluffy dog bounded up, and Beau felt scared. The neighbor smiled and said, “Do not be afraid. He is friendly.” Beau held out his hand, and the dog gave it a gentle lick! Just like the shepherds, Beau learned that kind, good words can help fear go away.","feel":"Cup your hands like you are holding a tiny lamb. Now look up, like the shepherds seeing the bright light. Can you show me your brave, wide-open eyes?","stones":[["SPOT","The shepherds felt afraid, but the angel’s good news helped them not be afraid."],["NAME","That strong, standing-tall feeling is called courage. Your mind might think, ‘I do not need to be afraid.’ Your body might feel steady, with strong shoulders."],["FIND","When have you felt brave after someone said, “Do not be afraid”?"],["FORK","The shepherds could stay afraid, or listen to the angel. They listened and heard about Jesus."],["CALM","Hug your knees like a little lamb. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for Jesus, Your perfect Lamb.”"]],"remember":"Jesus is Heavenly Father’s perfect Lamb, sent to save me!","ask":["“What were the shepherds taking care of?”","“What did the angel tell the shepherds?”","“What did the neighbor tell Beau about the dog?”"],"share":["When has the good news of Jesus helped you feel less afraid?","What does it mean to you that Jesus is Heavenly Father’s Lamb?","Which grandparent or ancestor faced fear with faith? Share their story."]},"b12-w4-n5":{"story":["Long before Jesus was born, Isaiah told what Heavenly Father said. “The people who walked in darkness have seen a great light.”","Many people felt sad and lost then, like walking in the dark. Isaiah promised that a great light would come to them.","The night Jesus was born, a bright light shone around the shepherds. It was the Lord’s own shining glory.","When Jesus grew up, He said, “I am the light of the world.” He said whoever follows Him will not walk in darkness. Isaiah was talking about Jesus!","A light shows us the way, and so does Jesus. Tonight, look at one little light and remember Jesus."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout loves to look and discover, and tonight he looks for Christmas lights that point to Jesus. In Scout’s world today: On a Christmas drive, Scout pressed his nose to the window. He spotted twinkly lights and a bright star on a tree. Mom said, “Each one can remind us of Jesus.” Just like Isaiah, Scout learned that light helps us remember Jesus.","feel":"Open and close your fingers like twinkly Christmas lights. Blink, blink, blink! Now hold them still and bright. Can you show me your shining lights?","stones":[["SPOT","The people waited in the dark, then Jesus came like a great light, and they felt wonder."],["NAME","That wide-eyed, sparkly, wow feeling is called wonder. Your mind might think, ‘How beautiful!’ Your body might feel tingly, with big shining eyes."],["FIND","When have you felt wonder looking at beautiful lights?"],["FORK","We can stay in the dark, or follow Jesus’s light. Jesus said those who follow Him will have light."],["CALM","Close your eyes and picture one soft, warm light. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for Jesus, my light.”"]],"remember":"Every Christmas light can remind me of Jesus, the Light of the World!","ask":["“What did the people in darkness see?”","“Who said, “I am the light of the world”?”","“What did Scout see on the Christmas drive?”"],"share":["When has Jesus brought light to a dark time in your life?","Which Christmas tradition helps our family remember Jesus best?","Which grandparent or ancestor had a Christmas tradition that pointed to Jesus? Share it."]},"b12-w4-n6":{"story":["Long before Jesus was born, Isaiah said a special child would come. He would be called the Prince of Peace.","The night Jesus was born, an angel told the shepherds the happy news.","Suddenly, many, many angels were there with him! They were praising Heavenly Father, telling how great and good He is.","The angels said, “Glory to Heavenly Father in the highest!” They said, “On earth peace, good will toward men.” Jesus came to bring peace, a calm, safe feeling deep inside. He came to teach kindness to everyone, too.","Kind words help bring peace. Tonight, lie very still and feel calm inside. Tomorrow, share a kind word, just like the angels shared good news."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow is warm and gentle, and tonight the angels told of peace and kindness for everyone. In Willow’s world today: Willow made a Christmas card with a big red heart for the mail carrier. When he came, she handed it to him and said, “Thank you!” He smiled a great big smile. Just like the angels said, Willow learned that good will, kindness to everyone, brings peace on earth.","feel":"Lift your arms up high, then float them down slowly, soft as a feather. Rest them gently on your blanket. Can you show me your peaceful arms?","stones":[["SPOT","The angels joyfully praised Heavenly Father, because Jesus, the Prince of Peace, was born."],["NAME","That calm, quiet, all-is-well feeling is called peace. Your mind might think, ‘Everything is okay.’ Your body might feel soft and still, with slow breaths."],["FIND","When have you felt calm and peaceful inside?"],["FORK","We can choose grumpy words, or kind words. Jesus, the Prince of Peace, taught us to choose kindness."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly, then breathe out softly. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for peace.”"]],"remember":"Jesus is the Prince of Peace, and He brings peace to my heart!","ask":["“What did the many angels do?”","“What did the angels say would be on earth?”","“Who did Willow make a Christmas card for?”"],"share":["When have you felt the peace Jesus brings?","How can our family share peace and good will this Christmas?","Which grandparent or ancestor was a peacemaker? Share a story about them."]},"b12-w4-n7":{"story":["Long ago, Isaiah told what Heavenly Father said. One day people would say, “This is the Lord; we have waited for Him.” They would say, “He will save us!”","The night Jesus was born, the angels went back into heaven. The shepherds said, “Let us go to Bethlehem and see!”","They hurried and found Mary, Joseph, and the baby lying in a manger. The One they had waited for was here!","The shepherds told everyone about the baby, and all who heard were amazed. Mary kept these things in her heart. The shepherds went back praising Heavenly Father.","Jesus came because Heavenly Father loved the world so much, He gave His Son. Good news is for sharing! Tomorrow, tell someone, “Jesus is born!”"],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau is brave, and tonight the shepherds hurried to find Jesus and then told everyone the good news. In Beau’s world today: At the Christmas program, Beau’s tummy felt fluttery. Then he stood tall and said his line loud and clear: “Jesus is born!” Just like the shepherds, Beau learned that good news about Jesus is for sharing.","feel":"Wiggle your feet fast, like shepherds hurrying to Bethlehem. Now stop and hold your arms out wide. Can you show me your happy, hurrying feet?","stones":[["SPOT","The shepherds hurried to find baby Jesus, and they were so excited to tell everyone."],["NAME","That bubbly, can’t-wait, jumpy feeling is called excitement. Your mind might think, ‘Let’s go see!’ Your body might feel bouncy, with quick feet and a bright smile."],["FIND","When have you been so excited you could hardly wait?"],["FORK","The shepherds could stay in the field, or hurry to see Jesus. They hurried, then shared the good news."],["CALM","Hold your hands together like a gift. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for giving us Jesus.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father gave us Jesus, the best Christmas gift of all!","ask":["“Where did the shepherds find baby Jesus?”","“What did the shepherds do after they saw Him?”","“What did Beau say at the Christmas program?”"],"share":["What has waiting on the Lord taught you?","How can our family share the good news of Jesus this Christmas?","Which grandparent or ancestor shared their love of Jesus with others? Share their story."]},"b2-w1-n1":{"story":["Adam and Eve were the first mom and dad on the earth. Heavenly Father blessed them.","When Adam was 130 years old, a new baby boy came. They named him Seth. Seth could have gone his own way. But he chose to obey the Lord.","Seth grew up and had a baby boy, named Enos. The family began to pray to the Lord. The Lord blessed them.","Adam and Eve had many more sons and daughters. Their children had children, and those children had children too! Adam lived 930 years, and his family grew bigger and bigger.","Every new baby made the family bigger and happier. Your family is a gift from Heavenly Father too. Tonight, give someone in your family a big hug."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow has a warm heart for her family, and tonight is about Adam’s big, growing family. In Willow’s world today: Willow’s aunt brought home a new baby. Willow touched his tiny hand and whispered, “Welcome to our family!” Just like Adam’s family, Willow learned that every new baby makes a family bigger and full of love.","feel":"Hold your arms out wide, like a big family hug. Now pull them in close and squeeze softly. Can you show me your big family hug?","stones":[["SPOT","Adam and Eve’s family grew bigger and bigger, and they felt happy and blessed."],["NAME","That warm, together, snuggled-in feeling is called belonging. Your mind might think, ‘I am part of a family.’ Your body might feel cozy and warm inside."],["FIND","When have you felt happy to be part of your family?"],["FORK","Seth could go his own way, or he could follow the Lord. Seth chose to follow the Lord."],["CALM","Hug your pillow softly. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for my big family.”"]],"remember":"We are all part of Adam’s big family, and Heavenly Father loves every one!","ask":["“What was the name of Adam and Eve’s baby boy?”","“Did Adam’s family stay small or grow big?”","“What did Willow whisper to the new baby?”"],"share":["What does being part of our family mean to you?","How have you seen Heavenly Father bless our family?","Which grandparent or ancestor would you love our children to know? Share a story about them."]},"b2-w1-n2":{"story":["Long ago, a baby named Enoch was born into Adam’s big family. His dad’s name was Jared.","Jared taught Enoch all of Heavenly Father’s ways. Enoch learned the good things Heavenly Father wants us to do.","When Enoch grew up, he had a son named Methuselah. The scriptures say that Enoch walked with Heavenly Father.","That means Enoch stayed close to Him, like holding hands on a walk. Enoch could have walked his own way. Instead, he stayed close for 300 years! Then Heavenly Father took Enoch to be with Him.","You can walk with Heavenly Father too. He is near you all day. Tonight, tell Him about your day, like talking on a walk together."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren loves to think quietly, and walking with Heavenly Father begins with quiet listening. In Wren’s world today: Wren held Dad’s hand on an evening walk. She told him all about her day, and he listened. Just like Enoch, Wren learned that staying close to Heavenly Father feels like a walk with someone who loves you.","feel":"Under your blanket, walk your feet slowly. Step, step, step, close beside Heavenly Father. Can you show me your slow, happy walking feet?","stones":[["SPOT","Enoch stayed close to Heavenly Father every day, and he felt safe and loved."],["NAME","That safe, close, hand-in-hand feeling is called trust. Your mind might think, ‘He is right beside me.’ Your body might feel calm and steady, like slow footsteps."],["FIND","When have you felt safe holding someone’s hand?"],["FORK","Enoch could walk his own way, or walk with Heavenly Father. Enoch chose to walk with Heavenly Father."],["CALM","Take a slow breath in and out. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, I want to walk with You.”"]],"remember":"I can walk with Heavenly Father every day, just like Enoch!","ask":["“Who taught Enoch Heavenly Father’s ways?”","“Who did Enoch walk with?”","“What did Wren tell Dad on their walk?”"],"share":["When have you felt close to Heavenly Father?","What helps you stay close to Him each day?","Which parent, grandparent or ancestor taught you Heavenly Father’s ways? Share a story."]},"b2-w1-n3":{"story":["Adam’s family kept a special book. They called it a book of remembrance, a book to help them remember.","When they prayed, Heavenly Father’s Spirit helped them know what to write. They wrote down the names in their family: moms, dads and children.","They also taught their children to read and write. Their words were clean and true.","Why write it all down? People can forget, but a book remembers. Years later, Enoch said the book helped them know their family, even Adam.","Your family can remember too. Tonight, ask a grown-up to tell you about a grandma or grandpa. Heavenly Father knows and loves them all."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout loves to find out new things, and Adam’s children found wonderful things in their family’s book. In Scout’s world today: Scout found Grandpa’s old family book in a box. It had pictures and names of family from long ago. Scout asked, “Who is this?” Just like Adam’s children, Scout learned that a family book helps us remember the people we love.","feel":"Open your hands like a big book. Now write your name on your hand with one finger. Can you show me how you write?","stones":[["SPOT","Adam’s children learned to read and write, and they felt glad."],["NAME","That bright, I-can-do-it feeling is called excitement. Your mind might think, ‘I am learning something new!’ Your body might feel bouncy, with a big smile."],["FIND","When have you felt excited to learn something new?"],["FORK","Adam’s family could forget, or they could write things down. They chose to write and remember."],["CALM","Close your hands like a closed book. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me remember You always.”"]],"remember":"Our family can remember Heavenly Father and each other, just like Adam’s family!","ask":["“What did Adam’s family write in their special book?”","“What did the children learn to do?”","“What did Scout find in Grandpa’s box?”"],"share":["What do you want our children to always remember about Heavenly Father?","What family stories should we write down together?","Which grandparent or ancestor’s name should our children learn first? Tell them why."]},"b2-w1-n4":{"story":["Enoch was walking among the people. Heavenly Father’s Spirit came down from heaven and stayed with him.","Many people had stopped listening to the Lord. So the Lord said, “Enoch, tell them to repent.” To repent is to turn from wrong and choose what is right.","Enoch bowed down low. He asked, “Why me? I am only a boy. The people do not like me, and I talk slowly.”","The Lord said, “Go and do what I asked. Open your mouth, and I will give you the words.” He told Enoch to say, “Choose today to follow the Lord who made you.”","Enoch felt small, but he trusted the Lord and went. When you feel small, the Lord can help you be brave too."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau is brave, and tonight Enoch learned to be brave even when he felt small. In Beau’s world today: Beau had to say one line at church. His voice felt tiny. He said a little prayer, opened his mouth, and said it! Just like Enoch, Beau learned that the Lord helps us when we feel small.","feel":"Curl up tiny under your blanket. Now stretch up tall and brave! Can you show me your brave, tall stretch?","stones":[["SPOT","Enoch felt small and unsure, but the Lord said, “Go,” and promised to help him."],["NAME","That small, shaky, not-sure feeling is called nervousness. Your mind might think, ‘I am too little.’ Your body might feel wobbly knees and a fluttery tummy."],["FIND","When have you felt too small to do something?"],["FORK","Enoch could say no, or he could trust the Lord and go. Enoch chose to trust and go."],["CALM","Take one big, brave breath. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me be brave like Enoch.”"]],"remember":"When I feel small, the Lord will help me be brave!","ask":["“How did Enoch feel when the Lord called him?”","“What did the Lord promise to give Enoch?”","“What did Beau do when his voice felt tiny?”"],"share":["When has the Lord helped you do something that felt too hard?","When have you felt Heavenly Father give you the right words?","Which grandparent or ancestor did something brave for the Lord? Share their story."]},"b2-w1-n5":{"story":["The Lord told Enoch, “My Spirit is with you. Walk with Me.”","Then the Lord told Enoch to put soft, wet clay on his eyes. “Wash them,” He said, “and you will see.”","That might seem like a funny thing to do. But Enoch did just what the Lord said.","Then Enoch could see wonderful things! He saw the spirits Heavenly Father had made. He saw things other people’s eyes could not see. So people called Enoch a seer, a man the Lord helps to see.","Heavenly Father helps you see good things too. Tonight, think of one good thing He showed you today."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout loves to look and discover, and tonight Enoch saw wonderful things he had never seen before. In Scout’s world today: Scout held Grandpa’s magnifying glass over a flower. He saw tiny, hidden lines he had never noticed! Just like Enoch, Scout learned that Heavenly Father can help us see more than we knew.","feel":"Cover your eyes softly with your hands. Now slowly open them wide! Can you show me your big, wonder-filled eyes?","stones":[["SPOT","Enoch washed his eyes and saw wonderful things, and he felt amazed."],["NAME","That big, wide-eyed, wow feeling is called amazement. Your mind might think, ‘Wow, look at that!’ Your body might feel big open eyes and a tingly, happy face."],["FIND","When have you seen something that made you say, “Wow”?"],["FORK","Enoch could skip the clay, or he could do just what the Lord said. Enoch obeyed, and he could see."],["CALM","Close your eyes gently. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me see the good You show me.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father can help me see what is good and true!","ask":["“What did Enoch put on his eyes?”","“What did Enoch see after he washed his eyes?”","“What did Scout look at with the magnifying glass?”"],"share":["When has Heavenly Father helped you see something more clearly?","How can our family help our children notice Heavenly Father’s blessings?","Which grandparent or ancestor saw the good in everyone? Share a story."]},"b2-w1-n6":{"story":["Enoch stood on the hills and spoke in a loud voice. He told the people their wrong ways had to stop. Many people were upset.","People came out of their tents to hear him. They said, “A wild man has come!”","But nobody hurt Enoch, because he walked with Heavenly Father. A man named Mahijah asked, “Who are you?” Enoch said, “My father taught me Heavenly Father’s ways.”","Then Enoch taught, “Heavenly Father made the heavens and the earth. We know our fathers, even Adam. We wrote them in a book of remembrance.” As he spoke, the people shook.","Enoch could have hidden, but he stood tall and taught. Heavenly Father can help you be brave and tell what is true."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau is brave, and tonight Enoch stood on a hill and taught everyone about Heavenly Father. In Beau’s world today: At family night, Beau stood up and said, “Heavenly Father made the whole world!” His voice was strong and clear. Just like Enoch, Beau learned that we can be brave when we share what is true.","feel":"Sit up tall, like Enoch on the hill. Cup your hands by your mouth. Can you show me your brave teaching voice in a whisper?","stones":[["SPOT","Enoch taught on the hills even when people were upset, and he felt brave."],["NAME","That strong, standing-tall feeling is called courage. Your mind might think, ‘Heavenly Father is with me.’ Your body might feel a tall back and a steady voice."],["FIND","When have you been brave enough to speak up?"],["FORK","Enoch could hide from the people, or he could teach them. Enoch chose to stand tall and teach."],["CALM","Put your hand on your chest and breathe slowly. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me share what is true.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father helps me be brave and share what is true!","ask":["“Where did Enoch stand to teach?”","“Who taught Enoch Heavenly Father’s ways?”","“What did Beau say at family night?”"],"share":["When have you shared something true about Heavenly Father?","What true things do you hope our children will share with courage?","Which grandparent or ancestor taught you Heavenly Father’s ways? Share what they taught."]},"b2-w1-n7":{"story":["Enoch told the people what Heavenly Father had taught Adam long before.","Heavenly Father told Adam, “Turn from wrong. Be baptized in the name of Jesus Christ.” Baptized means going all the way under the water and back up. It is a promise to follow Jesus.","He said, “Teach these things to your children.”","Adam prayed to the Lord. The Spirit of the Lord carried Adam into the water and back up. Then the Holy Ghost, the quiet helper who comforts us, came upon him. A voice from heaven called Adam His son.","Today, prophets teach that children can be baptized when they are eight. Tonight, feel how much Heavenly Father loves you."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow has a warm, gentle heart, and the Holy Ghost is a warm, gentle helper. In Willow’s world today: Willow watched her cousin be baptized. When her cousin came up out of the water, Willow felt warm and peaceful inside. Just like Adam, Willow learned that the Holy Ghost brings a quiet, happy feeling.","feel":"Lie down flat and still, like calm water. Now sit up slowly, like coming up out of the water. Can you show me your slow, peaceful rise?","stones":[["SPOT","Adam was baptized and received the Holy Ghost, and he felt clean and full of peace."],["NAME","That clean, quiet, glowing feeling is called peace. Your mind might think, ‘Heavenly Father loves me.’ Your body might feel calm and warm, like a soft hug inside."],["FIND","When have you felt warm and peaceful inside?"],["FORK","Adam could go his own way, or he could follow the Lord. Adam chose to be baptized and follow Jesus."],["CALM","Rest your hands softly on your heart. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for the Holy Ghost.”"]],"remember":"I can be baptized and follow Jesus, just like Adam!","ask":["“Who was baptized in tonight’s story?”","“Who is the quiet helper who comforts us?”","“How did Willow feel at her cousin’s baptism?”"],"share":["How did you feel on the day you were baptized?","How can our family teach these things freely to our children?","Which grandparent or ancestor’s baptism or faith story can you share with our children?"]},"b2-w2-n1":{"story":["Enoch led the people who loved the Lord. Enoch had great faith, which means he trusted the Lord all the way.","One day, an army came to fight against the Lord’s people.","Enoch spoke the words the Lord gave him. The ground shook, and the mountains moved away! The rivers turned and ran a new way.","The army was afraid, and it went far away. The mountains did not move because Enoch was big and strong. They moved because Enoch trusted the Lord, and the Lord has all power.","You can trust Heavenly Father too, even when things feel big. Tonight, tell Him about one big thing, and trust Him to help."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau is brave, and Enoch was brave because he trusted the Lord. In Beau’s world today: Beau felt scared to get in the pool at swim class. He said a little prayer and held his teacher’s hands. Then he stepped in! Just like Enoch, Beau learned that trusting Heavenly Father helps us be brave.","feel":"Stretch your arms up high like a tall mountain. Now slide them slowly to the side, like a mountain moving. Can you show me your moving mountain?","stones":[["SPOT","Enoch spoke the Lord’s words, and the mountains moved. Enoch felt sure and brave."],["NAME","That strong, steady, sure feeling is called faith. Your mind might think, ‘Heavenly Father can do it.’ Your body might feel tall and strong, with a steady heart."],["FIND","When have you trusted Heavenly Father to help you?"],["FORK","Enoch could be afraid, or he could trust the Lord. Enoch chose to trust and speak."],["CALM","Press your feet down like strong mountains. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me trust You.”"]],"remember":"When I trust Heavenly Father, I can be brave like Enoch!","ask":["“What moved when Enoch spoke?”","“Who did Enoch trust?”","“What did Beau do before he stepped into the pool?”"],"share":["When has faith in Heavenly Father helped you do something hard?","What can our family trust the Lord to help us with this week?","Which grandparent or ancestor had great faith? Share a story about them."]},"b2-w2-n2":{"story":["In Enoch’s day, many people fought and were unkind. But Enoch’s people chose to follow the Lord.","So the Lord came and lived with His people! They lived in righteousness. That is a big word for doing what is right and good.","The Lord’s bright glory was on His people. The nations all around knew how great the Lord was.","The Lord blessed their land. The people grew and did well, even high up on the mountains. The Lord who lived with them was Jesus.","Jesus loves to be near people who choose good. Tonight, think of one good thing you did today. Jesus is near you when you choose good."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau is brave, and it takes courage to choose what is right when others do not. In Beau’s world today: At the playground, some kids laughed when a boy fell down. Beau did not laugh. He helped the boy up and smiled at him. Just like Enoch’s people, Beau learned that choosing what is right takes courage.","feel":"Hold your hands up high and spread your fingers like bright light. Now slowly bring the light down onto your heart. Can you show me your glowing heart?","stones":[["SPOT","Enoch’s people chose what was right, and the Lord came to live with them. They felt happy."],["NAME","That bright, close, happy feeling is called joy. Your mind might think, ‘The Lord is near me.’ Your body might feel light and warm, with a big smile."],["FIND","When have you felt happy because you chose what was right?"],["FORK","They could follow unkind people, or follow the Lord. They chose the Lord, and He lived with them."],["CALM","Hold your hands together near your heart. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me choose the right.”"]],"remember":"When I choose the right, I can feel the Lord near me!","ask":["“Who came to live with Enoch’s people?”","“What does righteousness mean?”","“What did Beau do when the boy fell?”"],"share":["When have you felt the Lord close to you?","How can we make our home a place where the Lord would love to be?","Which grandparent or ancestor chose what was right when it was hard? Share their story."]},"b2-w2-n3":{"story":["The Lord gave Enoch’s people a special name. He called them Zion.","He called them Zion because they were of one heart and one mind. They loved each other, and they all wanted to follow the Lord together.","They did what was right. And there were no poor people among them.","No one in Zion went without food, clothes, or a home. Why? Everyone cared for each other, so everyone had enough. Jesus wants His people to love each other like that.","You can help make a little Zion at home. Tonight, think of one thing you can share or one way to help tomorrow."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow has a warm, caring heart, and in Zion everyone cared for each other. In Willow’s world today: Willow’s friend forgot her snack at preschool. Willow broke her crackers in half and shared them. Both girls smiled. Just like the people of Zion, Willow learned that sharing helps everyone have enough.","feel":"Put both hands on your heart. Now open your arms wide, like sharing a big hug with everyone. Can you show me your one-big-heart hug?","stones":[["SPOT","The people of Zion loved each other and cared for everyone. They felt close and happy together."],["NAME","That warm, together, belonging feeling is called love. Your mind might think, ‘We all belong together.’ Your body might feel soft and warm in your chest."],["FIND","When have you felt close to your family or friends?"],["FORK","They could keep things just for themselves, or care for each other. They chose to care, so no one was poor."],["CALM","Hug your pillow gently. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me love and share like Zion.”"]],"remember":"Zion means one heart and one mind, loving and caring together!","ask":["“What name did the Lord give His people?”","“Were any people in Zion poor?”","“What did Willow share with her friend?”"],"share":["When have you seen people work together with one heart?","What can our family do this week to be more like Zion?","Which grandparent or ancestor loved to share with others? Tell about them."]},"b2-w2-n4":{"story":["Enoch kept teaching the Lord’s people what was right. Then Enoch built a city. It was called the City of Holiness, and it was also called Zion.","Its people followed the Lord, so it was holy, very special to Heavenly Father.","Enoch talked with the Lord. He said, “Surely Zion will be safe forever.” The Lord said He had blessed Zion.","Then the Lord showed Enoch something wonderful. After a long time, the whole city of Zion was taken up into heaven! The Lord said, “This is My home forever.”","Heavenly Father wants you to come home to Him too. Tonight, think of how happy heaven will be. He loves you and wants you with Him forever."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout loves to discover new things, and tonight we discover a city that went up to heaven! In Scout’s world today: Scout built a city with blocks. He made a little home for every toy and a park where all of them could play. Just like Enoch, Scout learned that a happy city is one where everyone is cared for.","feel":"Lie flat and reach your arms up. Now lift them slowly higher and higher, like Zion going up to heaven. Can you show me your gentle rising arms?","stones":[["SPOT","Enoch built Zion, and the Lord took it up to heaven. Enoch felt sure Zion was safe."],["NAME","That calm, cozy, protected feeling is called peace. Your mind might think, ‘I am safe with Heavenly Father.’ Your body might feel soft and still, like a warm blanket."],["FIND","When have you felt safe and peaceful?"],["FORK","The people could live any way they wanted, or follow the Lord. They followed Him, and He took them to heaven."],["CALM","Pull your blanket up to your chin. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me be ready to come home to You.”"]],"remember":"Zion was a holy city, and the Lord took it home to heaven!","ask":["“What did Enoch build?”","“Where did the Lord take the city of Zion?”","“What did Scout make for every toy?”"],"share":["What do you picture when you think of heaven?","How can our home feel more like the City of Holiness?","Which grandparent or ancestor do you hope to see again in heaven? Share a memory of them."]},"b2-w2-n5":{"story":["The Lord let Enoch see all the people on the earth. Many of them were being very unkind.","Then Enoch saw something surprising. Heavenly Father looked at those people, and He cried.","Enoch asked, “How can You cry? You are holy and so great. You are kind forever.”","The Lord said, “These are your brothers and sisters. I made them, and I let them choose. I asked them to love one another and to choose Me, their Father. But they do not love.” He cried because their choices would bring them sadness.","Heavenly Father loves you that much too. Tonight, think of someone you can be kind to tomorrow."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow has a tender heart, and tonight we learn how tender Heavenly Father’s heart is. In Willow’s world today: At the park, two boys grabbed for the same swing, and one boy cried. Willow felt sad inside. She showed them how to take turns. Just like Enoch, Willow learned that Heavenly Father feels sad when His children are unkind.","feel":"Touch your cheek gently, like wiping away a tear. Now cross your arms softly over your heart. Can you show me your caring heart?","stones":[["SPOT","Heavenly Father saw His children being unkind, and He cried. He felt very sad."],["NAME","That heavy, tender, hurting feeling is called sadness. Your mind might think, ‘I wish they were kind.’ Your body might feel a lump in your throat and wet eyes."],["FIND","When have you felt sad because someone was unkind?"],["FORK","The people could love one another, or be unkind. Many chose to be unkind, but we can choose love."],["CALM","Take a slow, gentle breath. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me be kind to Your children.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father loves all His children and cares how we treat each other!","ask":["“What did Heavenly Father do when He saw the unkind people?”","“What did the Lord ask His children to do?”","“How did Willow help the two boys?”"],"share":["When have you felt how much Heavenly Father loves you?","How can our family show Heavenly Father that we love one another?","Which grandparent or ancestor had a tender heart for others? Share a story."]},"b2-w2-n6":{"story":["The Lord showed Enoch things that would happen a long, long time later.","Enoch saw the day Jesus would come to live on the earth! Enoch’s heart was full of joy.","The Lord said, “I am Messiah, the Rock of Heaven.” Messiah is a name for Jesus, the Savior Heavenly Father promised to send. Whoever climbs up on this strong Rock will never fall.","Enoch asked to see more. He saw Jesus on the cross, giving His life for us. Even the earth was sad. Then Enoch saw good people rise up beside Jesus, wearing shining crowns.","Jesus is your strong Rock too. Tonight, say, “Jesus is my Rock,” and feel how safe His love is."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren loves to wonder about big things, and tonight Enoch wondered about Jesus. In Wren’s world today: Wren saw a picture of baby Jesus at church. She wondered what it was like when He came. She asked her teacher, who smiled and told her. Just like Enoch, Wren learned that when we ask about Jesus, we learn wonderful things.","feel":"Climb your fingers up your blanket, like climbing a big, strong rock. Now hold on tight with both hands. Can you show me how you hold on to Jesus?","stones":[["SPOT","Enoch saw that Jesus would come to the earth, and he felt full of joy."],["NAME","That big, glad, bubbling feeling is called joy. Your mind might think, ‘Jesus is coming!’ Your body might feel light, with a big smile and a happy heart."],["FIND","When have you felt happy thinking about Jesus?"],["FORK","Enoch could keep wondering by himself, or ask the Lord. Enoch asked, “Show me,” and the Lord showed him."],["CALM","Fold your hands on your heart. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for sending Jesus.”"]],"remember":"Enoch saw Jesus coming long ago, and Jesus is my Savior too!","ask":["“Who did Enoch see coming to the earth?”","“What does Messiah mean?”","“Who did Wren ask about Jesus?”"],"share":["When did you first know that Jesus is the Messiah, our Savior?","How has Jesus been a strong rock for our family?","Which grandparent or ancestor loved to talk about Jesus? Share what they said."]},"b2-w2-n7":{"story":["The Lord made Enoch a promise about the time before Jesus comes again.","He said, “I will send goodness from heaven and truth out of the earth.” That truth will tell about Jesus. Prophets teach that this truth includes the Book of Mormon, found in a hill.","Good people from everywhere will gather to a holy city called Zion.","Then Enoch and his city will come to meet them! The Lord said they will hug and kiss each other. And the earth will rest and be peaceful for a thousand years.","Tonight, give someone you love a big hug, like Zion’s happy hug someday."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout loves to search, and the Lord promised that truth would come out of the earth. In Scout’s world today: Scout dug in the garden with his sister and found a smooth, shiny stone. He washed it off to see it shine. Just like the promise to Enoch, Scout learned that wonderful things can come out of the earth.","feel":"Pretend to dig in the dirt with your hands. Now lift up a treasure and hold it close to your heart. Can you show me your treasure hug?","stones":[["SPOT","The Lord promised that Zion will come again, and Enoch’s city will come back. That brings hope."],["NAME","That bright, looking-forward feeling is called hope. Your mind might think, ‘Something wonderful is coming!’ Your body might feel light and tingly, with a smile."],["FIND","When have you looked forward to something wonderful?"],["FORK","People could stay far from the Lord, or gather to Him. Good people from everywhere will gather to Zion."],["CALM","Hug yourself softly. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me be ready for Zion.”"]],"remember":"Zion will come again someday, with hugs and joy!","ask":["“Where did the Lord say truth would come from?”","“What will the people do when Enoch’s city comes back?”","“What did Scout find in the garden?”"],"share":["What truth has helped you know that Jesus is real?","How can our family get ready for Jesus to come again?","Which grandparent or ancestor are you excited to hug again someday? Share why."]},"b2-w3-n1":{"story":["Noah loved the Lord and listened to Him. His three sons listened too. But many people had stopped listening to the Lord.","So the Lord sent Noah to teach them. Noah said, “Believe in Jesus Christ, repent, and be baptized in His name.” To repent is to say sorry, stop the wrong, and try again.","But the people would not listen. Noah’s heart hurt for them. Still, Noah kept teaching, again and again.","The Lord was pleased with Noah. Noah walked with Heavenly Father. He stayed close to Him, like a child holding a parent’s hand.","When you make a mistake, you can repent too. Jesus will help you try again. Tonight, hold someone’s hand and say, “I will walk with Heavenly Father.”"],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau is brave, and Noah was brave to keep teaching what was right, even when people would not listen. In Beau’s world today: At the park, some kids were throwing sand. Beau said, “Let’s stop. Sand can hurt eyes.” The kids laughed, but Beau kept playing kindly. Just like Noah, Beau learned that we can keep doing right, even when others do not.","feel":"Lie straight and tall like a brave prophet. Cup your hands by your mouth, like Noah calling to the people. Can you show me your brave, kind face?","stones":[["SPOT","Noah taught the people, but they would not listen. Noah’s heart hurt, and still he kept going."],["NAME","That strong, steady, keep-going feeling is called courage. Your mind might think, ‘I will do what is right.’ Your body might feel tall and strong, with a steady heart."],["FIND","When have you kept doing right when others did not?"],["FORK","Noah could stop teaching, or he could keep teaching. Noah kept teaching the Lord’s words, again and again."],["CALM","Put your hand over your heart. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me walk with You every day.”"]],"remember":"I can walk with Heavenly Father, and Jesus helps me try again!","ask":["“What did Noah teach the people?”","“Who helps us when we repent?”","“What did Beau say to the kids at the park?”"],"share":["When has repenting brought you peace?","How do you keep choosing the right when others around you do not?","Which grandparent or ancestor stood up for what was right? Share their story."]},"b2-w3-n2":{"story":["The Lord told Noah to build a very big boat called an ark. A great flood was coming. Water would cover all the land.","The Lord told Noah just how to make it. It was longer than a football field! It had three floors, a window, and a door in its side.","Noah covered the wood with pitch, inside and out. The thick, sticky pitch would keep the water out.","Why did the ark need to be so big? It had to hold Noah’s family, two of every animal, and food for all.","It was a very big job, but Noah did all that the Lord asked. Tonight, think of a job for tomorrow. Do it one step at a time."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren loves to think carefully, and Noah carefully built the ark just the way the Lord said. In Wren’s world today: Wren helped Dad make cookies. She listened to each step: first the butter, then the sugar, then the flour. Just like Noah, Wren learned that listening carefully and doing each step helps good things happen.","feel":"Pretend to hold a hammer. Tap, tap, tap on your blanket to build the ark. Now spread your arms out wide. Can you show me how big the ark was?","stones":[["SPOT","The Lord gave Noah a very big job, and Noah kept working until it was done."],["NAME","That ready, I-can-do-it feeling is called determination. Your mind might think, ‘I can do this, one step at a time.’ Your body might feel busy hands and a strong back."],["FIND","When have you worked hard to finish a big job?"],["FORK","Noah could say the job was too big, or he could start building. Noah built the ark, just as the Lord said."],["CALM","Wiggle your fingers, then let them rest. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me listen and obey.”"]],"remember":"When I listen and obey, Heavenly Father helps me do big things!","ask":["“What did the Lord ask Noah to build?”","“Why did the ark need to be so big?”","“What did Wren make with Dad?”"],"share":["When has obeying the Lord, one step at a time, blessed your life?","What big job has Heavenly Father helped you finish?","Which grandparent or ancestor worked hard to obey the Lord? Share their story."]},"b2-w3-n3":{"story":["When the ark was ready, the Lord spoke to Noah. He said, “Come into the ark with all your family.” He saw that Noah chose what was right.","Then the animals came to Noah, two by two. There were big animals, birds, and tiny crawling things.","Noah went in with his wife, his three sons, and their three wives. That was eight people in all!","Why did they come two by two? Each pair was a mother and a father. After the flood, they could have babies. Every kind of animal would live on.","Heavenly Father cared for every animal, from the biggest to the smallest. He cares for you too. Tonight, think of your favorite animal and thank Him for it."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow has a warm, gentle heart, and tonight Noah’s family kept every animal safe in the ark. In Willow’s world today: Willow helped Grandma feed the chickens. She scattered the corn softly so the little chicks could get some too. Just like Noah, Willow learned that Heavenly Father wants us to take care of His animals.","feel":"Hold up two fingers on each hand. Walk them slowly across your blanket, two by two, into your pillow ark. Can you show me your quiet animal walk?","stones":[["SPOT","The animals came two by two, and Noah’s family brought them in to keep them safe."],["NAME","That soft, gentle, take-care feeling is called caring. Your mind might think, ‘I will keep you safe.’ Your body might feel soft hands and a warm heart."],["FIND","When have you gently taken care of a pet or animal?"],["FORK","Noah could leave the animals outside, or he could bring them in. Noah brought them all into the ark."],["CALM","Hug your favorite stuffed animal softly. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for all the animals.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father cares for every creature, big and small, and me too!","ask":["“How did the animals come into the ark?”","“How many people went into the ark?”","“What did Willow feed with Grandma?”"],"share":["What has caring for an animal taught you about Heavenly Father’s love?","How have you seen Heavenly Father care for small things in your life?","Which grandparent or ancestor loved animals or the land? Share a story about them."]},"b2-w3-n4":{"story":["Noah, his family, and the animals were all inside the ark. Then the Lord Himself shut the door.","It rained for forty days and forty nights. Water came up from deep in the earth and down from the sky. The ark floated up, high above the ground.","The people who would not listen were lost in the flood. That was very sad. But inside the ark, Noah’s family and the animals were safe.","The scriptures say Heavenly Father “remembered Noah” and every animal in the ark. He had not forgotten them. He sent a wind, and the water began to go down.","Heavenly Father remembers you too, even when thunder rumbles and rain falls. Tonight, listen to the quiet, and feel that He is near."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau is brave, and it takes courage to trust Heavenly Father while the rain goes on and on. In Beau’s world today: One night, thunder rumbled outside Beau’s window. Beau pulled his blanket close and said a little prayer. Soon he felt calm. Just like Noah, Beau learned that Heavenly Father remembers us, even in a storm.","feel":"Pat your blanket softly with your fingers, like rain on the roof. Pitter, patter. Now rock gently side to side, like the floating ark. Can you show me your gentle rocking?","stones":[["SPOT","The rain fell for a long time, but Noah’s family was safe in Heavenly Father’s care."],["NAME","That cozy, calm, held-close feeling is called feeling safe. Your mind might think, ‘Heavenly Father is taking care of me.’ Your body might feel warm, loose, and snuggly."],["FIND","When have you felt safe and cozy while a storm was outside?"],["FORK","Noah’s family could stay outside, or they could trust the Lord and go into the ark. They went in, and they were safe."],["CALM","Snuggle under your blanket and breathe slowly. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for remembering me.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father remembers me, even when storms come!","ask":["“Who shut the door of the ark?”","“How many days and nights did it rain?”","“What did Beau do when the thunder rumbled?”"],"share":["When did you feel Heavenly Father remember you during a hard time?","What helps our family feel safe in life’s storms?","Which grandparent or ancestor kept their faith through a hard time? Share their story."]},"b2-w3-n5":{"story":["At last the ark rested on the mountains of Ararat. Noah waited a long time. Then he sent out a big black bird called a raven.","Next he sent out a dove. She found no place to rest her feet, so she flew back. Noah reached out his hand and pulled her inside.","Noah waited seven more days and sent her out again. That evening she came back with a fresh olive leaf in her beak!","Leaves grow on trees, so Noah knew the water had gone down! Seven days later, the dove flew off and did not come back.","Waiting was hard, but Noah kept watching. Heavenly Father can help you wait too. Tonight, think of something good you are waiting for."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout loves to look for clues, and Noah sent out the birds to find out if the land was dry. In Scout’s world today: Scout planted a bean seed in a little cup. Every morning he looked for a sprout. One day, a tiny green leaf popped up! Just like Noah, Scout learned that when we wait and keep looking, good things can come.","feel":"Hook your thumbs together and flap your hands like a dove’s wings. Fly them up high, then bring them back to your chest. Can you show me your dove coming home?","stones":[["SPOT","Noah waited a long time, then felt glad and hopeful when he saw the olive leaf."],["NAME","That bright, looking-forward feeling is called hope. Your mind might think, ‘Something good is coming!’ Your body might feel light, with a smile and lifted shoulders."],["FIND","When have you waited and waited for something good?"],["FORK","Noah could give up waiting, or he could keep watching. Noah kept watching, and the dove brought good news."],["CALM","Fold your hands like a little bird resting. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me wait with hope.”"]],"remember":"When I wait with faith, Heavenly Father is with me!","ask":["“What did the dove bring back to Noah?”","“What did the leaf tell Noah?”","“What popped up in Scout’s cup?”"],"share":["When has waiting on the Lord brought you a blessing?","What signs of hope has Heavenly Father shown you?","Which grandparent or ancestor waited patiently for a blessing? Share their story."]},"b2-w3-n6":{"story":["At last everyone came out of the ark onto dry land. Noah built an altar, a special place to thank and worship the Lord.","There Noah gave gifts to the Lord. Gifts like these helped people remember that Jesus would come to save them.","Then Heavenly Father made a covenant with Noah, a promise He would always keep. Never again would a flood cover the whole earth.","He set a rainbow in the clouds as a sign of His promise. He said when He sees the rainbow, He will remember. Every rainbow says, “I keep My promises.”","Heavenly Father always keeps His promises. Tonight, think of one promise you can keep tomorrow, like sharing or helping."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow’s warm heart loves keeping promises, and Heavenly Father made a loving promise with Noah. In Willow’s world today: Willow promised her friend she would share her crayons at preschool. The next day, Willow remembered and shared all her rainbow colors. Just like Heavenly Father with Noah, Willow learned that keeping a promise shows love.","feel":"Lift your arms up high and sweep them over your head in a big rainbow. Now hold your hands together over your heart. Can you show me your rainbow?","stones":[["SPOT","Noah’s family came out onto dry land, and Heavenly Father gave them a loving promise."],["NAME","That warm, steady, I-can-count-on-You feeling is called trust. Your mind might think, ‘Heavenly Father keeps His promises.’ Your body might feel calm and settled, with a soft smile."],["FIND","When has someone kept a promise to you?"],["FORK","Noah could forget to thank the Lord, or he could build an altar. Noah built an altar to worship Him."],["CALM","Trace a rainbow in the air with one finger. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for keeping Your promises.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father always keeps His promises, and the rainbow helps me remember!","ask":["“What did Heavenly Father put in the clouds?”","“What did Heavenly Father promise Noah?”","“What did Willow share with her friend?”"],"share":["What promise to Heavenly Father means the most to you, and why?","When have you seen Heavenly Father keep a promise to you?","Which grandparent or ancestor kept their promises to the Lord faithfully? Share their story."]},"b2-w3-n7":{"story":["After the flood, Noah’s family grew into many, many people. They all spoke the same words.","They baked bricks hard in the fire. They said, “Let us build a city and a tower up to heaven.”","They also said, “Let us make a name for ourselves.” They wanted everyone to think they were big and great. They wanted to do it their own way.","So the Lord mixed up their words, and they could not understand each other! They stopped building. The Lord spread them out over all the earth. The city was called Babel.","We do not get to heaven by being the biggest or the best. Jesus is the way back to Heavenly Father. Tomorrow, try being kind instead of being the biggest."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren likes to stop and think, and tonight’s story helps us think about doing things the Lord’s way. In Wren’s world today: Wren built a tall block tower and shouted, “Look at me! I’m the best!” Her friend looked sad. So Wren said, “Let’s build one together.” Just like the people at Babel, Wren learned that being kind matters more than being the biggest.","feel":"Stack your fists, one on top of the other, like a tall tower. Now let your hands float softly down to your heart. Can you show me your gentle hands?","stones":[["SPOT","The people wanted to be big and important, so the Lord stopped their tower."],["NAME","That quiet, gentle, I-need-Heavenly-Father feeling is called being humble. Your mind might think, ‘I will follow Jesus.’ Your body might feel soft shoulders and quiet hands."],["FIND","When have you let someone else have a turn to shine?"],["FORK","The people could listen to the Lord, or they could try to be great on their own. They chose their own way."],["CALM","Rest your hands softly in your lap. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me follow Jesus, not show off.”"]],"remember":"Jesus is the way to Heavenly Father, not a tall tower!","ask":["“What did the people want to build?”","“What did the Lord do to their words?”","“What did Wren do when her friend looked sad?”"],"share":["When has following Jesus helped you set aside pride?","How can our family do things the Lord’s way instead of our own?","Which grandparent or ancestor served others quietly and humbly? Share their story."]},"b2-w4-n1":{"story":["Long ago, Abraham lived in the land of the Chaldeans. He already loved doing what was right.","Abraham saw that Heavenly Father’s blessings bring more happiness, more peace, and more rest. Peace is a calm, quiet feeling inside, like still water on a pond.","So Abraham wanted to learn more. He wanted to keep Heavenly Father’s commandments and grow even better at doing right.","Abraham wanted to be “a prince of peace,” someone who brings peace to others. Heavenly Father blessed him. Abraham became a High Priest, to serve and bless people.","You can grow a little each day too. Tonight, think of one kind thing to do tomorrow. Then notice the happy, peaceful feeling it brings."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren loves to wonder and learn, just like Abraham, who wanted to know more about Heavenly Father. In Wren’s world today: Wren helped set the table, and it felt so good. Wren thought, ‘What else can I do?’ So she helped fold the towels too. Just like Abraham, Wren learned that doing right brings happy, peaceful feelings.","feel":"Put your feet together under the blanket, like standing on a path. Now take tiny walking steps with your feet. Can you show me how you follow?","stones":[["SPOT","Abraham wanted more of Heavenly Father’s happiness and peace, so he chose to grow."],["NAME","That reaching, want-to-grow feeling is called eagerness. Your mind might think, ‘I want to do more good!’ Your body might feel bouncy, with a big smile."],["FIND","When have you wanted to learn or do something good?"],["FORK","Abraham could stay just as he was, or he could keep growing. Abraham chose to grow closer to Heavenly Father."],["CALM","Fold your hands softly. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me follow You more each day.”"]],"remember":"I can follow Heavenly Father a little more each day!","ask":["“What did Abraham want more of?”","“What did Abraham want to be?”","“What did Wren help fold?”"],"share":["When has doing what is right brought you peace?","What is one way you want to grow closer to Heavenly Father?","Which grandparent or ancestor kept trying to follow Heavenly Father? Share their story."]},"b2-w4-n2":{"story":["Many people in Abraham’s land prayed to statues they had made. These statues could not hear or help anyone.","Some men who chose very wrong things tried to hurt Abraham. They tied him up.","Abraham lifted up his voice and prayed to the Lord. The Lord heard him! An angel stood by Abraham and untied him.","Then the Lord said, “Abraham, Abraham, My name is Jehovah. I have heard you, and I have come to save you.” Jehovah is Jesus, before He was born on earth.","Heavenly Father and Jesus hear your prayers too. Tonight, fold your arms and pray to Heavenly Father. Tell Him what you need, and feel safe in His care."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau is brave and has faith, just like Abraham, who prayed to the Lord when he needed help. In Beau’s world today: A loud storm woke Beau in the night. Beau felt scared, so he folded his arms and prayed. Soon Mom came in and held him close. Just like Abraham, Beau learned that Heavenly Father hears us when we pray.","feel":"Cup your hands by your mouth, like Abraham calling to the Lord. Now wrap your arms around yourself, snug and safe. Can you show me your safe, cozy hug?","stones":[["SPOT","Abraham was in danger and prayed, and then he felt safe and thankful."],["NAME","That calm, held-close feeling is called feeling safe. Your mind might think, ‘The Lord is with me.’ Your body might feel loose and warm, with slow breaths."],["FIND","When have you felt safe after feeling scared?"],["FORK","Abraham could give up, or he could pray to the Lord. Abraham chose to pray."],["CALM","Hold your blanket close. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for hearing my prayers.”"]],"remember":"Jesus heard Abraham’s prayer, and He hears mine too!","ask":["“What did Abraham do when he needed help?”","“Who stood by Abraham and set him free?”","“What did Beau do during the storm?”"],"share":["When has the Lord answered a prayer for help in your life?","How do you know that Jesus hears your prayers?","Which grandparent or ancestor prayed for help in a hard time? Share their story."]},"b2-w4-n3":{"story":["One day the Lord told Abraham, “Leave your country and your father’s house. Go to a land that I will show you.”","Abraham did not know what the new land was like. But he trusted the Lord. His wife, Sarah, and his brother’s son, Lot, went with him.","They took all their things and went a long, long way. There were no cars or trains then.","Abraham and Lot prayed, and the Lord came to Abraham. He promised to bless all the families of the earth through Abraham. At last they came to Canaan.","Going somewhere new can feel a little unsure. But the Lord goes with you, just like He went with Abraham. Tonight, say, “The Lord is with me.”"],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout loves new places and discoveries, just like Abraham, who walked to a land he had never seen. In Scout’s world today: Scout’s family moved to a new house. Scout felt a little unsure, but he held Dad’s hand and explored each new room. Just like Abraham, Scout learned that the Lord is with us in new places.","feel":"Make your fingers walk across your blanket, step, step, step, like Abraham on his long trip. Can you show me your walking fingers?","stones":[["SPOT","Abraham left his home for a new land, and he trusted the Lord."],["NAME","That steady, holding-hands feeling is called trust. Your mind might think, ‘The Lord knows the way.’ Your body might feel calm in your tummy, with steady steps."],["FIND","When have you gone somewhere new and trusted someone?"],["FORK","Abraham could stay home, or he could follow the Lord. Abraham packed up and followed."],["CALM","Hold your own hand gently. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, please lead me like You led Abraham.”"]],"remember":"The Lord leads me, just like He led Abraham!","ask":["“Where did the Lord tell Abraham to go?”","“Who went with Abraham on the long trip?”","“What did Scout do in his new house?”"],"share":["When did you follow the Lord without knowing the whole way?","How has the Lord blessed our family?","Which grandparent or ancestor moved to a new place? Share their story."]},"b2-w4-n4":{"story":["Abraham and Lot had many sheep, cows, and tents. There was not enough grass and water for all their animals in one place.","So the men who cared for Abraham’s animals and Lot’s animals began to quarrel. They argued in angry voices.","Abraham was Lot’s uncle. He said to Lot, “Please, let’s not quarrel. We are family.”","Then Abraham said, “Look at all the land! You choose first. If you go left, I will go right.” Lot chose the green Jordan valley, with lots of water. Abraham stayed in Canaan.","Abraham let Lot choose first, and the quarreling stopped. Tomorrow, you can let a friend go first too. That is how a peacemaker helps."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow has a warm, kind heart, like Abraham, who cared more about peace than getting the best. In Willow’s world today: Willow and her friend both wanted the red cup. Willow said, “You pick first.” Her friend smiled, and they both felt happy. Just like Abraham, Willow learned that letting others choose first brings peace.","feel":"Point one hand left and one hand right. Now open both hands wide, like saying, “You choose!” Can you show me your kind, sharing hands?","stones":[["SPOT","The helpers were angry and quarreling, then Abraham and Lot felt peace."],["NAME","That soft, quiet-inside feeling is called peace. Your mind might think, ‘We do not need to fight.’ Your body might feel loose and soft, with slow, easy breaths."],["FIND","When have you let someone else go first?"],["FORK","Abraham could take the best land, or let Lot choose first. Abraham let Lot choose."],["CALM","Let your shoulders drop slowly. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me be a peacemaker.”"]],"remember":"When I let others choose first, I help bring peace!","ask":["“Why did the helpers quarrel?”","“Who got to choose the land first?”","“What did Willow say about the red cup?”"],"share":["When has giving up your own way brought peace to our family?","Who in your life is a good peacemaker?","Which grandparent or ancestor was kind and generous to others? Share a story about them."]},"b2-w4-n5":{"story":["Abraham had just brought Lot safely home. Then Abraham met Melchizedek, the king of Salem. Melchizedek was a priest who served Heavenly Father.","Melchizedek brought out bread and wine to share. Sharing food was a kind way to welcome someone.","Then Melchizedek blessed Abraham. He said Abraham was blessed by the Most High, who owns heaven and earth.","Then Abraham gave Melchizedek a tithe, one part out of every ten. Everything we have comes from Heavenly Father. A tithe gives a little back to Him.","Children can pay tithing too, like one coin out of ten. Tonight, think of a good gift Heavenly Father gave you, and thank Him."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren loves to think about big ideas, like why Abraham gave part of all he had back to the Lord. In Wren’s world today: Wren got ten shiny coins for her birthday. She counted them carefully. Then she put one coin in a tithing envelope. Just like Abraham, Wren learned that giving to Heavenly Father feels happy and good.","feel":"Hold up all ten fingers. Now wiggle just one finger, like the one part we give to the Lord. Can you show me your giving finger?","stones":[["SPOT","Melchizedek blessed Abraham, and Abraham felt thankful and wanted to give."],["NAME","That happy, open-hands feeling is called generosity. Your mind might think, ‘I want to give too!’ Your body might feel warm in your chest, with open hands."],["FIND","When have you given something away and felt happy?"],["FORK","Abraham could keep everything, or give a tithe to the Lord. Abraham gave a tithe of all."],["CALM","Open your hands on your blanket. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for everything You give me.”"]],"remember":"Everything is a gift from Heavenly Father, and I can give back!","ask":["“What did Melchizedek bring out to share?”","“What did Abraham give to Melchizedek?”","“How many coins did Wren put in the envelope?”"],"share":["What blessings have come to you from paying tithing?","When has a priesthood blessing helped you or our family?","Which grandparent or ancestor was faithful in giving to the Lord? Share their story."]},"b2-w4-n6":{"story":["The Lord let Abraham see and hear Him in a vision. He said, “Do not be afraid, Abraham. I am your shield.”","A shield is what a soldier holds up to stay safe. So the Lord meant, “I will keep you safe.”","But Abraham said, “I have no children.” He and Sarah had no baby, and they were getting old.","So the Lord took Abraham outside. He said, “Look up at the sky. Count the stars, if you can. Your family will be like that!” And Abraham believed the Lord.","The scriptures teach that all who follow Jesus become part of Abraham’s family too. Tonight, look for a star or picture one. The Lord keeps His promises."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout loves to look closely and wonder, just like Abraham looking up at all the stars. In Scout’s world today: On a camping trip, Scout lay on a blanket and tried to count the stars. He counted to ten, then laughed. There were too many! Just like Abraham, Scout learned that the Lord’s promises are bigger than we can count.","feel":"Lie back and reach both arms up high. Touch the stars with your fingertips, one, two, three. Can you show me how you count the stars?","stones":[["SPOT","Abraham wondered how he could have a family, then he believed the Lord."],["NAME","That sure, hopeful feeling is called faith. Your mind might think, ‘The Lord keeps His promises.’ Your body might feel calm and strong, with deep, slow breaths."],["FIND","When have you believed something good would happen?"],["FORK","Abraham could stop hoping, or believe the Lord. Abraham believed the Lord’s promise."],["CALM","Look up and blink slowly, like twinkling stars. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, I believe Your promises.”"]],"remember":"The Lord keeps His promises, and I can believe Him!","ask":["“What did the Lord tell Abraham to count?”","“What does a shield do?”","“What happened when Scout tried to count the stars?”"],"share":["When has the Lord kept a promise to you?","What helps you keep believing when you have to wait?","Which grandparent or ancestor waited faithfully for a blessing? Share their story."]},"b2-w4-n7":{"story":["Abraham used to be named Abram. When he was ninety-nine, the Lord came to him. He said, “Walk before Me, and be perfect.”","The Lord made a covenant with him. A covenant is a two-way promise. Abram would follow the Lord, and the Lord would bless him.","Then the Lord gave Abram a new name, Abraham. It meant he would be father to many big families, called nations.","The Lord changed Sarai’s name to Sarah. He said she would be a mother of nations too. And He promised them a baby boy named Isaac!","Prophets teach this: when you are baptized, you make a covenant. You take the name of Jesus. Tonight, say your own name. Heavenly Father knows it and loves you."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow has a warm heart for family, and tonight the Lord gave Abraham and Sarah a forever family promise. In Willow’s world today: Willow asked Mom, “Why did you give me my name?” Mom hugged her and said, “We chose it with love, just for you.” Just like Abraham and Sarah, Willow learned that Heavenly Father knows and loves each of us by name.","feel":"Hug your arms like you are holding a tiny baby. Now rock gently side to side. Can you show me how you hold a baby?","stones":[["SPOT","The Lord gave Abraham and Sarah new names and promised a baby. Abraham felt joy."],["NAME","That bright, bubbling-over feeling is called joy. Your mind might think, ‘Heavenly Father keeps His promises!’ Your body might feel light and happy, like you want to laugh."],["FIND","When have you felt joy about happy news?"],["FORK","Abraham could forget the Lord, or keep His covenant. Abraham chose to walk with the Lord."],["CALM","Put your hand on your heart. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for making promises with me.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father keeps His covenants, and I can keep mine!","ask":["“What new name did Abram get?”","“What baby did the Lord promise Abraham and Sarah?”","“What did Willow ask Mom?”"],"share":["What covenants have you made, and how have they blessed you?","What does taking the name of Jesus mean to you?","Which grandparent or ancestor kept their covenants? Share their story."]},"b1-w1-n1":{"story":["Moses took care of sheep near a big mountain.","There Moses saw a bush with fire in it. But the bush did not burn up! Moses said, “I will go and see this great sight.”","The Lord called from the bush, “Moses, Moses.” Moses said, “Here am I.” The Lord said, “Take off your shoes. This ground is holy.”","The ground was holy because the Lord was there. Moses asked what name to tell the people. The Lord said, “I AM.” Many years later, Jesus said those same words, “I am.” The Lord who talked to Moses was Jesus!","Moses stopped to look and listen. Tonight, be still like Moses and look at one thing Jesus made. Jesus knows your name too."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout loves to look closer, just like Moses did when he walked over to see the glowing bush. In Scout’s world today: Scout saw a shiny bug on a leaf. Instead of running past, he knelt down and looked very quietly. Just like Moses, Scout learned that when we stop and look, we can find wonderful things Heavenly Father made.","feel":"Hold your hands up like a little bush. Wiggle your fingers like soft, warm light. Now hold very still, like Moses listening. Can you show me your quiet, listening face?","stones":[["SPOT","Moses saw something new and amazing, and he felt full of wonder."],["NAME","That wide-eyed, want-to-know feeling is called curiosity. Your mind might think, ‘What is that? I want to see!’ Your body might feel tingly, with big open eyes."],["FIND","When have you wanted to look closer at something new?"],["FORK","Moses could walk away, or he could go and see. Moses took the road of looking and listening."],["CALM","Take one slow breath. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me listen to Jesus.”"]],"remember":"Jesus is the Lord who talked to Moses, and He knows your name too!","ask":["“What did Moses see on the bush?”","“Who was the Lord talking to Moses?”","“What did Scout find on the leaf?”"],"share":["When did you first feel that Jesus knew you?","How can our family make our home a holy place?","Which grandparent or ancestor loved to learn about Jesus? Share a story about them."]},"b1-w1-n2":{"story":["Long ago, Heavenly Father’s people lived in Egypt. The king made them work very hard, and they were not free.","The Lord was going to set His people free. He told Moses that every family must get ready for a special night.","Each family chose one little lamb, a baby sheep. It had to be perfect, with nothing wrong at all.","The Lord kept every family safe that night. Many years later, John the Baptist saw Jesus coming. John said, “Look! The Lamb of God!” That perfect little lamb was a picture of Jesus. Jesus is perfect, and He came to save us.","Jesus cares for you like a gentle shepherd holds a lamb. Tonight, hold your blanket softly and feel safe in His love."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow has a soft, warm heart, and tonight’s story is about a gentle little lamb. In Willow’s world today: At a farm, Willow held a tiny lamb. She used soft hands and a quiet voice so the lamb felt safe. Just like the families who chose their lambs with care, Willow learned that gentle love helps others feel safe.","feel":"Curl up small, like a sleepy little lamb. Now hug yourself very softly. Can you show me your gentle, cozy lamb hug?","stones":[["SPOT","The families chose a perfect lamb, and they felt safe and cared for."],["NAME","That soft, snuggly, safe feeling is called peace. Your mind might think, ‘I am cared for.’ Your body might feel warm and calm, like a heavy blanket."],["FIND","When have you felt safe and cared for?"],["FORK","The families could ignore the Lord, or do what He said. They chose to listen and get ready."],["CALM","Rest your hand on your heart. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for Jesus.”"]],"remember":"The perfect little lamb helps us remember Jesus, Heavenly Father’s perfect Lamb!","ask":["“What did each family choose?”","“Who did John the Baptist call the Lamb?”","“How did Willow keep the tiny lamb safe?”"],"share":["What helps you feel that Jesus came to save you?","How does knowing Jesus is perfect and gentle help you each day?","Which grandparent or ancestor taught you about Jesus? Share what you remember about them."]},"b1-w1-n3":{"story":["Moses led Heavenly Father’s people through a wide, dry land. There were no fields to grow food. Soon the people were hungry and worried.","The Lord heard them. He told Moses, “I will rain bread from heaven for you.”","In the morning, small round flakes lay on the ground like frost. The people asked each other, “What is it?” So they called it manna.","Moses said, “This is the bread the Lord has given you.” It was white and tasted sweet, like honey crackers. Many years later, Jesus said, “I am the bread of life.” Bread fed their bodies every day, and Jesus feeds our hearts.","Tonight, thank Heavenly Father for one good gift, like your food or your family."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau is brave and trusts Heavenly Father, even when worried, just like the people learned to trust the Lord for bread. In Beau’s world today: Beau felt worried on the first day of preschool. He said a little prayer and walked in bravely. Just like the hungry people in the wilderness, Beau learned that Heavenly Father gives us what we need.","feel":"Wiggle your fingers down like tiny flakes falling from the sky. Now scoop them up with both hands. Can you show me how you gather the bread?","stones":[["SPOT","The people were hungry and worried, then they felt thankful for the bread."],["NAME","That warm, full, happy-inside feeling is called thankfulness. Your mind might think, ‘Heavenly Father takes care of me.’ Your body might feel warm in your chest, with a big smile."],["FIND","When have you felt thankful for something you needed?"],["FORK","The people could keep worrying, or they could trust the Lord. They gathered the bread He sent."],["CALM","Rub your tummy in slow circles. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for my food.”"]],"remember":"Jesus is the bread of life, and He gives us what we need!","ask":["“What was on the ground in the morning?”","“What did Jesus say He is?”","“What did Beau do when he felt worried?”"],"share":["When has Heavenly Father given you just what you needed?","How does Jesus feed your spirit, like bread feeds your body?","Which grandparent or ancestor trusted Heavenly Father in a hard time? Share their story."]},"b1-w1-n4":{"story":["Heavenly Father’s people walked a long, hard way. They got grumpy and spoke unkindly about the Lord and Moses.","Then snakes came, and people who were bitten got very sick. They said, “We were wrong. Please pray for us.” So Moses prayed.","The Lord said, “Make a snake and put it on a pole. Everyone who looks at it will live.” Moses made it of shiny brass.","Everyone who looked at it got well! Many years later, Jesus said He would be lifted up, like that snake. Jesus was lifted up on the cross, and then He rose again. He can heal us all.","Looking to Jesus means trusting Him. When you feel hurt or sad, look to Him. Tonight, say, “I trust Jesus.”"],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren loves to wonder why, and tonight has a big wonder: how could just looking help? In Wren’s world today: Wren fell and scraped her knee. Her mom said, “Look at me.” Wren looked up, felt calmer, and let her mom help. Just like the people who looked at the pole, Wren learned that looking to someone who loves us brings help.","feel":"Lift one arm up high, like a tall pole. Now look up at your hand with big, trusting eyes. Can you show me how you look to Jesus?","stones":[["SPOT","The people said sorry, looked at the pole as the Lord said, and felt hope."],["NAME","That looking-up, it-will-be-okay feeling is called hope. Your mind might think, ‘Help is coming.’ Your body might feel lighter, with soft, easy shoulders."],["FIND","When have you felt hope that things would be okay?"],["FORK","The people could look away, or they could look as the Lord said. They looked, and they got well."],["CALM","Look up and take a slow breath. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me look to Jesus.”"]],"remember":"When we look to Jesus, He helps us and heals us!","ask":["“What did Moses put on the tall pole?”","“What happened when the people looked?”","“Who helped Wren after she fell?”"],"share":["When has looking to Jesus helped you in a hard time?","What helps you remember to look to Jesus each day?","Which grandparent or ancestor found hope in Jesus? Share their story."]},"b1-w1-n5":{"story":["Long ago, there were no streetlights or flashlights. At night, the paths were very dark.","So people carried a little clay lamp with oil and a tiny flame. It lit just the next few steps.","A man who loved the Lord wrote a song to Him. He sang, “Your word is a lamp for my feet. It is a light for my path.”","The Lord’s words are in the scriptures, the books Heavenly Father gave us. Like a little lamp, they show us the next good step. Jesus said the scriptures tell about Him. He also said, “I am the light of the world.”","If the dark feels big, remember Jesus is your light. Tonight, ask Heavenly Father to help you take the next step."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau is brave in the dark, and tonight’s story is about a little light that helps us keep going. In Beau’s world today: Beau walked down the dark hall for a drink of water. He held a small night-light and took one step at a time. Just like the man with his little lamp, Beau learned that a little light is enough for the next step.","feel":"Cup your hands together like a little clay lamp. Hold it near your feet. Now wiggle your toes, one tiny step at a time. Can you show me your brave little steps?","stones":[["SPOT","The song writer felt safe, because the Lord’s words lit his way like a lamp."],["NAME","That steady, I-can-do-it feeling is called courage. Your mind might think, ‘I can take one more step.’ Your body might feel strong and tall, with steady feet."],["FIND","When have you felt brave in the dark?"],["FORK","We could stay stuck in the dark, or follow the light. The song writer chose to follow the Lord’s word."],["CALM","Hold your hands like a little lamp. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me follow Jesus.”"]],"remember":"The scriptures are a lamp for my feet, and they lead me to Jesus!","ask":["“What did people carry on dark paths?”","“What did the song say is like a lamp?”","“What helped Beau walk down the dark hall?”"],"share":["Which scripture has been a light for you?","How can our family read the scriptures together more often?","Which grandparent or ancestor loved the scriptures? Share a story about them."]},"b1-w1-n6":{"story":["Isaiah told the people what Heavenly Father said. Their king and his people were afraid of enemies coming.","Isaiah lived long, long before Jesus was born. But Heavenly Father showed Isaiah that a special baby boy would come.","Isaiah said the baby would be called Immanuel. That name means the Lord is with us. The book of Matthew tells us this baby is Jesus!","Isaiah also said, “Unto us a child is born.” He would be called Wonderful and the Prince of Peace. These are names for Jesus too! His words gave the scared people hope.","Jesus is with you too, even in the dark. Tonight, whisper His names: Wonderful, Prince of Peace. Let His peace fill your heart."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren loves to wonder about big ideas, like a prophet knowing about Jesus long before He was born. In Wren’s world today: Wren saw a big wrapped gift and wondered what was inside. Mom smiled and said, “Soon!” Wren waited and waited. Just like the people who waited for baby Jesus, Wren learned that Heavenly Father’s promises are worth waiting for.","feel":"Rock your arms gently, like holding a tiny baby. Now give your baby a soft, sleepy smile. Can you show me how gently you can rock?","stones":[["SPOT","Isaiah shared happy news that Jesus would come, news that brings joy."],["NAME","That bright, bubbly, happy feeling is called joy. Your mind might think, ‘Jesus came for me!’ Your body might feel light and sparkly, with a big smile."],["FIND","When have you felt joy about happy news?"],["FORK","The people could forget Isaiah’s words, or remember them. Faithful people remembered and waited for Jesus."],["CALM","Hug yourself softly. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that Jesus is with me.”"]],"remember":"Immanuel means the Lord is with us, and Jesus is with me!","ask":["“What name did Isaiah say the baby would have?”","“Who was Isaiah talking about?”","“What did Wren wait for?”"],"share":["When have you felt that Jesus is with you?","Which name of Jesus means the most to you, and why?","Which grandparent or ancestor had a favorite name for Jesus? Share their story."]},"b1-w1-n7":{"story":["The Lord told Jonah to go warn the people of Nineveh. They were doing bad things.","But Jonah ran away on a ship. The Lord sent a great wind, and the sailors were afraid.","Jonah knew the storm came because he ran away. He said, “Throw me into the sea.” They did, and the sea grew still.","The Lord sent a big fish to swallow Jonah. Inside the fish, Jonah prayed to the Lord. After three days, the fish put Jonah on dry land! Jesus later said Jonah’s three days were like His own. Jesus rose again on the third day!","Heavenly Father hears you too, wherever you are. Even in a dark place, He is with you. Tonight, say a little prayer to Him."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow has a warm heart, and tonight Heavenly Father showed tender care to Jonah, even after Jonah ran away. In Willow’s world today: Willow’s little brother ran by and knocked over her blocks. He came back and said, “Sorry.” Willow gave him a hug. Just like Heavenly Father with Jonah, Willow learned that love gives second chances.","feel":"Rock side to side, like a ship on soft waves. Now lie still and fold your arms, like Jonah praying. Can you show me your quiet praying arms?","stones":[["SPOT","Jonah prayed in a hard place, and the Lord heard him. Jonah felt comforted."],["NAME","That safe, held, someone-hears-me feeling is called comfort. Your mind might think, ‘Heavenly Father hears me.’ Your body might feel soft and still, like a gentle hug."],["FIND","When have you felt comforted after you prayed?"],["FORK","Jonah could keep running, or he could pray. Jonah prayed, and the Lord helped him."],["CALM","Fold your arms and breathe slowly. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for always hearing me.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father always hears my prayers, and Jesus rose again!","ask":["“Where was Jonah when he prayed?”","“What did the big fish do after three days?”","“What did Willow give her little brother?”"],"share":["When has Heavenly Father heard your prayer in a hard place?","How has Jesus given you a second chance?","Which grandparent or ancestor prayed through a hard time? Share their story."]},"b1-w2-n1":{"story":["Moses was taken up onto a very high mountain. There he saw Heavenly Father face to face and talked with Him.","Heavenly Father’s glory, His bright, holy light, was on Moses. That light made Moses able to stand near Him.","Heavenly Father said, “Moses, you are My son.” He said it again and again.","Heavenly Father had a special work for Moses. He said Moses was like His Son, Jesus. Then He showed Moses some of the many things He had made.","You are Heavenly Father’s child too. Tonight, put your hand on your heart and say, “I am Heavenly Father’s child.” He knows you, and He loves you."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow is warm and gentle, and tonight Heavenly Father spoke tender words to Moses. In Willow’s world today: At preschool, a new girl sat all alone. Willow sat by her and said, “I’m glad you’re here. You can be my friend.” The girl smiled. Just like Heavenly Father with Moses, Willow learned that loving words help others feel safe and special.","feel":"Lie very still. Pretend a soft, bright light is shining all around you, warm like a blanket. Smile a peaceful smile. Can you show me how you glow?","stones":[["SPOT","Heavenly Father called Moses “My son,” and Moses felt loved and special."],["NAME","That warm, safe, held-close feeling is called being loved. Your mind might think, ‘I belong to Heavenly Father.’ Your body might feel warm and soft, like a hug."],["FIND","When have you felt warm and loved by someone in your family?"],["FORK","Moses could feel small and alone, or he could believe Heavenly Father’s words. Moses believed he was Heavenly Father’s son."],["CALM","Hug yourself gently. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that I am Your child.”"]],"remember":"I am Heavenly Father’s child, and He loves me!","ask":["“Where did Moses go?”","“What did Heavenly Father call Moses?”","“Who did Willow sit by at preschool?”"],"share":["When have you felt that you are Heavenly Father’s child?","What helps you feel Heavenly Father’s light in our home?","Which grandparent or ancestor taught you that Heavenly Father loves you? Share a story about them."]},"b1-w2-n2":{"story":["Then Satan came to Moses. Satan wants people to choose wrong. He said, “Moses, worship me.” He wanted Moses to love and obey him most.","Moses said, “Who are you? I am a son of Heavenly Father!” Then Moses said, “Go away, Satan.”","But Satan did not go. He shouted very loudly, and Moses began to feel afraid.","So Moses prayed to Heavenly Father, and Heavenly Father made him strong. Moses spoke in the name of Jesus, Heavenly Father’s Only Son. He said, “Go away, Satan!” And Satan left!","When a wrong choice calls to you, you can say no, like Moses. Tonight, fold your arms and ask Heavenly Father to make you strong."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau is brave, and tonight Moses was brave and said no to Satan. In Beau’s world today: At the park, a big kid told Beau to push a little girl off the swing. Beau stood tall and said, “No. I will be kind.” Just like Moses, Beau learned that we can say no to wrong choices and stay close to Heavenly Father.","feel":"Put your hands together like Moses praying. Now sit up tall and strong, like a big mountain. Take a deep breath. Can you show me your brave, strong face?","stones":[["SPOT","Satan tried to trick Moses, and Moses felt brave and strong after he prayed."],["NAME","That tall, strong, steady feeling is called courage. Your mind might think, ‘I can choose the right.’ Your body might feel strong in your arms and tall in your back."],["FIND","When have you said no to something wrong?"],["FORK","Moses could do what Satan said, or he could pray and say no. Moses prayed and chose Heavenly Father."],["CALM","Fold your arms gently. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, please help me be brave like Moses.”"]],"remember":"I am a child of Heavenly Father, and He makes me strong!","ask":["“What did Moses say he was?”","“What did Moses do to get strong?”","“What did Beau say at the park?”"],"share":["When has prayer given you strength to choose the right?","How do you remember whose child you are?","Which grandparent or ancestor stood strong for what was right? Share their story."]},"b1-w2-n3":{"story":["Satan was gone. Moses looked up to heaven. The Holy Ghost, a quiet helper who teaches what is true, filled him.","Heavenly Father said, “Moses, I have chosen you. I am with you.”","Then Moses saw the whole earth, every tiny bit of it. Heavenly Father’s Spirit helped him see it all.","Then Moses saw all the people on the earth. There were as many as the grains of sand by the sea. Moses saw every single one! Prophets teach that each one is Heavenly Father’s child.","Heavenly Father knows you too! Tonight, think of the people you love. Say each name, and remember He knows every one."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout loves to look and see, and tonight Moses saw the whole earth and every person on it. In Scout’s world today: At the store, Scout watched all the people walking by: tall people, small people, babies, and grandmas! Just like Moses, Scout learned that every person is one of Heavenly Father’s children.","feel":"Make your hands into a little telescope. Look slowly around your room, like Moses looking at the whole earth. Can you show me your big, looking eyes?","stones":[["SPOT","Moses saw every person on the earth, and he felt amazed."],["NAME","That wow, eyes-open, so-big feeling is called wonder. Your mind might think, ‘There are so many people!’ Your body might feel wide-eyed, with a big, slow breath."],["FIND","When have you seen a whole lot of people at once?"],["FORK","Moses could look away, or he could keep looking and learning. Moses kept looking at all Heavenly Father showed him."],["CALM","Take a slow breath in and out. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for knowing every person.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father knows every person on the earth, and He knows me!","ask":["“What did Moses see?”","“How many people did Moses see?”","“Who did Scout see at the store?”"],"share":["When have you felt that Heavenly Father knows you personally?","How can we see others as Heavenly Father’s children?","Which grandparent or ancestor loved people of every kind? Share a story about them."]},"b1-w2-n4":{"story":["Moses had seen so many things. He prayed, “Why did You make these things? And how did You make them?”","Heavenly Father said He made them for His own good reason. He made them through His Son, Jesus.","Then Heavenly Father said, “Worlds without number have I created.” That is more worlds than anyone can count!","No person could count them all. But Heavenly Father counts every one. He said, “They are Mine, and I know them.”","If Heavenly Father knows every world, He knows you too! Tonight, think of the big night sky. You are His, and He loves you."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren loves to wonder about big things, and tonight Moses asked how and why Heavenly Father made everything. In Wren’s world today: At the library, Wren found a book with pictures of planets. “Who made all these?” she asked her mom. Her mom said Heavenly Father made them through Jesus. Just like Moses, Wren learned that it is good to ask big questions.","feel":"Hold your arms out wide. Slowly turn your hands in circles, like many worlds turning. Then hug them close. Can you show me your wondering face?","stones":[["SPOT","Moses asked Heavenly Father big questions, and he felt full of wonder."],["NAME","That quiet, thinking, big-question feeling is called wondering. Your mind might think, ‘How did Heavenly Father make it all?’ Your body might feel still and calm, with eyes looking up."],["FIND","When have you asked a big question about the world?"],["FORK","Moses could keep his questions inside, or he could ask Heavenly Father. Moses prayed and asked."],["CALM","Rest your hands on your tummy. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for making the world through Jesus.”"]],"remember":"Jesus made worlds without number, and Heavenly Father knows them all!","ask":["“What did Moses ask Heavenly Father?”","“Who made the worlds for Heavenly Father?”","“What did Wren find at the library?”"],"share":["What big question have you asked Heavenly Father in prayer?","Where do you feel close to Heavenly Father in His creations?","Which grandparent or ancestor loved the stars or the outdoors? Share a story about them."]},"b1-w2-n5":{"story":["Moses asked Heavenly Father for one more thing. He said, “Please tell me about this earth and the people on it.”","Heavenly Father said, “The heavens are many. People cannot count them. But I count them, for they are Mine.”","He said there is no end to His works. Then He told Moses something very important.","He said, “This is my work and my glory.” His work and His greatest joy is to help us live forever. Because of Jesus, everyone will live again someday. He wants us to have eternal life, a happy life with Him forever.","You are Heavenly Father’s work and His joy! He wants you to come home to Him. Tonight, hug someone you love, because they are His joy too."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow has a warm, caring heart, and tonight we learn that Heavenly Father’s work is caring for us. In Willow’s world today: Willow held her baby brother’s hands while he learned to walk. When he took three steps all by himself, Willow clapped and cheered! Just like Moses learned about Heavenly Father’s joy, Willow learned that helping someone grow brings great joy.","feel":"Open your arms wide, like a big welcome-home hug. Now wrap them around yourself. Can you show me how Heavenly Father welcomes you home?","stones":[["SPOT","Heavenly Father told Moses His work is bringing us home, and Moses learned how loved we are."],["NAME","That warm, cared-for, safe feeling is called being treasured. Your mind might think, ‘Heavenly Father wants me home.’ Your body might feel warm and cozy in your chest."],["FIND","When have you felt that someone really cared about you?"],["FORK","Moses could think people are too small to matter, or he could believe Heavenly Father’s words. Moses believed."],["CALM","Hold your hands over your heart. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for loving me.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father’s work and joy is bringing me home to Him!","ask":["“What is Heavenly Father’s work?”","“Because of Jesus, what will happen to everyone?”","“What did Willow help her baby brother do?”"],"share":["How have you seen Heavenly Father working in your life?","What does eternal life mean to you?","Which grandparent or ancestor are you excited to see again someday? Share a story about them."]},"b1-w2-n6":{"story":["The Lord talked with Abraham face to face, like one friend talks with another. He told Abraham about the things He had made.","Abraham saw many great stars. One was nearest to Heavenly Father’s home. The Lord said its name was Kolob.","The Lord put His hand on Abraham’s eyes. Abraham saw so many things the Lord had made. He could not see the end of them!","It was nighttime when the Lord made Abraham a promise. Abraham’s family would grow and grow. It would be like the stars and the sand, too many to count!","Heavenly Father made the stars, and He made you. Tonight, think of the stars above your home. He is watching over you."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout loves to look and discover, and tonight Abraham saw more stars than he could count. In Scout’s world today: Scout and his dad went outside after dark. Scout tried to count the stars: one, two, three, ten, twenty, so many! Just like Abraham, Scout learned that Heavenly Father’s creations are too many to count.","feel":"Point up to the ceiling. Pretend to count the stars, tapping one, two, three. Now spread your fingers wide like twinkling stars. Can you show me your twinkle?","stones":[["SPOT","The Lord showed Abraham so many stars, and Abraham felt amazed."],["NAME","That big, sparkly, oh-wow feeling is called amazement. Your mind might think, ‘Heavenly Father made so much!’ Your body might feel still, with your mouth open wide."],["FIND","When have you looked up at the night sky and said, “Wow”?"],["FORK","Abraham could stop listening, or he could keep talking with the Lord. Abraham kept listening, like a good friend."],["CALM","Blink slowly, like a sleepy star. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for the stars.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father made the stars, and He made me too!","ask":["“What did the Lord show Abraham?”","“What is the name of the great star?”","“What did Scout try to count?”"],"share":["When has the night sky helped you feel close to Heavenly Father?","What promises has Heavenly Father made to our family?","Which grandparent or ancestor helped our family grow strong in the gospel? Share their story."]},"b1-w2-n7":{"story":["Before we were born, we lived with Heavenly Father as spirits. Your spirit is the real you inside, the part that thinks and loves.","The Lord showed Abraham those spirits. He said, “Abraham, you are one of them. You were chosen before you were born.”","Heavenly Father’s plan was to make an earth for us. Here we would learn to do what He asks.","The Lord asked, “Whom shall I send?” Jesus said, “Here am I, send me.” Another spirit said it too. The Lord said, “I will send the first.” That was Jesus, who came to save us.","Prophets teach that you chose Heavenly Father’s plan too. Tonight, think of one way you can help tomorrow. Then say, like Jesus, “Here am I!”"],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau is brave, and tonight Jesus bravely said, “Here am I, send me.” In Beau’s world today: Beau’s mom asked, “Who can help carry the groceries?” Beau jumped up and said, “I will!” Just like Jesus, Beau learned that saying “I will help” is a brave and loving choice.","feel":"Raise your hand high, nice and tall. Say softly, “Here am I!” Then bring your hand down to your heart. Can you show me your ready-to-help hand?","stones":[["SPOT","Heavenly Father needed someone to send, and Jesus was ready and willing to help."],["NAME","That ready, eager, I-can-help feeling is called being willing. Your mind might think, ‘I will go. I will help.’ Your body might feel bouncy and ready to jump up."],["FIND","When have you said “I will help” to someone?"],["FORK","Jesus could stay quiet, or He could say, “Send me.” Jesus chose to come and help us all."],["CALM","Put your hand on your heart. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for sending Jesus.”"]],"remember":"Jesus said, “Here am I, send me,” because He loves us!","ask":["“Where did we live before we were born?”","“What did Jesus say to Heavenly Father?”","“How did Beau help his mom?”"],"share":["What do you know about the life we lived before we were born?","How does knowing Jesus said “Send me” help you trust Him?","Which grandparent or ancestor said yes when the Lord called? Share their story."]},"b1-w3-n1":{"story":["In the very beginning, the earth had no shape. It was empty and dark, with deep water all around.","Heavenly Father had a plan. He told Moses that He made the earth through His Son. So Jesus made the earth for Heavenly Father, just as He planned.","Then, in the dark, Heavenly Father said, “Let there be light.” And there was light!","Heavenly Father saw that the light was good. He called the light Day. He called the dark Night. Evening came, then morning. That was the very first day!","Every morning, the dark sky turns gray, then pink, then bright. That light is part of Heavenly Father’s good plan. Tonight, look at a soft light and remember that morning will come."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren loves to wonder about big things, like how the whole world began. In Wren’s world today: Wren woke up early and watched the window. The dark sky turned gray, then pink, then bright. Wren whispered, “Morning came!” Just like the very first day, Wren learned that Heavenly Father sends light after the dark.","feel":"Squeeze your eyes shut, dark like the night. Now open them wide and spread your fingers like bright light! Can you show me your sunshine hands?","stones":[["SPOT","Light came into the dark, and Heavenly Father saw that it was good."],["NAME","That big, quiet, wow feeling is called wonder. Your mind might think, ‘How did He do that?’ Your body might feel very still, with wide-open eyes."],["FIND","When have you looked at something and felt, “Wow”?"],["FORK","Jesus could do it His own way, or follow Heavenly Father’s plan. Jesus followed the plan, and light came."],["CALM","Put your hands over your heart. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for light and for Jesus.”"]],"remember":"Jesus made the world for Heavenly Father, and He made light to fill the dark!","ask":["“What did Heavenly Father say in the dark?”","“Who made the earth for Heavenly Father?”","“What did Wren see out the window?”"],"share":["When has Heavenly Father brought light into a dark time in your life?","How do you know Heavenly Father has a plan for you?","Which grandparent or ancestor brought light to our family? Share a story about them."]},"b1-w3-n2":{"story":["On the second day, Heavenly Father made the sky. It sat between the water below and the water above. He called the sky Heaven.","On the third day, He gathered the water into one place. Then dry land showed up! He called the dry land Earth. He called the water Seas.","Next, green grass grew. Plants and fruit trees of every kind grew too. Heavenly Father saw that it was good.","Each plant and tree had its own seeds inside. An apple seed grows an apple tree, never a pear tree. Each kind makes more of its own kind.","Jesus made all this for Heavenly Father, following His plan. Tonight, curl up small like a tiny seed. Rest now, and grow a little more."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout loves to explore and find out how things grow, just like the plants on the third day. In Scout’s world today: Scout found a fluffy dandelion and blew. Puff! Tiny seeds floated away. His dad said each seed could grow a new dandelion. Just like in the Creation story, Scout learned that every seed grows its own kind.","feel":"Curl up small like a seed in the ground. Now slowly stretch your arms up high like a tall tree. Can you show me your tree branches?","stones":[["SPOT","Dry land and green plants came, and the world was full of new, good things."],["NAME","That bright, want-to-find-out feeling is called curiosity. Your mind might think, ‘What will this seed grow into?’ Your body might feel bouncy, with busy, looking eyes."],["FIND","When have you wanted to find out how something grows?"],["FORK","Jesus could make just one plant, or many kinds. Following Heavenly Father’s plan, He made every kind, each with seeds."],["CALM","Wiggle your toes like little roots. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for the land and the trees.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father made the sky, the land, the seas, and every tree, and it was good!","ask":["“What did Heavenly Father call the dry land?”","“What did the plants and trees have inside?”","“What did Scout blow on?”"],"share":["What part of Heavenly Father’s creation helps you feel close to Him?","What small seed of faith has grown big in your life?","Which grandparent or ancestor loved to garden or grow things? Share a story about them."]},"b1-w3-n3":{"story":["On the fourth day, Heavenly Father put lights in the sky.","He made a big light to rule the day. That is the sun. He made a smaller light for the night. That is the moon. He made the stars too!","These lights show when day ends and night begins. They help us count days and years. They mark seasons too, like summer and snowy winter.","Why put lights in the night sky? To give light on the earth, even in the dark! Heavenly Father saw that it was good. That was the fourth day.","So the night is never all dark. Following the plan, Jesus gave the night the moon and stars. Tonight, peek at the moon or a star, then snuggle down brave."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau is brave and ready for each new season, and tonight Heavenly Father made lights to mark the days and seasons. In Beau’s world today: Beau woke up and saw the first snow! He put on his boots and stepped out bravely into the cold. “It’s winter!” he said. Just like in the Creation story, Beau learned that Heavenly Father gave us days and seasons.","feel":"Make a big round sun with your arms. Now make a small moon with your hands. Twinkle your fingers like stars. Can you show me your twinkling stars?","stones":[["SPOT","Heavenly Father filled the sky with the sun, moon, and stars, so even night has light."],["NAME","That safe, cozy, I’m-okay feeling is called peace. Your mind might think, ‘The dark has light in it.’ Your body might feel loose and warm, with slow, soft breaths."],["FIND","When have you felt safe in the dark?"],["FORK","The night could be all dark, or have lights in it. Following Heavenly Father’s plan, Jesus gave the night the moon and stars."],["CALM","Pull your blanket up snug. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for the moon and stars that shine at night.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father made the moon and stars, so the night has light too!","ask":["“What big light did Heavenly Father make for the day?”","“What lights did He make for the night?”","“What did Beau see when he woke up?”"],"share":["When has Heavenly Father helped you feel safe in a hard or dark time?","How do the sun, moon, and stars remind you of Heavenly Father?","Which grandparent or ancestor helped you feel brave as a child? Share a story about them."]},"b1-w3-n4":{"story":["On the fifth day, Heavenly Father filled the water with living things. Fish swam in the seas. Great big whales swam in the deep!","Then He made birds. They flew high across the open sky.","Heavenly Father saw that it was good. Then He blessed the fish and birds with a happy gift.","He told them, “Have many babies! Fill the seas, and fill the earth with birds.” That is why there are so many fish and birds today! That was the fifth day.","Jesus filled the seas and sky for Heavenly Father, just as He planned. Tonight, flap your arms like soft wings. Then fold them in and rest."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout loves to look and discover, and the fish and birds were brand-new things to find. In Scout’s world today: At his grandma’s house, Scout sprinkled food for her goldfish. The fish swam up to eat. Then a little bird sang outside the window! Just like in the Creation story, Scout learned that Heavenly Father filled the water and sky with life.","feel":"Swish your hands together like a fish’s tail. Now flap your arms like a bird flying high. Can you show me your quiet, gliding wings?","stones":[["SPOT","The seas and sky filled with fish and birds, and the world was full of happy life."],["NAME","That bright, giggly, look-at-that feeling is called delight. Your mind might think, ‘A bird! A fish! So many!’ Your body might feel bouncy, with a big smile."],["FIND","When have you smiled at a bird or a fish?"],["FORK","Jesus could leave the seas and sky empty, or fill them with life. Following Heavenly Father’s plan, He filled them."],["CALM","Fold your wing-arms in close. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for every fish and bird.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father blessed the fish and birds, and He blesses me too!","ask":["“What great big animals swam in the deep?”","“What did Heavenly Father tell the fish and birds?”","“What did Scout feed at his grandma’s house?”"],"share":["How has Heavenly Father blessed our family with good things this week?","What part of nature reminds you of Heavenly Father’s care?","Which grandparent or ancestor loved birds or animals? Share a story about them."]},"b1-w3-n5":{"story":["On the sixth day, Heavenly Father made animals for the land. Cows, crawling bugs, and wild animals came. Each kind made more of its own kind.","Then Heavenly Father spoke to Jesus. “Let us make man in our image,” He said. That means people would be made to look like Them.","So a man and a woman were made, Adam and Eve. Heavenly Father blessed them. He put them in charge of the animals.","He gave them plants and fruit for food. Then He looked at everything He had made. It was very good! That was the sixth day.","You were made in Heavenly Father’s image too. You are His child! Tonight, look at your hands and say, “Heavenly Father made me.”"],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow is warm and gentle with every living thing, and tonight Heavenly Father made animals and people. In Willow’s world today: Willow met her new baby cousin. She touched his tiny hand very softly. Willow thought, “He is Heavenly Father’s child, just like me.” Just like Adam and Eve, Willow learned that everyone is made in Heavenly Father’s image.","feel":"Hold up your hands and look at them. Wiggle each finger. Now give yourself a soft hug. Can you show me your gentle, loving hug?","stones":[["SPOT","Heavenly Father made Adam and Eve like Him, and everything He made was very good."],["NAME","That warm, belong-to-Him feeling is called love. Your mind might think, ‘I am His child.’ Your body might feel warm in your chest, like a cozy hug."],["FIND","When have you felt loved just for being you?"],["FORK","Jesus could stop with the animals, or follow the whole plan. Jesus followed it, and Adam and Eve were made."],["CALM","Hug yourself softly. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for making me like You.”"]],"remember":"I am made in Heavenly Father’s image, and I am His child!","ask":["“What did Heavenly Father make on the sixth day?”","“Who were the first man and woman?”","“Who did Willow meet?”"],"share":["When have you felt that you are truly a child of Heavenly Father?","How can we help our children see Heavenly Father’s image in everyone?","Which grandparent or ancestor showed Heavenly Father’s kindness to others? Share a story about them."]},"b1-w3-n6":{"story":["Six days had passed. The heavens and the earth were finished. Everything in Heavenly Father’s plan was made.","On the seventh day, His work was done. So Heavenly Father rested from all His work.","Then He blessed the seventh day and made it holy. Holy means set apart, very special to Heavenly Father.","Each week we have a holy day of rest too, called the Sabbath. We go to church and think about Heavenly Father and Jesus.","Tonight, rest just like Heavenly Father did on the seventh day. Lie very still. Let your arms and legs grow soft, and feel the quiet peace inside."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren loves quiet times to wonder and think, and tonight’s story is about a day for being still. In Wren’s world today: On Sunday, Wren put her toys away and sat with her mom. They looked at a picture of Jesus and sang a soft song. Just like on the seventh day, Wren learned that resting can feel peaceful and happy.","feel":"Stretch your arms way up high, like all the busy days. Now let them float down, slow and still. Can you show me your quiet, resting body?","stones":[["SPOT","The work was done, so Heavenly Father rested and made the seventh day holy."],["NAME","That soft, still, quiet feeling is called peace. Your mind might think, ‘I can slow down now.’ Your body might feel heavy and calm, like a warm blanket."],["FIND","When have you felt calm and quiet inside?"],["FORK","Heavenly Father could keep working, or rest. He rested, and He blessed the seventh day."],["CALM","Let your arms and legs go soft. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me make Sunday a special day.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father blessed the seventh day, a special day to rest!","ask":["“What did Heavenly Father do on the seventh day?”","“What does holy mean?”","“What did Wren do with her mom on Sunday?”"],"share":["What helps you feel close to Heavenly Father on the Sabbath?","How could our family make Sunday more restful and special?","How did a grandparent or ancestor keep the Sabbath day? Share a story about them."]},"b1-w3-n7":{"story":["Heavenly Father planted a garden in a place called Eden. Beautiful trees grew there, with fruit good to eat. He put Adam there to take care of it.","Heavenly Father said, “It is not good for the man to be alone.” He brought the animals to Adam. Whatever Adam called each one, that was its name!","But none of the animals was the right helper for Adam. So Heavenly Father made Eve and brought her to Adam.","Now Adam was not alone anymore. Prophets teach that Heavenly Father married Adam and Eve. They became one family.","Family is part of Heavenly Father’s plan, and so is your family. Tonight, give someone in your family a hug. You are never alone either."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau is learning to be brave and helpful, like Adam, who took care of the garden. In Beau’s world today: At the park, Beau saw a wrapper on the grass. He felt a little shy, but he picked it up and put it in the trash. Just like Adam in Eden, Beau learned that Heavenly Father wants us to take care of the earth.","feel":"Pretend you are in the garden. Point to a lion, a bunny, and a bird, and give each one a name. Can you show me how you name them?","stones":[["SPOT","Heavenly Father brought Eve to Adam, and they were not alone anymore. They were a family."],["NAME","That warm, together, we-belong feeling is called belonging. Your mind might think, ‘I have a family who loves me.’ Your body might feel snug and warm, like a big hug."],["FIND","When have you felt glad to be with your family?"],["FORK","Adam and Eve could live apart, or together as a family. They took the road Heavenly Father planned: family."],["CALM","Wrap your arms around your pillow like a hug. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, please bless my family tonight.”"]],"remember":"Family is part of Heavenly Father’s plan, and my family is too!","ask":["“Who gave the animals their names?”","“Who did Heavenly Father bring to Adam?”","“What did Beau pick up at the park?”"],"share":["How has being part of a family helped you feel Heavenly Father’s love?","What does Heavenly Father’s plan for families mean to you?","Which grandparents or ancestors began our family? Share how they met and married."]},"b1-w4-n1":{"story":["Long before the world was made, we all lived with Heavenly Father. He had a plan for us to come to earth and grow.","Satan said, “Send me! Not one person will be lost. Give me Your honor.” He wanted all the praise for himself.","Satan also wanted to take away our agency, our freedom to choose. Satan would not follow Heavenly Father’s plan.","Jesus said, “Father, Your will be done, and the glory be Yours forever.” He meant, “We will do it Your way.” Heavenly Father chose Jesus, and Satan had to leave.","You are free to choose too, because Heavenly Father loves you. Tonight, pick one kind thing to do tomorrow, like sharing a snack."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau is brave, and choosing what is right takes courage, just like Jesus showed. In Beau’s world today: At snack time, there was one cookie left. Beau wanted it, but he broke it in half and gave his friend a piece. Just like Jesus, Beau learned that choosing Heavenly Father’s way is a brave and happy choice.","feel":"Hold out both hands. Pretend one hand holds a good choice. Lift that hand up high, like Jesus saying yes to Heavenly Father. Can you show me your brave, choosing face?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus chose Heavenly Father’s plan, and He was strong and full of love."],["NAME","That strong, steady feeling is called courage. Your mind might think, ‘I can choose the right.’ Your body might feel tall, with a strong, straight back."],["FIND","When have you chosen the right thing, even when it was hard?"],["FORK","Jesus could want the glory, or give it to Heavenly Father. Jesus took Heavenly Father’s way."],["CALM","Put your hands over your heart. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me choose the right.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father lets me choose, and I can choose like Jesus!","ask":["“Who did Heavenly Father choose?”","“What did Satan want to take away?”","“What did Beau do with the last cookie?”"],"share":["When has being free to choose helped you grow?","How has choosing Heavenly Father’s way brought you joy?","Which grandparent or ancestor made a brave, good choice? Share their story."]},"b1-w4-n2":{"story":["Adam and Eve lived in a beautiful garden called Eden. Heavenly Father said, “You may choose for yourself. But do not eat from this one tree.”","Satan, who tries to trick us, spoke through a snake. He told Eve the fruit would open her eyes.","The fruit looked good to eat and pretty to see. Eve wanted to be wise. She ate some, and she gave some to Adam.","Now they could tell good from bad. They sewed fig leaves together to make clothes. Prophets teach this was part of the plan, so they could learn.","You are learning good from bad too, like a ripe tomato from a weed. Tonight, think of one good choice you made today."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout loves to learn new things, and Eve wanted to learn and be wise. In Scout’s world today: Scout wanted to know which plants in the garden were good to eat. His grandpa showed him a ripe red tomato and a weed. Just like Adam and Eve, Scout learned that Heavenly Father helps us learn good from bad.","feel":"Hold your hands like you are holding a round fruit. Look at it closely. Now open your eyes wide, like learning something new. Can you show me your wide-open eyes?","stones":[["SPOT","Eve saw the pretty fruit and wanted to be wise. She felt a big wish to learn."],["NAME","That eager, wondering feeling is called curiosity. Your mind might think, ‘I want to learn more!’ Your body might feel buzzy in your tummy, with bright, wide eyes."],["FIND","When have you really wanted to learn something new?"],["FORK","Eve could leave the fruit, or she could eat it. Eve and Adam chose to eat, and they began to learn."],["CALM","Rub your hands together slowly. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me learn good from bad.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father helps me learn good from bad, every day!","ask":["“Where did Adam and Eve live?”","“What did Eve want to be?”","“What did Grandpa show Scout in the garden?”"],"share":["What is something important you learned by making a choice?","How has Heavenly Father helped you learn good from bad?","Which grandparent or ancestor taught you right from wrong? Share how."]},"b1-w4-n3":{"story":["Adam and Eve had eaten the fruit. Later, in the cool of the day, they heard the Lord in the garden. They felt afraid, so they hid among the trees.","The Lord called to Adam, “Where are you?” The Lord already knew. He was calling Adam to come out.","Adam answered, “I heard You, and I was afraid, so I hid.” The Lord asked, “Did you eat from the tree?”","Adam told what happened. “Eve gave me the fruit, and I ate.” Eve told what happened too. “The snake tricked me, and I ate.” They told the truth.","When you make a mistake, you do not need to hide. Heavenly Father and Jesus love you. Tell the truth, and They will help you try again."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren likes to stop and think, and Adam and Eve had to think about telling the truth. In Wren’s world today: Wren spilled juice on the rug and wanted to hide behind the couch. She stopped, thought, and told her dad, “I spilled it.” Her dad gave her a hug. Just like Adam and Eve, Wren learned that telling the truth is better than hiding.","feel":"Pull your blanket over your head, like hiding. Now peek out and wave! Can you show me how you come out and say, “Here I am”?","stones":[["SPOT","Adam and Eve felt afraid and hid. Then they came out and told what happened."],["NAME","That shaky, want-to-hide feeling is called fear. Your mind might think, ‘Oh no, what will happen?’ Your body might feel a tight tummy and a fast heart."],["FIND","When have you felt like hiding after a mistake?"],["FORK","Adam and Eve could keep hiding, or they could come out. They came out and told the truth."],["CALM","Take a slow breath and let your tummy go soft. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me tell the truth.”"]],"remember":"I do not need to hide, because Heavenly Father loves me!","ask":["“Where did Adam and Eve hide?”","“What did the Lord call out to Adam?”","“What did Wren tell her dad?”"],"share":["When did telling the truth help you feel closer to Heavenly Father?","How can our family make it safe to tell the truth?","Which grandparent or ancestor was known for being honest? Share a story."]},"b1-w4-n4":{"story":["The Lord made a promise. One day, a child from Eve’s family would win over Satan, the trickster.","Prophets teach that this child is Jesus, the Savior. A Savior rescues us. Jesus would help everyone come back to Heavenly Father.","Then Heavenly Father made Adam and Eve warm coats from animal skins. He was taking care of them.","Now it was time to leave the garden. Adam would work the ground to grow food. Leaving was part of the plan, so they could have a family.","Heavenly Father takes care of you too. Tonight, snuggle under your warm blanket. Jesus is your Savior, and He loves you."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow loves to care for others, just like Heavenly Father cared for Adam and Eve. In Willow’s world today: Willow’s grandpa felt chilly on the porch. Willow brought him a soft blanket and tucked it around his knees. Just like Heavenly Father with Adam and Eve, Willow learned that love helps keep others warm and safe.","feel":"Pull your blanket up to your chin. Hug it tight, like a warm, soft coat. Can you show me your snuggly, cozy face?","stones":[["SPOT","Heavenly Father made warm coats for Adam and Eve. They were loved and cared for."],["NAME","That warm, safe, snuggly feeling is called love. Your mind might think, ‘Heavenly Father takes care of me.’ Your body might feel warm and soft all over."],["FIND","When has someone helped you feel warm and loved?"],["FORK","Adam and Eve could give up, or keep going with Heavenly Father’s help. They kept going, working and praying."],["CALM","Wrap your arms around yourself in a gentle hug. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for Jesus.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father takes care of me, and Jesus is my Savior!","ask":["“What did Heavenly Father make for Adam and Eve?”","“Who is the Savior that was promised?”","“What did Willow bring her grandpa?”"],"share":["When have you felt Heavenly Father taking care of you?","How did you come to know that Jesus is your Savior?","Which grandparent or ancestor made something warm for your family? Share about them."]},"b1-w4-n5":{"story":["Outside the garden, Adam and Eve prayed to Heavenly Father. They heard the Lord’s voice answer them.","The Lord asked them to give Him the firstborn lambs of their flocks. This gift, called a sacrifice, was their very best.","Adam obeyed, even before he knew why. After many days, an angel of the Lord came to him.","The angel asked why Adam gave this gift. Adam said, “I do not know, but the Lord asked me to.” The angel taught that the lamb was like Jesus. Jesus would give His life to save us.","You can obey too, just like Adam. When we obey and ask, Heavenly Father helps us understand. Tonight, tell Him in your prayer one way you will obey."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren loves to wonder why, just like Adam did when the angel taught him. In Wren’s world today: Wren asked her mom, “Why do we pray before we eat?” Her mom said, “To thank Heavenly Father for our food.” Wren smiled and folded her arms. Just like Adam, Wren learned that when we obey and ask, Heavenly Father helps us understand.","feel":"Fold your arms and bow your head. Now be very still, like Adam and Eve listening for the Lord’s voice. Can you show me your quiet praying arms?","stones":[["SPOT","Adam obeyed before he knew why. Then the angel taught him about Jesus, and he understood."],["NAME","That quiet, calm, safe feeling is called peace. Your mind might think, ‘Heavenly Father hears me.’ Your body might feel still and soft, with slow breaths."],["FIND","When have you felt quiet and peaceful when you prayed?"],["FORK","Adam could wait to know why, or obey right away. Adam obeyed, and then he learned about Jesus."],["CALM","Fold your arms and close your eyes. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for Jesus.”"]],"remember":"When I pray and obey, I learn about Jesus!","ask":["“What did Adam and Eve do outside the garden?”","“Who came to teach Adam?”","“What did Wren ask her mom?”"],"share":["When did you obey before you understood why?","How has prayer helped you feel close to Jesus?","Which grandparent or ancestor taught you to pray? Share that memory."]},"b1-w4-n6":{"story":["Adam praised Heavenly Father. He said, “Because of my choice, my eyes are open. In this life I will have joy!”","Eve heard him and was glad. She said, “Without our choice, we would never have had children.”","Eve said they would never have known good from bad. They would never feel the joy of Jesus saving them.","Leaving the garden was part of Heavenly Father’s happy plan. Adam and Eve praised Him together. Then they taught their sons and daughters all these things.","You are part of that happy plan too! Tonight, think of something new you learned to do. Feel the joy inside, then hug your family."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout loves to discover things, and Adam and Eve discovered the joy in Heavenly Father’s plan. In Scout’s world today: Scout tried and tried to zip his own coat. One morning, zip! He did it all by himself. Scout ran to tell everyone! Just like Adam and Eve, Scout learned that learning and growing bring joy.","feel":"Stretch your arms up high and wiggle your fingers, like joy bubbling up. Now give yourself a big hug. Can you show me your happiest smile?","stones":[["SPOT","Adam and Eve saw that Heavenly Father’s plan was good, and they felt full of joy."],["NAME","That big, bright, bubbly feeling is called joy. Your mind might think, ‘I am so glad!’ Your body might feel light and warm, with a big smile."],["FIND","When have you felt so happy you wanted to tell someone?"],["FORK","Adam and Eve could be sad about the garden, or glad about the plan. They were glad and praised Heavenly Father."],["CALM","Put your hands on your smiling cheeks. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for my family.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father’s plan is a happy plan, and I am part of it!","ask":["“Who said, “We would never have had children”?”","“What did Adam and Eve teach their children?”","“What did Scout learn to do all by himself?”"],"share":["When have you felt joy because of Heavenly Father’s plan?","What do you most want our children to know about Jesus?","Which grandparent or ancestor taught their family about Jesus? Share about them."]},"b1-w4-n7":{"story":["Adam and Eve had two sons, Cain and Abel. Cain grew food. Abel took care of sheep.","Both brothers brought gifts to the Lord. The Lord was glad for Abel’s gift. But Cain’s heart was not right, and he got very angry.","The Lord said, “If you do well, you will be welcomed.” But Cain held on to his anger. He hurt Abel so badly that Abel died. Prophets teach that Abel’s spirit was safe with Heavenly Father.","The Lord asked Cain, “Where is Abel?” Cain said, “Am I my brother’s keeper?” Yes! The Lord wants us to watch over each other.","Jesus helps us let go of anger and choose love. Tonight, say, “I will be a keeper too.”"],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow has a tender heart for others, and tonight is about caring for family. In Willow’s world today: On the walk home, Willow held her little sister’s hand at every corner and kept her close. Just like the Lord taught Cain, Willow learned that we are meant to watch over our brothers and sisters.","feel":"Hold out your arms like you are giving a big hug. Now wrap them gently around your pillow. Can you show me your gentle, caring hug?","stones":[["SPOT","Cain let his anger grow, and he hurt his brother. The Lord wants brothers to love each other."],["NAME","That hot, stormy feeling is called anger. Your mind might think, ‘That is not fair!’ Your body might feel hot cheeks and tight fists."],["FIND","When have you felt angry with a brother, sister, or friend?"],["FORK","Cain could let go of his anger, or keep it. Cain kept it, but we can choose love."],["CALM","Open your fists and make your hands soft. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me choose love when I am angry.”"]],"remember":"I can watch over my brothers and sisters with love!","ask":["“What did Abel take care of?”","“What did Cain ask the Lord?”","“How did Willow watch over her little sister?”"],"share":["When has a brother, sister, or friend watched over you?","How can our family help each other choose love over anger?","Which grandparent or ancestor took good care of their family? Share a story."]},"b7-w1-n1":{"story":["Long, long ago there was a king named Solomon. A king is the one who makes the rules for a whole land. Solomon built a temple. A temple is a holy house, a special quiet place built for Heavenly Father. Building it took many, many years and many, many workers.","When Solomon died, his son Rehoboam was going to be the new king. The people came to him. They were tired, because they had carried stones and wood and worked hard for a long time. They said, \"Please be kind to us. Please make the work lighter.\" Rehoboam said, \"Give me three days to think about it.\"","First he asked the old men who had helped his father for years. They were wise. Wise means they had lived a long time and learned what really works. They said, \"Be kind to them, and they will love you always.\" Then Rehoboam asked his young friends, who had not learned that yet. They said, \"Be tough! Be bossy!\"","Rehoboam picked his friends' idea. He used big, hard, bossy words. The people felt hurt, and they walked away. The land that had been one big family of people split in two, like one group of friends becoming two groups who do not play together anymore. All of that started with the words a king chose.","You have wise people too. Heavenly Father gave you a mom or a dad, and prophets, and teachers who have already learned things you are still learning. Tonight, when someone kind asks you to do a hard thing, you can stop and really listen, even when a louder voice inside you says no."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Cup your hands behind your ears like big bunny ears. Now listen very carefully — can you hear quiet sounds in the room? That is good listening! Rehoboam forgot to listen to the wise men, and it made everything hard. Let’s practice listening to the people who love us.","stones":[["SPOT","Rehoboam heard two ideas. He picked the loud, bossy one, and the tired people walked away sad."],["NAME","He might have felt big and strong on the outside. Your mind might think, \"If I say it loud, they will do what I want.\" Your body might feel hot in your cheeks and tight in your hands."],["FIND","Can you find a time today when someone asked you to do something and you did not want to? Maybe at bath time, or when it was time to share a toy."],["FORK","There were two roads. One road: say the bossy words. The other road: take a breath and listen to the kind, wise voice. Rehoboam took the bossy road, and the people went away."],["CALM","Put one hand flat on your tummy and feel it go up and down three slow times. Then whisper, \"Heavenly Father, help me listen with a soft heart.\""]],"remember":"Kind words keep people together!","ask":["“Who did Rehoboam listen to, and who could he have listened to?”","“How do you think the tired people felt when the king said bossy words?”","“Who is one wise person you can listen to tomorrow?”"],"share":["Think of a time you followed wise counsel from a parent, leader, or the prophet. How did it bless your life? Share that story tonight.","Recall a moment you learned the hard way from not listening. What did you learn, and how can you share it gently with your child?","Is there a piece of counsel from a grandparent or ancestor your family still treasures? Tell your child where it came from."]},"b7-w1-n2":{"story":["Long ago, King Rehoboam would not listen to the wise old men. So his kingdom broke in two. One king made gold calves for people to pray to.","Later, many people stopped following the Lord. So Elijah told King Ahab, “No rain for years, until I say so.”","Then the Lord told Elijah to hide by a little stream called a brook. Elijah trusted the Lord and went.","Every morning and every evening, big black birds called ravens came. They brought Elijah bread and meat! He drank water from the brook. The Lord took care of Elijah.","The Lord takes care of you too. Tonight, think of one food you ate today. Say thank you to Heavenly Father for it."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Flap your arms like a bird carrying food. Now “fly” your hands over and drop pretend bread into someone’s lap. That’s what the ravens did for Elijah! Heavenly Father sent them so Elijah would not be hungry. Think about all the ways He takes care of YOU.","stones":[],"remember":"Heavenly Father takes care of you every single day! He gives you food, a home, and people who love you. Sometimes His help comes in surprising ways. When you wonder if He sees you, remember the ravens.","ask":["Who brought Elijah food?","Who sent the ravens to help him?"],"share":["When has the Lord provided for your family in an unexpected way? Describe the “ravens” He sent.","Think of a time help arrived just when you needed it. How did you recognize the Lord’s hand?","Did a grandparent or ancestor have a story of being cared for during a hard time? Share how the Lord sustained them."]},"b7-w1-n3":{"story":["With no rain, the brook dried up. The Lord sent Elijah to a town called Zarephath. A woman whose husband had died was picking up sticks.","Elijah asked her for water and a little bread. She said, “I have only a handful of flour and a little oil.”","Elijah said, “Do not be afraid. Make me a little cake first. The Lord says your flour and oil will not run out.”","She could have kept her food. But she trusted the Lord and shared. There was always more flour and oil! They ate for many days.","When we share and trust the Lord, He takes care of us. Tonight, think of one thing you can share tomorrow."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Pretend to pour from a jar — tip your hand and say “glug, glug, glug.” Now pour again… and again… it never runs out! That’s the miracle Heavenly Father gave the widow. He turned her little bit into more than enough.","stones":[],"remember":"When you share, Heavenly Father is so pleased! Sharing your snack, your toys, or your turn can feel hard. But God blesses people who give with a happy heart.","ask":["What did the widow share with Elijah?","What happened to her flour and oil?"],"share":["When have you given generously while you yourself were short, and seen the Lord make up the difference?","Think of someone who blessed your family through their generosity. Tell your child that person’s story.","Did an ancestor sacrifice or share during hard times? Describe their faith and what came of it."]},"b7-w1-n4":{"story":["One day the woman’s little boy got very, very sick. Then he stopped breathing.","His mother was so sad. Elijah said, “Give me your son.” He carried the boy upstairs and laid him on his own bed.","Elijah prayed with all his heart. He lay over the boy three times. He said, “O Lord, please let this child’s life come back.”","The Lord heard Elijah, and the boy lived again! Elijah carried him down and said, “See, your son lives.” His mother said, “Now I know your words from the Lord are true.”","The Lord who heard Elijah was Jesus. Prophets teach that because of Jesus, everyone will live again. Tonight, pray for someone who is sick or lonely."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Fold your arms and bow your head like Elijah praying. Whisper, “Please help, Heavenly Father.” Feel how quiet and peaceful that is. Heavenly Father heard Elijah, and He hears you too.","stones":[],"remember":"When something feels too big or too scary, YOU can pray! Heavenly Father loves to hear from you. He listened to Elijah, and He listens to little children just the same.","ask":["What did Elijah do when the boy was sick?","Did Heavenly Father hear his prayer?"],"share":["Recall a prayer that was powerfully answered in your life. How did it strengthen your testimony?","Think of a time you prayed for someone else and saw the Lord work. Share that experience.","Does your family have a story of healing or rescue through prayer, passed down from a grandparent? Tell it tonight."]},"b7-w1-n5":{"story":["Many people prayed to a pretend god named Baal.","Elijah called everyone to Mount Carmel. He asked, “How long will you go back and forth? If the Lord is the true one, follow Him!”","Elijah said, “The one who answers with fire is the true one.” Baal’s helpers called out, but no one answered.","Elijah built the Lord a stone table called an altar. He had water poured on it three times! He prayed, “Hear me, O Lord, so these people will know You.” Fire came down and burned it all up. It even dried up the water! The people bowed and said, “The Lord is the true one!”","The Lord was Jesus. Tonight, say, “I choose to follow Jesus.”"],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Wiggle your fingers up high like flickering flames, then bring them whooshing down — that’s the fire from heaven! Heavenly Father showed everyone He was real. Make your hands strong and say, “The Lord, He is God!”","stones":[],"remember":"There is only ONE true God — your Heavenly Father! He is more real and more powerful than anything pretend. We don’t follow toys, or money, or anything else more than Him. We choose Him first.","ask":["What came down from heaven for Elijah?","Who is the one true God?"],"share":["When did you receive a clear witness that God is real and present in your life? Share that moment.","What “Baals” (distractions or idols) have you set aside to follow the Lord more fully?","Did an ancestor make a bold choice to follow God over the world? Describe their courage to your child."]},"b7-w1-n6":{"story":["Elijah felt tired and sad. He thought he was all alone. He lay under a desert tree. An angel touched him and said, “Get up and eat.” Bread and water were by his head!","Then Elijah walked to a mountain cave. The Lord said, “Go out and stand on the mountain.”","A big wind broke the rocks, but the Lord was not in the wind. Then the ground shook. The Lord was not there either.","Next came a fire. The Lord was not in the fire. Then came a still, small voice, soft as a whisper. Elijah covered his face and stepped out to listen.","Heavenly Father speaks to us softly too, through the Holy Ghost. Tonight, lie still and listen to your quiet heart."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Make a big loud “WHOOOSH” wind sound, then stomp like an earthquake, then “crackle” like fire. Now… get very, very still and quiet. Shhh. That stillness is where Elijah felt God. Can you feel how peaceful quiet can be?","stones":[],"remember":"Heavenly Father usually speaks in a quiet, gentle way — through the Holy Ghost. It’s a warm, peaceful feeling in your heart. To hear Him, you have to be calm and listen.","ask":["Was God in the loud wind, earthquake, or fire?","Where did Elijah finally feel God?"],"share":["Describe a time you felt the still, small voice clearly. What was happening, and what did you do?","How do you create stillness in a noisy life so you can hear the Spirit? Share your practice.","Did a grandparent or ancestor follow a quiet prompting that changed their life — or yours? Tell that story."]},"b7-w1-n7":{"story":["Long ago, in a land called Israel, there was a king named Ahab. A king is the man who tells everybody what to do. King Ahab wanted to go fight in a war, and he wanted someone to tell him it was a good idea.","So he called for prophets. A prophet is someone Heavenly Father talks to, so the prophet can tell the people what Heavenly Father says. But these men were not listening to Heavenly Father. They were listening to the king. They all smiled and said, \"You will win! You will win!\" They said it because the king liked hearing it, and they wanted the king to like them.","There was one more man named Micaiah. Micaiah really did listen to Heavenly Father. The king's helper walked with him and whispered, \"Say something happy, like all the others did.\" Micaiah shook his head. He said, \"I will only say what Heavenly Father tells me to say.\" That took a brave heart, because he was walking in to tell a king something the king did not want to hear.","Micaiah stood in front of the king and told the truth gently: if you go to this war, it will not go well. The king got angry and sent Micaiah away to a locked room. But Heavenly Father was with Micaiah, and what Micaiah said was true. He did not change his words to make someone smile.","Sometimes you have a Micaiah moment. Someone says, \"Just say you didn't do it.\" Or you broke something and your tummy goes tight and the easy words want to jump out. Tonight you can do the brave thing Micaiah did: say the true thing, in a small voice if you need to. Heavenly Father is right there with you while you say it, just like He was with Micaiah."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Stand up tall and strong like brave Micaiah. Put your hand on your heart and say, “I will tell the truth!” It takes courage to be honest when others want you to fib. Feel how good and clean it feels to tell the truth.","stones":[["SPOT","Everyone wanted Micaiah to say the easy, happy thing. He said the true thing instead."],["NAME","Micaiah probably felt scared. Your mind might think, \"If I tell the truth, they will be mad at me.\" Your body might feel a tight tummy, hot cheeks, or feet that want to run away."],["FIND","Can you find a time today or this week when the true words were hard to say? Maybe about a spill, a toy, or who did it first."],["FORK","There were two roads. One road: say what everybody wanted to hear. The other road: say what was true. Micaiah took the true road, even when it was hard."],["CALM","Put one hand flat on your tummy and breathe out slow, like blowing a feather. Then whisper, \"Heavenly Father, help me say true words. Amen.\""]],"remember":"I can say the true thing, even when it is hard!","ask":["“Why did all those other men tell the king he would win?”","“What did Micaiah say he would only do?”","“When is it hardest for you to tell the truth — and who can help you?”"],"share":["Recall a time being honest was costly but right. How did the Lord bless your courage?","When has someone’s honesty (or your own) protected your family? Share the story.","Is there an ancestor known for integrity? Describe how their honesty shaped your family’s values."]},"b7-w2-n1":{"story":["Elijah and Elisha walked together. Elijah said, “Stay here.” But Elisha said, “I will not leave you.”","At the Jordan River, Elijah rolled up his big coat, called a mantle. He hit the water with it. The water opened, and they walked across on dry ground!","As they walked and talked, a chariot of fire came between them. It was a cart pulled by horses of fire! Elijah went up to heaven in a strong, spinning wind.","Elijah’s mantle fell down. Elisha picked it up and hit the water, just as Elijah had. It opened again! The other prophets said, “Elijah’s spirit is on Elisha!”","Heavenly Father still gives us prophets today to help us follow Jesus. Tonight, think of someone good you can follow."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Whirl your arms around like a spinning whirlwind going up to the sky — wheee! That’s how Elijah went to heaven. Then pretend to pick up his special coat, like Elisha did. Now YOU can be a helper who follows God’s prophet.","stones":[],"remember":"Heavenly Father always gives us a living prophet to lead us! Today we have a prophet too. He helps us know what God wants. When we follow the prophet, we follow Heavenly Father.","ask":["How did Elijah go up to heaven?","Who became the new prophet after him?"],"share":["When has following the living prophet blessed or protected your family? Share a specific example.","What counsel from a prophet have you chosen to follow on faith before fully understanding why?","Did an ancestor sacrifice to follow a prophet (a pioneer journey, a calling, a move)? Tell their story."]},"b7-w2-n2":{"story":["A mother came to Elisha, who spoke for Heavenly Father. Her husband had died, and she owed money. A man was coming to take her two sons away to work.","Elisha asked, “What do you have in your house?” She said, “Only one pot of oil.”","Elisha said, “Borrow empty jars from all your neighbors. Not just a few! Then shut the door and pour.”","She borrowed many jars. Her sons brought them, and she poured and poured. Every jar filled up! When there were no more jars, the oil stopped. Elisha said, “Sell the oil and pay what you owe.” Her family could stay together.","Heavenly Father knows what your family needs. Tonight, hug someone you love. He takes care of all of you."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Pretend you’re holding a jar and pouring oil — tip your hand and watch it fill up… and another… and another! It just keeps coming! That’s the miracle God gave the widow. He took her little bit and made it more than enough. Sweet Boys & Girls","stones":[],"remember":"When you obey, Heavenly Father can do amazing things! The widow listened to the prophet and got a miracle. God knows what your family needs. He can make your “little bit” into plenty.","ask":["What did the widow have at the start?","What happened when she poured her oil?"],"share":["When has obedience to a prompting or counsel opened the way for the Lord to provide?","Recall a time your family’s needs were met in a way only God could arrange.","Did a grandparent or ancestor experience the Lord multiplying their small means? Describe it."]},"b7-w2-n3":{"story":["In a town called Shunem lived a kind woman. Whenever Elisha came by, she fed him.","She told her husband, “He is a holy man who serves the Lord. Let’s make him a little room.” They put in a bed, a table, a stool and a lamp.","Elisha promised her a baby son, and he came! Years later, the boy got very sick and died.","She laid him on Elisha’s bed and hurried to Elisha. Elisha came, shut the door and prayed. The boy grew warm. He sneezed seven times and opened his eyes! Prophets teach that because of Jesus, everyone will live again.","The woman made room for Elisha, and the Lord blessed her. Tonight, think of a way to make room for someone."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Pretend to fluff a pillow and pat a cozy bed — you’re making a nice room for someone special! That’s what the kind woman did for the prophet. Now give a warm, welcoming wave like you’re saying, “Come in, you’re welcome here!”","stones":[],"remember":"Being kind and welcoming makes Heavenly Father happy! You can make room for others — share your space, your snack, or a smile. The kind woman served the prophet, and God blessed her.","ask":["What did the kind woman build for the prophet?","How did Heavenly Father bless her?"],"share":["When has quiet service you offered come back to bless you unexpectedly? Share it.","Think of someone who “made room” for your family with hospitality or kindness. Tell their story.","Did an ancestor open their home or heart to others? Describe their example of welcome."]},"b7-w2-n4":{"story":["Naaman led the soldiers of a land called Syria. But he had a sickness on his skin.","A little girl from Israel helped Naaman’s wife. She said, “I wish Naaman could see the prophet in Israel! He would heal him.”","So Naaman came to Elisha’s door. Elisha sent word: “Wash in the Jordan River seven times. Then you will be clean.”","Naaman was angry and went away. He wanted Elisha to do something grand. His helpers said, “If the prophet asked a big thing, you would do it!” So Naaman washed seven times. His skin became soft, like a little child’s!","Jesus told about Naaman too. Small choices to obey can bring big blessings. Tomorrow, do one small thing your grown-up asks."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Let’s count and dip together! Pretend to dip down in the water seven times — “one, two, three, four, five, six, seven!” Now pop up clean and healthy like Naaman! Doing the simple thing God asked made all the difference.","stones":[],"remember":"Do the simple things Heavenly Father asks, even if they seem small! Saying prayers, being kind, listening, and obeying might feel plain. But those little things bring big blessings.","ask":["What did Elisha tell Naaman to do?","Was Naaman healed when he finally obeyed?"],"share":["When has obeying a “small and simple” commandment brought you unexpected blessings?","Recall a time pride almost kept you from a blessing, and humility opened the door. Share it.","Did a child or humble person ever lead someone in your family to truth (like the servant girl)? Tell it."]},"b7-w2-n5":{"story":["Some young men learned from Elisha, who spoke for Heavenly Father. They said, “Our home is too small. Let’s cut trees by the Jordan River and build more room.”","Elisha went with them. They chopped trees with axes, tools with heavy iron heads.","Splash! One axe head fell into the water and sank. The young man cried, “Oh no, master! It was borrowed!”","He told Elisha right away. Elisha asked, “Where did it fall?” Then Elisha cut a stick and threw it in. The heavy iron axe head floated up! Elisha said, “Pick it up.”","Heavenly Father cares about little worries too. Tonight, think of one small worry, and tell Him about it in a prayer."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Pretend to swing an axe — chop, chop, chop — then “whoops!” let it splash into the water. Now use your finger to make the axe head float up, up to the top! Heavenly Father cared about that little lost axe. He cares about your little worries too.","stones":[],"remember":"No problem is too small for Heavenly Father! Maybe you lost a toy, or you’re worried about something little. You can still talk to Him about it. He cared about a lost axe head, and He cares about YOU.","ask":["What did the man lose in the water?","What did Elisha do to help?"],"share":["When has the Lord helped you with something “small” that mattered to you? Share it.","How does knowing God cares about little things change the way you pray?","Did a grandparent or ancestor have a tender story of a small but meaningful answer to prayer? Tell it."]},"b7-w2-n6":{"story":["One night, the king of Syria sent soldiers all around Elisha’s city. They had horses and chariots, carts pulled by horses.","In the morning, Elisha’s helper saw them. He was afraid. He said, “Oh no, master! What shall we do?”","Elisha said, “Do not be afraid. There are more with us than with them.”","Elisha prayed, “Lord, please open his eyes, so he can see.” Then the helper saw it! The mountain was full of horses and chariots of fire around Elisha! Heavenly Father was keeping them safe. Later, Elisha had the soldiers fed and sent home.","Heavenly Father is with you too. Even in the dark, you are never alone. Tonight, say a little prayer and feel Him near."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Cover your eyes with your hands like the scared helper. Now open them wide and look all around — “Wow! Horses of fire everywhere!” Heavenly Father had helpers all around them the whole time. He has helpers around you too, even when you can’t see them.","stones":[],"remember":"When you feel scared, remember: Heavenly Father’s helpers are all around you! You can’t always see them, but they are there. God is stronger than anything that scares you. You are never alone.","ask":["What did Elisha’s helper see after Elisha prayed?","Who has more helpers — God’s side or the enemy’s?"],"share":["When have you felt unseen help or protection from the Lord? Describe it.","How do you help your children feel God’s nearness when they are afraid?","Did an ancestor experience a miraculous protection or rescue? Share that faith-building story."]},"b7-w2-n7":{"story":["A long, long time ago there was a city with a big stone wall all around it, like a fence made of rocks. Soldiers from another land camped outside the wall. Nobody could go out, so nobody could bring food in. Everyone inside got very, very hungry. Then a man named Elisha told them something surprising. Elisha was a prophet. A prophet is someone Heavenly Father talks to, so he can tell the people what Heavenly Father says. Elisha said, \"Tomorrow there will be plenty of food.\" Some people did not believe him. They had waited so long that their hearts felt tired.","Outside the city gate sat four men. A gate is the big door in the wall. The men were sick, so they stayed outside where no one else could catch their sickness. They were hungry too. They said to each other, \"If we just sit here, we stay hungry. Let's walk over to the other camp and ask for food.\" So they went, slow and quiet, in the evening light.","And the camp was empty! Heavenly Father had made the soldiers hear a big noise, and they hurried away and left everything behind - bread and meat, clothes, silver and gold. The four men ate and ate until their tummies were full. They felt warm and happy and safe.","Then they stopped. One of them said, \"This is good news. We cannot keep it to ourselves. Everyone in the city is hungry. We have to tell them!\" They could have stayed by the food and said nothing. Instead they ran all the way back to the gate and shouted the happy news. Soon the whole city had food, just like Elisha said.","You have good news too. Jesus loves you. Heavenly Father loves you. That is the happiest news there is, and it is for sharing, not for keeping in a pocket. Tomorrow you can give one piece of good news to someone: tell your brother he did a good job, or tell Mom something happy, or share a snack with a friend who has none. Tonight you can start by telling Heavenly Father thank you for the good news."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Cup your hands around your mouth and call out, “I have good news!” Now run in place like the four men hurrying to tell everyone. Sharing good news makes everyone happy! The best news of all is that Heavenly Father loves us.","stones":[["SPOT","The four men found a whole camp full of food when they were starving. Then they remembered the hungry people back in the city."],["NAME","Their feeling was happy, and then it turned into caring. Your mind might think, \"Everybody should know this!\" Your body might feel warm and bouncy in your chest, like you want to run and tell."],["FIND","When did you find out something happy - a treat, a surprise, a new friend - and want to tell somebody right away? Who did you tell first?"],["FORK","Road one: keep the food and say nothing. Road two: run back and tell the hungry people. The four men took road two, and the whole city ate."],["CALM","Put your hand flat on your tummy and feel it go up and down, three slow breaths. Then whisper, \"Heavenly Father, thank you for good news. Help me share it tomorrow.\""]],"remember":"Good news is for sharing!","ask":["“What did the four men find in the empty camp?”","“Why did they run back to the city instead of staying with the food?”","“Who is one person you can give happy news to tomorrow?”"],"share":["When has sharing the gospel or your testimony brought joy to you or someone else? Share it.","Who shared the “good news” of the gospel with your family line? Tell that person’s story.","How can your family share God’s love with someone this week? Make a small plan together."]},"b7-w3-n1":{"story":["Hezekiah became king of Judah when he was twenty-five years old. He did what was right in the Lord’s eyes.","Many people prayed to statues called idols instead of the Lord. So Hezekiah broke the idols into pieces.","Long ago, Moses had made a brass snake. Now people burned sweet smoke to it, as if it were an idol. So Hezekiah broke that too.","The scriptures say, “He trusted in the Lord.” Hezekiah held on tight to the Lord and kept His commandments. And the Lord was with him. The Lord he trusted was Jesus.","You can trust Jesus too, like jumping into loving arms. Tonight, hug your pillow tight. Jesus is always there to hold on to."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Reach out and grab a grown-up’s hand and hold on tight — don’t let go! That’s how Hezekiah held onto Heavenly Father, trusting Him completely. Holding God’s hand keeps us safe and brave. Who can you trust and hold onto?","stones":[],"remember":"Trusting Heavenly Father means believing He loves you and will help you. King Hezekiah trusted God and got rid of anything that pulled him away. You can trust God too — He is always there for you.","ask":["What did Hezekiah get rid of so the people could follow God?","Did Hezekiah trust the Lord?"],"share":["What does it look like in YOUR life to “trust in the Lord”? Share a recent example.","What “idols” or distractions have you cleared away to draw closer to God?","Did an ancestor make a courageous choice to put God first? Tell their story."]},"b7-w3-n2":{"story":["A big army from far-off Assyria came near Jerusalem. Its king sent King Hezekiah a letter.","The letter said, “The Lord you trust will not keep Jerusalem safe.” It was a scary letter. What would Hezekiah do?","Hezekiah carried the letter to the temple, the Lord’s house. There he spread it out before the Lord.","Then Hezekiah prayed, “O Lord, You made heaven and earth. Lean down close and hear me. Please save us, so all the world will know You are the Lord.”","Hezekiah did not hide his worry. He gave it to the Lord. Tonight, bring one worry to Heavenly Father too. He always hears, and He wants to help."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Pretend you have a worry in your hands. Now “spread it out” and lay it down in front of you, like Hezekiah laid the letter before God. Take a deep breath out — whoosh — and let the worry go. Heavenly Father can carry our worries for us.","stones":[],"remember":"When you’re worried or scared, take it to Heavenly Father! Hezekiah didn’t keep his worry inside — he spread it out before God in prayer. You can tell Heavenly Father exactly what’s bothering you.","ask":["What did Hezekiah do with the scary letter?","Did Heavenly Father help him?"],"share":["When have you “laid a problem before the Lord” and felt peace or deliverance? Share it.","How do you teach your children to take worries to God instead of carrying them alone?","Did a grandparent or ancestor face a frightening trial with faith? Describe how the Lord helped them."]},"b7-w3-n3":{"story":["King Hezekiah became very sick. The prophet Isaiah came and told him he would not get well.","Hezekiah turned his face to the wall and prayed. “Lord, remember how I have followed You with all my heart.” Then he cried and cried.","Isaiah started to leave. But before he crossed the middle yard, the Lord sent him back.","The Lord said, “I have heard your prayer. I have seen your tears. I will heal you. I will add fifteen years to your life.” The Lord who saw his tears was Jesus.","Jesus sees your tears too, and He cares. When you feel sad, you can pray like Hezekiah. Jesus is close to you."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Put your hand over your heart and say a quiet “thank you” to Heavenly Father, like Hezekiah did when he got better. Feel your heart beating — that’s a gift from God! Heavenly Father gives us each day. Let’s be thankful for the days we have.","stones":[],"remember":"Heavenly Father hears your prayers, even when you’re sad! Hezekiah prayed when he was sick, and God answered him. You can pray when you feel sick, sad, or scared.","ask":["What did Hezekiah do when he was very sick?","How did Heavenly Father answer him?"],"share":["When has the Lord answered a prayer offered in deep sorrow or sickness? Share it.","How has gratitude for each day shaped your faith? Tell your child about a day you felt especially thankful.","Did an ancestor experience healing or extra years they credited to God? Describe their testimony."]},"b7-w3-n4":{"story":["A long, long time ago there was a boy named Josiah. He was only eight years old when he became the king. Eight! That is just a little older than you. A king is the person who gets to say what happens in the whole land, so that was a very big job for a small boy.","The kings before Josiah had stopped listening to Heavenly Father. The temple - the special house where people went to be close to Heavenly Father - had gotten old and broken, and nobody had fixed it for years and years. Josiah said, \"Let's mend it.\" So workers came with wood and stone and began to fix the temple up.","While they were mending it, a helper found something hidden away: a scroll, a long roll of paper with Heavenly Father's words written on it. The people had lost it so long ago that they had forgotten what it said. A man named Shaphan read it out loud to Josiah. When Josiah heard it, he tore his coat. Long ago, when people were very, very sad, they would tear their clothes so everyone could see how sad their heart was. Josiah was sad because his people had not been listening. But he did not stay stuck in sad - he said, \"Now we will listen,\" and he sent his helpers to learn what Heavenly Father wanted them to do next.","Josiah never said, \"I am too little.\" He was young, and he still chose to listen. Heavenly Father saw his soft, kind heart, and that made Heavenly Father glad. Being little did not make Josiah's good choices little.","You are small too, and you can still choose to listen. When Mom or Dad says it is time to clean up, or when you feel sorry about something you did, you can do what Josiah did: be honest about it, and then choose the listening road. Tonight, before you close your eyes, think of one small good thing you can do tomorrow morning."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Stand up tall and proud, even though you’re little — like young King Josiah! Put on a pretend crown and say, “I can choose the right!” You don’t have to be big to be good. Heavenly Father loves your good choices, no matter how little you are.","stones":[["SPOT","Josiah heard Heavenly Father's words, and he found out his people had not been listening for a long time."],["NAME","Josiah felt sad, and a little sorry. Your mind might think, 'Oh no, we did it wrong.' Your body might feel heavy in your tummy, or hot behind your eyes."],["FIND","When did you feel that sorry feeling? Maybe when you broke something, or said an unkind word, or forgot to listen the first time."],["FORK","There were two roads. One road: hide it and pretend you did not hear. The other road: say sorry and start listening. Josiah took the listening road - and he was only a boy."],["CALM","Put your warm hand flat on your tummy and take one slow breath out, long and soft. Then whisper, 'Heavenly Father, help me listen like Josiah. Thank you for loving me.'"]],"remember":"I am little, and my good choices are big!","ask":["“How old was Josiah when he became king?”","“What did the helpers find hidden in the temple, and why were the people surprised?”","“What is one small thing you can listen to and do tomorrow?”"],"share":["Recall a time a child taught YOU about goodness or faith. Share what you learned.","What good habits did you begin young that still bless you? Tell your child about them.","Did an ancestor show great faith or character as a child or youth? Describe their early goodness."]},"b7-w3-n5":{"story":["Josiah was only eight years old when he became king! Even as a boy, he did what was right in the Lord’s eyes.","Years later, workers fixed up the temple, the Lord’s house. There Hilkiah the priest found a lost book of the Lord’s laws.","The book was read to King Josiah, and he tore his clothes. Back then, people tore their clothes to show they felt very sorry.","Josiah gathered all the people, small and great, and read them the book. He made a covenant, a promise, to follow the Lord with all his heart. All the people promised too.","Like Josiah, you can follow Jesus with all your heart. Tonight, rest your hand on your heart and say, “I will follow Jesus.”"],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Pretend to open a big, special book and hug it close to your heart. The scriptures are a treasure! Run your finger across a pretend page like you’re reading God’s words. Heavenly Father speaks to us through the scriptures. Aren’t we lucky to have them?","stones":[],"remember":"The scriptures are a special treasure from Heavenly Father! They are full of His words, just for us. King Josiah was so happy to find them and obey them. You can listen to scripture stories every day, like right now!","ask":["What special book did the workers find in the temple?","How did King Josiah feel when he heard God’s words?"],"share":["When has a scripture come alive and guided you at just the right moment? Share it.","How did the scriptures become a treasure in your own life? Tell your story.","Did a grandparent or ancestor cherish the scriptures? Describe their love of God’s word."]},"b7-w3-n6":{"story":["A long time ago there was a king named Josiah. He was still young. Long before he was born, God had given His people a book of His words — a big roll of paper called a scroll. But the people stopped reading it, and the scroll got lost in God's house for many, many years.","One day the workers were fixing up God's house, and they found it! A helper named Shaphan took the scroll to the king and read it out loud. Josiah listened, and his heart went soft and sorry. He pulled at his clothes until they tore. Back then, tearing your clothes was how people showed everyone, \"I am so sad, and I am so sorry.\"","Josiah wanted to know what Heavenly Father thought. So he sent his helpers to a woman named Huldah. Huldah was a prophetess — that means God gave her His words to say, the way He does for prophets. She listened to the Lord, and then she said His message out loud, brave and clear.","Huldah told the helpers that the Lord had seen Josiah's soft, sorry heart. Josiah had listened. Josiah had cried. And because of that, God said He would take care of him and give him peace. The king was so glad Huldah told him true words from God.","Tonight, someone might say a true thing to you, even when it is a hard thing to hear. Maybe Mom says, \"It is time to stop.\" Maybe a friend says, \"That hurt me.\" You can be like Josiah and let your heart go soft and listen. Heavenly Father loves a soft, listening heart like Josiah's — and He loves yours."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Cup your hand to your ear and listen carefully, like Josiah’s helpers listening to Huldah. Now nod your head “yes” to show you’ll obey. Heavenly Father gives us messengers to share His words. When we listen, we stay close to God.","stones":[["SPOT","Someone reads Josiah true words from God, and he listens instead of looking away."],["NAME","Josiah felt sorry and a little bit sad. Your mind might think, 'Oh no, I did it wrong.' Your body might feel hot cheeks, or a heavy, squeezy tummy."],["FIND","When did someone tell you a true thing today — maybe 'please share' or 'that was too rough'? Where did you feel it in your body?"],["FORK","Two roads. One road says, 'No I didn't!' and turns away. The other road says, 'I'm sorry, I will try again,' like Josiah. Josiah took the listening road."],["CALM","Put your hand flat on your tummy and let out one slow, long breath. Then whisper, 'Heavenly Father, help me have a soft heart that listens.'"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father loves a soft heart that listens!","ask":["“What did the workers find in God's house, and who read it to the king?”","“Why did Josiah tear his clothes — what feeling was he showing?”","“When is it hard for you to listen, and what could you say instead?”"],"share":["When has the Lord taught you through an inspired leader or friend? Share it.","Who are the “Huldahs” in your life who speak truth to you? Tell your child about one of them.","Did an ancestor have a spiritual gift used to bless others? Describe how God worked through them."]},"b7-w3-n7":{"story":["A long, long time ago there was a boy named Josiah. He was only eight years old when he became king of a land called Judah. That is very young to be a king!","In Josiah's land, the people had forgotten Heavenly Father. The kings before him taught them to pray to little statues instead. And the book with Heavenly Father's words in it, the scriptures, was lost. Nobody read it, because nobody could find it.","One day some workers were fixing the temple. The temple was Heavenly Father's special house, where people went to pray. Deep inside, the workers found the lost book! They carried it to King Josiah and read it out loud to him. Josiah listened. His heart felt soft and sorry, because now he knew his people had not been doing what Heavenly Father asked.","So Josiah called everybody to come. He read Heavenly Father's words out loud, so every single person could hear. Then he made a promise. He said, \"We will love Heavenly Father and do what He says.\" And all the people stood up and promised too. Josiah helped them take away every statue. A statue is a pretend god. It cannot see you or hear you or help you. Josiah wanted his people to talk to the real Heavenly Father, who truly listens. Then everybody ate a special dinner called the Passover. That dinner helped them remember how Heavenly Father kept His people safe long, long ago. Nobody had eaten it for a very long time.","You have promises too. Little ones, like \"I will share,\" or \"I will come when Mommy calls.\" A promise you make to Heavenly Father has a special name. It is called a covenant. That just means a promise between you and Him. Josiah's people forgot their promise for a long time. Then they heard Heavenly Father's words, and they remembered, and they started again. Heavenly Father was glad. Tonight you can do what they did. Remember one promise, and start again tomorrow. Jesus will help you keep it."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Put your hand over your heart and make a promise: “I will follow Heavenly Father.” That’s a covenant — a special promise to God! Josiah and all the people did this together. Promises to God make our hearts strong. Let’s keep our promises!","stones":[["SPOT","Today somebody made a promise. Maybe you, maybe a grown-up. Can you spot one promise that happened today?"],["NAME","When you remember a promise you forgot to keep, that feeling is called sorry. Your mind might think, \"Oh no, I said I would, and I didn't.\" Your body might feel heavy in your tummy, or hot and pink in your cheeks."],["FIND","Find a time you felt that. Maybe you said you would put the blocks away, and then you didn't. Where were you? Who was with you?"],["FORK","There were two roads. One road hides and says, \"I didn't do anything.\" The other road is Josiah's road: say sorry out loud, and start again. Which road did you take?"],["CALM","Put your hand flat on your chest. Take one slow breath in, and one slow breath out. Then whisper, \"Heavenly Father, help me keep my promises tomorrow.\""]],"remember":"Heavenly Father always hears me, and keeping my promise to Him makes my heart strong!","ask":["“What did the workers find when they were fixing the temple, Heavenly Father's house?”","“A statue cannot hear you.","Who DOES hear you when you pray?”","“What is one little promise you can keep tomorrow?”"],"share":["Which covenant or promise to God means the most to you, and why? Share it simply.","When has keeping a promise — even a hard one — strengthened your family?","Did an ancestor make and keep sacred covenants? Describe their faithfulness to your child."]},"b7-w4-n1":{"story":["Asa was a good king of Judah. He asked his people to look for the Lord and keep His commandments.","One day a very big army came to fight. Asa’s army was much smaller.","So Asa prayed, “Help us, O Lord, for we rest on You.” He leaned on the Lord, like a tired child leans on a dad.","The Lord helped Judah, and the big army ran away. Then Heavenly Father’s Spirit came upon a prophet named Azariah. He told Asa, “The Lord is with you while you are with Him.”","Jesus says it too: “Come unto me, and I will give you rest.” Tonight, lean back on your pillow. Let your body feel soft and safe."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Make yourself small and say “uh oh, the problem is so big!” Now reach up high to Heavenly Father and call, “Help me, Lord!” With God, even big problems become small. He is stronger than anything you face!","stones":[],"remember":"No problem is too big for Heavenly Father! Asa had a tiny army and a giant enemy, but he trusted God and won. When something feels too big for you, call on the Lord.","ask":["Was Asa’s army big or small?","Who helped Asa win against the giant army?"],"share":["When did you face something far bigger than your own strength, and God carried you through?","How do you turn to the Lord first when problems feel overwhelming? Share your habit.","Did an ancestor overcome impossible odds through faith? Tell that story of God’s deliverance."]},"b7-w4-n2":{"story":["A long, long time ago there was a young king named Asa. He was king of a land called Judah. Before Asa was king, many people there had stopped praying to Heavenly Father. They bowed down to statues made of wood and stone, and asked the statues for help. But statues cannot hear and statues cannot help. Only Heavenly Father can.","One day a man named Azariah came to see King Asa. Azariah was a prophet. A prophet is someone Heavenly Father gives a message to, so he can tell everybody what Heavenly Father wants them to know. Azariah said, \"The Lord is with you when you are with Him. If you look for God, you will find Him.\" When the scriptures say \"the Lord,\" they mean Heavenly Father and His Son, Jesus. Azariah was telling Asa: Heavenly Father is not hiding. Look for Him and He is right there.","Then Azariah said, \"Be strong. Do not let your hands be weak.\" When people get tired, their arms go floppy and they put their work down. So floppy, weak hands is a picture that means giving up. Azariah was saying, \"Keep going, Asa! Don't put the good work down.\" Azariah said that right then because the good work Asa had started was hard, and hard things make people want to stop.","Asa listened. He felt brave again. He took away the statues so people would pray to Heavenly Father instead. Then he called everyone together, and all the people promised, out loud, to look for Heavenly Father with their whole heart. And the scriptures say they found Him.","You have hard good things too. Sharing when you don't feel like sharing. Climbing into bed when you want one more game. Being gentle with your brother or sister all day long. Tonight, pick one good thing you want to keep doing tomorrow, even when it gets hard. You don't have to do it alone - Heavenly Father is with you when you are with Him."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Flex your arms and make your strongest muscles! Say loudly, “Be strong! Don’t give up!” That’s the message God gave to King Asa. When you feel like quitting, be strong and keep doing good. God sees all your good work!","stones":[["SPOT","Asa's good work got hard. A prophet told him, \"Be strong. Don't let your hands go floppy.\""],["NAME","Asa may have felt tired, and a little like giving up. Your mind might think, \"This is too hard. I want to stop.\" Your body might feel heavy arms, droopy shoulders, and a big sigh."],["FIND","When did you feel that today? Maybe when your shoes wouldn't go on, or your tower kept falling down, or you were told to pick up the toys again."],["FORK","Two roads. One road: put it down and walk away. The other road: take a breath and try one more time. Asa took the second road - he kept going, and the good work got finished."],["CALM","Squeeze your hands tight, then let them go soft and floppy, three times. Then whisper, \"Heavenly Father, please help me be strong and keep going.\""]],"remember":"When the good thing is hard, I can be strong and keep going, because Heavenly Father is right here with me!","ask":["“What did Azariah tell King Asa to do when the work got hard?”","“What does it mean when the scriptures say \"don't let your hands be weak\"?”","“What is one good thing you want to keep doing tomorrow, even if it's hard?”"],"share":["When did “be strong and don’t give up” describe a season of your life? How did God reward your faithfulness?","What good work are you tempted to quit, and how does this promise encourage you?","Did an ancestor persevere through long, hard effort with faith? Share their endurance."]},"b7-w4-n3":{"story":["Many years later, another king made trouble for King Asa. This time, Asa did not ask the Lord for help.","Instead, he sent silver and gold to the king of Syria for help.","Then Hanani, a prophet who saw what others could not, came to Asa. He said, “You leaned on the king of Syria, not on the Lord.”","Hanani said, “When you trusted the Lord before, He helped you.” He also said, “The eyes of the Lord run all over the earth.” The Lord sees everyone and helps those whose whole heart is His. The Lord is Jesus.","Jesus sees you right now, snug in your bed. Tonight, give Him your whole heart. You can trust Him first."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Point to your eyes, then point all around the room — Heavenly Father’s eyes see everywhere, looking for people to help! Now point up and say, “I’ll trust You first, Heavenly Father.” Even when we forget, we can always turn back to God.","stones":[],"remember":"Remember to trust Heavenly Father in everything — even the little things! Asa was a good king, but one time he forgot to ask God first. We all forget sometimes, and that’s okay. We can always turn back and trust Him again.","ask":["What did Asa forget to do when a new problem came?","Who is always watching, ready to help us?"],"share":["When did you try to solve something on your own before remembering to involve the Lord? What did you learn?","How do you “turn back” to God after forgetting Him? Share your way of recommitting.","Did an ancestor return to faith after a wandering season? Tell that hopeful story of coming back."]},"b7-w4-n4":{"story":["A long, long time ago there was a good king named Jehoshaphat. He was king of a land called Judah, where Heavenly Father's people lived. Jehoshaphat loved Heavenly Father very much, and he wanted his people to love Him too. So he went walking all through his land, from the big city to the little towns, helping people remember Heavenly Father again. Some of them had forgotten.","While he walked, the king saw a problem. When two people had a fight — maybe about a sheep, or about whose field was whose — somebody had to listen to both of them and say what was fair. That person is called a judge. A judge is like a grown-up who hears both sides and then says, \"Here is the fair thing to do.\" Judah needed good judges in every town.","So King Jehoshaphat picked kind, careful people and said, \"You be the judges.\" Then he told them something they would never forget: \"Be careful. When you say what is fair, you are not just doing it for people. You are doing it for Heavenly Father.\" He meant that Heavenly Father was listening too, and Heavenly Father always wants what is fair.","The king said one more thing. He said, \"Be brave and do your work with a glad heart, and the Lord will be with you when you do what is good.\" \"The Lord\" means Heavenly Father. The judges said yes. They would listen to everybody, even quiet people and poor people, and they would tell the truth about what was fair.","You are a little judge sometimes too. When you and your friend both want the red cup, somebody has to say what is fair. When you decide to share, or take turns, or let the smallest one go first — that is you doing the fair thing. And just like the king said, Heavenly Father is near you when you do it. Tonight you can think of one person you will be fair to tomorrow."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Hold out both hands flat like a balance scale and keep them even and level. That’s being fair — treating everyone the same! Jehoshaphat wanted everyone to be treated fairly. Can you keep your hands nice and even, like fairness?","stones":[["SPOT","Think of a time today when two people both wanted the same thing — the same toy, the same seat, the same turn."],["NAME","That feeling is called wanting-it-first. Your mind might think, \"But I had it! It's mine!\" Your body might feel hot in your cheeks, with tight hands that want to hold on."],["FIND","When did you feel that today? Maybe at the table, maybe on the rug, maybe with a brother or sister or a friend."],["FORK","There were two roads. One road: grab it and hold tight. The other road: stop, breathe, and say, \"You go first, then me.\" Which road did your feet walk down?"],["CALM","Open your tight hands flat on the blanket, like you are letting the toy go. Take one slow breath. Then whisper, \"Heavenly Father, help me be fair tomorrow. Amen.\""]],"remember":"When I am fair, Heavenly Father is right there with me!","ask":["“What is a judge, and what did King Jehoshaphat tell his judges to remember?”","“Why did the king want the judges to listen to everybody, even the quiet ones?”","“Who is one person you can be fair to tomorrow, and what will you do?”"],"share":["When has being fair or just been difficult but right for you? Share the experience.","How do you teach fairness and kindness in your home? Tell about a time it mattered.","Did an ancestor stand for justice or treat others fairly when it was hard? Describe their integrity."]},"b7-w4-n5":{"story":["One day King Jehoshaphat heard that a big army was coming. He felt afraid. He asked everyone in Judah to fast: to skip food and pray very hard.","People came from every town to the Lord’s house. There Jehoshaphat prayed for help.","He said, “We do not know what to do. But our eyes are upon You.” They looked to the Lord, like a child looks up to a parent.","All of Judah stood before the Lord with their families, even the little ones. Heavenly Father hears little ones too. Jesus loved little children and blessed them.","When you do not know what to do, look to Heavenly Father together. Tonight, kneel with your family and pray."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Point to your eyes, then look up toward heaven and say, “Our eyes are upon Thee!” That means, “I’m looking to You, Heavenly Father!” When you don’t know what to do, look up to God. He always knows the way.","stones":[],"remember":"When you don’t know what to do, look to Heavenly Father — “our eyes are upon Thee!” Jehoshaphat didn’t know how to win, so he kept his eyes on God. And God saved them! You can look to Him!","ask":["What did Jehoshaphat pray when he was scared?","What did the people do instead of fighting?"],"share":["When have you prayed “I don’t know what to do, but my eyes are on You,” and seen God act? Share it.","How do you “keep your eyes on the Lord” during uncertain times? Tell your child your practice.","Did an ancestor face a battle they couldn’t win alone, and watch the Lord deliver them? Tell that story."]},"b7-w4-n6":{"story":["Uzziah became king when he was only sixteen years old. He did what was right in the Lord’s eyes.","While a wise man named Zechariah lived, Uzziah looked to the Lord. As long as he did, the Lord helped him do well.","Uzziah dug many wells and loved growing things. The Lord helped him until he was very strong.","But then Uzziah became proud. He felt bigger than everyone, even the Lord. He went into the temple to do a job only the priests could do. The priests said, “This is not for you, Uzziah.” But he got angry.","Jesus is never proud. He is humble, gentle and ready to listen. Tonight, cheer for someone else, the way Jesus would."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Puff up your chest big and proud, then let all the air out and bow your head humbly. Ahh, that feels better! Heavenly Father loves a humble heart. Let’s stay thankful and remember our blessings come from Him.","stones":[],"remember":"Stay humble and remember that your blessings come from Heavenly Father! King Uzziah did great things, but then he got proud and forgot God. Being humble means saying “thank you” to God and following His rules.","ask":["What happened to Uzziah’s heart when he became strong?","Does Heavenly Father want us proud or humble?"],"share":["When did success tempt you toward pride, and how did you stay (or return to) humble? Share gently.","How do you remember that your blessings come from God? Tell about a habit of gratitude.","Did an ancestor stay humble through prosperity or success? Describe their grounded faith."]},"b7-w4-n7":{"story":["King Hezekiah sent letters through all the land. He invited everyone to the Lord’s house for the Passover.","The Passover was a special meal remembering when the Lord set His people free. Prophets teach that its little lamb was a picture of Jesus.","The letters said, “Come back to the Lord. He is kind and loves to forgive. He will not turn away from you.”","Many came, but some were not made ready the right way. Hezekiah prayed, “Good Lord, forgive everyone whose heart wants to find You.” The Lord heard him and healed the people. There was great joy!","Jesus invites you too, every day. Tonight, think of someone you can invite to play. Everyone is welcome with Him."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Wave your arm like you’re inviting friends over: “Come, come celebrate with God!” Now clap and cheer for the happy celebration! Heavenly Father loves it when we come to Him. There is so much joy in worshiping together. Can you feel the happiness?","stones":[],"remember":"Heavenly Father always invites you to come close to Him — and it brings great joy! King Hezekiah invited everyone to celebrate God, and those who came were so happy. You can come to Heavenly Father anytime, through prayer, church, and good choices.","ask":["What did King Hezekiah invite everyone to do?","How did the people feel when they came to celebrate God?"],"share":["When have you felt the joy of returning or drawing closer to God? Share that feeling.","How does your family find joy in worshiping together? Tell about a meaningful time.","Did an ancestor gather family to God or invite others back to faith? Describe their loving invitation."]},"b6-w1-n1":{"story":["Long ago, there was no food in Bethlehem. So Naomi’s family moved far away, to a land called Moab.","In Moab, Naomi’s husband died. Her two sons married Ruth and Orpah. The sons died too. Naomi was sad and far from home.","Then Naomi heard the Lord had given food back home. She told Ruth and Orpah, “Go back to your mothers’ homes.” Orpah kissed Naomi and went home.","But Ruth held on tight to Naomi. Ruth said, “Where you go, I will go. Your people will be my people, and your Heavenly Father my Heavenly Father.” So they walked to Bethlehem together.","Ruth stayed because she loved Naomi. Jesus stays close to you like that. Tonight, think of someone sad to stay close to."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Put your arm around someone next to you, or give yourself a hug. That’s what Ruth did for Naomi — she stayed close when Naomi needed her most. Real love means staying with people even when things are hard. Close your eyes and think of someone you love. Can you picture yourself staying with them and helping them?","stones":[],"remember":"YOU can be loyal like Ruth! When your friend is sad, stay with them. When your sibling needs help, don’t leave. When things get hard, don’t give up on people you love. Ruth didn’t run away when Naomi was sad — she stayed and helped. You can do the same thing!","ask":["What did Ruth say to Naomi?","Why did Ruth stay with her?"],"share":["Tell your children about a time you stayed beside someone — a friend, a spouse, a family member — when it would have been easier to walk away. What did that loyalty cost you, and what did it give back?","When have you felt Heavenly Father’s love come through during a hard season? How did that teach you what His love feels like?","Is there a grandparent or ancestor in our family known for their loyalty or devotion? Share their story so your children can carry it forward."]},"b6-w1-n2":{"story":["Ruth and Naomi needed food. Ruth said, “Let me go to the field and pick up grain.”","The Lord told farmers to leave some grain behind. It was “for the poor and the stranger,” people from far away. Then hungry people could eat too.","So Ruth went to the field of Boaz, from Naomi’s family. She picked up grain the workers left behind.","Boaz said, “Stay in my field, and drink our water.” Ruth asked, “Why are you so kind to me?” Boaz had heard how Ruth loved Naomi. He told his workers to drop extra grain for her.","In a new place, Ruth found a kind helper. Tonight, thank Heavenly Father for your helpers. Tomorrow, be a kind helper too."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Pretend you’re picking things up from the ground — bend down, pick up, put in a basket. Do it a few times. That’s hard work! Ruth did that ALL day to get food for Naomi. Your arms would get tired! But Ruth kept working because she loved Naomi. Think about how it feels when you work hard to help someone you love.","stones":[],"remember":"YOU can work hard to help people too! When you clean up your toys, you’re helping your family. When you help set the table, you’re being useful. When you do your chores without complaining, you’re being like Ruth! Hard work shows love. Ruth could have complained or been lazy, but she worked happily to help Naomi.","ask":["How did Ruth get food?","What did Boaz do to help her?"],"share":["Tell your children about a time you worked hard — maybe quietly, maybe when no one noticed — to take care of our family. What kept you going?","When have you seen Heavenly Father provide for you through honest work, the way He provided for Ruth in the fields?","Share a story of an ancestor whose hard work blessed the people who came after them — including you."]},"b6-w1-n3":{"story":["Boaz was a good man. He wanted to take care of Ruth and Naomi. So Boaz and Ruth got married.","Then the Lord gave them a baby boy! Naomi had been very sad. Now she held the baby close and took care of him.","Naomi’s neighbors said, “Blessed be the Lord!” They gave the baby a name. They called him Obed.","Obed grew up and had a son named Jesse. Jesse’s son was David, who became a king. Many years later, Jesus was born into that same family! So Ruth is part of Jesus’s family.","You are part of a family too, and Heavenly Father loves each one. Tonight, say the name of each person in your family. Each one is a gift from Him."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Smile really big and hug yourself tight. That’s how happy endings feel! Ruth started out sad — her husband died, she had no food, everything was hard. But she stayed faithful and kind, and look what happened! She got a loving husband, a baby, and Naomi was taken care of. When you do what’s right, even when it’s hard, Heavenly Father gives you blessings.","stones":[],"remember":"Sometimes things are hard for a while. Maybe you’re sad, or scared, or something isn’t going well. But if you keep being kind and working hard like Ruth, good things will come! Heavenly Father sees everything you do. He sees when you’re kind, when you work hard, when you’re loyal. And He WILL bless you for it, just like He blessed Ruth!","ask":["What blessings did Ruth receive for being faithful and kind?"],"share":["Tell your children about a hard chapter in your life that you stayed faithful through — and a blessing on the other side that you didn’t see coming.","How did you come to know that Heavenly Father keeps His promises, even when the waiting was long?","What blessing in our family today can you trace back to someone’s faithfulness — yours, or an ancestor’s? Help your children see the thread."]},"b6-w1-n4":{"story":["Hannah’s husband, Elkanah, loved her very much. But Hannah had no children. She felt so sad that she cried and would not eat.","Each year, the family went to the Lord’s house at Shiloh. There, Hannah went to pray.","Hannah prayed in her heart. Her lips moved, but no sound came out. She asked the Lord for a baby boy.","She promised, “I will give him to the Lord all his life.” Eli, who helped at the Lord’s house, saw her. Eli told her, “Go in peace. May Heavenly Father give you what you asked.” Hannah was not sad anymore.","Heavenly Father heard Hannah’s quiet prayer. He hears yours too. Tonight, tell Him what is in your heart."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Put your hands together like you’re praying. Now close your eyes and imagine praying about something you REALLY want. That’s what Hannah did! She prayed with all her heart. When you pray like that — really meaning it, really trusting Heavenly Father — He hears you. Open your eyes. Did you feel how powerful prayer can be?","stones":[],"remember":"When you want something really badly, YOU can pray like Hannah! Tell Heavenly Father what you need. Pray with all your heart. Then trust that He will answer in the best way. Sometimes He says yes right away. Sometimes He makes you wait. Sometimes He says yes in a different way than you expected. But He ALWAYS hears you!","ask":["What did Hannah pray for?","Did Heavenly Father answer her prayer?"],"share":["Tell your children about a prayer you once prayed with your whole heart. What were you asking for, and how did Heavenly Father answer?","When have you felt that Heavenly Father truly heard you could feel?","Share a prayer you prayed for your children before or after they were born. Let them know they were wanted and prayed for, the way Hannah prayed for Samuel."]},"b6-w1-n5":{"story":["The Lord gave Hannah a baby boy! She named him Samuel. She said, “I asked the Lord for him.”","When Samuel was a little older, Hannah took him to the Lord’s house. She told Eli, “I prayed for this child, and the Lord gave him!”","Samuel would live there and help. Hannah kept her promise to the Lord.","Hannah prayed, “My heart rejoices in the Lord!” She was so glad! She said, “There is no rock like the Lord.” A rock is strong and safe, and so is He. After that, Hannah came every year with a little coat she made for Samuel.","Your heart can be glad too. Tonight, thank Heavenly Father for one thing that makes you glad."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Imagine your mommy has to leave you somewhere for a long time. You’d miss her SO much! That’s what happened to Samuel. But he was brave. He knew he was doing something important for Heavenly Father. Give yourself a brave squeeze on your shoulders. That’s what it feels like to do something hard because it’s right.","stones":[],"remember":"Sometimes Heavenly Father asks us to do hard things. Maybe go to a new school. Maybe share something special. Maybe be brave when you’re scared. Samuel was just a little boy when he went to live at the temple — probably younger than you! But he was brave and obedient. YOU can be brave too when Heavenly Father asks you to do hard things.","ask":["Why did Hannah take Samuel to the temple?","Was Samuel obedient?"],"share":["Tell your children about something precious you once gave back to Heavenly Father — your time, a plan, a dream — trusting Him with it.","When did serving Heavenly Father, even when it was hard or meant giving something up, bring you a peace you can still feel?","Share a memory of a parent, grandparent, or ancestor who gave their best to the Lord. What did their example plant in you?"]},"b6-w1-n6":{"story":["Young Samuel helped Eli at the Lord’s house. Eli was old, and his eyes could not see well. One night, the lamp still glowed, and Samuel lay down to sleep.","A voice called, “Samuel!” Samuel ran to Eli. “Here I am! You called me.” Eli said, “I did not call. Lie down again.”","It happened again, and then a third time. Then Eli understood. The Lord was calling Samuel!","Eli said, “If He calls, say, ‘Speak, Lord, for Your helper is listening.’” The Lord came and called, “Samuel, Samuel.” Samuel answered, “Speak; for Your helper is listening.” The Lord who called was Jesus.","The Lord can speak to you too, often as a quiet, warm feeling. Tonight, lie very still and listen, just like Samuel."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Be very, very quiet. Put your finger to your lips. Shhhh. Now listen really carefully. Can you hear tiny sounds? That’s how Samuel had to listen to hear Heavenly Father’s voice. It wasn’t loud like thunder. It was quiet and gentle. Heavenly Father speaks to you too, but you have to be still and quiet to hear Him.","stones":[],"remember":"Heavenly Father speaks to YOU through the Holy Ghost! It’s like a quiet feeling in your heart that tells you what’s right. But you have to be quiet and listen, just like Samuel. When you pray, don’t just talk — be quiet and listen too. When you’re trying to make a choice, listen to that feeling inside. That’s Heavenly Father talking to you!","ask":["Who was calling Samuel’s name?","What did Samuel say when he listened?"],"share":["Tell your children about a time you felt a quiet prompting from the Holy Ghost and followed it. What happened because you listened?","How did you first learn to recognize the still, small voice in your own life?","Share a story of a prompting that protected or guided our family — one you want your children to remember as they learn to hear Him too."]},"b6-w1-n7":{"story":["Long ago, Heavenly Father's people had a special golden box. Inside it were flat stones, and on the stones were His rules for being good and kind. The box helped the people remember that Heavenly Father was close to them, like a hug they could see.","One day the people were going to fight. They had not been listening to Heavenly Father for a long time, and they were scared. So they said, \"Let's carry the box with us! Then we will win.\" But the box was never magic. It was only a reminder of Heavenly Father. The other army won the fight and carried the golden box away to their own city.","Those people put the box next to a big stone statue they called their god. A statue is just rock. It cannot see or hear or love anybody. In the morning the statue had fallen over on the floor. They stood it back up. The next morning it had fallen over again, and it broke.","The people in that city said, \"This God is too strong for us. We must send the box home.\" They put it on a cart, and the cart went back to Heavenly Father's people, and the people were so happy to see it again. Then the prophet Samuel told them something important: the box was never the strong part. Heavenly Father is the strong part. He wants our hearts, not just a box.","Tonight, if something feels big and scary to you, remember that nothing is bigger than Heavenly Father. You don't need a special box to talk to Him. You can talk to Him right there in your bed, and He hears you every single time."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Stand up and make yourself as big and strong as you can — stretch your arms wide, stand on your tiptoes, make a strong face! That’s how POWERFUL Heavenly Father is — even MORE powerful than that! Nothing is stronger than God. Not enemies, not false gods, not anything! When you trust in Heavenly Father, you have the STRONGEST helper ever!","stones":[["SPOT","Tonight, when the light went off, the room got dark and your tummy went tight."],["NAME","That feeling is called scared. Your mind might think, \"Something is in here with me.\" Your body might feel a squeezy tummy, fast breathing, and a tight grip on your blanket."],["FIND","When was another time you felt that? Maybe at the doctor, or when a big dog barked, or when Mom went out of the room."],["FORK","Road one: hide under the covers and let the scared get bigger. Road two: take a breath and talk to Heavenly Father. Which road did you take tonight?"],["CALM","Put your warm hand flat on your tummy and breathe out slow, like blowing a feather. Whisper: \"Heavenly Father, I feel scared. Please help me feel calm. I know Thou art near me.\""]],"remember":"Nothing is bigger than Heavenly Father, and He is right here with me!","ask":["“What was inside the special golden box, and what did it help the people remember?”","“Why couldn't the stone statue help those people?”","“What is one thing that feels scary to you, and what could you whisper to Heavenly Father about it?”"],"share":["Tell your children about a time you were truly afraid, and Heavenly Father’s power carried you through it.","When have you seen that no problem and no fear is bigger than Heavenly Father? How did you come to trust His strength?","Share a moment in our family’s history when you saw God’s hand protect or rescue someone. Let your children know they come from people God watched over."]},"b6-w2-n1":{"story":["Samuel was now old. For many years, he had taught the people what Heavenly Father said.","One day the leaders of Israel came to him. They said, “Give us a king, like all the other lands have.”","This made Samuel sad. So Samuel prayed to the Lord about it.","The Lord said, “They have not turned away from you. They have turned away from Me, their King.” The Lord was Jesus, and He was already their King! Still the people said, “We want a king!” So the Lord said, “Make them a king.”","When Samuel felt sad, he prayed. Tonight, you can tell Heavenly Father anything that makes you sad. Jesus is your King, and He will always take care of you."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Stand up tall like you’re a king with a crown. Now bow down low. The Israelites wanted a human king they could see, but they already HAD the best King ever — Heavenly Father! Sometimes we want what others have and forget to be grateful for what Heavenly Father has already given us. Close your eyes and think of three blessings you already have.","stones":[],"remember":"The Israelites did that! They wanted a king because everyone else had one. But wanting things just because others have them isn’t always wise. Instead, YOU can: be grateful for what you have, trust Heavenly Father’s plan, and remember that He knows what’s best.","ask":["Why did Israel want a king?","Was this what Heavenly Father wanted?"],"share":["Tell your children about a time you stopped wanting what others had and saw how blessed you already were.","When did trusting Heavenly Father’s plan — instead of copying someone else’s — turn out to be exactly right for you?","Share something you’re deeply grateful for in our family that you might have overlooked if you were busy comparing. Let your children hear what you treasure."]},"b6-w2-n2":{"story":["Saul was a tall young man. One day his father’s donkeys were lost. Saul and a helper went to find them.","They walked through many lands, but found no donkeys! Saul said, “Let’s go home, or my father will worry.”","The helper said, “A man of Heavenly Father lives here. Let’s ask him the way.” It was Samuel.","The day before, the Lord told Samuel, “Tomorrow I will send you a man.” When Samuel saw Saul, the Lord said, “This is the man!” The Lord had chosen Saul to lead His people. Samuel told Saul, “Your donkeys are found.”","Heavenly Father knew where Saul was going, and He knows where you are too. Tonight, thank Him. When you feel lost, you can ask for help."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Pretend you’re looking for something lost — look left, look right, look behind you. That’s what Saul was doing! He was just trying to find donkeys, doing a regular job for his father. But Heavenly Father was watching and had a special plan. When YOU do your regular jobs — like cleaning your room or helping your family — Heavenly Father sees you! He might be preparing you for something special too!","stones":[],"remember":"When you’re doing everyday things like chores, homework, or helping your family, remember: Heavenly Father is watching! Saul was doing a simple job — looking for lost animals. But that job led him to Samuel, and then to becoming king! YOU never know when doing a good job on small things will lead to big blessings. So do your best, even in little tasks!","ask":["What was Saul looking for?","Who did he find instead?"],"share":["Tell your children about an ordinary, everyday moment that Heavenly Father used to lead you somewhere unexpected and good.","When have you seen that doing small things faithfully opened a door you never could have planned?","Share how a “small” decision by you or an ancestor changed the whole course of our family. Help your children see God in the ordinary."]},"b6-w2-n3":{"story":["Samuel walked with Saul to the edge of the city. He had a message from Heavenly Father just for Saul.","Samuel poured a little bottle of oil on Saul’s head and kissed him. The oil showed the Lord had chosen Saul to lead His people.","Many years later, Heavenly Father chose Jesus for the most important job of all. So Jesus is called Christ, the Anointed One.","Samuel said, “The Spirit of the Lord will come upon you.” As Saul turned to walk away, Heavenly Father gave him “another heart.” It was like a brand-new heart, ready for his new job.","Heavenly Father can help your heart get ready too. Tonight, rest your hands over your heart and feel His love."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Stand up as tall as you can — stretch way up! Saul was SO tall, taller than everyone! But even though he was tall and chosen to be king, he felt scared and hid. Put your hands over your face like you’re hiding. Sometimes when we’re asked to do big things, we feel scared. But Heavenly Father will help you, just like He helped Saul! Take your hands away — be brave!","stones":[],"remember":"Sometimes YOU might be asked to do something that feels too big or scary! Maybe speak in Primary, make a new friend, or try something new. You might want to hide like Saul did! But when Heavenly Father asks you to do something, He will help you be brave enough. You don’t have to be perfect or feel ready — just be willing!","ask":["How did Samuel show Saul was chosen?","Why was Saul hiding?"],"share":["Tell your children about a time Heavenly Father asked you to do something that scared you — and you said yes anyway.","When did you feel Heavenly Father make you brave enough, even when you didn’t feel ready?","Share a moment a parent or grandparent stepped forward in courage when God called. What did their willingness teach you about your own?"]},"b6-w2-n4":{"story":["Samuel told Saul, “Wait seven days, until I come.” Then Samuel would offer a sacrifice, a gift to the Lord. Those gifts helped people remember that Jesus would come.","Saul waited and waited. Enemies gathered close by, and many people went away. Saul felt worried and in a hurry.","After seven days, Samuel still had not come. So Saul did not wait any longer. He offered the gift himself.","Just as Saul finished, Samuel came! Samuel asked, “What have you done?” He said Saul had not kept what the Lord commanded. The Lord wanted Saul to wait for His prophet.","Waiting can be hard, and Heavenly Father will help you wait. Waiting is one way to obey. Tonight, take three slow breaths and practice waiting."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Let’s practice waiting! Close your eyes and count slowly: 1... 2... 3... 4... 5. That was only five seconds! Saul had to wait SEVEN DAYS! But he couldn’t wait. Tap your foot like you’re impatient — tap, tap, tap. That’s how Saul felt. Waiting is HARD! But sometimes Heavenly Father asks us to wait, and we need to obey even when it’s hard.","stones":[],"remember":"Saul couldn’t wait for Samuel, and it got him in BIG trouble! YOU can practice patience by: waiting your turn, not interrupting when others are talking, waiting without complaining, and trusting that good things come to those who wait.","ask":["What was Saul supposed to wait for?","What happened when he didn’t wait?"],"share":["Tell your children about a time you were tempted to rush ahead of Heavenly Father — and what you learned about waiting on Him.","When has patience and obedience, even when it was hard, protected you or brought a blessing?","Share a family story about the cost of impatience or the reward of waiting faithfully. Let your children learn it through your experience instead of their own."]},"b6-w2-n5":{"story":["The Lord gave King Saul a job and told him how to do it. But Saul did not obey all the way. He kept the best sheep and the big, strong cows called oxen.","Then Samuel came. Saul said, “I have done what the Lord said!”","But Samuel heard sheep saying, “Baa!” and oxen saying, “Moo!” Samuel asked, “What is this sound I hear?”","Saul said the animals were for a sacrifice, a gift to the Lord. Samuel said, “To obey is better than sacrifice.” The Lord wants listening hearts more than any gift. Saul said, “I was afraid of the people.”","Your best gift to Heavenly Father is listening and obeying. Tonight, tell Him one way you will obey, even when others do not."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Let’s try this: a grown-up will gently ask you to do something. When they say “Touch your nose,” you touch your nose. Good! Now imagine they said “Touch your nose” but you touched your ears instead and said “It’s close enough!” That’s what Saul did! Put your finger on your ear. Does that feel like obeying? No! Real obedience means doing EXACTLY what you’re asked, not almost.","stones":[],"remember":"Sometimes YOU might want to obey only partway. Maybe Mommy says “clean your whole room” but you only make your bed. Or Daddy says “no treats before dinner” but you sneak one. That’s like Saul! Partial obedience isn’t obedience. Heavenly Father wants us to obey completely, not make excuses or do it our own way. When someone asks you to do something, do ALL of it, not just the easy parts!","ask":["What did God tell Saul to do?","Did Saul obey all the way?"],"share":["Tell your children about a time you chose to obey Heavenly Father fully partway would have been easier.","How did you come to know that Heavenly Father wants our obedience more than our excuses?","Share a memory of someone in our family known for quiet, complete obedience to God. What did their example build in you?"]},"b6-w2-n6":{"story":["The Lord sent Samuel to Bethlehem, to a man named Jesse. The Lord said, “I have chosen a king from his sons.”","Jesse’s son Eliab came. He was tall and fine-looking. Samuel said, “This must be the one!”","But the Lord said, “Do not look at how tall he is. People look on the outside, but the Lord looks on the heart.”","Seven sons came, but the Lord had not chosen them. The youngest, David, was out caring for the sheep. When David came, the Lord said, “This is he.” Samuel poured oil on David’s head to show he was chosen.","Heavenly Father sees your heart too. He sees every kind thing you do. Tonight, tell Him one kind thing you did."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Look in a pretend mirror. What do you see on the OUTSIDE? Your face, your hair, your clothes. Now put your hand over your heart. THAT’S what Heavenly Father sees! He looks at what’s INSIDE — if you’re kind, honest, faithful, and loving. You can’t see a heart in a mirror, but Heavenly Father can see your heart all the time! Make your heart happy by being kind!","stones":[],"remember":"Sometimes people might judge you by how you look, how tall you are, or what clothes you wear. But Heavenly Father doesn’t care about that! HE looks at your heart. YOU can have a good heart like David by: being kind to everyone, telling the truth, helping others, and loving Heavenly Father. It doesn’t matter if you’re the smallest or the youngest — if you have a good heart, you’re special to God!","ask":["Why didn’t God choose the older, bigger brothers?","What did God see in David?"],"share":["Tell your children about a time you felt judged by the outside — and how it changed you to know Heavenly Father sees your heart.","When have you felt truly seen Heavenly Father for who you really are inside?","Share who in our family had the kind of heart God loves — kind, honest, faithful. Tell your children the story, so they know whose heart they carry."]},"b6-w2-n7":{"story":["King Saul had stopped following the Lord. Now he often felt troubled and unhappy inside.","His helpers said, “Let us find someone who plays the harp well.” When the king felt unhappy, soft music could help him feel well.","One helper said, “Jesse’s son David plays well, and the Lord is with him.” So Saul sent for David.","Saul loved David very much. When Saul felt troubled, David plucked the strings of his harp. Then Saul felt refreshed and well, like new again.","David played to help someone else feel better. Soft music can help hearts feel calm. Tonight, hum a gentle song for someone you love."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Hum a soft, pretty tune. Feel how peaceful that is? Now make angry sounds — “GRRRR!” Feel how different that is? King Saul felt angry and troubled. But when David played beautiful music, Saul felt peaceful again. Music can change how we feel! When you’re upset, try singing a Primary song. When you’re scared, hum your favorite hymn. Music is a gift from Heavenly Father!","stones":[],"remember":"YOU have special talents and gifts too! Maybe you can sing, draw, tell jokes, give hugs, or help others. Whatever your talent is, you can use it to help people like David did! David didn’t just play music for himself — he used it to help King Saul feel better. When you use your gifts to help others, you’re being like Jesus!","ask":["What was David’s talent?","How did he use it to help Saul?"],"share":["Tell your children about a talent or gift Heavenly Father gave you, and a time you used it to help someone who was hurting.","When have you felt that using your gift for someone else was really serving Heavenly Father?","Share a talent that runs in our family — music, kindness, making people laugh, working with your hands. Where did it come from, and who passed it to you?"]},"b6-w3-n1":{"story":["David went down to a little stream. He chose five smooth stones from the water. He put them in his shepherd’s bag.","He held his sling, a strap for throwing stones far. Then David walked toward the giant.","Goliath had a sword, a spear, and a shield. David said, “I come to you in the name of the Lord.” David was trusting the Lord, not a sword.","David said, “The battle is the Lord’s.” He wanted everyone to know the Lord saves without swords. David threw one stone, and the giant fell down. David had no sword at all!","When you have a hard thing to do, the Lord is with you. Tonight, tell Him one hard thing. You never have to be brave alone."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Stand up tall like a giant — be as big as you can! Now shrink down small like little David. Sometimes problems feel like GIANTS to us! But with Heavenly Father, we can be brave like David. Now pretend to swing a sling and let go! Even small kids can do big things when Heavenly Father helps them. You’re strong with God on your side!","stones":[],"remember":"YOU might face “giants” in your life too! Maybe a giant fear of the dark, a giant problem at school, or a giant feeling of being scared. But you don’t have to fight your giants alone! Heavenly Father will help you, just like He helped David. You can pray for courage, remember God is with you, and be brave even when you’re small. The battle is the Lord’s — He fights for YOU!","ask":["Why was David brave?","Who helped David beat Goliath?"],"share":["Tell your children about a “giant” you once faced — something that felt far too big for you — and how Heavenly Father helped you face it.","When did you learn that you didn’t have to be the strongest, only willing, because the battle was really the Lord’s?","Share a story of a parent or ancestor who faced something impossible with faith. What did their courage do for yours?"]},"b6-w3-n2":{"story":["After the giant fell, David talked with King Saul. Saul’s son Jonathan, a prince, was there listening.","Jonathan’s heart felt tied to David’s heart. Jonathan loved David as much as he loved himself. The two became best friends.","Then Jonathan and David made a covenant. A covenant is a big, serious promise, like a hug made of words. They made it because they loved each other.","Jonathan took off his own robe and gave it to David. He gave him his sword, his bow, and his belt too. Jonathan gave his very best to his friend.","Jesus calls us His friends too, and He gave His very best for us. Tonight, think of a friend. What could you share with them tomorrow?"],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Stretch your arms out wide for a big hug. Now give yourself a big squeeze! That’s how Jonathan and David loved each other — like brothers! Now think of your best friend or your sibling. Send them a big invisible hug right now. Real friends share, help each other, and are happy when good things happen to their friend. That’s a treasure!","stones":[],"remember":"YOU can be a friend like Jonathan! When your sibling or friend does something good, be HAPPY for them, not jealous! When you have something special, SHARE it with people you love. When your friend is sad, HELP them feel better. A true friend is one of God’s greatest gifts! You can be that kind of friend by sharing your toys, being happy when others succeed, helping when someone is hurt, and never being mean.","ask":["What did Jonathan give David?","How can YOU be a good friend?"],"share":["Tell your children about a true friend who loved you the way Jonathan loved David — and what that friendship has meant to your life.","When have you had to choose to be genuinely happy for someone else’s success instead of jealous? How did Heavenly Father change your heart?","Share a friendship from our family’s past — yours, or a grandparent’s — that lasted through everything. Let your children see what loyal love looks like up close."]},"b6-w3-n3":{"story":["A long time ago, a man named Saul was the king. A king is the leader of the whole land. A young man named David helped keep the people safe, and the people were so glad.","When David and the soldiers came home, the people came out singing. Their song said Saul had won thousands and David had won even more. In that time, people sang numbers to say thank you, like we clap when someone does something good.","King Saul heard the song, and his face got tight and sad. He wanted to be the best one. From that day, when Saul looked at David, his heart hurt inside.","That hurt feeling has a name. It is called jealous. Jealous is when someone else gets something good, and instead of feeling happy, you feel sore and achy inside. Saul kept that feeling and did not let it go, and one day he was so upset he threw his spear. Heavenly Father kept David safe, and David stepped away.","David did not throw anything back. David kept being kind and kept doing his work, even when the king was unkind. That was the hard road, and David chose it anyway. Tonight, if someone else gets the turn or the treat or the \"good job,\" you can choose the kind road too, just like David."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Make a big GRUMPY face. Cross your arms. That’s how jealous feels — yucky and grumpy! Now make a HAPPY face and clap for someone else. That feels better, right? When your friend gets something good, choose to be HAPPY for them, not jealous. Push that jealousy feeling away! Smile big and clap! That’s how to keep your heart happy.","stones":[["SPOT","Everyone was singing about David, and King Saul was standing right there hearing it."],["NAME","Saul felt jealous. His mind might think, 'They like him more than me.' His body might feel hot in the face, tight in the tummy, and like his hands want to squeeze."],["FIND","When did you feel that? Maybe when someone else got picked first, or got the big piece, or got a 'good job' and you didn't."],["FORK","There were two roads for Saul. One road: let the sore feeling stay and be unkind. The other road: say 'I'm glad for you' and go play. Saul took the first road. David took the kind one."],["CALM","Put one hand flat on your tummy and let out one long slow breath, like blowing a feather. Then whisper, 'Heavenly Father, help my heart be glad for them. Thank you for loving me.'"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father loves me, and I can be glad when good things happen to someone else!","ask":["“How do you think Saul's tummy felt when he heard the people singing about David?”","“When was a time someone else got something good and your heart felt sore?”","“What is one kind thing you could say tomorrow when a friend gets a turn before you?”"],"share":["Tell your children about a time someone was unkind to you, and you chose not to be unkind back. What helped you make that choice?","When have you felt jealousy try to creep into your heart, and how did turning to Heavenly Father help you let it go?","Share a memory of someone in our family who stayed gentle even when they were treated unfairly. What did their example teach you?"]},"b6-w3-n4":{"story":["King Saul was upset that the people loved David. Saul wanted to hurt him, so David and his men hid.","Saul brought soldiers to look for David. One day Saul went into a cave. He did not know David was hiding inside!","David’s men whispered, “Now is your chance!” David quietly cut a little piece from Saul’s robe. Then he said, “I will not hurt the king the Lord chose.”","Outside, David called, “Look at this piece of your robe! I did not hurt you.” Saul cried and said, “You were good to me.” Another night, David found Saul asleep. Again, David did not hurt him.","Being kind when someone is unkind is very brave. Tonight, ask Heavenly Father to help you be kind."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Hold out your hands like you’re holding something fragile. That’s what David did — he held back when he could have hurt Saul. Now imagine someone is being mean to you. What’s harder — getting them back, or being kind? Being kind is HARDER! But it’s better. Put your hands on your heart. That’s where kindness lives. Choose kindness, even when it’s hard!","stones":[],"remember":"It’s so tempting to be mean BACK! But David showed us a better way — be KIND, even to people who aren’t kind to you. When someone hurts your feelings: don’t hit back, don’t say mean things back, pray for them, and be kind anyway. That’s what Jesus teaches us! It’s HARD, but it’s right. When you choose kindness, you’re being like David and like Jesus!","ask":["What could David have done to Saul?","What did he do instead?","Why?"],"share":["Tell your children about a time you could have gotten away with something wrong — but chose to do right because Heavenly Father was watching.","When did showing mercy to someone who had hurt you bring you a peace you still remember?","Share a story of integrity from our family’s past — a parent or ancestor who did the right thing quietly, when it cost them something."]},"b6-w3-n5":{"story":["A rich man named Nabal had many sheep. David’s men had kept his shepherds safe, like a wall around them. So David asked for some food.","But Nabal said unkind words and gave nothing. David got very angry and set out with his men.","Nabal’s wife, Abigail, was wise and kind. She quickly packed bread, raisins, and fig cakes. Then she rode a donkey to meet David.","Abigail bowed low and said, “Please forgive me.” Her gentle words helped David calm down. He said, “Bless the Lord for sending you to meet me!” Abigail was a peacemaker. She kept a fight from starting.","Jesus said peacemakers are children of Heavenly Father. Tonight, practice saying one gentle word, just like Abigail."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Take a deep breath in... and let it out slowly. That’s what we do when we feel angry. Abigail used WISE, KIND words to stop trouble. Now make a peaceful face — eyes soft, lips smiling gently. That’s the face of a peacemaker! When people are angry, soft kind words can fix things faster than mean words.","stones":[],"remember":"When people are mad, YOU can be a peacemaker like Abigail! Use kind words. Say “I’m sorry” if you can. Help fix what’s broken. Sometimes a peaceful word stops a big fight! Wise Abigail used her brain AND her heart. You can too! When your sibling is mad, when your friend is upset, when someone is angry — try kind words first. They work like magic! Heavenly Father loves peacemakers.","ask":["How did Abigail stop the fight?","Can YOU be a peacemaker?"],"share":["Tell your children about a time your calm, kind words stopped a fight or healed a hard moment in our family.","When have you felt Heavenly Father give you the right words at exactly the right time? How did you know they came from Him?","Share a memory of a peacemaker in our family — someone whose gentleness held people together. What did you learn from watching them?"]},"b6-w3-n6":{"story":["When David was just a boy, a prophet named Samuel came to his house. A prophet is someone Heavenly Father talks to. Samuel poured a little oil on David's head. Pouring oil was the way people said, \"Heavenly Father has picked you. You are set apart for something special.\" Samuel was saying David would be king one day. But David did not become king that day. He went right back outside to watch his sheep.","David waited a long, long time. He grew up. He played his harp. He kept his sheep safe. A man named Saul was the king first, and Saul was not kind to David. David could have grabbed the crown - a crown is the gold hat a king wears. He did not grab it. He waited for Heavenly Father's time, even when waiting felt hard.","Then one day Saul was gone, and all the families of Israel came to David together. Israel was Heavenly Father's people, and for a while those families had not been together at all. They said, \"We want you to be our king!\" David was thirty years old. He was king for forty years. That is a long, long time - longer than you have been alive, and longer than your mom or dad has been a mom or dad.","David made a city called Jerusalem his home city, and people began to call it the City of David, because that is where the king lived. Heavenly Father helped David, and David grew strong and good. And here is something lovely: a long, long time later, Jesus was born into David's own family, in David's own little town. Heavenly Father was getting things ready for Jesus, even way back then.","Tonight, something in your life might feel like waiting too. Waiting for your turn on the swing. Waiting to be big enough. Waiting does not mean Heavenly Father forgot you. David waited years and years, and Heavenly Father remembered him every single day."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Pretend you’re putting a crown on your head — slowly, carefully. Now sit up straight like a king or queen! David waited a LONG time to become king. He could have grabbed the throne by force, but he didn’t. He waited for Heavenly Father’s perfect time. Patience is a powerful thing! Take a deep breath — being patient feels peaceful.","stones":[["SPOT","David was told he would be king when he was a little boy - and then he had to wait and wait and wait before it happened."],["NAME","Waiting can feel itchy on the inside. Your mind might think, \"It's never going to be my turn!\" Your body might feel wiggly legs, tight fists, or a hot face."],["FIND","Can you think of a time today you had to wait? For the swing? For a snack? For a grown-up to stop talking?"],["FORK","One road is grabbing, pushing, or whining until you get it. The other road is waiting with calm hands, like David did. David took the waiting road - and Heavenly Father took care of him."],["CALM","Put both hands flat on your tummy and blow one slow breath out, like blowing a feather. Then whisper, \"Heavenly Father, please help me wait with calm hands.\""]],"remember":"Heavenly Father remembers me while I wait!","ask":["“How old was David when he finally got to be king?”","“What is one thing you had to wait for today?”","“What can your hands do while you wait instead of grabbing?”"],"share":["Tell your children about something you waited a long, long time for — and how Heavenly Father’s timing turned out better than your own.","How did you come to trust that the waiting wasn’t wasted — that Heavenly Father was preparing you the whole time?","Share a promise or blessing that took years to unfold in our family. Help your children see that God’s timing is part of His love."]},"b6-w3-n7":{"story":["The leaders of the people made David their king. David was thirty years old, and he was king for forty years.","Then David brought the Lord’s ark, a holy box, into Jerusalem, his city. The ark showed the Lord was with His people.","David was so happy that he danced before the Lord with all his might! The people shouted for joy and blew trumpets.","Later, the Lord sent David a message through Nathan, His prophet. The Lord promised, “Your family will have a king forever.” Many years later, Jesus was born into David’s family. Jesus is the King forever!","Jesus loves you. Tonight, dance your fingers on your blanket. Jesus is your King forever!"],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Stand up and DANCE! Wiggle, spin, jump, clap! Do a silly happy dance! That’s how David danced for Heavenly Father! He didn’t care if he looked silly — he just wanted to praise God! Now sing “I Am a Child of God” or hum your favorite Primary song. When we praise Heavenly Father with our whole hearts, it makes Him smile!","stones":[],"remember":"You don’t have to dance like David (but you can!). You can sing songs to Him, say “I love you, Heavenly Father” in your prayers, smile when you think of Him, be happy in church, and share your joy with others! Don’t be shy about loving God! David didn’t care if people thought he looked silly — he just praised God.","ask":["Why was David dancing?","How can YOU show Heavenly Father you love Him?"],"share":["Tell your children about a time your heart was so full of love for Heavenly Father that you couldn’t keep the joy inside.","When have you worshipped or praised God without caring what anyone thought — and how did it feel to give Him all of you?","Share how worship or music has lifted your heart through hard times. Did a parent or grandparent pass that gift of praise down to you?"]},"b6-w4-n1":{"story":["A long, long time ago there was a king named David. A king is the leader of a whole country. When David was a boy he kept sheep, and he sang happy songs to Heavenly Father. He grew up and lived in a big city called Jerusalem, in a house with a flat roof you could walk on, like a porch on top.","It was springtime, and springtime was when the king went out with his soldiers. But this year David stayed home. One evening he was walking on his roof, and he looked down and saw a woman named Bathsheba. She was already part of another family. She was somebody else's wife. She was not David's, and David knew it. But he kept looking, and he kept wanting. Wanting something that is not ours happens to everybody sometimes! The very best thing to do is look away right away and think about something else.","David did not look away. He sent for her, and he made a wrong choice. Then, to hide that wrong choice, he made another one, and another one, until a good man named Uriah was gone. One wrong choice is like one little snowball: if we do not stop it, it rolls and rolls and gets bigger and bigger. David hurt a family, and that made Heavenly Father very sad.","David tried to hide it all. He hoped nobody would ever know. But Heavenly Father knew. Heavenly Father knows us the way a mommy or daddy knows their own child - all the way through, because He loves us so much. And because He loved David, Heavenly Father did not leave him alone in his hiding. Tomorrow we will hear about a man named Nathan, who helped David stop hiding and tell the truth.","Tonight you get to practice the thing David forgot. When you see something you want that is not yours - a toy, a cookie, somebody else's turn - you can look away. Looking away is a strong, brave thing to do. And if you ever do make a wrong choice, you never have to hide it. You can tell Mommy or Daddy, and you can tell Heavenly Father. When we are really sorry and we turn around and do better, Jesus can help us be clean again. That is why Jesus came."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Put your hands over your eyes like you’re hiding. Can you still see Heavenly Father? Can He still see you? Yes! Heavenly Father can ALWAYS see us, even when we hide. David tried to hide his bad choices, but Heavenly Father knew. Take your hands off your eyes. Heavenly Father loves you whether you’re being good or making mistakes — but He wants you to make GOOD choices.","stones":[["SPOT","David saw something that was not his - a woman who belonged to another family. Instead of looking away, he kept looking and kept wanting, and then he made a wrong choice, and then more wrong choices to hide it."],["NAME","The feeling is called wanting-what-is-not-mine. Your mind might think, 'I want that. I want it NOW.' Your body might feel hot in your face, or tight in your tummy, or like your hands really want to grab."],["FIND","Can you remember a time you wanted something that belonged to somebody else? Maybe a friend's toy, or the last cookie, or a turn that was not your turn?"],["FORK","There were two roads for David. One road: look away and think of something else. The other road: keep looking and keep wanting. David took the keep-looking road, and it led him and a whole family somewhere sad."],["CALM","Tonight, turn your head gently to the side - that is 'looking away.' Then take one slow breath and whisper, 'Heavenly Father, help me look away and choose good. Thank you for Jesus.'"]],"remember":"I can look away and choose good!","ask":["“What could David have done the very first second he saw something that was not his?”","“When you want something that is not yours, where in your body do you feel it?”","“Who can you tell when you make a wrong choice, instead of hiding it?”"],"share":["Tell your children about a time you stopped a wrong choice early — you looked away, you walked away — and how that protected you.","When have you felt comforted knowing Heavenly Father sees everything and is always near?","Share a simple lesson you learned the hard way about small choices leading to bigger ones. What do you hope your children learn from your story?"]},"b6-w4-n2":{"story":["King David made a very wrong choice that hurt others. So the Lord sent Nathan, His prophet, to help David see.","Nathan told David a story. A rich man had many sheep. A poor man had one little lamb. It slept in his arms, like his own little girl.","But the rich man took the poor man’s only lamb. David got angry and said, “That man had no pity!”","Nathan said, “You are the man.” The story was about David! David said, “I have sinned against the Lord.” The Lord took his sin away. David prayed, “Make a clean heart in me.”","Because of Jesus, we can feel clean inside again. Tonight, if you are sorry for something, say so. He helps hearts feel new."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Put your hands together and bow your head. Imagine telling Heavenly Father, “I’m sorry.” Real sorry comes from your heart, not just your mouth. David felt SO sad about his bad choices that he cried real tears. When you do something wrong, you might feel that sad feeling too. That’s good! It means your heart knows the difference between right and wrong. Now lift your head — when we say sorry, Heavenly Father forgives us!","stones":[],"remember":"Everyone makes mistakes — even kings, even moms and dads, even YOU! REPENT! That means: 1) Say “I’m sorry” to Heavenly Father, 2) Say “I’m sorry” to anyone you hurt, 3) Try to fix what you broke if you can, and 4) Try not to do it again. When you repent, Heavenly Father forgives you! He doesn’t stay mad — He’s so happy when we come back to Him.","ask":["What did David do when he realized he was wrong?","What does it mean to repent?"],"share":["Tell your children about a time you said a heartfelt “I’m sorry” — to a person or to Heavenly Father — and felt the relief of being forgiven.","How did you come to know that Heavenly Father always welcomes us back when we truly repent?","Share a time forgiveness — given or received — healed something in our family. Let your children see that repentance is a gift, not a punishment."]},"b6-w4-n3":{"story":["King David grew old, and his son Solomon became king. Solomon was young, with so many people to lead!","One night at Gibeon, the Lord came to Solomon in a dream. The Lord was Jesus. He said, “Ask what I shall give you.”","Solomon said, “I am like a little child. I don’t know how to lead. Please give me an understanding heart, to know good from bad.”","An understanding heart is a heart that knows what is right. Solomon’s wish pleased the Lord. He gave Solomon a wise heart. He gave him riches too, which he had not asked for.","Heavenly Father can give you a wise heart, just like Solomon. Tonight, ask Him to help you know what is right tomorrow."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Pretend a wish-granting genie just said you can have ANYTHING. What would you ask for? Maybe candy? A toy? A pony? Solomon could have asked for anything too — but he asked for WISDOM, the ability to make good choices! Tap your forehead three times. That’s your brain — wisdom helps your brain make good choices. When we ask Heavenly Father for things that help us be better, He’s so happy with us!","stones":[],"remember":"It’s okay to ask for help with your hopes and dreams. But the BEST things to pray for are things like Solomon asked for: wisdom, kindness, courage, patience, faith, and love. Heavenly Father loves it when we ask to be BETTER, not just to GET more stuff. The next time you pray, try asking Heavenly Father to help you be wise, kind, or brave!","ask":["What did Solomon ask Heavenly Father for?","Why did this please God so much?"],"share":["Tell your children about a time you prayed for something better than “stuff” — for wisdom, patience, or strength — and how Heavenly Father answered.","When did Heavenly Father give you wisdom you didn’t have on your own, right when you needed it most?","Share a piece of wisdom passed down to you from a parent or grandparent that still guides your choices today."]},"b6-w4-n4":{"story":["Two mothers came to King Solomon with one little baby boy. Each mother said, “This baby is mine!”","No one else had been in the house to see. So how could the king know who the real mother was?","Solomon gave them a wise test. It would show which mother loved the baby most. The baby stayed safe the whole time.","The real mother’s heart ached for her son. She cried, “Please give her the baby. Just keep him safe!” So Solomon gave the baby to the real mother. Everyone saw that Heavenly Father had made Solomon wise.","Tonight, give a hug to someone who loves you. Heavenly Father gave you people who love you, and He loves you too."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Hold an imaginary baby in your arms — gently, gently, like it’s the most precious thing! That’s how a real mommy holds her baby. The real mother loved her baby SO much that she’d give him up rather than see him hurt. That’s REAL love. Now think of the people you love most — your mommy, daddy, brothers and sisters. How much do you love them? Real love means wanting what’s best for them, even if it’s hard for you.","stones":[],"remember":"Solomon used the wisdom Heavenly Father gave him to find the truth. YOU can ask for wisdom too! When you have a hard problem to solve — like figuring out who took your toy, or what’s the right thing to do — pray to Heavenly Father. He gives wisdom to those who ask! Also, real love means wanting what’s best for someone, even when it’s hard. The real mother loved her baby more than herself.","ask":["How did Solomon know which mother was the real mother?","What does real love look like?"],"share":["Tell your children about a time you put what was best for someone you love ahead of what you wanted for yourself.","When have you felt Heavenly Father’s kind of love — love that gives, even when it’s hard — either from Him or for your own family?","Share a story of sacrificial love in our family’s history. Who loved someone that much, and how did it shape the family you have today?"]},"b6-w4-n5":{"story":["King Solomon built a temple, a house for the Lord. It would be a holy place, so it was built with care.","The big stones were cut and made ready somewhere else first. Then they were carried to the temple, ready to set in place.","So no one heard a hammer, an axe, or any iron tool there. The Lord’s house grew up quietly, stone by stone.","Inside, Solomon covered the walls with pure gold. He carved palm trees and open flowers. It took seven years to finish the Lord’s house! Today there are temples all over the world, holy houses of the Lord.","Tonight, be very still for a moment, like the quiet temple. Someday you can go to the Lord’s house too."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Pretend you’re holding the most precious, most beautiful thing in the world. Hold it gently. Look at it carefully. That’s how Solomon felt about the temple! He wanted EVERYTHING to be perfect for Heavenly Father. Now think about how special temples still are today. We have temples in our church! They’re beautiful places where families can be together forever. When you grow up, you can go to the temple too!","stones":[],"remember":"Solomon gave Heavenly Father his BEST work, his BEST materials, his BEST effort. YOU can give Heavenly Father your best too! You can do your best at school, give your best at chores, sing your best in Primary, behave your best in church, and pray your best prayers. Don’t just do “good enough” — try your very BEST for Heavenly Father! And someday, you can go to the temple — a special place even better than Solomon’s.","ask":["What was the temple like?","Why did Solomon want it to be so beautiful?"],"share":["Tell your children about a time you gave Heavenly Father your very best effort — not “good enough,” but your best — and how it felt.","When have you felt closest to Heavenly Father in a temple or holy place? What made it sacred to you?","Share what the temple has meant in our family across generations. Help your children see the blessing of giving God our best."]},"b6-w4-n6":{"story":["King Solomon stood before the people at the temple. He spread his hands toward heaven and prayed.","He said, “Lord, there is no one like You, in heaven or on earth. You keep Your promises.” Solomon knew even the whole sky could not hold the Lord.","He asked, “When Your people pray toward this house, hear them in heaven. And when You hear, forgive.” To forgive is to take a wrong away.","Solomon prayed on his knees, with hands up high. Then he stood and blessed the people. He said, “The Lord be with us. May He never leave us.”","Heavenly Father hears your prayers. Tonight, fold your arms and pray for people you love."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Get on your knees right where you are. Now spread out your arms toward the sky like Solomon did. Doesn’t that feel like you’re reaching up to Heavenly Father? When you pray like this — humble, with your heart open — Heavenly Father hears you. Solomon prayed not just for himself, but for everyone! You can pray for other people too. Who do you want to pray for tonight?","stones":[],"remember":"When YOU pray, Heavenly Father HEARS you! Just like Solomon prayed and the cloud came, your prayers are heard by God. You don’t have to be in a temple to pray. You can pray in your bedroom, in the car, at school, when you’re scared, when you’re happy — anywhere! And like Solomon, you can pray for OTHER people too: your family, your friends, people who are sick, people who are sad. Praying for others is one of the best things you can do!","ask":["What did Solomon ask Heavenly Father to do?","Does Heavenly Father hear OUR prayers?"],"share":["Tell your children about a specific prayer of yours that Heavenly Father answered — proof to you that He really hears.","How did you come to know, for yourself, that your prayers are heard?","Share a time you prayed for someone else and saw Heavenly Father answer. Let your children know they have been carried in your prayers, too."]},"b6-w4-n7":{"story":["When the temple was done, the Lord came to Solomon again. The Lord was Jesus. He said, “I have heard your prayer.”","He said, “I have made this house holy for My name forever. My eyes and My heart will always be there.”","He told Solomon to walk with Him with a good heart, like David. Then Solomon’s family would always have a king.","But when Solomon was old, he let his heart turn away from the Lord. That was a sad choice. Every day, we can choose to keep our hearts close to the Lord.","Tonight, put your hand on your heart. Jesus loves you, and He will help your heart stay close to Him."],"traveler":"","why":"","feel":"Put both hands over your heart. Feel it beating? That’s the heart Heavenly Father wants! He wants your WHOLE heart, all the time. Now imagine pulling something little out of your heart — that’s what happens when we let little bad things in. Solomon did that, little by little, until his heart was far from God. Push your hands back to your heart and squeeze tight. Keep your heart close to Heavenly Father always!","stones":[],"remember":"It’s not enough to love Heavenly Father just once. We have to choose Him EVERY day, our WHOLE life! Solomon was wise and built a beautiful temple, but later he stopped following God. Don’t be like older Solomon — be faithful your WHOLE life! You can keep your heart close to Heavenly Father by praying every day, going to church, reading scriptures, choosing the right, and remembering Him always. Even when you grow up, keep loving Heavenly Father!","ask":["What did Heavenly Father warn Solomon about?","How can we keep our hearts close t o God?"],"share":["Tell your children about a time in your life when staying close to Heavenly Father — every day — made all the difference.","When have you felt your heart begin to drift, and what brought you back to Him?","Share how a parent or grandparent kept their heart close to God their whole life. What did their steadiness teach you about staying faithful?"]},"b3-w1-n1":{"story":["Abraham and his wife, Sarah, lived in a tent. One hot afternoon, Abraham sat by his tent door.","He looked up and saw three men standing near him. They were holy visitors sent by the Lord. Abraham ran to meet them and bowed low.","“Please rest here under the tree,” Abraham said. “I will bring water to wash your feet, and some bread.”","Long ago, people walked far on hot, dusty roads. Cool water on tired feet felt so good! Abraham hurried to Sarah, and she made bread. He brought milk and good food. He stood by them under the tree while they ate.","You can be kind to tired people too. Tonight, think of one person you can help tomorrow, and smile."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow has a warm, giving heart, just like Abraham when he hurried to care for his tired guests. In Willow’s world today: A new friend came over to play. Willow shared her best toys and poured her friend a cup of water. Just like Abraham, Willow learned that being kind to guests shows Heavenly Father’s love.","feel":"Make your fingers run fast across your blanket, like Abraham hurrying to his guests. Now bow your head gently. Can you show me your kind, welcoming smile?","stones":[["SPOT","Abraham saw tired travelers, and he felt happy to help them."],["NAME","That warm, helping feeling is called kindness. Your mind might think, ‘How can I help?’ Your body might feel warm in your chest and quick in your feet."],["FIND","When have you helped someone feel welcome?"],["FORK","Abraham could keep sitting by his tent, or he could hurry to help. Abraham took the road of kindness."],["CALM","Hold your hands open like a gift. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me be kind tomorrow.”"]],"remember":"When we are kind to others, we show Heavenly Father’s love!","ask":["“Who came to visit Abraham?”","“What did Abraham bring for their tired feet?”","“What did Willow share with her friend?”"],"share":["When has someone welcomed you with great kindness?","How can our family help guests feel loved in our home?","Which grandparent or ancestor was known for welcoming others? Share a story about them."]},"b3-w1-n2":{"story":["The three visitors asked Abraham, “Where is Sarah?” Abraham said, “In the tent.”","Then the Lord said, “Sarah will have a baby boy.” Sarah was listening at the tent door. She and Abraham were very, very old. They were much too old for a baby, she thought.","So Sarah laughed quietly to herself. The Lord asked, “Why did Sarah laugh? Is any thing too hard for the Lord?”","Sarah was afraid, so she said, “I did not laugh.” But the Lord knew she did. The Lord was Jesus, speaking for Heavenly Father. He keeps every promise, and nothing is too hard for Him!","Is something hard for you? Tonight, tell the Lord about it. He can help with big things and little things."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren loves to wonder about big questions, like the one the Lord asked Sarah. In Wren’s world today: Wren held a tiny seed and looked up at a tall tree. “How can something so small grow so big?” she wondered. Just like Sarah, Wren learned that nothing is too hard for the Lord.","feel":"Put your hand over your mouth and giggle softly, like Sarah. Now open your arms wide, wide, wide. Can you show me how big the Lord’s power is?","stones":[["SPOT","Sarah heard surprising news, and she laughed because it seemed too hard to be true."],["NAME","That surprised, can-it-be-true feeling is called wonder. Your mind might think, ‘How could that happen?’ Your body might feel giggly, with wide eyes and raised eyebrows."],["FIND","When have you heard news so surprising that you giggled?"],["FORK","Sarah could think it was too hard, or she could trust the Lord. Sarah learned the Lord keeps His promises."],["CALM","Fold your hands under your chin. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, I know nothing is too hard for You.”"]],"remember":"Nothing is too hard for the Lord!","ask":["“Who laughed inside the tent?”","“What did the Lord say Sarah would have?”","“What did Wren wonder about the tiny seed?”"],"share":["When has the Lord helped you with something that felt too hard?","Which promise from Heavenly Father means the most to you?","Which grandparent or ancestor trusted the Lord in a hard time? Share their story."]},"b3-w1-n3":{"story":["The Lord told Abraham about a city called Sodom. The people there kept choosing very wrong things. Their city might be destroyed.","Abraham’s nephew Lot lived in Sodom. Abraham cared about the people there. He asked the Lord, “If fifty good people live there, will You save it?”","The Lord said, “If I find fifty good people, I will save the city.”","Abraham asked again and again. Forty-five? Forty? Thirty? Twenty? Ten? Each time the Lord said yes, He would save the city. The Lord was full of mercy, a great kindness that keeps giving another chance.","Abraham prayed for other people, and the Lord listened. Tonight, pray for someone you love. The Lord hears you."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau is brave enough to ask for big things, just like Abraham, who kept asking the Lord to save the city. In Beau’s world today: Beau’s grandpa was sick. At dinner, Beau bravely asked, “Can I pray for Grandpa?” He prayed, “Please bless Grandpa to feel better.” Just like Abraham, Beau learned that we can pray for others, and the Lord listens.","feel":"Hold up ten fingers, like the ten good people Abraham asked about. Now fold your hands to pray. Can you show me your brave, praying hands?","stones":[["SPOT","Abraham worried about the people in the city, and he felt brave enough to keep asking."],["NAME","That bold, caring, keep-asking feeling is called courage. Your mind might think, ‘I will ask again.’ Your body might feel tall and steady, with a strong heart."],["FIND","When have you bravely asked for help for someone else?"],["FORK","Abraham could stay quiet, or he could ask the Lord again and again. Abraham kept praying for the people."],["CALM","Fold your hands and take a slow breath. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, please bless someone who needs You.”"]],"remember":"We can pray for others, and the Lord is full of mercy!","ask":["“Who did Abraham pray for?”","“What did the Lord say each time Abraham asked?”","“Who did Beau pray for at dinner?”"],"share":["When has someone’s prayer for you blessed your life?","Who can our family pray for this week?","Which grandparent or ancestor prayed for our family? Share what you know about them."]},"b3-w1-n4":{"story":["Lot lived in a city where people chose very wrong things. Two angels from Heavenly Father came to help.","Early in the morning, the angels said, “Hurry! Take your wife and your two daughters.” But Lot was slow.","So the angels took Lot, his wife and his daughters by the hand. The Lord was being kind to them, and the angels led them out.","The angels said, “Run to the mountain! Do not look back.” Sadly, Lot’s wife looked back, and she became a tall post of salt. Lot and his daughters kept going, and the Lord kept them safe.","The Lord wants to lead you by the hand too. Tonight, hold Mom’s or Dad’s hand and think, “I will keep going with Jesus.”"],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau is brave and keeps going forward, just like Lot and his daughters when the angels led them to safety. In Beau’s world today: At a busy store, Beau’s dad said, “Hold my hand and stay close.” Beau wanted to run back to the toys. Instead, he held Dad’s hand and kept walking. Just like Lot and his daughters, Beau learned that it is safe to follow someone who loves you.","feel":"Reach out your hand like an angel is taking it. Now march your feet under the blanket, forward, forward. Can you show me how you keep going?","stones":[["SPOT","The angels led Lot’s family by the hand, and they felt hurried but cared for."],["NAME","That steady, hold-my-hand, keep-going feeling is called trust. Your mind might think, ‘I will follow.’ Your body might feel your hand held tight and your feet ready."],["FIND","When have you held someone’s hand and felt safe?"],["FORK","Lot’s family could look back, or they could keep going forward. Lot and his daughters kept going to safety."],["CALM","Hold your own hand softly. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me keep going with Jesus.”"]],"remember":"The Lord leads us by the hand, so we keep going forward!","ask":["“Who took Lot’s family by the hand?”","“What did the angels say not to do?”","“Whose hand did Beau hold at the store?”"],"share":["When has the Lord led you away from something that was not good for you?","What helps our family keep looking forward to Jesus?","Which grandparent or ancestor left something behind to follow the Lord? Share their story."]},"b3-w1-n5":{"story":["The Lord had made Abraham and Sarah a promise. They would have a baby boy, even though they were very, very old.","They waited many, many years. Then, just as the Lord said, Sarah had a baby boy! Abraham was one hundred years old.","Abraham named the baby Isaac. Sarah held her little son close.","Once, Sarah had laughed because a baby seemed too hard to be true. Now she laughed again, a happy laugh! She said, “Heavenly Father has made me laugh. Everyone who hears will laugh with me.” The Lord had kept His promise.","The Lord keeps His promises to you too. That is a happy thing! Tonight, give a little giggle and snuggle in."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren loves to think about how things come true, and tonight the Lord’s promise to Sarah came true. In Wren’s world today: Wren waited and waited for her baby cousin to be born. Every day she wondered, “Is it today?” Then the baby came! Wren held his tiny hand and laughed. Just like Sarah, Wren learned that waiting can end in great joy.","feel":"Rock your arms gently, like you are holding baby Isaac. Now give a soft, happy giggle. Can you show me Sarah’s joyful smile?","stones":[["SPOT","Sarah and Abraham finally held their baby boy, and they felt full of joy."],["NAME","That bubbly, bright, laughing feeling is called joy. Your mind might think, ‘It came true!’ Your body might feel giggly in your tummy, with a big smile."],["FIND","When have you felt so happy that you laughed out loud?"],["FORK","Sarah could stop hoping, or she could keep trusting the Lord. Sarah trusted, and the Lord kept His promise."],["CALM","Rock your arms slowly and breathe. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for happy surprises.”"]],"remember":"The Lord always keeps His promises!","ask":["“What was the baby’s name?”","“How old was Abraham when Isaac was born?”","“Whose tiny hand did Wren hold?”"],"share":["When has the Lord kept a promise to you, even after a long wait?","What makes our family laugh with joy together?","Which grandparent or ancestor waited a long time for a blessing? Share their story."]},"b3-w1-n6":{"story":["Hagar was Sarah’s helper. Her son Ishmael was Abraham’s son too. The Lord told Abraham to send them away, and promised to bless Ishmael.","Abraham gave them bread and water. They walked into a wide, dry land. Soon the water was all gone.","Hagar laid Ishmael in the shade of a bush. She sat down a little way off and cried.","Heavenly Father heard the boy. An angel called from heaven, “Do not be afraid, Hagar. Lift up your boy and hold his hand.” Then Heavenly Father opened her eyes. She saw a well full of water! Ishmael drank, and Heavenly Father stayed with him.","Heavenly Father hears you too. When you feel sad, tell Him. He knows what you need."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow has a tender heart for anyone who is sad, and tonight Heavenly Father heard a boy who was crying. In Willow’s world today: At the park, Willow heard a little boy crying. She knelt down and asked, “Are you okay?” Then she helped him find his mom. Just like Hagar, Willow learned that Heavenly Father sends helpers to those who are sad.","feel":"Cup your hands like a little bowl. Pretend to fill it at the well and take a slow, cool sip. Can you show me your happy, thankful face?","stones":[["SPOT","Hagar and Ishmael were thirsty and sad, then they felt comforted when they found water."],["NAME","That soft, safe, hugged-inside feeling is called comfort. Your mind might think, ‘Heavenly Father heard me.’ Your body might feel calm, with slow breaths and soft shoulders."],["FIND","When have you felt comforted after you were sad?"],["FORK","Hagar could give up, or she could listen to the angel. Hagar listened, lifted up her boy, and found water."],["CALM","Take a slow, cool breath. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for always hearing me.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father hears us, even when we cry!","ask":["“What did Hagar and Ishmael run out of?”","“What did Hagar see when her eyes were opened?”","“Who did Willow help at the park?”"],"share":["When have you felt Heavenly Father hear you in a hard moment?","How can our family help someone who feels alone?","Which grandparent or ancestor was comforted by Heavenly Father in a hard time? Share their story."]},"b3-w1-n7":{"story":["Heavenly Father asked Abraham to do something very hard. He asked him to offer his son Isaac on a mountain.","Abraham loved Isaac, but he trusted Heavenly Father. Isaac carried the wood up the mountain.","Isaac asked, “My father, here is the fire and the wood. But where is the lamb?” Abraham said, “Heavenly Father will give us a lamb.”","Abraham built an altar for the offering and laid the wood on it. Then an angel called, “Do not hurt the boy!” Abraham saw a ram caught by its horns in a bush. He offered the ram instead of Isaac.","This was a picture of something bigger. Heavenly Father would give His own Son, Jesus, for us. Tonight, whisper, “Thank You for Jesus.”"],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout loves to ask questions, just like Isaac, who asked, “Where is the lamb?” In Scout’s world today: At church, Scout whispered to his dad, “Why do we eat the bread and drink the water?” Dad said, “To remember that Heavenly Father gave us Jesus.” Just like Isaac, Scout learned that asking questions helps us learn about Heavenly Father’s love.","feel":"Pretend to carry a bundle of wood on your back, step, step, up the mountain. Now look up and wonder. Can you show me how Isaac carried the wood?","stones":[["SPOT","Abraham trusted Heavenly Father, and he felt thankful when the Lord gave a ram."],["NAME","That steady, quiet, believing feeling is called faith. Your mind might think, ‘Heavenly Father will help.’ Your body might feel calm and strong inside, like standing on a rock."],["FIND","When have you trusted Heavenly Father with something hard?"],["FORK","Abraham could say no, or he could trust Heavenly Father. Abraham trusted, and the Lord gave a ram."],["CALM","Put your hand on your heart. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for giving us Jesus.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father gave His Son, Jesus, because He loves us!","ask":["“What did Isaac carry up the mountain?”","“What did the Lord give instead of Isaac?”","“What did Scout ask his dad at church?”"],"share":["When have you felt how much Heavenly Father loves you through Jesus?","What helps you think of Jesus when we take the sacrament?","Which grandparent or ancestor showed great faith in Heavenly Father? Share a story about them."]},"b3-w2-n1":{"story":["Abraham wanted his son Isaac to marry a good woman from Abraham’s own family. So he sent his helper far away to find her.","The helper traveled with ten camels. One evening he stopped by a well, where women got water. He prayed, “Let the right girl offer water for my camels too.”","Before he finished praying, Rebekah came with a water jar on her shoulder.","The helper asked for a drink. Rebekah said, “Drink, my lord.” Then she said, “I will get water for your camels too.” She ran back to the well again and again. Ten thirsty camels drink lots of water!","Rebekah did a little extra. Tonight, think of one extra kind thing you can do tomorrow."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow has a warm, giving heart, just like Rebekah, who helped a tired traveler and his thirsty camels. In Willow’s world today: Willow saw her neighbor carrying heavy grocery bags. Willow carried one bag to the door. Then she went back for another one. Just like Rebekah, Willow learned that a kind heart does a little extra.","feel":"Pretend to hold a big water jar on your shoulder. Now pour, pour, pour for the thirsty camels! Can you show me your happy helping face?","stones":[["SPOT","Rebekah gave water to a tired man and all his camels, and she felt glad to help."],["NAME","That warm, happy-to-help feeling is called kindness. Your mind might think, ‘I can help!’ Your body might feel warm in your chest, with busy, happy hands."],["FIND","When have you felt happy helping someone?"],["FORK","Rebekah could give water to the man only, or she could help the camels too. She chose to do a little extra."],["CALM","Open and close your helping hands. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me be kind tomorrow.”"]],"remember":"Kind hearts help a little extra, just like Rebekah!","ask":["“What did Rebekah carry to the well?”","“Who else did Rebekah give water to?”","“What did Willow carry for her neighbor?”"],"share":["When has someone’s kindness felt like an answer to your prayer?","How can our family do a little extra for others this week?","Which grandparent or ancestor was known for helping others? Share a story about them."]},"b3-w2-n2":{"story":["Abraham’s helper told Rebekah’s family about his prayer at the well. Her family said, “This is from the Lord.”","But Isaac lived far, far away. Going meant leaving her home and her family. Her family asked her, “Will you go with this man?”","Rebekah said, “I will go.” That was a brave answer! She trusted that the Lord was leading her.","Rebekah rode a camel a long way. One evening she saw Isaac walking in a field. Isaac married Rebekah, and he loved her. He had been sad since his mother, Sarah, died. Now his heart felt comforted.","Heavenly Father helps us be brave in new places. Tonight, say softly, “I will go,” and think of something new to try."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau is brave, and Rebekah was brave when she said yes to a long trip to a new home. In Beau’s world today: Beau felt nervous on his first day at a new preschool. He held Dad’s hand, took a deep breath, and said, “I will go.” Soon he made a new friend. Just like Rebekah, Beau learned that Heavenly Father helps us be brave in new places.","feel":"Sit up tall like Rebekah riding a camel. Rock gently side to side, riding far away. Can you show me your brave, ready face?","stones":[["SPOT","Rebekah left home for a new place, and she felt brave and trusting."],["NAME","That strong, ready-to-try feeling is called courage. Your mind might think, ‘I can do new things!’ Your body might feel tall and steady, with a strong back."],["FIND","When have you felt brave going somewhere new?"],["FORK","Rebekah could stay home, or she could go to Isaac. She took the road of saying, “I will go.”"],["CALM","Take one big, brave breath. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me be brave like Rebekah.”"]],"remember":"I can be brave and go where Heavenly Father leads me!","ask":["“What did Rebekah say when her family asked her?”","“Who did Rebekah marry?”","“Where did Beau feel nervous?”"],"share":["When did you feel Heavenly Father leading you to a new place?","How can our family help each other be brave with new things?","Which grandparent or ancestor moved far away from home? Share their story."]},"b3-w2-n3":{"story":["Isaac and Rebekah wanted a baby, but no baby came. So Isaac prayed to the Lord for Rebekah, and the Lord heard him.","Soon two babies were growing inside Rebekah. They pushed and wiggled so much that she asked the Lord about it.","The Lord answered her. Then two baby brothers were born on the same day. They were twins!","The first baby was red and hairy all over. He was named Esau. The second baby came out holding Esau’s heel! He was named Jacob. Esau grew up loving to hunt in the fields. Jacob was quiet and stayed home by the tents.","Heavenly Father made each of us different. Tonight, say, “Heavenly Father made me just me.”"],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout loves to find out new things, and tonight he discovers how two twin brothers could be so different. In Scout’s world today: Scout met twins at the library. One twin loved loud dinosaur books. The other loved quiet puzzle books. Both were fun friends. Just like Isaac and Rebekah’s twin boys, Scout learned that Heavenly Father makes each person special.","feel":"Hold up two fingers like twin babies. Wiggle one fast like busy Esau. Hold one still like quiet Jacob. Can you show me your twin fingers?","stones":[["SPOT","Isaac and Rebekah prayed for a baby, and they felt happy when twins came."],["NAME","That bright, bouncy, thankful feeling is called joy. Your mind might think, ‘Our prayer was answered!’ Your body might feel light and smiley all over."],["FIND","When have you felt joy because a prayer was answered?"],["FORK","Isaac could stop hoping, or he could pray to the Lord. Isaac took the road of praying."],["CALM","Fold your hands softly. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for making me me.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father hears our prayers and makes each of us special!","ask":["“Who did Isaac pray for?”","“What was baby Jacob holding when he was born?”","“What did each twin love at the library?”"],"share":["When has Heavenly Father answered a prayer you prayed for a long time?","What makes each person in our family special?","Which grandparent or ancestor prayed hard for their family? Share what you know."]},"b3-w2-n4":{"story":["Jacob’s father sent him far away to find a wife. At sunset, Jacob lay down outside, with stones for his pillow.","Jacob dreamed of a ladder on the ground. Its top reached heaven! Heavenly Father’s angels went up and down it.","The Lord stood above it and said, “I am with you. I will keep you safe wherever you go. I will not leave you.”","Jacob woke up. “The Lord is in this place,” he said, “and I did not know it!” A ladder helps us climb step by step. So Jacob’s dream can help us think of Jesus. He helps us come back to Heavenly Father.","The Lord is close to you too. Lay your head down and whisper, “The Lord is here.”"],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren loves to wonder about big things, and tonight Jacob dreamed of a ladder reaching all the way to heaven. In Wren’s world today: Wren lay on the grass, looking up at the stars. She wondered, “Is heaven far away?” Mom said, “Heavenly Father is always near you.” Just like Jacob, Wren learned that the Lord is with us wherever we go.","feel":"Climb your hands up a pretend ladder, one step, two steps, three. Now float them down softly like angels. Can you show me your climbing hands?","stones":[["SPOT","Jacob dreamed of a ladder to heaven, and he felt wonder and peace."],["NAME","That quiet, wide-eyed, amazed feeling is called wonder. Your mind might think, ‘Heaven is close to me!’ Your body might feel still and soft, with a calm heart."],["FIND","When have you felt wonder about heaven?"],["FORK","Jacob could forget his dream, or he could remember the Lord was near. Jacob remembered: “The Lord is in this place.”"],["CALM","Lay your head softly on your pillow. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for being near me.”"]],"remember":"The Lord is with me wherever I go!","ask":["“What did Jacob use for a pillow?”","“Who went up and down the ladder?”","“What did Wren look at in the sky?”"],"share":["When have you felt the Lord with you in a hard or lonely place?","What small steps help our family come closer to Heavenly Father?","Which grandparent or ancestor felt the Lord near on a long journey? Share their story."]},"b3-w2-n5":{"story":["Jacob came to a well in a faraway land. A big, heavy stone covered it.","Rachel came, taking care of her father’s sheep. Jacob rolled the big stone away and gave her sheep a drink.","Rachel was from Jacob’s mother’s family. Jacob was so glad to find her that he kissed her hello and cried.","Jacob loved Rachel. He told her father, Laban, “I will work seven years to marry Rachel.” Seven years is a long, long time! But it felt like a few days, because Jacob loved her so much.","Love makes waiting and working feel happy. Tonight, think of someone you love and one thing you can do for them."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren likes to think about how things feel, and Jacob found that love made seven long years feel short. In Wren’s world today: Wren planted a seed in Grandma’s garden. She had to wait many days to see it sprout. Wren watered it every morning because she loved Grandma. Just like Jacob, Wren learned that love makes waiting feel shorter.","feel":"Pretend to roll a big, heavy stone. Push, push, push! Now wipe your forehead and smile. Can you show me your strong, loving push?","stones":[["SPOT","Jacob worked seven years for Rachel, and he felt happy because he loved her."],["NAME","That warm, full-heart, caring feeling is called love. Your mind might think, ‘I want to help you.’ Your body might feel warm and soft in your chest."],["FIND","When has love made you want to help someone?"],["FORK","Jacob could give up on the long wait, or he could keep working. Jacob kept working, because he loved Rachel."],["CALM","Hug your pillow softly. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me show love with my hands.”"]],"remember":"Love makes waiting and working feel happy!","ask":["“What did Jacob roll away from the well?”","“How many years did Jacob work for Rachel?”","“What did Wren water every morning?”"],"share":["When has love helped you wait for something good?","How do we show love in our family by working together?","How did your grandparents or ancestors meet and come to love each other? Share their story."]},"b3-w2-n6":{"story":["Jacob was going home with his big family. Long ago, he had been unfair to his brother, Esau. Now Esau was coming, and Jacob was afraid. He prayed for help.","One night, Jacob was alone. A man came and wrestled with him, pushing and pulling until morning.","The man said, “Let me go, for it is morning.” Jacob held on. He said, “I will not let you go until you bless me.”","The man asked, “What is your name?” He said, “Jacob.” The man said, “Now your name will be Israel.” Israel can mean “let Heavenly Father win,” choosing His way. Then the man blessed Jacob.","Tonight, hold your blanket tight like Jacob held on. You can choose Heavenly Father’s way too."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau is brave and strong, and Jacob bravely held on all night long for a blessing. In Beau’s world today: Beau wanted to play his own game, but his little sister was sad. Beau stopped and thought, “What would Heavenly Father want?” He let her choose. Just like Jacob, Beau learned that letting Heavenly Father lead brings happy blessings.","feel":"Grab your blanket with both hands and hold on tight, like Jacob. Now let your arms go soft. Can you show me your strong, holding-on hands?","stones":[["SPOT","Jacob held on all night for a blessing, and he felt brave and blessed."],["NAME","That steady, keep-holding-on feeling is called faith. Your mind might think, ‘Heavenly Father will help me!’ Your body might feel strong arms and a steady heart."],["FIND","When have you kept trying and not given up?"],["FORK","Jacob could let go, or he could hold on for a blessing. Jacob held on until morning."],["CALM","Squeeze your hands, then let them go soft. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, please lead me.”"]],"remember":"Like Israel, I can let Heavenly Father lead me!","ask":["“What did Jacob want from the man?”","“What new name did Jacob get?”","“Who did Beau let choose the game?”"],"share":["When did you hold on to Heavenly Father through something hard?","How can our family let Heavenly Father lead our choices?","Which grandparent or ancestor kept their faith through a hard time? Share their story."]},"b3-w2-n7":{"story":["Jacob saw Esau coming. Years before, Jacob had been unfair to Esau, and Esau had been very angry. Would Esau still be mad?","Jacob walked out in front of his family. He bowed down to the ground seven times. It showed Esau, “You matter to me.”","Then Esau ran to meet Jacob! He hugged him and kissed him. The two brothers cried together.","Jacob gave Esau a gift of animals. Esau said, “I have enough, my brother.” Jacob said Esau’s kind face was like seeing Heavenly Father’s face. Esau was not mad anymore, and he loved his brother again.","Jesus teaches us to forgive. Tonight, think of someone you can forgive, and give your pillow a big hug."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow has a warm, loving heart, and tonight two brothers found their way back to love. In Willow’s world today: Willow’s brother knocked over her block tower. Willow felt mad. Then he said, “I’m sorry,” and Willow gave him a big hug. They built a new tower together. Just like Esau, Willow learned that forgiving makes hearts happy again.","feel":"Open your arms wide like Esau running to Jacob. Now wrap them around yourself in a big hug. Can you show me your forgiving hug?","stones":[["SPOT","Esau ran to hug Jacob, and the brothers felt happy and loved again."],["NAME","That soft, let-it-go, loving feeling is called forgiveness. Your mind might think, ‘I am not mad anymore.’ Your body might feel light, like a heavy bag set down."],["FIND","When have you forgiven someone and hugged them?"],["FORK","Esau could stay angry, or he could forgive. Esau took the road of running to hug his brother."],["CALM","Breathe out slowly and let your shoulders drop. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me forgive like Jesus.”"]],"remember":"Forgiving makes families happy again, just like Esau and Jacob!","ask":["“How many times did Jacob bow down?”","“What did Esau do when he saw Jacob?”","“What did Willow’s brother knock over?”"],"share":["When has forgiving someone brought peace into your heart?","How can our family forgive each other more quickly?","Which grandparent or ancestor helped mend a family hurt? Share what happened."]},"b3-w3-n1":{"story":["Joseph was seventeen years old. He helped his big brothers take care of their father’s sheep.","Their father was Jacob. Jacob loved Joseph more than his other sons. Joseph was born when Jacob was old.","Jacob made Joseph a coat of many colors. It was a special gift that showed Jacob’s love.","Joseph’s brothers saw the coat. They saw their father loved Joseph most. A hot, grumpy feeling grew inside them, called jealous. They could not say kind words to Joseph anymore.","Heavenly Father and Jesus love each one of us with all Their hearts. No one is left out. If a grumpy feeling comes, take a breath and say a kind word."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren likes to stop and think about feelings, and tonight Joseph’s brothers had a big one. In Wren’s world today: Wren’s little brother got a new red ball. Wren felt hot and grumpy inside. She stopped, took a breath, and asked, “Can we play together?” Just like Joseph’s brothers needed to learn, Wren learned that kind words can help a jealous feeling grow small.","feel":"Put your hands on your tummy. Breathe in slowly. Now breathe out and let a grumpy feeling float away like a balloon. Can you show me your calm, kind face?","stones":[["SPOT","Jacob gave Joseph a special coat, and Joseph’s brothers felt jealous."],["NAME","That hot, grumpy, I-want-it feeling is called jealousy. Your mind might think, ‘That is not fair!’ Your body might feel tight in your tummy and hot in your face."],["FIND","When have you felt jealous of someone?"],["FORK","The brothers could speak kindly, or stay grumpy. Sadly, they stayed grumpy. We can choose kind words."],["CALM","Hug your pillow gently. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me be kind when I feel jealous.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father loves every one of His children!","ask":["“What did Jacob make for Joseph?”","“How did Joseph’s brothers feel?”","“What did Wren ask her little brother?”"],"share":["When have you felt Heavenly Father’s love just for you?","How do you help each child in our family feel loved?","Which grandparent or ancestor made you feel especially loved? Share a story about them."]},"b3-w3-n2":{"story":["One night Joseph had a dream. He told his brothers, “We were tying bundles of grain in the field.”","“My bundle stood up tall. Your bundles bent low around it, like they were bowing to mine.”","Then Joseph had another dream. The sun, the moon, and eleven stars bowed down to him.","His brothers were upset. Jacob asked, “Will your family really bow down to you?” But Jacob kept thinking about the dream. Many years later, his brothers really did bow to Joseph!","Heavenly Father knows what will happen, even far ahead. Tonight, look for the moon and stars Jesus made for Heavenly Father. Wonder about them, like Jacob wondered."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout loves to ask questions, and Joseph’s dreams made everyone ask, “What does it mean?” In Scout’s world today: Scout saw the moon shining in his window. He asked his dad, “Who made the moon?” Dad smiled and said, “Jesus made it for Heavenly Father.” Just like Jacob, Scout learned that it is good to wonder and think about big things.","feel":"Lift your hands high like the moon and stars. Now bend them down softly, like a gentle bow. Can you show me your gentle bow?","stones":[["SPOT","Joseph had two amazing dreams. His brothers felt upset, but Jacob wondered."],["NAME","That quiet, thinking, curious feeling is called wonder. Your mind might think, ‘What could it mean?’ Your body might feel still and soft, with your eyes looking up."],["FIND","When have you wondered about something big?"],["FORK","Jacob could forget the dream, or keep thinking about it. Jacob chose to keep thinking and wondering."],["CALM","Look up at your ceiling like a starry sky. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, please help me learn from You.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father knows what will happen, and He can teach us too!","ask":["“What bowed down in Joseph’s first dream?”","“What bowed down in Joseph’s second dream?”","“What did Scout see in his window?”"],"share":["When has Heavenly Father taught you through a dream or a quiet thought?","What things from the scriptures do you keep thinking about, like Jacob did?","Which grandparent or ancestor had a dream or feeling that guided our family? Share their story."]},"b3-w3-n3":{"story":["Jacob said, “Joseph, please go see if your brothers and the sheep are well.” Joseph said, “Here I am,” and he went.","But his brothers were still jealous. When Joseph came, they took off his coat of many colors. They put him in an empty hole with no water.","Then some men came by with camels, going to Egypt.","The brothers sold Joseph to those men for twenty pieces of silver. The men took Joseph far away to Egypt. Joseph was far from home, but he was not alone. Heavenly Father was watching over him.","Jesus knows how it feels when people are not kind. Even when you feel lonely, He stays close. Tonight, say a little prayer and feel Him near."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau is brave, and Joseph needed a brave heart when he had to go far from home. In Beau’s world today: Beau slept at his cousin’s house for the very first time. His tummy felt wobbly so far from home. Beau took a deep breath and said a little prayer. Just like Joseph, Beau learned that he is never alone, because Heavenly Father is with him.","feel":"Make your arms strong and still, like a tall tree. Take one big, brave breath in and out. Can you show me your brave face?","stones":[["SPOT","Joseph was taken far from home, and he felt very sad and alone."],["NAME","That small, far-from-home feeling is called loneliness. Your mind might think, ‘I miss my family.’ Your body might feel heavy in your chest and wobbly in your tummy."],["FIND","When have you felt far from home or alone?"],["FORK","Joseph could give up, or keep doing good. Joseph kept doing good, and the Lord was with him."],["CALM","Hold your hands together tight. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for always being with me.”"]],"remember":"Even far from home, Heavenly Father is with you!","ask":["“What did Joseph’s brothers take from him?”","“Where did the traders take Joseph?”","“What did Beau do at his cousin’s house?”"],"share":["When have you felt far from home but still close to Heavenly Father?","How has Jesus comforted you when others were unkind?","Which grandparent or ancestor moved far from home? Share how the Lord helped them."]},"b3-w3-n4":{"story":["In Egypt, a man named Potiphar bought Joseph. Joseph had to work in Potiphar’s house.","Joseph worked hard. The Lord was with Joseph and helped everything he did go well.","Potiphar saw that the Lord was with Joseph. So Potiphar put Joseph in charge of his whole house and fields.","Then someone said Joseph did a bad thing. It was not true. Joseph was put in a locked prison. But the Lord was still with Joseph and was kind to him. Soon the man in charge of the prison put Joseph in charge there too!","The Lord is Jesus, and He is with you too. When things are not fair, He can help you keep doing good. Tonight, whisper, “Thank You for being with me.”"],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau keeps going when things are hard, just like Joseph kept doing good in Egypt. In Beau’s world today: Beau’s block tower fell, and his friend said it was Beau’s fault. It was not. Beau felt sad, but he kept helping clean up. Just like Joseph, Beau learned that he can keep doing good, even when things are not fair.","feel":"Make your arms strong and lift a pretend basket up high. Hold it steady, steady. Can you show me your strong, steady arms?","stones":[["SPOT","Joseph was blamed for something he did not do, but he kept doing good."],["NAME","That steady, keep-on-going feeling is called being faithful. Your mind might think, ‘I will keep doing good.’ Your body might feel strong in your arms and calm in your chest."],["FIND","When have you kept doing good when something felt unfair?"],["FORK","Joseph could get grumpy and stop trying, or keep doing good. Joseph kept doing good, and the Lord blessed him."],["CALM","Press your feet down softly, strong and steady. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me keep doing good.”"]],"remember":"The Lord was with Joseph, and the Lord is with you too!","ask":["“Who was with Joseph in Egypt?”","“Who put Joseph in charge of his house?”","“What did Beau do when his tower fell?”"],"share":["When has the Lord been with you in a hard or unfair time?","How have you seen the Lord bless the work of your hands?","Which grandparent or ancestor kept doing good in hard times? Share their story."]},"b3-w3-n5":{"story":["The king of Egypt was angry with two of his helpers. One brought the king his drinks. One was his baker. They were put in Joseph’s prison.","Joseph took care of them. One morning he looked at them and saw sad faces. He asked, “Why do you look so sad today?”","They said, “We had dreams, and no one can tell us what they mean.”","Joseph said, “Heavenly Father knows what dreams mean. Please tell me.” The drink helper told his dream. Joseph said that in three days, he would serve the king again. And he did!","Jesus notices when we are sad, just like Joseph did. Tonight, think of someone who looked sad today. Tomorrow, you can be kind to them."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow has a warm heart that notices sad faces, just like Joseph did in the prison. In Willow’s world today: At preschool, Willow saw a girl sitting alone with a sad face. Willow sat down beside her and shared her crayons. Soon the girl smiled. Just like Joseph, Willow learned that noticing a sad face is a way to show love.","feel":"Make a sad face, then a happy face. Now gently pat your blanket like a kind friend. Can you show me your kind, caring face?","stones":[["SPOT","The king’s helpers felt sad, and Joseph noticed and cared about them."],["NAME","That soft, caring, I-want-to-help feeling is called compassion. Your mind might think, ‘How can I help?’ Your body might feel warm in your heart and gentle in your hands."],["FIND","When have you noticed someone feeling sad?"],["FORK","Joseph could think only of his own trouble, or help others. Joseph chose to notice and help."],["CALM","Put your hand on your heart. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me notice who needs a friend.”"]],"remember":"When we notice sad faces and help, we are like Jesus!","ask":["“How did the king’s helpers feel?”","“Who did Joseph say knows what dreams mean?”","“What did Willow share at preschool?”"],"share":["When has someone noticed you were sad and helped you?","How has Heavenly Father helped you understand something you could not figure out alone?","Which grandparent or ancestor always noticed people in need? Share a story about them."]},"b3-w3-n6":{"story":["Pharaoh was the king of Egypt. One night he dreamed of seven fat cows by the river. Then seven skinny cows came and ate them up!","He dreamed again. Seven full heads of grain grew on one stalk. Seven thin ones swallowed them up.","Pharaoh woke up worried. No wise man knew what it meant. The drink helper remembered Joseph, so Pharaoh called for him.","Pharaoh said, “I hear you can tell what dreams mean.” Joseph said, “It is not me. Heavenly Father will give Pharaoh a good answer.” Joseph said seven years of lots of food would come. Then seven hungry years would follow.","Joseph gave the honor to Heavenly Father. Tonight, think of something you do well. Thank Heavenly Father for helping you."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren likes to think deep thoughts, and tonight Joseph knew that wisdom comes from Heavenly Father. In Wren’s world today: Wren finished a hard puzzle. Her grandpa said, “You are so smart!” Wren smiled and said, “Heavenly Father helped me think.” Just like Joseph, Wren learned that our good gifts come from Heavenly Father.","feel":"Hold up seven fingers. Puff your cheeks out for the fat cows. Now suck them in for the thin cows. Can you show me your thin-cow face?","stones":[["SPOT","Pharaoh had strange dreams and felt worried, and Joseph gently helped him."],["NAME","That soft, thankful, not-bragging feeling is called humility. Your mind might think, ‘Heavenly Father helped me.’ Your body might feel calm and warm inside."],["FIND","When have you said thank you for help instead of bragging?"],["FORK","Joseph could say, “I am so smart,” or give the honor to Heavenly Father. Joseph honored Heavenly Father."],["CALM","Fold your arms softly. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for every good gift.”"]],"remember":"Our good gifts come from Heavenly Father!","ask":["“What animals did Pharaoh dream about?”","“Who did Joseph say would give the answer?”","“Who did Wren say helped her think?”"],"share":["When have you felt Heavenly Father help you with a gift or talent?","How can our family remember to thank Heavenly Father for our blessings?","Which grandparent or ancestor used their talents to serve the Lord? Share their story."]},"b3-w3-n7":{"story":["Pharaoh asked, “Can we find another man like this, who has Heavenly Father’s Spirit?” So Pharaoh put Joseph in charge of all Egypt.","For seven years the fields grew lots and lots of food. Joseph gathered the grain and saved it in every city.","There was so much grain, it was like the sand by the sea. No one could count it!","Then seven hungry years came, when food would not grow. But Egypt had bread! Joseph opened the big rooms full of grain. People from many lands came to Joseph for food.","Joseph got ready ahead of time, and many people were fed. Heavenly Father takes care of you every day. Tonight, help get something ready for tomorrow."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout loves to search and find, and Joseph found a way to save food for hungry people. In Scout’s world today: Scout and his mom picked lots of apples. Scout said, “Let’s save some for winter!” They put the apples away together. Just like Joseph, Scout learned that getting ready ahead of time can help many people.","feel":"Pretend to scoop grain with both hands and pile it up high. Scoop, scoop, pile! Can you show me how much grain Joseph saved?","stones":[["SPOT","Joseph saved food in the good years, and the hungry people felt cared for."],["NAME","That cozy, ready, all-taken-care-of feeling is called being prepared. Your mind might think, ‘We will be okay.’ Your body might feel calm and full, like after a warm dinner."],["FIND","When have you been glad you got ready ahead of time?"],["FORK","Joseph could let the food be eaten up, or save some for later. Joseph saved, and many people were fed."],["CALM","Rest your hands on your tummy. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for my food and my family.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father helps us get ready, and He takes care of us!","ask":["“Where did Joseph save the grain?”","“Who came to Joseph for food?”","“What did Scout save for winter?”"],"share":["How has the Lord helped our family get ready for hard times?","When have you seen Heavenly Father provide for our family?","Which grandparent or ancestor saved and shared in hard times? Share their story."]},"b3-w4-n1":{"story":["Far away, where Jacob lived, no food would grow. Jacob’s big family was hungry.","Jacob heard there was grain in Egypt. He told his sons, “Go and buy food, so we may live.”","Ten brothers went. Jacob did not send Benjamin, the youngest. He was afraid something might happen to him.","The ruler selling the grain was their brother Joseph! Long ago, they had sold him away. Now they bowed low to him. Joseph knew them, but they did not know him. Joseph remembered his dreams about them bowing.","Heavenly Father was helping this family all along. When something is hard, ask Him for help tonight. He is helping you."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren likes to wonder about big things, just like Joseph did when he remembered his dream from long ago. In Wren’s world today: Wren watched a caterpillar wrap itself in a cozy cocoon. Every day she waited and wondered. One morning, a butterfly came out! Just like Joseph, Wren learned that Heavenly Father’s plans can take time, but they come true.","feel":"Swing your arms slowly, like a brother walking a long road. Now bow down low, like the brothers bowed to Joseph. Can you show me your gentle bow?","stones":[["SPOT","The family was hungry and worried. Joseph was surprised to see his brothers again."],["NAME","That wide-eyed, oh-my feeling is called surprise. Your mind might think, ‘I did not expect that!’ Your body might feel jumpy, with big eyes and an open mouth."],["FIND","When have you been surprised to see someone?"],["FORK","The family could give up, or they could look for help. Jacob sent his sons to Egypt to find food."],["CALM","Rest your hands on your tummy and breathe in slowly. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for my food.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father was helping Joseph’s family all along!","ask":["“Why did the brothers go to Egypt?”","“Who was the ruler selling the grain?”","“What came out of the cocoon?”"],"share":["When did you see Heavenly Father’s help only later, looking back?","How can our family get ready for hard times?","Which grandparent or ancestor lived through a time without enough food? Share their story."]},"b3-w4-n2":{"story":["Soon the food from Egypt was all gone. But the ruler had said, “Do not come back without your youngest brother.”","Jacob did not want Benjamin to go. Judah said, “I will take care of him. If I do not bring him back, blame me.” So Jacob let Benjamin go.","Jacob sent gifts of honey, nuts, and almonds. He prayed the ruler would be kind to them.","In Egypt, Joseph saw Benjamin, his little brother with the same mother. Joseph said, “Heavenly Father be kind to you, my son.” Then he hurried to his room and cried. He loved his brother so much.","Jacob trusted Heavenly Father with someone he loved. Tonight, pray for someone you love. Heavenly Father loves them too."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau is brave, and Jacob was brave when he trusted Heavenly Father and let Benjamin go. In Beau’s world today: Beau’s dad went away on a trip. Beau missed him and felt worried, so he prayed for Dad every night. Then Dad came home with a big hug! Just like Jacob, Beau learned that we can trust Heavenly Father with the people we love.","feel":"Hold your hands together like you are holding something special. Now open them slowly and let it go. Can you show me your brave, trusting hands?","stones":[["SPOT","Jacob was worried to let Benjamin go. Joseph felt so much love he cried."],["NAME","That calm, safe, leaning-in feeling is called trust. Your mind might think, ‘Heavenly Father will help.’ Your body might feel your shoulders drop and your breath slow down."],["FIND","When have you trusted someone to keep you safe?"],["FORK","Jacob could keep Benjamin home while the family went hungry, or he could trust Heavenly Father. Jacob chose to trust and pray."],["CALM","Hug your pillow gently. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, please keep my family safe tonight.”"]],"remember":"We can trust Heavenly Father with the people we love!","ask":["“Who promised to take care of Benjamin?”","“What did Joseph do when he saw Benjamin?”","“What did Beau do when he felt worried?”"],"share":["When did you trust Heavenly Father with someone you love?","How has someone in our family kept a promise to you?","Which grandparent or ancestor prayed for their family in a hard time? Share their story."]},"b3-w4-n3":{"story":["The brothers were ready to go home. Joseph told his helper to put his silver cup in Benjamin’s sack of grain.","On the road, the helper caught up to them. He looked in every sack. The cup was in Benjamin’s sack!","The brothers tore their clothes. People did that then when they felt very sad. They all went back to Joseph.","Judah came close. He said, “Please let me stay and work for you instead of the boy. Let him go home to our father.” Judah loved Benjamin and his father that much. Judah’s love can help us think of Jesus, who gave Himself for us.","Jesus loves you that much! Tonight, think of one way to help someone in your family tomorrow."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau is brave, and Judah was brave when he offered to stay so Benjamin could go home. In Beau’s world today: At the park, a little boy got scared at the top of the slide and began to cry. Beau climbed up, sat beside him, and slid down with him. Just like Judah, Beau learned that brave love stays close to someone who needs help.","feel":"Sit up tall like Judah and put your hand on your chest. Say softly, “I will help.” Can you show me your brave, kind face?","stones":[["SPOT","The brothers were scared and sad. Judah loved Benjamin and his father very much."],["NAME","That strong, steady, I-will-help feeling is called courage. Your mind might think, ‘I can help my brother.’ Your body might feel tall and strong, with a steady heart."],["FIND","When have you been brave to help someone else?"],["FORK","Judah could go home and leave Benjamin, or he could offer to stay. Judah chose to take Benjamin’s place."],["CALM","Hold your own hand and give it a soft squeeze. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me love like Jesus.”"]],"remember":"Judah offered to take Benjamin’s place, and Jesus gave Himself for us!","ask":["“Where was the silver cup found?”","“What did Judah offer to do?”","“How did Beau help the little boy?”"],"share":["When has someone stood up for you in a hard moment?","What does it mean to you that Jesus gave Himself for us?","Which grandparent or ancestor gave up something for the family? Share their story."]},"b3-w4-n4":{"story":["Joseph could not hold in his feelings anymore. He sent everyone else out. He cried so loud that people outside heard.","Joseph said, “I am Joseph! Is my father still alive?” His brothers were so surprised and afraid, they could not answer.","Joseph said, “Come close to me, please. I am Joseph your brother, the one you sold into Egypt.”","He said, “Do not be sad or angry with yourselves. Heavenly Father sent me here first to save lives.” Joseph hugged Benjamin, and they both cried. He kissed all his brothers. Joseph forgave them, letting go of the hurt and loving them again.","Jesus helps us forgive too. When someone makes you sad, ask Him tonight to help your heart feel kind."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow has a warm, gentle heart, just like Joseph, who hugged his brothers and forgave them. In Willow’s world today: Willow’s friend said a mean word, and Willow felt sad. Later her friend said, “I’m sorry.” Willow gave her a hug and said, “I forgive you. Let’s play!” Just like Joseph, Willow learned that forgiving makes our hearts feel warm and light.","feel":"Open your arms wide like Joseph. Now wrap them around yourself in a big, warm hug. Can you show me your forgiving hug?","stones":[["SPOT","The brothers felt afraid and sad. Joseph felt love and wanted them close."],["NAME","That warm, light, letting-go feeling is called forgiveness. Your mind might think, ‘I still love you.’ Your body might feel soft and warm, with open arms."],["FIND","When have you forgiven someone who hurt your feelings?"],["FORK","Joseph could stay angry, or he could forgive. Joseph forgave his brothers and hugged them close."],["CALM","Hug your blanket close. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me forgive like Joseph.”"]],"remember":"Joseph forgave his brothers, and Jesus helps us forgive too!","ask":["“What did Joseph tell his brothers?”","“Which brother did Joseph hug and cry with?”","“What did Willow say to her friend?”"],"share":["When has forgiving someone brought you peace?","How has Jesus helped you let go of a hurt?","Which grandparent or ancestor was known for a forgiving heart? Share a story about them."]},"b3-w4-n5":{"story":["The brothers told Jacob, “Joseph is alive! He is a ruler in Egypt!” At first, Jacob could not believe it.","Then Jacob saw the wagons Joseph sent to carry him. His heart felt happy again. He said, “Joseph is alive. I will go and see him.”","On the way, Heavenly Father spoke to Jacob in the night. He said, “Do not be afraid to go to Egypt. I will go with you.”","Joseph rode out in his chariot to meet his father. He hugged Jacob and cried a long time. Jacob said, “I have seen your face. You are still alive!”","Heavenly Father goes with you too. Tonight, think of a place you will go tomorrow. He will be with you there."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout loves going on adventures, and Jacob took a long trip to find his son. In Scout’s world today: Scout rode in the car a long way to visit Grandma. He watched out the window and wondered, “Are we almost there?” Then Grandma ran out for a hug! Just like Jacob, Scout learned that Heavenly Father is with us on every trip.","feel":"Rock side to side like you are riding in a bumpy wagon. Now stop and reach out for a big hug. Can you show me your happy hug?","stones":[["SPOT","Jacob traveled far, and he felt so happy to see Joseph alive."],["NAME","That bright, bubbly, so-glad feeling is called joy. Your mind might think, ‘You are here!’ Your body might feel warm and bouncy, with a great big smile."],["FIND","When have you felt joy seeing someone you love?"],["FORK","Jacob could stay home afraid, or he could trust and go. Jacob went, and Heavenly Father went with him."],["CALM","Wrap your arms around yourself. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for going with me.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father goes with us wherever we go!","ask":["“What did Joseph send to carry Jacob?”","“What did Joseph do when he saw his father?”","“Who ran out to hug Scout?”"],"share":["When have you felt Heavenly Father with you on a hard journey?","What happy family reunion do you remember most?","Which grandparent or ancestor moved far away to a new place? Share their story."]},"b3-w4-n6":{"story":["Jacob was very old, and his eyes could not see well. Joseph brought his two sons to him. Jacob sat up in his bed.","Jacob asked, “Who are these?” Joseph said, “My sons, whom Heavenly Father gave me.” Jacob said, “Bring them to me. I will bless them.”","Jacob kissed and hugged them. He said, “I did not think I would see your face. Now Heavenly Father lets me see your children too!”","Jacob put his hands on the boys’ heads. He asked Heavenly Father to give them good things. That is a blessing. Jacob said Heavenly Father had fed him all his life long.","Tonight, think of the people who love you. Heavenly Father loves your family and wants to bless each one."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow has a tender heart, and Jacob tenderly hugged his grandsons and blessed them. In Willow’s world today: Willow sat on Grandpa’s lap. He put his arm around her and said, “I am so glad you are in our family.” Willow felt warm and loved. Just like Jacob’s grandsons, Willow learned that family love is a gift from Heavenly Father.","feel":"Place your hands gently on top of your head. Now pat softly, like a loving blessing. Can you show me your gentle, loving hands?","stones":[["SPOT","Jacob was so happy to see his grandsons. They were loved by their grandpa."],["NAME","That warm, safe, held-close feeling is called being loved. Your mind might think, ‘My family loves me.’ Your body might feel cozy and calm, like a snug hug."],["FIND","When have you felt loved by someone in your family?"],["FORK","Jacob could just rest in his bed, or he could bless his grandsons. Jacob sat up and blessed them."],["CALM","Snuggle into your pillow. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for taking care of me.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father takes care of us all our life long!","ask":["“Who did Joseph bring to see Jacob?”","“What did Jacob do for his grandsons?”","“What did Grandpa tell Willow?”"],"share":["How has Heavenly Father taken care of you all your life long?","What do you pray for most when you pray for our children?","Which grandparent or ancestor blessed or prayed for you? Share that memory."]},"b3-w4-n7":{"story":["Jacob grew old and died, and his family missed him. Joseph’s brothers were afraid. They thought, “Now Joseph may pay us back for the wrong we did.”","They sent a message: “Please forgive us.” Joseph cried. The brothers bowed and said, “We will work for you.”","Joseph said, “Do not be afraid. You meant to hurt me. But Heavenly Father meant it for good, to save many people.”","Heavenly Father made good come from a sad, hard thing. Joseph said, “I will take care of you and your little ones.” He spoke kindly to his brothers.","Heavenly Father can make good come from hard things in your life too. Jesus can help you forgive, just like Joseph. Tonight, whisper, “I trust You.”"],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren likes to wonder about big things, like how Heavenly Father can make good come from hard things. In Wren’s world today: Rain spoiled Wren’s picnic. She felt sad and wondered why. Then a rainbow came out, and there were puddles to jump in! Just like Joseph, Wren learned that Heavenly Father can bring good from hard days.","feel":"Squeeze your hands into tight little fists, like a hard day. Now open them slowly, like a flower. Can you show me hard things turning to good?","stones":[["SPOT","The brothers were afraid. Joseph felt love and spoke kindly to them."],["NAME","That calm, quiet, it-will-be-okay feeling is called hope. Your mind might think, ‘Heavenly Father can make this good.’ Your body might feel calm, with slow, easy breaths."],["FIND","When did a hard day turn into something good?"],["FORK","Joseph could pay his brothers back, or he could forgive. Joseph forgave them and took care of them."],["CALM","Lay your open hands on your blanket. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for bringing good from hard things.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father can turn hard things into good!","ask":["“Why were the brothers afraid?”","“What did Joseph say Heavenly Father meant?”","“What came out after the rain?”"],"share":["When has Heavenly Father brought good from a hard time in your life?","Who might our family need to forgive and speak kindly to?","Which grandparent or ancestor found good after a hard time? Share their story."]},"b3-w5-n1":{"story":["Long ago, Jacob’s big family lived in Egypt. Jacob was also called Israel, so they were the Israelites. They grew and grew!","Then a new king called Pharaoh ruled Egypt. He did not know Joseph, who had once helped Egypt.","Pharaoh saw so many Israelites, and he was afraid. So he made them work very hard. They made bricks all day and built big cities for him.","The hard work made them tired and sad. But their families kept growing. Heavenly Father had not forgotten His people. They cried out to Him, and He heard them.","Heavenly Father sees tired people, and He wants us to see them too. Tonight, think of someone who works hard for you. Give them a big hug."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow has a tender heart, and tonight’s story is about people who were tired and sad. In Willow’s world today: Willow’s dad came home tired from work. Willow brought him a cup of water and sat close beside him. Just like the Israelites, Willow learned that Heavenly Father sees tired people, and He wants us to notice them too.","feel":"Pretend to carry a heavy brick. Oof, so heavy! Now set it down and let your arms rest. Can you show me your restful, quiet face?","stones":[["SPOT","The Israelites worked very hard and felt tired and sad, but Heavenly Father saw them."],["NAME","That heavy, droopy, worn-out feeling is called tired. Your mind might think, ‘This is so hard.’ Your body might feel slow and heavy in your arms and legs."],["FIND","When have you felt very tired after something hard?"],["FORK","The Israelites could feel all alone, or call out to Heavenly Father. They cried out to Him, and He heard them."],["CALM","Let your arms go soft and floppy. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for seeing me when I am tired.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father sees me on happy days and hard days!","ask":["“What did the new king make the Israelites do?”","“Did Heavenly Father forget His people?”","“What did Willow bring her tired dad?”"],"share":["When have you felt Heavenly Father see you during a hard time?","How has hard work helped our family grow stronger?","Which grandparent or ancestor worked very hard for their family? Share a story about them."]},"b3-w5-n2":{"story":["In Egypt lived two Israelite women named Shiphrah and Puah. They were midwives, who helped mothers when new babies were born.","The king of Egypt was afraid of the Israelites. He made a cruel rule against their baby boys. He told Shiphrah and Puah to obey it.","But Shiphrah and Puah loved Heavenly Father more than they feared the king. They would not obey the cruel rule. They kept the baby boys safe!","Heavenly Father was good to Shiphrah and Puah. He blessed them with families of their own. And the Israelite families kept growing.","Choosing what is right takes a brave heart, even when others choose wrong. Tonight, make a strong arm and say, “I can choose the right!”"],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau is brave, and tonight two brave women chose what was right. In Beau’s world today: At recess, some kids did not want a new girl in their game. Beau smiled and said, “She can play with us!” Just like Shiphrah and Puah, Beau learned that brave hearts choose what is right.","feel":"Sit up tall and make two strong arms. Now open your hands gently, like holding a tiny baby. Can you show me your brave, gentle hands?","stones":[["SPOT","The midwives chose Heavenly Father, not the king’s rule. They felt brave and strong."],["NAME","That strong, steady, standing-tall feeling is called courage. Your mind might think, ‘I will do what is right.’ Your body might feel tall, with a strong, steady heart."],["FIND","When have you been brave and chosen the right thing?"],["FORK","The midwives could obey the king’s cruel rule, or obey Heavenly Father. They obeyed Heavenly Father and kept the babies safe."],["CALM","Squeeze your hands, then let them go soft. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me be brave like Shiphrah and Puah.”"]],"remember":"I can be brave and choose the right, just like Shiphrah and Puah!","ask":["“What were the names of the two brave midwives?”","“What did the midwives do for the babies?”","“What did Beau say about the new girl?”"],"share":["When have you had to choose the right when it was hard?","Who has helped you feel brave enough to follow Heavenly Father?","Which grandparent or ancestor stood up for what was right? Share their story."]},"b3-w5-n3":{"story":["An Israelite mother had a beautiful baby boy. But the king’s cruel rule made Egypt unsafe for baby boys.","So she hid him for three months. When she could not hide him anymore, she made a basket. She made it from tall river grass.","She covered it with sticky tar so no water could get in. Now it could float like a tiny boat! She laid her baby inside.","She set the basket in the tall grass at the river’s edge. His big sister stood a little way off and watched over him. This baby was Moses!","Heavenly Father watches over you, and so do the people who love you. Tonight, think of a little one you can watch over too."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout loves to watch and find out what happens next, just like the baby’s big sister by the river. In Scout’s world today: Scout’s baby sister fell asleep in her crib. Scout stood quietly by the door and watched over her. Just like Moses’s big sister, Scout learned that watching over little ones is a loving thing to do.","feel":"Curl up small, like a baby in a basket. Now rock gently side to side, like floating on soft water. Can you show me your sleepy, floating baby?","stones":[["SPOT","The mother made a safe little basket. She trusted Heavenly Father and felt hopeful."],["NAME","That quiet, holding-on, hoping feeling is called trust. Your mind might think, ‘Heavenly Father will help.’ Your body might feel calm, with soft, loose shoulders."],["FIND","When have you trusted someone to keep you safe?"],["FORK","The mother could give up, or do her best and trust Heavenly Father. She made the basket and trusted Him."],["CALM","Rock gently, like floating in a basket. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, please watch over me tonight.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father watches over me, just like He watched over baby Moses!","ask":["“What did the mother make for her baby?”","“Who watched the basket by the river?”","“Who did Scout watch over?”"],"share":["When have you felt Heavenly Father watching over our children?","How has doing your best and trusting Heavenly Father helped you?","Which grandparent or ancestor kept their family safe in a hard time? Share their story."]},"b3-w5-n4":{"story":["One day the king’s daughter, a princess, came to the river to wash. Her helpers walked along the river.","The princess saw the little basket in the tall grass. She sent her helper to bring it. She opened it, and a baby was crying!","The princess felt tender and kind toward him. She said, “This is one of the Israelites’ babies.”","His big sister asked, “Shall I find a lady to care for him?” The princess said, “Go.” The sister brought the baby’s own mother! Later the princess named him Moses, because she drew him out of the water.","A tender heart helps a crying little one. Tonight, think of the kind people who care for you. Each one is a gift from Heavenly Father."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow has a kind, tender heart, just like the princess who felt sorry for the crying baby. In Willow’s world today: Willow’s baby cousin was crying in his car seat. Willow sang him a soft song and gently held his tiny hand. Soon he stopped crying and smiled. Just like the princess, Willow learned that a tender heart helps a crying little one.","feel":"Pretend to lift a little basket out of the water. Hold it close and rock it softly. Can you show me your kind, gentle arms?","stones":[["SPOT","The princess heard the baby cry, and she felt tender and kind toward him."],["NAME","That soft, caring, I-want-to-help feeling is called compassion. Your mind might think, ‘Poor little one, I can help.’ Your body might feel warm in your heart and gentle in your hands."],["FIND","When have you wanted to help someone who was crying?"],["FORK","The princess could walk away, or care for the crying baby. She chose to care for him, and he was safe."],["CALM","Hug your pillow softly. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me be kind like the princess.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father sends kind people to help us, and I can be kind too!","ask":["“What did the princess find in the tall grass?”","“Who came to take care of baby Moses?”","“How did Willow help her baby cousin?”"],"share":["Who has Heavenly Father sent to help our family?","When has someone’s kindness made a big difference in your life?","Which grandparent or ancestor was known for being kind? Share a story about them."]},"b3-w5-n5":{"story":["Moses grew up as the princess’s son. Later, the king became angry with him. So Moses went far away to a land called Midian.","Moses sat down to rest by a well, a deep hole of water. Seven sisters came to get water for their father’s sheep.","Then grumpy shepherds came and chased the sisters away. But Moses stood up and helped them! He got water for all their sheep.","The sisters went home and told their father, Reuel. He said, “Invite him in to eat!” Moses stayed with their family. Later, Moses married Zipporah, one of the sisters.","Moses did not just sit and watch. He stood up to help. Tonight, think of someone you can help tomorrow."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau is brave, and tonight Moses bravely stood up to help the seven sisters. In Beau’s world today: At snack time, a bigger boy grabbed all the cups before a little girl could get one. Beau said kindly, “Let’s share,” and gave her a cup. Just like Moses at the well, Beau learned that brave helpers stand up for others.","feel":"Make your arms strong like Moses. Now pull up a big bucket of water, hand over hand. Can you show me your strong helping arms?","stones":[["SPOT","Grumpy shepherds chased the sisters away. Moses felt brave and stood up to help them."],["NAME","That strong, ready-to-help feeling is called caring. Your mind might think, ‘That is not fair. I can help!’ Your body might feel strong in your arms and quick in your feet."],["FIND","When have you helped someone who needed a friend?"],["FORK","Moses could stay sitting by the well, or stand up and help. Moses stood up and helped the sisters."],["CALM","Rest your strong arms on your blanket. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me be a brave helper.”"]],"remember":"I can be a brave helper, just like Moses at the well!","ask":["“Where did Moses sit down to rest?”","“How did Moses help the seven sisters?”","“What did Beau say at snack time?”"],"share":["When has someone stood up for you or helped you?","How can our family help people who are treated unkindly?","Which grandparent or ancestor moved far away to a new land? Share their story."]},"b3-w5-n6":{"story":["The Lord, who was Jesus, spoke to Moses. He said, “Go to Pharaoh, the king of Egypt. Bring My people out of Egypt.”","But Moses felt very small. He said, “Who am I, that I should go?” The Lord said, “I will be with you.”","Moses said, “I am slow at talking.” The Lord said, “Who made your mouth? I did. I will teach you what to say.”","The Lord sent Moses’s brother, Aaron, to help. Aaron could speak well, and he was glad to see Moses. Aaron met Moses on the mountain and gave him a kiss!","When you feel small or shy, Jesus is with you too. Tonight, put your hand on your heart and say, “I am never alone.”"],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren likes to think about big questions, like the one Moses asked: “Who am I?” In Wren’s world today: Wren had to sing in front of her class. She felt small and shy. Her teacher stood close beside her, and Wren sang her song. Just like Moses, Wren learned that hard things feel easier when someone is with us.","feel":"Curl up very small, like Moses felt. Now slowly grow tall and stretch your arms up high. Can you show me how tall you grow?","stones":[["SPOT","Moses felt small and shy, but the Lord promised, “I will be with you.”"],["NAME","That small, wobbly, not-sure feeling is called shyness. Your mind might think, ‘I cannot do this.’ Your body might feel tight in your tummy and quiet in your voice."],["FIND","When have you felt small or shy about something hard?"],["FORK","Moses could say no, or trust the Lord and go. Moses trusted the Lord and went, with Aaron by his side."],["CALM","Put one hand on your tummy and breathe slowly. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for being with me.”"]],"remember":"When I feel small, Jesus is with me!","ask":["“What did Moses say when he felt small?”","“Who did the Lord send to help Moses?”","“Who stood close while Wren sang?”"],"share":["When have you felt too small for something the Lord asked of you?","How has the Lord helped you know what to say?","Which grandparent or ancestor did something hard with the Lord’s help? Share their story."]},"b3-w5-n7":{"story":["Moses and Aaron went to Pharaoh, the king of Egypt. They said, “The Lord says, Let My people go!” But Pharaoh said, “No.”","Then Pharaoh made the Israelites’ work even harder. Moses felt sad. He asked the Lord, “Why?”","The Lord, who was Jesus, said, “My name is Jehovah. I have heard My people cry. I have remembered My covenant.”","A covenant is a special promise, one the Lord always keeps. Long ago, He promised Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob a land for their family. Now He said, “I will bring you out. You will be My people.”","Heavenly Father and Jesus always keep Their promises. Tonight, hook pinkies with your grown-up and make a little promise. Then keep it tomorrow!"],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout loves to find out what new words mean, and tonight he learns the word covenant. In Scout’s world today: Scout promised his mom he would feed the dog every morning. Each morning, Scout remembered and filled the bowl. Just like Moses, Scout learned that keeping a promise shows love.","feel":"Hook your two pinkies together, like making a promise. Hold them tight! Can you show me your promise-keeping pinkies?","stones":[["SPOT","Moses felt sad when things got harder, but the Lord said, “I have remembered.”"],["NAME","That safe, sure, held-tight feeling is called hope. Your mind might think, ‘Heavenly Father will keep His promise.’ Your body might feel calm and warm in your chest."],["FIND","When has someone kept a promise to you?"],["FORK","Moses could give up, or keep listening to the Lord. Moses kept listening and told the people the Lord’s promise."],["CALM","Hold your pinkies together and breathe slowly. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for remembering me.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father always keeps His promises, and He remembers me!","ask":["“What did Moses and Aaron ask Pharaoh?”","“What is a covenant?”","“What did Scout promise to do each morning?”"],"share":["When have you seen Heavenly Father keep a promise to you?","What covenants have you made, and how do they bless our family?","Which grandparent or ancestor faithfully kept their promises to the Lord? Share about them."]},"b5-w1-n1":{"story":["Heavenly Father’s people were traveling in the desert. The Lord asked them to build Him a holy tent, called the tabernacle. Then He could be near them.","Moses said, “Whoever has a willing heart, bring a gift to the Lord.” That is a heart that says, “I want to give!”","Men and women came with gold rings and bracelets. They brought soft cloth of blue, purple and red. They brought wood, oil and shiny stones. Women spun yarn by hand.","Every morning, the people brought even more. The workers told Moses, “The people bring much more than enough!” So Moses told them, “You can stop now!”","Giving gladly makes a heart feel warm. Tonight, think of one thing you can share tomorrow."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow has a warm, giving heart, just like the people who gladly brought their gifts to the Lord. In Willow’s world today: At snack time, Willow had three crackers. Her friend had none. Willow smiled and gave her friend two crackers, happy to share. Just like the people with willing hearts, Willow learned that giving gladly makes hearts warm.","feel":"Cup your hands together like you are holding a gift. Now lift them up gently and open them wide. Can you show me your happy giving hands?","stones":[["SPOT","The people gave their best things to the Lord, and they felt glad."],["NAME","That warm, open-handed, glad feeling is called generosity. Your mind might think, ‘I want to share!’ Your body might feel warm and light in your chest."],["FIND","When have you shared something and felt glad inside?"],["FORK","The people could keep their things, or give to the Lord. They gave so gladly, it was more than enough!"],["CALM","Open your hands softly on your blanket. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me have a willing heart.”"]],"remember":"When I give with a willing heart, I give to the Lord!","ask":["“What did the Lord ask His people to build?”","“What gifts did the people bring?”","“What did Willow share with her friend?”"],"share":["When have you seen someone give to the Lord with a willing heart?","What can our family give to help Heavenly Father’s work?","Which grandparent or ancestor gave gladly to others? Share a story about them."]},"b5-w1-n2":{"story":["The Lord’s holy tent needed a builder. The Lord chose a man named Bezaleel. He even called him by name!","The Lord filled Bezaleel with His Spirit. The Spirit made him wise, so he knew how to make beautiful things.","Bezaleel could shape gold, silver and brass. He could cut pretty stones and carve wood.","The Lord also put it in Bezaleel’s heart to teach. He and a man named Aholiab showed others how to do the work. Moses called everyone with a wise, willing heart to come and help.","Heavenly Father gives you gifts too, like kind words and helping hands. When you learn something new, you can help someone else learn it too."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau is brave to try new things, and Bezaleel bravely used his gifts for the Lord’s big work. In Beau’s world today: Beau learned to zip his coat all by himself. Then he bravely showed his little sister how to do it too. Just like Bezaleel, Beau learned that Heavenly Father helps us learn and then teach others.","feel":"Pretend to hold a little hammer. Tap, tap, tap gently on your blanket! Now carve slowly with one finger. Can you show me your busy, helping hands?","stones":[["SPOT","The Lord chose Bezaleel and filled him with His Spirit. Bezaleel felt ready to help."],["NAME","That ready, I-can-do-it feeling is called confidence. Your mind might think, ‘Heavenly Father will help me.’ Your body might feel tall and strong, with steady hands."],["FIND","When have you learned to do something new?"],["FORK","Bezaleel could keep his gifts to himself, or use them for the Lord. He built and taught others too."],["CALM","Rest your hands on your tummy. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for my gifts.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father gives me gifts, and I can use them to help!","ask":["“Who did the Lord choose to build His tent?”","“What did the Lord fill Bezaleel with?”","“What did Beau teach his little sister?”"],"share":["What gift from Heavenly Father do you use to serve others?","When has the Spirit helped you do something hard?","Which grandparent or ancestor had skillful hands? Share something they made or did."]},"b5-w1-n3":{"story":["The Lord said, “Let them make Me a holy place to live among them.” The people were traveling, so they made a holy tent they could carry.","Its soft curtains were blue, purple and red, with angels woven in.","Bezaleel made a wooden box called the ark of the covenant. It was a box, not a boat! He covered it with gold.","On the lid were two golden angels with wings spread high. Moses put the stone tablets of the Lord’s commandments inside. They were part of the Lord’s covenant, His two-way promise with His people.","The Lord wants to be close to you too. The temple is His holy house today. He is near you tonight."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout loves to explore, and tonight there is a whole holy tent and a golden box to discover. In Scout’s world today: At church, Scout looked closely at a picture of the temple. He asked, “Why is it so special?” Mom said, “It is the Lord’s house.” Just like the people who built the tabernacle, Scout learned that Heavenly Father wants to be close to us.","feel":"Make a little tent over your head with both arms. Now peek out very quietly. Can you show me your soft, quiet holy-place face?","stones":[["SPOT","The people made a holy tent for the Lord, and they felt happy He would be near."],["NAME","That quiet, careful, love-filled feeling is called reverence. Your mind might think, ‘This is a holy place.’ Your body might feel calm and still, with soft hands."],["FIND","When have you felt quiet and calm in a special place?"],["FORK","The people could build it any way they liked, or the Lord’s way. They made it just as the Lord said."],["CALM","Fold your arms and close your eyes. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for wanting to be near me.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father wants to be close to me, and I can be close to Him!","ask":["“What did the people build so the Lord could be near?”","“What did Moses put inside the golden ark?”","“What did Scout ask about the temple?”"],"share":["When have you felt close to Heavenly Father in the temple?","How can our home feel like a holy place?","Which grandparent or ancestor loved the temple? Share a story about them."]},"b5-w1-n4":{"story":["The workers made holy clothes for Aaron, Moses’ brother. Aaron served the Lord at His holy tent.","They made a gold plate for Aaron’s hat. On it they wrote, “Holiness to the Lord.” It showed Aaron’s work was just for the Lord.","Moses looked at all the work. It was done just the way the Lord said! So Moses blessed the people.","Moses set up the holy tent. Then a cloud covered it, and the glory of the Lord filled it. His bright glory was so great, even Moses could not go in! When the cloud lifted, the people traveled on. When it stayed, they waited.","Heavenly Father shows you the way too. Tonight, think of a time He helped you."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren loves to wonder quietly, and tonight the Lord’s glory filled His holy tent. In Wren’s world today: Wren lay on the soft grass and watched a big white cloud float slowly by. She thought about the Lord’s cloud in the desert. Just like the people of Israel, Wren learned that Heavenly Father is near and leads us.","feel":"Lift your arms slowly up high like a soft, puffy cloud. Now float them gently down onto your blanket. Can you show me your quiet cloud arms?","stones":[["SPOT","The Lord’s glory filled His holy tent. The people felt amazed and full of wonder."],["NAME","That big, quiet, wide-eyed feeling is called awe. Your mind might think, ‘The Lord is here!’ Your body might feel very still and tingly all over."],["FIND","When have you felt quiet wonder at something Heavenly Father made?"],["FORK","The people could go their own way, or follow the Lord’s cloud. They waited and followed the cloud."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly, soft as a cloud. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, please lead me.”"]],"remember":"Holiness to the Lord! I belong to Him, and He will lead me!","ask":["“What words were on Aaron’s gold plate?”","“What filled the Lord’s holy tent?”","“What did Wren watch in the sky?”"],"share":["When have you felt the Lord lead your life?","What do the words “Holiness to the Lord” on our temples mean to you?","Which grandparent or ancestor followed the Lord’s guidance? Share a story about them."]},"b5-w1-n5":{"story":["The Lord spoke to Moses from His holy tent. He taught about offerings, gifts the people gave the Lord at His altar.","A family could bring a lamb or a young calf. It had to be perfect, with nothing wrong at all. They gave it because they wanted to.","If someone did wrong without meaning to, they brought an offering too. They put a hand on its head, and the Lord forgave them.","Why did it have to be perfect? Prophets teach that offerings helped people remember the Savior who would come. That Savior is Jesus. He is perfect, and He gave Himself for us.","Jesus came for you. Tonight, put your hand on your heart and feel thankful for Him."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren loves to think about what things mean, and tonight each offering helped people think of Jesus. In Wren’s world today: Every night, Dad sings Wren the same soft song. When Dad is away at work, Wren hums it and remembers his love. Just like the people with their offerings, Wren learned that special things help us remember someone we love.","feel":"Cup your hands like you are holding a soft little lamb. Now rest one hand gently on its head. Can you show me your gentle, careful hands?","stones":[["SPOT","The people brought perfect gifts to the Lord, and they felt thankful and forgiven."],["NAME","That warm, thankful, full-heart feeling is called gratitude. Your mind might think, ‘Thank You, Jesus!’ Your body might feel warm and soft in your heart."],["FIND","When have you felt thankful for someone who helped you?"],["FORK","The people could forget the Lord, or bring their gifts and remember Him. They brought their offerings."],["CALM","Put your hand on your heart. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for Jesus, my Savior.”"]],"remember":"The perfect offerings helped people remember Jesus, the Savior who would come!","ask":["“What did the lamb or calf have to be like?”","“Who did the offerings help people remember?”","“What helps Wren remember Dad’s love?”"],"share":["How does the sacrament help you remember Jesus each week?","When have you felt forgiven by the Lord?","Which grandparent or ancestor taught you to remember Jesus? Share a story about them."]},"b5-w1-n6":{"story":["Once a year came a special day, the Day of Atonement. It was a day for coming close to the Lord again, clean inside.","Everyone makes wrong choices, called sins. Sins can make us feel far from Heavenly Father.","Aaron put both his hands on a goat’s head. He said out loud all the wrong things the people had done.","Then a man led the goat far away into the empty desert. It was like the people’s sins were carried far away! The Lord promised, “You will be clean from all your sins.” This can help us remember Jesus, who carries our sins away.","When you say sorry, Jesus helps carry your sins away too. Your heart can feel clean and light again."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau is brave, and it takes a brave heart to say sorry and start fresh. In Beau’s world today: Beau knocked over his sister’s block castle. He felt bad inside. Beau bravely said, “I am sorry,” and helped her build it again. Just like the people on the special day, Beau learned that saying sorry helps our hearts feel clean and light.","feel":"Pretend you have a heavy backpack on. Now take it off and toss it far away! Can you show me your light, happy shoulders?","stones":[["SPOT","The people’s sins were carried far away. They felt clean, light and happy."],["NAME","That light, clean, fresh-start feeling is called relief. Your mind might think, ‘I can try again!’ Your body might feel light, like a heavy bag was lifted off."],["FIND","When have you said sorry and felt better inside?"],["FORK","The people could hold on to their sins, or let the Lord take them away. They came to the Lord."],["CALM","Let your shoulders drop softly. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for Jesus, who takes my sins away.”"]],"remember":"Jesus can carry my sins away and help me feel clean inside!","ask":["“Where did the goat go?”","“Who carries our sins away?”","“What did Beau say to his sister?”"],"share":["When have you felt Jesus lift something heavy from your heart?","How do we help each other start fresh in our family?","Which grandparent or ancestor taught you about forgiveness? Share a story about them."]},"b5-w1-n7":{"story":["The Lord told Moses, “Be holy, because I, the Lord, am holy.” He wanted His people to be good and kind, like Him.","The Lord said, “When you cut your grain, leave the corners of your field. Leave some grapes on the vines too.”","Why? Some people were poor, and some were newcomers with no field. They could come and gather that food to eat.","The Lord said, “Love your neighbor as yourself.” He said to stand up when an older person comes in, to honor them. Many years later, Jesus taught, “Love your neighbor,” too.","Your neighbors are all around you, at home and at church. Tonight, think of one way to make room for someone tomorrow."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow has a warm, caring heart, and tonight the Lord teaches us to care for everyone. In Willow’s world today: At church, Willow saw an older lady looking for a seat. Willow stood up and said, “You can sit here!” The lady smiled. Just like the Lord taught His people, Willow learned that loving others means making room for them.","feel":"Pretend to cut tall grain with your hands. Swish, swish! Now stop and leave a little corner. Can you show me your sharing hands?","stones":[["SPOT","The Lord taught His people to share food and honor older people, so everyone felt loved."],["NAME","That warm, caring, make-room feeling is called love. Your mind might think, ‘I can help them.’ Your body might feel soft and warm, with a big smile."],["FIND","When have you shared with someone or helped an older person?"],["FORK","The farmers could keep all their grain, or leave some for others. The Lord asked them to share."],["CALM","Hug your pillow softly. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me love my neighbor as myself.”"]],"remember":"The Lord wants me to love my neighbor as myself!","ask":["“What did the farmers leave at the corners of the field?”","“Who could gather the food left in the field?”","“Who did Willow give her seat to?”"],"share":["Who has been a loving neighbor to our family?","How can we show respect and care for older people near us?","Which grandparent or ancestor shared what they had with others? Share a story about them."]},"b5-w2-n1":{"story":["Every morning the Lord sent manna, bread from heaven. But some people cried, “We want meat! We miss the fish and melons we ate in Egypt.”","Moses felt tired and sad. He told the Lord, “I cannot carry all these people alone. It is too heavy for me.”","The Lord said, “Bring seventy wise older leaders. They will help you carry the load.”","The Lord came down in a cloud and put His Spirit on the seventy. Two men in the camp had the Spirit too. Joshua said, “Stop them!” But gentle Moses said, “I wish all the Lord’s people had His Spirit!”","Heavy things feel lighter when we share them. Tomorrow, look for one way to help carry a load at home."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow has a warm, helping heart, and tonight the Lord sent seventy helpers to Moses. In Willow’s world today: Willow’s mom had lots of grocery bags to carry in from the car. Willow said, “I can help!” and carried the bread. Just like the seventy elders, Willow learned that when we help, heavy things feel lighter.","feel":"Pretend to lift a big, heavy bag all by yourself. Oof! Now pretend a friend grabs the other side. Can you show me how light it feels now?","stones":[["SPOT","Moses felt tired from caring for so many people, so the Lord sent seventy helpers."],["NAME","That heavy, too-much feeling is called overwhelmed. Your mind might think, ‘I cannot do it all by myself!’ Your body might feel droopy shoulders and tired arms."],["FIND","When have you felt like you had too much to do?"],["FORK","Moses could give up, or tell the Lord how he felt. Moses told the Lord, and the Lord sent helpers."],["CALM","Let your shoulders drop down soft. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for my helpers.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father sends helpers, and I can be a helper too!","ask":["“What did the Lord send every morning?”","“How many elders came to help Moses?”","“What did Willow carry for her mom?”"],"share":["When has the Lord sent someone to help carry your load?","Who in our family or neighborhood could use a helper this week?","Which grandparent or ancestor was a great helper? Share a story about them."]},"b5-w2-n2":{"story":["Heavenly Father’s people came near the land He promised them. The Lord said, “Send men to look at the land.” So Moses sent twelve men, one from each family group.","Moses said, “Be brave, and bring back some fruit.” Joshua and Caleb went too.","They found grapes so big, two men carried one bunch on a pole! They brought figs and pomegranates, red fruits full of juicy seeds.","After forty days, the men came back. They showed everyone the fruit and said, “This land flows with milk and honey!” There was so much good food, it was like milk and honey pouring out.","Heavenly Father gives you good gifts too. Tonight, name one food you are thankful for."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout loves to explore, just like the twelve men who went to look at the new land. In Scout’s world today: Scout and his dad went out to the garden to see what had grown. Under the leaves, Scout found a big, red tomato! Just like the twelve men, Scout learned that Heavenly Father’s good gifts are worth looking for.","feel":"Pretend to carry a long pole on your shoulder. It is so heavy with grapes! Wobble, wobble. Can you show me your strong grape-carrying shoulders?","stones":[["SPOT","The twelve men explored the new land and found giant grapes. They felt amazed."],["NAME","That wow, look-at-this feeling is called amazement. Your mind might think, ‘I never saw grapes so big!’ Your body might feel wide eyes and an open mouth."],["FIND","When have you seen something that made you say, “Wow”?"],["FORK","The men could stay at camp, or go look like Moses asked. They went, and found the good land."],["CALM","Hug your knees and breathe slowly. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for good gifts.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father’s promises are good and sweet, like big grapes!","ask":["“How many men went to look at the land?”","“How did they carry the big grapes?”","“What did Scout find in the garden?”"],"share":["What promise from the Lord has come true in your life?","What sweet blessings has our family found this year?","Which grandparent or ancestor moved to a new land? Share their story."]},"b5-w2-n3":{"story":["The twelve men came back. Caleb said, “We can do it!” But most of the men were afraid. They said, “The people there are giants! We felt as small as grasshoppers.”","That night the people cried. They wanted to go back to Egypt.","Joshua and Caleb said, “The land is very good! Do not turn away from the Lord. Do not be afraid. The Lord is with us.”","But the people did not trust the Lord. So He said they would stay in the desert for forty years. Then their children would go into the good land. The Lord said Caleb followed Him all the way.","When you feel afraid, remember Joshua and Caleb. Tonight, snuggle down brave and whisper, “The Lord is with me.”"],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau is brave, and tonight Joshua and Caleb were brave when everyone else was afraid. In Beau’s world today: At swim class, the big pool looked very deep, and Beau’s tummy felt wobbly. He held his teacher’s hand and said, “I can try.” Just like Joshua and Caleb, Beau learned that the Lord helps us be brave.","feel":"Curl up tiny like a little grasshopper. Now stretch up tall and strong like brave Caleb. Can you show me your tall, brave Caleb pose?","stones":[["SPOT","Most of the men were afraid, but Joshua and Caleb trusted the Lord and felt brave."],["NAME","That steady, standing-tall feeling is called courage. Your mind might think, ‘The Lord is with me.’ Your body might feel a strong back and steady feet."],["FIND","When have you been brave even when you felt a little scared?"],["FORK","They could listen to their fears, or trust the Lord. Joshua and Caleb chose to trust the Lord."],["CALM","Stretch tall, then snuggle down. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me be brave like Caleb.”"]],"remember":"The Lord is with me, so I do not have to be afraid!","ask":["“What did the afraid men say they felt like?”","“What did Joshua and Caleb say about the Lord?”","“What did Beau say at the pool?”"],"share":["When has trusting the Lord helped you face something big?","What “giants” is our family facing, and how can we trust the Lord together?","Which grandparent or ancestor followed the Lord with their whole heart? Share their story."]},"b5-w2-n4":{"story":["Heavenly Father’s people came to a dry place with no water. Thirsty and grumpy, they argued with Moses and Aaron. So Moses and Aaron bowed low before the Lord.","The Lord said, “Take your stick. Speak to the rock in front of everyone, and water will come out.”","But Moses was upset with the people. He hit the rock two times instead. Lots of water came out for everyone!","The Lord said Moses and Aaron had not trusted Him. So they would not lead the people into the new land. Moses kept serving the Lord, and the Lord still loved him.","When you make a mistake, Heavenly Father still loves you. Next time, you can try again the Lord’s way."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren likes to think things over, and tonight Moses learned to do things the Lord’s way. In Wren’s world today: Mom showed Wren how to pet the kitty gently. Wren hurried and patted too hard, and the kitty ran off. Next time, she petted softly, the way Mom showed her. Just like Moses, Wren learned that the Lord’s way is best.","feel":"Make a fist and tap the bed. Bonk, bonk! Now open your hand and speak softly instead. Can you show me your gentle speaking voice?","stones":[["SPOT","The people were thirsty and grumpy. Moses felt upset and hit the rock instead of speaking."],["NAME","That hot, can’t-wait feeling is called impatience. Your mind might think, ‘I will do it my way, fast!’ Your body might feel hot cheeks and hurry-up hands."],["FIND","When have you felt in a hurry to do things your way?"],["FORK","Moses could speak to the rock, or hit it. He hit it, but the Lord still gave water and still loved him."],["CALM","Fold your hands and breathe slowly. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me do things Your way.”"]],"remember":"I can trust the Lord and do things His way!","ask":["“What did the Lord tell Moses to do to the rock?”","“What came out of the rock?”","“How did Wren pet the kitty the second time?”"],"share":["When has doing something the Lord’s way made a difference for you?","How can our family stay patient when we feel upset?","Which grandparent or ancestor learned from a mistake? Share their story."]},"b5-w2-n5":{"story":["King Balak was afraid of Heavenly Father’s people. He asked Balaam to curse them, to wish bad things on them. But the Lord told Balaam, “Do not curse them, for they are blessed.”","Later, Balaam rode off on his donkey. An angel of the Lord stood in the road. Only the donkey could see him!","The donkey turned off the road. She squished Balaam’s foot on a wall. Then she lay down.","Balaam was cross and hit her. Then the Lord let the donkey talk! She said, “What have I done to you?” Then the Lord opened Balaam’s eyes. He saw the angel and bowed down.","Heavenly Father helps us stop and find the right way. Tonight, lie still and listen."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout loves to notice things, and tonight a little donkey noticed what Balaam missed. In Scout’s world today: Scout was running to the swings when his dog sat down and would not move. Scout looked, and there was a big, muddy puddle right ahead! Just like Balaam, Scout learned that it is good to stop and look for the right way.","feel":"Plant your hands and knees like a donkey that will not move. Now wiggle your long donkey ears! Can you show me your silly donkey ears?","stones":[["SPOT","The donkey saw the angel and stopped. Balaam was cross, then surprised when she talked!"],["NAME","That eyes-wide, I-did-not-expect-that feeling is called surprise. Your mind might think, ‘A talking donkey?’ Your body might feel a jump, high eyebrows and a big gasp."],["FIND","When has something surprised you so much that you gasped?"],["FORK","Balaam could stay cross, or listen to the angel. Balaam bowed down and listened."],["CALM","Lie very still, like a resting donkey. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, open my eyes to see Your way.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father can open my eyes to see the right way!","ask":["“Who saw the angel first?”","“What did the donkey say to Balaam?”","“Why did Scout’s dog stop?”"],"share":["When has the Lord stopped you or turned you onto a better path?","How can our family listen better for the Lord’s voice?","Which grandparent or ancestor felt warned or guided by the Lord? Share their story."]},"b5-w2-n6":{"story":["King Balak took Balaam up high to look at Israel’s tents. Balak wanted Balaam to curse them, to say bad words against them.","But Balaam said, “How can I curse people the Lord has not cursed?” Instead he blessed them: “How lovely are your tents!”","Balak was cross. Balaam said, “I can only say what the Lord tells me.”","Then Balaam saw far into the future. He said, “A Star will come out of Jacob.” Jacob’s big family was Israel. Prophets teach that this Star is Jesus! Stars shine in the dark and help travelers find their way.","Like Balaam, you can choose kind, blessing words. Tonight, picture a bright star and think of Jesus, who lights your way."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau is brave, and Balaam said the Lord’s words even when a king wanted something else. In Beau’s world today: At the park, a friend grabbed the swing first and said, “Too slow!” Beau wanted to say grumpy words back. Instead he smiled and said, “Okay, I’ll go next!” Just like Balaam, Beau learned that we can choose kind, blessing words.","feel":"Reach your hands up high and open your fingers wide like a star. Twinkle, twinkle! Can you show me how a star shines in the dark?","stones":[["SPOT","King Balak wanted bad words, but Balaam spoke the Lord’s good words. Balaam felt sure."],["NAME","That sure, steady, I-know feeling is called confidence. Your mind might think, ‘I will say what is right.’ Your body might feel a tall back and a calm, strong voice."],["FIND","When have you said kind words when others were not kind?"],["FORK","Balaam could say what the king wanted, or what the Lord wanted. Balaam said the Lord’s words."],["CALM","Put a hand on your heart. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me say kind and true words.”"]],"remember":"Jesus is the bright Star who lights my way!","ask":["“What did King Balak want Balaam to do?”","“Who was the Star that Balaam talked about?”","“What did Beau say when his friend took the swing?”"],"share":["When have you felt Jesus light your way like a star?","How can our family use blessing words with each other this week?","Which grandparent or ancestor taught you about Jesus? Share a story about them."]},"b5-w2-n7":{"story":["The Lord told Moses, “Climb this mountain. See the land I give My people.” But because Moses hit the rock, he would not lead them into it.","Moses cared about the people. He prayed, “Please give them a new leader. Do not let them be like sheep with no shepherd to lead them.”","The Lord said, “Take Joshua. He has the Spirit with him.”","So Moses laid his hands on Joshua in front of the priest and everyone. He shared some of his honor with Joshua, so the people would follow him. Leaders today are still called with hands laid on them.","Jesus is our Good Shepherd, and He cares for your family. Like Moses, you can look out for others too."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow has a caring heart, and Moses cared that the people would have a shepherd. In Willow’s world today: On a walk, Willow saw her baby brother’s teddy fall out of the stroller. She ran back, picked it up and tucked it in beside him. Just like Moses, Willow learned that caring means looking out for others.","feel":"Pretend you are a kind shepherd. Gently gather your little lambs close, one, two, three! Can you show me how a shepherd hugs a lamb?","stones":[["SPOT","Moses knew he would not go into the land, but he cared and asked for a new shepherd."],["NAME","That soft, warm, looking-out-for-you feeling is called care. Your mind might think, ‘I want them to be safe.’ Your body might feel a warm, full heart."],["FIND","When have you wanted someone else to be safe and happy?"],["FORK","Moses could think only of himself, or think of the people. Moses thought of the people and asked for a leader."],["CALM","Give yourself a soft hug. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for Jesus, my Shepherd.”"]],"remember":"Jesus, my Good Shepherd, always takes care of His sheep!","ask":["“What did Moses ask the Lord for?”","“Who did the Lord choose to lead next?”","“What did Willow pick up for her baby brother?”"],"share":["When have you felt the Lord’s help through a priesthood blessing or a leader?","How can our family pray for and help our leaders?","Which grandparent or ancestor was a good leader or teacher? Share a story about them."]},"b5-w3-n1":{"story":["Heavenly Father’s people were almost to their new home. First, old Moses gave them some last words to keep.","Moses said, “Hear, O Israel. Love the Lord with all your heart, all your soul and all your might.” That means all of you!","“Keep these words in your heart,” Moses said. “Teach them to your children.”","“Talk about them when you sit and when you walk,” he said. “Talk about them when you lie down and get up.” Moses said to write them on the doorposts too. Families would see them every day and not forget.","Right now, as you lie down, you are hearing the Lord’s words! Put your hand on your heart. Whisper, “I love you, Heavenly Father.”"],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow has a big, loving heart, and tonight Moses teaches the most important thing: love the Lord with all your heart. In Willow’s world today: Willow made a card for Grandma. She used her best colors and drew every heart slowly and carefully. Just like Moses taught, Willow learned that loving with all your heart means giving your very best.","feel":"Put both hands on your heart. Now open your arms wide, as big as your love. Can you show me how big your love is?","stones":[["SPOT","Moses taught the people to love the Lord with all their hearts. They felt warm love."],["NAME","That warm, big, full-heart feeling is called love. Your mind might think, ‘I love Heavenly Father so much!’ Your body might feel warm and full in your chest."],["FIND","When have you felt a big, warm love in your heart?"],["FORK","The people could forget the Lord’s words, or keep them in their hearts. Moses asked them to remember and teach them."],["CALM","Put your hand on your heart. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, I love You with all my heart.”"]],"remember":"I love the Lord with all my heart!","ask":["“What did Moses say to love the Lord with?”","“Where did families write the Lord’s words?”","“What did Willow make for Grandma?”"],"share":["What helps you love the Lord with all your heart, soul and might?","How can our family talk about the Lord when we sit, walk and lie down?","Which grandparent or ancestor taught their children about the Lord? Share a story about them."]},"b5-w3-n2":{"story":["Moses said, “Remember the forty years the Lord led you through the wilderness.” It was a wild, dry land.","When the people were hungry, the Lord fed them manna. It was a new food, like bread from heaven.","And guess what? For forty years their clothes did not wear out. Their feet did not get puffy and sore!","Why did the Lord feed them this way? Moses said, “People do not live by bread only. They live by every word from the Lord.” Bread fills our tummies. The Lord’s words fill our hearts. Long after, Jesus said these same words!","Pat your tummy, then pat your heart. Heavenly Father takes care of both."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout loves to discover amazing things, and tonight he finds out that clothes did not wear out for forty years! In Scout’s world today: At lunch, Scout ate his sandwich while Mom read a scripture story. Scout listened and asked questions between bites. Just like Moses taught, Scout learned that we need bread for our bodies and the Lord’s words for our spirits.","feel":"Pretend to pick up tiny manna flakes, one, two, three. Now pat your clothes, still good as new! Can you show me your surprised face?","stones":[["SPOT","The Lord fed His people and kept their clothes from wearing out. They were cared for."],["NAME","That safe, taken-care-of feeling is called trust. Your mind might think, ‘Heavenly Father gives me what I need.’ Your body might feel calm and soft in your shoulders."],["FIND","When has someone given you just what you needed?"],["FORK","The people could forget how the Lord fed them, or remember it. Moses remembered, and he taught them to remember too."],["CALM","Rub your tummy softly. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for my food and for Your words.”"]],"remember":"I need Heavenly Father’s words, just like I need food!","ask":["“What food did the Lord send from heaven?”","“What did not wear out for forty years?”","“What did Scout listen to at lunch?”"],"share":["Which words of the Lord feed your spirit the most?","When has the Lord given our family just what we needed?","Which grandparent or ancestor trusted the Lord in hard, hungry times? Share their story."]},"b5-w3-n3":{"story":["Moses told the people about their new home. “It is a good land,” he said, “with little rivers and springs.”","It had wheat and barley for bread. It had grapes, figs and red pomegranates full of juicy seeds. It had olive oil and honey too!","Moses said, “When you are full, thank the Lord for the good land.”","Then Moses said, “Be careful not to forget the Lord.” People with many good things can forget who gave them. Remember, the Lord loved His people and brought them out of Egypt.","Every good thing you have comes from Heavenly Father. Tonight, think of three good things from today. Say, “Thank you, Heavenly Father,” for each one."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren loves to stop and wonder, and tonight Moses asks the people to stop and remember who gave them every good thing. In Wren’s world today: On her birthday, Wren got a new picture book from Grandpa. Before she opened it, she ran to hug him and say, “Thank you!” Just like Moses taught, Wren learned that we thank the ones who give us good things, and Heavenly Father most of all.","feel":"Pretend to eat a juicy pomegranate seed. Pop! Now pat your tummy, happy and full. Can you show me your thankful face?","stones":[["SPOT","The people would have a good land with lots of food. Moses wanted them to feel thankful."],["NAME","That happy, full, thank-you feeling is called gratitude. Your mind might think, ‘Heavenly Father gave me this!’ Your body might feel warm and smiley all over."],["FIND","When have you felt so thankful you wanted to say thank you?"],["FORK","The people could enjoy the good land and forget, or enjoy it and remember. Moses asked them to remember the Lord."],["CALM","Fold your arms and smile. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for every good thing.”"]],"remember":"Every good thing comes from Heavenly Father, and I will remember Him!","ask":["“What fruits grew in the good land?”","“Who did Moses say not to forget?”","“Who did Wren thank for her birthday book?”"],"share":["What helps you remember the Lord when life is full and good?","What good things can our family thank Heavenly Father for tonight?","Which grandparent or ancestor always remembered to thank the Lord? Share a story about them."]},"b5-w3-n4":{"story":["Moses taught the people how to care for each other. Some families would not have enough food or clothes.","Moses said, “Do not shut your hand to a poor person. Open your hand wide, and give what he needs.” A shut hand keeps everything. An open hand shares.","Moses said to give with a glad heart. Then the Lord would bless all their work.","Moses told them why. “Remember, you were not free in Egypt.” The Lord set them free because He loved them. Now they could be kind to others, as the Lord was kind to them.","Hold your hand out flat and wide open. That is a sharing hand! Tonight, think of someone you can share with tomorrow."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow is warm and tender, and tonight Moses teaches the people to open their hands wide to help others. In Willow’s world today: Willow and Mom packed her outgrown coat in a box for children who need warm clothes. Willow folded it with care and smiled. Just like Moses taught, Willow learned that an open hand helps others feel loved.","feel":"Make two tight fists. Now open your hands wide, wide, wide, like you are giving a gift. Can you show me your sharing hands?","stones":[["SPOT","Moses taught the people to share with the poor. Sharing helps people feel loved and cared for."],["NAME","That kind, giving, open-heart feeling is called being generous. Your mind might think, ‘I want to share with you.’ Your body might feel light and happy in your chest."],["FIND","When have you shared something and felt happy inside?"],["FORK","They could close their hands and keep everything, or open them wide. Moses asked them to open their hands and share."],["CALM","Open your hands softly on your blanket. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me share with a glad heart.”"]],"remember":"I can open my hand wide and share!","ask":["“What did Moses say to do with your hand?”","“Who did the Lord set free from Egypt?”","“What did Willow give to children who need it?”"],"share":["When has someone opened their hand to help our family?","Who could our family share with this week?","Which grandparent or ancestor was generous to people in need? Share a story about them."]},"b5-w3-n5":{"story":["For many years, Moses told the people what Heavenly Father said. Now Moses was old, and he made them a promise.","Moses said, “The Lord will send you a Prophet like me. Listen to Him.”","The Lord said, “I will put My words in His mouth.” This Prophet would say just what Heavenly Father wanted, every time.","Who was that Prophet? Long after, Peter, one of Jesus’s helpers, said it was Jesus! And Jesus Himself said, “I am the one Moses spoke about.” Like Moses, Jesus led the people and taught them Heavenly Father’s words.","Moses’s promise was like a clue that led to Jesus. Tonight, cup your hand by your ear. You can listen to Jesus too."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout loves to solve mysteries, and tonight Moses gives a big clue: a Prophet is coming! Who could it be? In Scout’s world today: Scout’s dad hid a toy truck and gave a clue: “Look where we keep the books.” Scout searched and found it on the shelf! Just like Peter, Scout learned that a good clue can lead you to something wonderful.","feel":"Cup your hand behind your ear. Now lean in close, like you hear something important. Can you show me how you listen to Jesus?","stones":[["SPOT","Moses promised a Prophet would come. The people could feel hopeful, waiting for Him."],["NAME","That looking-forward, something-good-is-coming feeling is called hope. Your mind might think, ‘Someone wonderful is coming!’ Your body might feel bright and bouncy inside."],["FIND","When have you waited for something good that you knew was coming?"],["FORK","People could ignore the Prophet, or listen to Him. Peter listened to Jesus and followed Him."],["CALM","Cup your hand by your ear and breathe slowly. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me listen to Jesus.”"]],"remember":"Jesus is the Prophet Moses promised, and I will listen to Him!","ask":["“What did Moses promise the Lord would send?”","“Who was the Prophet like Moses?”","“Where did Scout find the hidden toy?”"],"share":["How do you listen to Jesus in your own life?","How can our family listen to the prophet today?","Which grandparent or ancestor followed the prophet? Share a story about them."]},"b5-w3-n6":{"story":["All of Heavenly Father’s people stood together before the Lord. The moms and dads were there, and the little children too!","That day they made a covenant with Heavenly Father. A covenant is a two-way promise, like holding hands and promising to stay close.","Moses said the Lord’s words were very close, in their mouths and hearts.","Moses said, “I have set before you life and good.” The Lord’s way brings good things. Turning away brings sad things. So Moses said, “Choose life! Love the Lord, obey Him, and hold on to Him.”","You get to choose too. Tonight, hold your blanket tight, like holding on to the Lord. You can choose His good way."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau is brave, and choosing the right way, even when it is hard, takes a brave heart. In Beau’s world today: Dad said, “Time to pick up the toys.” Beau wanted to keep playing! But he stopped and chose to help. Just like Moses taught, Beau learned that choosing the good way makes everyone happier.","feel":"Hold out both hands like two roads. Now pick one and hug it close, like holding on to the Lord. Can you show me your brave, choosing face?","stones":[["SPOT","Moses showed the people two ways. He hoped they would bravely choose the Lord’s good way."],["NAME","That strong, sure, I-can-do-it feeling is called courage. Your mind might think, ‘I will choose the right way.’ Your body might feel tall and strong in your back."],["FIND","When have you made a good choice that was hard?"],["FORK","The people could turn away from the Lord, or choose life. That day they made a covenant with the Lord."],["CALM","Hold your blanket close. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me choose Your way tomorrow.”"]],"remember":"I can choose the Lord’s good way every day!","ask":["“Who came to stand before the Lord?”","“What did Moses tell the people to choose?”","“What did Beau choose to do at clean-up time?”"],"share":["When did you choose the Lord’s way, and how did it bless you?","What covenants has our family made with Heavenly Father?","Which grandparent or ancestor chose the Lord when it was hard? Share their story."]},"b5-w3-n7":{"story":["Moses was 120 years old, but his eyes saw clearly. He climbed a tall mountain called Nebo.","From the top, the Lord showed Moses the promised land. He saw hills and Jericho, the city of palm trees.","The Lord said, “This is the land I promised to Abraham, Isaac and Jacob.” They were the people’s grandfathers. Moses would not go in, but he saw it.","Then Moses’s work on earth was done. The Lord took Moses to be with Him, and the people missed him. But Moses had put his hands on Joshua’s head. Joshua was filled with wisdom to lead them.","Heavenly Father always gives His people someone to help them. Tonight, think of someone who leads you kindly. Thank Heavenly Father for them."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau is brave, and tonight Moses climbs a tall mountain and Joshua is ready to lead. In Beau’s world today: Beau’s teacher asked him to lead the line to the playground. He felt a little nervous, but he stood tall and walked carefully. Just like Joshua, Beau learned that Heavenly Father helps us be brave when it is our turn to lead.","feel":"Pretend to climb a tall mountain, step, step, step. Now shade your eyes with your hand and look far away. Can you show me what Moses saw?","stones":[["SPOT","Moses saw the promised land. The people missed Moses, but the Lord gave them Joshua."],["NAME","That calm, cared-for, safe-again feeling is called comfort. Your mind might think, ‘Heavenly Father still takes care of us.’ Your body might feel soft and calm, like a warm hug."],["FIND","When have you missed someone, and someone helped you feel better?"],["FORK","Joshua could say, “I can’t,” or trust the Lord and lead. Joshua trusted the Lord, and the people listened to him."],["CALM","Rest your hands softly on your blanket. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for always taking care of us.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father always takes care of His people!","ask":["“What did Moses see from the mountain top?”","“Who was the new leader after Moses?”","“Where did Beau lead the line?”"],"share":["When has the Lord comforted you after someone you love was gone?","Which leaders has the Lord given our family, and how do they help us?","Which grandparent or ancestor finished their work faithfully? Share a story about them."]},"b5-w4-n1":{"story":["Moses’s work on earth was done. Now the Lord chose Joshua to lead His people.","Joshua had a big new job. He had to lead everyone across a river into the promised land.","The Lord said, “Be strong and of a good courage. Do not be afraid.” He meant, be brave, even if you feel a little scared.","The Lord who spoke to Joshua was Jesus. He said, “I was with Moses, and I will be with you. I will never leave you.” Then the people said, “We will go wherever you send us!”","Jesus is with you too, even in the dark. Tonight, make a strong arm. You can be brave, because Jesus is with you."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau is brave, and tonight the Lord told Joshua to be strong and brave. In Beau’s world today: Beau felt shy on his first day in a new class. He took a big breath and said, “Hi, I’m Beau.” Just like Joshua, Beau learned that he can be brave because Jesus is with him.","feel":"Sit up tall and make two strong arms, like Joshua. Now put one hand on your heart. Can you show me your strong, brave face?","stones":[["SPOT","Joshua had a big new job, and the Lord helped him feel brave."],["NAME","That strong, steady, I-can-do-it feeling is called courage. Your mind might think, ‘I can do hard things.’ Your body might feel tall and strong in your chest."],["FIND","When have you felt brave doing something new?"],["FORK","Joshua could be afraid and stay back, or trust the Lord. Joshua chose to be strong and brave."],["CALM","Make a strong arm, then let it rest. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me be brave.”"]],"remember":"Be strong and brave, because Jesus is with you everywhere you go!","ask":["“Who did the Lord choose to lead His people?”","“What did the Lord tell Joshua to be?”","“What did Beau say in his new class?”"],"share":["When has the Lord helped you be strong and brave?","How does reading the scriptures each day help you feel the Lord near?","Which grandparent or ancestor did something brave with faith? Share a story about them."]},"b5-w4-n2":{"story":["Joshua sent two men to quietly look at the city of Jericho. A woman named Rahab let them stay at her house.","Her house was built right into the city wall. Then the king’s men came looking for Joshua’s two men!","Rahab hid them on her flat roof. She tucked them under stalks of flax, a plant for making cloth.","Rahab said, “I know the Lord is in heaven above and on earth below.” She let the men down from her window on a red cord. They promised to keep her family safe. “Tie this red cord in your window,” they said. It was a promise you could see!","Rahab was kind and brave. Tonight, think of someone you can be kind to tomorrow."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow has a warm, kind heart, and Rahab was kind to two strangers who needed help. In Willow’s world today: A new family moved in next door. Willow felt shy, but she held Dad’s hand, knocked on their door and said, “Welcome!” Just like Rahab, Willow learned that being kind can take a brave heart.","feel":"Hold your hands up, like you are tying a red cord in a window. Now pull it snug. Can you show me your kind, helping hands?","stones":[["SPOT","Rahab helped the spies stay safe, and she felt kind and brave inside."],["NAME","That warm, helping, open-heart feeling is called kindness. Your mind might think, ‘I can help you.’ Your body might feel soft and warm in your heart."],["FIND","When have you helped someone who needed a friend?"],["FORK","Rahab could send the spies away, or keep them safe. Rahab chose to be kind and brave."],["CALM","Hug your pillow gently. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me be kind like Rahab.”"]],"remember":"Being kind can be brave, and Heavenly Father loves every kind thing you do!","ask":["“Where did Rahab hide the spies?”","“What color was the cord in Rahab’s window?”","“What did Willow say to the new neighbors?”"],"share":["When has someone’s kindness helped you feel the Lord’s love?","How can our family welcome people who feel alone?","Which grandparent or ancestor was known for kindness to strangers? Share a story about them."]},"b5-w4-n3":{"story":["Heavenly Father’s people came to the Jordan River. It was the time for picking crops, and the river was very full.","Joshua said, “Get your hearts ready. Tomorrow the Lord will do wonders!”","The priests, men chosen for the Lord’s special work, went first. They carried the ark, a gold-covered box that held the Lord’s commandments.","When the priests’ feet touched the water, the river stopped! Far away, the water stood up in a heap. The priests stood on dry ground, and every family walked across. The Lord was with Joshua, just as He was with Moses.","Heavenly Father can make a way for you too. Tonight, walk two fingers across your blanket, like a family crossing the river."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout loves to see amazing things, and tonight a whole river stopped so people could walk across. In Scout’s world today: Scout wanted to wash his hands, but the sink was too high. Dad brought a little step stool, and Scout climbed up and reached the water! Just like the families at the river, Scout learned that Heavenly Father helps us find a way.","feel":"Walk two fingers across your blanket, like families crossing the dry river. Tiptoe, tiptoe! Can you show me your happy, dry-feet walk?","stones":[["SPOT","The river stopped, and the people walked across feeling amazed and happy."],["NAME","That big, surprised, wow feeling is called wonder. Your mind might think, ‘Heavenly Father can do anything!’ Your body might feel tingly, with wide-open eyes."],["FIND","When have you seen something that made you say, “Wow”?"],["FORK","The priests could stay back from the deep water, or step in. They trusted the Lord and stepped in."],["CALM","Wiggle your toes, then hold them still. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for making a way.”"]],"remember":"The Lord made a dry path through the river, and He can help me too!","ask":["“What happened when the priests stepped into the river?”","“What did the priests carry?”","“How did Scout reach the sink?”"],"share":["When has the Lord opened a way for you when you could not see one?","What wonders of the Lord have you seen in our family?","Which grandparent or ancestor trusted the Lord on a hard journey? Share a story about them."]},"b5-w4-n4":{"story":["After everyone crossed the river, the Lord told Joshua to choose twelve men. There was one from each of the twelve big families.","Each man went to the middle of the dry river, where the priests stood. He lifted a big stone onto his shoulder.","They carried the twelve stones to camp. Joshua set them up so everyone would remember.","Joshua said, “One day your children will ask, ‘What do these stones mean?’ Tell them, ‘The Lord dried up the river. We walked across on dry land!’” The stones would help families tell the story again and again.","Tonight, think of one good thing Heavenly Father has done for your family. Keep it in your heart, like a little stone to remember."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren loves to think and remember, and tonight’s story is about stones that helped people remember. In Wren’s world today: Wren found a smooth stone on a family walk. She put it on her shelf. When she sees it, she remembers that happy day. Just like Joshua’s people, Wren learned that remembering helps us feel Heavenly Father’s love.","feel":"Pretend to pick up a heavy stone and carry it on your shoulder. Now set it down gently. Can you show me how you set down your stone?","stones":[["SPOT","The people set up twelve stones, and they felt thankful to remember."],["NAME","That warm, thank-you, I-remember feeling is called gratitude. Your mind might think, ‘Heavenly Father helped us!’ Your body might feel warm and happy in your heart."],["FIND","When have you remembered something good Heavenly Father did?"],["FORK","The people could forget the dry river, or set up stones to remember. They chose to remember and teach their children."],["CALM","Hold your hand still, like a quiet stone. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for all You do.”"]],"remember":"I can remember the good things Heavenly Father has done for me!","ask":["“How many stones did the men carry?”","“What will the children ask about the stones?”","“What did Wren put on her shelf?”"],"share":["What does our family keep to help us remember the Lord’s help?","When did remembering a past blessing help you through a hard day?","Which grandparent or ancestor’s story of faith should our family always remember? Share it now."]},"b5-w4-n5":{"story":["The city of Jericho had tall walls, and its gates were shut tight. The Lord, who was Jesus, gave Joshua a plan.","For six days, the people walked around the city once a day. Priests carried the ark. Seven priests blew trumpets made from sheep’s horns.","On the seventh day, they walked around seven times. Then Joshua said, “Shout! The Lord has given you the city!”","The people shouted, and the walls fell down flat! Rahab and her whole family were kept safe, just as the men promised. Later the scriptures said the walls fell by faith. The people trusted the Lord and kept walking.","You can trust the Lord too, with big things and small things. Tonight, rest in His care."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau is brave, and it took brave faith to keep walking and trust the Lord’s plan. In Beau’s world today: Beau planted seeds, but nothing came up for many days. Beau kept watering them every morning. At last, tiny green sprouts popped up! Just like Joshua’s people, Beau learned that trusting Heavenly Father can mean waiting and doing what is right.","feel":"Walk your fingers in a circle around your pillow. Now lift your arms for a quiet whisper-shout, “Hooray!” Can you show me your happy hooray?","stones":[["SPOT","The people trusted the Lord’s plan, and the walls fell. They felt joyful."],["NAME","That steady, I-believe, trusting feeling is called faith. Your mind might think, ‘The Lord has a good plan.’ Your body might feel calm and strong inside."],["FIND","When have you trusted someone, even when you had to wait?"],["FORK","The people could give up walking, or keep trusting the Lord. They kept walking and trusting."],["CALM","Rest both hands on your tummy. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, I trust Your plan.”"]],"remember":"When I trust the Lord and do what He says, wonderful things happen!","ask":["“How many times did they walk around on the seventh day?”","“Who was kept safe with her family?”","“What did Beau do every morning?”"],"share":["When did trusting the Lord’s timing bless you?","What hard “walls” has faith helped our family get past?","Which grandparent or ancestor kept trusting the Lord through a long wait? Share a story about them."]},"b5-w4-n6":{"story":["Joshua gathered all the people in a valley between two mountains. The priests stood in the middle with the ark.","Joshua wrote the Lord’s commandments that Moses taught onto big stones. Everyone watched him write.","Then Joshua read all the Lord’s words out loud. He did not skip one word that Moses had taught!","Everyone was there: the moms, the dads, the visitors, and the little children too. The children heard every word, just like the grown-ups. Many years later, Jesus said, “Let the little children come to me.”","The Lord’s words are for you too, just like those children. Tonight, lie still and listen with quiet ears."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout loves to learn new things, and tonight everyone, even the children, learned the Lord’s words. In Scout’s world today: Scout learned a short scripture by heart. He said it to Grandpa on a video call, and Grandpa clapped! Just like the children with Joshua, Scout learned that the Lord’s words are for children too.","feel":"Cup your hands behind your ears, like big listening ears. Now hold very still and quiet. Can you show me your best listening ears?","stones":[["SPOT","Joshua read the Lord’s words, and everyone, even children, felt glad to hear."],["NAME","That quiet, leaning-in, want-to-hear feeling is called reverence. Your mind might think, ‘These are words from the Lord.’ Your body might feel still and soft, with calm hands."],["FIND","When have you listened carefully to the scriptures?"],["FORK","Joshua could read to the grown-ups only, or to everyone. Joshua read every word to everyone, even children."],["CALM","Fold your hands softly. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me listen to Your words.”"]],"remember":"The Lord’s words are for everyone, and that means me too!","ask":["“What did Joshua read to the people?”","“Did the little children get to listen?”","“Who did Scout say his scripture to?”"],"share":["Which scripture verse has helped you most, and why?","How can we make family scripture time something our children look forward to?","Which grandparent or ancestor read the scriptures to their family? Share a memory of them."]},"b5-w4-n7":{"story":["Many years later, Joshua was very old. He called Heavenly Father’s people together and said, “Hold on tight to the Lord.”","Then he gathered them at a place called Shechem. He reminded them of all the good things the Lord had done for them.","Joshua said, “Choose today whom you will serve.” He meant, choose whom you will love and follow.","Joshua said, “As for me and my family, we will serve the Lord.” The people answered, “We will serve the Lord and obey His voice!” Joshua set up a big stone under an oak tree. It would help them remember their promise.","Your family can choose the Lord too. Tonight, hold hands with your grown-up. Say together, “We will serve the Lord.”"],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow loves her family with a warm heart, and tonight Joshua chose the Lord for his whole family. In Willow’s world today: Willow’s family knelt by the couch for family prayer. Willow asked, “Can I say it tonight?” Just like Joshua’s family, Willow learned that families can choose to serve the Lord together.","feel":"Hold your two hands together, like a family holding hands. Now give them a gentle squeeze. Can you show me your together hands?","stones":[["SPOT","Joshua chose the Lord for his family, and the people felt glad and sure."],["NAME","That snug, together, happy-home feeling is called belonging. Your mind might think, ‘Our family loves the Lord.’ Your body might feel warm, like a family hug."],["FIND","When have you felt close to your family while praying together?"],["FORK","The people could serve other things, or serve the Lord. They chose the Lord and promised to obey."],["CALM","Give yourself a soft family hug. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, our family chooses You.”"]],"remember":"As for me and my house, we will serve the Lord!","ask":["“Who did Joshua choose to serve?”","“What did Joshua set up to help people remember?”","“What did Willow ask at family prayer?”"],"share":["When did you choose for yourself to serve the Lord?","What is one way our family can serve the Lord this week?","Which grandparent or ancestor chose the Lord for their family? Share their story."]},"b5-w5-n1":{"story":["When Joshua was alive, Heavenly Father’s people served the Lord.","But their children grew up and forgot the Lord. They bowed down to statues that could not hear or help. Enemies took their things, and the people were very sad.","So the people cried to the Lord, and He heard them. The Lord sent judges, leaders He chose to help His people.","The first judge was Othniel. The scriptures call him a deliverer, someone who comes to set people free. The scriptures call Jesus the Deliverer too. He sets us free and brings us home to Heavenly Father.","When we forget, we can always come back. Tonight, thank Jesus in a little prayer for one kind thing He has done."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren loves to wonder and think, and tonight’s story asks us to remember the Lord. In Wren’s world today: Wren was playing so happily that she forgot to come when Mom called. Then she remembered and ran back. Mom gave her a big hug! Just like the people who cried to the Lord, Wren learned that He is always glad when we come back.","feel":"Put one finger on your head like you are thinking. Now open your hands wide, like the Lord sending help. Can you show me your remembering face?","stones":[["SPOT","The people forgot the Lord and felt sad, then the Lord sent help."],["NAME","That warm, safe, rescued feeling is called relief. Your mind might think, ‘Help came! I am okay now.’ Your body might feel your shoulders drop and your breath slow down."],["FIND","When have you felt relief because someone came to help you?"],["FORK","The people could keep forgetting, or they could cry to the Lord. They cried to Him, and He sent help."],["CALM","Let out one slow breath. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me always remember Jesus.”"]],"remember":"Jesus is our great Deliverer, and He always comes to help!","ask":["“What did the people forget?”","“Who did the Lord send to help?”","“What did Wren do when she remembered?”"],"share":["When has the Lord rescued you or our family in a hard time?","What helps you remember Jesus every day?","Which grandparent or ancestor kept remembering the Lord? Share their story."]},"b5-w5-n2":{"story":["For twenty years, a king named Jabin was hard on Heavenly Father’s people. They cried to the Lord.","Deborah told the people what the Lord said, and she led Israel. People came to her for help under a palm tree.","Deborah called a man named Barak. She said, “The Lord says, go to Mount Tabor with ten thousand men.”","Barak said, “If you will go with me, I will go.” Deborah said, “I will surely go with you,” and she went. Later she said, “The Lord has gone ahead of you!” And the Lord saved His people.","Jesus goes ahead of you too, and He sends helpers to stay beside you. Tonight, think of something big for tomorrow. Jesus goes first, and you can follow."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau is brave, like Barak, who went where the Lord asked with Deborah by his side. In Beau’s world today: Beau felt nervous at the dentist. Beau’s dad said, “I will stay right beside you.” Beau held his hand and sat in the big chair. Just like Barak, Beau learned that the Lord sends helpers and goes ahead of us.","feel":"Point one finger forward, like the Lord leading the way. Now march your feet under the blanket, following. Can you show me your brave marching feet?","stones":[["SPOT","Barak had a big job to do, and he felt brave with Deborah beside him."],["NAME","That strong, ready-to-go feeling is called courage. Your mind might think, ‘I can do this with help.’ Your body might feel tall and steady, with strong legs."],["FIND","When have you felt brave because someone was right beside you?"],["FORK","Barak could stay home, or he could go where the Lord asked. Barak went, with Deborah beside him."],["CALM","Squeeze your hands together, then let go. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for going ahead of me.”"]],"remember":"The Lord goes ahead of us, so we can be brave!","ask":["“Where did Deborah sit when people came to her?”","“Who went with Barak?”","“Who stayed beside Beau at the dentist?”"],"share":["Who has the Lord sent to walk beside you in a hard time?","When have you felt the Lord go ahead of you?","Which grandmother or ancestor was a strong woman of faith like Deborah? Share her story."]},"b5-w5-n3":{"story":["People from another land, the Midianites, kept taking Israel’s food. So Gideon hid by a grape press to beat the grain from his wheat.","An angel of the Lord came and sat under an oak tree. He said to Gideon, “The Lord is with you, you mighty, brave man!”","Gideon did not feel mighty or brave. “My family is poor,” he said. “I am the littlest in my father’s house.”","The Lord said, “Surely I will be with you.” Prophets teach that the Lord who spoke to Gideon was Jesus. Jesus saw how brave Gideon could become, not just how small he felt.","Jesus sees you that way too. Tonight, put your hand on your heart. Say softly, “The Lord is with me.”"],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow is warm and kind, and tonight the Lord speaks kindly to Gideon when he feels small. In Willow’s world today: Willow’s friend was learning to ride a bike and said, “I can’t do it.” Willow walked beside her and cheered, “You can! Keep trying!” Just like the Lord with Gideon, Willow learned that kind words help small hearts feel strong.","feel":"Curl up small like a little seed. Now slowly stretch your arms up tall and strong. Can you show me your mighty, brave arms?","stones":[["SPOT","Gideon felt small and unsure, and the Lord said, “I will be with you.”"],["NAME","That small, shaky, I-can’t feeling is called feeling unsure. Your mind might think, ‘I am too little for this.’ Your body might feel wobbly knees and a quiet voice."],["FIND","When have you felt too small to do something?"],["FORK","Gideon could say, “I can’t,” or he could trust the Lord. Gideon chose to listen and trust."],["CALM","Give yourself a gentle hug. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that Jesus is with me.”"]],"remember":"When I feel small, the Lord is with me!","ask":["“Where did the angel sit?”","“What did the angel call Gideon?”","“What did Willow cheer for her friend?”"],"share":["When did you feel too small for something the Lord asked of you?","How has the Lord helped you grow stronger?","Which grandparent or ancestor did something brave with the Lord’s help? Share it."]},"b5-w5-n4":{"story":["Gideon had a big job to do. He wanted to be sure Heavenly Father would help him save Israel.","So he put a fleece, the soft wool from a sheep, on the ground. He asked, “Let only the wool get wet with dew. Let the ground stay dry.”","Early the next morning, the ground was dry. But the wool was wet! Gideon squeezed out a whole bowl of water.","Gideon asked just one more time. “Now let the wool be dry and the ground be wet.” That night Heavenly Father did it. He was patient with Gideon and helped him know.","Heavenly Father is patient with you too. He hears the questions in your heart. Tonight, pray to Him. Then lie still and listen."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout loves to look closely and find out, just like Gideon checking the fleece early in the morning. In Scout’s world today: Scout wondered if Heavenly Father hears little kids. He asked his dad. Then he said a prayer and felt warm inside. Just like Gideon, Scout learned that Heavenly Father hears us and helps us know.","feel":"Lay your hands flat like a soft fleece. Wiggle your fingers like tiny dewdrops falling. Now squeeze your hands like Gideon. Can you show me your squeezing hands?","stones":[["SPOT","Gideon wanted to be sure, and he felt calm when Heavenly Father answered."],["NAME","That calm, settled, now-I-know feeling is called peace. Your mind might think, ‘Heavenly Father heard me.’ Your body might feel soft and still, like a warm blanket."],["FIND","When have you felt peace after you prayed?"],["FORK","Gideon could worry by himself, or he could ask Heavenly Father. Gideon asked, and Heavenly Father answered."],["CALM","Rest your hands on your blanket. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for hearing me.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father is patient and hears every prayer I say!","ask":["“What did Gideon put on the ground?”","“Was the fleece wet or dry the first morning?”","“What did Scout do when he wondered?”"],"share":["How has Heavenly Father answered one of your prayers?","When has Heavenly Father been patient with you?","Which grandparent or ancestor taught you to pray? Share a memory of them."]},"b5-w5-n5":{"story":["Many men came to help Gideon free Israel from the Midianites. The Lord said, “There are too many.” He wanted Israel to know the Lord saved them.","Anyone who felt afraid could go home. Then the Lord said, “Bring the rest down to the water.”","Only three hundred men drank from their hands. The Lord said, “By these three hundred I will save you.”","At night, each man held a trumpet and a clay jar. A lamp was hidden inside. They blew the trumpets and broke the jars. The lights shone! Every man stood in his place, and the Midianites ran away.","The Lord does not need us to be big. Tonight, hold up a pretend lamp. Your little light can shine for Him."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau is brave, like the three hundred men who stood still in the dark and trusted the Lord. In Beau’s world today: Beau’s night-light went out in the dark. He held his little flashlight, took a big breath, and said a prayer. Just like Gideon’s three hundred men, Beau learned that the Lord helps us be brave in the dark.","feel":"Hold one hand up high like a lamp. Put your other hand to your mouth like a trumpet. Toot softly! Can you show me your shining lamp?","stones":[["SPOT","Gideon’s small group felt brave, and they trusted the Lord in the dark."],["NAME","That steady, standing-still, trusting feeling is called faith. Your mind might think, ‘The Lord will help us.’ Your body might feel strong and calm in your chest."],["FIND","When have you trusted the Lord even when you felt small?"],["FORK","The three hundred could go home afraid, or they could trust the Lord. They stood in their places and trusted."],["CALM","Hold your lamp hand close to your heart. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me trust You.”"]],"remember":"The Lord can do big things with a small group who trust Him!","ask":["“How many men did the Lord choose?”","“What was hidden inside each jar?”","“What did Beau hold when it was dark?”"],"share":["When has the Lord helped our small family do something big?","When have you trusted the Lord in a dark time?","Which grandparent or ancestor trusted the Lord when it was hard? Share their story."]},"b5-w5-n6":{"story":["Manoah and his wife wanted a baby, but they had no children. One day an angel of the Lord came to her with happy news.","The angel said, “You will have a son.” The boy’s uncut hair would show a promise to Heavenly Father. He would begin to free Israel.","She told her husband. Manoah prayed, “Please send the man again. Teach us what to do for the child.”","Heavenly Father heard Manoah’s prayer, and the angel came again. Later, baby Samson was born. He grew, and the Lord blessed him.","Prophets teach that Heavenly Father knew you before you were born. Tonight, give your mom or dad a big hug. Your family is a gift from Him."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren likes to wonder about big things, like how Heavenly Father knew Samson before he was born. In Wren’s world today: Wren looked at her baby pictures with Grandma. “Did Heavenly Father know me then?” she asked. Grandma smiled and said, “Yes, He did.” Just like Manoah’s family, Wren learned that Heavenly Father knows and loves every child.","feel":"Rock your arms gently like you are holding a tiny baby. Hum a soft little song. Can you show me your gentle rocking arms?","stones":[["SPOT","Manoah and his wife heard a happy promise, and they prayed to learn more."],["NAME","That glowing, waiting, something-good feeling is called hope. Your mind might think, ‘Something good is coming!’ Your body might feel light and happy in your heart."],["FIND","When have you hoped for something good that was coming?"],["FORK","Manoah could just wait, or he could pray for help. Manoah prayed, and Heavenly Father heard him."],["CALM","Fold your arms and rest your head. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for knowing me.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father knows every child and blesses them as they grow!","ask":["“Who came to Manoah’s wife?”","“What did Manoah pray for?”","“What did Wren look at with Grandma?”"],"share":["How did you feel when our child first came into our family?","How has the Lord blessed our children as they grow?","Which grandparent or ancestor prayed for our family? Share what you know."]},"b5-w5-n7":{"story":["Samson grew up very strong. His strength came from the Lord. His long hair showed his special promise to Heavenly Father.","But Samson made poor choices. He told his secret to a woman named Delilah, and his hair was cut. The Lord’s strength left him.","Samson’s enemies, the Philistines, took him and locked him away. It was a sad, hard time. But slowly, his hair began to grow again.","Then Samson prayed, “O Lord, remember me. Please make me strong, just this once.” The Lord heard Samson’s prayer and gave him strength again.","Even after a wrong choice, Heavenly Father still remembers you. Jesus helps you turn back and start again. Tonight, talk to Him in a little prayer and feel His love."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow has a tender heart, and she knows Heavenly Father listens with love, even after a wrong choice. In Willow’s world today: Willow drew with a crayon on the wall, even though she knew it was wrong. She felt sad inside. She told Mom, helped wipe it clean and said a little prayer. Just like Samson, Willow learned that Heavenly Father hears us when we turn back to Him.","feel":"Make your arms strong like Samson. Now fold your hands and bow your head softly. Can you show me how you pray with all your heart?","stones":[["SPOT","Samson felt weak, so he prayed, asking the Lord to remember him."],["NAME","That small, quiet, please-help-me feeling is called hope. Your mind might think, ‘The Lord can still help me.’ Your body might feel a soft ache, then warmth."],["FIND","When have you felt sorry and wanted to make things right?"],["FORK","Samson could give up, or he could pray to the Lord. Samson prayed, and the Lord heard him."],["CALM","Fold your hands gently. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, please remember me and help me choose the right.”"]],"remember":"Even after a wrong choice, I can pray, and the Lord hears me!","ask":["“Where did Samson’s strength come from?”","“What did Samson ask the Lord?”","“What did Willow do after she drew on the wall?”"],"share":["When has prayer helped you start again after a mistake?","How has Jesus given you strength when you felt weak?","Which grandparent or ancestor kept praying through hard times? Share their story."]},"b16-w1-n1":{"story":["After feeding the big crowd, Jesus sent His friends across the lake. He went up a mountain alone to pray. That night, a strong wind tossed their boat.","Then they saw someone walking on the water! “It is a ghost!” they cried. They were so afraid.","Jesus called, “Cheer up. It is I. Do not be afraid.” Peter said, “Lord, if it is You, tell me to come.” Jesus said, “Come.”","Peter stepped out and walked on the water toward Jesus! Then he looked at the wind and waves. He got scared and began to sink. “Lord, save me!” Right away Jesus caught him.","When something feels scary, look at Jesus, not the waves. Tonight, whisper, “Jesus, help me.” Jesus will catch you too."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big heart, brave start!” Peter was brave enough to step out of the boat, and when he got scared and sank, Jesus caught him right away. Beau knows that brave people get scared, too. In Beau’s world today: Beau wants to swim to Dad in the deep end. He pushes off, kicking hard. Halfway there he looks down at the deep blue water and panics. “Dad!” Dad’s strong hand grabs his. “I’ve got you. Look at me, not the water.” Beau breathes, and they swim the rest together. Just like Peter, Beau learned that when fear makes us sink, Jesus reaches out and catches us.","feel":"Rock side to side like a boat in the wind. Now reach one hand way out, like Peter grabbing for Jesus, and hold on tight. Can you show me your holding-on hand?","stones":[["SPOT","The disciples saw Jesus on the water and were afraid. Peter walked, got scared, and sank. Jesus caught him."],["NAME","That I’m-going-under, help-me feeling is called afraid. Your mind might think, “I can’t do this!” Your body might feel your heart pound and your breath go fast."],["FIND","When did you feel afraid, and someone reached out and caught you?"],["FORK","Peter could keep looking at Jesus, or look at the waves. He looked at the waves, sank, and Jesus caught him anyway."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel a strong hand holding yours. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me keep my eyes on Jesus.”"]],"remember":"Jesus says, “It is I. Be not afraid,” and He always reaches out His hand!","ask":["“What did the disciples think they saw walking on the water?”","“What happened when Peter looked at the wind and waves?”","“What did Beau’s dad say when he grabbed Beau’s hand?”"],"share":["Share a time you took your eyes off Jesus, began to sink, and felt Him catch you.","Where is Jesus saying, “Come,” to you right now, and what makes you afraid to step out?","Did a grandparent or ancestor step out of the boat in faith? Tell their story."]},"b16-w1-n2":{"story":["Jesus went home to Nazareth, the town where He grew up. On the Sabbath, the day of rest, He taught there.","At first they were amazed. “Where did He learn all this? How can He do such mighty works?”","Then they said, “Isn’t this the carpenter, Mary’s son?” They had watched Him grow up. So they thought He was just one of them.","Jesus said, “People honor a prophet everywhere but in his own town.” They would not believe, so He healed only a few sick people. It surprised Jesus. But He did not quit. He went on to other villages, teaching.","Sometimes people close to us do not believe us. Jesus knows how that feels. Tonight, tell Him, then keep going like He did."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm has a tender heart, and tender hearts feel it when people they love let them down. Jesus’s own town would not believe Him, and Willow understands how that feels. In Willow’s world today: Willow learned to do a cartwheel and shows her big cousins. They barely look up. “That’s not a real one,” one says. Willow’s shoulders droop. Then she remembers that Jesus’s own town didn’t believe Him either. She does another cartwheel anyway, for herself. Just like Jesus in Nazareth, Willow learned that we can keep doing good even when the people around us don’t believe.","feel":"Lift your shoulders high, then let them droop, like being let down. Now lift your chin and stand tall anyway. Can you show me your keep-going face?","stones":[["SPOT","The people of Nazareth would not believe in Jesus. He felt disappointed, and He kept on teaching."],["NAME","That I-hoped-you’d-believe-me, let-down feeling is called disappointed. Your mind might think, “I thought they would understand.” Your body might feel droopy shoulders and a sigh."],["FIND","When did you hope someone would believe in you, and they did not?"],["FORK","Jesus could give up after Nazareth, or go on to the other villages. He kept going."],["CALM","Breathe out slowly and let the sigh go. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me keep going like Jesus.”"]],"remember":"Even when others don’t believe, we can keep doing good like Jesus!","ask":["“What town did Jesus go back to?”","“Why wouldn’t the people believe Jesus?”","“What did Willow do after her cousins didn’t care about her cartwheel?”"],"share":["Share a time someone close to you did not believe in your faith, and how you kept going.","How do you keep your heart open so Jesus can do mighty works in your family?","Did a grandparent or ancestor stay faithful when their own family or town did not believe? Tell about them."]},"b16-w1-n3":{"story":["Jesus’s twelve helpers came back from teaching in the villages. They told Jesus all they had done and taught.","So many people came and went that the helpers had no time to eat. Jesus saw how tired they were. Tired helpers need rest before they can help again.","He said, “Come away to a quiet place, and rest a while.” So they sailed away together in a boat.","Later they crossed the lake again. The people knew Jesus right away! They carried the sick to Him. They begged to touch just the edge of His robe. Everyone who touched Him was made well.","Jesus knows when you are tired, too. Tonight, snuggle down and rest a while. Let your body feel heavy and peaceful."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm knows that helpers need rest, too. Jesus saw His tired friends and said, “Come away and rest a while.” Willow thinks that is one of the kindest things He ever said. In Willow’s world today: Willow helped at the ward service project all morning, carrying boxes and handing out water. Now she is wobbly tired. Mom spreads a blanket under a tree and says, “Come rest a while.” Willow lies down, watches the leaves, and feels peaceful all over. Just like the apostles, Willow learned that Jesus wants His helpers to rest, and rest brings peace.","feel":"Pretend to row a boat to a quiet place, row, row, row. Now stop, lie back, and let your arms float at your sides. Can you show me your resting arms?","stones":[["SPOT","The apostles were tired from helping. Jesus took them to a quiet place to rest, and they felt peaceful."],["NAME","That soft, quiet, all-is-well feeling is called peaceful. Your mind might think, “I can rest now.” Your body might feel heavy, warm, and loose."],["FIND","When did you rest after a busy day, and your whole body felt peaceful?"],["FORK","The apostles could keep working until they dropped, or go with Jesus and rest. They rested with Him."],["CALM","Breathe out slowly and let your body sink into the bed. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for rest.”"]],"remember":"Jesus says, “Come away and rest a while,” because He loves His helpers!","ask":["“What did Jesus tell His tired apostles to do?”","“What did the sick people beg to touch?”","“Where did Willow rest after the service project?”"],"share":["Share a time you felt Jesus inviting you to rest, and what happened when you did.","How do you make room for quiet rest with the Savior in a busy week?","Did a grandparent or ancestor have a special way of resting and renewing their faith? Tell about it."]},"b16-w1-n4":{"story":["In Jerusalem there was a pool called Bethesda. Many sick people lay around it.","They waited for the water to stir. The first one in would be made well.","One man had been sick for thirty-eight years. That is a very long time! Jesus saw him lying there. He asked, “Do you want to be made well?”","The man said, “I have no one to help me into the pool.” But Jesus did not need the pool. He said, “Get up, pick up your mat, and walk.” Right away the man was well! He picked it up and walked!","Does something feel like it will never change? Jesus wants to help you. Tonight, tell Him what you hope for, then try once more tomorrow."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer pauses and ponders. The man had waited thirty-eight years and felt like nothing would ever change. Then Jesus walked up, and hope came back. In Wren’s world today: Wren has been trying to learn to whistle for months. Nothing but air. “I’ll never do it,” she sighs. Grandpa sits down. “Do you want to learn?” “Yes.” “Then try once more, like this.” She puckers, blows, and a tiny whistle comes out! Wren’s eyes go wide. Just like the man at the pool, Wren learned that when we feel discouraged, Jesus can still make things new.","feel":"Lie flat and heavy, like you cannot get up. Now hear Jesus say, “Rise!” and sit up, pick up your pretend bed, and march in place. Can you show me your rise-and-walk march?","stones":[["SPOT","The man had been sick for thirty-eight years and felt like nothing would change. Jesus healed him."],["NAME","That nothing-ever-changes, why-try feeling is called discouraged. Your mind might think, “It will never happen for me.” Your body might feel heavy and slumped."],["FIND","When did you feel like giving up on something, and then it finally happened?"],["FORK","The man could keep waiting for the pool, or listen to Jesus and rise. He listened and rose."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel a little lift in your chest. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me not give up.”"]],"remember":"When you feel discouraged, Jesus can say, “Rise,” and make things new!","ask":["“How many years had the man been sick?”","“What question did Jesus ask the man?”","“What did Wren finally learn to do with Grandpa’s help?”"],"share":["Share a time you felt stuck for years and Jesus made a change.","What is Jesus asking you, “Do you want to be made well?” about right now?","Did a grandparent or ancestor wait a long time for healing or change? Tell their story."]},"b16-w1-n5":{"story":["A huge crowd followed Jesus up a hill, about five thousand men! Jesus asked Philip, “Where can we buy bread for all these people?”","Philip said, “Even lots of money could not buy each one a bite!”","Then Andrew said, “A boy here has five barley loaves and two small fish. But what is that for so many?”","Jesus had everyone sit on the grass. He took the boy’s loaves and thanked Heavenly Father. Then His friends passed out bread and fish, as much as everyone wanted! Afterward they gathered the leftovers. They filled twelve baskets!","A little lunch in Jesus’s hands fed thousands. Tonight, think of one small thing you can share tomorrow. Jesus can make it enough."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker loves that a kid was the hero of this story. The boy handed over his whole lunch, five little loaves and two fish, and Jesus turned it into dinner for thousands. Scout wants to give like that. In Scout’s world today: At the park, a family’s picnic blew away in the wind. Scout looks at his snack bag: one sandwich and an apple. He walks over. “You can have mine.” Then other kids see, and they bring their snacks, too. Soon there is a whole new picnic. Just like the boy with the loaves, Scout learned that when we give what we have, Jesus can make it enough.","feel":"Hold out both hands like you are offering your lunch. Now watch it grow: spread your arms wider and wider, enough for everyone! Can you show me your giving hands?","stones":[["SPOT","A boy gave his five loaves and two fish to Jesus. He felt generous, and Jesus fed thousands."],["NAME","That here-take-mine, open-hands feeling is called generous. Your mind might think, “They need it more.” Your body might feel light and warm."],["FIND","When did you share something you really wanted to keep?"],["FORK","The boy could keep his lunch for himself, or give it to Jesus. He gave it all."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and open your hands on the blanket. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me give what I have.”"]],"remember":"Give Jesus what you have, and He can make it enough for everyone!","ask":["“What did the boy have in his lunch?”","“How many baskets of leftovers were gathered?”","“What did Scout give the family whose picnic blew away?”"],"share":["Share a time you gave something small and Heavenly Father made it enough.","What is the five loaves and two fish you can offer Jesus this week?","Did a grandparent or ancestor give generously, even from a little? Tell about them."]},"b16-w1-n6":{"story":["The next day, the crowd came looking for Jesus again. He said, “You came because you ate and were full. Work for food that lasts forever.”","They said, “Long ago, our fathers ate manna in the desert, bread from heaven.”","Jesus said, “My Father gives you the true bread from heaven. I am the bread of life. Whoever comes to Me will never be hungry.”","Why did Jesus call Himself bread? Back then, people ate bread every day to stay alive. Jesus keeps our spirits alive, the part inside us that loves. When we take the sacrament bread, we remember Him.","Your tummy gets hungry, and so does your spirit. Tonight, say a prayer or sing about Jesus. Feel your spirit fill up."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer pauses and ponders. Jesus said, “I am the bread of life,” and Wren thought about it for a long time: our spirits get hungry, too, and Jesus is what fills them. In Wren’s world today: Wren is grumpy and cannot say why. Mom says, “Let’s read a story about Jesus.” They read about the loaves and fish, and Wren feels calmer and warmer inside. “I think my spirit was hungry,” she says. Mom smiles. “Jesus is the bread of life.” Just like the crowd, Wren learned that Jesus is the bread of life who fills our hungry spirits.","feel":"Rub your tummy and make a hungry face. Now pretend to eat warm bread, mmm, and let your face go soft and happy. Can you show me your filled-up face?","stones":[["SPOT","The people were hungry for more bread. Jesus said He is the bread of life who fills our spirits."],["NAME","That empty, I-need-something feeling is called hungry. Your mind might think, “Something is missing.” Your body might feel an empty tummy or a restless heart."],["FIND","When did you feel empty or restless inside, and something good filled you up?"],["FORK","The people could chase bread that spoils, or come to Jesus for bread that lasts. Jesus asks us to come to Him."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly, like smelling warm bread. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for Jesus, my bread of life.”"]],"remember":"Jesus is the bread of life, and whoever comes to Him will never be hungry inside!","ask":["“What did Jesus call Himself?”","“What bread fell from heaven long ago in the desert?”","“What made Wren feel better when she was grumpy?”"],"share":["Share how Jesus has fed your spirit when you were hungry inside.","What “food that spoils” do you chase, and how do you come back to the bread of life?","Did a grandparent or ancestor find daily strength in scripture or the sacrament? Tell about them."]},"b16-w1-n7":{"story":["Jesus taught that He was the bread of life. Many followers said, “This is hard to hear. Who can listen to this?”","So many of them turned around and went away. They did not walk with Jesus anymore.","Jesus turned to His twelve closest friends. He asked them, “Will you go away too?”","Peter answered, “Lord, who else would we go to? Your words give us life forever. We believe, and we are sure. You are the Christ, the Son of the living God.” Peter could have left with the crowd. But he stayed, because he knew who Jesus was.","Sometimes others walk away from what is right. You can stay, like Peter did. Tonight, tell Jesus, “I want to stay with You.”"],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big heart, brave start!” When everyone else walked away, Peter stayed and said, “Lord, to whom shall we go?” Staying when others leave is one of the bravest things Beau knows. In Beau’s world today: The kids at the park decide the new boy is weird and walk away from him. Beau’s friends say, “Come on, Beau.” Beau looks at the new boy, alone by the slide, and says, “I’m staying.” He and the boy play until the streetlights come on. Just like Peter, Beau learned that being loyal means staying with Jesus even when others walk away.","feel":"Turn your head and look away, like the people who walked off. Now turn back, plant your feet, and fold your arms, staying put. Can you show me your staying feet?","stones":[["SPOT","Many people walked away from Jesus. Peter stayed and said, “To whom shall we go?” He felt loyal."],["NAME","That I’m-not-leaving, I’m-with-you feeling is called loyal. Your mind might think, “There is nowhere better to be.” Your body might feel planted feet and a steady heart."],["FIND","When did you stay with someone even when others left?"],["FORK","Peter could walk away with the crowd, or stay with Jesus. Peter stayed."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and plant your feet in your heart. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, I will stay with Jesus.”"]],"remember":"When others walk away, we can stay with Jesus, because He has the words of eternal life!","ask":["“What did many people do when Jesus’s words were hard?”","“What did Peter say when Jesus asked if the apostles would leave, too?”","“Why did Beau stay at the park?”"],"share":["Share a time you chose to stay with Jesus when it would have been easier to walk away.","What “hard saying” have you had to trust Jesus with, like Peter?","Did a grandparent or ancestor stay loyal to the gospel when others left? Tell their story."]},"b16-w2-n1":{"story":["Near Tyre and Sidon, a woman came to Jesus. She was not one of the children of Israel, His own people.","She cried, “Lord, please be kind to me! My daughter is sick.” At first Jesus did not say a word. His friends said, “Send her away.”","But the woman knelt down. “Lord, help me.”","Jesus said, “It is not right to give the children’s bread to the dogs.” She said, “Yes, Lord. But even the dogs eat the crumbs that fall.” Even a crumb of Jesus’s help was enough for her! Jesus said, “Woman, your faith is great!” Her daughter was made well that very hour.","The woman kept asking, even when it was hard. Tonight, keep asking Heavenly Father for what you need."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm would do anything for someone she loves. The woman would not stop asking Jesus to help her daughter, and her warm, determined heart won His praise. In Willow’s world today: Willow’s little brother lost his stuffed lamb at the store. Willow asks the first worker. “Sorry, no.” She asks a second. “No.” She asks at the front desk and will not leave. The lady checks one more box, and there is Lamby. Willow’s brother squeals. Just like the woman from Tyre, Willow learned that determined faith keeps asking, and Jesus honors it.","feel":"Kneel or sit up and hold out your hands, asking. Wait. Ask again. Ask one more time, and nod yes with a big smile! Can you show me your never-give-up hands?","stones":[["SPOT","The woman kept asking Jesus to heal her daughter, even when it was hard. She felt determined, and He healed her."],["NAME","That I-won’t-give-up, I’ll-ask-again feeling is called determined. Your mind might think, “My child needs this.” Your body might feel firm knees and steady eyes."],["FIND","When did you keep asking for something important even after you were told no?"],["FORK","The woman could go away quietly, or keep asking with faith. She kept asking."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your hands rest. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me have great faith like her.”"]],"remember":"Keep asking with faith, and Jesus will call your faith great!","ask":["“What did the woman ask Jesus to do?”","“What did she say about the crumbs?”","“How many people did Willow ask before she found Lamby?”"],"share":["Share a time you kept praying for a child or loved one and Jesus answered.","What has taught you to keep asking even when heaven seems quiet?","Did a grandparent or ancestor show determined faith for their family? Tell their story."]},"b16-w2-n2":{"story":["Jesus and His friends walked near a town called Caesarea Philippi. Jesus asked, “Who do people say that I am?”","They said, “Some say John the Baptist. Some say Elias, or Jeremiah, or another prophet.”","Then Jesus asked the most important question. “But who do you say that I am?”","Peter said, “You are the Christ, the Son of the living God.” Jesus said, “You are blessed, Peter. No person told you this. My Father in heaven showed you.” Jesus would build His church on this rock: truth from Heavenly Father. He promised Peter the keys, the power to lead His church.","You can ask Heavenly Father too. Tonight, kneel and ask, “Is Jesus really the Christ?” Then listen for a warm, calm feeling inside."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer pauses and ponders until she is sure. Peter did not guess. Heavenly Father showed him, and he knew. Wren loves the moment when wondering turns into knowing. In Wren’s world today: Wren asks Grandma, “How do you know Jesus is real?” Grandma says, “I asked Heavenly Father, and He told me in my heart.” That night Wren kneels and asks, too. A warm, calm feeling fills her chest. “I know,” she whispers. Just like Peter, Wren learned that Heavenly Father can show us that Jesus is the Christ, and then we know.","feel":"Tap your chin like you are wondering, hmm, hmm. Now put both hands on your heart and nod slowly, sure and certain. Can you show me your I-know nod?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus asked, “Who do you say I am?” Peter said, “Thou art the Christ,” because Heavenly Father showed him. He felt certain."],["NAME","That I-know-it-for-sure feeling is called certain. Your mind might think, “Heavenly Father told me.” Your body might feel a warm, calm chest and a steady voice."],["FIND","When did you wonder about something, and then you just knew?"],["FORK","Peter could repeat what others said, or say what Heavenly Father showed him. He said what he knew."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and put your hands on your heart. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, show me that Jesus is the Christ.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father can show you that Jesus is the Christ, and then you will know!","ask":["“What did Peter say when Jesus asked who He was?”","“Who showed Peter that Jesus is the Christ?”","“What did Wren do after she talked to Grandma?”"],"share":["Share how you came to know that Jesus is the Christ.","When has the Holy Ghost shown you something no person could have told you?","Did a grandparent or ancestor receive a testimony by revelation? Tell what they said."]},"b16-w2-n3":{"story":["A father knelt before Jesus. “Lord, please help my son. He is very sick. Your friends could not heal him.”","Jesus said, “Bring him to Me.” The boy had been sick since he was little. The father said, “If You can do anything, please help us!”","Jesus said, “If you can believe, all things are possible.”","The father cried, “Lord, I believe! Help my unbelief!” Part of him believed, and part of him was scared. He gave both parts to Jesus. Jesus healed the boy, took his hand, and lifted him up.","Later Jesus said faith as small as a mustard seed can move a mountain. Tonight, pray, “I believe. Please help the part of me that is scared.”"],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big heart, brave start!” The father was honest: “I believe. Help my unbelief.” Beau thinks it takes a brave heart to trust Jesus with the believing part and the not-sure part. In Beau’s world today: Beau’s dog is lost. He prays, “Heavenly Father, I believe You can bring her home. Help the part of me that’s scared You won’t.” He puts up signs with Dad. Two days later, a neighbor calls. Beau hugs his dog and thinks, “He helped both parts.” Just like the father, Beau learned that we can trust Jesus with our faith and our doubts at the same time.","feel":"Hold up one hand: this is the believing part. Hold up the other: this is the not-sure part. Now bring them together and fold them to pray. Can you show me both hands praying?","stones":[["SPOT","The father believed a little and doubted a little. He trusted Jesus with both, and Jesus healed his son."],["NAME","That I-believe-and-I’m-also-scared feeling is called trust. Your mind might think, “Jesus, help all of me.” Your body might feel hands folded and a deep breath."],["FIND","When did you believe something good would happen, but part of you was still scared?"],["FORK","The father could hide his doubt, or bring all of it to Jesus. He brought it all, and Jesus helped."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly with your hands folded. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, I believe. Help my unbelief.”"]],"remember":"Say, “I believe. Help my unbelief,” and Jesus will help both parts!","ask":["“What did the father say when Jesus told him to believe?”","“What did Jesus say about faith as small as a mustard seed?”","“What did Beau pray when his dog was lost?”"],"share":["Share a time you prayed, “I believe, help my unbelief,” and how Jesus answered.","What mountain in your life needs mustard-seed faith right now?","Did a grandparent or ancestor trust Heavenly Father through doubt? Tell about them."]},"b16-w2-n4":{"story":["Jesus and His friends came to Capernaum, a town by the lake. Each year, men paid money to care for the temple.","Some men asked Peter, “Doesn’t your Teacher pay the temple money?” “Yes,” said Peter.","Inside, Jesus spoke first. “Peter, do kings make their own children pay? Or other people?” “Other people,” said Peter. “Then the children are free,” said Jesus.","The temple was His Father’s house, so Jesus did not have to pay. But He did not want to upset anyone. He said, “Throw a hook into the lake. Open the first fish’s mouth, and you will find a coin. Pay for Me and for you.”","Jesus paid kindly when He did not have to. Tonight, plan one kind thing for tomorrow."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see, what could it be?” A coin inside a fish’s mouth? Scout would have run straight to the lake with a fishing pole, and he would have laughed out loud when he found it. In Scout’s world today: Scout cracks open his piggy bank to help pay for his sister’s lost library book. He doesn’t have to, Mom said, but he wants to. Inside, under the pennies, he finds a shiny old coin Grandpa hid there as a surprise. “Whoa!” Scout laughs. “Just like the fish!” Just like Peter, Scout learned that when we do the kind thing, Heavenly Father can surprise us with what we need.","feel":"Pretend to throw a fishing line into the lake, whoosh. Reel it in, reel, reel. Open the fish’s mouth, and gasp: a coin! Can you show me your surprised gasp?","stones":[["SPOT","Peter needed a coin for the temple. Jesus sent him to a fish, and the coin was in its mouth. Peter felt surprised."],["NAME","That whoa, how-did-that-get-there feeling is called surprised. Your mind might think, “I didn’t expect that!” Your body might feel a gasp and a laugh."],["FIND","When did you find something in a surprising place?"],["FORK","Jesus could refuse to pay, since He did not have to, or pay kindly so no one was upset. He chose the kind way."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your laugh settle into a smile. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for taking care of what I need.”"]],"remember":"Jesus did the kind thing even when He didn’t have to, and He always provides!","ask":["“Where did Jesus tell Peter to find the coin?”","“Why did Jesus pay the temple money even though He didn’t have to?”","“What did Scout find in his piggy bank?”"],"share":["Share a time Heavenly Father provided for you in a surprising way.","When have you done the kind thing to avoid upsetting others, even when you didn’t have to?","Did a grandparent or ancestor see Heavenly Father provide in an unexpected way? Tell their story."]},"b16-w2-n5":{"story":["Some leaders came from Jerusalem to watch Jesus. They saw His friends eat without washing their hands the leaders’ special way.","These leaders had many extra rules, made by people long ago. They asked, “Why don’t Your friends keep our old rules?”","Jesus said, “You keep your own rules, but you let go of Heavenly Father’s.”","Heavenly Father’s commandment says, “Honor your father and your mother.” To honor means to love, respect, and help. But the leaders let a man call his money a gift to Heavenly Father. Then he did not help his parents. Jesus taught that helping parents matters more than made-up rules.","Tonight, honor your mom or dad. Give a hug, say thank you, or help with one small job."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big heart, brave start!” Jesus told the leaders that honoring your father and mother is Heavenly Father’s own commandment, and Beau honors his parents with a big heart. In Beau’s world today: Mom asks Beau to help carry the laundry upstairs. His friends are waiting outside. He wants to say, “Later.” Instead he says, “Yes, Mom,” grabs the basket, and runs it up. Mom says, “Thank you, Beau.” He feels tall as he runs out to play. Just like Jesus taught, Beau learned that honoring his father and mother is one of Heavenly Father’s most important commandments.","feel":"Put your hand over your heart and bow your head toward your mom or dad, like a knight honoring the king. Now give them a big hug. Can you show me your honor bow?","stones":[["SPOT","The leaders cared about their own rules. Jesus said honoring your parents is Heavenly Father’s commandment."],["NAME","That I-respect-you, I’ll-take-care-of-you feeling is called honor. Your mind might think, “They matter.” Your body might feel a bowed head and helping hands."],["FIND","When did you help your mom or dad right away, and it made you feel good?"],["FORK","Beau could say, “Later,” to his mom, or honor her and help. He helped."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly with your hand on your heart. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me honor my mom and dad.”"]],"remember":"Honor your father and mother, because Heavenly Father asked you to!","ask":["“What commandment did Jesus remind the leaders about?”","“What does honor mean?”","“What did Beau do when his mom asked for help with the laundry?”"],"share":["Share how you have honored your own parents, and what it has meant to you.","What traditions do you keep, and do they help you keep Heavenly Father’s commandments?","Tell your child about a grandparent or ancestor who honored their parents well."]},"b16-w2-n6":{"story":["Jesus came to a land of ten cities. People brought Him a man who could not hear. He could hardly talk, either.","Jesus took the man away from the crowd. He put His fingers in the man’s ears and touched his tongue. The man could not hear words, but he could feel Jesus’s touch.","Then Jesus looked up to heaven and sighed. He said, “Ephphatha!” That means, “Be opened.”","Right away the man’s ears opened, and he spoke plainly! Jesus asked the people not to tell. But they told everyone! They said, “He has done all things well. He makes the deaf hear and helps people talk.”","Tonight, be still and listen. What can you hear? Thank Heavenly Father for your ears, and for Jesus."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker loves to hear every sound, the birds, the wind, the river. Jesus said, “Be opened,” and a man heard for the first time. Scout thinks that is the most amazing thing. In Scout’s world today: Scout’s grandpa gets new hearing aids. At dinner, Grandpa suddenly stops. “I can hear the clock ticking,” he says. “I can hear you chewing!” Everyone laughs. Scout watches Grandpa’s face and thinks, “He’s hearing everything, like the man Jesus healed.” Just like the crowd in Decapolis, Scout learned that Jesus does all things well.","feel":"Cover your ears tight so it’s quiet. Now whisper, “Be opened,” and take your hands away. Listen! What do you hear? Can you show me your listening ears?","stones":[["SPOT","The man could not hear or speak. Jesus said, “Be opened,” and he could. The people felt amazed."],["NAME","That I-can’t-believe-it, He-did-it feeling is called amazed. Your mind might think, “He does all things well!” Your body might feel wide eyes and an open mouth."],["FIND","When did you hear or see something so amazing that you could not keep quiet?"],["FORK","The people could keep quiet like Jesus asked, or burst with amazement. They could not stop praising Him."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and listen to the quiet. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that Jesus does all things well.”"]],"remember":"Jesus does all things well, so listen and be amazed!","ask":["“What does Ephphatha mean?”","“What did the people say about Jesus after the man was healed?”","“What could Scout’s grandpa suddenly hear at dinner?”"],"share":["Share a time Jesus opened your ears or heart to hear something new.","What has Jesus done in your life that makes you say, “He has done all things well”?","Did a grandparent or ancestor experience a healing that amazed your family? Tell about it."]},"b16-w2-n7":{"story":["John, one of Jesus’s friends, came to Him with a worry. “Teacher, a man was doing good in Your name. He was not one of us, so we told him to stop.”","Jesus said, “Do not stop him. Whoever is not against us is on our side.”","Jesus wanted His friends to be glad when anyone does good.","Then Jesus said something small and wonderful. Anyone who gives you a cup of water for His sake will be rewarded. That land was hot and dusty. A drink of water was a real kindness. Jesus notices even little kind things.","Who could use a little kindness from you tomorrow? Tonight, plan one small gift, like a cup of cold water. Jesus will notice."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” Jesus said even a cup of cold water given in His name is remembered, and Willow is always thinking of small ways to help. In Willow’s world today: The mail carrier looks hot and tired on Willow’s street. Willow runs inside, fills a cup with cold water and ice, and brings it out. “For you,” she says. The mail carrier drinks it all. “You just made my whole day,” she says. Willow glows. Just like Jesus taught, Willow learned that small kindnesses, even a cup of cold water, are remembered by Him.","feel":"Pretend to fill a cup with cold water, careful not to spill. Now hand it gently to someone who is thirsty. Can you show me your cup-of-water hands?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus said even a cup of cold water given in His name is remembered. His disciples felt thoughtful about small kindnesses."],["NAME","That what-can-I-do-for-you, noticing feeling is called thoughtful. Your mind might think, “They look thirsty.” Your body might feel your eyes looking around and your hands ready."],["FIND","When did you notice something small that someone needed, and you did it?"],["FORK","John wanted to stop the man doing good, or be glad for him. Jesus said be glad."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your helping hands rest. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me notice small ways to be kind.”"]],"remember":"Jesus remembers even a cup of cold water given in His name!","ask":["“What did Jesus say about the man doing good who was not one of them?”","“What small gift did Jesus say would not lose its reward?”","“Who did Willow bring a cup of cold water to?”"],"share":["Share a small kindness someone gave you that you have never forgotten.","Who in your life is doing good that you could cheer for instead of judge?","Was there a grandparent or ancestor known for small, thoughtful kindnesses? Tell about them."]},"b16-w3-n1":{"story":["Jesus’s friends came to Him with a question. “Who is the greatest in the kingdom of heaven?”","Jesus called a little child over. He stood the child right in the middle of them all.","Then Jesus said, “Change, and become like little children. If you don’t, you cannot come into the kingdom of heaven.”","Why a child? A little child knows they need help. Jesus said, “Whoever is humble like this little child is the greatest.” Being humble is like a child reaching up for a grown-up’s hand. Jesus said little ones are treasured. “In heaven, their angels always see My Father’s face.”","You are small, and you are treasured, too. Tonight, reach your hands up and say, “Heavenly Father, I need You.”"],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer pauses and ponders. Jesus said that in heaven, the angels of little children always see Heavenly Father’s face. Wren wonders at that, and she feels treasured. In Wren’s world today: At the family party, the grown-ups are talking about big things. Grandpa stops, lifts Wren onto his lap, and says, “This one is the most important person at this table.” Everyone smiles at her. Wren feels small and treasured at the same time. Just like the little child Jesus held, Wren learned that Heavenly Father treasures every little one and watches over them.","feel":"Make yourself small like a little child, knees tucked. Now feel a big, warm hand lift you up, and sit up tall in the middle of everyone. Can you show me your treasured sit?","stones":[["SPOT","The disciples asked who was greatest. Jesus set a child in the middle and said little ones are treasured in heaven."],["NAME","That I’m-precious-to-Him, watched-over feeling is called treasured. Your mind might think, “Heavenly Father sees me.” Your body might feel warm, small, and safe."],["FIND","When did a grown-up make you feel like the most important person in the room?"],["FORK","The disciples could try to be the biggest, or become humble like a child. Jesus asked them to be like a child."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel watched over. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that I am treasured.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father treasures every little child, and their angels see His face!","ask":["“Who did Jesus stand in the middle of the disciples?”","“What did Jesus say the angels of little children do in heaven?”","“What did Grandpa say about Wren at the family party?”"],"share":["Share what it means to you to become like a little child before Heavenly Father.","How do you show the little ones in your life that they are treasured?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who treasured children."]},"b16-w3-n2":{"story":["Jesus told a little story. A man had a hundred sheep. One sheep wandered away and got lost.","A lost sheep cannot find its own way home. So the man left the ninety-nine other sheep. He went up into the hills to look for the lost one.","Jesus said that when the man found it, he was so happy! He was happier over that one sheep than the ninety-nine safe ones.","Why did Jesus tell this story? He wanted everyone to know that each little one matters. Jesus said Heavenly Father does not want even one little one to be lost.","Jesus is the Good Shepherd. If you ever feel lost, He comes looking for you. Tonight, say, “Thank You, Jesus, for finding me.”"],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm knows the scared feeling of being lost and the warm whoosh of being found. The shepherd went looking for one little sheep and carried it home, and Willow thinks that is Jesus exactly. In Willow’s world today: Willow turns around at the fair, and Mom is gone. Everything is loud and big. Her lip trembles. Then she hears, “Willow!” Mom is running toward her with her arms out. Willow runs, too, and Mom scoops her up. “I came looking the second I didn’t see you.” Just like the lost sheep, Willow learned that Jesus comes looking for us, and He is so happy when He finds us.","feel":"Curl up small and quiet, like a lost sheep behind a rock. Now hear the shepherd call your name, pop up, and let him lift you onto his shoulders. Can you show me your found-sheep stretch?","stones":[["SPOT","One sheep wandered off and got lost. The shepherd came looking, found it, and was so happy."],["NAME","That somebody-came-for-me, I’m-safe-now feeling is called found. Your mind might think, “He didn’t forget me.” Your body might feel a big breath out and arms wrapped around you."],["FIND","When did you feel lost, and someone came looking for you?"],["FORK","The shepherd could stay with the ninety-nine, or go find the one. He went to find the one."],["CALM","Breathe out slowly, like being scooped up safe. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that Jesus comes to find me.”"]],"remember":"Jesus is the Good Shepherd, and He comes looking for every lost little one!","ask":["“How many sheep did the man have, and how many got lost?”","“How did the shepherd feel when he found the lost sheep?”","“What did Willow’s mom do when she couldn’t see Willow at the fair?”"],"share":["Share a time you felt lost and Jesus came looking for you.","Who is the one that Jesus might be sending you to find right now?","Did a grandparent or ancestor come back to the gospel after wandering? Tell their story."]},"b16-w3-n3":{"story":["Jesus taught His friends what to do when someone does something wrong to you.","He said, “Go and talk to him, just the two of you.” Don’t tell everyone else first, and don’t stay mad. Talk kindly, face to face.","Jesus said, “If he listens, you have won your brother back.” The hurt is mended, and you are friends again.","Then Jesus made a promise. “If two of you agree and ask, My Father in heaven will do it.” He said, “Where two or three gather in My name, I am there.”","When your family prays together, Jesus is right there. Tonight, hold someone’s hand and pray together."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker is always looking for Jesus. Jesus gave him a clue: “Where two or three are gathered in My name, there am I.” Now Scout knows exactly where to find Him. In Scout’s world today: Scout is nervous about the thunderstorm, so the whole family kneels together in the living room to pray. Dad prays, then Mom, then Scout. When they finish, the room feels warm and calm. “Jesus is here,” Scout says. “He promised.” Just like Jesus promised, Scout learned that when we gather in His name, He is with us, and that is comforting.","feel":"Hold up two fingers, then three. Now hold the hand of someone next to you and fold your arms together. Jesus is here! Can you show me your gathered hands?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus promised that where two or three gather in His name, He is there. The disciples felt comforted."],["NAME","That He’s-right-here-with-us, not-alone feeling is called comforted. Your mind might think, “Jesus promised to be here.” Your body might feel warm, calm, and close."],["FIND","When did praying or sitting with your family make you feel that Jesus was close?"],["FORK","When someone hurts us, we can tell everyone else, or go talk kindly to them. Jesus says go talk kindly."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel the people who love you close by. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that Jesus is here with us.”"]],"remember":"Where two or three gather in Jesus’s name, He is right there with them!","ask":["“What did Jesus say to do first when your brother does something wrong to you?”","“Where did Jesus promise to be when two or three gather in His name?”","“What did Scout’s family do together during the thunderstorm?”"],"share":["Share a time you felt Jesus in the midst when your family or ward gathered.","Is there someone you need to go and talk to kindly, face to face?","Did a grandparent or ancestor hold family prayer or gather the family in faith? Tell about it."]},"b16-w3-n4":{"story":["Peter asked Jesus, “How many times should I forgive my brother? Seven times?” Jesus said, “Not seven times. Seventy times seven!” That is more times than you could ever count.","Jesus told a story. A servant owed a king more money than he could ever pay.","The servant fell down and begged, “Please be patient with me!” The king felt sorry for him. He forgave all of it.","But that servant would not forgive a man who owed him just a little. He had him put in prison. The king was very upset. “I forgave you. Shouldn’t you forgive him, too?”","Jesus said to forgive from your heart, the way Heavenly Father forgives us. Tonight, think of someone, and whisper, “I forgive you.”"],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big heart, brave start!” Forgiving someone seventy times seven takes a big, brave heart. Beau has been forgiven so much that he wants to forgive big, too. In Beau’s world today: Beau’s friend Noah keeps bumping his tower over, three times in one afternoon. Beau feels his fists tighten. Then he remembers how Mom forgave him for breaking her vase. He takes a breath. “It’s okay, Noah. Let’s build it together this time.” Just like the king’s servant should have done, Beau learned that because we are forgiven so much, we forgive others from the heart.","feel":"Count on your fingers: one, two, three, four, five, six, seven. Now wiggle all your fingers fast, way too many to count! Open your hands wide. Can you show me seventy-times-seven forgiving hands?","stones":[["SPOT","The king forgave his servant a huge debt. Jesus taught us to forgive each other the same way, from the heart."],["NAME","That let-it-go, I’m-not-holding-it feeling is called forgiving. Your mind might think, “I’ve been forgiven, so I’ll forgive.” Your body might feel loose fists and a soft face."],["FIND","When did you forgive someone who had hurt you more than once?"],["FORK","The servant could forgive the small debt like he was forgiven, or refuse. Jesus asks us to forgive from the heart."],["CALM","Breathe out slowly and open your fists. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me forgive like You forgive me.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father forgives us so much, so we forgive others seventy times seven!","ask":["“How many times did Jesus say to forgive?”","“What did the king do about the servant’s huge debt?”","“What did Beau say to Noah after his tower fell the third time?”"],"share":["Share a time you forgave from the heart because you remembered how much you had been forgiven.","Who do you need to forgive seventy times seven, and what makes it hard?","Did a grandparent or ancestor forgive someone in a way that changed your family? Tell their story."]},"b16-w3-n5":{"story":["Jesus chose seventy more helpers. He sent them out two by two, to every town He would visit.","He said, “The harvest is great, but the workers are few.” Many people were ready to hear about Jesus. They were like a field of ripe grain, waiting to be gathered.","He told them, “Say, ‘Peace be to this house.’ Heal the sick. Tell them Heavenly Father’s kingdom has come near.”","The seventy came back full of joy! “Lord, even the evil spirits obey us in Your name!” Jesus said to be happiest about something better. “Your names are written in heaven.”","You can be Jesus’s helper, too. Tonight, plan one kind thing to share tomorrow with a friend, two by two."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see, what could it be?” The seventy got to go out two by two to see new towns and share good news. Scout would have been the first to pack his bag. In Scout’s world today: The Primary teacher asks for two helpers to deliver cookies to a lonely neighbor. Scout’s hand shoots up. He and his friend Ava walk the plate over, say, “We brought these because Jesus loves you,” and the neighbor’s eyes get shiny. Scout runs back eager to go again. Just like the seventy, Scout learned that going out to share Jesus’s good news fills us with joy.","feel":"Pretend to put on your shoes, tie them tight, and march out the door two by two. Knock on a door and say, “Peace to this house!” Can you show me your two-by-two march?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus sent seventy helpers out two by two. They went eagerly and came back full of joy."],["NAME","That let’s-go, I-can’t-wait-to-help feeling is called eager. Your mind might think, “Send me!” Your body might feel quick feet and a hand shooting up."],["FIND","When did you want to help so much that your hand shot up before anyone else’s?"],["FORK","The seventy could stay home where it was easy, or go out two by two. They went."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your eager feet rest. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, I am ready to help You.”"]],"remember":"The harvest is great, and you can be one of Jesus’s eager helpers!","ask":["“How did Jesus send the seventy helpers out?”","“What did Jesus say to be happiest about?”","“What did Scout and Ava say when they delivered the cookies?”"],"share":["Share a time you were sent to serve and came back full of joy.","What does it mean to you that your name is written in heaven?","Did a grandparent or ancestor serve a mission or go two by two? Tell their story."]},"b16-w3-n6":{"story":["A man who knew the scriptures asked Jesus a question. The scriptures say, “Love your neighbor as yourself.” So he asked, “But who is my neighbor?”","Jesus told a story. Robbers hurt a man on the road and left him there.","A priest saw him and walked past on the other side. A temple helper did the same.","A Samaritan came by. Samaritans and Jews did not get along. But he felt sorry and stopped. He wrapped his hurts, took him to an inn, and paid for his care. Jesus asked, “Who was a neighbor?” The man said, “The one who was kind.” Jesus said, “Go, and do the same.”","Your neighbor is anyone who needs help. Tonight, think of someone you can help tomorrow."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm has a heart full of compassion. Two people walked past the hurt man, but the Samaritan stopped and cared. That is exactly what Willow would do. In Willow’s world today: A boy falls off his scooter at the park and scrapes his knee. Some kids keep playing. Willow runs over, sits down next to him, and gives him her water bottle and a bandage from her pocket. “Does it hurt a lot?” she asks. He nods, then smiles. Just like the Good Samaritan, Willow learned that compassion means stopping to help whoever is hurting.","feel":"Walk your fingers past a pretend hurt person, nope, keep walking. Now turn around, kneel down, and gently wrap a bandage around your own arm. Can you show me your stopping-to-help hands?","stones":[["SPOT","Two people walked past the hurt man. The Samaritan felt compassion, stopped, and took care of him."],["NAME","That I-see-you’re-hurt, I-can’t-walk-past feeling is called compassion. Your mind might think, “He needs me.” Your body might feel your feet stop and your hands reach out."],["FIND","When did you see someone hurt or sad and stopped to help?"],["FORK","The Samaritan could walk past like the others, or stop and help. He stopped."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and make your hands gentle. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me be a good neighbor.”"]],"remember":"Your neighbor is anyone who needs help, so go and do the same!","ask":["“Who walked past the hurt man without helping?”","“What did the Samaritan do for the hurt man?”","“What did Willow give the boy who fell off his scooter?”"],"share":["Share a time someone unexpected was a Good Samaritan to you.","Who is lying by the side of your road right now, and what would it take to stop?","Did a grandparent or ancestor stop to help strangers? Tell about them."]},"b16-w3-n7":{"story":["Jesus came to a village. A woman named Martha welcomed Him into her house.","Her sister Mary sat down at Jesus’s feet. She listened to every word He said.","But Martha was busy, busy, busy serving. There was so much to do! She began to feel upset.","Martha said, “Lord, don’t You care that my sister left me to work alone? Tell her to help me!” Jesus said gently, “Martha, Martha, you are worried about many things. Only one thing is needed. Mary has chosen the good part. It will not be taken from her.”","Jesus was not saying that helping is bad. But with Jesus right there, listening to Him came first. Tonight, sit still and listen, like Mary."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer says, “Pause and ponder.” Mary paused everything and sat at Jesus’s feet to listen. Wren knows that stopping to listen is the good part. In Wren’s world today: Grandma comes to visit, and Wren wants to show her every toy, every drawing, every book, all at once. Grandma pats the couch. “Come sit with me, and let’s just talk.” Wren sits, and Grandma tells her a story from when she was little. Wren forgets all about the toys. Just like Mary, Wren learned that when Jesus is near, the best thing is to stop being busy and listen.","feel":"Wiggle your hands and feet fast, busy, busy, busy like Martha! Now stop, fold your hands in your lap, and sit perfectly still like Mary. Can you show me your listening stillness?","stones":[["SPOT","Martha was busy and upset. Mary sat and listened to Jesus. Jesus said Mary chose the good part."],["NAME","That stop-everything-and-listen feeling is called focused. Your mind might think, “This matters most.” Your body might feel still hands and open ears."],["FIND","When did you stop being busy and really listen to someone you love?"],["FORK","Martha could stay busy and upset, or sit down with Mary and listen. Jesus invited her to choose the good part."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your hands be still. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me sit still and listen to Jesus.”"]],"remember":"When Jesus is near, stop being busy and sit down to listen!","ask":["“What was Mary doing while Martha was busy?”","“What did Jesus say Mary had chosen?”","“What did Wren’s grandma ask her to do instead of showing all her toys?”"],"share":["Share a time you chose the good part and sat with Jesus instead of staying busy.","What is making you worried and troubled about many things, and how could you sit at His feet?","Did a grandparent or ancestor know how to be still with Heavenly Father? Tell about them."]},"b16-w4-n1":{"story":["Every year, Heavenly Father’s people kept a happy feast for seven days. They lived in little huts made of branches.","The huts helped them remember long ago. Heavenly Father had cared for their people in the desert. When they were thirsty, He gave them water from a rock!","On the last great day, Jesus stood up. He called out in a loud voice.","“If anyone is thirsty, let him come to Me and drink.” Jesus meant a thirsty heart, one that needs love and peace. He said rivers of living water would flow from those who believe. He was talking about the Holy Ghost, who fills our hearts.","Is your heart thirsty tonight? Tell Jesus, “Please fill my heart.” He invites everyone to come."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker has felt really thirsty after a long hike, when all he can think about is water. Jesus said, “If anyone is thirsty, come to Me.” Scout knows exactly where to run. In Scout’s world today: Scout hikes to the waterfall with Dad on a hot day. His water bottle is empty and his mouth is dry. Then they reach the falls, and Dad fills his bottle from the cold spring. Scout drinks and drinks. “That’s the best water ever,” he says. Dad says, “Jesus is like that for our hearts.” Just like the crowd at the feast, Scout learned that when we are thirsty inside, Jesus is the living water.","feel":"Pant like you are thirsty after a long hike, huff, huff. Now cup your hands, scoop up cool water, and drink a long, slow drink. Ahh! Can you show me your refreshed face?","stones":[["SPOT","The people remembered water in the desert. Jesus said, “If anyone is thirsty, come to Me.” Thirsty hearts came."],["NAME","That dry, I-need-something-to-fill-me feeling is called thirsty. Your mind might think, “I need a drink.” Your body might feel a dry mouth and tired legs."],["FIND","When were you so thirsty that water tasted like the best thing in the world?"],["FORK","The people could stay thirsty, or come to Jesus and drink. Jesus invites everyone to come."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly, like a cool drink of water. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for Jesus, my living water.”"]],"remember":"If anyone is thirsty, come to Jesus and drink!","ask":["“What did Heavenly Father give His people in the desert when they were thirsty?”","“What did Jesus say to do if you are thirsty?”","“Where did Scout and Dad fill the water bottle?”"],"share":["Share a time you were thirsty inside and Jesus filled you with living water.","How has the Holy Ghost flowed out of you to bless someone else?","Did a grandparent or ancestor find living water in Jesus during a dry season? Tell their story."]},"b16-w4-n2":{"story":["One morning, Jesus was teaching in the temple. Some leaders brought a woman who had done something very wrong.","They said, “Moses’ law says to punish her with stones. What do You say?” They were trying to trap Jesus.","Jesus bent down and wrote on the ground with His finger.","They kept asking. Jesus stood up. “Whoever has never done wrong may throw the first stone.” One by one, the leaders walked away. Not one stone was thrown. “Is no one left to punish you?” Jesus asked. “No one, Lord.” Jesus said, “Neither do I condemn you. Go, and sin no more.”","When you do wrong and feel ashamed, Jesus gives you a fresh start. Tonight, tell Him sorry, and try again tomorrow."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm knows the feeling of wanting to hide after doing wrong. The woman stood there ashamed, and Jesus did not throw a stone. He gave her a new start. In Willow’s world today: Willow drew on the wall with a marker. When Dad finds it, she hides behind the door, cheeks burning. Dad kneels down. “Come here. I’m not going to yell. Let’s clean it together, and next time use paper.” Willow’s shame melts into a hug. Just like the woman in the temple, Willow learned that Jesus does not condemn us; He forgives and tells us to do better.","feel":"Hide your face in your hands, like you want to disappear. Now hear Jesus say, “Neither do I condemn you,” and slowly lower your hands and lift your face. Can you show me your new-start face?","stones":[["SPOT","The woman had done wrong and stood ashamed. The leaders wanted to punish her. Jesus forgave her and sent her to do better."],["NAME","That I-want-to-hide, everyone-knows feeling is called ashamed. Your mind might think, “I did something bad.” Your body might feel hot cheeks and a face turned down."],["FIND","When did you do something wrong and want to hide your face?"],["FORK","The leaders could throw stones, or drop them and walk away. Jesus helped them drop the stones."],["CALM","Breathe out slowly and let your face lift. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that Jesus forgives me and helps me do better.”"]],"remember":"Jesus doesn’t throw stones; He forgives and says to go and do better!","ask":["“What did Jesus do when the leaders asked Him their question?”","“What did the leaders do after Jesus spoke?”","“What did Willow’s dad say when he found the marker on the wall?”"],"share":["Share a time Jesus met your shame with mercy instead of condemnation.","When have you dropped a stone you were ready to throw at someone?","Did a grandparent or ancestor show mercy to someone who had done wrong? Tell about them."]},"b16-w4-n3":{"story":["Jesus said, “I am the light of the world. Whoever follows Me will not walk in darkness.”","Why did Jesus say light? In the dark, you can’t see the path. You bump into things. A light shows you where to step.","Jesus told the people who believed Him, “Keep living My words. Then you are truly My followers.”","“You shall know the truth, and the truth shall make you free.” Some said, “We are already free!” But Jesus said doing wrong traps us, like a rope around our feet. “If the Son makes you free, you will be free indeed.” Jesus, the Son, can untie the rope.","When you feel stuck or in the dark, follow Jesus’s light. Tonight, say, “Jesus, light my way.”"],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer pauses and ponders which way to go. Jesus said, “I am the light of the world. Follow Me, and you will not walk in darkness.” Now Wren always knows where to look for the path. In Wren’s world today: The power is out, and Wren has to get to the bathroom in the dark hallway. She cannot see her feet. Then Mom clicks on a flashlight and shines it down the hall. “Just follow the light.” Wren walks right down the bright path, not scared at all. Just like the people who followed Jesus, Wren learned that His light guides us and His truth makes us free.","feel":"Close your eyes and feel around in the dark, careful, careful. Now open them and point to a pretend light down the hall, and walk your fingers right along the bright path. Can you show me your follow-the-light fingers?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus said He is the light of the world and His truth makes us free. The people who followed felt guided."],["NAME","That I-can-see-which-way-to-go feeling is called guided. Your mind might think, “Follow the light.” Your body might feel sure feet and open eyes."],["FIND","When did a light help you find your way in the dark?"],["FORK","People could walk in the dark on their own, or follow Jesus’s light. Jesus invites us to follow."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and picture a warm light on your path. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for Jesus, my light.”"]],"remember":"Follow Jesus, the light of the world, and His truth will make you free!","ask":["“What did Jesus call Himself?”","“What did Jesus say the truth will do?”","“How did Wren find her way down the dark hallway?”"],"share":["Share a time Jesus’s light guided you through a dark stretch.","What truth from Jesus has made you free?","Did a grandparent or ancestor follow Jesus’s light through hard times? Tell their story."]},"b16-w4-n4":{"story":["Jesus saw a man who had been blind since the day he was born.","Jesus spat on the ground and made a little mud. He put the mud on the man’s eyes. “Go wash in the pool of Siloam.”","The man went and washed, and he came back seeing!","Some leaders were upset that Jesus healed on the Sabbath, the day of rest. They said, “This man Jesus is a sinner.” The man said bravely, “I don’t know about that. One thing I know: I was blind, and now I see.” They sent him away. Jesus found him. “Do you believe on the Son of God?” “Lord, I believe.”","You can say what you know, too. Tomorrow, tell someone one thing you know about Jesus."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big heart, brave start!” The man stood in front of the angry leaders and said, “One thing I know: I was blind, and now I see.” Beau thinks that is as bold as it gets. In Beau’s world today: A boy at school says, “Church is boring and fake.” Beau’s heart thumps, but he says, “I don’t know about all that. One thing I know: when I pray, I feel peaceful.” The boy shrugs, but Beau stands a little taller. He said what he knew. Just like the man born blind, Beau learned that we can boldly say what we know about Jesus.","feel":"Cover your eyes with both hands. Pretend to wash your face in a pool, splash, splash. Now open your eyes, stand tall, and say, “Now I see!” Can you show me your bold stand?","stones":[["SPOT","The man born blind was healed. When the leaders argued, he boldly said what he knew."],["NAME","That I’ll-say-what-I-know, not-backing-down feeling is called bold. Your mind might think, “This is true, and I’ll say it.” Your body might feel a straight back and a clear voice."],["FIND","When did you say something true even though someone might not like it?"],["FORK","The man could stay quiet to keep the leaders happy, or boldly tell what Jesus did. He told the truth."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and stand tall in your heart. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me boldly say what I know.”"]],"remember":"One thing I know: Jesus opens eyes, and we can boldly say so!","ask":["“What did Jesus put on the blind man’s eyes?”","“What did the man say when the leaders called Jesus a sinner?”","“What did Beau say when the boy said church was fake?”"],"share":["Share the “one thing I know” of your own testimony.","When have you had to stand boldly for what Jesus did for you?","Did a grandparent or ancestor boldly share their testimony when it was hard? Tell their story."]},"b16-w4-n5":{"story":["Jesus told the people about a sheepfold, a safe pen where sheep stayed. It had one door.","A robber sneaks in some other way. But the shepherd comes in through the door. He calls each sheep by name and leads them out.","He walks in front, and the sheep follow him. Why? They know his voice. They run away from a stranger’s voice.","The people did not understand. So Jesus said plainly, “I am the door of the sheep.” Whoever comes in through Him will be safe and find good grass. “I came so that you can have life, full and overflowing.”","You can learn Jesus’s voice by hearing His words and feeling the Holy Ghost. Tonight, listen quietly for the voice you know."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big heart, brave start!” Sheep are brave enough to follow their shepherd anywhere because they know his voice. Beau listens carefully for Jesus’s voice so he can follow Him, too. In Beau’s world today: At the crowded fair, Beau cannot see Dad. Then, through all the noise, he hears one voice: “Beau!” He knows it instantly and runs straight to it. Dad lifts him up. “You knew my voice,” Dad says. Beau grins. “I always know your voice.” Just like the sheep, Beau learned that when we pay attention, we learn Jesus’s voice and follow Him.","feel":"Cup both hands behind your ears like big sheep ears. Listen! A stranger’s voice? Shake your head no. The Shepherd’s voice? Nod and walk your fingers toward it. Can you show me your listening sheep ears?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus said the sheep know their shepherd’s voice and follow him. The people who listened felt attentive."],["NAME","That ears-up, I-know-that-voice feeling is called attentive. Your mind might think, “That’s Him.” Your body might feel still ears and ready feet."],["FIND","When did you hear someone you love call your name, and you knew their voice right away?"],["FORK","The sheep could follow a stranger’s voice, or the shepherd’s. They follow the shepherd they know."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and listen to the quiet. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me know and follow Jesus’s voice.”"]],"remember":"Jesus is the door, and His sheep know His voice and follow Him!","ask":["“What did Jesus say He is?”","“Why do the sheep follow the shepherd?”","“How did Beau find his dad at the crowded fair?”"],"share":["Share how you have learned to recognize the Savior’s voice in your life.","What strangers’ voices are loudest around you, and how do you tune to the Shepherd?","Did a grandparent or ancestor teach your family to listen for the Spirit? Tell about them."]},"b16-w4-n6":{"story":["Jesus said, “I am the good shepherd.” A shepherd takes care of sheep, and everyone listening knew it.","Jesus said a hired man only watches sheep for pay. When a wolf comes, he runs away. He does not really care about the sheep.","But the good shepherd stays. Jesus said, “I know My sheep, and My sheep know Me.”","“I lay down My life for the sheep.” Jesus meant He would give His life for us. Then He would take it again and live! “No one takes it from Me. I give it.” He said other sheep would hear His voice, too.","You are one of Jesus’s sheep, and He knows your name. Tonight, snuggle in and say, “Thank You, Good Shepherd.”"],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” The Good Shepherd knows every sheep by name and gives His life for them, and that makes Willow feel cherished. In Willow’s world today: Willow has a bad dream and cries out. Mom comes right away, sits on the bed, and strokes her hair until the scared feeling is gone. “I’d come every time,” Mom whispers. Willow snuggles down. She feels cherished, like a lamb with the Good Shepherd. Just like the sheep, Willow learned that Jesus is the Good Shepherd who cherishes each of us and gave His life for us.","feel":"Hold your arms like you are cradling a little lamb. Rock it gently, side to side. Now hold it close to your heart. Can you show me how you hold something cherished?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus said He is the Good Shepherd who knows His sheep and gives His life for them. The sheep are cherished."],["NAME","That He-knows-my-name, He’d-give-anything-for-me feeling is called cherished. Your mind might think, “I matter to Him.” Your body might feel held, warm, and safe."],["FIND","When did someone show you that you were worth everything to them?"],["FORK","The hired man runs when the wolf comes; the Good Shepherd stays. Jesus stays for His sheep."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel held like a lamb. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for Jesus, my Good Shepherd.”"]],"remember":"Jesus is the Good Shepherd, He knows you by name, and He gave His life for you!","ask":["“What did Jesus call Himself?”","“What does the hired man do when a wolf comes, and what does the Good Shepherd do?”","“What did Willow’s mom do when she had a bad dream?”"],"share":["Share how you know Jesus is your Good Shepherd who knows you by name.","What does it mean to you that He laid down His life and took it up again?","Did a grandparent or ancestor feel cherished by the Savior through a hard time? Tell their story."]},"b16-w4-n7":{"story":["It was winter. Jesus was walking in the temple, along a covered porch called Solomon’s porch.","Some leaders gathered around Him. “If You are the Christ, tell us plainly.” Jesus said, “I told you, and you did not believe.”","Then Jesus said the words His sheep love most.","“My sheep hear My voice. I know them, and they follow Me. I give them eternal life, life with Heavenly Father forever. No one can snatch them out of My hand.” He said no one can snatch them from His Father’s hand, either. “I and My Father are one.”","Jesus holds you tight, like a dad holding your hand on slippery rocks. Tonight, squeeze your hand and say, “Jesus holds me.”"],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer pauses and ponders the biggest promise Jesus ever made to His sheep: “No one can snatch them out of My hand.” Wren holds onto that thought, and it holds onto her. In Wren’s world today: Wren and Dad cross a rushing creek on wet rocks. Her foot slips, and her heart jumps. But Dad’s hand is wrapped tight around hers, and she does not fall. “I’ve got you,” he says. “I won’t let go.” Wren breathes again. She is safe in his hand. Just like the sheep, Wren learned that when we follow Jesus, we are held safe in His hand, and no one can take us away.","feel":"Make one hand into a little sheep and set it in your other hand. Close the big hand gently around it. Try to shake it loose, no! It’s safe. Can you show me your safe-in-His-hand hands?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus said His sheep hear His voice, follow Him, and are held in His hand. His sheep felt safe."],["NAME","That held-tight, nobody-can-take-me feeling is called safe. Your mind might think, “He won’t let go.” Your body might feel a steady hand around yours and a calm heart."],["FIND","When did someone hold your hand so tight that you knew you would not fall?"],["FORK","The leaders could listen and believe, or keep arguing. Jesus’s sheep chose to hear His voice."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel held. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that I am safe in Your hand and in Jesus’s hand.”"]],"remember":"Jesus’s sheep hear His voice, and no one can take them out of His hand!","ask":["“What did Jesus say His sheep do?”","“Can anyone snatch Jesus’s sheep out of His hand?”","“What did Wren’s dad say when her foot slipped on the rocks?”"],"share":["Share a time you felt held in the Savior’s hand when everything else felt shaky.","What does it mean to you that Jesus and Heavenly Father are one in caring for you?","Did a grandparent or ancestor trust that they were held in Heavenly Father’s hand? Tell their story."]},"b20-w1-n1":{"story":["In Jerusalem, an angry crowd grabbed Paul at the temple. Soldiers took him and carried him up the stairs of their fort.","Paul stood on the stairs and waved his hand. Everyone grew quiet, and Paul told his story.","“I used to hurt people who followed Jesus,” he said. “I put them in prison.”","“Then a great light shone around me. A voice said, ‘Saul, why do you hurt Me?’ It was Jesus! The light left me blind. Then a good man named Ananias came. He said, ‘Brother Saul, see again!’ And I could see.”","Paul was not afraid to tell what Jesus did for him. Tonight, tell someone one way Jesus has helped you."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big faith, brave heart!” Paul stood above the crowd that wanted to hurt him and calmly told how Jesus changed him. A steady voice before an angry crowd is the bravest kind. In Beau’s world today: Beau’s cousins tease him about church. He takes a breath and says, “I go because I love Jesus, and He helped me stop being scared of the dark.” The cousins go quiet. One asks, “Really?” Beau nods. Just like Paul on the stairs, Beau learned that telling your own true story can quiet a crowd.","feel":"Climb up the stairs, step, step, step. Turn and hold up your hand for quiet. Now say, “Let me tell you my story,” in a steady voice. Can you show me your steady voice?","stones":[["SPOT","An angry crowd grabbed Paul, but from the stairs he told them calmly how Jesus changed him. He felt steady."],["NAME","That calm-while-they-yell feeling is called steady. Your mind might think, “I know what’s true.” Your body might feel planted feet, a slow heartbeat."],["FIND","When did you stay calm and tell the truth while someone was upset?"],["FORK","Paul could stay silent and be carried off, or ask to speak and tell his story. He told it."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and plant your feet in the bed. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me tell my story with a steady heart.”"]],"remember":"Paul told his story on the stairs, and you can tell yours too!","ask":["“Where did Paul stand when he spoke to the crowd?”","“What did Ananias call Paul?”","“Why did Beau say he goes to church?”"],"share":["Share your own conversion story the way Paul told his: short, true, and brave.","Who in your life needs to hear your story, not a sermon?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor whose story of faith you treasure."]},"b20-w1-n2":{"story":["The captain brought Paul before the leaders. Paul said, “I have tried to do right before Heavenly Father all my life.”","Some leaders believed people would live again after they die. Others said no. Paul said, “I am on trial for the hope that the dead will rise!”","The leaders began to shout and argue. The soldiers hurried Paul away, back to the fort.","That night, the Lord stood by Paul. “Be of good cheer, Paul,” He said, to make Paul’s heart brave. “You told about Me in Jerusalem. Now you will tell about Me in Rome.”","The Lord did not leave Paul alone on that hard night. He stands by you too. Tonight, whisper His words: “Be of good cheer.”"],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” On Paul’s worst night, the Lord came and stood by him and said, “Be of good cheer.” Willow thinks that is the warmest thing in a cold fort. In Willow’s world today: Willow sleeps at the hospital with Grandma, and the room is beepy and strange. She curls up scared. Then Grandma holds her hand all night long. In the morning Willow says, “I wasn’t alone.” Grandma squeezes. “Neither was I.” Just like Paul, Willow learned that Jesus does not leave us alone on hard nights.","feel":"Curl up small and lonely in a dark fort. Now feel someone stand right beside you and put a hand on your shoulder. Sit up and smile, “good cheer!” Can you show me your good-cheer smile?","stones":[["SPOT","After a loud, scary day, the Lord stood by Paul at night and said, “Be of good cheer.” Paul felt comforted."],["NAME","That someone-is-right-here feeling is called comforted. Your mind might think, “I’m not alone.” Your body might feel a warm shoulder, a slow sigh."],["FIND","When did someone stay beside you on a scary night?"],["FORK","Paul could lie awake in fear, or hold on to the Lord’s words. He held on."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel Jesus standing right beside your bed. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that I am never alone.”"]],"remember":"The Lord stood by Paul and said, “Be of good cheer,” and He stands by you too!","ask":["“What did Paul say he was on trial for?”","“Who stood by Paul that night?”","“Who held Willow’s hand all night at the hospital?”"],"share":["Share a night the Lord stood by you instead of taking the trouble away.","How do you “be of good cheer” when the hard thing isn’t over yet?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who felt the Lord beside them in a dark time."]},"b20-w1-n3":{"story":["One morning, more than forty men made a mean, secret plan. They promised not to eat or drink until they had hurt Paul.","But Paul’s nephew, his sister’s son, heard about the plan. He hurried into the fort and told Paul.","Paul called a soldier. “Take this young man to the captain,” he said. The captain took his hand and listened.","The young man told him the secret plan. “Tell no one you told me,” the captain said. That night, hundreds of soldiers took Paul safely away to the governor. They even gave Paul an animal to ride.","Paul’s nephew paid attention and spoke up, and Paul was kept safe. If you see something wrong, tell a grown-up you trust."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see!” Paul’s nephew heard something and went straight to tell. Four hundred soldiers rode out because one boy spoke up. Listening is a superpower. In Scout’s world today: Scout hears two big kids planning to lock his little sister in the shed as a “joke.” He runs to his dad and tells him. Dad walks out to the yard and the plan ends before it starts. Scout feels like a secret guard. Just like Paul’s nephew, Scout learned that noticing and telling a grown-up can keep someone safe.","feel":"Cup your ear and listen, listen. Gasp! Run on tiptoe to the fort. Whisper the secret to the captain. Now gallop with the horsemen through the night! Can you show me your night gallop?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul’s nephew heard the secret plan, paid attention, and told the captain. He felt alert."],["NAME","That something’s-not-right feeling is called alert. Your mind might think, “I must tell someone.” Your body might feel perked-up ears and quick feet."],["FIND","When did you notice something was wrong and tell a grown-up?"],["FORK","The nephew could keep quiet and stay out of it, or go tell. He went."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your alert ears rest. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for people who watch out for me.”"]],"remember":"One young listener spoke up, and Paul was carried to safety!","ask":["“How many men promised not to eat until they hurt Paul?”","“Who heard the plan and told Paul?”","“What did Scout do when he heard the big kids’ plan?”"],"share":["Share a time you felt prompted to speak up and it protected someone.","Who are the “nephews” in your life, the quiet ones who notice things?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who was protected by someone paying attention."]},"b20-w1-n4":{"story":["Now Paul stood before Felix, the governor who ruled the land. Men came to say bad things about Paul.","“I came to Jerusalem to worship,” Paul said. “I believe what the prophets wrote. I believe the dead will rise again.”","“So I always try to keep my conscience clean.” A conscience is the quiet voice inside that helps you know right from wrong.","Paul kept nothing wrong hiding in his heart, so he was not afraid. Felix heard Paul talk about doing right, and he trembled. “Go for now,” Felix said. “When I have time, I will call you.” Two years went by, and Felix still waited.","If something is hiding in your heart, tell your mom or dad tonight. Your heart will feel light."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer says, “Wow, look at that!” Paul could stand before a governor with nothing to hide, because he kept his conscience clean. Wren wonders how light that feels. In Wren’s world today: Wren took two cookies when Mom said one. All day her tummy feels heavy. At bedtime she tells Mom. Mom hugs her. “Thank you for telling me.” Wren lies down and feels light as a feather. Now there’s nothing hiding inside. Just like Paul, Wren learned that a clean conscience feels light and lets you look anyone in the eye.","feel":"Stand straight in front of a governor. Put a hand on your heart and say, “Nothing to hide!” Now brush off your shoulders, clean, clean. Can you show me your nothing-to-hide stand?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul worked to keep a conscience without offense, so he could stand before the governor without fear. He felt clean."],["NAME","That nothing-hiding-inside feeling is called clean. Your mind might think, “Nothing to hide.” Your body might feel light shoulders, easy breath."],["FIND","When did telling the truth make your tummy feel light again?"],["FORK","Felix could choose Jesus today, or say, “later, when I have time.” He said later, and never chose."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel your heart clean and light. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me keep my conscience clear.”"]],"remember":"Paul kept a clean heart, and a clean heart can stand before anyone!","ask":["“Who was Felix?”","“What is a conscience?”","“What did Wren tell Mom at bedtime?”"],"share":["Share a time a clear conscience gave you courage.","Is there a “later” you have been telling the Lord that should become “now”?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who was known for an honest, clean heart."]},"b20-w1-n5":{"story":["King Agrippa came to visit the city of Caesarea. Paul was a prisoner there, in chains. The king said, “You may speak.”","Paul told about a light brighter than the noon sun. He told about the voice of Jesus.","“Jesus sent me to turn people from darkness to light,” Paul said. He meant helping people find Jesus, like a light in a dark room.","The king said, “Paul, you almost talk me into following Jesus.” Paul was not grumpy. “I pray that you and everyone here would be like me,” he said. “Except for these chains!”","Paul kept hoping for the king. Tonight, say a prayer for someone you love."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big faith, brave heart!” Paul stood in chains and wished a king could have what he had. Wanting good for the one holding you prisoner is brave hope. In Beau’s world today: Beau’s friend Tyler says, “I almost want to come to your church, but my dad says no.” Every Sunday Beau says, “We’ll save you a seat.” Months later, Tyler’s whole family walks in. Beau waves them to the seat he saved. Just like Paul, Beau learned to keep hoping for people, even when their answer is “almost.”","feel":"Hold out your hands in pretend chains. Stretch one hand toward a king and tell your story. Lift your chained hands and say, “I wish you had what I have!” Can you show me your hopeful hands?","stones":[["SPOT","King Agrippa said, “You almost persuade me.” Paul kept wishing joy on him anyway. Paul felt hopeful."],["NAME","That maybe-someday feeling is called hopeful. Your mind might think, “Not yet, but maybe.” Your body might feel reaching hands and a lifted chin."],["FIND","When did you keep hoping for someone even after they said no?"],["FORK","Paul could be bitter at the king, or wish him the same joy. He wished him joy."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and picture someone you hope for in His hands. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, I won’t give up.”"]],"remember":"Agrippa said “almost,” and Paul kept hoping anyway!","ask":["“Who came to visit Caesarea?”","“What did King Agrippa say to Paul?”","“What did Beau save for Tyler every Sunday?”"],"share":["Share a time someone was “almost persuaded” and how you kept loving them.","Who are you still hoping will one day say yes to Christ?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who prayed for years for someone to come to faith."]},"b20-w1-n6":{"story":["Paul sailed toward Rome on a ship with two hundred seventy-six people. Paul warned them it was not safe, but they sailed anyway.","Soon a wild wind tossed the ship. For many days there was no sun and no stars. Everyone stopped hoping.","Then Paul stood up. “Be of good cheer!” he said. “An angel of Heavenly Father stood by me last night.”","“He said, ‘Do not be afraid, Paul. Everyone on this ship will be safe.’ I believe Heavenly Father!” The ship broke apart near land. Some people swam, and some floated on boards. All two hundred seventy-six people reached land safely!","When you feel scared, remember that Heavenly Father keeps His promises. Tonight, tell Him what scares you."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see!” Everyone had given up, and Paul said, “An angel stood by me. We will all be safe.” Scout loves that every person was counted safe. In Scout’s world today: The power goes out in a thunderstorm and Scout’s little cousins cry. Scout finds the flashlight. “Dad said the storm will pass by bedtime, and Dad is right about storms.” He hands out crackers. By bedtime, stars are out. Just like Paul on the ship, Scout learned that holding on to a promise can calm everyone around you.","feel":"Rock side to side like a ship in a storm, whoa! Now stand up tall like Paul and say, “Be of good cheer!” Pass out pretend bread to everyone. Can you show me your be-of-good-cheer stand?","stones":[["SPOT","Everyone gave up hope in the storm, but an angel told Paul they would all be saved, and they were. They felt reassured."],["NAME","That it’s-going-to-be-okay feeling is called reassured. Your mind might think, “He promised.” Your body might feel a loosening grip and slower breath."],["FIND","When did a promise from someone you trust calm you in a scary time?"],["FORK","Paul could give up with everyone else, or stand up and share the angel’s promise. He stood up."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your grip loosen like a sailor who is safe. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, I trust Your promise.”"]],"remember":"Paul said, “Be of good cheer, not one of you will be lost,” and everyone made it to land!","ask":["“What did the angel tell Paul?”","“How many people made it safely to land?”","“What did Scout tell his cousins during the storm?”"],"share":["Share a storm in your life where the Lord’s promise held you.","How do you pass out “bread” and give thanks when everyone else is afraid?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who crossed an ocean or weathered a great storm in faith."]},"b20-w1-n7":{"story":["The travelers came to an island called Melita. It was cold and rainy. The kind island people built a big fire for them.","Paul gathered sticks for the fire. A snake slid out from the heat and bit his hand!","Paul shook the snake off into the fire. He was not hurt at all. The people watched and waited, but Paul was fine.","Publius, the chief of the island, welcomed them. His father was sick with a fever. Paul prayed, put his hands on him, and he was healed! Other sick people came and were healed too. Then the island people gave the travelers what they needed.","Kindness went around and came back. Tonight, think of one kind thing you can do tomorrow."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” The islanders built a fire for shivering strangers, and Paul healed their sick. Willow loves kindness that goes both ways. In Willow’s world today: A family with a broken-down car knocks at Willow’s door in the rain. Willow brings towels and hot cocoa while Dad calls a tow truck. A week later a thank-you box arrives with homemade jam and a note: “You warmed us up.” Willow feels warm all over. Just like the islanders and Paul, Willow learned that kindness to strangers comes back around.","feel":"Shiver, brrr, cold and wet! Now gather sticks and build a crackling fire. Shake a pretend snake off your hand, flick! Then lay gentle hands on someone sick. Can you show me your warming-up hands?","stones":[["SPOT","The islanders built a fire for wet strangers, and Paul healed their sick. Everyone felt kindly."],["NAME","That I-want-to-warm-you-up feeling is called kindly. Your mind might think, “They need help.” Your body might feel busy hands and a soft smile."],["FIND","When did you help a stranger, or did a stranger help you?"],["FORK","The islanders could leave the shipwrecked strangers in the rain, or build them a fire. They built the fire."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel a warm fire glowing in your chest. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me warm someone up tomorrow.”"]],"remember":"The islanders built a fire, Paul healed their sick, and kindness went around!","ask":["“What did the islanders build for the wet travelers?”","“What happened when the snake bit Paul?”","“What came in the thank-you box for Willow?”"],"share":["Share a time a stranger’s kindness blessed you, or yours blessed them.","How did Paul turn a shipwreck into a mission? What is your “island”?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor known for welcoming strangers in."]},"b20-w2-n1":{"story":["Paul had not been to Rome yet, but he loved the believers there. So he wrote them a long letter.","“Heavenly Father loves you,” Paul wrote. “The whole world is talking about your faith. I pray for you all the time. I want to come see you!”","Then Paul wrote a brave line. “I am not ashamed of the good news of Jesus.”","Paul meant, “I will never hide it.” People had laughed at Paul for talking about Jesus. Some had locked him up. But Paul knew the good news is strong. It has Heavenly Father’s power to save everyone who believes.","You never have to hide that you love Jesus. Tomorrow, tell someone one thing you love about Him."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see!” Paul wrote, “I am not ashamed of the gospel.” Scout found out why: it is the power of Heavenly Father to save people. Who would hide that? In Scout’s world today: At show-and-tell, Scout brings his scriptures. A kid snickers. Scout opens it and says, “This tells how Jesus helps people. It helps me too.” The teacher smiles. Two kids ask to see it at recess. Just like Paul, Scout learned that you don’t hide the gospel, because it is power to help people.","feel":"Unroll a long pretend scroll and read it. Now put your hand on your heart and say, “I am not ashamed!” Hold the scroll up high for everyone to see. Can you show me your not-ashamed hold?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul wrote to Rome, “I am not ashamed of the gospel, for it is the power of Heavenly Father to save.” He felt unashamed."],["NAME","That I-won’t-hide-what-I-love feeling is called unashamed. Your mind might think, “This is good news.” Your body might feel a lifted head, open hands."],["FIND","When did you share something you love even though someone might laugh?"],["FORK","Paul could keep the gospel quiet to avoid trouble, or say it out loud. He said it out loud."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel the good news warm in your chest. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, I am not ashamed of Jesus.”"]],"remember":"Paul said, “I am not ashamed of the gospel,” and you don’t have to be either!","ask":["“How did Paul talk to the believers in Rome?”","“What did Paul say he was not ashamed of?”","“What did Scout bring to show-and-tell?”"],"share":["Share a time you chose not to be ashamed of the gospel.","Where in your life does the gospel need to be a little louder?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who was openly, unashamedly faithful."]},"b20-w2-n2":{"story":["Paul wrote a true thing about every person: “All have sinned.” To sin is to choose something Heavenly Father asked us not to do.","Big people and little people all make wrong choices. Nobody is perfect.","But Paul had happy news. Heavenly Father’s help is a free gift, and Jesus came to rescue us.","“Hardly anyone would die even for a good person,” Paul wrote. “But Heavenly Father showed His love like this. While we were still sinners, Christ died for us.” Jesus did not wait for us to be good first. He loved us while we were still making mistakes.","You are loved before you are perfect. Tonight, whisper, “Thank you, Jesus, for loving me.”"],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” Jesus loved us while we were still making mistakes, not after we got everything right. Willow thinks that is the warmest truth. In Willow’s world today: Willow breaks a lamp and hides under the table, sure Mom will be furious. Mom kneels down and opens her arms. “Come here. I love you even when you break things.” Willow crawls out and cries into her shoulder. Just like Paul wrote, Willow learned that she is loved while she is still making mistakes.","feel":"Hide your face in your hands, oops, I made a mistake. Now feel two big arms open wide for you anyway. Crawl out and give the biggest hug. Can you show me your loved-anyway hug?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul wrote that all have sinned, but Christ died for us while we were still sinners. We are treasured."],["NAME","That loved-before-I-was-good feeling is called treasured. Your mind might think, “He wants me.” Your body might feel a melting chest, a wet smile."],["FIND","When did someone love you right in the middle of your mistake?"],["FORK","We could wait to be perfect before coming to Jesus, or come now, as we are. Come now."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let yourself be loved exactly as you are. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for loving me first.”"]],"remember":"While we were still sinners, Christ died for us, that is how loved you are!","ask":["“What does it mean to sin?”","“When did Christ die for us?”","“What did Mom say when Willow was under the table?”"],"share":["Share a moment you felt Christ’s love before you had “fixed” yourself.","How does knowing you are loved first change how you treat your own mistakes?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who felt rescued by the Savior’s grace."]},"b20-w2-n3":{"story":["Paul told the people in Rome about Abraham, from long, long ago. Heavenly Father made Abraham a big promise.","Abraham’s family would grow as many as the stars! But Abraham was about one hundred years old. His wife Sarah was too old to have a baby.","Still, Abraham believed Heavenly Father. He did not wobble. He was sure Heavenly Father could do what He promised.","Abraham’s trust is called faith. It is believing Heavenly Father’s promise before you see it come true. And Heavenly Father kept it! Sarah had a baby boy named Isaac. From Isaac came a family as big as the stars.","Heavenly Father keeps His promises to you too. Tonight, thank Him for one promise He has kept."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big faith, brave heart!” Abraham was a hundred years old and still believed the promise of a baby. Beau wants faith that big. In Beau’s world today: Beau’s seed packet says “grows in 10 days.” Day eight, nothing. His brother laughs, “It’s dead.” Beau waters it anyway. “The packet promised.” Day ten, a tiny green curl pokes up. Beau yells, “It did what it said!” Just like Abraham, Beau learned that faith keeps believing a promise even when it looks impossible.","feel":"Walk slowly like a very old man with a cane. Look up at the stars and count, one, two, three, too many! Now stand up tall: “I believe!” Can you show me your I-believe stand?","stones":[["SPOT","Abraham was old with no children, but he believed Heavenly Father’s promise of a son. He felt faithful."],["NAME","That I-believe-He-can feeling is called faithful. Your mind might think, “He promised, He will.” Your body might feel steady eyes, a calm heart."],["FIND","When did you keep believing something good would happen, and it did?"],["FORK","Abraham could laugh at the impossible promise, or believe it. He believed."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and picture a sky full of stars. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, I believe You can do what You promised.”"]],"remember":"Abraham believed Heavenly Father, and faith like that makes us right with Him!","ask":["“What did Heavenly Father promise Abraham?”","“How old was Abraham?”","“What poked up on day ten in Beau’s pot?”"],"share":["Share a promise from the Lord you kept believing when it looked impossible.","Where do you need to be “fully persuaded” right now?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor whose faith held through long waiting."]},"b20-w2-n4":{"story":["Paul wrote that because of Jesus, we can have peace with Heavenly Father. Then he said something surprising: “We are glad even in tribulations.”","Tribulations are hard, bumpy times. Why would anyone be glad about those?","Paul showed that hard times are like steps on a stair. Each step takes us higher. Hard times help us grow patience, the strength to keep going.","Patience grows experience: we learn we really can get through. Then we have hope that good things are coming. And Heavenly Father’s love fills our hearts through the Holy Ghost. So hard times are never for nothing.","Is something hard for you right now? Keep trying, one step at a time. Tonight, ask Heavenly Father to help you be patient."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer says, “Wow, look at that!” Paul said trouble grows patience, patience grows experience, and experience grows hope, like a staircase. Wren loves seeing where the steps lead. In Wren’s world today: Wren wants to ride her bike without training wheels. She falls. And falls. Grandpa says, “Every fall is a step.” She keeps going. One day she wobbles, then flies down the sidewalk. “I waited, I practiced, and now I know I can!” Just like Paul wrote, Wren learned that trouble, patience, and practice climb up to hope.","feel":"Climb a staircase with your feet. Step one: trouble, make a tired face. Step two: patience, take a deep breath. Step three: experience, nod. Step four: hope, arms up! Can you show me your hope arms?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul said hard times grow patience, patience grows experience, and experience grows hope. He felt patient."],["NAME","That I-can-keep-going feeling is called patient. Your mind might think, “One more step.” Your body might feel slow breaths and tired-but-willing legs."],["FIND","When did something hard teach you that you could keep going?"],["FORK","In hard times we can give up, or let them grow patience in us. Let them grow patience."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel Heavenly Father’s love poured into your heart. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me be patient.”"]],"remember":"Tribulation grows patience, patience grows experience, and experience grows hope!","ask":["“What does tribulation mean?”","“What grows out of patience?”","“What did Grandpa tell Wren about falling?”"],"share":["Share a tribulation that grew patience and hope in you.","Which step of the staircase are you on right now, and what might come next?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor whose hard times made them more hopeful, not less."]},"b20-w2-n5":{"story":["Long ago, Adam, the very first man, made a wrong choice. Because of it, sin and death came into the world.","But Paul wrote about a free gift. Jesus Christ gave it, and it is much bigger than Adam’s wrong choice.","A gift is something you do not earn or pay for. Someone gives it to you because they love you.","Paul wrote that wrong choices pay back death, like pay for work. “But the gift of Heavenly Father is eternal life through Jesus.” That gift is living with Heavenly Father forever. We cannot earn it. We open it with thankful hearts and follow Jesus.","The best gift is already given. Tonight, say, “Thank you, Heavenly Father, for Jesus.”"],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” Paul said eternal life is a gift, not wages you earn. Willow loves gifts you could never buy. In Willow’s world today: Willow saves coins to buy Mom a birthday present, but only has forty cents. Dad sees her tears and hands her twenty dollars. “This is a gift, not a loan.” Willow buys the scarf and can hardly stop saying thank you. Just like Paul wrote, Willow learned that the best things are gifts you receive with a thank-you, not wages you earn.","feel":"Pretend to work hard, dig, dig, and get your little wages. Now receive a giant gift box with both arms. Untie the bow, lift the lid, gasp! Say, “Thank You!” Can you show me your gift gasp?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul wrote that sin earns death, but eternal life is a gift from Heavenly Father through Jesus. We feel grateful."],["NAME","That I-didn’t-earn-this feeling is called grateful. Your mind might think, “Thank You, thank You.” Your body might feel full arms and a happy wiggle."],["FIND","When did someone give you a gift you could never have bought yourself?"],["FORK","We can try to earn Heavenly Father’s love with our work, or receive His gift with thanks. Receive it."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and hold the gift of Jesus close to your heart. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for Your gift.”"]],"remember":"The gift of Heavenly Father is eternal life through Jesus Christ our Lord!","ask":["“What is a gift?”","“What is the gift of Heavenly Father?”","“What did Dad give Willow for Mom’s present?”"],"share":["Share how receiving the gift of grace has changed you.","Where are you still trying to earn what the Lord has already given?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who lived with deep gratitude for the Savior’s gift."]},"b20-w2-n6":{"story":["Paul wrote to the people about being baptized. A person goes all the way under the water, then comes back up.","Going under the water is like Jesus being laid in the tomb. The old you, with its wrong choices, goes down too.","Coming up out of the water is like Jesus rising, alive again!","Jesus rose from the dead. So Paul said we should “walk in newness of life.” That means a brand-new start, walking a new way with Jesus. Down like His burial, up like His rising. Then you walk out new.","Jesus can make you new, every single day. Tonight, think of one kind, new thing you can do tomorrow."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see!” Scout wondered why baptism goes all the way under. Paul told him: down like Jesus’s burial, up like His rising. Now he sees it. In Scout’s world today: Scout watches his big sister get baptized. She goes all the way under, then comes up with water streaming and a huge smile. Scout whispers to Dad, “Down like the tomb, up like Easter!” Dad squeezes his shoulder. “You’ve got it.” Just like Paul explained, Scout learned that baptism tells the story of Jesus’s burial and rising.","feel":"Stand tall, then sink down, down, under the pretend water, like a tomb. Hold it. Now burst up with a big breath and a smile, brand new! Can you show me your brand-new burst?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul said baptism is like being buried with Jesus and rising with Him to walk in newness of life. We feel new."],["NAME","That fresh-start, clean-slate feeling is called new. Your mind might think, “I can begin again.” Your body might feel like a deep breath after a bath."],["FIND","When did you get a fresh start after a bad day?"],["FORK","After baptism we can walk the old way, or walk in newness of life. Walk the new way."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly like coming up out of the water, fresh and new. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for new beginnings.”"]],"remember":"Down like the tomb, up like Easter, baptism means walking in newness of life!","ask":["“What is going under the water like?”","“What is coming up out of the water like?”","“What did Scout whisper to Dad at the baptism?”"],"share":["Share what your own baptism has come to mean to you.","Where do you need a “newness of life” this week?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor’s baptism and what it changed for your family."]},"b20-w2-n7":{"story":["Paul wrote that everyone can see there is a Maker. Look at a mountain, a sunset, or a baby’s tiny fingers. They show us Heavenly Father made them.","Then Paul wrote, “The goodness of Heavenly Father leads you to repentance.”","Repentance means feeling sorry for a wrong choice and turning around to do right.","What helps us turn around? Not being scared. It is Heavenly Father’s goodness! He keeps sending sunshine and food and people who love us. When we see how good He is, our hearts get soft. We want to come back to Him.","Heavenly Father’s kindness is calling you home. Tonight, if you need to say sorry, say it."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big faith, brave heart!” It takes a brave heart to turn around, and Paul says Heavenly Father’s kindness is what makes us want to. Kindness beats fear. In Beau’s world today: Beau slammed his door after a fight with Mom. Later he finds a plate of warm cookies outside his room with a note: “I love you. Come out when you’re ready.” The kindness melts him. He comes out and says sorry first. Just like Paul wrote, Beau learned that kindness, not fear, is what leads a heart back home.","feel":"Cross your arms and turn your back, grumpy. Now smell warm cookies, sniff, sniff. Feel a gentle rope of kindness pull you around, turn, turn. Open your arms and come home. Can you show me your turn-around?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul wrote that Heavenly Father’s goodness, not fear, leads us to repentance. We feel drawn to Him."],["NAME","That His-kindness-pulls-me feeling is called drawn. Your mind might think, “He is so good.” Your body might feel soft shoulders, feet that turn."],["FIND","When did someone’s kindness make you want to say sorry?"],["FORK","Heavenly Father could scare us back, or draw us with goodness. He draws us with goodness."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and count three kind things He gave you today. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, Your goodness leads me home.”"]],"remember":"The goodness of Heavenly Father leads us to repentance, His kindness calls us home!","ask":["“How can people see that there is a Maker?”","“What leads us to repentance?”","“What did Beau find outside his door?”"],"share":["Share a time the Lord’s kindness, not fear, turned your heart.","How can you lead someone back with goodness instead of pressure?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor whose kindness drew family members back to faith."]},"b20-w3-n1":{"story":["Paul wrote a letter to Jesus’s followers in Rome. He told them something very honest.","He wrote, “The good I want to do, I do not do.” And the wrong things he hated? Sometimes he did those instead.","Even Paul made mistakes when he meant to do right! It felt like a tug-of-war inside him. One part pulled toward good, and one part pulled the wrong way.","Paul felt sad. He asked, “Who will rescue me?” Then he answered with joy: “I thank Heavenly Father, through Jesus Christ our Lord!” Paul could not win that tug-of-war alone. Jesus is the one who rescues us.","Did you mess up today? Say sorry to Jesus tonight, and try again tomorrow."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see!” Even Paul said, “The good I want to do, I don’t do!” Scout was relieved: everyone has that war inside, and Jesus rescues. In Scout’s world today: Scout promised himself he would not yell at his brother today. By lunch, he yelled. He stomps to Mom. “I wanted to be good and I wasn’t!” Mom says, “That’s why we have Jesus. Say sorry and try again.” Scout does. Just like Paul, Scout learned that the war inside is real, and Jesus is the one who rescues us.","feel":"Pull one hand toward good, and the other hand pulls the other way, tug, tug, a war inside! Now drop both hands and reach up to Jesus. Let Him pull you up. Can you show me your reach-up?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul said, “The good I want to do, I do not do.” He asked who would rescue him, and answered: Jesus. He felt honest."],["NAME","That I’ll-tell-the-truth-about-myself feeling is called honest. Your mind might think, “I need help.” Your body might feel a deep breath, open palms."],["FIND","When did you want to do good, but did the wrong thing anyway?"],["FORK","Paul could pretend he was perfect, or be honest and ask for rescue. He was honest."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let Jesus be your rescuer tonight. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for Jesus, who rescues me.”"]],"remember":"The good I want to do is hard, but Jesus rescues me, thanks be to Heavenly Father!","ask":["“What did Paul say he did, even though he hated it?”","“Who did Paul say rescues us?”","“What did Scout promise himself, and what happened?”"],"share":["Share a time you felt Paul’s “war inside” and how Christ rescued you.","How do you respond to your own failures: run to Christ, or hide?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who was honest about their weaknesses and kept trusting Christ."]},"b20-w3-n2":{"story":["Paul wrote to Jesus’s followers in Rome. He wanted them to know whose children they were.","He said the Holy Ghost leads us and speaks to our hearts. Everyone He leads is a child of Heavenly Father.","We do not have to live scared, like a servant with a hard master. Instead, we are adopted. Adopted means a family chooses a child to be their very own, forever.","So we can call out, “Abba, Father!” the way a little one calls, “Daddy!” The Holy Ghost whispers to our hearts, “You are His child.” And His children get to share everything with Jesus.","You belong to Heavenly Father. Tonight, talk to Him like a child talks to a loving dad."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” Paul said we can call Heavenly Father “Abba,” like a little one says “Daddy.” Willow thinks that is the coziest word there is. In Willow’s world today: Willow’s friend Nora was adopted when she was two. Nora shows Willow a paper with a gold seal. “This says I’m theirs forever, and I get everything they have, even the dog.” Willow thinks, “That’s what Paul said about Heavenly Father.” Just like Paul wrote, Willow learned that we are adopted, chosen children who can call Heavenly Father “Abba.”","feel":"Shrink down scared like a slave in bondage. Now feel a family pick you up and say, “You’re ours forever!” Reach both arms up and say, “Abba!” Can you show me your Abba reach?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul wrote that we are adopted children who can cry “Abba, Father,” and heirs with Christ. We feel belonging."],["NAME","That I’m-theirs-forever feeling is called belonging. Your mind might think, “I am His child.” Your body might feel safe arms and a cozy chest."],["FIND","When did you feel completely sure that you belonged to your family?"],["FORK","We can live scared like a slave, or trust that we are His children. Trust that you are His."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel Heavenly Father’s arms around you like a daddy’s. Whisper, “Abba, Father, I am Yours.”"]],"remember":"The Spirit whispers, “You are a child of Heavenly Father,” so call Him Abba!","ask":["“What does “Abba” mean?”","“What is adoption?”","“What did Nora’s paper with the gold seal say?”"],"share":["Share a time the Spirit bore witness to you that you are His child.","What changes when you pray to “Abba” instead of a distant ruler?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who knew they were a child of Heavenly Father, and how it showed."]},"b20-w3-n3":{"story":["Paul wrote to Jesus’s friends in Rome. Life was hard for them, and some people were cruel to them.","So Paul asked a big question. “If Heavenly Father is for us, who can be against us?” He gave us His own Son, Jesus. Surely He will give us every good thing.","Then Paul named hard things. Trouble. Being hungry. Being chased. Danger. Could any of them pull us away from Jesus’s love?","“No!” Paul wrote. Not life, and not dying. Not angels, or things today, or things to come. Not high places or deep places. Nothing can pull us from Heavenly Father’s love in Jesus.","Wherever you go, His love goes too. Hug yourself and say, “Nothing can pull me away.”"],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big faith, brave heart!” Paul listed every scary thing and said none can separate us from Jesus’s love. Beau thinks that list is a shield. In Beau’s world today: Beau’s family is moving far away. He cries, “Will Heavenly Father still know where I am?” Dad opens Romans 8. “Nor height, nor depth, nor anything else. Not even a new house.” Beau wipes his eyes. “Not even a new house,” he repeats. Just like Paul, Beau learned that nothing anywhere can separate us from the love of Heavenly Father in Jesus.","feel":"Hold up a big invisible shield. Now pretend scary things come: wind, whoosh! Hunger, rumble! Dark, boo! Say to each one, “You can’t separate me!” Stand strong. Can you show me your shield stand?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul listed every scary thing and said none of it can separate us from the love of Heavenly Father in Jesus. He felt secure."],["NAME","That nothing-can-pull-me-away feeling is called secure. Your mind might think, “His love holds me.” Your body might feel a strong back, a quiet heart."],["FIND","When did you feel safe because someone’s love was bigger than the scary thing?"],["FORK","We can let fear convince us we are alone, or believe nothing can separate us from His love. Believe it."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel His love around you like a shield. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, nothing can separate me from You.”"]],"remember":"Nothing can separate us from the love of Heavenly Father in Christ Jesus!","ask":["“What question did Paul ask about Heavenly Father being for us?”","“What can separate us from the love of Christ?”","“What did Beau worry about when his family moved?”"],"share":["Share a time you felt the Lord’s love hold you through something frightening.","What on Paul’s list have you faced, and how did His love stay?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who stayed secure in the Lord’s love through great loss."]},"b20-w3-n4":{"story":["Paul wrote, “Whoever calls on the name of the Lord shall be saved.” Anyone can call on Jesus, and He will save them.","But Paul wondered about people who had never heard of Jesus. How could they call on Him? How could they believe in someone no one had told them about?","Someone would have to go and tell them. Long ago there were no phones. Good news traveled on someone’s feet.","Paul remembered old words from the scriptures. “How beautiful are the feet of them that bring good news!” Feet get tired and dusty. But feet that walk far to tell about Jesus are beautiful feet.","Your feet can carry good news too. Tomorrow, take something kind to someone. Tell them about Jesus."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer says, “Wow, look at that!” Paul said feet are beautiful when they carry good news. Wren looked at her dusty feet and wondered. In Wren’s world today: Wren’s family bakes bread for the new family down the road. Wren carries it the whole way, hot in a towel. On the way she decides to tell them about Primary. The mom says, “We’d love to come!” Wren skips home. “My feet did good news!” Just like Paul wrote, Wren learned that feet are beautiful when they carry good news about Jesus.","feel":"Wiggle your toes. Now march them down a long road, step, step, step. Knock on a door and say, “I have good news!” Point to your feet: beautiful! Can you show me your good-news march?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul wrote that the feet of those who bring good news are beautiful. We feel eager to go."],["NAME","That I-want-to-go-tell feeling is called eager. Your mind might think, “Who needs to hear?” Your body might feel bouncy feet, a quick heartbeat."],["FIND","When did you walk somewhere to bring someone good news?"],["FORK","We can keep good news to ourselves, or let our feet carry it to someone. Let your feet carry it."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and wiggle your toes under the blanket. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, show me where to carry Your good news.”"]],"remember":"How beautiful are the feet of those who bring good news about Jesus!","ask":["“Who did Paul say shall be saved?”","“Why are feet beautiful?”","“What did Wren carry down the road?”"],"share":["Share a time someone’s “beautiful feet” brought the gospel to you.","Where could your feet carry good news this week?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who walked far to share or receive the gospel."]},"b20-w3-n5":{"story":["Long ago, people brought animals to the temple as gifts for Heavenly Father. Paul said there was a new kind of gift.","He told the believers in Rome, “Give your bodies as a living gift.” Give your hands, your feet, your whole self, to do good.","Then Paul used a picture. A body has many members, many parts. Eyes, ears, hands, and feet all have different jobs.","Your eye cannot walk, and your foot cannot see. But together they work just right. Paul said Jesus’s followers are like one body too. Some teach, some give, and some show kindness with a happy heart.","Everyone has a gift, and every gift matters. Tomorrow, use your gift to help someone."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see!” Paul said the church is a body, and every part has a different job. Scout thinks he might be the eyes. In Scout’s world today: At the ward service project, Scout is too small to carry boxes. He feels useless. Then the leader says, “Scout, you have sharp eyes. Find every box with a red label.” Scout finds all twelve. “The eyes can’t carry, but the hands can’t find!” Just like Paul wrote, Scout learned that every member of the body has a job, and every job matters.","feel":"Point to your eyes, blink, blink, they see. Point to your ears, they hear. Wiggle your hands, they help. Stomp your feet, they go. Now hug yourself, one body! Can you show me your one-body hug?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul said we are many members in one body, and every one has a gift to use. We feel useful."],["NAME","That I-have-a-job-to-do feeling is called useful. Your mind might think, “The body needs me.” Your body might feel ready hands, a steady chest."],["FIND","When did you find out you were good at something the group needed?"],["FORK","We can wish we had someone else’s gift, or use our own with all our heart. Use your own."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel every part of your body working together. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me use my gift.”"]],"remember":"We are many members in one body, and your gift matters!","ask":["“What did Paul say to give to Heavenly Father instead of an animal?”","“What are members?”","“What job did Scout get at the service project?”"],"share":["Share how you discovered your gift and where it fits in the body of Christ.","Which member of the body do you tend to overlook or undervalue?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor whose quiet gift held the church family together."]},"b20-w3-n6":{"story":["Paul wrote to the believers in Rome. Some people were being mean to them.","Paul told them, “Bless the people who are cruel to you.” That means wish good things for them, not bad things. “If someone is mean to you, do not be mean back.”","“As much as you can,” Paul said, “live in peace with everyone.” Do not try to get even. Heavenly Father will make things right.","Then Paul said something surprising. “If your enemy is hungry, feed him. If he is thirsty, give him a drink.” Mean cannot beat mean. Only good can win. “Do not let evil beat you,” Paul said. “Beat evil with good.”","Is someone hard to be kind to? Tomorrow, try one kind thing for them."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” Paul said if your enemy is hungry, feed him, and that only good beats evil. Willow has tested it. It works. In Willow’s world today: A girl named Kenzie knocked over Willow’s sandcastle and laughed. The next day Kenzie forgets her lunch. Willow’s tummy says, “Good, let her be hungry.” Her heart says, “Feed her.” She splits her sandwich. Kenzie whispers, “Sorry about your castle.” Just like Paul wrote, Willow learned that feeding your enemy can melt a hard heart, and good overcomes evil.","feel":"Make a mean face and shake a fist, no, no, don’t do that back! Now hold out half a pretend sandwich to someone who was mean. Watch their face melt. Can you show me your half-a-sandwich hand?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul said do not repay evil for evil, but feed your enemy and overcome evil with good. Willow felt gentle."],["NAME","That I-choose-kind-anyway feeling is called gentle. Your mind might think, “Good wins.” Your body might feel unclenched fists and a soft face."],["FIND","When did you do something kind for someone who had been mean to you?"],["FORK","Willow could let Kenzie go hungry, or share her sandwich. She shared."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your fists unclench under the blanket. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me be gentle and good.”"]],"remember":"Be not overcome of evil, but overcome evil with good!","ask":["“What should we do if our enemy is hungry?”","“What is the only thing that can overcome evil?”","“What did Willow share with Kenzie?”"],"share":["Share a time goodness overcame something mean in your life.","Who is the “enemy” you could feed this week?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who answered unkindness with kindness."]},"b20-w3-n7":{"story":["Paul wrote, “Owe no one anything, except to love each other.” Love is something we always owe. We keep giving it, again and again.","Paul remembered Heavenly Father’s rules. Do not steal. Do not hurt anyone. Do not lie. Do not want what is someone else’s.","Then Paul said every rule fits inside one sentence: “Love your neighbor as yourself.” Love never hurts anyone. So love keeps every rule at once.","At the end of his letter, Paul thanked his friends by name. Phebe had helped many people, and Paul too. Priscilla and Aquila had risked their lives to keep Paul safe. Love was real people helping each other.","Tomorrow, try to fill your day with love. Then you will keep every rule."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer says, “Wow, look at that!” Paul said all the rules fit inside one: love your neighbor. Wren wonders how one word can hold so much. In Wren’s world today: Wren’s class has a list of twenty rules on the wall. She can’t remember them all. Her teacher says, “Just do this one: be kind to everyone.” Wren tries it all day and never breaks a single rule on the list. “Kind covers everything!” Just like Paul wrote, Wren learned that love your neighbor is the one rule that keeps all the others.","feel":"Hold up ten fingers, ten rules, so many! Now fold them into one big heart with your hands. Hug the heart to your chest. Wave hello to Phebe and Priscilla! Can you show me your one-rule heart?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul said all the commandments fit inside “love your neighbor as yourself,” and thanked friends who lived it. We feel loving."],["NAME","That I-want-good-for-you feeling is called loving. Your mind might think, “How can I help?” Your body might feel a full heart and warm, open hands."],["FIND","When did loving someone make it easy to do the right thing?"],["FORK","We can try to remember every rule, or fill ourselves with love and keep them all. Fill up with love."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel love fill you like a cup. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, fill me with love for my neighbor.”"]],"remember":"Love your neighbor as yourself, love is the fulfilling of the law!","ask":["“What is the one thing we never finish owing?”","“What one sentence holds all the commandments?”","“What one rule did Wren’s teacher give her?”"],"share":["Share how loving someone made obedience natural rather than hard.","Who is the Phebe or Priscilla in your life, who risked something for you?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor whose love for others fulfilled the law without needing a list."]},"b20-w4-n1":{"story":["Paul wrote a letter to Jesus’s followers in Corinth, a city by the sea. Chloe’s family had told him some sad news.","The believers were arguing and taking sides. “I follow Paul!” some said. “I follow Apollos!” said others. “I follow Peter!” They argued about which teacher was best.","Paul begged them, “Please do not split apart. Be joined together, thinking the same way.”","“Is Jesus split into pieces?” Paul asked. “Did Paul die on the cross for you? No!” Paul said he planted the seed and Apollos watered it. But Heavenly Father made it grow. They were all workers together with Him.","In Jesus’s family, we are all one team. Tomorrow, help someone feel they belong."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big faith, brave heart!” The Corinthians were picking teams, and Paul said, “I planted, Apollos watered, Heavenly Father made it grow.” One team, and Beau knows the captain. In Beau’s world today: At church ball, the boys split into “Brother Lee’s kids” and “Brother Diaz’s kids” and start arguing. Beau stands in the middle. “We’re all on Jesus’s team. Mix it up!” They shuffle the sides, and the game is the best one all year. Just like Paul taught, Beau learned that in Jesus’s family there is only one team.","feel":"Point left, “my side!” Point right, “my side!” Now bring both hands together and lock your fingers tight, perfectly joined! Lift them up to the Captain. Can you show me your joined hands?","stones":[["SPOT","The Corinthians took sides, but Paul said there is one team, and Heavenly Father makes it grow. They felt united."],["NAME","That all-on-one-team feeling is called united. Your mind might think, “No sides.” Your body might feel linked arms and a shared heartbeat."],["FIND","When did a group stop taking sides and become one team?"],["FORK","The Corinthians could keep arguing about teachers, or be joined together in Jesus. Be joined together."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and lock your fingers together on your chest. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, join us together as one.”"]],"remember":"I planted, Apollos watered, but Heavenly Father made it grow, one team!","ask":["“What were the Corinthians arguing about?”","“Who makes the seed grow?”","“What did Beau tell the boys at church ball?”"],"share":["Share a time unity in your ward or family mattered more than being right.","Where might you be “taking sides” instead of being joined together in Christ?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who was a peacemaker in the church."]},"b20-w4-n2":{"story":["The believers in Corinth were not famous. Paul told them to look around at each other.","“Not many of you are wise, the way the world thinks,” he said. “Not many are mighty. Not many come from important families.” Most of them seemed ordinary.","But Paul said that was all right. Heavenly Father had chosen the weak and small things. He uses them to do big, surprising work.","Why does He do it that way? So no one can brag, “I did it all myself!” When small people do big things, everyone can see it was His power. Long ago, Heavenly Father chose a shepherd boy, David, to be king.","You are not too small. Tomorrow, try something brave and let Heavenly Father help you."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer says, “Wow, look at that!” Paul said Heavenly Father picks the small and weak to do the big and mighty. Wren is the smallest in her class, and now that seems best. In Wren’s world today: The Primary program needs someone to say the opening prayer, and the big kids all freeze. Tiny Wren raises her hand. Her voice shakes, but every word is clear. A grandma says, “That little prayer was the best part.” Just like Paul wrote, Wren learned that Heavenly Father chooses small things to do big work.","feel":"Curl up as tiny as you can, a little seed. Now feel Heavenly Father’s power lift you up, up, up into a giant tree with branches. Stretch wide! Can you show me your tiny-to-tall?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul said Heavenly Father chooses the weak and small to do mighty work, so the glory is His. The small felt chosen."],["NAME","That He-picked-little-me feeling is called chosen. Your mind might think, “He wants me.” Your body might feel a straighter back, a surprised smile."],["FIND","When did you do something big even though you were the smallest or youngest?"],["FORK","The weak can think, “I’m too small to matter,” or let Heavenly Father work through them. Let Him work."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel His strength filling your small body. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, I am small, but You are strong.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father chooses the weak things to confound the mighty, small can do big!","ask":["“Were most of the Corinthians wise and mighty?”","“Why does Heavenly Father use small, weak things?”","“What did Wren do at the Primary program?”"],"share":["Share a time the Lord did something big through you when you felt small.","Where are you waiting to feel “big enough” before you let Him use you?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who was ordinary in the world’s eyes and mighty in the Lord’s."]},"b20-w4-n3":{"story":["Paul wrote words people love to say. “Eye has not seen, nor ear heard,” he wrote. Heavenly Father has wonderful things ready for those who love Him.","Those things are bigger than anything we have seen or heard, or even imagined.","So how can we know about them? Paul said Heavenly Father shows them to us by His Spirit.","Paul asked, who knows what you are thinking? Only you know! In the same way, only His Spirit knows Heavenly Father’s thoughts. And He has given us His Spirit, the Holy Ghost. The Holy Ghost teaches our hearts what eyes cannot see.","Tonight, be very still and listen with your heart. The Holy Ghost can teach you too."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see!” Scout likes to see with his eyes. But Paul said some things are too big for eyes, and the Spirit shows them to hearts. In Scout’s world today: Scout asks Grandpa, “How do you know Heavenly Father is real if you can’t see Him?” Grandpa says, “Close your eyes. Do you know I love you?” “Yes.” “Can you see love?” “No.” “The Spirit tells your heart what eyes can’t.” Scout nods. Just like Paul wrote, Scout learned that the Spirit teaches our hearts what our eyes cannot see.","feel":"Cover your eyes, can’t see it. Cover your ears, can’t hear it. Now put your hands on your heart and let the Spirit teach you. Open your eyes, “Ohhh!” Can you show me your heart-learning face?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul said eye has not seen what Heavenly Father has prepared, but the Spirit reveals it to us. Scout felt taught."],["NAME","That my-heart-understands feeling is called taught. Your mind might think, “Oh, I see it now.” Your body might feel a warm chest and wondering eyes."],["FIND","When did you understand something in your heart that you could not see with your eyes?"],["FORK","We can trust only what our eyes see, or let the Spirit teach our hearts. Let the Spirit teach."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly with hands on your heart. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, show me what eyes cannot see.”"]],"remember":"Eye has not seen what Heavenly Father has prepared, but His Spirit shows our hearts!","ask":["“What has eye not seen and ear not heard?”","“Who reveals the things of Heavenly Father to us?”","“What did Grandpa tell Scout to close?”"],"share":["Share a time the Spirit taught you something no sense could have.","How do you make room for revelation when you want to see proof first?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who was taught by the Spirit in a way that shaped your family."]},"b20-w4-n4":{"story":["Paul had taught the believers in Corinth about Jesus. But they were still arguing and jealous, like little children.","So Paul wrote, “I could not talk to you as grown-ups. You were like babies in Jesus.”","“I fed you with milk, not meat,” he said. “You were not ready for meat yet.” Babies drink milk. Big kids with teeth can chew meat. Paul gave them simple truths first, like milk.","Paul also told them how they would grow. “I planted, Apollos watered, but Heavenly Father gave the increase.” He made them grow bigger. A baby cannot make itself grow. It drinks milk every day, and growing happens.","Tonight, pray and learn one little thing. Heavenly Father will help you grow, day by day."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” Paul fed the new believers milk before meat, because that is what little ones need. A teacher who waits is a warm teacher. In Willow’s world today: Willow wants to read her brother’s chapter book, but the words are too hard and she cries. Mom hands her a picture book with a few words a page. “Milk before meat.” Willow reads the whole thing out loud. “I’m growing!” Just like Paul taught, Willow learned that we start with simple things, and Heavenly Father does the growing.","feel":"Curl up like a baby and drink a pretend bottle, glug, glug. Now grow a little taller and nibble a pretend bite with your teeth. Stretch up tall, growing, growing! Can you show me your growing stretch?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul fed the new believers milk, simple truths, before meat, and said Heavenly Father gives the growing. They felt growing."],["NAME","That I’m-getting-bigger feeling is called growing. Your mind might think, “I couldn’t before.” Your body might feel a tall stretch, a proud grin."],["FIND","When did something hard become easy because you practiced the simple things first?"],["FORK","We can be upset that we are not big yet, or drink our milk and let Heavenly Father grow us. Drink your milk."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel yourself growing a tiny bit while you rest. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for growing me.”"]],"remember":"Milk before meat, and Heavenly Father gives the increase!","ask":["“What did Paul feed the Corinthians, milk or meat?”","“Who gives the increase?”","“What book did Mom give Willow?”"],"share":["Share how the Lord grew your testimony a little at a time.","What “milk” do you need to go back to in a busy season?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who taught you simple truths patiently."]},"b20-w4-n5":{"story":["Paul asked the believers in Corinth a question. “Do you not know that you are the temple of Heavenly Father?”","A temple is Heavenly Father’s holy house. People keep it clean, quiet, and beautiful, because it is His.","Paul said your body is like that. It is a temple of the Holy Ghost, who lives in you.","“You are not your own,” Paul said. “You were bought with a price.” Jesus gave His life for you. So you are very precious to Heavenly Father. Paul said to honor Him with your body, and let it shine for Him.","Take good care of your temple. Eat good food, rest, stay safe, and think good thoughts tonight."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big faith, brave heart!” Paul said Beau’s body is a temple, and the Spirit lives inside. Beau stands a little straighter now. In Beau’s world today: Beau’s friends dare him to drink a whole bottle of soda and jump off the shed roof. Beau says, “Nope. My body’s a temple, and I’m the guard.” One says, “Actually, that’s kind of cool.” Beau eats an apple instead. Just like Paul wrote, Beau learned that his body is a temple of Heavenly Father, and he is its guard.","feel":"Stand tall like a temple with your arms as pillars. Sweep it clean, sweep, sweep. Now stand guard at the door with a strong face. Pat your tummy: good food only! Can you show me your temple guard?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul said your body is a temple where the Holy Ghost lives, bought with a price. Beau felt respectful."],["NAME","That I’ll-take-care feeling is called respectful. Your mind might think, “This is holy.” Your body might feel a straight back, careful hands."],["FIND","When did you take care of something because it was precious?"],["FORK","Beau could do the dare to look cool, or guard his temple. He guarded it."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel the Spirit living quietly inside your temple. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for my temple.”"]],"remember":"Your body is a temple of the Holy Ghost, so take good care of it!","ask":["“What did Paul say your body is?”","“Who lives in your temple?”","“What did Beau say to the dare?”"],"share":["Share how seeing your body as a temple changed a choice you made.","What does “glorify Heavenly Father in your body” look like for you this week?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who treated their body with respect as a gift from Heavenly Father."]},"b20-w4-n6":{"story":["The believers in Corinth were bragging. But they had let a wrong thing stay among them.","Paul asked, “Don’t you know a little leaven leavens the whole lump?” Leaven is yeast, the tiny pinch that makes bread dough puff up.","One pinch is enough. Wait a while, and the whole lump puffs up big. Paul said one wrong thing grows that way too.","“Clean out the old leaven,” Paul said, “so you can be new.” At Passover long ago, families cleaned every bit of leaven from their homes. Because Jesus gave Himself for us, we can start fresh, honest and true.","Is there a little wrong thing to clean out? Tell Mom or Dad, and say sorry tonight."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer says, “Wow, look at that!” One pinch of yeast puffs up a whole loaf. Wren watched it in Grandma’s kitchen, and now she sees why Paul said clean out little wrongs. In Wren’s world today: Wren tells one tiny fib about brushing her teeth. The next day she fibs about homework. By Friday her tummy aches from fibs. She tells Mom everything. “The little one grew.” Mom nods. “Let’s clean it out.” Just like Paul warned, Wren learned that a little wrong can puff up big, so you clean it out early.","feel":"Sprinkle a tiny pinch of pretend yeast into the dough. Now watch it puff: grow bigger, bigger, BIGGER with your arms! Now sweep the old leaven out the door, sweep, sweep. Can you show me your clean-sweep?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul said a little leaven puffs up the whole lump, so clean out the old and be honest and true. Wren felt careful."],["NAME","That I’ll-watch-the-little-things feeling is called careful. Your mind might think, “Catch it small.” Your body might feel alert eyes and tidy hands."],["FIND","When did a little wrong thing grow bigger because you let it stay?"],["FORK","Wren could let the little fibs keep growing, or clean them out early. She cleaned them out."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and sweep every crumb of worry out the door. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me stay honest and true.”"]],"remember":"A little leaven leavens the whole lump, so clean out the little wrongs!","ask":["“What is leaven?”","“What happens to dough with a pinch of leaven?”","“What little thing grew in Wren’s week?”"],"share":["Share a time a small compromise grew until you had to clean it out.","What “old leaven” is the Lord inviting you to purge right now?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who was careful about small things and honest in all things."]},"b20-w4-n7":{"story":["Paul wrote a tender letter to the believers in Corinth. “I do not write to shame you,” he said. “I warn you as my dear children.”","Paul said they might have ten thousand teachers, but not many fathers. Paul had brought them the good news about Jesus. So he called himself their father, and loved them like his own children.","Then he said, “Be followers of me, as I follow Christ.”","Paul did not mean, “Follow me because I am great.” He meant, “Watch how I follow Jesus, and do the same.” So Paul sent Timothy, whom he loved like a son. Timothy would remind them how Paul followed Jesus.","Who helps you follow Jesus? Tomorrow, follow their good example, and be one too."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see!” Paul said, “Follow me as I follow Christ.” Scout likes a guide who walks ahead on the trail, as long as the guide is following Jesus. Then you know where the trail goes. In Scout’s world today: On a hike, Scout can’t tell which fork is right. Big cousin Mateo says, “Step where I step. I’m following the trail markers.” Scout watches Mateo’s boots and Mateo watches the markers. They reach the waterfall together. Just like Paul taught, Scout learned to follow people who are following Jesus.","feel":"Walk in place, following a guide ahead, step where he steps. Now look past the guide and point far ahead to Jesus. Keep walking, step, step. Can you show me your follow-the-guide walk?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul said, “Be followers of me, as I am of Christ,” and sent Timothy to show them. The Corinthians felt guided."],["NAME","That someone-shows-me-the-way feeling is called guided. Your mind might think, “Step where they step.” Your body might feel watching eyes, sure feet."],["FIND","When did someone’s good example show you where to put your feet?"],["FORK","We can follow anyone who seems cool, or follow people who are following Jesus. Follow the ones following Jesus."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and picture a kind guide walking ahead toward Jesus. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for good guides.”"]],"remember":"Be followers of me, as I also am of Christ, follow people who follow Jesus!","ask":["“What did Paul call himself to the Corinthians?”","“Who did Paul say he was following?”","“Who did Scout follow on the hike?”"],"share":["Share a person whose example led you closer to Christ.","Who is watching your feet right now, and where are they leading?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor whose example your family still follows."]},"b20-w5-n1":{"story":["In Corinth, some meat was first offered to idols, statues that could not help. Some people who loved Jesus said, “Idols are nothing, so the meat is fine.” Others felt it was wrong.","Paul agreed the idols were nothing. But being right was not the most important thing.","He wrote, “Don’t let your freedom become a stumbling block.” That is like a rock on a path that trips someone.","A brother might copy you, even if he felt it was wrong. That would hurt his heart, and Paul said it was a sin against Jesus. So Paul said, “If meat makes my brother trip, I will never eat meat.”","What could you give up to help a friend? Caring matters more than being right."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” Paul said he would give up meat forever rather than trip a friend. Watching out for a heart is warmer than winning an argument. In Willow’s world today: Willow’s friend Gracie’s family doesn’t watch cartoons with magic. At Willow’s house Willow reaches for her favorite show, then stops. “Let’s do puzzles instead.” Gracie relaxes. Later Mom says, “You gave up your show for her.” Willow shrugs. “She’s my friend.” Just like Paul, Willow learned to give up her own way rather than trip a friend’s heart.","feel":"Walk along happily, then see a block in a friend’s path. Stop! Bend down and move it out of the way. Now take your friend’s hand and walk on together. Can you show me your clear-the-path?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul would give up eating meat rather than make a brother stumble. He felt considerate."],["NAME","That I’m-thinking-of-you feeling is called considerate. Your mind might think, “How will this feel?” Your body might feel careful feet, gentle eyes."],["FIND","When did you give up something you liked so a friend would feel okay?"],["FORK","Paul could eat whatever he wanted because he was right, or give it up for his brother. He gave it up."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and think of one friend’s heart you want to protect. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me be considerate.”"]],"remember":"Paul said, “I will not make my brother stumble,” and neither will you!","ask":["“What is a stumbling block?”","“What did Paul say he would give up for his brother?”","“What did Willow do instead of watching her show?”"],"share":["Share a time you gave up a freedom for someone else’s conscience.","Is there a “liberty” of yours that might be tripping someone right now?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who was considerate of others’ weaknesses."]},"b20-w5-n2":{"story":["Paul used a running race to teach the people in Corinth. He wrote, “All the runners run, but only one gets the prize. So run to win!”","Paul said runners who want to win are careful in everything. They eat well, practice hard, and say no to things that slow them down.","Their prize was a crown that would not last. But Paul said we run for a crown that lasts forever.","Following Jesus is like that race. Paul said, “I run, and I know where I am going.” He made his body obey him, so he would not lose the prize.","When something feels hard, don’t quit. Keep running toward Jesus and the prize that lasts forever."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big faith, brave heart!” Paul said run the race like you mean to win, and train your body to obey. Beau laces up his shoes for that every morning. In Beau’s world today: Beau runs the one-mile fun run. He wants to quit on the hill. Then he hears Dad: “Eyes on the finish!” He pumps his arms and finishes. Dad says, “The real prize is you didn’t quit.” Just like Paul, Beau learned to run toward Jesus with his eyes on the finish and never give up.","feel":"Crouch at the starting line. Ready, set, go! Run in place, pump your arms, up the hill, don’t quit! Cross the finish line with arms up high! Can you show me your finish-line arms?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul said run the race to win, train hard, and keep your eyes on the prize that lasts forever. He felt determined."],["NAME","That I-will-not-quit feeling is called determined. Your mind might think, “Eyes on the finish.” Your body might feel pumping arms and a set jaw."],["FIND","When did you keep going even when you wanted to quit?"],["FORK","Paul could run without a goal, or train hard with his eyes on the forever prize. He trained."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly like a runner resting after a long race, well done. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me finish my race.”"]],"remember":"Run the race to win the crown that lasts forever!","ask":["“What did Paul say the runners run to get?”","“What kind of crown do we run for?”","“What did Dad say the real prize was?”"],"share":["Share what keeps you running when discipleship feels like a long hill.","What are you “temperate” about, and what still slows you down?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who finished their race faithfully."]},"b20-w5-n3":{"story":["Paul sent the people in Corinth a warning and a promise. The warning: “If you think you stand strong, be careful not to fall.”","Then the promise. Paul said every person feels temptation, a pull inside to do something wrong. You are not bad for feeling that pull.","Paul said Heavenly Father is faithful, so He always keeps His word. He will never let the pull grow bigger than you can handle.","With every temptation, He makes a way to escape, like a door out. You can walk away, pray, or find a grown-up.","Next time you feel that pull, look for the door Heavenly Father made. Then be brave and walk right through it."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer says, “Wow, look at that!” Paul said every temptation comes with a way to escape, built right in. Wren loves finding the hidden door, and there always is one. In Wren’s world today: Wren’s cousins want to sneak into the pantry for candy before dinner. Wren feels the pull. Then she remembers: there’s always a door. “I’m going to go help Grandma set the table.” She walks out, and the pull fades. Just like Paul promised, Wren learned that Heavenly Father always makes a way to escape temptation.","feel":"Feel a pull toward something wrong, lean, lean, uh oh. Now look around: there’s a door! Point to it, run to it, open it, and step through to safety. Phew! Can you show me your escape door?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul said temptation is common to everyone, but Heavenly Father always makes a way to escape. Wren felt brave."],["NAME","That I-can-walk-away feeling is called brave. Your mind might think, “There’s a door.” Your body might feel turning feet and a lifted chin."],["FIND","When did you feel pulled to do something wrong and find a way out?"],["FORK","Wren could follow her cousins into the pantry, or take the way of escape. She took the door."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and picture a safe door He built just for you. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for making a way out.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father is faithful, and He always makes a way to escape!","ask":["“What is temptation?”","“What does Heavenly Father make with every temptation?”","“Where did Wren go instead of the pantry?”"],"share":["Share a time the Lord opened a way of escape you did not expect.","What “doors” do you keep ready for your most common temptations?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who found a way out of a hard temptation."]},"b20-w5-n4":{"story":["Paul told about the night a false friend turned against Jesus. On that same night, the Lord Jesus took bread.","He gave thanks and broke the bread. He said, “Take and eat. This is my body, broken for you. Do this to remember me.”","After supper, Jesus took the cup. He said, “This cup is my new promise, made with my blood. Drink it to remember me.”","The bread and cup help us tell how Jesus died for us. Paul said we do this until He comes again. At church we call it the sacrament, bread and water to remember Him.","This week, hold the bread gently and think about Jesus. Whisper inside, “I remember you, Jesus.”"],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see!” Jesus said, “Do this in remembrance of Me,” and Paul passed it on. Scout loves that remembering Jesus is something you do with your hands every week. In Scout’s world today: During the sacrament, Scout’s little brother whispers, “Why bread?” Scout whispers back, “To remember Jesus’s body. The water is to remember His blood. He said to.” His brother holds the bread very carefully and closes his eyes. “I’m remembering.” Just like Paul taught, Scout learned that the sacrament is how we remember Jesus with our hands.","feel":"Hold a pretend piece of bread, small and careful. Close your eyes and remember Jesus. Now hold a tiny cup and sip, remembering again. Fold your arms, quiet and still. Can you show me your remembering face?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus said, “This do in remembrance of Me,” and the sacrament helps us remember Him each week. We feel remembering."],["NAME","That thinking-of-Him-on-purpose feeling is called remembering. Your mind might think, “For me.” Your body might feel still hands, a full heart."],["FIND","What helps you remember Jesus during the sacrament?"],["FORK","We can take the bread and water without thinking, or remember Jesus on purpose. Remember Him."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and remember one thing Jesus did for you. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me always remember Jesus.”"]],"remember":"Jesus said, “This do in remembrance of Me,” so remember Him every week!","ask":["“What did Jesus say the bread was for?”","“What do we call the bread and water at church?”","“What did Scout’s brother ask during the sacrament?”"],"share":["Share what the sacrament has come to mean to you over the years.","How do you prepare your heart to “remember Him” each Sunday?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor for whom the sacrament was sacred, and what they taught you."]},"b20-w5-n5":{"story":["Paul wrote about gifts, but not the kind wrapped in paper. He meant good things a person can do to help others.","“There are many kinds of gifts,” Paul said, “but the same Spirit.” The Spirit, the Holy Ghost, gives every one of them.","To one person He gives wisdom to know what is right. To another, faith, or healing the sick. To another, speaking new languages.","Nobody gets every gift, but everyone gets one. And Paul said each gift is given to help everyone. Your gift is not just for you.","Some gifts are loud, and some are quiet, like being kind. Tonight, think of one gift you have. How can you share it tomorrow?"],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” Paul said every gift comes from one Spirit and is meant to help everyone. Willow loves that every gift is for giving. In Willow’s world today: Willow can’t sing like her sister or build like her brother, and she feels left out. Then Grandpa says, “When I was sad, who sat with me?” “Me.” “That’s your gift, Willow. Comfort. We’d fall apart without it.” Willow feels seen. Just like Paul taught, Willow learned that every person has a gift from the Spirit, and every gift helps everyone.","feel":"Hold out your hands and receive a gift from the Spirit. Now pass it to the person next to you. Receive another, pass it on. Keep the gifts going around! Can you show me your gift-passing hands?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul said there are many gifts, but one Spirit gives them all, to help everyone. Willow felt appreciated."],["NAME","That my-gift-matters feeling is called appreciated. Your mind might think, “They need what I have.” Your body might feel warm cheeks, a quiet glow."],["FIND","When did someone notice a gift in you that you didn’t see?"],["FORK","Willow could feel left out because her gift was quiet, or use it gladly. She used it."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel the Spirit’s gift resting in you. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for my gift.”"]],"remember":"Many gifts, one Spirit, and every gift is for helping everyone!","ask":["“Who gives all the different gifts?”","“Are gifts just for the person who has them?”","“What did Grandpa say Willow’s gift was?”"],"share":["Share a gift of the Spirit you have received and how it has blessed others.","Whose quiet gift in your ward or family have you overlooked?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor whose spiritual gift blessed your family line."]},"b20-w5-n6":{"story":["Paul wanted Jesus’s followers to care for one another. So he told about a body. “The body has many parts,” he said. “So it is with Christ.”","Then Paul made it funny. The foot said, “I am not a hand, so I don’t belong.” Is it still part of the body? Of course!","What if the whole body were one big eye? How would it hear? Every part has its own job.","Paul said the eye cannot tell the hand, “I don’t need you.” The parts that seem weakest are needed. When one part hurts, the whole body hurts with it.","Paul said, “Now you are the body of Christ.” That means you! Tonight, wiggle your toes and remember: you are needed."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big faith, brave heart!” Paul said the eye can’t tell the hand, “I don’t need you.” Even a brave arm needs a foot. In Beau’s world today: Beau breaks his pinky toe and can’t run at all. “It’s just a pinky toe!” But every step hurts. Grandpa chuckles. “Smallest part, and the whole body limps. That’s Paul’s lesson.” Beau hobbles to the couch. “Never again ‘just a pinky toe.’” Just like Paul wrote, Beau learned that every part of the body of Christ is needed, even the smallest.","feel":"Be a foot, stomp, stomp. Be a hand, wave, wave. Be an ear, listen. Be an eye, blink. Now say, “I need you!” to each part and hug your whole body. Can you show me your whole-body hug?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul said the body has many members and every one is needed, especially the small ones. Beau felt needed."],["NAME","That the-body-needs-me feeling is called needed. Your mind might think, “I matter here.” Your body might feel a strong heartbeat, planted feet."],["FIND","When did you find out a small part of something was actually very important?"],["FORK","A part can say, “I don’t belong,” or believe it is needed by the whole body. Believe you are needed."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel every part of your body resting together. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that I am needed.”"]],"remember":"You are the body of Christ, and every member is needed!","ask":["“What did the foot say in Paul’s story?”","“Can the eye say to the hand, “I don’t need you”?”","“What did Beau learn from his pinky toe?”"],"share":["Share a time a “weak” member of the body turned out to be essential.","Who in your ward feels like a pinky toe, and how can you honor them?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who made every member of the family feel needed."]},"b20-w5-n7":{"story":["Paul wrote about charity, loving others the way Jesus loves. “If I talk like angels but have no charity, I’m a noisy cymbal.” A cymbal is loud, but it cannot love.","Paul said, “With faith to move mountains but no charity, I am nothing.”","Then Paul told what charity is like. Charity is patient, and it is kind. It does not want what others have, and it does not brag.","Charity is not selfish, and it is not quick to get mad. “Charity never faileth,” Paul said. Faith, hope and charity stay, “but the greatest of these is charity.”","Tomorrow, try one charity thing. Wait for your turn, share, or stay calm when something breaks."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer says, “Wow, look at that!” Paul said you could move mountains and still be nothing without charity. Wren wonders at a love greater than mountains, and wants it. In Wren’s world today: Wren’s family picks one word to live for a week. Wren picks charity. She waits patiently for her turn, shares without being asked, and doesn’t get mad when her brother breaks her crayon. On Sunday Dad says, “Wren, you were our charity this week.” Just like Paul wrote, Wren learned that the greatest gift is charity, loving like Jesus loves.","feel":"Clang pretend cymbals, crash, crash, loud but empty! Now stop and put both hands over your heart. Let love fill it up, bigger, bigger, and open your arms to everyone. Can you show me your charity arms?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul said without charity we are nothing, and charity is patient, kind, and never fails. Wren felt charitable."],["NAME","That I-love-like-Jesus feeling is called charitable. Your mind might think, “Patient, kind, forever.” Your body might feel a soft heart and open arms."],["FIND","When did someone show you patient, kind love that didn’t give up on you?"],["FORK","We can be loud and talented without love, or fill up with charity first. Fill up with charity."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let charity fill your heart. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, give me the pure love of Christ.”"]],"remember":"Faith, hope, and charity, but the greatest of these is charity!","ask":["“What is charity?”","“Name one thing charity is, from Paul’s list.”","“What word did Wren pick to live for a week?”"],"share":["Share how you have felt the pure love of Christ, and how it changed you.","Which line of Paul’s list do you most need right now?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor whose charity never failed your family."]},"b24-w1-n1":{"story":["John was sent away to a little island called Patmos for teaching about Jesus. One Sunday, he heard a voice behind him, loud like a trumpet.","John turned and saw Jesus among seven golden candlesticks. His face shone like the sun. John fell down at His feet.","Then Jesus did a very kind thing. He laid His right hand on John. He said, “Fear not.”","Jesus was telling John not to be afraid. He said, “I was dead, and now I am alive forever.” Then He told John to write what he saw in a book.","When you feel scared or lost, remember Jesus’s hand on John. Tonight, whisper, “Fear not,” and know Jesus is alive and near."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big faith, brave heart!” John saw Jesus shining like the sun and fell down, and Jesus put a hand on him: “Fear not. I am alive for evermore.” That hand makes Beau brave. In Beau’s world today: Beau gets lost at the fair, and his knees shake. Then a big hand lands on his shoulder. “Fear not, buddy.” It’s Dad. Beau’s knees stop shaking. “That’s what Jesus did for John,” he says later. Just like John, Beau learned that Jesus’s hand on your shoulder says, “Fear not, I am alive.”","feel":"Hear a trumpet voice and turn around. See a face shining like the sun! Fall down low. Feel a hand on your shoulder: “Fear not.” Stand up slowly, brave. Can you show me your fear-not stand?","stones":[["SPOT","John fell down before shining Jesus, but Jesus laid His hand on him: “Fear not, I am alive.” Beau felt reassured."],["NAME","That His-hand-is-on-me feeling is called reassured. Your mind might think, “He’s alive and here.” Your body might feel a warm shoulder, steady knees."],["FIND","When did a hand on your shoulder make you stop being afraid?"],["FORK","John could stay face-down in fear, or rise at Jesus’s “Fear not.” He rose."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel Jesus’s hand on your shoulder. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that Jesus is alive.”"]],"remember":"Jesus said fear not, for He liveth and is alive for evermore!","ask":["“Where was John when he saw Jesus?”","“What did Jesus say when He put His hand on John?”","“Whose hand landed on Beau’s shoulder at the fair?”"],"share":["Share a time the living Christ said “Fear not” to you.","What fear would melt if you felt His right hand on your shoulder tonight?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who knew with certainty that Jesus lives."]},"b24-w1-n2":{"story":["Jesus had John write letters to seven groups of people who loved Him. The first went to the church in a city called Ephesus.","Jesus began with kind words: “I know your works.” He saw how hard they worked and how patient they were.","But then Jesus said something sad. “You have left your first love.”","First love is the warm, happy way you love at the very start. They still worked hard, but their hearts had cooled, like a fire burning low. So Jesus said to remember, turn back, and do what they did at first.","What did you once love doing for Jesus? Tonight, remember how it felt, and do it again."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” Jesus told Ephesus, “You left your first love. Remember, and do the first works.” Going back to that first warmth is the warmest thing. In Willow’s world today: Willow feeds her bunny every day, but has stopped holding him. Grandma asks, “Remember the first day you got him?” Willow remembers cuddling him for an hour. She picks him up and holds him close. “First love,” she whispers. Just like the believers in Ephesus, Willow learned to remember her first love and do the first works again.","feel":"Work hard, scrub, scrub, with a tired, cold face. Now stop and remember: hand on your heart, feel the first warm feeling. Smile like the first day. Do the first works! Can you show me your first-love smile?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus said Ephesus had left their first love, so remember and do the first works. Willow felt rekindled."],["NAME","That the-warmth-is-back feeling is called rekindled. Your mind might think, “I remember.” Your body might feel a warming chest, soft hands."],["FIND","When did you remember how much you loved something at first, and feel it again?"],["FORK","We can keep working with a cold heart, or remember our first love and warm it up. Warm it up."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and remember the first time you felt Jesus loved you. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, bring back my first love.”"]],"remember":"Remember your first love, and do the first works!","ask":["“What did Jesus praise the church in Ephesus for?”","“What had they left?”","“What did Willow do with her bunny?”"],"share":["Share what your “first love” for the Savior felt like, and what rekindles it.","Are you doing the works but missing the warmth? What were the first works?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor whose love for the Lord stayed warm to the end."]},"b24-w1-n3":{"story":["Jesus wrote to a small church in Philadelphia, a name meaning brotherly love. The people there were not big or strong.","But Jesus had good news for them. “I have set before you an open door,” He said. “No one can shut it.”","Why? Jesus said, “You have a little strength, and you have kept My word.”","Just a little strength! Jesus did not say, “Come back when you are bigger.” He opened the door now. Then He said, “Hold on tight to what you have.”","You may feel small, but you can keep Jesus’s word. Tonight, tell Him, “I will keep trying,” and watch for His open door."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see!” Jesus told the little church, “You have a little strength, and you kept My word, so I set an open door before you.” In Scout’s world today: Scout wants to join the big kids’ reading club but only reads small books. He keeps reading every day anyway. The librarian notices. “You keep showing up. I’m opening a spot.” Scout walks in, book held high. Just like the church in Philadelphia, Scout learned that a little strength and keeping Jesus’s word is enough for Him to open a door.","feel":"Flex a tiny muscle, a little strength. Hold tight to a pretend scripture, kept it! Now see a door swing open, creak, and nobody can shut it. Walk through! Can you show me your open-door walk?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus told the little church with a little strength that He set an open door before them. Scout felt encouraged."],["NAME","That my-little-is-enough feeling is called encouraged. Your mind might think, “He opened a door.” Your body might feel a lifted chin, stepping feet."],["FIND","When did your small, steady effort open a door for you?"],["FORK","The little church could give up because they were small, or keep His word with their little strength. They kept it."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and picture a door Jesus opened just for you. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me hold fast what I have.”"]],"remember":"I have set before thee an open door, and no man can shut it!","ask":["“What does Philadelphia mean?”","“How much strength did the church have?”","“What did the librarian open for Scout?”"],"share":["Share a door the Lord opened for you when you had only a little strength.","What are you holding fast right now so that no one takes your crown?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor with “a little strength” who kept His word and found doors opened."]},"b24-w1-n4":{"story":["Jesus sent a letter to a church in a rich city called Laodicea. He told them, “You are not cold, and you are not hot.”","Think of a drink that is not cold and not hot, just lukewarm. Nobody wants that! The people had money and said they needed nothing. Their love for Jesus was lukewarm.","But Jesus did not give up on them. He said one of the kindest things in the Bible.","“I stand at the door, and knock. If anyone hears My voice and opens the door, I will come in.” Then Jesus would eat supper with them, like a friend.","Jesus knocks gently and waits. Tonight, you can pray, “Come in, Jesus,” and open your heart to Him."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” Jesus said, “I stand at the door and knock.” Willow pictures Him waiting politely, and runs to open it. In Willow’s world today: Willow’s family sets an empty chair at Sunday dinner. “That’s for Jesus,” Dad says, “who wants to sup with us.” One night Willow prays, “And Jesus, come in. We opened the door.” The whole table goes quiet and warm. Just like the letter to Laodicea, Willow learned that Jesus knocks gently and waits for us to open the door and let Him in.","feel":"Be lukewarm, blah, shrug. Now listen: knock, knock, knock! Run to the door and swing it open wide. Welcome Jesus in and pull out a chair. Pass Him the bread! Can you show me your door swing?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus said, “I stand at the door and knock. If you open, I will come in and sup with you.” Willow felt welcoming."],["NAME","That come-in-and-sit feeling is called welcoming. Your mind might think, “He’s at my door.” Your body might feel running feet, a wide-open door."],["FIND","When did you hear someone knock and feel so happy to open the door?"],["FORK","We can leave Jesus knocking outside, or open the door and let Him in. Open the door."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and hear Jesus’s gentle knock, then open the door. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, I am letting Jesus in.”"]],"remember":"Behold, I stand at the door and knock, open, and He will come in!","ask":["“What is lukewarm?”","“What does Jesus do at the door?”","“Who is the empty chair for at Willow’s table?”"],"share":["Share a time you opened the door to the Savior and felt Him sit down with you.","Where is your faith lukewarm, and what would it take to hear His knock there?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor whose home always had the door open to the Lord."]},"b24-w1-n5":{"story":["Next, John saw a door open in heaven. A trumpet voice said, “Come up here, and I will show you.”","John saw a throne, a king’s chair. Heavenly Father sat on it. A rainbow went all around it, green like an emerald.","Heavenly creatures with six wings stood around the throne. All day and all night they sang, “Holy, holy, holy.”","Holy means perfectly good, all the way through. Twenty-four elders laid their golden crowns before the throne. They said He is worthy, because He made all things.","When you see a rainbow, remember Heavenly Father’s throne. Tonight, whisper, “Holy, holy, holy,” and thank Him for everything He made."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer says, “Wow, look at that!” John saw a throne in heaven with an emerald rainbow around it, and everyone singing “Holy, holy, holy.” A rainbow that never fades! In Wren’s world today: After a storm, a double rainbow arches over the hills. Wren stares. “John saw a rainbow around Heavenly Father’s throne,” she tells Grandpa. “Maybe He sent this one so we’d remember.” They say, “Holy, holy, holy,” very quietly. Just like John, Wren learned that Heavenly Father sits on a throne surrounded by a rainbow, and all heaven worships Him.","feel":"See a door open in heaven and rise up, up. Draw a giant rainbow over your head, emerald green! Sing softly, “Holy, holy, holy,” and lay a crown down. Can you show me your rainbow arch?","stones":[["SPOT","John saw Heavenly Father’s throne with a rainbow around it, and heaven singing “Holy, holy, holy.” Wren felt worshipful."],["NAME","That I-want-to-sing-to-Him feeling is called worshipful. Your mind might think, “Holy, holy, holy.” Your body might feel a bowed head, a soft voice."],["FIND","When did you see something so beautiful that you wanted to thank Heavenly Father?"],["FORK","We can keep our crowns, or lay them down and worship the One who made all things. Lay them down."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and picture the emerald rainbow around His throne. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, Thou art worthy.”"]],"remember":"There is a rainbow round about the throne, and heaven sings holy, holy, holy!","ask":["“What did John see around the throne?”","“What did the heavenly beings sing?”","“What did Wren and Grandpa say under the double rainbow?”"],"share":["Share a moment of true worship, when you felt His holiness and glory.","What “crown” of yours could you lay down before His throne?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor whose worship of Heavenly Father was deep and real."]},"b24-w1-n6":{"story":["John saw Heavenly Father holding a rolled-up book. It was shut with seven wax seals, so no one could peek inside.","A strong angel called out, “Who is worthy to open the book?” No one in heaven or on earth could open it. No one could even look inside.","John cried hard, because no one was good enough to open the book.","Then an elder said, “Weep not. Look!” There stood a Lamb who had given His life. The Lamb was Jesus! He took the book. Heaven sang, “You are worthy, for You gave Your life for us.”","Tonight, if something feels too hard, look to Jesus, like John did. He can do what no one else can."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see!” Nobody could open the book and John cried. Then someone said, “Weep not, look!” and there was the Lamb. “Look” is the best answer to crying. In Scout’s world today: Scout’s puzzle has a missing piece and he cries, sure it’s gone. Grandpa says, “Weep not. Look.” He points under the rug, and there it is. Scout fits it in. “The Lamb was the missing piece for the whole book,” Grandpa says. Just like John, Scout learned that when nothing else is enough, Jesus the Lamb is worthy.","feel":"Hold a sealed scroll and try to open it, stuck! Cry a little, boo hoo. Now hear, “Weep not, look!” Wipe your eyes and see the Lamb. Lift the scroll: worthy! Can you show me your weep-not look?","stones":[["SPOT","No one could open the sealed book and John wept, but the Lamb, Jesus, was worthy and took it. Scout felt comforted."],["NAME","That someone-is-worthy feeling is called comforted. Your mind might think, “Weep not, look.” Your body might feel wiped eyes and a lifting chin."],["FIND","When were you sad because something seemed impossible, and then someone showed you the answer?"],["FORK","John could keep weeping, or look where the elder pointed. He looked."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your tears dry, the Lamb is worthy. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that Jesus is worthy.”"]],"remember":"Weep not, the Lamb is worthy to open the book!","ask":["“Why did John cry?”","“Who was worthy to open the book?”","“Where was Scout’s missing puzzle piece?”"],"share":["Share a time you wept because nothing was enough, and the Lamb was.","What sealed book are you still weeping over instead of looking to Him?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who found Jesus to be the answer to their deepest sorrow."]},"b24-w1-n7":{"story":["When Jesus, the Lamb, took the book, heaven burst into song. John heard many angels all around the throne.","How many angels? Ten thousand times ten thousand, and thousands of thousands! That is more angels than anyone could ever count.","With a loud voice they sang, “Worthy is the Lamb!” They sang it because Jesus gave His life for us.","Then John heard even more voices. Every creature in heaven, on the earth, and in the sea joined in! They praised Heavenly Father on His throne, and the Lamb, forever.","You can add your voice to that great song. Tonight, sing or whisper, “Worthy is the Lamb!” Every creature is singing it with you."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big faith, brave heart!” Ten thousand times ten thousand angels and every creature singing “Worthy is the Lamb.” Beau wants his voice in that song. In Beau’s world today: At the Christmas program, the Primary sings “Worthy is the Lamb,” and Beau sings so loud the row in front turns around. Grandma says, “You sang like one of the ten thousand times ten thousand.” Beau grins. “Practicing.” Just like John saw, Beau learned that every creature will one day sing to the Lamb, and he can sing now.","feel":"Count angels, ten thousand times ten thousand, too many! Be every creature: flap like a bird, swim like a fish, stomp like an elephant! Shout, “Worthy is the Lamb!” Can you show me your every-creature song?","stones":[["SPOT","John heard ten thousand times ten thousand angels and every creature sing, “Worthy is the Lamb.” Beau felt jubilant."],["NAME","That sing-as-loud-as-I-can feeling is called jubilant. Your mind might think, “Worthy is the Lamb!” Your body might feel a full chest, an open mouth."],["FIND","When did you sing or cheer so loud you forgot to be shy?"],["FORK","We can stay quiet, or add our voice to every creature’s song. Add your voice."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and hum heaven’s song very quietly. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, glory be to You and the Lamb forever.”"]],"remember":"Worthy is the Lamb! Blessing and honour and glory to Him forever and ever!","ask":["“How many angels did John hear?”","“Who will sing to the Lamb?”","“What did Grandma say about Beau’s singing?”"],"share":["Share a time music or worship made you feel part of heaven’s choir.","What keeps your voice quiet in the song every creature will sing?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who sang praises to the Lamb with their whole heart."]},"b24-w2-n1":{"story":["John saw four angels standing at the four corners of the earth. They held back the winds, so not one leaf on a tree would blow.","Another angel came from the east, where the sun comes up. He carried Heavenly Father’s seal, a mark that says, “This one belongs to Me.”","He called to the four angels to wait. Nothing could be hurt until Heavenly Father’s servants were sealed on their foreheads.","So the winds stayed still while His people were marked as His own. John heard how many were sealed, a very big number.","Like a name inside your jacket, Heavenly Father knows you are His. Tonight, touch your forehead and say, “I am His.”"],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer says, “Wow, look at that!” Four angels held back the wind until every one of His people was sealed as His. A world holding its breath so no one gets missed! In Wren’s world today: Before the family hike, Mom writes each kid’s name inside their jacket. “So everyone knows you’re mine.” Wren traces the letters. “Like the seal in John’s story.” Mom smiles. “Nothing starts until you’re marked as mine.” Just like John saw, Wren learned that Heavenly Father marks His people as His own.","feel":"Be a strong angel holding back the wind with both arms, hold it! Now touch your forehead: sealed, His! Look around, nobody missed. Then let the wind go, whoosh. Can you show me your sealed-forehead touch?","stones":[["SPOT","Angels held back the winds until the servants of Heavenly Father were sealed as His own, none missing. Wren felt claimed."],["NAME","That marked-as-His feeling is called claimed. Your mind might think, “I’m His.” Your body might feel a touched forehead, a belonging heart."],["FIND","When did someone write your name on something so everyone would know you belonged?"],["FORK","We can wonder if we are counted, or trust that He has sealed us as His own. Trust it."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and touch your forehead, sealed as His. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for marking me as Yours.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father seals His servants as His own, and not one is missed!","ask":["“What were the four angels holding back?”","“What does a seal show?”","“What did Mom write inside Wren’s jacket?”"],"share":["Share how it feels to know you are sealed as His before any storm begins.","What “winds” are being held back in your life while He marks you as His own?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who knew they belonged to Heavenly Father, come what may."]},"b24-w2-n2":{"story":["John saw a crowd too big to count, from every country and language. They stood before Heavenly Father’s throne and Jesus, the Lamb.","They wore white robes and held palm branches. With loud voices, they praised Heavenly Father and the Lamb.","An elder asked John, “Who are these in white?” Then he told John they had come through very hard times.","Jesus, who gave His life for them, had made their robes white. Now they would never be hungry or thirsty again. And Heavenly Father would wipe away all tears from their eyes.","Heavenly Father sees every tear you cry. Tonight, be gentle like Him, and dry a tear for someone you love."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” John saw a crowd from every nation in white robes, and Heavenly Father wiping away all their tears. The warmest thing in heaven. In Willow’s world today: Willow’s little cousin cries and cries after falling off the swing. Willow sits her on her lap and wipes each tear with her thumb, one by one. “All gone.” Her cousin hiccups and smiles. “That’s what Heavenly Father does.” Just like John saw, Willow learned that Heavenly Father will wipe away every tear.","feel":"Wave a palm branch in a huge crowd! Put on a white robe. Rub your hungry tummy, then eat, full! Feel a gentle thumb wipe one tear, then another, all gone. Smile. Can you show me your tear-wipe?","stones":[["SPOT","John saw a multitude in white robes, and heard that Heavenly Father shall wipe away all tears. Willow felt comforted."],["NAME","That every-tear-wiped feeling is called comforted. Your mind might think, “No more crying there.” Your body might feel dry cheeks, a hiccupy smile."],["FIND","When did someone wipe away your tears until they were all gone?"],["FORK","We can think our tears don’t matter, or trust that He will wipe every one. Trust Him."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let His gentle hand wipe away today’s tears. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for wiping my tears.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father shall wipe away all tears from their eyes!","ask":["“What color were the robes?”","“What will Heavenly Father wipe away?”","“How did Willow wipe her cousin’s tears?”"],"share":["Share a tear you look forward to having wiped away by His own hand.","Whose tears could you wipe this week, as His hands on earth?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who came through great tribulation and now, you trust, has no more tears."]},"b24-w2-n3":{"story":["John saw an angel at the golden altar, a special table before the throne. The angel held a golden cup for burning incense.","Incense is something that makes sweet-smelling smoke when it burns. The angel was given lots and lots of it.","He offered the incense together with the prayers of all of Heavenly Father’s people.","Then John watched the sweet smoke rise up, carrying the prayers with it. Up it went from the angel’s hand, all the way before Heavenly Father. The prayers of ordinary people reached His throne!","Like campfire smoke rising to the stars, your prayers go up to Heavenly Father. Tonight, say a prayer and picture it rising, sweet, all the way to Him."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see!” John saw the prayers of the saints go up before the throne like sweet smoke. Scout wondered where prayers go, and now he knows. In Scout’s world today: At the campfire, Scout watches smoke curl up into the stars. “Where does it go?” “Up,” says Grandpa. “Like prayers. All the way up to Heavenly Father.” Scout says a prayer and watches it rise. Just like John saw, Scout learned that every prayer rises up to Heavenly Father and is received as something sweet.","feel":"Hold a little golden cup on a chain. Whisper a prayer into it. Now watch the sweet smoke rise, up, up, trace it with your finger to the throne. Can you show me your rising prayer?","stones":[["SPOT","John saw an angel offer the prayers of the saints with incense, rising before Heavenly Father. Scout felt heard."],["NAME","That my-prayer-went-up feeling is called heard. Your mind might think, “He received it.” Your body might feel lifted eyes, a quiet chest."],["FIND","When did you feel sure that a prayer of yours reached Heavenly Father?"],["FORK","We can wonder if our prayers go anywhere, or trust that they rise before His throne. Trust that they rise."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and send one prayer up like sweet smoke. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for receiving my prayers.”"]],"remember":"The prayers of the saints rise up before Heavenly Father like sweet smoke!","ask":["“What is incense?”","“What rose up with the smoke?”","“What did Scout watch rise from the campfire?”"],"share":["Share a time you felt your prayers rising and being received.","What prayer have you stopped saying because you doubted it reached Him?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor whose prayers you believe rose up sweet before His throne."]},"b24-w2-n4":{"story":["A mighty angel came down from heaven. A rainbow was on his head, and his face shone like the sun.","He held a little open book. A voice from heaven told John to take it. So John said, “Give me the little book.”","The angel said something surprising: “Take it, and eat it up.”","Eat a book? John ate it, and it was sweet as honey in his mouth. It was a picture of taking Heavenly Father’s words all the way inside. Then John would teach them to many people.","Tonight, ask someone to read you a scripture, and say it back. Let His sweet words go all the way inside."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer says, “Wow, look at that!” An angel told John to eat a little book, and it tasted like honey. Wren wonders what the scriptures taste like. In Wren’s world today: Grandma reads Wren a scripture and then hands her a spoonful of honey. “So you remember His words are sweet.” Wren lets the honey melt. The next morning she asks for “a honey scripture” before breakfast. It becomes their thing, every single day. Just like John, Wren learned that taking Heavenly Father’s words inside is as sweet as honey.","feel":"See a giant angel with a rainbow on his head, one foot on the sea, one on land! Take the little book. Eat it, chomp, mmm, honey! Rub your tummy. Can you show me your honey-book chomp?","stones":[["SPOT","An angel gave John a little book and said to eat it, and it was sweet as honey in his mouth. Wren felt nourished."],["NAME","That His-words-are-sweet feeling is called nourished. Your mind might think, “Sweet as honey.” Your body might feel a happy tongue, a warm middle."],["FIND","When did a scripture feel sweet, like something good to eat?"],["FORK","We can leave the words on the page, or take them all the way inside. Eat the book."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and taste the sweetness of one of His words. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, Your words are sweet as honey.”"]],"remember":"Take Heavenly Father’s words inside, and they are sweet as honey!","ask":["“What was on the angel’s head?”","“What did the little book taste like?”","“What does Grandma give Wren after a scripture?”"],"share":["Share a scripture that was “sweet as honey” to you.","Where are His words still only on the page, instead of eaten and inside you?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who fed on the scriptures like honey."]},"b24-w2-n5":{"story":["Before the world was made, there was a war in heaven. The enemy of all that is good lost, and was thrown out.","Then John heard a loud, happy voice in heaven. It said the accuser was thrown down.","An accuser points and says, “Look what you did wrong!” He said that about Heavenly Father’s children day and night.","How did they beat him? First, by the blood of the Lamb. Jesus gave His life to pay for our wrongs. Second, by their testimony, saying what they know: “Jesus is real.”","When a mean little voice says you are bad, you can answer back. Say, “Jesus paid for that, and I know He is real.”"],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big faith, brave heart!” The accuser was thrown out, and Heavenly Father’s children overcame him by the Lamb and their testimony. Beau has both, so Beau wins. In Beau’s world today: A voice in Beau’s head keeps saying, “You’re bad. Remember what you did.” Beau remembers. “Jesus paid for that,” he says out loud. “And I know He’s real.” The voice goes quiet. He won. Just like John saw, Beau learned that the blood of the Lamb and the word of his testimony overcome the accuser.","feel":"Hear an accusing finger, “You did wrong!” Hold up one hand: the Lamb paid! Hold up the other: I know Jesus is real! Push the accuser out the door! Victory arms! Can you show me your overcame-him arms?","stones":[["SPOT","They overcame the accuser by the blood of the Lamb and the word of their testimony. Beau felt victorious."],["NAME","That the-accuser-is-quiet feeling is called victorious. Your mind might think, “Jesus paid.” Your body might feel raised arms, a winning heart."],["FIND","When did a mean voice in your head go quiet because you remembered Jesus?"],["FORK","Beau could believe the accusing voice, or stand on the Lamb and his testimony. He stood on them."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let the accuser’s voice fade. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that I overcome by Jesus.”"]],"remember":"They overcame by the blood of the Lamb, and by the word of their testimony!","ask":["“What is an accuser?”","“What two things overcame the accuser?”","“What did Beau say out loud to the mean voice?”"],"share":["Share a time your testimony silenced the accuser.","What accusation do you still listen to, that the Lamb has already paid for?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who overcame by the Lamb and their testimony."]},"b24-w2-n6":{"story":["John saw another angel flying through the sky! He carried the everlasting gospel, the good news of Jesus that never ends.","Who was the good news for? Every nation, every family, and every language. Everyone on the earth!","The angel called out with a loud voice. He said to honor and worship Heavenly Father, who made heaven, earth, and sea.","Latter-day Saints think of the angel Moroni here. Moroni helped bring the Book of Mormon, so the good news could spread.","The good news is still flying, and you can help carry it. Tonight, pray for one friend. Tomorrow, tell them, “Jesus loves you.”"],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see!” John saw an angel flying with the everlasting gospel for every nation and language. Scout wants to help carry it. In Scout’s world today: Scout’s family makes paper airplanes with “Jesus loves you” on the wings and gives them to kids at the park. A girl who speaks another language catches one, and her mom reads it to her. The girl beams. “Every tongue,” Scout says, launching another. Just like John saw, Scout learned that the everlasting gospel is for every nation, and he can help carry it.","feel":"Spread your arms and fly through the sky like an angel, swoosh! Hold the good news tight. Call out, “Worship Him that made heaven and earth!” Fly to every nation, swoop! Can you show me your gospel flight?","stones":[["SPOT","John saw an angel flying with the everlasting gospel for every nation, kindred, tongue, and people. Scout felt eager."],["NAME","That I-want-to-carry-the-news feeling is called eager. Your mind might think, “Everyone needs to hear.” Your body might feel flying arms, ready feet."],["FIND","When did you share something about Jesus with someone, and see them light up?"],["FORK","We can keep the good news to ourselves, or help the angel carry it to every nation. Help carry it."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and picture the angel flying over your house. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, I will help carry Your good news.”"]],"remember":"An angel flies with the everlasting gospel to every nation, kindred, tongue, and people!","ask":["“What was the flying angel carrying?”","“Who was the gospel for?”","“What did Scout’s family write on the paper airplanes?”"],"share":["Share how the everlasting gospel reached you, and whom it reached through you.","Which person near you is still waiting for the good news?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who helped carry the gospel to a new place or people."]},"b24-w2-n7":{"story":["John wrote about Heavenly Father’s patient people. They kept His commandments and kept believing in Jesus, even when it was hard.","Then John heard a voice from heaven say, “Write.” The voice said that people who die believing in the Lord are blessed.","Blessed means truly happy, held safe in Heavenly Father’s love.","The Spirit said they get to rest from all their hard work. And their works follow them! Every kind thing they did comes along. None of it is lost.","Nothing good you do is ever lost. Tonight, think of one kind thing you did today. Thank Heavenly Father for it."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” John heard that people who die in the Lord rest, and their works follow them. Willow pictures all the warm things following them home. In Willow’s world today: At Great-Grandma’s funeral, people stand up one after another. “She knitted my baby’s blanket.” “She brought soup when I was sick.” “She taught me to pray.” Willow whispers to Mom, “Her works are following her.” Mom nods. Just like John heard, Willow learned that those who die in the Lord rest, and all their good works follow them.","feel":"Work hard, knit, stir soup, fold your hands to pray. Now lie down and rest, ahh. Watch all your good works line up and follow you, like a trail of light! Can you show me your works-follow trail?","stones":[["SPOT","John heard that those who die in the Lord rest from their labours, and their works do follow them. Willow felt peaceful."],["NAME","That nothing-good-is-lost feeling is called peaceful. Your mind might think, “It all follows them.” Your body might feel a soft sigh, a settled chest."],["FIND","When did you hear about a good thing someone did long ago that still matters?"],["FORK","We can think a life’s kindness disappears, or trust that it follows us home. Trust that it follows."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and picture a kind thing you did following you. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, good is never lost.”"]],"remember":"Blessed are the dead who die in the Lord; they rest, and their works do follow them!","ask":["“What does blessed mean?”","“What follows people who die in the Lord?”","“What did people say about Great-Grandma at the funeral?”"],"share":["Share the works of someone you loved that you know followed them.","What works are you building that will follow you?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor whose works still follow them in your family."]},"b24-w3-n1":{"story":["Jesus’s friend John saw a picture of heaven. He saw a sea of glass glowing with fire, like a shining mirror.","On it stood people who had stayed true to Jesus through hard things. They held Heavenly Father’s harps.","They sang the song of Moses and the song of Jesus, the Lamb. Long ago, Moses sang after the Lord led His people through the sea.","Now these people sang because, with Jesus, they had made it through. “Lord, everything You do is great and wonderful,” they sang. “Your ways are fair and true.”","Is something hard for you right now? Keep going with Jesus. Tonight, hum a happy song, just like the singers on the sea of glass."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big faith, brave heart!” The overcomers stood on a sea of glass with harps and sang the song of Moses and the Lamb. Beau wants to be there, singing. In Beau’s world today: Beau finishes the hardest hike of his life, and at the top his family sings a hymn. “This is the sea of glass,” Dad says. Beau sings loudest, because he made it. Just like the people John saw, Beau learned that those who overcome by Jesus will stand and sing the song of the Lamb.","feel":"Walk through the Red Sea like Moses, walls of water high! Stand on a sea of glass, still and shining. Strum a harp. Sing, “Great and marvellous are Thy works!” Can you show me your victory song?","stones":[["SPOT","John saw the overcomers on a sea of glass with harps, singing the song of Moses and the Lamb. Beau felt triumphant."],["NAME","That I-made-it feeling is called triumphant. Your mind might think, “Great and marvellous!” Your body might feel tired legs, a singing chest."],["FIND","When did you finish something hard and want to sing?"],["FORK","We can quit before the end, or overcome by Jesus and sing on the sea of glass. Overcome."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and picture the shining sea and a harp. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, just and true are Your ways.”"]],"remember":"Great and marvellous are Thy works, Lord Heavenly Father Almighty!","ask":["“What were the people standing on?”","“Whose two songs did they sing?”","“What did Beau’s family do at the top of the hike?”"],"share":["Share a “song of Moses” moment, a victory the Lord brought you through.","What are you still walking through that will one day be a song?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who, you trust, is singing the song of the Lamb now."]},"b24-w3-n2":{"story":["John heard a huge sound in heaven. It was like a big crowd, rushing water, and thunder all at once.","The voices shouted, “Alleluia!” which means “Praise the Lord!” Heavenly Father has all power, and He is King.","“Be glad!” they said. “The wedding of the Lamb has come!” Jesus is the Lamb. His bride is His people, dressed in clean, white clothes.","A wedding has a big, happy feast. In John’s picture, it means Jesus and His people together, glad forever. An angel said to write, “Happy are those invited to the Lamb’s wedding supper.”","Jesus wants you at that feast too. Your name is on the invitation. Tonight, say a soft little “Alleluia!” and thank Heavenly Father."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” An angel said, “Blessed are they which are called to the marriage supper of the Lamb.” The biggest invitation ever, and Willow loves being invited. In Willow’s world today: A wedding invitation comes with Willow’s own name on it in gold. At the feast there are candles and cake and dancing. “John said there’s a supper like this with Jesus,” she tells Grandma, “and I’m invited.” Just like John heard, Willow learned that everyone who loves Jesus is invited to the feast of the Lamb.","feel":"Shout, “Alleluia!” like a waterfall and thunder! Now open an invitation with your name in gold, ooh. Walk into a feast, sit down, and lift a pretend cup to the Lamb! Can you show me your feast seat?","stones":[["SPOT","An angel said, “Blessed are they which are called to the marriage supper of the Lamb.” Willow felt invited."],["NAME","That a-seat-with-my-name feeling is called invited. Your mind might think, “He wants me there.” Your body might feel a happy skip, a reaching hand."],["FIND","When did you get an invitation with your name on it, and feel wanted?"],["FORK","We can think the feast is for other people, or believe the invitation has our name on it. Believe it."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and picture your seat at the Lamb’s table. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for the feast to come.”"]],"remember":"Blessed are they which are called to the marriage supper of the Lamb!","ask":["“What does Alleluia mean?”","“What does a wedding mean, in the story?”","“What color was Willow’s name on her invitation?”"],"share":["Share how the hope of the marriage supper of the Lamb shapes your days.","Are you living as someone with a place at that table? What would change?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor you hope to sit beside at the feast."]},"b24-w3-n3":{"story":["John wrote this for Jesus’s friends, who were having a hard time. He wanted them to know Jesus will make everything right.","John saw heaven open wide. Out came a rider on a white horse. His name was Faithful and True, because He always keeps His word.","It was Jesus! His eyes shone like fire. He wore many crowns, not just one, because He is King over every king.","The armies of heaven rode behind Him on white horses. They wore clean, white clothes. On His robe was written His name: KING OF KINGS, AND LORD OF LORDS.","Every king has a King over him, and it is Jesus. Tonight, stand up tall and say, “I will follow Jesus, my King.”"],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big faith, brave heart!” Jesus rode out of heaven on a white horse with many crowns, Faithful and True, King of Kings. Beau is in His army. In Beau’s world today: At the parade, a man in uniform rides a white horse, and the crowd stands. Beau whispers, “Is that the king?” Dad says, “No, but imagine when the real King of Kings rides out.” Beau stands up straight, practicing. Just like John saw, Beau learned that Jesus is Faithful and True, the King of all kings, and he follows Him.","feel":"See heaven open! Ride a white horse, clip, clop. Put on many crowns, one, two, three! Put on a clean white robe and follow the King. Salute the King of Kings! Can you show me your King salute?","stones":[["SPOT","John saw Jesus ride out on a white horse, called Faithful and True, with many crowns, King of Kings. Beau felt loyal."],["NAME","That I-know-my-King feeling is called loyal. Your mind might think, “Faithful and True.” Your body might feel a straight salute, following feet."],["FIND","When did you decide to follow someone because they were faithful and true?"],["FORK","We can follow any leader who looks strong, or follow the King who is Faithful and True. Follow Him."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and picture Jesus on the white horse, Faithful and True. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, Jesus is my King.”"]],"remember":"Jesus is called Faithful and True, King of Kings and Lord of Lords!","ask":["“What was Jesus called when He rode out?”","“How many crowns were on His head?”","“What did Beau do at the parade?”"],"share":["Share what it means to you that your King is called Faithful and True.","Which earthly “king” competes for your loyalty?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who was loyal to the King of Kings above every other power."]},"b24-w3-n4":{"story":["John saw a new heaven and a new earth. The old, broken things were gone.","Then John saw a holy city coming down from heaven, called New Jerusalem. It was lovely, like a bride all dressed up for her wedding.","A big voice from heaven spoke. It said Heavenly Father would live right there with His people. He would be close, not far away.","And Heavenly Father will wipe away every tear from their eyes. There will be no more dying, no more crying, and no more pain.","Is something making you sad tonight? Tell Heavenly Father all about it. One day He will wipe every tear away, for you too."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer says, “Wow, look at that!” John saw a new heaven and earth, and every tear wiped away, no more pain. An ending that good! In Wren’s world today: Wren’s old dog Pepper can’t run anymore. Wren cries into his fur. Grandma says, “John saw a day with no more pain. Not for you, not for Pepper.” “No more owies?” “None.” Wren whispers it to Pepper. Just like John saw, Wren learned that one day Heavenly Father will live with us and there will be no more tears or pain.","feel":"See the old world fade and a new one appear, sparkly! Watch a city float down like a bride. Feel every tear wiped away, every owie gone. Stretch: no more pain! Can you show me your no-more-tears stretch?","stones":[["SPOT","John saw a new heaven and earth, and heard that Heavenly Father will dwell with us, no more tears or pain. Wren felt hopeful."],["NAME","That the-ending-is-good feeling is called hopeful. Your mind might think, “No more crying there.” Your body might feel a lifted face, an easy breath."],["FIND","What owie or sad thing are you most looking forward to being gone forever?"],["FORK","We can think the sad things will never end, or trust the ending John saw. Trust the ending."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and picture the day with no more tears. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for the new heaven and earth.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father shall wipe away all tears, and there shall be no more pain!","ask":["“What did John see coming down from heaven?”","“Name two things that will be no more.”","“What did Wren whisper to Pepper?”"],"share":["Share which “former thing” you most long to see passed away.","How does knowing the ending change how you carry today’s tears?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor you look forward to seeing with no more tears."]},"b24-w3-n5":{"story":["An angel took John up a great, high mountain. From there, John saw the holy city coming down from heaven.","It shone like a bright jewel, clear as crystal. It had a high wall with twelve gates, and an angel at every gate.","The wall was made of a shining jewel-stone called jasper. The city was pure gold, so clear it looked like glass.","Each gate was one giant pearl. The street was pure gold too. The city did not need the sun or the moon. Heavenly Father’s glory made it bright, and Jesus, the Lamb, is its light.","Heaven is bright and beautiful, and Jesus is there. Tonight, look at a light and think of Him."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see!” An angel showed John a city of clear gold with gates of pearl, and no sun, because the Lamb is the light. In Scout’s world today: Scout finds a chip of gold-colored rock in the creek and holds it up to the sun. “Imagine a whole city made of this,” Grandpa says. Scout squints. “And Jesus is the light instead of the sun.” He keeps that rock. Just like John saw, Scout learned that a city of gold and pearl is waiting, lit by Jesus Himself.","feel":"Climb a great high mountain, step, step. Look down at a city of clear gold, so bright! Walk through a gate of one giant pearl. Look up: no sun, just Jesus! Can you show me your city gaze?","stones":[["SPOT","An angel showed John the holy city of clear gold with pearl gates, lit by the Lamb instead of the sun. Scout felt dazzled."],["NAME","That I-can’t-stop-looking feeling is called dazzled. Your mind might think, “Gold like glass!” Your body might feel wide-open eyes and a dropped jaw."],["FIND","What is the most beautiful place you have ever seen, and what made it shine?"],["FORK","We can imagine heaven as dull and far away, or look at what John saw. Look at it."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and picture the golden city with Jesus as its light. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for the city.”"]],"remember":"The city was pure gold, like clear glass, and the Lamb is the light thereof!","ask":["“What were the gates made of?”","“Why does the city not need the sun?”","“What did Scout find in the creek?”"],"share":["Share which detail of the holy city stirs your hope the most.","How does “the Lamb is the light thereof” change how you see your dark days?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who lived with the holy city in view."]},"b24-w3-n6":{"story":["The angel showed John a river inside the holy city. Its water was clean and clear, like crystal. It was the river of the water of life.","The river flowed from the throne of Heavenly Father and of Jesus, the Lamb.","On both sides of the river grew the tree of life. It had twelve kinds of fruit, with new fruit every month.","Even its leaves were for healing, to make people well. Nothing will be broken there anymore. Heavenly Father’s people will see His face. There will be no night, because Heavenly Father gives them light.","When you drink water tonight, think of the river of life. Heavenly Father can make every broken thing well again."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer says, “Wow, look at that!” A crystal river from the throne, a tree with twelve fruits and healing leaves, and no night. Which fruit first? In Wren’s world today: Wren’s family picnics by a mountain stream, and she drinks with cupped hands. “John saw a river like this, from Heavenly Father’s throne, and a tree with twelve fruits.” Her brother asks, “Which fruit?” “All of them.” Just like John saw, Wren learned that Heavenly Father’s city has a river of life, a tree of life, and no more night.","feel":"Scoop water from the river of life and drink, ahh! Pick fruit from the tree of life, twelve kinds! Press a healing leaf on an owie, all better. Look up: no night! Can you show me your river-and-tree?","stones":[["SPOT","John saw a river of life from the throne, a tree of life with healing leaves, and no more night. Wren felt refreshed."],["NAME","That cool-water feeling is called refreshed. Your mind might think, “All is well.” Your body might feel a cool drink inside, no more tired."],["FIND","When did a cool drink or a shady tree make you feel brand new?"],["FORK","We can think nothing can heal what is broken, or trust the river and the tree. Trust them."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and picture yourself under the tree of life. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for the tree of life.”"]],"remember":"A river of life, a tree of life, and no night there, for the Lord gives them light!","ask":["“Where did the river come from?”","“How many kinds of fruit did the tree have?”","“Which fruit did Wren want to try?”"],"share":["Share what “they shall see His face” means to you.","What in you or your family needs the leaves of the tree for healing?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who longed to see His face."]},"b24-w3-n7":{"story":["John heard a promise for all who keep going, even when it is hard. They will inherit all things, like children sharing all their Father has.","Near the end of the Bible, Jesus said, “I am coming soon. I am bringing a reward for what each one has done.”","Jesus said, “I am Alpha and Omega, the beginning and the end.” Alpha is the first letter of John’s alphabet. Omega is the last. Jesus is the A and the Z, the start and the finish.","Jesus said one more time, “Surely I come quickly.” John answered with a happy yes: “Amen. Even so, come, Lord Jesus.”","Jesus is coming again. Tonight, put your shoes by the door and whisper, “Even so, come, Lord Jesus.”"],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big faith, brave heart!” Jesus said, “Surely I come quickly,” and John said, “Even so, come, Lord Jesus.” Beau says it every night, and means it. In Beau’s world today: Dad has been away for months, and tonight the phone says he’s coming home tomorrow. “Even so, come,” he whispers, thinking of Dad and of Jesus at once. He puts his shoes by the door, ready for both. Just like John, Beau learned that we can wait for Jesus to come with a brave, ready heart: “Even so, come.”","feel":"Hear Jesus say, “I come quickly!” Make an A and a Z with your fingers. Put your shoes by the door, ready. Fold your hands: “Even so, come, Lord Jesus!” Can you show me your even-so-come?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus said, “Surely I come quickly,” and John answered, “Even so, come, Lord Jesus.” Beau felt expectant."],["NAME","That He’s-coming feeling is called expectant. Your mind might think, “Even so, come.” Your body might feel ready feet, a heart that can’t sit still."],["FIND","When were you waiting for someone wonderful to come, and could hardly sleep?"],["FORK","We can dread the end, or say with John, “Even so, come, Lord Jesus.” Say it with John."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your excited heart rest, ready. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, even so, come, Lord Jesus.”"]],"remember":"He that overcometh shall inherit all things, and even so, come, Lord Jesus!","ask":["“What does the one who overcomes inherit?”","“What do Alpha and Omega mean?”","“What did John say when Jesus said, “I come quickly”?”"],"share":["Share whether you can pray “Even so, come” with a ready heart.","What would you want to be found doing when He comes quickly?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who lived expecting the Lord’s return."]},"b24-w4-n1":{"story":["In a little town called Nazareth lived Mary. She was going to marry a man named Joseph.","Heavenly Father sent the angel Gabriel to Mary. “The Lord is with you,” Gabriel said. Mary felt troubled and wondered what he meant.","“Fear not, Mary,” said Gabriel. “You will have a baby boy, and you will name Him JESUS. He will be called the Son of the Highest.”","Mary asked, “How can this be?” Gabriel said the Holy Ghost would come to her. Her baby would be called “the Son of God.” Then he said, “With Heavenly Father nothing is impossible.”","Mary trusted and said, “Be it unto me according to thy word.” That was her brave yes. Tonight, you can say yes to Heavenly Father too."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” The angel told Mary something huge, and she said, “Be it unto me according to thy word.” A willing yes like that starts every good thing. In Willow’s world today: Mom asks Willow to hold the new baby cousin all through Christmas dinner so his mom can eat. A big job for little arms. Willow takes a breath. “Be it unto me,” she whispers, and holds out her arms. Just like Mary, Willow learned that saying a willing yes to Heavenly Father is where miracles begin.","feel":"See an angel appear, gasp! Hear, “Fear not, Mary.” Tilt your head, wondering. Now fold your hands, bow your head, and say softly, “Be it unto me.” Smile a brave, willing smile. Can you show me your be-it-unto-me?","stones":[["SPOT","The angel told Mary she would have a son named Jesus, and Mary said, “Be it unto me.” Willow felt willing."],["NAME","That whatever-You-say feeling is called willing. Your mind might think, “Yes, Heavenly Father.” Your body might feel a bowed head, open arms."],["FIND","When did you say yes to something big because you trusted the one asking?"],["FORK","Mary could say, “I’m too young, too scared,” or say, “Be it unto me.” She said yes."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and fold your hands like Mary. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, be it unto me according to Your word.”"]],"remember":"Mary said, “Be it unto me according to thy word,” and Christmas began!","ask":["“What was the angel’s name?”","“What name did the angel say to give the baby?”","“What did Willow whisper before holding her baby cousin?”"],"share":["Share a “be it unto me” moment when you said yes to the Lord.","What is the Lord asking of you that needs a willing yes right now?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor whose willing yes to the Lord blessed your family."]},"b24-w4-n2":{"story":["Joseph was going to marry Mary. She would have a baby, and he did not understand. Joseph was a good, kind man. He thought about quietly going away.","While he was thinking, an angel came to him in a dream. “Joseph, do not be afraid to take Mary as your wife,” the angel said.","“Her baby is from the Holy Ghost. You will name Him JESUS, for He will save His people from their sins.”","The prophet Isaiah had said He would be called Emmanuel, “God with us.” Joseph woke up and did what the angel said. He stayed, and he named the baby Jesus.","When something feels confusing, you can trust Heavenly Father too. Tonight, hug your family and stay close, like Joseph."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big faith, brave heart!” Joseph did not understand, but an angel said, “Fear not,” and Joseph trusted and stayed. Staying when you don’t understand is brave. In Beau’s world today: Beau’s family takes in a foster baby, and it is loud and confusing. Dad says, “Joseph didn’t understand either. He just stayed and helped.” Beau rocks the crying baby until she sleeps. “I’m staying,” he whispers. “Like Joseph.” Just like Joseph, Beau learned that trusting Heavenly Father means staying and helping, even when you don’t understand.","feel":"Scratch your head, confused. Now lie down and dream, and see an angel say, “Fear not!” Wake up, stand tall, and say, “I’ll stay.” Cradle a baby and whisper, “Jesus.” Can you show me your Joseph stay?","stones":[["SPOT","An angel told Joseph, “Fear not,” and Joseph trusted, stayed, and named the baby Jesus. Beau felt trusting."],["NAME","That I’ll-stay-anyway feeling is called trusting. Your mind might think, “Fear not.” Your body might feel planted feet and gentle, helping hands."],["FIND","When did you stay and help with something you did not understand?"],["FORK","Joseph could quietly step away, or trust the angel and stay. He stayed."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and picture Joseph waking and choosing to stay. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, I will trust You like Joseph.”"]],"remember":"Joseph did as the angel said, and called His name Jesus!","ask":["“How did the angel speak to Joseph?”","“What does Emmanuel mean?”","“What did Beau whisper while rocking the baby?”"],"share":["Share a time you trusted the Lord and stayed when you did not understand.","Where might the Lord be saying “fear not” to you about something confusing?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who stayed faithful through something they did not understand."]},"b24-w4-n3":{"story":["Long ago, a ruler named Caesar Augustus made a rule. Everyone had to go to their family’s own town to be counted.","Joseph’s family came from Bethlehem, King David’s town. So Joseph and Mary went there, a long way from Nazareth. Mary’s baby was almost ready to be born.","While they were in Bethlehem, the time came. Mary’s first baby boy was born.","Mary wrapped Him snug in soft cloths. She laid Him in a manger, a box that animals eat from. There was no room for them in the inn, where travelers stayed.","Jesus, the King of heaven, lay in a feeding box. He came small and plain, for you. Tonight, kneel by your bed and thank Heavenly Father for baby Jesus."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer says, “Wow, look at that!” The King of Kings was born in a stable and laid in a feeding box. A King who started so small! In Wren’s world today: Wren’s family sets up the nativity, and Wren puts the baby in the manger last. She looks at the tiny figure a long time. “He was a real baby, in a real feeding box.” Grandma nods. “The King of Kings.” Just like that night in Bethlehem, Wren learned that the King of heaven came small and low, into a manger, for us.","feel":"Walk a bumpy road to Bethlehem, step, step, so tired. Knock on the inn, no room! Find a stable, wrap a baby snug, and lay Him gently in a manger. Can you show me your gentle manger lay?","stones":[["SPOT","Mary wrapped her son in swaddling clothes and laid Him in a manger, because there was no room in the inn. Wren felt awed."],["NAME","That a-King-came-this-small feeling is called awed. Your mind might think, “In a feeding box.” Your body might feel still hands, a long quiet look."],["FIND","When did something small and humble turn out to be the most important thing?"],["FORK","We can look past the manger as too plain, or kneel and see the King in it. Kneel and see."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and picture the tiny baby in the straw. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for sending Jesus.”"]],"remember":"She laid Him in a manger, because there was no room in the inn!","ask":["“Why did Joseph and Mary travel to Bethlehem?”","“What is a manger?”","“Which nativity piece did Wren put in last?”"],"share":["Share what the manger teaches you about how the Lord comes to us.","Where in your life is there “no room,” and how could you make room for Him?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor and the way they celebrated the birth of Jesus."]},"b24-w4-n4":{"story":["That same night, shepherds were out in the fields near Bethlehem. They stayed out all night, keeping their sheep safe.","Suddenly an angel came, and the Lord’s bright glory shone all around them. The dark field filled with light, and the shepherds were very scared.","The angel said, “Fear not! I bring you good tidings, happy news, of great joy. It is for all people.”","“Today in David’s town, a Savior is born, Christ the Lord.” That baby was Jesus, come to save us. Then the sky filled with angels praising Heavenly Father. They said, “On earth peace, good will toward men.”","That happy news is for you too. Tonight, look out at the dark sky. Say, “Thank you, Heavenly Father, for baby Jesus!”"],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see!” The angel told the shepherds, “I bring you good tidings of great joy,” and the whole sky filled with angels. Scout would have looked up fast. In Scout’s world today: On Christmas Eve, Scout’s family lies on blankets on a dark hill. Dad reads the shepherd story. Scout stares at the sky, half expecting it to fill up. “Good tidings of great joy. To all people. That’s me.” Just like the shepherds, Scout learned that the good news of Jesus’s birth is great joy for all people.","feel":"Sit in a dark field, watching sheep, shh. Suddenly, light! Cover your eyes! Hear, “Fear not!” Look up as the sky fills with angels and sing, “Glory in the highest!” Can you show me your sky-full look?","stones":[["SPOT","An angel told the shepherds, “I bring you good tidings of great joy,” and the sky filled with angels. Scout felt overjoyed."],["NAME","That best-news-ever feeling is called overjoyed. Your mind might think, “That’s me!” Your body might feel a lifted face and a bursting chest."],["FIND","When did you hear news so good you wanted to tell everyone?"],["FORK","The shepherds could stay sore afraid, or hear “fear not” and rejoice. They rejoiced."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and picture the sky full of angels singing peace. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, glory to You in the highest.”"]],"remember":"Fear not, for behold, I bring you good tidings of great joy, which shall be to all people!","ask":["“Who was out in the fields that night?”","“What did the angel say he brought?”","“Where did Scout’s family go on Christmas Eve?”"],"share":["Share when the good tidings of great joy became personally yours.","To whom could you carry good tidings this Christmas?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor and their favorite part of the Christmas story."]},"b24-w4-n5":{"story":["When the angels went back into heaven, the shepherds said, “Let’s go to Bethlehem! Let’s see what the Lord told us about.”","They hurried and found Mary, Joseph, and baby Jesus lying in a manger. It was just as the angel said.","Then the shepherds told everyone what the angel had said about this child. Everyone who heard it wondered.","But Mary kept all these things and pondered them in her heart. To ponder is to think slowly, like turning a treasure over in your hands. The shepherds went back to their sheep, praising Heavenly Father.","Christmas can be loud and fast. Tonight, be still like Mary. Hold a quiet thought about baby Jesus in your heart."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer says, “Wow, look at that!” The shepherds ran to see the baby, and Mary kept all these things and pondered them in her heart. Wren is a ponderer too. In Wren’s world today: After the Christmas program, everyone is loud and happy, but Wren sits by the tree with the baby Jesus figure in her lap, just thinking. Grandma sits beside her. “Pondering?” Wren nods. “Like Mary.” They sit quietly a long time. Just like Mary, Wren learned to keep the story of Jesus and ponder it in her heart.","feel":"Run with haste to Bethlehem, run, run! Peek into the stable and see the baby. Now be Mary: hand on your heart, hold everything there, quiet and still, pondering. Can you show me your pondering heart?","stones":[["SPOT","The shepherds hurried and told everyone, but Mary kept all these things and pondered them in her heart. Wren felt pondering."],["NAME","That holding-it-quietly feeling is called pondering. Your mind might think, “I’ll keep this.” Your body might feel a hand on your heart, a slow gaze."],["FIND","When did something wonderful happen that you wanted to hold quietly and think about?"],["FORK","We can let Christmas go by loud and fast, or keep it and ponder it in our hearts. Keep it."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and hold the Christmas story in your heart like Mary. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, I will keep these things.”"]],"remember":"Mary kept all these things, and pondered them in her heart!","ask":["“Where did the shepherds hurry to?”","“What did Mary do with all these things?”","“What was in Wren’s lap by the tree?”"],"share":["Share what you keep and ponder in your heart about the Savior’s birth.","Are you more a shepherd who runs and tells, or a Mary who ponders?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who pondered the things of Heavenly Father in their heart."]},"b24-w4-n6":{"story":["When Jesus was born, wise men came from far away in the east. They had seen His star in the sky.","They asked in Jerusalem, “Where is the one born King of the Jews? We saw His star, and we came to worship Him.”","On they went, and the star led the way. It stopped right over the place where young Jesus was. The wise men were so glad!","They went into the house and saw the young child with Mary, His mother. They knelt and worshipped Him. They opened their treasures: gold, and two sweet-smelling gifts, frankincense and myrrh.","The wise men looked for Jesus, and they found Him. Look for Jesus, and you will find Him too. Tonight, tell Jesus you love Him."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see!” The wise men saw a star, followed it across the world, and found Jesus. Scout’s favorites, because they looked, and went. In Scout’s world today: Scout’s family does a Christmas star hunt: a paper star hidden each night with a clue. Scout finds the last star taped above the manger on Christmas morning. “They sought Him, and they found Him,” he says, and kneels like the wise men. Just like the wise men, Scout learned that those who seek Jesus with their whole heart will find Him.","feel":"Spot a new star, point! Pack your treasures and travel far, step, step. See the star stop over a house. Go in, kneel, and give your gifts: gold, frankincense, myrrh! Can you show me your wise-man kneel?","stones":[["SPOT","The wise men followed His star until it stood over the child, and worshipped Him with gifts. Scout felt seeking."],["NAME","That I’ll-keep-looking feeling is called seeking. Your mind might think, “Follow the star.” Your body might feel walking feet and eyes on the sky."],["FIND","When did you search a long time for something and finally find it?"],["FORK","The wise men could stay home and wonder about the star, or follow it. They followed."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and picture the star stopping right over Jesus. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, lead me to Jesus like the star.”"]],"remember":"The wise men followed the star, and found Him, and worshipped Him!","ask":["“What did the wise men see in the sky?”","“What three gifts did they bring?”","“Where was the last star in Scout’s hunt?”"],"share":["Share your own journey of seeking the Savior and finding Him.","What “star” is leading you closer to Him this season?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who sought the Lord across a long distance or a long time."]},"b24-w4-n7":{"story":["When Jesus was a tiny baby, Mary and Joseph took Him to Jerusalem. They brought Him to the temple, Heavenly Father’s holy house.","An old man named Simeon was good and faithful. The Holy Ghost had promised he would see the Savior before he died. He had waited a long time.","That day the Spirit led Simeon into the temple. He took baby Jesus in his arms and praised Heavenly Father.","Simeon said, “Now I can go in peace, for I have seen Your Savior.” Then old Anna, who served at the temple, gave thanks too. She told others about Jesus.","Simeon and Anna waited and trusted, and Heavenly Father kept His promise. Tonight, tell Heavenly Father what you are waiting for, and trust Him."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” Old Simeon held baby Jesus and said he could go in peace. Willow thinks of her grandparents holding a baby. In Willow’s world today: On Christmas Day, Willow’s great-grandpa, ninety-two, holds the new baby cousin. “I waited my whole life to see this,” he says, eyes wet. Willow tells him about Simeon. He smiles. “Now lettest thou thy servant depart in peace.” Just like Simeon and Anna, Willow learned that those who wait faithfully will see the Savior and be filled.","feel":"Be very old, bent over, waiting. See a baby and straighten up! Take Him in your arms, gently. Lift your face: “Mine eyes have seen!” Then be Anna and tell everyone! Can you show me your Simeon arms?","stones":[["SPOT","Old Simeon took baby Jesus in his arms and said his eyes had seen the salvation. Willow felt fulfilled."],["NAME","That I-waited-and-saw feeling is called fulfilled. Your mind might think, “Mine eyes have seen.” Your body might feel full arms, a peaceful sigh."],["FIND","When did you wait a long time for something wonderful, and it finally came?"],["FORK","Simeon could give up waiting, or trust the promise until the day came. He trusted."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and picture old Simeon holding the baby King. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that I have seen Jesus.”"]],"remember":"Mine eyes have seen Thy salvation, a light to lighten the Gentiles!","ask":["“What had the Holy Ghost promised Simeon?”","“What did Anna do when she saw Jesus?”","“What did Great-Grandpa say after hearing about Simeon?”"],"share":["Share how your own eyes have “seen His salvation” this year.","What are you still faithfully waiting for, like Simeon and Anna?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who, like Simeon, lived to see the Lord’s promises to your family fulfilled."]},"b14-w1-n1":{"story":["There was a wedding party in a town called Cana. Jesus, His friends, and His mother, Mary, were there.","In the middle of the party, the wine ran out. Mary told Jesus, “They have no wine.”","Then Mary said to the helpers, “Whatever He tells you, do it.” She trusted Him.","Six big stone jars stood nearby. Jesus said, “Fill the jars with water.” Water did not seem like it would help, but the helpers obeyed. They filled the jars to the top. The man in charge tasted it. It was the best wine of all!","This was Jesus’s first miracle, and His friends believed in Him. Tonight, tell Jesus, “I trust You,” just like Mary did."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer pauses and ponders. Mary did not know how Jesus would help, but she trusted Him and said, “Do whatever He tells you.” Wren loves that kind of quiet trust. In Wren’s world today: Wren’s dad says, “Hold my hand and close your eyes. I have a surprise.” Wren does not know where they are going, but she holds on tight. When she opens her eyes, there is a new swing in the backyard. “I knew you had something good,” she says. Just like Mary, Wren learned that we can trust Jesus even before we see what He will do.","feel":"Pretend to pour water from a big, heavy jar, slow and careful. Now take a sip and make a surprised, happy face. Can you show me your best-wine-ever face?","stones":[["SPOT","The wine ran out at the wedding. Mary trusted Jesus and told the helpers to do whatever He said."],["NAME","That I-know-He-can, I’ll-do-what-He-says feeling is called trust. Your mind might think, “He will take care of it.” Your body might feel calm and steady."],["FIND","When did you do what someone asked before you knew why, because you trusted them?"],["FORK","The helpers could say, “Water won’t help,” or fill the jars like Jesus said. They filled them to the top."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your hands rest open. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me trust Jesus.”"]],"remember":"When we do whatever Jesus tells us, He can do wonderful things!","ask":["“What ran out at the wedding in Cana?”","“What did Jesus tell the helpers to fill the jars with?”","“What was the surprise in Wren’s backyard?”"],"share":["Share a time you trusted Jesus before you could see how He would help.","What has Jesus asked you to do that seemed small, but turned out to matter?","Did a grandparent or ancestor trust Heavenly Father through something hard? Tell their story."]},"b14-w1-n2":{"story":["It was Passover, a special holiday for Heavenly Father’s people. Jesus went to Jerusalem, to the temple. It was Heavenly Father’s holy house, a place to pray.","But in the temple yard, people were not praying. It was a noisy market! Men were selling cows, sheep, and doves. Other men sat at money tables.","Jesus did not walk past. This house was for praying, not selling.","So Jesus drove the animals out and tipped over the money tables. He said, “Take these things away! Do not make My Father’s house a market.” He was keeping His Father’s house holy.","Jesus loved His Father’s house with His whole heart. Church is Heavenly Father’s house too. Tonight, say, “I will help keep church quiet and kind.”"],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave has a big heart and a brave start. Jesus stood up for His Father’s house when nobody else would. Beau knows that protecting what is holy takes courage. In Beau’s world today: Some big kids are running and shouting in the chapel after church. Beau walks over and says, “This is Heavenly Father’s house. Let’s go run outside.” The kids look surprised, then they follow him out to the grass. Just like Jesus, Beau learned that we can bravely protect the places that belong to Heavenly Father.","feel":"Stand your arms up tall like strong walls protecting something special. Hold them steady. Now fold them softly, like reverent arms at church. Can you show me your reverent arms?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus found a noisy market in Heavenly Father’s house. He felt protective, and He cleared it out."],["NAME","That I-will-keep-this-safe feeling is called protective. Your mind might think, “Not here, this is special.” Your body might feel strong and tall."],["FIND","When did you protect something special, like a little sister or a favorite toy?"],["FORK","Jesus could walk past the market, or protect His Father’s house. Jesus protected it."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and fold your arms gently. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, I love Your house.”"]],"remember":"The temple and church are Heavenly Father’s house, and we keep them holy!","ask":["“What did Jesus find in the temple courtyard instead of people praying?”","“What did Jesus say the temple was supposed to be?”","“What did Beau say to the big kids running in the chapel?”"],"share":["Share how you feel in the temple or at church, and why it is holy to you.","When have you had to stand up to protect something sacred?","Did a grandparent or ancestor help build or care for a church or temple? Tell about it."]},"b14-w1-n3":{"story":["Nicodemus was a leader who knew the scriptures well. He had questions about Jesus.","One night, Nicodemus went to find Jesus. He said, “Teacher, we know Heavenly Father sent You. No one could do Your miracles alone.”","Jesus said a person must be born again to see Heavenly Father’s kingdom. Nicodemus was puzzled. “How can a grown man be born again?” he asked.","Jesus said we must be born of water and of the Spirit. That means being baptized and getting the Holy Ghost, like a brand-new life. Jesus said the Spirit is like the wind. You cannot see it, but you can hear it blow.","You can feel the Holy Ghost, warm and quiet, like a gentle wind. Tonight, listen for that quiet feeling."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker asks, “What could it be?” Nicodemus had a puzzling question, and he went looking for the answer, even in the dark. Scout loves a question that needs seeking. In Scout’s world today: Scout feels the wind push his kite up high. “Where does the wind come from?” he asks. Mom says, “You can’t see it, but you can feel it and see what it does.” Scout holds the string, feeling the tug, and says, “Like the Holy Ghost!” Just like Nicodemus, Scout learned that when we are puzzled, we can bring our questions to Jesus.","feel":"Blow a soft breath on your hand, like the wind. You cannot see it, but you can feel it! Now blow once more, slow and gentle. Can you show me your wind breath?","stones":[["SPOT","Nicodemus heard Jesus say “born again” and did not understand. He felt puzzled, and he kept asking."],["NAME","That hmm, I-don’t-get-it feeling is called puzzled. Your mind might think, “How can that be?” Your body might feel a scrunched-up forehead and a tilted head."],["FIND","When did you hear something you did not understand, and you wanted to ask about it?"],["FORK","Nicodemus could stay home with his questions, or go find Jesus. He went, even at night."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your forehead go smooth. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me understand.”"]],"remember":"When we are puzzled, we can bring our questions to Jesus!","ask":["“When did Nicodemus go to see Jesus?”","“What did Jesus say the Spirit is like?”","“What did Scout feel pushing his kite up high?”"],"share":["Share a question you brought to Heavenly Father and how He helped you understand.","What did being baptized and receiving the Holy Ghost mean to you?","Did a grandparent or ancestor search for truth and find it? Tell their story."]},"b14-w1-n4":{"story":["Jesus taught Nicodemus something very important. He said, “Heavenly Father so loved the world, that He gave His only Son.”","Everyone who believes in Jesus will not be lost. They will live with Heavenly Father forever. Jesus came to save people.","Jesus said He is like a light that came into a dark world. People who do good come to the light.","Meanwhile, John the Baptist was still baptizing people. His friends said, “Now everyone is going to Jesus!” John was not upset. He said, “He must grow greater, and I must grow smaller.”","John was happy to step back so everyone could see Jesus. Heavenly Father loves you so much that He gave Jesus for you. Tonight, say, “Thank You, Heavenly Father.”"],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” Tonight’s story is the warmest truth there is: Heavenly Father loved the world so much that He gave His Son. In Willow’s world today: Willow is sick in bed, and Mom stays home from work to take care of her. Mom brings soup and reads three stories. Willow whispers, “You gave up your whole day for me.” Mom says, “That’s what love does.” Just like Jesus taught, Willow learned that real love gives, and Heavenly Father gave His very best for us.","feel":"Open your arms as wide as they can go, like you are hugging the whole world. Now wrap them around yourself. Can you show me a hug that big?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus said Heavenly Father loved the world so much that He gave His Son. The people who believed felt loved."],["NAME","That warm, wrapped-up, He-gave-everything-for-me feeling is called loved. Your mind might think, “He really wants me.” Your body might feel warm all over."],["FIND","When did someone give up something for you, and you felt how much they loved you?"],["FORK","John could be upset that everyone went to Jesus, or be glad and step back. John was glad."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and put your hand on your heart. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for giving Jesus for me.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father loved the world so much that He gave His Son, for you!","ask":["“Who did Heavenly Father give because He loved the world so much?”","“What did John say about Jesus becoming greater?”","“What did Willow’s mom do when Willow was sick?”"],"share":["Share what John 3:16 means to you and how you have felt Heavenly Father’s love.","When have you been glad to step back so someone else could shine?","How did a grandparent or ancestor show your family what giving love looks like?"]},"b14-w1-n5":{"story":["Jesus walked through a land called Samaria. Jews and Samaritans did not get along.","At noon, Jesus sat by a deep well, tired. A Samaritan woman came to get water. Jesus said, “Will you give Me a drink?”","The woman was surprised. “You are a Jew. Why ask me?” Jesus said, “If you knew Me, you would ask Me for living water.”","“You have no bucket!” she said. Jesus said well water leaves you thirsty again. His living water is like a spring inside that never runs dry. Then He told her about her life. She said, “The Christ will come.” Jesus said, “I am He.”","Jesus knew all about her and was still kind. He knows you, too. Tonight, tell Him about your day."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer pauses and ponders. Jesus knew all about the woman at the well, and instead of walking past, He sat and talked with her. Wren thinks being truly known is a wonder. In Wren’s world today: Wren is quiet at dinner, pushing her peas around. Grandpa says, “You’re thinking about your lost kitty, aren’t you?” Wren’s eyes fill up. “How did you know?” Grandpa says, “I know you.” Wren leans on his arm and feels understood. Just like the woman at the well, Wren learned that Jesus knows all about us and still sits down to talk with us.","feel":"Pretend to pull a heavy bucket up from a deep well, hand over hand. Now take a long, cool drink. Can you show me your thirsty-then-happy face?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus talked with the woman at the well and knew all about her. She felt understood, and she believed."],["NAME","That somebody-really-knows-me feeling is called understood. Your mind might think, “He sees the real me.” Your body might feel your shoulders relax and your eyes get soft."],["FIND","When did someone know just how you felt without you saying a word?"],["FORK","The woman could walk away from the stranger, or keep talking with Jesus. She stayed and listened."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly, like a cool drink of water. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that Jesus knows me.”"]],"remember":"Jesus knows all about you, and He still wants to sit and talk with you!","ask":["“Where was Jesus sitting when the woman came to get water?”","“What did Jesus say He could give her instead of well water?”","“What did Grandpa know Wren was thinking about?”"],"share":["Share a time you felt that Jesus truly knew you and loved you anyway.","What does the living water of Jesus mean in your daily life?","Did a grandparent or ancestor reach out to someone others ignored? Tell about it."]},"b14-w1-n6":{"story":["The woman at the well was so excited! She left her water jar right there and ran back to town.","She called, “Come, see a Man who told me all I ever did! Is this the Christ?” The people hurried out to see Jesus.","Jesus’s friends said, “Teacher, eat.” Jesus said doing Heavenly Father’s work was like food to Him.","Jesus pointed to the fields. “Look! They are ready to harvest.” Many people were ready to hear about Him, like ripe grain. Jesus stayed in the town two days. Many believed. They said, “We heard Him ourselves. He is the Savior of the world.”","The woman could not keep good news to herself. Tonight, think of someone you can tell about Jesus."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see!” The woman ran to town shouting, “Come, see!” and the whole town came looking. That is Scout’s favorite kind of excitement. In Scout’s world today: Scout finds a bird’s nest with three blue eggs in the hedge. He runs inside yelling, “Everybody, come see!” His whole family tiptoes out to look. His little sister whispers, “Wow.” Scout grins. Sharing it was even better than finding it. Just like the woman at the well, Scout learned that when we find Jesus, the best thing is to say, “Come, see!”","feel":"Pretend to set down a heavy jar, then run in place as fast as you can under the covers. Stop, cup your hands around your mouth, and call, “Come, see!” Can you show me your come-see call?","stones":[["SPOT","The woman found out Jesus was the Christ. She felt excited and ran to tell the whole town."],["NAME","That bursting, I-can’t-keep-it-in feeling is called excited. Your mind might think, “Everyone has to see this!” Your body might feel your legs want to run and your voice get loud."],["FIND","When did you find something so good that you ran to show somebody?"],["FORK","The woman could keep quiet about Jesus, or run and tell everyone. She ran to town."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your legs rest. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me tell others about Jesus.”"]],"remember":"When we find Jesus, we say, “Come, see!” so others can find Him, too!","ask":["“What did the woman leave behind when she ran to town?”","“How many days did Jesus stay in the town?”","“What did Scout find in the hedge?”"],"share":["Share a time you could not wait to tell someone about Jesus or the gospel.","Who in your town or family has not yet come to see Jesus for themselves?","Did a grandparent or ancestor bring neighbors or friends to the gospel? Tell what they did."]},"b14-w1-n7":{"story":["Jesus came back to Cana, where He had turned water into wine. A man who worked for the king had a very sick son.","The father went to find Jesus. He begged, “Please come down to my house and heal my child.”","Jesus did not go with him. Instead, Jesus said, “Go your way. Your son lives.”","The father believed Jesus’s words, and he started home. On the way, his helpers met him. “Your son is living!” The fever had left at the very hour Jesus spoke. So the father and his whole house believed.","The father believed before he could see his son. That is faith. Tonight, you can trust Jesus, too, even before you see."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big heart, brave start!” The father walked twenty miles for his son, then turned around and went home with nothing but Jesus’s word. That is brave faith. In Beau’s world today: Beau’s hamster is sick. Beau kneels by his bed and prays with his whole heart. Then he wipes his eyes and says, “Heavenly Father heard me. Whatever happens, He’s taking care of it.” He feels a little stronger inside. Just like the nobleman, Beau learned that faith means trusting Jesus’s words even before we see what happens.","feel":"March your legs under the covers like a long, long walk. Now stop, turn around, and walk home with a calm, sure smile. Can you show me your faith walk?","stones":[["SPOT","The father begged Jesus to heal his son. Jesus said, “Your son lives.” The father believed and felt faith."],["NAME","That I-take-Him-at-His-word feeling is called faith. Your mind might think, “If Jesus said it, it’s true.” Your body might feel steady feet and a calm heart."],["FIND","When did you believe something good would happen because someone you trust promised it?"],["FORK","The father could keep begging Jesus to come, or believe His word and go home. He believed and went."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your feet rest. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help my faith grow.”"]],"remember":"Faith is believing Jesus’s words before we see them come true!","ask":["“What did the nobleman ask Jesus to do?”","“What did Jesus say instead of going to the man’s house?”","“What did Beau do when his hamster was sick?”"],"share":["Share a time you acted on Jesus’s word before you could see the outcome.","When has Heavenly Father answered a prayer for someone you love?","Did a grandparent or ancestor have great faith for a sick child or family member? Tell their story."]},"b14-w2-n1":{"story":["Jesus went up a mountain and prayed to Heavenly Father all night long.","In the morning, Jesus chose twelve of His followers. He named them apostles. An apostle is a helper Jesus sends out to teach about Him.","He chose Peter, Andrew, James, John, Matthew, and more, twelve in all.","They were not kings or rich men. Some caught fish for a living. Matthew had collected taxes. But they had willing hearts, and Jesus taught them. Jesus still calls apostles to lead His church today. He calls people like you to help, too.","Jesus prayed all night before He chose. Tonight, you can pray about a choice you need to make."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker loves to find out what he is good at. Jesus looked at ordinary fishermen and saw apostles. Scout thinks being chosen for a job is one of the best feelings. In Scout’s world today: Scout’s teacher needs a helper to feed the class fish every morning. She looks around the room and says, “Scout, I choose you.” Scout sits up tall. Every day after that, he is the first one in the door with the fish food. Just like the twelve, Scout learned that Jesus chooses ordinary people and helps them do great things.","feel":"Pretend to climb up a mountain, step by step, with your arms. At the top, fold your arms to pray. Now point at twelve friends, one, two, three... Can you show me your choosing finger?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus prayed all night, then chose twelve ordinary men to be His apostles. They felt chosen."],["NAME","That He-picked-me, I-have-a-job feeling is called chosen. Your mind might think, “He believes in me.” Your body might feel tall and ready."],["FIND","When did someone choose you to help, and you sat up tall?"],["FORK","Jesus could choose rich and important men, or ordinary ones with willing hearts. He chose willing hearts."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and sit tall in your heart. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me be ready when You need me.”"]],"remember":"Jesus chooses ordinary people, like you, to help Him do great things!","ask":["“What did Jesus do all night before He chose the twelve?”","“What is an apostle?”","“What job did Scout’s teacher choose him for?”"],"share":["Share a time you felt chosen by Heavenly Father for a calling or a task.","How does it strengthen you to know that the apostles were ordinary people?","Did a grandparent or ancestor serve in a calling that mattered to your family? Tell about it."]},"b14-w2-n2":{"story":["Big crowds followed Jesus. So He went up a mountain, sat down, and began to teach.","Jesus started with the word blessed. Blessed means happy because Heavenly Father is close to you.","Jesus said, “Blessed are they that mourn,” because sad people will be comforted. “Blessed are the meek,” the gentle ones. “Blessed are the merciful,” the kind ones.","“Blessed are the peacemakers,” Jesus said, the ones who help others stop fighting. Even when people are unkind because you follow Him, you are blessed. A great reward waits for you in heaven.","Real happiness comes from being close to Heavenly Father. Tonight, think of one way to be a peacemaker tomorrow."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm has a gentle, merciful heart, just like the people Jesus called blessed. Being meek, kind, and a peacemaker is exactly who Willow is. In Willow’s world today: Two friends are fighting over the last swing. Willow says, “I’ll count to twenty, and then you switch.” Both friends nod. They take turns, and soon they are laughing together. Willow feels a warm, happy glow. She was a peacemaker. Just like Jesus taught, Willow learned that being gentle, kind, and a peacemaker makes us blessed.","feel":"Sit up like Jesus sitting on the mountain. Now put your hands together, open them like a book, and lay them on your heart. Can you show me your blessed heart?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus sat on the mountain and taught who is blessed: the gentle, the kind, the peacemakers. The people felt blessed."],["NAME","That happy-because-He-is-with-me feeling is called blessed. Your mind might think, “I am cared for.” Your body might feel warm and peaceful."],["FIND","When did you feel blessed, like Heavenly Father was right there with you?"],["FORK","Willow’s friends could keep fighting, or let a peacemaker help. They took turns, and everyone was happy."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your face go soft. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for all my blessings.”"]],"remember":"Real happiness comes from being close to Heavenly Father, not from being rich or strong!","ask":["“Where did Jesus sit to teach the big crowd?”","“What does the word blessed mean?”","“How did Willow help her two friends stop fighting over the swing?”"],"share":["Share which of the Beatitudes means the most to you, and why.","When have you been comforted in sadness, as Jesus promised?","Was there a grandparent or ancestor known as a peacemaker in your family? Tell about them."]},"b14-w2-n3":{"story":["Jesus kept teaching on the mountain. He said, “You are the salt of the earth.” Back then, salt kept food from going bad and made it taste good. His followers make the world better, like salt.","Then Jesus said, “You are the light of the world.” A city on a hill cannot be hidden.","Jesus said no one lights a candle and hides it under a basket. They put it up high, so it lights the whole house.","Jesus said, “Let your light so shine,” so people see the good you do. Then they will thank Heavenly Father. Your kindness is like a candle.","Your light shines when you are kind, honest, and brave. Tonight, think of one way to let your light shine!"],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big heart, brave start!” It takes a brave heart to let your light shine where everyone can see it, instead of hiding it under a basket. That is Beau all over. In Beau’s world today: At the store, Beau sees a man drop his wallet. Beau picks it up, runs after him, and says, “Sir, you dropped this.” The man smiles and says, “What a good boy. Thank you!” Beau walks back to Mom, standing tall. His light was shining. Just like Jesus taught, Beau learned that when we let our light shine, people see our good works and think of Heavenly Father.","feel":"Make your hands into a little cup and hide a pretend candle inside. Now open them wide and lift them up high, so the light can shine. Can you show me your shining light?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus said we are the light of the world. When His followers heard it, they felt confident to do good."],["NAME","That I-can-do-this, let-them-see feeling is called confident. Your mind might think, “I’m not going to hide.” Your body might feel a straight back and a lifted chin."],["FIND","When did you do something good where other people could see it?"],["FORK","We can hide our light under a basket, or put it up high so it shines. Jesus says put it up high."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and lift your chin. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help my light shine for You.”"]],"remember":"Let your light shine, so people see your good works and think of Heavenly Father!","ask":["“What two things did Jesus say His followers are like?”","“Where should you put a candle so it gives light to everyone?”","“What did Beau do when the man dropped his wallet?”"],"share":["Share a time you saw someone’s good works and it pointed you to Heavenly Father.","Where is Heavenly Father asking you to let your light shine right now?","Was there a grandparent or ancestor whose goodness lit the way for your family? Tell about them."]},"b14-w2-n4":{"story":["Long ago, Heavenly Father gave the rule, “Do not kill.” Jesus taught that the rule starts in the heart. He said not to be angry without a reason. Do not call people mean names.","Jesus said something surprising. Suppose you bring a gift to Heavenly Father. Then you remember your brother is upset with you.","Jesus said, “Leave your gift. First go and make it right with your brother. Then come and give your gift.”","Long ago, people said, “If someone hurts you, hurt them back.” But Jesus said, “If someone hits your cheek, turn the other cheek.” Jesus wants us to answer meanness with kindness.","Tonight, if you feel angry, take a breath. Use kind words to make it right."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer pauses and ponders before she acts. When anger comes, Wren stops, thinks, and chooses to make it right, just like Jesus taught. In Wren’s world today: Wren’s brother knocks over her block tower. Her face gets hot, and her hands make fists. She wants to knock his down, too. Instead she takes a breath and says, “That made me mad. Can you help me build it again?” He says, “Sorry,” and they build it taller. Just like Jesus taught, Wren learned that anger is a feeling we can stop and make right, instead of hitting back.","feel":"Make tight fists and scrunch up your face like you are angry. Hold it. Now open your hands, let your face go soft, and breathe out slowly. Can you show me your angry-to-soft hands?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus taught to make things right instead of hitting back. The people felt their anger and learned what to do with it."],["NAME","That hot, tight, want-to-hit feeling is called angry. Your mind might think, “That’s not fair!” Your body might feel hot cheeks and tight fists."],["FIND","When did you feel hot and angry, like when someone broke something of yours?"],["FORK","Wren could knock her brother’s tower down, or tell him how she felt and make it right. She made it right."],["CALM","Breathe out slowly and open your hands. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me be a peacemaker.”"]],"remember":"When you feel angry, stop, make it right, and answer meanness with kindness!","ask":["“What did Jesus say to do first if your brother is upset with you?”","“What did Jesus say to do if someone hits your cheek?”","“What did Wren say when her brother knocked over her tower?”"],"share":["Share a time you made things right with someone before coming to Heavenly Father.","How do you handle anger in a way that follows Jesus?","Did a grandparent or ancestor answer meanness with kindness? Tell their story."]},"b14-w2-n5":{"story":["Jesus said, “Love your enemies,” even the people who are unkind to you. Everyone was surprised. Love them?","Jesus said, “Do good to them that hate you. Pray for them that hurt you.” Anyone can be nice to people who are nice back. Even people who do wrong do that.","Heavenly Father makes the sun rise on good people and bad people. He sends rain on everyone’s fields. He is kind even to people who never say thank you.","Jesus said, “Be merciful, just as your Father is merciful.” That means being kind even when someone was not kind to you. When we do that, we are like Heavenly Father.","Is there someone who was unkind to you? Tonight, say a little prayer for them."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” Loving people who are kind is easy. Willow’s warm heart is big enough to be kind even to someone who was not kind to her. In Willow’s world today: A girl at preschool said Willow’s drawing was ugly. It hurt. The next day the girl forgets her mittens, and her hands are cold at recess. Willow walks over and says, “You can wear one of mine.” The girl looks surprised, then says, “Thank you.” Just like Jesus taught, Willow learned that being kind to someone who was unkind is being like Heavenly Father.","feel":"Hold your hands up like the sun shining warm on everyone. Now wiggle your fingers down like rain falling on every field. Can you show me sun and rain for everyone?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus said to love your enemies and pray for them. The people who listened chose to be kind."],["NAME","That warm, I-choose-to-be-nice-anyway feeling is called kind. Your mind might think, “I will be good to them.” Your body might feel soft hands and a gentle voice."],["FIND","When were you kind to someone who had not been kind to you?"],["FORK","Willow could ignore the girl with cold hands, or share her mitten. Willow shared."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and open your hands. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me love like You do.”"]],"remember":"Love your enemies and pray for them, and you will be like Heavenly Father!","ask":["“What did Jesus say to do for people who are mean to you?”","“Who does Heavenly Father make the sun shine on?”","“What did Willow share with the girl who had cold hands?”"],"share":["Share a time you prayed for someone who hurt you and what changed.","How has Heavenly Father been merciful to you when you did not deserve it?","Did a grandparent or ancestor forgive or help someone who had wronged them? Tell about it."]},"b14-w2-n6":{"story":["Jesus said, “Judge not,” so do not decide other people are bad. He said, “Forgive, and you will be forgiven.”","Then Jesus gave a funny picture. Your brother has a tiny speck of dust in his eye. You want to pull it out.","But you have a big, heavy log of wood in your own eye! How can you see his little speck?","It is a silly picture, and that helps us remember it. We see other people’s little mistakes and forget our own big ones. Jesus said, “First take the log out of your own eye. Then you will see clearly to help your brother.”","Before you tattle on someone, look at yourself first. Then help them with love."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer pauses and ponders before she points a finger. Jesus said to check for the log in our own eye first, and that is the kind of thinking Wren does best. In Wren’s world today: Wren starts to tattle, “Mom, Eli didn’t put his shoes away!” Then she looks down. Her own shoes are in the middle of the hall. She giggles, picks up her shoes, and says, “Never mind. Eli, let’s both put them away.” Just like Jesus taught, Wren learned to take the log out of her own eye before pointing at someone’s speck.","feel":"Pretend to pull a tiny speck off your eyelash, very gently. Now pretend to pull a giant log out of your other eye. Heave! Can you show me how big that log is?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus taught about the speck and the log. The people laughed, and learned to be gentle with others and honest with themselves."],["NAME","That soft, careful, I-won’t-point-fingers feeling is called gentle. Your mind might think, “I make mistakes, too.” Your body might feel relaxed hands and a kind face."],["FIND","When did you start to tattle on someone, and then remember you had done the same thing?"],["FORK","Wren could tattle on Eli, or fix her own shoes first. She fixed her own first, then helped him."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your pointing finger rest. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me be gentle.”"]],"remember":"Take the log out of your own eye first, then gently help with the speck!","ask":["“What is a speck, and what is a log?”","“What did Jesus say to take out first?”","“What did Wren notice about her own shoes?”"],"share":["Share a time you noticed a log in your own eye before judging someone.","How do you try to be gentle with others’ mistakes and honest about your own?","Was there a grandparent or ancestor who never judged people harshly? Tell about them."]},"b14-w2-n7":{"story":["Jesus asked, “Why call Me Lord and not do what I say?” Then He told a story about two builders.","The first builder dug down deep and built his house on rock. A flood came, and the river rushed hard against it. But the house stood strong on the rock.","The second builder did not dig. He built on soft ground, like sand. The same flood came, and that house fell down flat.","Jesus said the first builder hears His words and does them. The second hears but does not. Doing what Jesus says keeps us strong.","Each time you obey Jesus, you build on the rock. Tonight, name one thing Jesus asks you to do."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big heart, brave start!” A house on the rock stands steady in the storm, and Beau wants to be that kind of steady: hearing Jesus’s words and doing them. In Beau’s world today: Beau builds a sandcastle at the beach, and a wave washes it away. So he moves up to the big flat rock and builds there. When the next wave comes, his castle on the rock stays! “That’s where I’m building from now on,” he says. Just like the wise builder, Beau learned that doing what Jesus says builds a life that stays steady in the storm.","feel":"Make your body wobbly like a house on sand. Wobble, wobble! Now make it strong and still, like a house on a rock. Can you show me your rock-steady body?","stones":[["SPOT","One house stood in the storm, and one fell. The people learned that doing Jesus’s words makes them steady."],["NAME","That strong, I-won’t-fall-over feeling is called steady. Your mind might think, “I am standing on the rock.” Your body might feel firm feet and a straight back."],["FIND","When did you feel steady and safe, even when something hard was happening?"],["FORK","The builders could dig deep on the rock, or build fast on the sand. The wise builder chose the rock."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel your bed holding you steady. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me build on Jesus.”"]],"remember":"Hear Jesus’s words and do them, and your house will stand strong in every storm!","ask":["“What did the wise builder build his house on?”","“What happened to the house built on the sand?”","“Where did Beau build his second sandcastle?”"],"share":["Share a storm in your life that you stood through because you were built on Jesus.","What does hearing and doing Jesus’s words look like in your week?","How did a grandparent or ancestor build a steady life on Jesus? Tell about them."]},"b14-w3-n1":{"story":["Jesus taught about doing good. He said, “Do not do it just so people will see you.”","Some people gave to the poor with a big show. Jesus said, “Do not blow a trumpet when you give.” Give quietly instead.","Some people also prayed on busy street corners. They wanted everyone to see them pray.","Jesus said, “When you pray, go into your room and shut the door.” Heavenly Father does not want a show. He wants your real heart. He sees what is done in secret, and He knows what you need.","You can do kind things when nobody is watching. Heavenly Father sees. Tonight, say a quiet prayer in your own words."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer pauses and ponders what is real. Jesus said Heavenly Father sees what we do in secret, and Wren likes being good when nobody is watching but Him. In Wren’s world today: Wren finds her baby brother’s favorite blanket under the couch. Nobody is around. She could leave it. Instead she folds it and puts it in his crib. Nobody says, “Good job.” But Wren smiles, because she knows Heavenly Father saw. Just like Jesus taught, Wren learned that Heavenly Father sees what we do in secret, and that is what counts.","feel":"Pretend to blow a loud trumpet, “Toot, toot!” Now put a finger on your lips, “Shh,” and quietly fold your arms to pray. Can you show me your quiet, secret prayer?","stones":[["SPOT","Some people showed off their giving and praying. Jesus taught to do it in secret. The people who listened felt sincere."],["NAME","That real-not-for-show, just-me-and-Heavenly-Father feeling is called sincere. Your mind might think, “Only He needs to see.” Your body might feel quiet and calm."],["FIND","When did you do something good and nobody saw but Heavenly Father?"],["FORK","We can do good to be seen, or do it quietly for Heavenly Father. Jesus says do it quietly."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly with a finger on your lips. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, You see me, and that is enough.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father sees what you do in secret, and He loves your real heart!","ask":["“What did some people do so everyone would see them give money?”","“Where did Jesus say to go when you pray?”","“What did Wren do with her brother’s blanket when nobody was watching?”"],"share":["Share a time you served in secret and felt Heavenly Father’s quiet reward.","How do you keep your prayers real and simple, in your own words?","Did a grandparent or ancestor serve quietly, without wanting credit? Tell about them."]},"b14-w3-n2":{"story":["Some people thought long prayers with lots of words were best. So Jesus taught a short, simple prayer.","It begins, “Our Father which art in heaven, hallowed be thy name.” We say His holy name with love.","We ask that His will be done on earth, as in heaven. We ask for our daily bread, the food we need today.","We ask Him to forgive our wrongs, as we forgive others. Then Jesus said, “If ye forgive men, your heavenly Father will also forgive you.”","Is something unkind still stuck in your heart? Tonight, pray the way Jesus taught, and forgive. Your heart will feel light and soft."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” A forgiving heart is the warmest kind, and Jesus taught us to forgive others the way Heavenly Father forgives us. In Willow’s world today: Willow’s cousin broke her favorite crayon and did not say sorry. At bedtime Willow prays, “Please forgive me for the things I did wrong today.” Then she remembers the crayon. “And I forgive my cousin,” she adds. Her heart feels light and soft. Just like the Lord’s Prayer teaches, Willow learned that when we forgive others, Heavenly Father forgives us.","feel":"Hold up a pretend broken crayon. Now open your hand and let it go, like letting go of being upset. Fold your arms and bow your head. Can you show me your forgiving hands?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus taught the Lord’s Prayer and said to forgive others. The people who prayed it felt forgiving."],["NAME","That letting-go, I’m-not-holding-it-against-you feeling is called forgiving. Your mind might think, “It’s okay now.” Your body might feel light, with open hands."],["FIND","When did you forgive someone, and your heart felt lighter?"],["FORK","Willow could stay upset about the crayon, or forgive her cousin. Willow forgave."],["CALM","Breathe out slowly and open your hands. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, I forgive, and please forgive me.”"]],"remember":"If you forgive others, Heavenly Father will forgive you!","ask":["“What are the first words of the prayer Jesus taught?”","“What did Jesus say to ask for each day?”","“Who did Willow forgive in her prayer?”"],"share":["Share how the Lord’s Prayer has shaped the way you pray.","Who do you need to forgive so your own heart can be free?","Did a grandparent or ancestor forgive someone in a way that changed your family? Tell their story."]},"b14-w3-n3":{"story":["Jesus said, “Do not save up treasures on earth.” Little moths can chew holes in fine clothes. Rust can eat away metal, and robbers can steal.","“Instead,” Jesus said, “save up treasures in heaven.” Nothing can spoil those treasures or take them away.","Treasures in heaven are the good we do and the love we share. They last forever.","Jesus said, “Where your treasure is, there will your heart be also.” What we love most pulls our heart toward it. Jesus said no one can work for two bosses. We cannot put Heavenly Father and money both first.","Is there a toy you wish you had? Tonight, think of a treasure that lasts, like a hug from someone you love."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker is always looking for treasure. Jesus told him where the real treasure is: in heaven, where nothing can ruin it. Scout is content with that kind of treasure. In Scout’s world today: Scout’s friend got a shiny new scooter, and Scout’s is old and scratched. For a minute he feels grumpy. Then Dad takes him on a bike ride, and they stop to watch the sunset together. Scout thinks, “This is better than a new scooter.” Just like Jesus taught, Scout learned that the best treasures are the ones that last forever.","feel":"Pretend to open a treasure chest and pull out gold coins. Now let them fall away. Put both hands on your heart, where the real treasure is. Can you show me where your treasure is?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus taught to store treasures in heaven, not on earth. The people who listened felt content with what lasts."],["NAME","That I-have-enough, I-don’t-need-more feeling is called content. Your mind might think, “What I have is good.” Your body might feel relaxed and settled."],["FIND","When did you have something simple, like time with someone you love, and it felt like enough?"],["FORK","Scout could stay grumpy about the old scooter, or enjoy the bike ride with Dad. He enjoyed the ride."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and put your hands on your heart. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, You are my treasure.”"]],"remember":"Where your treasure is, your heart will be, so keep your treasure in heaven!","ask":["“What can ruin treasures on earth?”","“Where did Jesus say to store up treasures?”","“What did Scout do with his dad that was better than a new scooter?”"],"share":["Share a treasure in heaven that matters more to you than anything on earth.","How do you keep Heavenly Father first when money or things try to take His place?","Did a grandparent or ancestor choose heavenly treasure over earthly riches? Tell about it."]},"b14-w3-n4":{"story":["Jesus said, “Do not worry about food or clothes.” Heavenly Father knows you need them.","“Look at the birds,” Jesus said. “They do not plant seeds. Yet your heavenly Father feeds them.” You are worth much more than birds!","“Look at the lilies of the field,” He said. They do not work or make clothes. Yet even rich King Solomon, in his finest robes, was not as pretty.","If Heavenly Father dresses the grass so beautifully, He will surely care for you. Jesus said to seek His kingdom first. Then the rest would be given too.","When a worry flutters in your tummy, think of the birds and flowers. Tonight, tell Heavenly Father your worry, and let Him hold it."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm has a tender heart, and tender hearts sometimes worry. Jesus showed Willow the birds and the lilies, and now she knows who takes care of her. In Willow’s world today: Willow worries about her first day at a new school. Her tummy flutters. Mom walks her to the window and points at a robin pulling up a worm. “Heavenly Father feeds that little bird every day,” Mom says. “He’ll take care of you, too.” Willow’s tummy settles. Just like Jesus taught, Willow learned that Heavenly Father takes care of the birds and the lilies, and He will take care of her.","feel":"Flap your arms like a little bird that never worries. Now be a lily: stretch tall and let your petal hands open wide. Can you show me your lily petals?","stones":[["SPOT","The people worried about food and clothes. Jesus showed them the birds and lilies, and their worry turned to trust."],["NAME","That tight, what-if feeling is called worried. Your mind might think, “What if there isn’t enough?” Your body might feel a fluttery tummy and tight shoulders."],["FIND","When did you worry about something, and then it turned out fine?"],["FORK","Willow could keep worrying about school, or trust Heavenly Father to care for her. She trusted."],["CALM","Breathe out slowly and let your shoulders drop. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, I know You will take care of me.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father feeds the birds and dresses the lilies, and He will take care of you!","ask":["“What did Jesus say about the birds?”","“Who was not dressed as beautifully as the lilies?”","“What did Willow’s mom show her at the window?”"],"share":["Share a time Heavenly Father provided for you when you were worried.","What does seeking first the kingdom of Heavenly Father look like in your life?","Did a grandparent or ancestor trust Heavenly Father through hard times when there was little? Tell their story."]},"b14-w3-n5":{"story":["Jesus said, “Ask, and it shall be given you. Seek, and ye shall find. Knock, and it shall be opened.”","Jesus wanted the people to keep praying and never give up. Heavenly Father is always listening.","Then Jesus asked, “If your child asks for bread, will you give a stone?” Of course not! A loving mom or dad gives good gifts.","Jesus said Heavenly Father gives good things even more to those who ask Him. Then Jesus taught a rule people call the Golden Rule. “Treat others the way you want them to treat you.”","Is something hard for you, like tying your shoes? Tonight, ask Heavenly Father for help. Then keep trying, and keep asking."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big heart, brave start!” It takes a hopeful, brave heart to keep knocking on a door until it opens. Beau keeps asking Heavenly Father, because he knows He answers. In Beau’s world today: Beau wants to learn to tie his shoes. He tries and tries, and the laces keep slipping. He prays, “Heavenly Father, help me.” Then he asks Grandma to show him one more time. On the fourth try, a bow! “I kept asking,” Beau says, “and it worked.” Just like Jesus taught, Beau learned that when we keep asking, seeking, and knocking, Heavenly Father gives good things.","feel":"Knock on your headboard, knock, knock, knock. Now hold out your hands like a door opening wide. Can you show me the door opening?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus said ask, seek, knock, and Heavenly Father will answer. The people felt hopeful to pray."],["NAME","That keep-trying, the-door-will-open feeling is called hopeful. Your mind might think, “He will answer.” Your body might feel ready hands and bright eyes."],["FIND","When did you keep asking or trying, and then it finally worked?"],["FORK","Beau could give up on his shoelaces, or keep asking for help. He kept asking, and it worked."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your hands rest open. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for giving good things.”"]],"remember":"Ask, seek, knock, and Heavenly Father will open the door!","ask":["“What three things did Jesus say to do when we pray?”","“What is the Golden Rule?”","“How did Beau finally tie his shoes?”"],"share":["Share a time you kept knocking in prayer and the door opened.","What good gift has Heavenly Father given you that you asked for?","Did a grandparent or ancestor live by the Golden Rule? Tell how they treated people."]},"b14-w3-n6":{"story":["Jesus told about two gates. A wide gate leads to being lost, and many go that way. A narrow gate leads to life.","A wide gate is easy, with a big crowd. A narrow gate fits one person at a time. To follow Jesus, we choose the right way ourselves.","Jesus also warned about people who pretend to be good. They dress like gentle sheep, but inside they are wolves.","How can you tell? Jesus said, “Ye shall know them by their fruits.” A good tree grows good fruit. A bad tree grows bad fruit. So watch what people do.","Sometimes others want to go the wrong way. Tonight, think of one right choice you made today. That was the narrow gate!"],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see, what could it be?” Jesus told Scout how to look carefully: find the narrow gate, and check the fruit. Scout is a careful seeker. In Scout’s world today: A bigger boy at the park says, “Let’s climb over the fence into the old lot. Everyone does it.” Scout looks at the fence, looks at the sign, and says, “No, I’ll stay on this side.” He finds a cool beetle by the path instead. Just like Jesus taught, Scout learned to be careful, choosing the narrow way even when the crowd goes the wide way.","feel":"Hold your hands far apart like a big wide gate. Now bring them close, just enough for one person to squeeze through. Can you show me the narrow gate?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus taught about the narrow gate and good fruit. The people who listened learned to be careful about which way to go."],["NAME","That look-before-you-go, I’ll-check-first feeling is called careful. Your mind might think, “Which way is right?” Your body might feel slow steps and watching eyes."],["FIND","When did you stop to check if something was a good idea before you did it?"],["FORK","Scout could follow the big boy over the fence, or stay on the right side. Scout stayed."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your feet rest. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me choose the narrow way.”"]],"remember":"Choose the narrow gate, and know a tree by its fruit!","ask":["“Which gate did Jesus say leads to life, the wide one or the narrow one?”","“How can you tell a good tree from a bad tree?”","“What did Scout say when the big boy wanted to climb the fence?”"],"share":["Share a time you chose the narrow way when the crowd went the other way.","How do you judge teachings and voices by their fruit in your life?","Did a grandparent or ancestor stay on the narrow path when it was hard? Tell about them."]},"b14-w3-n7":{"story":["Jesus was finishing His big talk on the mountain. He said not everyone who calls Him “Lord, Lord” will enter heaven. It is those who do what Heavenly Father wants.","Nice words are not enough. We need to do what Jesus teaches.","So Jesus told about two builders. The wise man built his house on a rock, and it stood strong. The foolish man built on sand, and his house fell.","When Jesus finished, the people were amazed. The other teachers did not teach like this. Jesus spoke with power, like one who truly knows. And He did know, because He is the Son of God.","Tonight, pick one thing Jesus taught, like being kind. Do it, and be like the wise builder."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer says, “Pause and ponder, what a wonder!” The people were astonished at how Jesus taught, and Wren sat with her mouth open, too. In Wren’s world today: Wren’s grandpa fixes the broken music box. He does not read a book about it. He just knows. When it plays again, Wren’s mouth drops open. “How did you know how?” Grandpa smiles. “I made one when I was a boy.” Wren thinks, “He really knows.” Just like the people on the mountain, Wren learned that Jesus teaches with real power, because He really knows.","feel":"Open your mouth wide in an astonished “Oh!” and make your eyes big. Now close your mouth, nod slowly, and fold your arms to show you will do what He says. Can you show me your astonished face?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus finished His sermon, and the people were astonished at His power. They felt amazed."],["NAME","That mouth-open, never-heard-anything-like-it feeling is called amazed. Your mind might think, “He really knows!” Your body might feel big eyes and an open mouth."],["FIND","When did someone teach or show you something so well that your mouth dropped open?"],["FORK","The people could just say, “Lord, Lord,” or do what Jesus said. Jesus asks us to do it."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and close your mouth into a smile. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that Jesus’s words are true.”"]],"remember":"Jesus teaches with real power, so hear His words and do them!","ask":["“What did Jesus say about people who only say, “Lord, Lord”?”","“How did the people feel when Jesus finished teaching?”","“What did Wren’s grandpa fix without reading a book?”"],"share":["Share a time Jesus’s words came alive for you with real power.","What is the difference, for you, between saying “Lord, Lord” and doing His will?","Did a grandparent or ancestor live the words of Jesus, not just say them? Tell about them."]},"b14-w4-n1":{"story":["Jesus came to Capernaum. A centurion came to Him, a captain over a hundred soldiers.","He said, “Lord, my servant is sick at home and hurts badly.” Jesus said, “I will come and heal him.”","The centurion said, “Lord, I am not worthy to have You in my house. Speak the word only, and my servant shall be healed.”","He said his soldiers obeyed his words. He knew Jesus’s words were even stronger. Jesus was amazed. “I have not found so great faith,” He said. Then He told the centurion, “Go thy way.” The servant was healed that same hour.","The centurion did not brag. He was humble, and he trusted Jesus. Tonight, thank Heavenly Father for the good things you can do."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big heart, brave start!” The centurion was a strong soldier, but he bowed his heart before Jesus and said, “I am not worthy.” Beau knows that real strength is humble. In Beau’s world today: Beau is the fastest runner in his class. At the race, he wins, and everyone cheers. Instead of bragging, Beau says, “Heavenly Father gave me fast legs, and my big sister taught me to run.” His coach says, “That’s a champion’s heart.” Just like the centurion, Beau learned that being humble and trusting Jesus’s word is the greatest kind of faith.","feel":"Stand your arm up straight like a strong soldier. Now bow your head and lower your arm, soft and humble. Can you show me your humble bow?","stones":[["SPOT","The centurion said he was not worthy, and asked Jesus to just speak the word. He felt humble, and Jesus called it great faith."],["NAME","That I’m-not-the-biggest, He-is feeling is called humble. Your mind might think, “Jesus is greater than me.” Your body might feel a bowed head and soft shoulders."],["FIND","When did you do something well and gave the thanks to Heavenly Father or someone who helped you?"],["FORK","The centurion could demand that Jesus come, or humbly trust His word. He trusted the word."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and bow your head. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, I know Jesus’s word has power.”"]],"remember":"Jesus only has to speak the word, and His word has power!","ask":["“Who was sick at the centurion’s house?”","“What did the centurion ask Jesus to do instead of coming to his house?”","“What did Beau say after he won the race?”"],"share":["Share a time you felt unworthy, yet trusted Jesus’s word, and He answered.","Where do you need Jesus to simply speak the word in your life right now?","Did a grandparent or ancestor show humble faith that blessed your family? Tell their story."]},"b14-w4-n2":{"story":["One evening Jesus and His disciples got into a boat. They sailed across the lake. Jesus lay in the back on a pillow and fell asleep.","Then a great wind came. The waves splashed in, and the boat filled with water.","His friends were very scared. They woke Jesus and said, “Lord, save us!”","Jesus stood up. He told the wind and the waves, “Peace, be still.” The wind stopped, and the water became very calm. He asked, “Why are ye so fearful?” His friends said, “Even the wind and the sea obey Him!”","When you feel scared, you can go to Jesus too. Tonight, whisper, “Peace, be still,” and breathe slow."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker loves a boat ride, but even Scout gets scared in a storm. Then he looks and sees: Jesus is in the boat. And Jesus can say, “Peace, be still.” In Scout’s world today: Thunder booms, and the lights flicker. Scout dives under his blanket, heart pounding. Then Dad sits on the bed and says, “I’m right here.” Scout peeks out. Dad is calm. Scout breathes slower and whispers, “Peace, be still,” like Jesus. Just like the disciples, Scout learned that Jesus is in our boat, and He can calm every storm.","feel":"Rock side to side like a boat in big waves. Rock harder! Now stop, hold very still, and whisper, “Peace, be still.” Can you show me the calm sea?","stones":[["SPOT","A storm filled the boat with water, and the disciples were scared. Jesus calmed the storm."],["NAME","That heart-pounding, I-want-to-hide feeling is called scared. Your mind might think, “We’re in trouble!” Your body might feel your heart race and your hands grip tight."],["FIND","When did a storm or a loud noise make you feel scared?"],["FORK","The disciples could panic, or wake Jesus and trust Him. They went to Jesus."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your hands loosen. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, please say peace to my heart.”"]],"remember":"Jesus can say, “Peace, be still,” to every storm, even the one inside you!","ask":["“Where was Jesus when the storm came?”","“What did Jesus say to the wind and the waves?”","“What did Scout whisper when his dad sat on his bed?”"],"share":["Share a storm in your life when Jesus brought peace.","How do you remember that Jesus is in the boat when you feel afraid?","Did a grandparent or ancestor stay calm in a frightening time because of faith? Tell about it."]},"b14-w4-n3":{"story":["It was the Sabbath, Heavenly Father’s day of rest. Jesus went into the synagogue, where people met to pray. A man there had a hand that was dried up.","Some men watched Jesus closely. If He healed on the Sabbath, they would blame Him.","Jesus told the man, “Stand forth,” so everyone could see him.","Jesus asked, “Is it right to do good on the Sabbath, or harm?” Nobody answered. Jesus was sad their hearts were so hard. He told the man, “Stretch forth thine hand.” The man did, and it was well, just like his other hand!","Jesus showed that the Sabbath is a day for doing good. Tonight, think of someone you can help this Sunday."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big heart, brave start!” Jesus healed the man in front of the people who wanted to catch Him, because caring for people matters more than extra rules. In Beau’s world today: On Sunday, Beau’s neighbor, old Mr. Perez, drops his groceries in the driveway. Beau runs over and helps him carry every bag inside. Mr. Perez says, “Thank you, young man.” Beau thinks, “That’s what Sunday is for.” Just like Jesus, Beau learned that caring for people is always right, especially on Heavenly Father’s day.","feel":"Curl your hand up tight, like it cannot open. Now slowly stretch it out wide, strong and whole. Can you show me your stretched-out hand?","stones":[["SPOT","The man’s hand could not move. Jesus cared about him and healed him, even though some leaders were upset."],["NAME","That I-want-to-help-you feeling is called caring. Your mind might think, “He needs me.” Your body might feel your hands ready to help."],["FIND","When did you help someone who needed it, even when it was not easy?"],["FORK","Jesus could wait until the next day to avoid trouble, or heal the man right then. Jesus healed him."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and open your hands. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me care like Jesus.”"]],"remember":"It is always right to do good, and the Sabbath is a day for doing good!","ask":["“What was wrong with the man in the synagogue?”","“What did Jesus tell the man to do with his hand?”","“How did Beau help Mr.","Perez on Sunday?”"],"share":["Share a time you chose to do good on the Sabbath, and how it felt.","How do you keep the Sabbath a day for doing good in your family?","Did a grandparent or ancestor serve others on Sundays? Tell what they did."]},"b14-w4-n4":{"story":["Jesus sat in a boat to teach the crowd by the lake. He told a parable, a little story that teaches something big.","A farmer threw seeds. Some fell on the hard path, and birds ate them. Some fell on rocky ground. They had no roots, so the sun dried them up.","Some fell in thorns that choked them. Some fell on good soil and grew lots of grain.","The seed is Heavenly Father’s word, and the ground is like our hearts. A hard heart will not listen. A rocky heart gives up. Worries choke a thorny heart. A good heart keeps the word and grows.","Your heart can be good soil. Tonight, remember one thing about Jesus, and let it grow."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer says, “Pause and ponder.” Good soil is a heart that is ready: it listens, holds on, and lets the word grow. Wren gets her heart ready every time a story begins. In Wren’s world today: It is family scripture time, and Wren’s brother is bouncing on the couch. Wren sits still, folds her hands, and listens with her whole face. Later, when her friend is sad, Wren remembers the story and says, “Jesus loves you.” The seed grew. Just like the good soil, Wren learned that a ready heart lets Heavenly Father’s word take root and grow.","feel":"Make yourself a tiny seed, curled up small. Now slowly grow: stretch up, up, up, and open your leaf arms wide. Can you show me how tall you grew?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus told about four kinds of soil. The people with ready hearts let the word grow."],["NAME","That ears-open, heart-open, I’m-listening feeling is called ready. Your mind might think, “Tell me, I want to grow.” Your body might feel still and leaning in."],["FIND","When did you listen really carefully, and later remembered what you heard?"],["FORK","A heart can be hard like the path, or soft like good soil. Jesus asks us to be good soil."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly like a seed drinking water. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, let Your words grow in me.”"]],"remember":"Be good soil: hear Heavenly Father’s word, hold onto it, and let it grow!","ask":["“What happened to the seeds that fell on the hard path?”","“What is the seed in Jesus’s story?”","“What did Wren remember to tell her sad friend?”"],"share":["Share a time a scripture took root in your heart and grew into something good.","Which soil is your heart right now, and what would help it be good soil?","How did a grandparent or ancestor plant the word of Heavenly Father in your family?"]},"b14-w4-n5":{"story":["Jesus asked, “Do you hide a candle under a basket or a bed?” No! You set it up high to shine. Jesus’s good news is meant to shine too.","Then Jesus told another little story. Heavenly Father’s kingdom is like a mustard seed. It is one of the tiniest seeds of all.","But when it is planted, it grows bigger than all the garden plants. Its branches grow so big that birds rest in their shade.","His kingdom began small, with just a few friends following Jesus. But it would keep growing, bigger and bigger. Faith can start tiny too, and grow big.","Your faith may feel small. Tonight, water it with a little prayer, and let it grow."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker loves tiny things that turn into big things. A mustard seed is almost too small to see, and it grows into a giant bush. That makes Scout feel encouraged about his own little faith. In Scout’s world today: Scout plants one little sunflower seed in a paper cup. It looks like nothing. He waters it every day. Weeks later, it is taller than he is, with a flower as big as a plate! “That whole thing came from one tiny seed,” Scout says. Just like the mustard seed, Scout learned that small things, like a little faith, can grow very big with Heavenly Father.","feel":"Pinch your fingers together to hold a seed as tiny as a dot. Now spread your arms wider and wider, like branches where birds can rest. Can you show me how big it grew?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus said His kingdom is like a tiny mustard seed that grows huge. The people felt encouraged."],["NAME","That small-things-can-grow, keep-going feeling is called encouraged. Your mind might think, “My little bit matters.” Your body might feel lighter and ready to try."],["FIND","When did something small you did turn into something big and good?"],["FORK","The people could think the kingdom was too small to matter, or trust it would grow. Jesus said it would grow."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your arms rest. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help my faith grow like a mustard seed.”"]],"remember":"A tiny seed of faith can grow into something big and beautiful!","ask":["“Where should you put a candle so it gives light?”","“How big is a mustard seed, and how big does the plant grow?”","“What did Scout’s sunflower seed grow into?”"],"share":["Share how a small seed of faith in your life grew into something big.","What small step of faith could you take this week and let Heavenly Father grow?","Did a grandparent or ancestor start something small that grew to bless many? Tell about it."]},"b14-w4-n6":{"story":["Jesus walked to a town called Nain with His friends and a big crowd. Near the gate, they met people carrying a young man who had died.","His mother was there, crying. Her husband had died before, and this was her only son.","When Jesus saw her, His heart filled with love for her. He said, “Weep not.”","Jesus touched the bed, and the carriers stood still. He said, “Young man, I say unto thee, Arise.” The young man sat up and began to talk! Jesus gave him back to his mother. Everyone praised Heavenly Father.","Jesus went right to the sad mother. You can sit close to someone who is sad, too. Tonight, remember: when you are sad, Jesus sees you."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm has a tender heart. When Jesus saw the crying mother, He went right to her and said, “Do not cry.” Willow does the same thing when someone is sad. In Willow’s world today: Willow’s friend Mia is crying at the bus stop because her dog died. Willow does not know what to say. So she just sits down close, puts her arm around Mia, and stays. After a while Mia leans on her and says, “Thanks for staying.” Just like Jesus, Willow learned that when someone is sad, the first thing to do is go close and care.","feel":"Make a sad face and let your shoulders droop. Now imagine a kind hand on your shoulder, and lift your face slowly. Can you show me your lifted-up face?","stones":[["SPOT","The widow had lost her only son and was crying. Jesus saw her sadness and gave her son back to her."],["NAME","That heavy, crying, something-is-lost feeling is called sad. Your mind might think, “I miss them.” Your body might feel heavy shoulders and tears."],["FIND","When did you feel sad, and someone came close and stayed with you?"],["FORK","Jesus could walk past the funeral, or go to the crying mother. Jesus went to her."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let the tears be okay. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that Jesus sees me when I’m sad.”"]],"remember":"When you are sad, Jesus sees you and comes close!","ask":["“Who was crying at the town gate?”","“What did Jesus say to the young man?”","“What did Willow do when Mia was crying at the bus stop?”"],"share":["Share a time of sadness when you felt Jesus come close to you.","Who in your life is grieving, and how could you go close and stay?","Did a grandparent or ancestor comfort others in their sorrow? Tell about them."]},"b14-w4-n7":{"story":["Jesus ate dinner at the house of a man named Simon. A woman came who had done many wrong things.","She brought a pretty jar of sweet-smelling oil. She stood at Jesus’s feet and cried, and her tears fell on His feet.","She wiped them with her hair and poured on the sweet oil. She was sorry, and she loved Jesus very much.","Simon thought Jesus should not let her touch Him. But Jesus said her many sins were forgiven, for she loved much. Then He told her, “Thy sins are forgiven. Thy faith hath saved thee; go in peace.”","When you do something wrong, you can come to Jesus too. Tonight, say sorry, and let your heart feel light again."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” The woman’s sorry heart was so full of love that it spilled out in tears, and being forgiven made her love grow big. In Willow’s world today: Willow told a fib about brushing her teeth. All evening her tummy feels heavy. Finally she climbs into Mom’s lap and says, “I didn’t really brush. I’m sorry.” Mom hugs her tight. “I forgive you.” Willow feels light again. Just like the woman at Jesus’s feet, Willow learned that when we are truly sorry, Jesus forgives us, and our love for Him grows.","feel":"Hold a pretend little jar of perfume and tip it over, slow and careful. Now put your hands on your heart and take a big, light breath. Can you show me your forgiven breath?","stones":[["SPOT","The woman cried at Jesus’s feet because she was sorry. Jesus forgave her, and she felt peace."],["NAME","That heavy, I-wish-I-hadn’t feeling is called sorry. Your mind might think, “I need to make it right.” Your body might feel a heavy tummy and a lump in your throat."],["FIND","When did you do something wrong and felt heavy inside until you said sorry?"],["FORK","The woman could stay away because of her sins, or come to Jesus. She came, and He forgave her."],["CALM","Breathe out slowly and let the heavy go. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, I’m sorry. Thank You for forgiving me.”"]],"remember":"When we are sorry and come to Jesus, He forgives us, and we go in peace!","ask":["“What did the woman pour on Jesus’s feet?”","“What did Jesus say to the woman at the end?”","“What did Willow tell her mom she was sorry about?”"],"share":["Share a time you felt the peace of being forgiven by Jesus.","How has being forgiven much helped you love Jesus more?","Did a grandparent or ancestor teach your family about repentance and forgiveness? Tell about it."]},"b13-w1-n1":{"story":["Long ago lived a good old man named Zacharias. He and his wife, Elisabeth, had no children.","One day Zacharias went into the temple, Heavenly Father’s holy house. He burned sweet-smelling incense while the people prayed outside. An angel stood by the altar!","Zacharias shook with fear. The angel said, “Fear not. Your prayer is heard. Elisabeth will have a baby boy named John.”","“How can I know this? We are so old,” said Zacharias. The angel said, “I am Gabriel. You did not believe me, so you cannot talk until the baby comes.” Zacharias came out and could only wave his hands.","Heavenly Father hears every prayer, even ones we pray for a long time. Tonight, tell Him something you have been asking for."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer pauses and ponders. Zacharias saw something so big he could hardly take it in, and Wren loves to wonder about big, holy things. In Wren’s world today: Wren has asked Heavenly Father every night to help her baby cousin get better. Tonight Mom whispers that the baby came home from the hospital. Wren sits very still and feels her heart go thump, thump. “Heavenly Father really heard me!” Just like Zacharias, Wren learned that Heavenly Father hears every prayer, even when the answer takes a long time.","feel":"Lie very still and breathe in slowly, like Zacharias smelling the sweet incense. Now open your eyes wide, like he did when he saw the angel. Can you show me a surprised face?","stones":[["SPOT","Zacharias saw an angel in the temple. He was shaking and surprised, and then he heard happy news."],["NAME","That big, jumpy, wide-eyed feeling is called surprised. Your mind might think, “Is this real?” Your body might feel your heart beat fast."],["FIND","When did something surprise you so much your eyes went wide?"],["FORK","Zacharias could believe the angel’s words, or doubt them. He doubted at first. Later he learned to trust Heavenly Father."],["CALM","Put one hand on your heart. Breathe slowly until it is calm. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for hearing me.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father hears every prayer, even when the answer takes a long time!","ask":["“Who did Zacharias see standing by the altar in the temple?”","“What happened to Zacharias’s voice when he did not believe the angel?”","“What did Wren find out that made her heart go thump, thump?”"],"share":["Tell your child about a prayer you waited a long time for.","When has Heavenly Father surprised you with something better than you asked for?","Did a grandparent or ancestor keep praying for something for years? Share what happened."]},"b13-w1-n2":{"story":["In the little town of Nazareth lived a young woman named Mary. She was going to marry Joseph.","One day the angel Gabriel came to her. “Hello, Mary! The Lord is with you.” Mary was afraid.","Gabriel said, “Fear not. You will have a baby boy. Name Him Jesus. He will be the Son of the Highest, a King forever.”","Mary asked, “How can this be?” Gabriel said, “With Heavenly Father, nothing is impossible.” Mary trusted Him. She said, “I am the Lord’s servant. Let it be as you said.” That was a brave yes!","Heavenly Father sometimes asks us to do hard things. Tonight, think of one brave thing you can try. You can say yes, like Mary."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave has a big heart and a brave start. Mary said yes to something very hard before she knew how it would all work out. That is the bravest kind of yes. In Beau’s world today: Beau’s teacher asks who will be first to stand up and share. Beau’s tummy flutters. He does not know if he can do it. But he takes a big breath, raises his hand, and says, “I will try.” Just like Mary, Beau learned that being brave means saying yes to Heavenly Father even when we feel small.","feel":"Lie on your back and let your arms go soft, like you are resting. Now take a big breath and make your hand into a strong fist. Can you show me your brave fist?","stones":[["SPOT","Mary saw an angel and heard big news. She felt afraid, and then she felt brave enough to say yes."],["NAME","That shaky-but-strong, I-can-do-it feeling is called brave. Your mind might think, “I will try.” Your body might feel your chest get tall."],["FIND","When did you do something that felt hard, like a first day somewhere new?"],["FORK","Mary could say, “No, this is too hard,” or trust Heavenly Father and say yes. Mary said yes."],["CALM","Take one slow breath and let your shoulders drop. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me be brave like Mary.”"]],"remember":"With Heavenly Father, nothing is impossible, so we can say a brave yes!","ask":["“What was the name of the angel who visited Mary?”","“What name did the angel say Mary should give her baby?”","“What hard thing did Beau do at school?”"],"share":["Share a time you said yes to something hard for Heavenly Father.","What helps you trust Heavenly Father when you cannot see the way?","Was there a grandparent or ancestor who made a brave choice for their faith? Tell their story."]},"b13-w1-n3":{"story":["After the angel left, Mary hurried into the hills to see her cousin Elisabeth. Elisabeth was going to have a baby too, baby John.","Mary came in and called hello. The moment Elisabeth heard Mary’s voice, her baby jumped for joy!","The Holy Ghost filled Elisabeth. She knew Mary’s baby would be the Lord.","Elisabeth’s voice was loud and happy. “You are blessed, Mary, and so is your baby! You believed Heavenly Father’s words, and they will come true.”","Even a tiny baby can feel joy when Jesus is near. Tonight, think of someone who makes you want to jump for joy. Thank Heavenly Father for them."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm loves warm hugs and warm hearts. Tonight’s story is about two cousins whose joy spilled out in hugs and happy words, just like Willow. In Willow’s world today: Willow hears the doorbell and runs to the window. It is Grandma! Willow’s feet do a happy dance before the door even opens. She flings her arms around Grandma’s neck and squeals, “You came!” Just like Elisabeth, Willow learned that joy is Heavenly Father’s gift, and it grows when we share it.","feel":"Lie in bed and give yourself a big, squeezy hug. Now let your toes wiggle like a baby kicking for joy. Can you show me your happiest wiggle?","stones":[["SPOT","Mary came to visit, and the baby inside Elisabeth jumped. Elisabeth and Mary felt happy and full of joy."],["NAME","That bubbly, bouncy, can’t-sit-still feeling is called joy. Your mind might think, “This is the best!” Your body might feel light and jumpy."],["FIND","When did someone come to see you and your whole body felt happy?"],["FORK","Elisabeth could keep her joy quiet, or shout it out and bless Mary. She shared it with a loud, happy voice."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your smile stay. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for joy.”"]],"remember":"Joy is a gift from Heavenly Father, and it grows bigger when we share it!","ask":["“Who did Mary walk over the hills to visit?”","“What did the baby inside Elisabeth do when he heard Mary’s voice?”","“Who came to visit Willow and made her feet do a happy dance?”"],"share":["Tell about a moment of pure joy when you felt Heavenly Father near.","Who is the Elisabeth in your life, the friend who cheers for your faith?","Is there a grandparent or ancestor whose visits or letters brought your family joy?"]},"b13-w1-n4":{"story":["Mary stayed with Elisabeth. Her heart was so happy that her words came out like a song.","Mary said, “My heart tells how great the Lord is! I am so glad in Him who saves me. He looked kindly on plain, humble me.”","She said that He is strong and holy. He fills hungry people with good things. He lifts up the small and humble.","He keeps the promises He made long ago to Abraham. Now He was keeping them through Mary’s baby, Jesus. After three months, Mary went home.","You can sing a thank-you too. Tonight, name three gifts from Heavenly Father, like your bed and your family. Then hum, “Thank You!”"],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer likes to pause and ponder. Mary stopped to think about all that Heavenly Father had done, and her thinking turned into a thank-you song. In Wren’s world today: At bedtime, Wren looks around her room. She sees her soft blanket, her drawing on the wall, and Dad reading beside her. She whispers, “Heavenly Father gave me all of this.” Then she hums a little thank-you tune. Just like Mary, Wren learned that when we count our blessings, our hearts fill up with thanks.","feel":"Hold up one hand. For each finger, name something you are thankful for, and fold that finger down. Can you show me all five fingers folded into a thank-you fist?","stones":[["SPOT","Mary thought about everything Heavenly Father had done for her. She felt so thankful that she sang."],["NAME","That warm, full, glowing feeling is called thankful. Your mind might think, “I have so many good things.” Your body might feel warm in your chest."],["FIND","When did someone give you something that made you feel full of thanks?"],["FORK","Mary could keep her blessings to herself, or praise Heavenly Father out loud. Mary sang her thanks."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and smile. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for everything You give me.”"]],"remember":"When we count our blessings, our hearts sing thank-you to Heavenly Father!","ask":["“What did Mary do when her heart was full of happiness?”","“What did Mary say Heavenly Father does for hungry people?”","“What three things did Wren see in her room that made her thankful?”"],"share":["Share a blessing you are deeply grateful to Heavenly Father for.","When has Heavenly Father kept a promise to you or your family?","Did a grandparent or ancestor have a favorite hymn or song of thanks? Share a line of it."]},"b13-w1-n5":{"story":["At last Elisabeth had her baby, a little boy! Family and neighbors heard how kind Heavenly Father had been, and they were glad.","On his eighth day, it was time to name him. Everyone said, “Call him Zacharias, like his father.” But Elisabeth said, “No. His name is John.”","The people were puzzled. “Nobody in your family has that name!” They made signs to ask Zacharias.","He still could not talk. So he wrote on a little board, “His name is John.” Right then, he could talk again! He praised Heavenly Father. Everyone wondered what this child would grow up to be.","Zacharias obeyed the angel, and his voice came back. Tonight, use your voice to say something kind to your family."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker looks and sees, and asks, “What could it be?” The neighbors looked at baby John and wondered what he would grow up to be. Scout loves a good wondering. In Scout’s world today: Scout’s family brings home a new baby brother. Everyone guesses what his name will be. Then Dad says, “His name is Eli.” Scout grins at the tiny baby and says, “Hi, Eli! I wonder what you’ll be when you grow up.” Just like Zacharias, Scout learned that when we do what Heavenly Father asks, happy things follow.","feel":"Press your lips together tight, like Zacharias who could not talk. Now open your mouth and say, “His name is John!” Can you show me your happiest whisper?","stones":[["SPOT","Baby John was born, and Zacharias got his voice back. The whole family felt happy and amazed."],["NAME","That smiling, light, everything-is-good feeling is called happy. Your mind might think, “Hooray!” Your body might feel like clapping and grinning."],["FIND","When did something good happen in your family that made everyone smile?"],["FORK","Zacharias could name the baby after himself like everyone wanted, or obey the angel. He obeyed, and he could talk again."],["CALM","Take a slow breath and let a smile come. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for happy days.”"]],"remember":"When we obey Heavenly Father, happy things follow!","ask":["“What name did Elisabeth and Zacharias give their baby?”","“What happened to Zacharias as soon as he wrote the baby’s name?”","“What was the name of Scout’s new baby brother?”"],"share":["Tell your child about the day they joined your family and how you felt.","How did you choose your child’s name? Share what it means to you.","Do you know the story behind a grandparent’s or ancestor’s name? Tell it tonight."]},"b13-w1-n6":{"story":["When Zacharias could talk again, the Holy Ghost filled him. He praised Heavenly Father with words like a happy song.","“Blessed be the Lord!” he said. “He is sending the Savior, just as the prophets promised long ago.”","Then he looked at his tiny baby. “And you, little child, will get people ready for the Lord. You will teach that sins can be forgiven.”","All this comes from Heavenly Father’s tender mercy, His soft and gentle love. The sun comes up each morning and chases the dark away. Zacharias said Jesus would come like that sunrise, bringing light.","Jesus is gentle with us, like the morning sun. Tonight, use soft words with someone who feels sad."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm has a soft, caring heart. Tonight’s song is about Heavenly Father’s tender mercy, and tender is exactly the kind of warm that Willow is. In Willow’s world today: Willow’s little brother spills his milk and starts to cry. Willow does not scold him. She gets a towel, helps him wipe it up, and says softly, “It’s okay. Everybody spills sometimes.” Her brother stops crying and smiles. Just like Zacharias sang, Willow learned that Heavenly Father’s tender mercy is soft and kind, and we can share it.","feel":"Make your hands very soft, like you are holding a baby bird. Now gently pat your own cheek, slow and kind. Can you show me the gentlest touch you have?","stones":[["SPOT","Zacharias sang about Heavenly Father’s tender mercy, like a sunrise. He felt soft, gentle, and full of love."],["NAME","That soft, gentle, careful-with-you feeling is called tender. Your mind might think, “I want to be kind.” Your body might feel calm, with gentle hands."],["FIND","When did someone treat you gently after you made a mistake?"],["FORK","Willow could scold her brother, or be gentle. She chose to be tender, like Heavenly Father is with us."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly, like the sun rising. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for Your tender mercy.”"]],"remember":"Jesus is like the sunrise, bringing gentle light when it feels dark!","ask":["“What did Zacharias do when he could talk again?”","“What did Zacharias say his baby John would grow up to do?”","“How did Willow help her little brother when he spilled his milk?”"],"share":["Share a moment when you felt Heavenly Father’s tender mercy.","When has Jesus brought light into a dark or hard time for you?","Was there a grandparent or ancestor known for being gentle and merciful? Tell about them."]},"b13-w1-n7":{"story":["Joseph was a carpenter who made things from wood. He was going to marry Mary. Then he learned she was going to have a baby, and he was worried.","Joseph was a good, kind man. He did not want to hurt Mary. He thought about quietly ending the wedding plans.","Then an angel came to him in a dream. “Joseph, do not be afraid to marry Mary. Her baby is from the Holy Ghost.”","“Name Him Jesus, for He will save His people from their sins.” When Joseph woke up, he did just what the angel said.","When you feel worried, tell Heavenly Father. He can help you know what to do, just like He helped Joseph."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave has a big heart and a brave start. Joseph was worried, but he was brave enough to obey the angel right away, and that is Beau’s kind of courage. In Beau’s world today: Beau is worried about his first night sleeping at Grandpa’s house without Mom. His tummy feels tight. He says a prayer, then remembers Grandpa’s warm smile. He takes a big breath and says, “I can do this.” Just like Joseph, Beau learned that when we give our worries to Heavenly Father, He helps us be brave.","feel":"Scrunch your shoulders up to your ears like you are worried. Hold it. Now let them drop down, soft and easy. Can you show me your shoulders letting go?","stones":[["SPOT","Joseph did not know what to do about Mary. He felt worried, and then the angel helped him know."],["NAME","That tight, twisty, what-if feeling is called worried. Your mind might think, “What if it goes wrong?” Your body might feel tight in your tummy."],["FIND","When did you feel a what-if worry in your tummy?"],["FORK","Joseph could quietly walk away, or trust the angel and stay with Mary. Joseph obeyed Heavenly Father."],["CALM","Breathe out slowly and let your tummy go soft. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, I give You my worry.”"]],"remember":"When we give our worries to Heavenly Father, He shows us what to do!","ask":["“What did Joseph make with his hands for his job?”","“What name did the angel tell Joseph to give the baby?”","“What helped Beau feel brave about sleeping at Grandpa’s house?”"],"share":["Share a time you felt worried and Heavenly Father showed you what to do.","What does Emmanuel, “Heavenly Father is with us,” mean to you?","Did a grandparent or ancestor ever follow a prompting or dream from Heavenly Father? Tell that story."]},"b13-w2-n1":{"story":["Caesar, a great ruler, made a rule. Everyone had to go to their family’s home town to be counted.","Joseph’s family came from Bethlehem, King David’s town. So Joseph and Mary went from Nazareth to Bethlehem. Mary’s baby was almost ready to come.","Bethlehem was very full. There was no room for them in the inn, where travelers stayed.","In Bethlehem, baby Jesus was born. Mary wrapped Him in soft cloths. She laid Him in a manger, the hay box animals eat from. It was a humble bed for the King of the world.","Jesus came in a simple, quiet way, close to everyone. Tonight, snuggle up and thank Heavenly Father for your cozy place."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm knows that a warm heart makes any place cozy. The stable was not fancy, but with Mary, Joseph, and baby Jesus, it was full of peace and love. In Willow’s world today: The power goes out at Willow’s house, and the rooms get dark. Willow helps Mom light a candle, and the family snuggles under one big blanket. Willow smiles and says, “It’s still cozy, because we’re all together.” Just like Mary and Joseph, Willow learned that peace comes from love, not from fancy things.","feel":"Curl up small under your blanket like baby Jesus wrapped in soft cloths. Breathe slow and easy. Can you show me how still and peaceful you can be?","stones":[["SPOT","There was no room for Mary and Joseph. But in the quiet stable, with baby Jesus, they felt peaceful."],["NAME","That calm, cozy, everything-is-okay feeling is called peaceful. Your mind might think, “I am safe.” Your body might feel soft and slow."],["FIND","When did you feel peaceful, like snuggling in bed after a long day?"],["FORK","Mary and Joseph could grumble that there was no room, or be thankful for a quiet place. They made it a place of peace."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your whole body sink into bed. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for peace.”"]],"remember":"Jesus came to a simple stable so He could be close to everyone, even you!","ask":["“Why did Mary and Joseph have to walk all the way to Bethlehem?”","“Where did Mary lay baby Jesus after He was born?”","“What did Willow’s family do when the power went out?”"],"share":["Share a time your family felt peace in a simple or hard place.","What does it mean to you that Jesus was born in a stable, not a palace?","Did a grandparent or ancestor live simply but with great faith? Tell your child about their home."]},"b13-w2-n2":{"story":["That night, shepherds were in the fields near Bethlehem, keeping their sheep safe.","Suddenly an angel came, and bright light shone all around! The shepherds were afraid. The angel said, “Fear not! I bring good news of great joy.”","“Today a Savior is born, Christ the Lord. You will find Him wrapped in cloths, lying in a manger.”","Many angels praised, “Glory to Heavenly Father, and on earth peace!” When they went back to heaven, the shepherds said, “Let’s go see!” They hurried and found Mary, Joseph, and baby Jesus. Then they told everyone.","At first the shepherds were afraid. Then the good news filled them with joy. Tonight, tell someone the happy news: Jesus was born!"],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see, what could it be?” The shepherds heard the angel and said, “Let’s go see!” That is Scout’s favorite thing to say. In Scout’s world today: Scout hears a strange noise in the backyard at night. His heart thumps. Then Dad says, “Let’s go look together.” With the flashlight on, they find a little owl in the tree. Scout’s fear turns into a big smile. Just like the shepherds, Scout learned that when we go and see what Heavenly Father shows us, fear turns into joy.","feel":"Cover your eyes with your hands like the shepherds hiding from the bright light. Now peek through your fingers and open them wide. Can you show me your go-and-see eyes?","stones":[["SPOT","The angel came with bright light, and the shepherds felt afraid. Then they heard good news and felt joy."],["NAME","That jumpy, want-to-hide feeling is called afraid. Your mind might think, “What is that?” Your body might feel your heart pound and your legs want to run."],["FIND","When did something sudden, like a loud noise, make you feel afraid?"],["FORK","The shepherds could hide in the field, or go and see the baby. They hurried to Bethlehem."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel your blanket around you. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me not be afraid.”"]],"remember":"Fear not! Jesus is good news of great joy for everyone, even you!","ask":["“Who was watching sheep in the fields when the angel came?”","“What did the shepherds do after the angels went back to heaven?”","“What did Scout and his dad find in the backyard tree?”"],"share":["Share a time you felt afraid and Heavenly Father helped you be brave.","What is the “good news of great joy” about Jesus that you want your child to know?","Did a grandparent or ancestor have a favorite Christmas story or tradition about the shepherds?"]},"b13-w2-n3":{"story":["Mary and Joseph took baby Jesus to the temple in Jerusalem. There they gave thanks for Him.","A good old man named Simeon lived in Jerusalem. The Holy Ghost had promised he would see the Savior before he died. He waited and waited.","That day the Holy Ghost led Simeon to the temple. He took baby Jesus in his arms. “Now I can go in peace,” he said. “I have seen Your Savior!”","An old woman named Anna prayed in the temple day and night. When she saw Jesus, she gave thanks. She told others about Him.","Simeon and Anna waited a long time, and Heavenly Father kept His promise. When you wait, keep trusting Him."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer says, “Pause and ponder.” Simeon and Anna waited quietly for years, trusting Heavenly Father’s promise, and Wren understands that kind of patient wondering. In Wren’s world today: Wren plants a bean seed in a cup. Every morning she looks. Nothing. Still nothing. She waters it anyway and waits. On the ninth day, a tiny green sprout pokes up! Wren whispers, “It was growing the whole time.” Just like Simeon and Anna, Wren learned that Heavenly Father keeps His promises, and waiting is worth it.","feel":"Lie still and count slowly to ten in your head, like Simeon waiting for Jesus. Breathe softly while you count. Can you show me how patiently you can wait?","stones":[["SPOT","Simeon and Anna waited many years to see the Savior. They felt patient, and then full of joy."],["NAME","That quiet, keep-trusting, not-yet feeling is called patient. Your mind might think, “It will come.” Your body might feel still and calm."],["FIND","When did you have to wait a long time for something good?"],["FORK","Simeon could give up waiting, or keep trusting Heavenly Father’s promise. He kept trusting, and he saw Jesus."],["CALM","Take one slow breath in and one slow breath out. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, I will wait with You.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father keeps His promises, so we can wait with a happy heart!","ask":["“Where did Mary and Joseph take baby Jesus to give thanks?”","“What did Simeon say when he held baby Jesus in his arms?”","“What did Wren find in her cup on the ninth day?”"],"share":["Share a promise from Heavenly Father that took a long time to come true.","How has waiting for something helped your faith grow stronger?","Was there a grandparent or ancestor who waited faithfully for something? Tell about their patience."]},"b13-w2-n4":{"story":["Wise men in the east saw a new star. It meant a new King was born. They came a long way to worship Him.","King Herod heard about them and was upset. He wanted to be the only king. He said, “Find the child, then come tell me.” But Herod did not mean well.","The star went ahead and stopped over the house where Jesus was. The wise men were so happy! They bowed down and worshipped Him.","They gave Jesus treasures: gold, and sweet-smelling frankincense and myrrh. Then Heavenly Father warned them in a dream, so they went home another way.","The wise men gave Jesus their best. Tonight, give Jesus a gift too, like a kind word or a helping hand."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker loves to look up and ask, “What could it be?” The wise men looked up, saw a new star, and followed it all the way to Jesus. In Scout’s world today: Scout’s family goes on a treasure hunt in the park. He follows the clues, one by one. His heart races as he gets closer. Under the big tree he finds the prize, and he shouts, “I found it!” Just like the wise men, Scout learned that when we keep seeking Jesus, we find Him.","feel":"Lie on your back and point up at the ceiling like you see a bright star. Follow it slowly with your finger across the sky. Can you show me where your star stops?","stones":[["SPOT","The wise men saw the star stop over the house. They felt excited and full of joy to find Jesus."],["NAME","That bubbly, can’t-wait, almost-there feeling is called excited. Your mind might think, “We’re so close!” Your body might feel wiggly, with fast breath."],["FIND","When were you so excited that you could not wait another minute?"],["FORK","The wise men could go back and tell Herod, or obey Heavenly Father’s dream. They obeyed and went home another way."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your wiggles settle. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for leading me.”"]],"remember":"When we keep looking for Jesus, we find Him!","ask":["“What did the wise men see in the sky that made them travel so far?”","“What three gifts did the wise men give to Jesus?”","“Where did Scout find the prize at the end of the treasure hunt?”"],"share":["Share a time you followed a prompting and it led you to something good.","What is the best gift you can give Jesus right now in your life?","Did a grandparent or ancestor travel far to follow their faith? Tell about their journey."]},"b13-w2-n5":{"story":["After the wise men left, an angel came to Joseph in a dream. “Get up! Take the child and His mother to Egypt. King Herod wants to hurt Him.”","Egypt was a land far away. Joseph did not wait until morning. He left that very night with Mary and Jesus.","They stayed in Egypt, safe and sound, until King Herod died.","Then the angel came again. “It is safe now. Go home.” So Joseph brought his family back to Nazareth, where Jesus would grow up.","Joseph obeyed right away, and Heavenly Father kept Jesus safe. Tonight, when Mom or Dad asks you to do something, obey right away."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave has a big heart and a brave start. Joseph got up in the middle of the night and did a hard thing right away to keep his family safe. That is Beau’s kind of brave. In Beau’s world today: A storm rolls in while Beau is playing outside. Dad calls, “Come inside, now!” Beau does not wait or argue. He runs inside right away. Then he watches the rain from the window, warm and safe. Just like Joseph, Beau learned that obeying quickly keeps us safe in Heavenly Father’s care.","feel":"Pull your blanket up to your chin and wrap it tight around you, like a safe little tent. Take a slow breath inside. Can you show me your safe and cozy face?","stones":[["SPOT","Joseph heard the angel’s warning and left right away. Mary and Jesus felt safe because Joseph obeyed."],["NAME","That warm, protected, nothing-can-get-me feeling is called safe. Your mind might think, “Someone is watching over me.” Your body might feel relaxed and cozy."],["FIND","When did you feel safe, like being held or tucked in tight?"],["FORK","Joseph could wait until morning, or go right away. He obeyed that very night."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel your bed holding you. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for keeping me safe.”"]],"remember":"When we obey right away, Heavenly Father keeps us safe!","ask":["“Where did the angel tell Joseph to take Mary and Jesus?”","“Did Joseph wait until morning, or go right away?”","“What did Beau do when Dad called him inside from the storm?”"],"share":["Share a time you obeyed a prompting quickly and were kept safe.","How have you felt Heavenly Father watching over your family?","Did a grandparent or ancestor move far away to keep their family safe? Tell that story."]},"b13-w2-n6":{"story":["After their visit to the temple, Mary and Joseph took Jesus home to Nazareth. There Jesus grew up.","Joseph made things from wood, and Jesus learned to be a carpenter too. He helped His family, the way you help yours.","Jesus grew strong. He grew wise, learning more and more of what is true and good. And Heavenly Father’s love was with Him.","Jesus obeyed Mary and Joseph. He grew taller, and He grew wiser. Heavenly Father loved Him, and the people who knew Him loved Him too.","You are growing too, a little more every day. Tonight, think of one way you helped your family today. Heavenly Father’s love is with you while you grow."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” Jesus grew up in a home full of love, and Willow knows that love is what helps us grow. In Willow’s world today: Willow helps Dad set the table. She puts a fork by every plate, one by one. Dad says, “You’re growing up so well, Willow.” She feels warm all over and stands a little taller. Just like Jesus, Willow learned that we grow best when we are loved and when we help.","feel":"Lie flat and stretch your arms up over your head and your toes down, as long as you can. You are growing! Can you show me how tall you can stretch?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus grew up in Nazareth, helping and obeying. Heavenly Father’s love was with Him, and He felt loved."],["NAME","That warm, wrapped-up, someone-cares-for-me feeling is called loved. Your mind might think, “I belong here.” Your body might feel warm and soft."],["FIND","When did someone help you learn something new and you felt loved?"],["FORK","Jesus could do only what He wanted, or help and obey His family. Jesus chose to help and obey."],["CALM","Breathe in and feel your blanket hug you. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for loving me.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father’s love helps us grow strong and kind, just like Jesus!","ask":["“What town did Jesus grow up in?”","“What job did Joseph teach Jesus to do?”","“How did Willow help her dad at dinnertime?”"],"share":["Share something you learned as a child that helped you grow in faith.","How have you felt Heavenly Father’s love while you were growing and learning?","What did a grandparent or ancestor teach your family about working and helping at home?"]},"b13-w2-n7":{"story":["When Jesus was twelve, His family went to Jerusalem for the Passover. It was a holiday to remember how Heavenly Father saved His people.","On the way home, they thought Jesus was in the big group. But at day’s end, He was not there!","They hurried back and looked for three days. At last they found Jesus in the temple. He was listening to the teachers and asking questions.","Everyone was amazed at what He understood. Mary said, “We were so worried!” Jesus said, “I must be doing My Father’s work.” He meant Heavenly Father. Then He went home and obeyed them.","Mary and Joseph kept looking until they found Jesus. Tonight, you can look for Him too, when you pray."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer loves to ask questions and listen, just like twelve-year-old Jesus in the temple. And Wren knows the big whoosh of relief when something lost is found. In Wren’s world today: Wren cannot find her favorite stuffed bunny anywhere. She looks under the bed and in the closet. Her chest feels tight. Then Mom finds Bunny behind the couch, and Wren lets out a big, happy breath. “There you are!” Just like Mary and Joseph, Wren learned that the whoosh of relief is a gift, and Heavenly Father helps us find what we are looking for.","feel":"Take in a big, worried breath and hold it. Now let it all whoosh out, long and slow, like finding something you lost. Can you show me your biggest whoosh?","stones":[["SPOT","Mary and Joseph could not find Jesus for three days. When they found Him in the temple, they felt relieved."],["NAME","That whoosh, all-better, I-can-breathe-again feeling is called relieved. Your mind might think, “Oh good!” Your body might feel loose and light."],["FIND","When did you lose something and then feel a big whoosh when you found it?"],["FORK","Mary and Joseph could give up looking, or keep searching. They kept looking until they found Jesus."],["CALM","Let out one long, slow breath. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for helping me find what I need.”"]],"remember":"Jesus loved being in Heavenly Father’s house, and we can love it, too!","ask":["“Where did Mary and Joseph finally find Jesus?”","“What was Jesus doing with the teachers in the temple?”","“Where did Wren’s mom find her stuffed bunny?”"],"share":["Share a time you felt relief after Heavenly Father helped you find your way.","What do you love about being in Heavenly Father’s house?","Did a grandparent or ancestor love the temple or church? Share a memory of them there."]},"b13-w3-n1":{"story":["John was one of Jesus’s best friends. He wrote a book about Jesus. It begins, “In the beginning was the Word.”","The Word is a name for Jesus. Our words show what is in our hearts. Jesus shows us Heavenly Father’s heart.","Before the world was made, Jesus was with Heavenly Father. Jesus helped Him make everything: the stars, the sea, and the trees.","John wrote that Jesus is like a light. When it is dark, a light shows the way. Jesus’s light shines in the dark.","Then Jesus came to earth with a real body, just like yours. He lived with people, full of kindness and truth. Tonight, when your light goes off, whisper, “Jesus is my light.”"],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer says, “Pause and ponder, what a wonder!” Tonight’s story is the biggest wonder of all: Jesus helped make the world, and then He came to live in it. In Wren’s world today: Wren lies on the grass at night with Grandpa and looks up at the stars. There are so many! Grandpa says, “Jesus helped make every one.” Wren is quiet for a long time. Then she whispers, “Wow.” Just like John, Wren learned that Jesus is the light who made the world and came to be with us.","feel":"Cup your hands together in the dark, then slowly open them like a light turning on. Open them wider and wider. Can you show me your light shining?","stones":[["SPOT","John told about Jesus making the world and coming to earth. The people who knew Jesus felt wonder."],["NAME","That big, hushed, eyes-wide feeling is called wonder. Your mind might think, “Wow, how can it be?” Your body might feel still, with a quiet gasp."],["FIND","When did you see something so big or beautiful that you said, “Wow”?"],["FORK","People could walk in the dark, or follow the light of Jesus. John chose to follow the light."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and picture a soft light glowing. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for sending Jesus, my light.”"]],"remember":"Jesus is the light of the world, and the dark can never put Him out!","ask":["“Who helped Heavenly Father make the stars, the sea, and the trees?”","“What is Jesus like when it is dark and we cannot see?”","“What did Wren and Grandpa look at in the night sky?”"],"share":["Share a time Jesus was a light for you when things felt dark.","What does it mean to you that Jesus, who made the world, came to live with us?","Did a grandparent or ancestor love the night sky, nature, or creation? Share a memory."]},"b13-w3-n2":{"story":["Baby John grew up to be John the Baptist. Heavenly Father sent him with a job to do.","John’s job was to tell everyone about the Light, and the Light is Jesus. John was not the Light. He pointed to Jesus, like a friend pointing to the sunrise.","Jesus came into the world He had made. But many people did not know who He was. Some would not let Him in.","But some people believed in Jesus and let Him into their hearts. To all of them, Jesus gave a gift: power to become Heavenly Father’s children.","When we believe in Jesus and follow Him, we belong in Heavenly Father’s family. Tonight, say softly, “I am Heavenly Father’s child. I belong to Him.”"],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm knows that the best feeling is belonging to a family that loves you. Tonight’s story says that everyone who believes in Jesus belongs to Heavenly Father’s family. In Willow’s world today: Willow is new at church and does not know anyone. A girl walks over, takes her hand, and says, “Come sit with us.” Willow sits down in the row, and her heart feels warm. She belongs here. Just like John taught, Willow learned that we all belong in Heavenly Father’s family.","feel":"Wrap your arms around yourself and squeeze, like a big family hug. Now hold the hand of the person next to you. Can you show me your belonging squeeze?","stones":[["SPOT","John taught that everyone who believes in Jesus becomes Heavenly Father’s child. The people who believed felt like they belonged."],["NAME","That you-are-part-of-us, I-have-a-place feeling is called belonging. Your mind might think, “This is my family.” Your body might feel warm and settled."],["FIND","When did someone say, “Come sit with us,” and you felt like you belonged?"],["FORK","People could turn away from Jesus, or receive Him. Those who received Him became Heavenly Father’s children."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and put your hand on your heart. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that I am Your child.”"]],"remember":"When we believe in Jesus, we belong in Heavenly Father’s family!","ask":["“What was John the Baptist’s job?”","“What gift did Jesus give to everyone who believed in Him?”","“What did the girl at church say to Willow?”"],"share":["Share a time you felt that you truly belonged to Heavenly Father.","How does knowing you are Heavenly Father’s child change the way you live?","How did a grandparent or ancestor help your family feel like they belonged to Heavenly Father?"]},"b13-w3-n3":{"story":["John the Baptist taught by the Jordan River. He told people to repent. To repent is to stop doing wrong and come back to Heavenly Father.","Leaders in Jerusalem sent priests to ask him, “Who are you?” John told the truth. “I am not the Christ,” he said. He was not the promised Savior.","Isaiah had said a voice would call out in the wild, empty land. John said, “I am that voice.”","“Make the way ready for the Lord,” John said. “I baptize with water. But Someone much greater is coming.”","John said he was not good enough even to untie His shoes. He wanted people to look for Jesus. Tonight, be honest like John, and tell someone about Jesus."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker loves to find out who people really are. The priests came seeking answers, and John told them the honest truth: “I am not the Christ. I point to Him.” In Scout’s world today: Scout helps his big sister build a huge block tower. When Grandma says, “Scout, what a tower!” Scout says, “My sister did most of it. She’s really good at building.” His sister beams, and Scout feels glad inside. Just like John, Scout learned that being humble means pointing to someone greater instead of to yourself.","feel":"Point your finger at yourself. Now slowly turn it and point up toward heaven, like John pointing to Jesus. Can you show me your pointing-to-Jesus finger?","stones":[["SPOT","The priests asked John if he was the Christ. John said no. He felt humble, happy to point to Jesus."],["NAME","That small-but-happy, let-someone-else-shine feeling is called humble. Your mind might think, “This isn’t about me.” Your body might feel quiet and calm."],["FIND","When did you let someone else get the praise, and it felt good?"],["FORK","John could say he was important, or tell the truth and point to Jesus. John told the truth."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your shoulders rest. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me be humble like John.”"]],"remember":"Being humble means pointing to Jesus instead of to ourselves!","ask":["“What did John say when the priests asked if he was the Christ?”","“What does the word repent mean?”","“What did Scout say when Grandma praised the block tower?”"],"share":["Share a time you chose to be humble and give the credit to someone else.","How do you try to point your children to Jesus instead of to yourself?","Was there a grandparent or ancestor who served quietly without wanting praise? Tell about them."]},"b13-w3-n4":{"story":["One day John the Baptist saw Jesus coming toward him. John called out, “Look! The Lamb of God, who takes away the sin of the world!”","Why a Lamb? Long ago, people gave Heavenly Father a perfect little lamb. It showed they were sorry for the wrong things they did.","Jesus is Heavenly Father’s perfect gift. He takes away our wrong things when we are sorry.","John told everyone what he had seen. The Holy Ghost came down from heaven like a dove and stayed on Jesus. That was the sign Heavenly Father had promised John.","So John spoke up: “This is the Son of God!” Tonight, tell someone you love Jesus, just like John did."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave has a big heart and a brave start. John called out the truth about Jesus in front of everyone with a bold, strong voice, and that takes a brave heart. In Beau’s world today: At the park, a kid says, “Praying is silly.” Beau’s cheeks get hot. He takes a breath and says kindly but strongly, “I pray every night, and Heavenly Father listens.” Then he goes back to playing, feeling quiet and strong inside. Just like John, Beau learned that we can bravely and reverently say who Jesus is.","feel":"Fold your arms gently and bow your head, like you are in a quiet, holy place. Breathe softly. Can you show me your most reverent, still body?","stones":[["SPOT","John saw Jesus and called Him the Lamb of Heavenly Father. The people felt reverence, quiet and holy."],["NAME","That quiet, holy, hush-now feeling is called reverence. Your mind might think, “This is special and true.” Your body might feel still and soft."],["FIND","When have you felt quiet and holy inside, like in a prayer or at church?"],["FORK","John could keep quiet, or boldly tell everyone who Jesus was. John spoke up."],["CALM","Take one slow, quiet breath. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for sending Jesus, Your Son.”"]],"remember":"Jesus is the Lamb Heavenly Father sent to take away our sins!","ask":["“What did John call Jesus when he saw Him walking toward him?”","“What did John see come down from heaven and rest on Jesus?”","“What did Beau say when a kid said praying was silly?”"],"share":["Share your testimony that Jesus is the Lamb Heavenly Father sent for you.","When have you felt the Holy Ghost confirm that Jesus is the Son of Heavenly Father?","Did a grandparent or ancestor share their testimony of Jesus with your family? Tell what they said."]},"b13-w3-n5":{"story":["The next day John the Baptist stood with two friends who learned from him. One of the two was named Andrew.","Jesus walked by. John looked at Him and said, “Look! The Lamb of God!” The two friends heard him, and they followed Jesus down the road.","Jesus turned around and saw them following. He asked, “What are you looking for?”","They said, “Teacher, where do You live?” They wanted to know Him better. Jesus said, “Come and see.”","So they went with Him and saw where He lived. They stayed with Jesus the rest of that day. Jesus still says, “Come and see.” Tonight, tell Him one thing you want to learn about Him."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see, what could it be?” When Jesus said, “Come and see,” Andrew followed right away. That is exactly what Scout would do. In Scout’s world today: Scout’s teacher says, “I have something amazing in this box. Who wants to come and see?” Scout is the first one up. Inside is a butterfly just coming out of its cocoon. Scout stares and whispers, “I want to know everything about it.” Just like Andrew, Scout learned that when Jesus says, “Come and see,” the best thing to do is follow.","feel":"Put your hand over your eyes like you are looking far away down a road. Now lean forward like you are following someone. Can you show me your come-and-see lean?","stones":[["SPOT","Andrew and his friend followed Jesus and asked where He lived. They felt curious and eager to know Him."],["NAME","That I-want-to-know-more, leaning-in feeling is called curious. Your mind might think, “What will I find?” Your body might feel your eyes get wide and your feet want to go."],["FIND","When did you want to find out about something so much that you hurried to see?"],["FORK","Andrew could stay where he was, or follow Jesus down the road. He followed, and Jesus welcomed him."],["CALM","Take a slow breath and let your body settle. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me come and see Jesus every day.”"]],"remember":"Jesus says, “Come and see,” and He is waiting for you to follow!","ask":["“What did Jesus say when the two disciples asked where He lived?”","“What did the disciples do for the rest of that day?”","“What did Scout see inside the teacher’s box?”"],"share":["Share a time you accepted Jesus’s invitation to come and see, and what you found.","What have you learned about Jesus by spending time with Him in prayer and scripture?","Did a grandparent or ancestor ever invite someone to come and see the gospel? Tell what happened."]},"b13-w3-n6":{"story":["Andrew had spent the whole day with Jesus. His heart was too full to keep it inside. The first thing he did was find his brother, Simon.","Andrew said, “We have found the Messiah!” That was the Savior Heavenly Father had promised to send. Everyone had been waiting for Him.","Andrew did not just tell Simon. He brought Simon to Jesus, so Simon could meet Him too.","Jesus looked at Simon and said, “You are Simon. You will be called Cephas.” Cephas means a stone, like a big, strong rock. We know him as Peter.","Jesus could see that Simon would grow strong and steady, like a rock. When you find something wonderful, share it! Tonight, think of someone you can tell about Jesus."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” Andrew’s heart was so warm from being with Jesus that he ran to share it with his brother. Willow loves sharing good things with people she loves. In Willow’s world today: Willow learns a new song at church that makes her heart happy. On the way home she can hardly wait. She runs to her little brother and says, “Come here, I have to teach you this song!” They sing it together until bedtime. Just like Andrew, Willow learned that good news about Jesus is meant to be shared.","feel":"Lie still, then let your legs run in place under the blanket, like Andrew running to find his brother. Now stop and smile. Can you show me your running-to-share legs?","stones":[["SPOT","Andrew found Jesus and ran to tell his brother. He felt eager to share the good news."],["NAME","That can’t-wait, I-have-to-tell-you feeling is called eager. Your mind might think, “You have to hear this!” Your body might feel quick and bouncy."],["FIND","When did you learn something so good that you ran to tell someone?"],["FORK","Andrew could keep Jesus to himself, or bring his brother to meet Him. Andrew brought Simon to Jesus."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your legs rest. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me share Jesus with the people I love.”"]],"remember":"When we find Jesus, the best thing to do is bring someone else to Him!","ask":["“Who did Andrew run to tell about Jesus?”","“What new name did Jesus give Simon, and what does it mean?”","“What did Willow teach her little brother when she got home from church?”"],"share":["Share a time someone brought you closer to Jesus, and how it changed you.","Who in your life is waiting for you to share the gospel, like Andrew shared with Peter?","Which grandparent or ancestor first brought the gospel to your family? Tell their story."]},"b13-w3-n7":{"story":["Jesus said to a man named Philip, “Follow Me.” Philip did, and he hurried to find his friend Nathanael.","“We have found Him!” said Philip. “He is the One the prophets wrote about. He is Jesus from Nazareth.”","Nathanael was not sure. “Can anything good come out of Nazareth?” he asked. Philip did not argue. He just said, “Come and see.”","When Jesus saw Nathanael coming, He said, “Here is an honest man.” Nathanael was surprised. “How do You know me?” Jesus said, “I saw you under the fig tree, before Philip called you.”","Nathanael’s doubt turned to belief. He said, “Teacher, You are the Son of God!” Jesus knows you too, even when you are alone. Tonight, tell Him what is in your heart."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer pauses to think before she decides. Nathanael was not sure at first, but he was honest and came to see, and then he believed. Wren likes honest thinking like that. In Wren’s world today: Wren’s cousin says the new library has a reading tree house. Wren frowns. “I don’t think that’s real.” Her cousin says, “Come and see!” At the library, there it is, a real tree house full of books! Wren laughs. “I’m glad I came.” Just like Nathanael, Wren learned that it is okay to wonder, as long as we come and see for ourselves.","feel":"Scrunch up your nose and tilt your head like you are not so sure. Now nod your head slowly, yes, yes, yes. Can you show me your I-believe nod?","stones":[["SPOT","Nathanael was not sure anything good could come from Nazareth. He felt doubt, and then he came and believed."],["NAME","That hmm, I’m-not-so-sure feeling is called doubt. Your mind might think, “Is that really true?” Your body might feel a frown and crossed arms."],["FIND","When were you not sure about something, and then you found out it was true?"],["FORK","Nathanael could stay home with his doubt, or come and see Jesus. He came, and his doubt turned to faith."],["CALM","Take a slow breath and let your face soften. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me come and see, even when I’m not sure.”"]],"remember":"Jesus sees you and knows you, even when no one else is there!","ask":["“Where was Nathanael sitting when Jesus saw him?”","“What did Philip say when Nathanael was not sure about Jesus?”","“What did Wren find at the new library?”"],"share":["Share a time you had doubts, came to see for yourself, and found your faith.","When have you felt that Jesus saw you and knew you, even when you were alone?","Did a grandparent or ancestor ever doubt and then gain a testimony? Tell how it happened."]},"b13-w4-n1":{"story":["John the Baptist lived in the wild, empty land by the Jordan River. He wore rough camel’s hair and a leather belt. He ate wild honey and big grasshoppers called locusts.","Heavenly Father gave John a job: get people ready for Jesus. John called out, “Repent!” To repent is to turn from wrong and come back to Heavenly Father.","Long before, the prophet Isaiah said a voice would call, “Make the way ready!” Isaiah was talking about John.","Back then, people fixed bumpy roads before a king came. John helped people smooth the bumpy places in their hearts for Jesus.","People came from all around. They said sorry, and John baptized them in the river. Tonight, you can get your heart ready for Jesus too."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker loves an adventure in wild places. John lived in the wilderness, wore camel hair, ate honey, and did his job with all his might. Scout thinks that is the best kind of determined. In Scout’s world today: Scout is picking up every toy in the playroom before Grandma comes. It is a big job. His sister wants to quit, but Scout says, “We’re getting ready for Grandma! Let’s keep going.” They finish just as the doorbell rings. Just like John, Scout learned that getting ready for someone we love takes a determined heart.","feel":"March your feet under the covers like John walking through the desert. Keep going, keep going. Now stop and take a breath. Can you show me your keep-going march?","stones":[["SPOT","John had a big job: get people ready for Jesus. He felt determined, and he did not quit."],["NAME","That I-will-do-this, don’t-quit feeling is called determined. Your mind might think, “Keep going!” Your body might feel strong and steady."],["FIND","When did you keep working on something hard until it was done?"],["FORK","John could live an easy life, or do the job Heavenly Father gave him. John chose to prepare the way."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your feet rest. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me get my heart ready for Jesus.”"]],"remember":"We can make a straight path in our hearts for Jesus to come in!","ask":["“What did John the Baptist wear, and what did he eat?”","“What was John’s job from Heavenly Father?”","“What were Scout and his sister getting ready for?”"],"share":["Share a time you prepared your heart for Jesus and felt Him come closer.","What bumpy place in your life has Heavenly Father helped you make straight?","Was there a grandparent or ancestor who worked hard and never gave up? Tell your child about them."]},"b13-w4-n2":{"story":["Many people came to the river to be baptized by John. He said, “Show that you have really changed.” A good tree shows it is good by its fruit.","The people asked John, “Then what should we do?” He said, “Two coats? Give one to someone who has none. Share your food, too.”","Tax collectors came too. They gathered money for the rulers, and some took extra for themselves. John said, “Take only what is fair.”","Soldiers asked, “And what should we do?” John said, “Do not hurt anyone or tell lies about them. Be happy with your pay.”","A changed heart shows in kind hands. Tonight, think of one thing you can share tomorrow."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” John told the people to share their coats and their food, and sharing is one of Willow’s favorite ways to show love. In Willow’s world today: It is snack time at preschool, and a boy at Willow’s table forgot his snack. His face looks sad. Willow breaks her granola bar in half and slides one piece over. “Here, we can share.” The boy smiles, and Willow’s heart feels full. Just like John taught, Willow learned that a loving heart shows up in sharing hands.","feel":"Pretend to hold a cozy coat in your arms. Now reach out and hand it to someone who is cold. Can you show me your giving hands?","stones":[["SPOT","John told the people to share coats and food and be fair. The ones who listened felt generous."],["NAME","That open-hands, I-want-to-give feeling is called generous. Your mind might think, “You can have some of mine.” Your body might feel warm and light."],["FIND","When did you share something you really liked, and it made you feel good?"],["FORK","The people could keep both coats, or give one away. John taught them to give."],["CALM","Open your hands on your blanket, palms up. Breathe slowly. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me share.”"]],"remember":"A loving heart shows up in sharing hands!","ask":["“What did John say to do if you have two coats?”","“What did John tell the tax collectors to do?”","“What did Willow share with the boy who forgot his snack?”"],"share":["Share a time you gave something away and felt Heavenly Father’s joy.","How do you try to show a changed heart through your hands and actions?","Did a grandparent or ancestor give generously, even when they had little? Tell about them."]},"b13-w4-n3":{"story":["The people loved to listen to John. They wondered, “Could John be the Christ, the Savior we are waiting for?”","John knew what they were thinking. He said, “I baptize you with water. But Someone much greater than I am is coming.”","John said, “I am not good enough even to untie His shoes.” Back then, untying dusty shoes was the very smallest job a helper did.","So Jesus is far, far greater than John. John said Jesus would baptize with the Holy Ghost and with fire. The Holy Ghost fills hearts with light and makes them clean.","John kept teaching, always pointing to Jesus. Tonight, fold your arms and sit still. Feel how great Jesus is."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer says, “Pause and ponder, what a wonder!” John stopped to think about how great Jesus is, and his respect for Jesus filled every word he said. In Wren’s world today: At church, Wren watches her grandpa stand up and speak about Jesus in a soft, strong voice. Everyone is quiet. Wren folds her hands and sits very still. She thinks, “Grandpa loves Jesus so much.” Just like John, Wren learned that respect means knowing how great Jesus is and treating Him that way.","feel":"Pretend to kneel down and untie a sandal, slow and careful. Now sit up tall and bow your head. Can you show me your respect bow?","stones":[["SPOT","The people wondered if John was the Christ. John said Jesus was far greater. John felt deep respect for Jesus."],["NAME","That He-is-so-great, I-am-honored feeling is called respect. Your mind might think, “He is greater than me.” Your body might feel a bowed head and still hands."],["FIND","When have you felt respect for someone great, like a grandparent or a teacher?"],["FORK","John could let people think he was the Savior, or tell them about Jesus. John pointed to Jesus."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly with your head bowed. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me honor Jesus.”"]],"remember":"Jesus is greater than anyone, and we can honor Him with respect!","ask":["“What did John say he was not good enough to do for Jesus?”","“What did John say Jesus would baptize people with?”","“What did Wren watch her grandpa do at church?”"],"share":["Share a moment when you felt deep respect and awe for Jesus.","How has the Holy Ghost filled your heart with light?","Was there a grandparent or ancestor who showed great respect for sacred things? Tell about them."]},"b13-w4-n4":{"story":["Jesus came from Nazareth to the Jordan River. He asked John to baptize Him. John was surprised. “I need You to baptize me!” he said.","Jesus said, “Let it be so. It is right to do all that is good.” Jesus had never done anything wrong. But He wanted to obey Heavenly Father in everything.","So John baptized Jesus, under the water and up again.","When Jesus came up, the heavens opened. The Holy Ghost came down like a dove and rested on Him. A voice said, “This is My beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased.”","It was Heavenly Father, happy with His dearly loved Son. He is happy when you obey too. Tonight, tell Him one way you will obey tomorrow."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big heart, brave start!” Jesus bravely went first and showed us the way to be baptized, and Heavenly Father said He was pleased. That is the best well done ever. In Beau’s world today: Beau is at the pool, and the water is cold. The swim teacher says, “Who will go first?” Beau’s heart pounds, but he climbs in and puts his face in the water. Dad calls, “Well done, Beau!” Beau feels tall and warm inside. Just like Jesus, Beau learned that when we obey Heavenly Father, He is pleased with us.","feel":"Take a big breath and slowly sink down under your blanket. Now rise up and stretch your arms to the sky, like coming up out of the water. Can you show me your rising up?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus was baptized, and Heavenly Father said He was well pleased. Jesus felt loved and proud to obey."],["NAME","That warm, standing-tall, well-done feeling is called proud. Your mind might think, “I did the right thing.” Your body might feel tall, with a big breath."],["FIND","When did someone say well done to you, and you felt tall inside?"],["FORK","Jesus could skip baptism, since He had done nothing wrong, or obey Heavenly Father in everything. Jesus obeyed."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and stand tall in your heart. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, I want to make You happy.”"]],"remember":"When we obey Heavenly Father, He is pleased and says well done!","ask":["“Who baptized Jesus in the Jordan River?”","“What did Heavenly Father’s voice say when Jesus came up out of the water?”","“What brave thing did Beau do at the pool?”"],"share":["Share what your own baptism meant to you, or what you hope it will mean to your child.","When have you felt Heavenly Father was pleased with a choice you made?","Tell about a grandparent’s or ancestor’s baptism, or how they followed Jesus’s example."]},"b13-w4-n5":{"story":["Jesus went to Galilee, by a big lake. He taught, “Turn back to Heavenly Father, and believe the good news!”","By the water, Jesus saw two brothers, Simon and Andrew. They were fishermen, throwing their net into the lake. Jesus said, “Come, follow Me. I will make you fishers of men.”","What did He mean? They gathered fish into nets. Now Jesus would teach them to gather people to Heavenly Father.","Right away, Simon and Andrew left their nets and followed Him. Then Jesus saw James and John fixing nets in a boat with their father.","Jesus called them too. Right away they left the boat and went with Him. Tonight, think of one way you can follow Jesus tomorrow."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker is always ready to go. When Jesus said, “Come, follow Me,” the fishermen said yes right away, and Scout loves a right-away yes. In Scout’s world today: Scout is building a fort when Mom calls, “Who wants to help me take dinner to the Garcias?” Scout puts down his blocks and says, “I will!” On the way, he gets to carry the warm bread. It smells amazing. Just like the fishermen, Scout learned that being willing means saying yes to Jesus right away.","feel":"Pretend to throw a big fishing net out over your blanket. Now pull it back in, hand over hand. Can you show me how you drop the net and follow?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus called the fishermen, and they left their nets right away. They felt willing to follow Him."],["NAME","That ready, yes-I’ll-come feeling is called willing. Your mind might think, “I’ll do it!” Your body might feel your feet ready to go."],["FIND","When did someone ask you to help, and you said yes right away?"],["FORK","The fishermen could stay with their boats, or follow Jesus right away. They went with Jesus."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your feet relax. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, I am willing to follow Jesus.”"]],"remember":"When Jesus calls, we can say yes right away!","ask":["“What were Simon and Andrew doing when Jesus called them?”","“What did Jesus say He would make the fishermen into?”","“What did Scout help his mom take to the Garcias?”"],"share":["Share a time you dropped what you were doing to follow a prompting from Jesus.","How is Jesus asking you to be a fisher of men right now?","Did a grandparent or ancestor leave something behind to follow Jesus? Tell their story."]},"b13-w4-n6":{"story":["Jesus and His friends came to Capernaum, a town by the lake. On the Sabbath, the day of rest, Jesus taught in the meeting house.","The people were amazed. Jesus did not just repeat what others said. He taught like someone who truly knew Heavenly Father.","Then Jesus went to Simon Peter’s house. Peter’s wife’s mother was in bed, very sick with a fever. They told Jesus about her.","Jesus went to her, took her hand, and lifted her up. Right away the fever left her! She got up and helped serve them all.","That evening, people brought sick friends to the door. Jesus healed many, because He cares when people hurt. Tonight, pray for someone who feels sick or sad."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm has a heart full of compassion. Jesus took a sick woman’s hand and lifted her up, and that is exactly the kind of caring Willow loves. In Willow’s world today: Willow’s mom has a bad headache and lies down on the couch. Willow tiptoes over, brings her a cup of water, and gently pats her hand. “I’ll be quiet so you can rest, Mom.” Mom smiles with her eyes closed. Just like Jesus, Willow learned that compassion means noticing someone who hurts and helping them.","feel":"Hold out your hand like you are gently helping someone sit up. Pull slowly, soft and careful. Can you show me your helping hand?","stones":[["SPOT","Peter’s wife’s mother was sick. Jesus held her hand and healed her. Jesus felt compassion for her."],["NAME","That I-see-you’re-hurting, I-want-to-help feeling is called compassion. Your mind might think, “How can I help?” Your body might feel soft hands and a caring face."],["FIND","When did you see someone who was sick or sad, and you wanted to help?"],["FORK","Jesus could rest after teaching, or help the sick woman. Jesus went right to her and lifted her up."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and make your hands soft. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me care for people who are hurting.”"]],"remember":"Jesus cares when people are hurting, and we can care, too!","ask":["“Who was sick in bed at Simon Peter’s house?”","“What did the woman do as soon as Jesus healed her?”","“What did Willow bring her mom when she had a headache?”"],"share":["Share a time Jesus lifted you up when you were sick, sad, or weak.","Who needs your compassion this week, and what could you do for them?","Was there a grandparent or ancestor who cared for the sick? Tell what they did."]},"b13-w4-n7":{"story":["Jesus had helped people all day, until after the sun went down. Very early the next morning, while it was still dark, Jesus got up.","He went out to a quiet place, all by Himself. There He prayed to Heavenly Father.","Even Jesus, the Son of Heavenly Father, wanted to pray. He wanted to be close to His Father.","Soon Simon Peter and the others came looking for Him. “Everyone is looking for You!” they said. Jesus said, “Let us go to the other towns. I will teach there too. That is why I came.”","So Jesus went all through Galilee, teaching. You can pray like Jesus, in a quiet place. Tonight, kneel and talk to Heavenly Father."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave knows that even brave hearts need quiet time with Heavenly Father. Jesus got up early to pray before His big day, and that is where Beau’s bravery comes from, too. In Beau’s world today: Beau wakes up before everyone else. The house is quiet and the sky is just turning pink. He kneels by his bed and whispers a prayer, just him and Heavenly Father. When he stands up, he feels calm and ready for the day. Just like Jesus, Beau learned that quiet time with Heavenly Father fills us with calm and helps us know what to do.","feel":"Lie still and listen. Can you hear the quiet? Breathe in slowly and out slowly, three times, like a still morning. Can you show me your quietest breathing?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus got up early and prayed alone in a quiet place. He felt calm and ready for the day."],["NAME","That still, quiet, settled feeling is called calm. Your mind might think, “All is well.” Your body might feel slow breathing and loose, heavy arms."],["FIND","When have you felt calm and still, like early in the morning or right before sleep?"],["FORK","Jesus could sleep in after a long day, or get up early to pray. Jesus chose to pray."],["CALM","Take three slow breaths. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, I want to be close to You, like Jesus.”"]],"remember":"Quiet time with Heavenly Father fills us with calm, just like Jesus!","ask":["“When did Jesus get up to pray, and where did He go?”","“What did Jesus say He wanted to do in the other towns?”","“What did Beau do when he woke up before everyone else?”"],"share":["Share how quiet prayer has helped you know what to do.","When and where do you like to pray, and how does it bring you calm?","Did a grandparent or ancestor have a special time or place to pray? Tell about it."]},"b13-w5-n1":{"story":["After His baptism, the Spirit led Jesus into the desert. For forty days He ate nothing and grew hungry.","Satan came. “If You are the Son of God, turn these stones into bread.” Jesus said no: people need Heavenly Father’s words more than bread.","Satan took Him to the top of the temple. “Jump off! Angels will catch You.” Jesus said no. The scriptures say not to test the Lord.","Satan showed Him all the kingdoms of the world. “Bow down to me, and they are Yours.” Jesus said, “Go away, Satan! Worship only the Lord.” Then angels came to help Him.","Each time, Jesus remembered Heavenly Father’s words and said no. Tonight, practice saying, “No, I choose the right,” and feel strong."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big heart, brave start!” Jesus was hungry and tired, but He bravely said no to Satan three times. Saying no to something wrong is one of the bravest things you can do. In Beau’s world today: Beau sees a cookie on the counter. Mom said no treats before dinner. Nobody is looking. His hand reaches out, then stops. He remembers Mom’s words. “No,” he says to himself, and he walks away. He feels strong. Just like Jesus, Beau learned that remembering good words helps us say no to wrong things.","feel":"Reach your hand out slowly like you want to grab something. Now pull it back, hold up your hand, and say, “No!” Can you show me your strong no hand?","stones":[["SPOT","Satan tried to get Jesus to do wrong three times. Jesus felt tempted, but He said no every time."],["NAME","That I-want-to-but-I-shouldn’t feeling is called tempted. Your mind might think, “Nobody will know.” Your body might feel your hand reaching and your tummy twisting."],["FIND","When did you want to do something you knew was wrong, and you stopped?"],["FORK","Jesus could do what Satan said, or remember Heavenly Father’s words and say no. Jesus said no."],["CALM","Pull your hands in close and breathe slowly. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me choose the right.”"]],"remember":"Remembering Heavenly Father’s words helps us say no to wrong things, just like Jesus!","ask":["“How many days was Jesus in the wilderness without eating?”","“How did Jesus say no to Satan each time?”","“What did Beau do when he saw the cookie on the counter?”"],"share":["Share a time a scripture or good word helped you say no to temptation.","Which of Heavenly Father’s words do you hold onto when you feel tempted?","Did a grandparent or ancestor stand strong against something wrong? Tell what they did."]},"b13-w5-n2":{"story":["Jesus came home to Nazareth, where He grew up. On the Sabbath, He went to the meeting house, like always.","There they handed Him a long rolled-up paper called a scroll. It held the words of Isaiah, a prophet from long ago. Jesus stood up and read.","He read, “The Spirit of the Lord is upon Me. He sent Me to heal the brokenhearted, hearts that hurt with sadness.”","Jesus rolled up the scroll and sat down. Everyone stared. He said, “Today these words came true, right in front of you.” Isaiah was talking about Jesus!","When your heart feels sad, Jesus knows. He came to heal hearts, yours too. Tonight, tell Him what makes you sad, and let His love hold you."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer loves to pause and ponder old words that come true. Isaiah wrote about Jesus long ago, and Jesus said, “Today it has come true.” What a wonder! In Wren’s world today: Wren’s best friend is moving away, and Wren cries on the porch. Grandma sits down beside her, wraps her in a blanket, and says, “Jesus knows how a sad heart feels.” Wren leans in. The sad feeling is still there, but it feels held. Just like Isaiah promised, Wren learned that Jesus came to comfort brokenhearted people.","feel":"Pretend to unroll a long, long scroll with both hands, slowly. Now put your hand over your heart, where Jesus heals. Can you show me your hand on your heart?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus read that He came to heal the brokenhearted. The people who were hurting felt comfort."],["NAME","That someone-is-here-for-me, held-close feeling is called comfort. Your mind might think, “I am not alone.” Your body might feel your shoulders relax and your breath slow down."],["FIND","When were you sad, and someone sat close and made you feel comforted?"],["FORK","The people could ignore Jesus’s words, or let Him heal their hearts. Those who believed found comfort."],["CALM","Put both hands over your heart and breathe slowly. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for sending Jesus to comfort me.”"]],"remember":"Jesus came to heal hurting hearts, and He is close to you!","ask":["“What did Jesus read from in the synagogue in Nazareth?”","“Who was Isaiah talking about when he wrote those words?”","“What did Grandma say to Wren when her friend was moving away?”"],"share":["Share a time Jesus healed your broken heart or set you free from something.","Which of Jesus’s promises in Isaiah’s words means the most to you right now?","Did a grandparent or ancestor find comfort in Jesus during a hard time? Tell about it."]},"b13-w5-n3":{"story":["John the Baptist was put in prison. So Jesus went to Galilee. He moved to Capernaum, a town beside the lake.","Isaiah wrote long ago, “The people who sat in darkness saw a great light.” Isaiah was talking about Jesus coming there.","Why a light? Think of a dark room that feels scary. Someone turns on a lamp, and everything feels safe. Jesus is that light for the whole world.","In Capernaum, Jesus began to teach, “Repent!” To repent is to turn away from wrong and back to Heavenly Father. It is like stepping from the dark into the light.","When the dark feels scary, whisper, “Jesus is my light.” Then snuggle down, safe and warm."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see!” When a great light comes on, Scout can see everything, and nothing looks scary anymore. Jesus is that light. In Scout’s world today: Scout wakes up in the night, and his room is dark. The shadows look strange. He whispers, “Jesus is my light,” and clicks on his little lamp. Now he can see his bear, his books, and his blanket. He snuggles down, safe. Just like Isaiah said, Scout learned that Jesus is a great light that makes us feel safe.","feel":"Close your eyes tight and make everything dark. Now open them wide and imagine a great light turning on. Can you show me your light-is-on smile?","stones":[["SPOT","People were sitting in darkness, and Jesus came like a great light. They felt safe in His light."],["NAME","That the-light-is-on, nothing-can-get-me feeling is called safe. Your mind might think, “I can see now.” Your body might feel your shoulders drop and your breath slow."],["FIND","When did a light turning on make you feel safe in the dark?"],["FORK","People could stay in the dark, or come into Jesus’s light. Jesus invited everyone to come."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and picture a warm, bright light. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for Jesus, my light.”"]],"remember":"Jesus is a great light, and in His light we are safe!","ask":["“What town by the lake did Jesus move to?”","“What did Isaiah say the people in darkness would see?”","“What did Scout do when his room was dark and the shadows looked strange?”"],"share":["Share a time Jesus was a light for you in a dark or scary season.","What does the invitation to repent and come into the light mean to you?","Was there a grandparent or ancestor who came out of a dark time into Jesus’s light? Tell their story."]},"b13-w5-n4":{"story":["A big crowd pressed close to Jesus by the lake. Nearby, fishermen were washing their nets.","Jesus sat in Simon Peter’s boat and taught the people from the water. Then He said, “Go out to the deep water. Let down your nets.”","Peter said, “Master, we fished all night and caught nothing. But because You say so, I will.”","The net filled with so many fish that it began to break! Friends came to help, and both boats began to sink. Peter fell at Jesus’s knees, amazed. Jesus said, “Do not be afraid. From now on you will catch people.” They left everything and followed Him.","When something feels hard, try again like Peter. Tonight, say his words: “Because You say so, I will.”"],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker loves to try one more time and see what happens. Peter had fished all night and caught nothing, but he tried once more because Jesus said so. What he found amazed him. In Scout’s world today: Scout has been trying to ride his bike without training wheels all afternoon. He keeps falling. Dad says, “One more try. I believe in you.” Scout pushes off, and this time he rides all the way down the sidewalk! He yells, “I did it!” Just like Peter, Scout learned that when we try again because Jesus says so, amazing things can happen.","feel":"Pretend to pull a heavy net full of fish into the boat. Pull, pull, pull! Now let your arms flop down, amazed. Can you show me your amazed face?","stones":[["SPOT","Peter let down the net because Jesus said so, and it filled with fish. Peter felt amazed."],["NAME","That whoa, I-can’t-believe-it feeling is called amazed. Your mind might think, “How did that happen?” Your body might feel your mouth drop open and your eyes go big."],["FIND","When did something turn out so much better than you expected?"],["FORK","Peter could say, “We already tried,” or try again because Jesus said so. Peter let down the net."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your arms rest. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me trust Jesus and try again.”"]],"remember":"When Jesus asks us to try, we can say yes, because He says so!","ask":["“What did Jesus tell Simon Peter to do with his net?”","“What happened when Peter let down the net in the deep water?”","“What did Scout finally do on his bike?”"],"share":["Share a time you obeyed Jesus even when it seemed pointless, and what happened.","What is Jesus asking you to launch out and try right now?","Did a grandparent or ancestor try again after failing, and see Heavenly Father’s help? Tell about it."]},"b13-w5-n5":{"story":["A man in one town had leprosy, a sickness that made skin sores. People with leprosy had to live away from everyone. Nobody would touch them. That is a lonely way to live.","The man saw Jesus and fell on his face. “Lord, if You want to, You can make me clean.”","Jesus could have healed him from far away. Instead, He reached out and touched him. “I will. Be clean.”","Right away the sickness was gone! Jesus sent him to show the priest he was well. Soon big crowds came to Jesus.","Jesus touched the man nobody else would touch. He loves every person. Tomorrow, find someone sitting alone and ask, “Want to play with me?”"],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” The man with leprosy had not been touched by anyone for a long time. Jesus reached out and touched him, and that is Willow’s kind of love. In Willow’s world today: At recess, a new boy sits alone on the bench. Nobody is playing with him. Willow walks over, sits down next to him, and says, “Want to play with us?” His face lights up. They run to the swings together. Just like Jesus, Willow learned that reaching out to someone lonely is one of the kindest things we can do.","feel":"Wrap your arms around yourself and feel how it feels to be all alone. Now reach one arm out, like Jesus reaching to touch the man. Can you show me your reaching-out arm?","stones":[["SPOT","The man with leprosy lived away from everyone. He felt lonely, and then Jesus touched him and healed him."],["NAME","That nobody’s-near-me, left-out feeling is called lonely. Your mind might think, “Does anyone see me?” Your body might feel an empty ache in your chest."],["FIND","When did you feel lonely, like no one was playing with you?"],["FORK","Jesus could heal from far away, or reach out and touch the man. Jesus touched him."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and put your hand on your own arm, gently. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that Jesus sees me.”"]],"remember":"Jesus reaches out to the lonely, and we can reach out, too!","ask":["“What did the man with leprosy say to Jesus?”","“What did Jesus do that no one else would do?”","“What did Willow say to the new boy sitting alone?”"],"share":["Share a time you felt lonely and Jesus or a friend reached out to you.","Who in your life might feel like the man with leprosy, and how could you reach out?","Was there a grandparent or ancestor who befriended people others ignored? Tell about them."]},"b13-w5-n6":{"story":["Jesus was teaching in a packed house. Nobody else could get in the door.","Four men carried their friend, who could not walk, on a mat. They believed Jesus could heal him. But the crowd was in the way.","So the friends climbed up onto the flat roof. They opened it and lowered their friend right down to Jesus!","Jesus saw how much they believed. He said the man’s sins, the wrong things he had done, were forgiven. Some leaders grumbled, “Only Heavenly Father can forgive sins!” So Jesus showed His power. He said, “Get up, take your mat, and go home.” The man walked home, praising Heavenly Father!","Those friends did not give up. Tonight, plan one kind thing to help a friend."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave has a big heart and a brave start. The four friends did not give up, even when the door was blocked. They climbed up on the roof! Beau loves brave, hopeful friends like that. In Beau’s world today: Beau’s friend Max broke his leg and cannot come to the park. So Beau has an idea. He brings the park to Max, with a ball, a kite, and a picnic blanket in Max’s backyard. Max grins. “You didn’t forget me.” Just like the four friends, Beau learned that hopeful friends find a way to bring people to Jesus.","feel":"Pretend you are carrying one corner of a heavy mat with three friends. Lift it up, up, up to the roof. Now lower it down slowly. Can you show me your gentle lowering?","stones":[["SPOT","Four friends could not get their friend to Jesus. They felt hopeful, found a way, and he was healed."],["NAME","That it-can-still-happen, don’t-give-up feeling is called hopeful. Your mind might think, “There must be a way.” Your body might feel your eyes looking up."],["FIND","When did you keep hoping for something good, even when it looked hard?"],["FORK","The friends could give up at the crowded door, or find another way. They climbed up to the roof."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and look up. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me never give up on my friends.”"]],"remember":"Hopeful friends find a way to bring each other to Jesus!","ask":["“How did the four friends get their friend to Jesus?”","“What did Jesus tell the man to do after He healed him?”","“What did Beau bring to Max’s backyard?”"],"share":["Share a time a friend carried you to Jesus when you could not get there alone.","Who needs you to be one of the four friends right now?","Did a grandparent or ancestor never give up on someone they loved? Tell their story."]},"b13-w5-n7":{"story":["Matthew, also called Levi, was a tax collector. He took money from people for the rulers. Some tax collectors took extra, so people did not like them.","Jesus walked by and said, “Follow Me.” Matthew left everything and followed Him. Then he made a big feast, and many tax collectors came.","Some leaders grumbled, “Why does Jesus eat with those people?”","Jesus said, “Healthy people do not need a doctor. Sick people do.” Jesus is like a kind doctor. He came to help hearts that need to change.","Jesus did not walk past Matthew. He welcomes everyone, including you. Tomorrow, invite someone who is left out to sit with you."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer pauses to ponder people others walk past. Jesus stopped for Matthew when everyone else looked away, and Wren thinks that is a wonder worth remembering. In Wren’s world today: At the lunch table, a girl in a wrinkled shirt sits by herself. Some kids whisper. Wren picks up her tray, walks over, and sits down across from her. “Hi, I’m Wren. Want to eat together?” The girl’s whole face changes. Just like Jesus, Wren learned that welcoming someone everyone else leaves out is what love looks like.","feel":"Pat the bed beside you like you are saving a seat for a friend. Now wave your hand and say, “Come sit with me!” Can you show me your welcome wave?","stones":[["SPOT","Matthew was a tax collector nobody liked. Jesus called him and ate with him. Matthew felt welcome."],["NAME","That come-sit-with-us, you-belong-here feeling is called welcome. Your mind might think, “They want me!” Your body might feel a big smile and relaxed shoulders."],["FIND","When did someone save you a seat or invite you in, and you felt welcome?"],["FORK","Jesus could walk past Matthew like everyone else, or call him to follow. Jesus called him."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and smile. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for welcoming me, and help me welcome others.”"]],"remember":"Jesus welcomes everyone to His table, and we can, too!","ask":["“What was Matthew’s job before he followed Jesus?”","“What did Jesus say about who needs a doctor?”","“What did Wren do for the girl sitting alone at lunch?”"],"share":["Share a time Jesus welcomed you when you felt unworthy or left out.","Who do you need to welcome to your table, like Jesus welcomed Matthew?","Was there a grandparent or ancestor who made everyone feel welcome at their table? Tell about them."]},"b19-w1-n1":{"story":["After Jesus came back to life, He visited His apostles for forty days. He taught them about Heavenly Father’s kingdom.","Then Jesus said, “You will be My witnesses.” A witness sees something true, then tells others. “Tell about Me everywhere, to the ends of the earth.”","Then, while they watched, Jesus went up into the sky. A cloud hid Him from their eyes.","The apostles kept staring up. Suddenly two angels in white stood beside them. “Why are you looking up into heaven?” they asked. “This same Jesus will come back, the same way He went.” The apostles felt hopeful. They went back to Jerusalem, ready to tell.","You can be a witness too. Tonight, tell someone one true thing you know about Jesus."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see!” A witness looked, saw, and tells. Jesus told His friends to be witnesses to the ends of the earth. Scout wants in. In Scout’s world today: Scout saw a double rainbow over the barn, and nobody else was outside. He runs inside. “I saw it! Two rainbows!” Mom asks, “Are you sure?” Scout says, “I saw it with my own eyes. I’m a witness!” He draws it so everyone can see. Just like the apostles, Scout learned that when you have seen something true, you tell it.","feel":"Look way up on your tiptoes, watching Jesus go. Now look back down at your friends. Point to your eyes, then your mouth: see it, tell it! Can you show me your witness point?","stones":[["SPOT","The apostles watched Jesus go up into heaven. The angels said He will come back the same way. They felt hopeful."],["NAME","That He-will-come-back feeling is called hopeful. Your mind might think, “He promised.” Your body might feel a light chest and eyes that look up."],["FIND","When did someone promise to come back, and you felt sure they would?"],["FORK","The apostles could stand staring at the sky, or go and tell. They went and told."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and look up, then breathe out and look around. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me be a witness of Jesus.”"]],"remember":"Jesus said, “Ye shall be witnesses unto Me,” and you can be one too!","ask":["“What is a witness?”","“What did the two angels say to the apostles?”","“What did Scout see over the barn?”"],"share":["Share your testimony as a witness of Jesus Christ, in your own words.","Where is your “Jerusalem,” the place you are called to witness first?","How was a grandparent or ancestor a witness of Jesus in their community?"]},"b19-w1-n2":{"story":["Jesus’s friends were all together in one house. It was a feast day called Pentecost.","Suddenly a sound came from heaven, like a big rushing wind. It filled the whole house! Then something like little flames of fire rested on each of them.","They were filled with the Holy Ghost. He helped them speak in languages they had never learned.","A crowd from many lands gathered. Each one heard Jesus’s friends speaking his own language! Everyone was amazed. Then Peter stood up and bravely told them about Jesus. That day about three thousand people were baptized.","When you need to be brave, say a prayer. The Holy Ghost can bring a calm, warm feeling. He can help you stand up, like Peter."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big faith, brave heart!” Peter stood up before a huge crowd because the Holy Ghost filled him. Beau knows that kind of brave comes from heaven. In Beau’s world today: Beau is asked to say the prayer at the family reunion in front of forty people. His knees shake. He takes a breath and feels a warm, calm feeling settle over him. He prays in a strong voice. Grandpa whispers, “That was the Spirit, Beau.” Just like Peter, Beau learned that the Holy Ghost can make a shaky heart brave.","feel":"Whoosh like a mighty wind, swaying your arms. Now wiggle your fingers above your head like little flames. Stand up tall like Peter, ready to speak. Can you show me your Pentecost stand?","stones":[["SPOT","A wind filled the house and flames rested on each one. Everyone heard their own language. The people felt amazed."],["NAME","That wide-eyed, how-can-this-be feeling is called amazed. Your mind might think, “Heaven did this.” Your body might feel goosebumps and an open mouth."],["FIND","When did something happen that you could not explain, and your eyes got big?"],["FORK","Peter could hide in the house, or stand up and tell about Jesus. He stood up."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly like a soft wind and let it fill you up. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, fill me with Your Holy Ghost.”"]],"remember":"On Pentecost the Holy Ghost came like wind and fire, and three thousand believed!","ask":["“What sound filled the house?”","“What did the people from other countries hear?”","“What did Beau feel before he prayed at the reunion?”"],"share":["Share a time the Holy Ghost gave you words or courage you did not have.","How do you recognize the Spirit’s voice in your own life?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who was filled with the Spirit at a key moment."]},"b19-w1-n3":{"story":["Peter told a big crowd all about Jesus. Their hearts felt poked awake inside. They asked Peter, “What shall we do?”","Peter said, “Repent, and be baptized.” To repent is to feel sorry, turn around, and choose the right way. Peter promised they would get the Holy Ghost as a gift.","About three thousand people were baptized that day. They kept learning, and they prayed together.","The believers shared everything they had. When someone was in need, others sold things and gave the money. They ate together in their homes with glad hearts. And every day, more people joined them.","Sharing made their hearts glad, and it can make yours glad too. Who could you share something with tomorrow?"],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” The new believers ate together, prayed together, and shared everything with glad hearts. That is the family Willow loves. In Willow’s world today: A new family moves in next door with nothing in their fridge yet. Willow fills a basket with bread, apples, and her favorite cookies. She and Dad carry it over. The new little girl grins. “Want to play?” Willow’s heart feels full and glad. Just like the first believers, Willow learned that sharing makes one big glad family.","feel":"Pretend to fill a basket with food, scoop, scoop. Now carry it to a neighbor and hand it over with a big smile. Sit down and pretend to eat together, yum! Can you show me your sharing smile?","stones":[["SPOT","The believers shared their food and money so no one went without, and ate together every day. They felt glad."],["NAME","That full-table, all-have-enough feeling is called glad. Your mind might think, “We’re together.” Your body might feel a warm tummy and soft smile."],["FIND","When did you share something and feel happier than if you had kept it?"],["FORK","The believers could keep everything for themselves, or share with glad hearts. They shared."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and picture a long table full of friends. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for my family of believers.”"]],"remember":"The first believers shared everything with glad hearts, and the church grew every day!","ask":["“What question did the people ask Peter?”","“What did the believers do when someone was in need?”","“What did Willow put in the basket for the new neighbors?”"],"share":["Share how being part of the church family has blessed your life.","What did you do when your own heart was “pricked” by the Spirit?","How did a grandparent or ancestor share what they had with others in the church?"]},"b19-w1-n4":{"story":["Peter and John went to the temple to pray. By a gate called Beautiful sat a man who had never walked. Every day, people carried him there to ask for money.","He asked Peter and John for money. Peter said, “Look at us.” The man looked up, hoping for a gift.","Peter said, “I have no silver or gold. But I give you what I have. In the name of Jesus Christ, rise up and walk!”","Peter took his right hand and lifted him up. Right away his feet and ankles grew strong! He went into the temple walking, leaping, and praising Heavenly Father.","Peter gave something better than money. What can you give that costs nothing? Maybe a hug or a smile."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer says, “Wow, look at that!” The man asked for coins and got legs that could leap! Wren loves when the gift is bigger than the ask. In Wren’s world today: Wren asks Grandma for a sticker. Grandma says, “I don’t have stickers, but I have something better.” She pulls out Wren’s old baby quilt, mended and clean. Wren wraps up in it and laughs. “This is way better than a sticker!” Just like the man at the gate, Wren learned that Heavenly Father’s gifts can be better than what we asked for.","feel":"Sit with your legs out like the man at the gate. Hold out your hand for a coin. Now take Peter’s hand, stand up, and leap, leap, leap! Can you show me your praising leap?","stones":[["SPOT","The man asked for money, but Peter gave him strong legs in Jesus’s name. He went leaping and praising. He felt delighted."],["NAME","That better-than-I-asked-for feeling is called delighted. Your mind might think, “No way!” Your body might feel bouncy legs and a big laugh."],["FIND","When did you get something better than what you asked for?"],["FORK","Peter could say, “Sorry, no money,” and walk by, or give what he had in Jesus’s name. He gave."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel your strong legs. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for gifts better than gold.”"]],"remember":"Peter had no silver or gold, but he gave the man Jesus’s healing, and he leaped for joy!","ask":["“What did the man ask Peter and John for?”","“What did the man do after Peter lifted him up?”","“What did Grandma give Wren instead of a sticker?”"],"share":["Share a time the Lord gave you something better than what you asked for.","What do you have to give that is worth more than money?","How did a grandparent or ancestor give “what they had” when they had little?"]},"b19-w1-n5":{"story":["Peter and John taught that Jesus had risen from the dead. The temple leaders did not like it. They put Peter and John in prison for the night.","The next day the leaders asked, “How did you make the man walk?” Peter, filled with the Holy Ghost, said, “By the name of Jesus Christ.”","Heavenly Father had raised Jesus from the dead, Peter said. Jesus had made the man well.","The leaders said, “Do not teach about Jesus anymore.” Peter and John answered, “Should we listen to you, or to Heavenly Father? We cannot stop telling what we have seen and heard.”","Peter and John were kind and brave. You can say true things kindly and bravely too, even if someone laughs."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big faith, brave heart!” Peter and John were told to stop talking of Jesus, and they said, “We cannot.” Beau thinks it is the bravest sentence ever. In Beau’s world today: At the park, a bigger kid laughs, “Praying is for babies.” Beau’s face gets hot. He says, “I pray every night, and it helps me. I’m not going to stop.” He goes back to the swings, heart pounding, but it feels right. Just like Peter and John, Beau learned that you cannot stop telling what you know is true.","feel":"Stand tall with your hands behind your back like a prisoner. Now lift your chin and say in a strong voice, “We cannot stop!” Give a brave nod. Can you show me your bold nod?","stones":[["SPOT","The leaders told Peter and John to stop speaking of Jesus. They said, “We cannot stop speaking.” They felt bold."],["NAME","That I-will-say-what-is-true feeling is called bold. Your mind might think, “I can’t stay quiet.” Your body might feel a pounding heart, a high chin."],["FIND","When did you say something true even though someone wanted you to be quiet?"],["FORK","Peter and John could promise to stop, or keep telling what they had seen. They kept telling."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your pounding heart grow steady. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, give me a brave, kind voice.”"]],"remember":"Peter and John could not stop speaking the things they had seen and heard!","ask":["“Why were Peter and John put in prison?”","“What did they say when the leaders told them to stop?”","“What did Beau say to the bigger kid at the park?”"],"share":["Share a time you could not stay quiet about what you knew was true.","What have you “seen and heard” of the Savior that you must speak?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who spoke up for their faith when it was hard."]},"b19-w1-n6":{"story":["Peter and John went back to their friends. Everyone prayed together, and the place shook! They were all filled with the Holy Ghost.","All the believers were of one heart and one soul. They loved each other like one big family.","Nobody said, “This is mine, not yours.” They shared everything they had, so nobody went without.","Some people sold their land or houses. They brought the money to the apostles, who gave it to anyone in need. A man named Joses sold his field. He laid all the money at the apostles’ feet. The apostles called him Barnabas, which means “son of comfort.”","Sharing makes us one heart, too. Is there something you love that could help someone else?"],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” Barnabas sold his whole field and gave it all so no one went without. His name means “son of comfort.” Willow wants a name like that. In Willow’s world today: Willow’s class is collecting coats for kids who need them. Willow looks at her two coats and picks the warmer one, the purple one she loves. “Somebody needs it more,” she tells Mom. Walking home, her heart feels big. Just like Barnabas, Willow learned that giving your best makes everyone one heart.","feel":"Hold a big bag of coins. Walk it over and lay it gently at the apostles’ feet. Now put both hands on your heart and open them wide. Can you show me your one-heart hands?","stones":[["SPOT","The believers shared everything, and Barnabas gave all the money from his field. They felt generous."],["NAME","That I-want-to-give-my-best feeling is called generous. Your mind might think, “They need it more.” Your body might feel open hands and a warm chest."],["FIND","When did you give something you really liked, and feel bigger inside?"],["FORK","Barnabas could keep his field, or sell it and give it all. He gave it all."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly with hands on your heart, then breathe out and open them. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, make us one heart.”"]],"remember":"The believers were of one heart, and Barnabas gave everything he had!","ask":["“What does “one heart and one soul” mean?”","“What did Barnabas do with his field?”","“Which coat did Willow give away?”"],"share":["Share a time generosity in your ward or family made you feel “of one heart.”","What is your “field,” the thing you could give to lift someone?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor known for comforting or giving to others."]},"b19-w1-n7":{"story":["The apostles kept teaching about Jesus. The angry high priest put them in prison.","But that night an angel of the Lord opened the prison doors! “Go to the temple,” the angel said. “Tell the people all the words of this life.”","In the morning they taught in the temple. Guards came to the prison to get them. But it was empty!","The guards found them in the temple. “We told you not to teach about Jesus!” the leaders said. Peter said, “We should obey Heavenly Father first.” The leaders hurt them and let them go. But the apostles left glad, and kept on teaching.","You can put Heavenly Father first too. Even somewhere new, you can bow your head and pray."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see!” The guards looked in the locked prison and found nobody; the apostles were out teaching. Scout loves a mystery with an angel behind it. In Scout’s world today: Scout’s little brother Max says his friend tells him, “Don’t say the blessing at my house, it’s weird.” Scout says, “I’ll come. We’ll say it quietly together. Heavenly Father comes first.” At lunch they bow their heads, and Max’s friend bows too. Just like the apostles, Scout learned that we obey Heavenly Father first, and keep going with joy.","feel":"Hold the prison bars with both hands. Now watch the angel swing the doors open and tiptoe out! March to the temple and start teaching with your hands. Can you show me your out-the-door march?","stones":[["SPOT","The angel opened the prison, and the apostles went right back to teaching. Even after a hard day, they left rejoicing."],["NAME","That glad-even-when-it’s-hard feeling is called rejoicing. Your mind might think, “It was worth it.” Your body might feel light steps, a glad heart."],["FIND","When did doing the right thing cost you something, but you still felt glad?"],["FORK","The apostles could stay quiet to be safe, or obey Heavenly Father first. They obeyed Him first."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and picture a door swinging open to morning light. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, I will obey You and rejoice.”"]],"remember":"The apostles said, “We ought to obey Heavenly Father rather than men,” and they rejoiced!","ask":["“Who opened the prison doors at night?”","“Where did the guards find the apostles?”","“What did Scout and Max do together at lunch?”"],"share":["Share a time you chose to obey Heavenly Father first, even with a cost.","What helps you find joy when discipleship is hard?","How did a grandparent or ancestor keep their faith when others opposed them?"]},"b19-w2-n1":{"story":["More and more people believed in Jesus. Each day, food was shared with widows, women whose husbands had died. But some of these widows were being forgotten.","The twelve apostles called everyone together. “We need to keep praying and teaching,” they said. “Choose seven good men, full of the Holy Ghost, to help with the food.”","Everyone liked that idea. They chose seven helpers. One was Stephen, a man full of faith.","The apostles prayed and laid their hands on the seven helpers’ heads. Heavenly Father’s word kept growing, and more people believed. Stephen, full of faith and power, did great wonders.","Every helper matters to Heavenly Father. Tomorrow at dinner, look for someone who needs help, and help them."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big faith, brave heart!” Stephen was picked to serve tables, and he did it full of faith and power. Beau knows a helper job is a brave job when it is for Heavenly Father. In Beau’s world today: At the church dinner, Grandma Jo is sitting alone with no plate. Beau notices. He fills a plate with her favorites and carries it carefully, both hands. “For you, Grandma Jo.” She squeezes his hand. “You’re my helper tonight.” Just like Stephen, Beau learned that serving at the table is holy work.","feel":"Carry a heavy pretend tray, careful, careful. Set a plate in front of someone who was forgotten. Now bow your head while hands are laid on it for your new job. Can you show me your helper bow?","stones":[["SPOT","Some widows were forgotten, so seven helpers were chosen to serve them. Stephen served full of faith. He felt helpful."],["NAME","That I-can-do-my-part feeling is called helpful. Your mind might think, “I’ll take care of it.” Your body might feel steady hands and a ready smile."],["FIND","When did you notice someone being left out and do something about it?"],["FORK","The seven could say, “Serving tables isn’t important,” or serve with faith and power. They served."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel your hands grow calm and ready to serve. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, show me who needs a helper.”"]],"remember":"Seven helpers were chosen, and no widow was forgotten again!","ask":["“Who was being forgotten at mealtime?”","“How many helpers did the people choose?”","“What did Beau bring to Grandma Jo?”"],"share":["Share a calling or job of service that turned out to be holy work.","Who in your circle is being overlooked, and how could you serve them?","How did a grandparent or ancestor serve quietly behind the scenes in the church?"]},"b19-w2-n2":{"story":["Stephen spoke wisely about Jesus. Some men argued with him, but they could not win. So they told lies about him to the leaders.","All the leaders looked hard at Stephen. His face looked like the face of an angel!","The leaders grew very angry. But Stephen, full of the Holy Ghost, looked up into heaven.","He saw Jesus standing at Heavenly Father’s right hand! “Look,” said Stephen, “I see heaven open!” The angry men hurt him very badly. Still Stephen prayed, “Lord Jesus, take my spirit.” He forgave them too. Then Stephen died, and his spirit went home to Heavenly Father.","Stephen was not afraid, because he could see Jesus. When you feel sad, remember Jesus is in heaven, and He loves you."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer says, “Wow, look at that!” Stephen looked up and saw heaven open, with Jesus standing to welcome him. Wren thinks that is the most wonderful sight ever. In Wren’s world today: Wren’s old dog Biscuit dies. She cries into his blanket. Dad sits beside her. “Stephen saw heaven open when he was scared,” he says. “Jesus is there.” Wren looks up at the stars through the window and feels a quiet peace. Just like Stephen, Wren learned that looking up to Jesus brings peace even in the saddest moments.","feel":"Make your face glow soft and calm like an angel’s. Now look way up and point, “I see heaven open!” Fold your hands and whisper a forgiving prayer. Can you show me your looking-up face?","stones":[["SPOT","Stephen looked up and saw Jesus standing in heaven. Even when he was hurt, he prayed and forgave. He felt peaceful."],["NAME","That calm-even-when-it’s-hard feeling is called peaceful. Your mind might think, “Jesus is there.” Your body might feel soft shoulders, slow breaths."],["FIND","When were you sad or scared, but a quiet calm came anyway?"],["FORK","Stephen could be angry at the men, or forgive them like Jesus did. He forgave."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and look up, picturing Jesus standing to welcome you. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, give me Stephen’s peace.”"]],"remember":"Stephen looked up, saw Jesus, and forgave, and his face shone like an angel’s!","ask":["“What did Stephen’s face look like to the council?”","“What did Stephen see when he looked up?”","“What did Wren’s dad tell her about Stephen?”"],"share":["Share a time you felt heaven close during loss or fear.","Whom do you need to forgive the way Stephen forgave?","How did a grandparent or ancestor face death or loss with peace in Christ?"]},"b19-w2-n3":{"story":["An angel sent a helper named Philip to a desert road. There he saw an important man from far-away Ethiopia, riding in his chariot.","The man had come to Jerusalem to worship. He was reading from the prophet Isaiah.","Philip ran up and asked, “Do you understand what you are reading?” “How can I, unless someone helps me?” the man said.","He asked Philip to sit with him. Isaiah wrote about a quiet lamb who was hurt. Philip told him Isaiah was talking about Jesus. Soon they came to water. “May I be baptized?” the man asked. Philip baptized him, and the man went home joyful.","The man asked for help and found Jesus. When something is hard to understand, you can ask too."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see!” The man in the chariot asked, “How can I understand unless someone helps me?” Scout thinks asking for help is smart seeking. In Scout’s world today: Scout is reading his picture scriptures and gets stuck on a long word. He could skip it. Instead he carries the book to Grandpa. “What does this mean?” Grandpa explains, and the story opens like a door. “Now I get it!” Just like the man in the chariot, Scout learned that asking for help is how understanding comes.","feel":"Ride in a bumpy chariot, bounce, bounce. Hold a scroll and read with a puzzled face. Now run alongside like Philip and climb up to sit together. Can you show me your puzzled-to-happy face?","stones":[["SPOT","The man was reading about Jesus but did not understand. He asked for help, and Philip explained. He felt curious."],["NAME","That I-want-to-understand feeling is called curious. Your mind might think, “Who is this about?” Your body might feel a tilted head, leaning in."],["FIND","When did you not understand something, and asking someone made it clear?"],["FORK","The man could ride on confused, or invite Philip up to explain. He invited him up."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your questions settle like a scroll rolling closed. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me understand.”"]],"remember":"and Philip told him about Jesus!","ask":["“What was the man in the chariot reading?”","“What did the man ask when they came to water?”","“Who helped Scout with the long word?”"],"share":["Share a time the Spirit sent someone to you at just the right moment.","What scripture did someone open up for you, and how did it change you?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who helped others understand the scriptures."]},"b19-w2-n4":{"story":["A man named Saul was mean to the followers of Jesus. He took them away to prison. Then he set out for the city of Damascus.","Near Damascus, a light from heaven shone all around him. Saul fell to the ground. A voice said, “Saul, Saul, why are you hurting Me?”","“Who are You, Lord?” Saul asked. “I am Jesus.” Hurting Jesus’s friends was like hurting Jesus Himself.","Trembling, Saul asked, “Lord, what do You want me to do?” Jesus said, “Go into the city, and you will be told.” When Saul stood up, he could not see. His men led him by the hand into Damascus.","When you’ve been unkind, you can ask Jesus, “What should I do?” Then make it right."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big faith, brave heart!” One great light turned the church’s worst enemy around. Saul’s bravest act, Beau thinks, was asking what Jesus wanted. In Beau’s world today: Beau has bossed the little kids at recess all week. His teacher kneels down. “Beau, the little ones are scared of you.” It hits him like a bright light. “I’ll fix it,” he says, and goes to ask them what game they want. Just like Saul, Beau learned that when the light shows you are wrong, you ask what to do next.","feel":"Ride a horse, clip-clop. Now a bright light! Cover your eyes and drop to your knees. Reach out your hands and let a friend lead you. Can you show me your led-by-the-hand walk?","stones":[["SPOT","Saul saw a great light, heard Jesus, and could not see. He asked, “Lord, what do You want me to do?” He felt humbled."],["NAME","That I-was-wrong, show-me feeling is called humbled. Your mind might think, “I need to change.” Your body might feel low shoulders, a quiet voice."],["FIND","When did you find out you were wrong, and it made you want to change?"],["FORK","Saul could get up angry and keep going, or ask Jesus what to do. He asked."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let a soft light fill your closed eyes. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, show me what You want me to do.”"]],"remember":"Saul saw the light and asked Jesus what He wanted him to do!","ask":["“What did Saul do to the followers of Jesus before the light?”","“What question did Saul ask Jesus?”","“What did Beau do after his teacher talked to him?”"],"share":["Share a moment the Lord stopped you in your tracks and turned you around.","What is the Lord asking you to do right now?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor whose heart was changed by the Lord."]},"b19-w2-n5":{"story":["In Damascus lived Ananias, a follower of Jesus. Jesus spoke to him in a vision. “Go to the street called Straight and find Saul. He is praying.”","Ananias was worried. “Lord, this man has hurt Your people! He came here to take away everyone who prays to You.”","But Jesus said, “Go. I have chosen Saul to tell many people about Me.”","So Ananias went and put his hands on Saul. “Brother Saul,” he said, “Jesus sent me so you can see.” Right away something like fish scales fell from Saul’s eyes. He could see! Saul was baptized, and he taught that Jesus is the Son of God.","Ananias was scared, but he trusted Jesus. Who could you give a second chance tomorrow?"],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” Ananias was scared of Saul, but he trusted Jesus and called him “Brother.” Willow thinks that word healed more than Saul’s eyes. In Willow’s world today: A boy named Carter pushed Willow last year. Now he is on her soccer team. Mom says, “Give him a chance.” Willow’s tummy flips, but she goes. “Hi, Carter. Want to pass with me?” He looks surprised, then nods. By the end they are laughing. Just like Ananias, Willow learned that trusting Jesus can turn a scary person into a friend.","feel":"Shake your head, “That man? No way!” Now take a brave breath and walk down Straight Street. Put your hands gently on a friend’s head and say, “Brother!” Can you show me your gentle hands?","stones":[["SPOT","Ananias was afraid of Saul, but Jesus said, “Go.” He went and called him “Brother Saul.” He felt trusting."],["NAME","That scared-but-I’ll-go-anyway feeling is called trusting. Your mind might think, “Jesus knows.” Your body might feel a flippy tummy and steady feet."],["FIND","When did you do something scary because you trusted someone who loves you?"],["FORK","Ananias could stay home safe, or go to Saul as Jesus asked. He went."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel your flippy tummy go still. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, I trust You. I will go.”"]],"remember":"Ananias trusted Jesus, went to his enemy, and called him Brother Saul!","ask":["“Why was Ananias worried about going to Saul?”","“What were the first two words Ananias said to Saul?”","“What did Willow say to Carter?”"],"share":["Share a time you trusted the Lord and reached out to someone you feared.","Who might the Lord be asking you to call “brother” or “sister”?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who befriended someone others avoided."]},"b19-w2-n6":{"story":["Saul now taught that Jesus is the Savior Heavenly Father promised. People were amazed. “Didn’t this man hurt Jesus’s followers?” they asked.","Some angry men wanted to hurt Saul. They watched the city gates day and night, so he could not get out.","But Saul’s friends had a plan. One night they put him in a big basket. They let him down the city wall, and he got away safe!","In Jerusalem, the believers were afraid of Saul. Then Barnabas took him to the apostles. He told them how Saul had seen Jesus and spoken bravely. So they welcomed Saul.","Saul’s friends helped him when he needed it most. You can be a friend like Barnabas. Who could you stand beside tomorrow?"],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see!” Enemies watched every gate, but the believers found a way over the wall in a basket. Scout loves a clever escape and a friend who vouches for you. In Scout’s world today: Scout is new at school. At lunch an older boy, Eli, sits down. “This is Scout. He’s cool. He helped me find my lost bike.” Suddenly the whole table makes room. Scout feels safe, like somebody has his back. Just like Saul, Scout learned that Heavenly Father protects us through friends who stand up for us.","feel":"Curl up small in a basket. Now feel the rope lower you down, down, down the wall, wobble, wobble. Hop out and tiptoe away! Can you show me your basket crouch?","stones":[["SPOT","Friends lowered Saul over the wall in a basket, and Barnabas spoke up for him. Saul felt protected."],["NAME","That somebody-has-my-back feeling is called protected. Your mind might think, “I’m not alone.” Your body might feel a loose chest, a big breath out."],["FIND","When did a friend help you get out of a tight spot or speak up for you?"],["FORK","The believers could leave Saul to the gates, or lower him over the wall. They helped him escape."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel held safe like a basket on a rope. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for watching over me.”"]],"remember":"Friends lowered Saul over the wall in a basket, and Barnabas stood up for him!","ask":["“How did Saul escape from Damascus?”","“Who took Saul to the apostles and spoke up for him?”","“What did Eli tell the lunch table about Scout?”"],"share":["Share a time the Lord protected you through another person’s help.","For whom could you be a Barnabas, vouching for them when others doubt?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who was kept safe in a dangerous time."]},"b19-w2-n7":{"story":["In Joppa, a town by the sea, lived Tabitha, a follower of Jesus. She was always doing good. She made coats and clothes for widows, women whose husbands had died.","One day Tabitha got sick and died. Her friends laid her in a room upstairs and sent for Peter.","Peter came. The widows stood around him crying, showing him the coats Tabitha had made.","Peter sent everyone out. He knelt down and prayed. Then he said, “Tabitha, arise.” She opened her eyes and sat up! Peter helped her stand and showed everyone she was alive. Many believed in the Lord.","Jesus’s power can make a sad day glad. Tonight, thank Heavenly Father for someone who makes things for you with love."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer says, “Wow, look at that!” Tabitha sewed coats for widows, and Jesus’s power through Peter sat her right back up. Wren loves both miracles, sewing and sitting up. In Wren’s world today: Wren’s baby doll loses an arm and she sobs. Great-Grandma takes it to her sewing room. Soon the doll is back, arm on, in a brand-new dress. Wren gasps. “How did you DO that?” Great-Grandma winks. “Love and a needle.” Just like the widows of Joppa, Wren learned that hands that do good can bring wonder into a sad day.","feel":"Pretend to sew a coat, in and out with your needle. Now lie very still like Tabitha. Hear Peter say, “Arise!” and sit straight up with big eyes. Can you show me your Tabitha sit-up?","stones":[["SPOT","Tabitha, who sewed for the poor, died. Peter prayed and said, “Tabitha, arise,” and she sat up. Everyone felt wonder."],["NAME","That I-can’t-believe-my-eyes feeling is called wonder. Your mind might think, “How did that work?” Your body might feel a gasp, hands over your mouth."],["FIND","When did something turn from sad to wonderful right in front of you?"],["FORK","Peter could say, “I’m sorry, she’s gone,” or kneel and pray in Jesus’s name. He prayed."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly with a soft gasp of wonder, then let it out. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for miracles.”"]],"remember":"Tabitha did good for everyone, and Peter told her to arise, and she did!","ask":["“What did Tabitha make for the widows?”","“What did Peter say to Tabitha?”","“What did Great-Grandma do for Wren’s doll?”"],"share":["Share a time the Lord turned a sorrow into a wonder for you.","Whose “coats and dresses” (quiet good works) have blessed your life?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who, like Tabitha, was always doing good."]},"b19-w3-n1":{"story":["In a city by the sea lived a man named Cornelius. He was a captain in the Roman army. He and his whole family loved Heavenly Father.","Cornelius prayed all the time. He gave lots of help to poor people.","One afternoon an angel came to him. The angel said, “Cornelius, your prayers have come up before Heavenly Father. So have your gifts to the poor.”","Heavenly Father had noticed! The angel said, “Send men to Joppa for a man named Peter.” Cornelius did not wait and wonder. He sent them right away.","Heavenly Father sees the kind things you do, even quiet ones. Tonight, think of one kind thing you did today. He noticed it too."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” Cornelius prayed and gave to the poor, and an angel said Heavenly Father had noticed. Willow loves that heaven keeps track. In Willow’s world today: Every morning Willow leaves a note and a cookie by the mailbox for Mr. Ruiz, their mail carrier. She thinks nobody knows. One day a letter comes for her: “Dear Willow, your notes make my whole route happier.” Just like Cornelius, Willow learned that Heavenly Father sees the kind things we do when we think no one is watching.","feel":"Stand like a captain and salute! Now kneel to pray with folded hands. Open your eyes wide, an angel! Point far away toward Joppa and send your helpers. Can you show me your captain’s salute?","stones":[["SPOT","Cornelius prayed and gave to the poor. An angel said, “Heavenly Father has noticed.” Cornelius felt noticed."],["NAME","That somebody-saw-me feeling is called noticed. Your mind might think, “He was watching.” Your body might feel warm cheeks, a surprised smile."],["FIND","When did you find out someone had noticed a kind thing you did in secret?"],["FORK","Cornelius could wait and wonder, or send for Peter right away. He sent right away."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and remember that Heavenly Father sees you. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for noticing me.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father noticed Cornelius’s prayers and gifts, and He notices yours too!","ask":["“What was Cornelius’s job?”","“What did the angel say had come up before Heavenly Father?”","“What did Mr.","Ruiz’s letter say to Willow?”"],"share":["Share a time you felt the Lord had noticed your quiet prayers or service.","What small, unseen kindness could you offer this week?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor whose prayers were answered in a surprising way."]},"b19-w3-n2":{"story":["At noon, Peter went up on a flat roof to pray. He was hungry. Heavenly Father showed him a vision, like a dream while awake.","Heaven opened, and down came a big sheet, tied at the four corners. Inside were all kinds of animals and birds.","Peter’s people had a law: some animals were unclean, not to be eaten. A voice said, “Get up, Peter. Eat.” Peter said, “No, Lord! I have never eaten anything unclean.”","The voice said, “What Heavenly Father has made clean, do not call unclean.” This happened three times. Then three men came to the gate.","The Spirit told Peter, “Go with them.” Peter felt puzzled, but he went to find out. When something puzzles you, you can ask Heavenly Father."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer says, “Wow, look at that!” A sheet full of animals came down from heaven three times, and Peter had to puzzle out what it meant. Wren loves a heaven puzzle. In Wren’s world today: Wren’s family rule is “We don’t play with the Hadley kids, they’re wild.” Then Mom says, “I was wrong. I’m inviting them over.” Wren is puzzled. But the Hadley kids share their best sidewalk chalk, and Wren thinks, “Maybe wild just means fun.” Just like Peter, Wren learned that Heavenly Father sometimes changes what we thought we knew.","feel":"Climb the stairs to the rooftop, step, step. Rub your hungry tummy. Now look up: a giant sheet coming down! Tilt your head and scratch it, puzzled. Can you show me your puzzled face?","stones":[["SPOT","Peter saw a sheet full of animals and heard, “What Heavenly Father has made clean, do not call unclean.” He felt puzzled."],["NAME","That what-does-this-mean feeling is called puzzled. Your mind might think, “I don’t get it.” Your body might feel a scrunched forehead, a tilted head."],["FIND","When did something confusing turn out to have a good reason?"],["FORK","Peter could ignore the strange vision, or go with the men and find out. He went."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your forehead smooth out. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, I’ll trust You before I understand.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father told Peter three times that the good news is for everyone!","ask":["“What did Peter see coming down from heaven?”","“How many times did the sheet come down?”","“What did Wren decide “wild” might mean?”"],"share":["Share a time the Lord corrected something you were sure about.","Whom have you been calling “unclean” that the Lord has made clean?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who had to change a long-held belief."]},"b19-w3-n3":{"story":["Peter went with the men to Cornelius’s house. Cornelius had called his family and friends to listen.","Cornelius fell down at Peter’s feet. But Peter lifted him up. “Stand up,” Peter said. “I am just a man, like you.”","Peter’s people had a rule: do not visit people from other lands, like Cornelius. But Peter said, “Heavenly Father showed me not to call any person unclean.”","Peter said, “Heavenly Father welcomes everyone who loves Him and does right.” While Peter was still talking, the Holy Ghost came upon everyone listening! Then Cornelius and his family were baptized.","Heavenly Father wants everyone in His family. Tomorrow, welcome someone new. You could smile and say, “Come play with us!”"],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big faith, brave heart!” It took courage for Peter to walk into a Roman house when the rules said no. But Heavenly Father said everyone is welcome. Beau goes where He says. In Beau’s world today: A boy who only speaks Spanish joins Beau’s soccer team, and nobody passes to him. Beau jogs over, says “Amigo!” and points to the goal. He passes the ball. The new boy scores. Now everyone passes to him. Just like Peter, Beau learned that Heavenly Father welcomes every nation, and so should we.","feel":"Walk into a big house full of new people. Lift up a friend who is kneeling. Spread your arms wide, welcoming everyone from every nation! Can you show me your everyone-is-welcome arms?","stones":[["SPOT","Peter learned that Heavenly Father does not favor one kind of person. Cornelius’s family was baptized. They felt welcome."],["NAME","That there’s-a-place-for-me feeling is called welcome. Your mind might think, “I belong here.” Your body might feel open arms and relaxed shoulders."],["FIND","When did someone welcome you into a group where you felt like a stranger?"],["FORK","Peter could keep the old rule and stay away, or obey Heavenly Father and go in. He went in."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and spread your arms wide, then hug yourself. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You, everyone is welcome.”"]],"remember":"Peter learned that Heavenly Father welcomes every person in every nation!","ask":["“What did Peter say when Cornelius knelt at his feet?”","“What happened while Peter was still talking?”","“What word did Beau say to the new boy?”"],"share":["Share a time you learned the gospel is truly for everyone.","Who might feel like an outsider at church, and how can you welcome them?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who welcomed people others kept out."]},"b19-w3-n4":{"story":["Some of Jesus’s followers moved to a big city called Antioch. They told many people about the Lord Jesus, and a great many believed!","The church in Jerusalem heard the happy news. They sent a good man named Barnabas to see.","Barnabas saw Heavenly Father blessing the new believers, and he was glad. He told them, “Stay close to the Lord with all your heart.”","Then Barnabas went to Tarsus to find Saul to help him. Together they taught in Antioch for a whole year. There the believers were first called Christians, people who follow Christ.","If you love Jesus and follow Him, you are a Christian too. Tonight, say softly, “I belong to Jesus.”"],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see!” Barnabas went all the way to Tarsus to find Saul, and together they taught the first people ever called Christians. Scout loves a search that ends with a name. In Scout’s world today: Scout joins a new Primary class where the kids call themselves “the Shiners.” Scout wonders if he counts. Then Sister Pell hands him a paper star with his name on it. “Welcome, Shiner.” Now he belongs. Just like the believers in Antioch, Scout learned that belonging to Christ gives you a name worth keeping.","feel":"Walk a long, long road to Tarsus to find a friend, step, step, step. Found him! Link arms and walk back together. Now point to yourself and say, “Christian!” Can you show me your proud point?","stones":[["SPOT","Barnabas found Saul, and together they taught in Antioch, where believers were first called Christians. They felt belonging."],["NAME","That I’m-part-of-this feeling is called belonging. Your mind might think, “This is my family.” Your body might feel a straight back, a settled heart."],["FIND","When did you get a name or a place in a group and feel like you belonged?"],["FORK","Barnabas could teach alone, or go find Saul to help. He went and found him."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and settle into the family of Jesus. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that I belong to Christ.”"]],"remember":"In Antioch the believers were first called Christians, and you are one too!","ask":["“Who did the church in Jerusalem send to Antioch?”","“What were the believers first called in Antioch?”","“What name did the Primary kids call themselves?”"],"share":["Share what the name “Christian” means to you personally.","Who is your Barnabas, the one who brought you in or found you?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who was proud to be called a Christian."]},"b19-w3-n5":{"story":["King Herod put Peter in prison, with sixteen soldiers to guard him. But the church prayed and prayed for Peter.","One night Peter slept between two soldiers, chained up. Suddenly a light shone, and an angel tapped his side. “Get up quickly!” Peter’s chains fell right off his hands!","“Follow me,” said the angel. They walked past the guards. A big iron gate opened all by itself!","Peter went to a house where friends were praying, and he knocked. A girl named Rhoda heard his voice. She was so happy, she forgot to open the gate! She ran to tell everyone. Peter kept knocking until they let him in.","The church kept praying, and Heavenly Father answered. Tonight, pray for someone you love."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” Rhoda was so happy to hear Peter that she forgot to open the door! Willow has done that kind of happy forgetting. In Willow’s world today: Daddy has been away for two whole weeks. The doorbell rings and Willow hears his voice. She squeals, spins around, and runs to tell Mom, leaving him standing on the porch! Mom laughs, “Willow, open the door!” She does, and jumps into his arms. Just like Rhoda, Willow learned that sometimes joy is so big you forget to open the door.","feel":"Lie still like Peter asleep, then feel a tap, wake up quick! Shake off your chains. Now be Rhoda: hear a knock, gasp, and run away to tell everyone! Can you show me your overjoyed run?","stones":[["SPOT","The church prayed, an angel set Peter free, and Rhoda was so happy she forgot to open the door. She felt overjoyed."],["NAME","That so-happy-I-can’t-think feeling is called overjoyed. Your mind might think, “It’s really him!” Your body might feel spinning feet and a squeal."],["FIND","When were you so excited you forgot to do the obvious thing?"],["FORK","The believers could stop praying because it seemed hopeless, or keep praying. They kept praying."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your spinning joy settle into a happy hum. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for hearing us.”"]],"remember":"The church prayed, the angel opened the gate, and Rhoda heard Peter knock!","ask":["“What happened to Peter’s chains?”","“What did Rhoda forget to do?”","“Whose voice did Willow hear at the door?”"],"share":["Share a time a prayer was answered so fast or so fully it surprised you.","What are you praying for “without ceasing” right now?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who prayed through a long, hard night."]},"b19-w3-n6":{"story":["Saul, now called Paul, came with Barnabas to a town called Lystra. A man there had never walked.","Paul saw that the man had faith to be healed. Paul said loudly, “Stand up on your feet!” The man jumped up and walked!","The people did not know Heavenly Father. They shouted, “The gods have come down to us!” They brought oxen and flower crowns to worship Paul and Barnabas.","Paul and Barnabas ran into the crowd. “Why are you doing this? We are people just like you!” They pointed the people to Heavenly Father. He made the sky, the land, and the sea. He gives us rain and food.","When someone says you did something great, you can say, “Heavenly Father helped me.”"],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer says, “Wow, look at that!” The people wanted to worship Paul, and Paul said, “We are people like you!” Wren loves pointing past the helper to the Maker. In Wren’s world today: Wren wins the science fair and kids crowd around saying, “You’re a genius!” Her cheeks get hot. “My grandpa taught me, and Heavenly Father made the plants,” she says. Then she asks a shy boy to show her his project. Just like Paul, Wren learned to point the praise up to Heavenly Father instead of keeping it.","feel":"Shout, “Stand up!” and leap like the man. Now the crowd bows to you. Wave your hands, no, no! Point up to the sky, to the One who made it all. Can you show me your point-to-heaven?","stones":[["SPOT","The people tried to worship Paul and Barnabas. They said, “We are people like you,” and pointed up. They felt humble."],["NAME","That the-praise-isn’t-mine feeling is called humble. Your mind might think, “He did it.” Your body might feel hot cheeks, a finger pointing up."],["FIND","When did someone praise you, and you knew the credit belonged somewhere else?"],["FORK","Paul and Barnabas could enjoy being treated like gods, or point the people to Heavenly Father. They pointed up."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel the rain and food Heavenly Father gives. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, all the praise is Yours.”"]],"remember":"Paul said, “We are people like you,” and pointed everyone to Heavenly Father!","ask":["“What did the man in Lystra do when Paul told him to stand?”","“What did the people think Paul and Barnabas were?”","“Who did Wren say taught her and made the plants?”"],"share":["Share a time you had to redirect praise to the Lord.","What “empty things” tempt you to worship them instead of the living Father?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who stayed humble when honored."]},"b19-w3-n7":{"story":["The church in Antioch had good teachers, like Barnabas and Saul. One day they went without food so they could pray.","Then the Holy Ghost spoke: “Set apart Barnabas and Saul for My work.” So the church prayed and sent them off.","They sailed across the sea to teach about Jesus. In another city, almost the whole town came to hear them!","Some leaders were jealous. But Barnabas and Saul said, “The Lord made us a light for everyone.” Like a light, they showed people the way to Jesus. Many believed. Then the jealous leaders chased them out. Still, the disciples were full of joy!","Helping Heavenly Father brings joy, even on hard days. Who can you help tomorrow?"],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big faith, brave heart!” The Holy Ghost said, “Send them,” and off they went, joyful even when chased out of town. In Beau’s world today: Beau’s family is asked to help clean up after a flood two towns over. His friends are at the pool. Dad says, “We’re being sent.” Beau shovels mud all day, and a lady hugs him. Driving home, Beau feels a joy bigger than any pool day. Just like Barnabas and Saul, Beau learned that being sent by the Holy Ghost brings joy, even when it’s hard.","feel":"Kneel while hands are laid on your head. Now sail across the sea, rock, rock. Walk into a new town and speak boldly. Get shooed out and skip away with joy! Can you show me your joyful skip?","stones":[["SPOT","The Holy Ghost sent Barnabas and Saul. Even when they were chased out, the disciples were filled with joy."],["NAME","That hard-day, happy-heart feeling is called joyful. Your mind might think, “We did what He asked.” Your body might feel tired arms and a light chest."],["FIND","When did you do something hard for someone else and still feel happy?"],["FORK","The disciples could feel sorry for themselves after being chased out, or be filled with joy. They chose joy."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let the Holy Ghost fill you with quiet joy. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for sending me.”"]],"remember":"The Holy Ghost sent Barnabas and Saul, and the word grew and multiplied!","ask":["“Who told the church to set apart Barnabas and Saul?”","“How did the disciples feel after they were chased out of town?”","“What did Beau’s family do after the flood?”"],"share":["Share a time you were “sent” by the Spirit and found joy in the work.","What work is the Lord setting you apart for in this season?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who served a mission or was sent far from home."]},"b19-w4-n1":{"story":["Paul and his friends wanted to teach in one place. But the Holy Ghost said no. They tried another place, and the Spirit said no again.","So they came to Troas, by the sea. In a vision that night, Paul saw a man from Macedonia, across the sea. He begged, “Come over to Macedonia and help us!”","Now Paul knew where the Lord wanted them. Right away they got on a ship.","In Philippi, they went to a river where women prayed. A woman named Lydia sold purple cloth. As Paul spoke, the Lord opened her heart. Lydia and her family were baptized.","Sometimes Heavenly Father says no to lead us somewhere better. You can trust Him."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer says, “Wow, look at that!” The Spirit said no, no, and then showed Paul a man calling, “Come over and help us!” Wren loves when a no is really a bigger yes. In Wren’s world today: Wren wants to sell lemonade on her street, but Mom says, “Not today.” Then Mom says, “Let’s take it to the fire station instead.” The firefighters cheer, and one shows her the big truck. Wren whispers, “Way better.” Just like Paul, Wren learned that when one door closes, Heavenly Father may be opening a better one.","feel":"Try to walk left, no! Try right, no! Now see a man waving, “Come help us!” Sail on a ship. Then open your heart like a door with both hands. Can you show me your open-heart door?","stones":[["SPOT","The Spirit said no twice, then showed Paul where to go. Lydia’s heart opened, and she was baptized. They felt eager."],["NAME","That can’t-wait-to-go feeling is called eager. Your mind might think, “Let’s go now!” Your body might feel bouncing toes and bright eyes."],["FIND","When did a “no” turn into a better “yes” for you?"],["FORK","Paul could grumble about the closed doors, or sail right away where the Lord called. He sailed."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your eager bounce settle. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, open my heart to where You want me.”"]],"remember":"The man said, “Come over and help us,” and the Lord opened Lydia’s heart!","ask":["“What did the man in Paul’s vision say?”","“What did Lydia sell?”","“Where did Wren take her lemonade instead?”"],"share":["Share a time a closed door led you somewhere the Lord needed you.","Whose heart might the Lord be opening through your words right now?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who crossed a sea or a border to follow the Lord."]},"b19-w4-n2":{"story":["In Philippi, Paul helped a girl, and her masters got angry. Paul and Silas were hurt and put in prison. Their feet were locked in wooden blocks.","At midnight, Paul and Silas prayed and sang to Heavenly Father. The other prisoners listened.","Suddenly the ground shook! Every door opened, and every chain came loose. The jailer woke up, afraid everyone had run away.","But Paul called, “We are all here!” The jailer knelt and asked, “What must I do to be saved?” They said, “Believe on the Lord Jesus Christ.” The jailer washed where they were hurt, and his family was baptized.","Paul and Silas stayed to help the jailer, and he helped them too. Tomorrow, be gentle with someone who is hurting."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” Paul and Silas sang in the dark, then stayed to save the jailer, who washed their hurt backs. Willow thinks tender care flows both ways. In Willow’s world today: Willow’s cousin Mia breaks Willow’s favorite bracelet and cries, “I’m sorry!” Willow’s heart hurts, but she sees the tears. She gets a Band-Aid for the little scratch on Mia’s finger. “It’s okay,” she says. “We can make a new one together.” Just like the jailer and Paul, Willow learned that tender care can grow in the hardest places.","feel":"Sit with your feet stuck in pretend stocks. Now sing a quiet song in the dark. Feel the earthquake, shake, shake! Doors fly open. Then gently wash a friend’s sore back. Can you show me your gentle hands?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul and Silas sang in prison, then stayed to help the jailer, who washed their wounds. They all felt tender."],["NAME","That soft-and-careful feeling is called tender. Your mind might think, “Let me help you.” Your body might feel gentle hands, a soft voice."],["FIND","When did someone take care of you after you had been unkind to them, or you to them?"],["FORK","Paul and Silas could run free when the doors opened, or stay and save the jailer. They stayed."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and hum one quiet note like a song in the dark. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, make my heart tender.”"]],"remember":"Paul and Silas sang in the dark, and the jailer and his family were saved!","ask":["“What did Paul and Silas do at midnight?”","“What did the jailer ask Paul and Silas?”","“What did Willow do for Mia’s finger?”"],"share":["Share a time you sang or prayed in a dark hour and the Lord brought light.","Who needs you to stay instead of walk away, even when you could?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who found joy in a hard, dark time."]},"b19-w4-n3":{"story":["Some people in the city of Thessalonica stirred up trouble. So friends sent Paul and Silas away at night to Berea.","In Berea, Paul and Silas taught about Jesus at the meeting house. The people of Berea listened with eager, open hearts.","But they did not just nod along. Every single day, they searched the scriptures to see if it was true.","And it was true! The prophets long ago had promised Jesus would come. So many people in Berea believed in Jesus, women and men.","You can search the scriptures too. Tomorrow, open them with your mom or dad. Look for one thing about Jesus, and see for yourself!"],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see!” The people of Berea listened, then searched the scriptures every day to check. That is how Scout does things. In Scout’s world today: Scout’s friend says, “Jesus never got tired.” That night Scout asks Dad to help him look. They find Jesus asleep in the boat. “He did get tired!” Scout says. Dad grins. “That’s a Berean, right there. Always look for yourself.” Just like the people of Berea, Scout learned to listen eagerly and then search the scriptures to see for himself.","feel":"Cup your ear and listen hard. Now open a big pretend scripture book and run your finger down the page, searching, searching. Found it! Tap the page and nod. Can you show me your searching finger?","stones":[["SPOT","The Bereans listened eagerly and searched the scriptures every day to see if it was true. They felt eager."],["NAME","That I-want-to-find-out feeling is called eager. Your mind might think, “Let me check!” Your body might feel leaning shoulders and quick hands."],["FIND","When did you look something up for yourself instead of just believing?"],["FORK","The Bereans could just nod along, or search the scriptures to be sure. They searched."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and picture your scriptures open on your lap. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me find Your truth.”"]],"remember":"The Bereans searched the scriptures every day, and you can too!","ask":["“Where did Paul and Silas go after Thessalonica?”","“What did the people of Berea do every day?”","“What did Scout and Dad find out about Jesus?”"],"share":["Share a time you searched the scriptures to test something and found it true.","What question are you taking to the scriptures right now?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who loved to study the scriptures."]},"b19-w4-n4":{"story":["Paul came to Athens, a city full of statues of pretend gods. The people took Paul up a hill. “Tell us your new teaching,” they said.","Paul said, “I found an altar here with the words ‘TO THE UNKNOWN GOD.’ You do not know Him, but I will tell you about Him.”","“Heavenly Father made the world and everything in it. He gives everyone life and breath.”","“He is not far from any one of us. We are His children.” Some people laughed. But a man named Dionysius and a woman named Damaris believed.","Heavenly Father is close to you right now, even in the dark. You can talk to Him anytime."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big faith, brave heart!” Paul stood on a hill before the smartest people around and told them about the Father they did not know. Brave! In Beau’s world today: Beau is at a sleepover and the lights go out. A boy whispers, “Scared?” Beau says, “No. Heavenly Father is right here, even in the dark.” The boy scoots closer. “Tell me about Him?” So Beau does, until they fall asleep. Just like Paul, Beau learned that Heavenly Father is not far from any of us, and to say so bravely.","feel":"Climb a tall hill, step, step. Point at a statue, “To the Unknown!” Now spread your arms wide and say, “He is not far from us!” Can you show me your not-far arms?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul told the people of Athens that Heavenly Father is not far from any of us. He felt assured."],["NAME","That I-know-He’s-close feeling is called assured. Your mind might think, “He is right here.” Your body might feel a steady heartbeat and calm hands."],["FIND","When did you feel sure that Heavenly Father was close, even in a strange place?"],["FORK","Paul could stay quiet in the famous city, or stand up and tell them about Heavenly Father. He stood up."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel that in Him you live and move and breathe. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, You are not far from me.”"]],"remember":"Paul told Athens that Heavenly Father is not far from any one of us!","ask":["“What was written on the altar Paul found?”","“What did Paul say about how close Heavenly Father is?”","“What did Beau tell the boys when the lights went out?”"],"share":["Share a time you felt the Father “not far” when you reached for Him.","How do you talk about Heavenly Father with people who do not know Him?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who shared faith with people of different beliefs."]},"b19-w4-n5":{"story":["In the big city of Corinth, Paul met Aquila and his wife, Priscilla. They made tents, and so did Paul! So he stayed and worked with them.","Every Sabbath, the day to rest and worship, Paul taught about Jesus. Some people believed, but others argued and said unkind things.","One night the Lord spoke to Paul. “Do not be afraid. Keep speaking. I am with you. I have many people in this city.”","So Paul stayed in Corinth a year and a half, teaching. Many people believed and were baptized. Paul was never alone.","When things feel hard, remember the Lord’s words: “I am with you.” Keep trying, and keep being kind."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” Paul was discouraged, and the Lord said, “I am with you. I have many people in this city.” Willow thinks friends like Aquila and Priscilla are how He says it out loud. In Willow’s world today: Willow has invited her neighbor Sadie to Primary three times. Sadie always says no. Willow wants to quit. Grandma says, “Fear not. Keep being her friend.” The fourth time, Sadie says, “Okay, I’ll come.” Just like Paul, Willow learned that when the Lord says “Keep speaking,” good things are coming.","feel":"Sew a tent, in and out. Slump your shoulders, discouraged. Now hear the Lord say, “Fear not, I am with you!” Stand up straight and keep talking with your hands. Can you show me your keep-going stand?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul was discouraged, but the Lord said, “Fear not, I am with you. Keep speaking.” Paul felt encouraged."],["NAME","That I-can-keep-going feeling is called encouraged. Your mind might think, “He’s with me.” Your body might feel lifted shoulders and a second wind."],["FIND","When did someone tell you to keep going, and it turned out to be worth it?"],["FORK","Paul could leave the hard city, or stay because the Lord said, “I am with you.” He stayed."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel your shoulders lift. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for being with me. I’ll keep going.”"]],"remember":"The Lord told Paul, “Fear not, I am with you,” and He is with you too!","ask":["“What job did Paul, Aquila, and Priscilla share?”","“What did the Lord tell Paul in the vision?”","“What did Sadie finally say to Willow?”"],"share":["Share a time the Lord encouraged you to keep going when you wanted to quit.","Who are your Aquila and Priscilla, the friends who work beside you?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who kept serving through discouragement."]},"b19-w4-n6":{"story":["On Sunday in Troas, Jesus’s friends met to break bread and remember Jesus. Paul was leaving the next day. So he taught until midnight.","They were upstairs, three floors high, with many lamps. A young man named Eutychus sat in the window. He got sleepier and sleepier.","Eutychus fell fast asleep and fell out the window, all the way down! He lay very still, and everyone was scared.","But Paul went down and held him close. “Do not be worried,” Paul said. “His life is in him!” Eutychus was alive! His friends took him home, so comforted.","When something scary happens, we can stay calm like Paul. Tonight, give someone you love a gentle hug."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer says, “Wow, look at that!” A boy fell out a window, and Paul held him and said, “His life is in him!” Wren was never so scared, then so relieved. In Wren’s world today: Wren’s baby brother tumbles off the couch and goes quiet. Wren’s heart stops. Then Dad scoops him up, and he lets out a giant wail. “He’s okay,” Dad says. “Crying means he’s okay.” Wren laughs and cries at the same time. Just like Eutychus’s friends, Wren learned how good relief feels when Heavenly Father keeps someone safe.","feel":"Sit in a pretend window and listen to a long talk. Nod, nod, droop, droop, asleep! Gently tumble onto your pillow. Now let Paul scoop you up and sit up, alive! Can you show me your alive sit-up?","stones":[["SPOT","Eutychus fell from the window, and everyone was scared. Paul held him and said, “His life is in him.” They felt relieved."],["NAME","That phew-everything’s-okay feeling is called relieved. Your mind might think, “Thank goodness.” Your body might feel a huge breath out, wobbly legs."],["FIND","When were you scared for someone, and then found out they were okay?"],["FORK","Paul could panic with everyone else, or hold the boy and speak comfort. He held him."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly, then let out a big, long phew. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for keeping the people I love safe.”"]],"remember":"Paul held Eutychus and said, “His life is in him,” and everyone was comforted!","ask":["“Where was Eutychus sitting?”","“What did Paul say when he held Eutychus?”","“What did Dad say crying means?”"],"share":["Share a time a scare turned into relief and gratitude.","How has the Lord comforted you when you feared for someone’s safety?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who was kept safe in a close call."]},"b19-w4-n7":{"story":["Paul was going far away to Jerusalem. He might never see his friends from Ephesus again. So he asked their church leaders to meet him by the sea.","“You know how I lived with you,” Paul said. “I never wanted anyone’s silver or gold. My own hands worked to care for me and my friends.”","“Work hard and help the weak. Remember what the Lord Jesus said. ‘It is more blessed to give than to receive.’”","Then Paul knelt and prayed with them all. They cried and hugged him. Then they walked with him to the ship.","Tomorrow, give something away: a toy, a hug, or a kind word. Then feel how full your heart is!"],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see!” Paul worked with his own hands so he could give instead of take, and his friends cried at the ship. The secret was giving. In Scout’s world today: Scout saves his allowance for weeks for a remote-control car. At the store a boy’s mom says they can’t afford a birthday present. Scout buys the car and hands it to the boy. His hands are empty, but his heart has never felt so full. Just like Paul, Scout learned that it is more blessed to give than to receive.","feel":"Work hard with your hands, hammer, sew. Now hold out a gift with both hands and give it away. Kneel and pray with friends, then wave goodbye to a ship. Can you show me your giving hands?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul worked with his hands so he could give, and taught that giving is more blessed. His friends felt loved."],["NAME","That somebody-gave-me-their-best feeling is called loved. Your mind might think, “He really cared.” Your body might feel a tight hug and happy tears."],["FIND","When did someone give you their time or their work, and you felt loved?"],["FORK","Paul could take from his friends, or work hard and give to them. He gave."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and remember someone who gave to you. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for people who give me love.”"]],"remember":"Jesus said, “It is more blessed to give than to receive,” and Paul lived it!","ask":["“Why did Paul work with his own hands?”","“What did Jesus say is more blessed than receiving?”","“What did Scout do with the remote-control car?”"],"share":["Share a time giving blessed you more than receiving ever has.","What has the Lord given you that you could pass on this week?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who gave generously of their work, time, or love."]},"b18-w1-n1":{"story":["It was Jesus’s last night with His friends. He told them He was going away. Their hearts felt worried and heavy.","Jesus said, “Do not let your heart be troubled. Believe in Heavenly Father, and believe in Me.”","“In My Father’s house are many mansions, many beautiful homes. I go to get a place ready for you. I will come again and take you with Me.”","Thomas asked, “How can we know the way?” Jesus said, “I am the way, the truth, and the life.” Jesus is the path that leads us home to Heavenly Father.","Someone who loves you is getting a place ready for you. Tonight, whisper, “Jesus is getting a place ready for me.”"],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm knows how to soothe a troubled heart. Jesus told His worried friends, “Let not your heart be troubled,” and promised them a home. Willow feels calm just hearing it. In Willow’s world today: Willow’s family is moving to a new town, and her tummy is full of worries. Dad shows her a picture of the new house. “See that room with the window? I’m getting it ready just for you.” Willow looks at the picture a long time, and her heart goes calm. Just like the disciples, Willow learned that when Jesus promises to prepare a place for us, our troubled hearts can be calm.","feel":"Put both hands on your heart and feel it beating, maybe a little worried. Now breathe in slowly, breathe out slowly, and feel it settle. Can you show me your calm heart?","stones":[["SPOT","The disciples were troubled because Jesus was going away. He promised to prepare a place for them, and they felt calm."],["NAME","That settled, my-heart-isn’t-troubled feeling is called calm. Your mind might think, “He has a place ready for me.” Your body might feel slow breathing and soft hands."],["FIND","When were you worried, and someone’s promise made your heart calm?"],["FORK","The disciples could stay troubled, or believe Jesus’s promise. Jesus told them to believe."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly with your hands on your heart. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that Jesus is preparing a place for me.”"]],"remember":"Let not your heart be troubled; Jesus is preparing a place for you!","ask":["“What did Jesus say He was going to prepare?”","“What did Jesus say He is, when Thomas asked about the way?”","“What did Willow’s dad show her to calm her worries?”"],"share":["Share a time Jesus calmed your troubled heart with a promise.","What does it mean to you that Jesus is “the way, the truth, and the life”?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor you believe Jesus has prepared a place for."]},"b18-w1-n2":{"story":["On that same last night, Jesus kept talking with His friends. He said, “If you love Me, keep My commandments.” We show love by doing what He asks, not just by saying it.","Then Jesus made a promise. He would ask Heavenly Father to send another Comforter, a helper who comforts. The Comforter would stay with them forever.","He is the Holy Ghost, the Spirit of truth. He would live inside their hearts.","Jesus knew His friends would miss Him when He was gone. So He said, “I will not leave you alone. I will come to you.”","When you help without being asked, you show Jesus you love Him. Tonight, think of one thing Jesus asks, and say, “I can do that tomorrow.”"],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big heart, brave start!” Jesus said, “If you love Me, keep My commandments,” and Beau’s love is the kind that shows up in what he does, day after day. In Beau’s world today: Beau tells Mom, “I love you,” every morning. Today Mom is sick, and Beau sees the dishes piled up. Nobody asks him. He drags a stool to the sink and washes every one. Mom sees and says, “Now I really know you love me.” Just like Jesus taught, Beau learned that being faithful means showing our love by keeping His commandments.","feel":"Say, “I love You,” with your mouth. Now show it: pretend to help, to share, to pray, with your hands. Hold your two hands together, tight. Can you show me your love-and-do hands?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus said, “If you love Me, keep My commandments,” and promised the Comforter. The disciples felt faithful."],["NAME","That I’ll-keep-doing-what-He-asks feeling is called faithful. Your mind might think, “This is how I show I love Him.” Your body might feel busy hands and a steady heart."],["FIND","When did you show someone you loved them by doing something, not just saying it?"],["FORK","The disciples could love Jesus only with words, or keep His commandments. Jesus asked them to keep them."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly with your hands folded tight. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me show my love for Jesus by what I do.”"]],"remember":"If you love Jesus, keep His commandments, and He will never leave you alone!","ask":["“How did Jesus say we show that we love Him?”","“Who did Jesus promise to send so we would not be alone?”","“What did Beau do to show his mom he loved her?”"],"share":["Share how keeping a commandment has been a way of saying “I love You” to Jesus.","When has the Comforter stayed with you so you were not alone?","Did a grandparent or ancestor show their love for the Savior by faithful obedience? Tell their story."]},"b18-w1-n3":{"story":["Soon Jesus would go away. How would His friends remember everything He had taught them?","Jesus told them Heavenly Father would send the Comforter, the Holy Ghost. The Holy Ghost would teach them all things. He would help them remember every word Jesus had said.","Then Jesus gave His friends a gift. He said, “Peace I leave with you. My peace I give to you.”","“I do not give it the way the world gives,” Jesus said. The world’s peace leaves when things go wrong. Jesus’s peace stays, even on stormy days, because it comes from Him.","Jesus said, “Let not your heart be troubled, and do not be afraid.” Tonight, if a worry comes, say a little prayer and feel His quiet peace."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer pauses and ponders. Jesus said His peace is different from the world’s peace, because it stays. Wren has felt that quiet, staying kind of peace, and she treasures it. In Wren’s world today: A thunderstorm shakes the windows at bedtime. Wren is scared. Mom says, “Let’s pray.” They do, and the storm keeps going, but something inside Wren goes quiet and still. “The storm’s still here,” she says, “but I’m not scared anymore.” Mom says, “That’s Jesus’s peace.” Just like the disciples, Wren learned that Jesus gives a peace that stays, even when the storm does not stop.","feel":"Flutter your hands fast like a worried heart. Now hear Jesus say, “My peace I give you,” and slowly let your hands float down to rest on your blanket. Can you show me your peace hands?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus promised the Holy Ghost would teach them, and He gave them His peace. The disciples felt peace."],["NAME","That quiet-inside-even-when-it’s-loud-outside feeling is called peace. Your mind might think, “Jesus is with me.” Your body might feel still hands and a soft chest."],["FIND","When did you feel peaceful inside even though something scary was happening?"],["FORK","We can take the world’s peace that comes and goes, or Jesus’s peace that stays. Jesus offers His."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your hands rest. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for the peace Jesus gives me.”"]],"remember":"Jesus gives a peace that stays, so your heart does not have to be afraid!","ask":["“What did Jesus say the Holy Ghost would help us remember?”","“How is Jesus’s peace different from the world’s peace?”","“What did Wren feel inside while the storm kept going?”"],"share":["Share a time Jesus’s peace stayed with you when the storm did not stop.","How has the Holy Ghost brought Jesus’s words to your remembrance when you needed them?","Did a grandparent or ancestor carry the Savior’s peace through hard years? Tell about them."]},"b18-w1-n4":{"story":["Grapevines grew all over the land where Jesus lived. Branches grow from the vine, and grapes grow on the branches.","So Jesus used a picture His friends knew well. He said, “I am the vine, and you are the branches.”","Jesus said Heavenly Father is the gardener who cares for the branches. “Without Me you can do nothing,” Jesus said.","A branch gets its water and food from the vine. If it breaks off, it dries up and grows no grapes. When we stay close to Jesus, good things grow in us, like kindness.","We stay close to Jesus by praying and remembering His words. Tonight, say a little prayer, and be a strong green branch on His vine."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker loves to see how things grow. A branch connected to the vine gets everything it needs and grows big bunches of grapes. Scout wants to stay connected to Jesus like that. In Scout’s world today: Scout finds a branch that broke off the apple tree in the wind. Its leaves are already drooping. “It’s not connected anymore,” Dad says. Scout looks up at the branches still on the tree, green and full of little apples. “I want to be a connected branch,” he says. Just like the branches on the vine, Scout learned that staying connected to Jesus is how good things grow in us.","feel":"Stand one arm up like a strong vine. Now grow your other arm out from it like a branch, and open your fingers like grapes. Stay connected! Can you show me your vine and branch?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus said He is the vine and we are the branches. The disciples learned to stay connected, and they felt connected to Him."],["NAME","That I’m-attached-to-Him feeling is called connected. Your mind might think, “My strength comes from Jesus.” Your body might feel steady and growing, like a branch on the vine."],["FIND","When did you feel connected to someone who gave you what you needed?"],["FORK","A branch can stay connected to the vine, or break off and dry up. Jesus says stay connected."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly, like a branch drinking from the vine. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me stay connected to Jesus.”"]],"remember":"Jesus is the vine, you are the branch, and connected branches grow good fruit!","ask":["“What did Jesus say He is, and what are we?”","“What happens to a branch that is broken off the vine?”","“What did Scout find that had broken off the apple tree?”"],"share":["Share what keeps you connected to the vine day by day.","What fruit has grown in your life because you stayed connected to Jesus?","Did a grandparent or ancestor stay rooted in Christ through pruning seasons? Tell about them."]},"b18-w1-n5":{"story":["Jesus told His friends, “As the Father loves Me, I love you. Stay in My love.”","To stay, they should keep His commandments, as He kept His Father’s. He wanted their joy to be full.","Then He said, “Love one another, as I have loved you.”","“The greatest love is to give your life for your friends,” Jesus said. Soon Jesus would do just that. Then He said, “I have called you friends, not servants.” A servant just does the work without knowing the plan. But Jesus told His friends everything Heavenly Father told Him.","Jesus calls you His friend, too. Tonight, whisper, “Jesus is my friend,” and feel your heart grow warm."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” Jesus said, “I have called you friends,” and told His friends to love one another the way He loved them. Willow has never felt so loved. In Willow’s world today: Willow’s big cousin Jake is twelve, and the little kids are not allowed in his fort. Today he opens the door and says, “Willow, you’re my friend. You can come in.” Willow steps inside, and her heart feels so big it could float. Just like the disciples, Willow learned that Jesus calls us His friends and loves us with the greatest love.","feel":"Shake hands with a pretend friend, nice to meet you. Now pull that friend into a big hug, because Jesus’s friends get hugs. Can you show me your friend hug?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus said, “I have called you friends,” and that He would give His life for them. The disciples felt loved."],["NAME","That He-calls-me-friend feeling is called loved. Your mind might think, “Jesus wants me close.” Your body might feel a floating heart and warm cheeks."],["FIND","When did someone call you their friend, and your heart felt big?"],["FORK","The disciples could think of themselves as servants, or as friends of Jesus. Jesus called them friends."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your heart feel big. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that Jesus calls me His friend.”"]],"remember":"Jesus calls you His friend, and greater love has no one than this!","ask":["“What did Jesus say is the greatest love?”","“What did Jesus call His disciples instead of servants?”","“What did Willow’s cousin Jake say when he opened the fort?”"],"share":["Share what it means to you to be called a friend of Jesus.","How do you continue in His love on ordinary days?","Did a grandparent or ancestor speak of Jesus as a friend? Tell what they said."]},"b18-w1-n6":{"story":["Jesus told His friends what was coming. “You will be sad. But your sorrow will be turned into joy.”","He used a picture they knew. When a baby is born, the mom hurts. But holding her baby, she is so happy she forgets the hurt.","“You are sad now,” Jesus said. “But I will see you again, and no one can take your joy away.”","Then He said, “In the world you will have trouble. But be of good cheer! I have overcome the world.” Jesus is stronger than every hard thing. So we can be cheerful, even on hard days.","Tonight, tell your family one good thing about today, and be of good cheer."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big heart, brave start!” Jesus said, “Be of good cheer, I have overcome the world,” and Beau’s bravery is cheerful, because he knows who already won. In Beau’s world today: Beau’s soccer team lost badly, and his teammates are grumpy on the bench. Beau says, “Hey, we get to play again Saturday! And there are orange slices!” One kid laughs, then another. Soon the whole bench is smiling. Beau chose good cheer. Just like the disciples, Beau learned that we can be of good cheer in hard times, because Jesus has overcome the world.","feel":"Make a sad, drooping face, like sorrow. Now hear Jesus say, “Be of good cheer!” and let a huge smile spread across your face. Can you show me your good-cheer smile?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus said the disciples would be sad, but their sorrow would turn to joy, and to be of good cheer. They felt cheerful."],["NAME","That it’s-hard-but-I-can-still-smile feeling is called cheerful. Your mind might think, “Jesus already won.” Your body might feel a lifted chin and a real smile."],["FIND","When did something hard happen, and you found a reason to smile anyway?"],["FORK","The disciples could sink into sorrow, or be of good cheer because Jesus overcame. Jesus said be of good cheer."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let a smile stay. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that Jesus has overcome the world.”"]],"remember":"In the world you will have trouble, but be of good cheer: Jesus has overcome the world!","ask":["“What did Jesus say the disciples’ sorrow would turn into?”","“Why can we be of good cheer even when things are hard?”","“What did Beau say to his grumpy teammates?”"],"share":["Share a sorrow in your life that Jesus turned into joy.","How do you choose good cheer when tribulation comes?","Did a grandparent or ancestor stay cheerful through trouble because of faith? Tell about them."]},"b18-w1-n7":{"story":["When Jesus finished teaching, He looked up to heaven and prayed. His friends were right there with Him.","He said, “Father, the hour has come.” Then He told what living forever is about. It is truly knowing Heavenly Father and Jesus Christ, whom He sent.","Then Jesus prayed for everyone who would ever believe in Him. That means He prayed for you!","Jesus prayed that they all may be one, just like He and His Father. He wanted His followers close together, loving each other.","When your family kneels to pray together, you are one, just as Jesus prayed. Tonight, hold hands with your family and pray."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer pauses and ponders the fact that Jesus prayed for her, long before she was born. He prayed that all His friends would be one, together. Wren thinks that is a wonder. In Wren’s world today: Wren’s family is spread out all evening: Dad on the phone, Mom cooking, her brother in his room. Then it is family prayer time. Everyone kneels in a circle and holds hands. Wren looks around at all of them together and thinks, “This is what Jesus prayed for.” Just like Jesus prayed, Wren learned that being one, together in love, is what Jesus wants for His friends.","feel":"Hold up one finger on each hand, far apart. Now bring them together until they touch, and wrap your hands around each other. One! Can you show me your together hands?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus prayed for His friends and for us, that we would all be one. His friends felt together."],["NAME","That we’re-all-in-this-as-one feeling is called together. Your mind might think, “Jesus prayed for us to be one.” Your body might feel hands held in a circle and a warm chest."],["FIND","When did your whole family come together, and it felt right?"],["FORK","Jesus’s followers could pull apart, or be one as He prayed. Jesus prayed that we would be one."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel the people you love around you. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help us be one, like You and Jesus.”"]],"remember":"Jesus prayed that we would all be one, together in love!","ask":["“Who did Jesus pray for in His long prayer?”","“What did Jesus pray that His followers would be?”","“What did Wren’s family do that made her think of Jesus’s prayer?”"],"share":["Share what it means to you that Jesus prayed for you by name, in effect, that night.","What divides your family or ward, and how could you help them be one?","Did a grandparent or ancestor work to keep the family united in faith? Tell about them."]},"b18-w2-n1":{"story":["At the Passover supper, families remembered how Heavenly Father saved His people long ago. Jesus told Peter and John, “Go and get the Passover supper ready.”","They asked, “Where should we get it ready?” Jesus gave them a surprising answer.","“In the city, a man carrying a jar of water will meet you. Follow him into the house he goes into.”","“Ask the owner, ‘Where is the guest room for the Teacher and His friends?’ He will show you a big upstairs room, all ready.” Peter and John went, and everything was just as Jesus said.","Peter and John trusted Jesus before they could see the way. Jesus knows the way before we do. Tonight, tell Him, “I will follow You.”"],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker loves a clue. “Follow the man with the water jar” sounds like the start of a treasure hunt, and Peter and John trusted Jesus and followed it. Scout would have run the whole way. In Scout’s world today: Grandpa hands Scout a note: “Go to the big oak. Look for the red ribbon. Follow it.” Scout does not know where it leads, but he trusts Grandpa. The ribbon winds through the garden to a brand-new bird feeder with his name on it. “You knew the whole time!” Scout says. Just like Peter and John, Scout learned that when we trust Jesus’s words and follow, the way opens up.","feel":"Shade your eyes and look for a man with a water jar. There he is! Now tiptoe behind him, step, step, step, all the way to the house. Can you show me your following feet?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus told Peter and John to follow a man with a water jar. They trusted Him, and everything was just as He said."],["NAME","That I’ll-follow-even-before-I-understand feeling is called trusting. Your mind might think, “Jesus knows the way.” Your body might feel steady feet and curious eyes."],["FIND","When did you follow directions you did not understand, and they led somewhere good?"],["FORK","Peter and John could ask for a better explanation, or go and follow. They went."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your feet rest. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me trust Jesus and follow.”"]],"remember":"Jesus knows the way before we do, so we can trust and follow!","ask":["“Who were Peter and John told to follow in the city?”","“What kind of room did the owner show them?”","“What did Scout find at the end of the red ribbon?”"],"share":["Share a time you followed the Lord’s direction before you understood it, and it was just as He said.","What is Jesus asking you to prepare right now, trusting that He has already arranged it?","Did a grandparent or ancestor follow a prompting they did not understand? Tell their story."]},"b18-w2-n2":{"story":["At the Passover supper, Jesus’s friends started to argue. Each one wanted to be called the greatest.","Jesus said, “Kings boss people around, but not you. The greatest should be like the one who serves.” He said, “I am among you as one who serves.”","Then Jesus turned to Peter. He knew Peter would have a very hard night.","Jesus called Peter by his other name, Simon. “Satan wants to shake you, like wheat shaken in a sifting basket. But I have prayed for you, that your faith will not fail.” Jesus said, “When you turn back, help your brothers be strong.”","Jesus prayed for Peter before the hard thing came. He prays for you, too. Tonight you can rest, knowing you are loved."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm knows how good it feels to have someone on your side. Jesus told Peter, “I have prayed for you,” before Peter’s hardest night. Willow thinks that is the kindest kind of support. In Willow’s world today: Willow has a dentist appointment tomorrow, and she is nervous. At bedtime, Dad says, “I already prayed for you today, and I’ll be right there holding your hand.” Willow snuggles down. Knowing Dad already prayed makes tomorrow feel smaller. Just like Peter, Willow learned that Jesus prays for us before the hard thing comes, and that is support we can lean on.","feel":"Lean sideways like you might tip over. Now feel a strong, warm hand on your back holding you up, and lean into it. Can you show me your leaning-on-support body?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus told Peter, “I have prayed for you,” before Peter’s hard night. Peter was supported."],["NAME","That someone-is-already-praying-for-me feeling is called supported. Your mind might think, “I’m not facing this alone.” Your body might feel a hand on your back and a lighter chest."],["FIND","When did someone tell you they prayed for you, and it made you feel stronger?"],["FORK","Jesus could scold Peter for what was coming, or pray for him. Jesus prayed for him."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and lean back into your pillow. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that Jesus prays for me.”"]],"remember":"Jesus prays for you before the hard thing even comes, so you are never alone!","ask":["“What were the apostles arguing about at supper?”","“What did Jesus tell Peter He had done for him?”","“What did Willow’s dad say that made tomorrow feel smaller?”"],"share":["Share a time you felt the Savior had prayed for you before a trial.","Who could you strengthen, as Jesus asked Peter to do after turning back?","Did a grandparent or ancestor pray for you or your family before you were born? Tell about it."]},"b18-w2-n3":{"story":["After supper, Jesus went to the Mount of Olives with His friends. He told them, “Pray, so you will not give in to doing wrong.”","Then He walked a stone’s throw away, knelt down, and prayed.","“Father, if You are willing, take this cup away from Me. Yet not My will, but Thine, be done.” The cup was the hard, hurting work ahead.","Jesus chose Heavenly Father’s way. An angel came from heaven to make Him strong. Jesus was carrying all our sins and sadness, so He could heal us. It hurt so much that He prayed even harder.","When something hard comes, you can pray like Jesus did. Heavenly Father will send strength to you, too."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big heart, brave start!” Even Jesus, the bravest of all, needed strength in the garden, and Heavenly Father sent an angel. Beau knows that asking for strength is part of being brave. In Beau’s world today: Beau has to give a talk in Primary, and his knees are shaking. In the hall, he whispers, “Heavenly Father, please help me.” His grandma squeezes his shoulder and says, “You’ve got this.” He walks up to the microphone feeling a strength that was not there a minute ago. Just like Jesus in the garden, Beau learned that when the hard thing comes, Heavenly Father sends strength.","feel":"Kneel in your bed, or fold your arms, like Jesus praying. Now feel a gentle, strong hand on your shoulder, and sit up a little taller. Can you show me your strengthened shoulders?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus prayed, “Not My will, but Thine,” and an angel came to strengthen Him. He was strengthened for the hardest thing."],["NAME","That I-was-weak-and-now-I-can feeling is called strengthened. Your mind might think, “Heavenly Father sent help.” Your body might feel a steadying hand and a deeper breath."],["FIND","When did you feel too weak for something, and strength came after you asked?"],["FORK","Jesus could choose His own way, or Heavenly Father’s will. He said, “Not My will, but Thine.”"],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your shoulders rise with strength. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, strengthen me like You strengthened Jesus.”"]],"remember":"When the hardest thing came, Heavenly Father sent an angel to strengthen Jesus, and He strengthens you!","ask":["“What did Jesus pray in the garden?”","“Who came from heaven to strengthen Jesus?”","“What did Beau whisper before his Primary talk?”"],"share":["Share a time Heavenly Father sent strength when you had none left.","What cup are you asking to be taken away, and how are you praying “not my will”?","Did a grandparent or ancestor receive strength from heaven in a dark hour? Tell their story."]},"b18-w2-n4":{"story":["Men took Jesus away to the house of the high priest, the temple’s leader. Peter followed far behind. He sat down by a fire with the people there.","A girl looked at Peter in the firelight. “This man was with Jesus!” she said. Peter said, “I don’t know Him.”","Two more times, people said Peter was with Jesus. Two more times, Peter said no.","Just then a rooster crowed. Jesus turned and looked at Peter. Peter remembered: Jesus had said this would happen before the rooster crowed. Peter went outside and cried hard.","Jesus already knew, and He had already prayed for Peter. When we make mistakes, Jesus looks at us with understanding and love. Tonight, if you need to say sorry, you can."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm knows that a look can say everything. When Peter failed, Jesus turned and looked at him, and the look was full of understanding. Willow wants to look at people that way. In Willow’s world today: Willow’s little brother breaks Grandma’s teacup and freezes, waiting to be yelled at. Willow does not yell. She looks at him softly and says, “I know it was an accident. Let’s go tell Grandma together.” His shoulders drop, and he takes her hand. Just like Peter, Willow learned that Jesus looks at us with understanding when we fail, and we can look at others that way, too.","feel":"Make a scared, hiding face like Peter by the fire. Now imagine Jesus turning to look at you, soft and understanding. Let your face relax and your eyes get warm. Can you show me your understood face?","stones":[["SPOT","Peter said three times he didn’t know Jesus. Then Jesus turned and looked at him with understanding, and Peter wept."],["NAME","That He-knows-why-I-did-it, He’s-not-angry feeling is called understood. Your mind might think, “He sees that I was scared.” Your body might feel dropping shoulders and tears that heal."],["FIND","When did you do something wrong out of fear, and someone understood instead of yelling?"],["FORK","Jesus could look at Peter with anger, or with understanding. He looked with understanding."],["CALM","Breathe out slowly and let your shoulders drop. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that Jesus understands me.”"]],"remember":"When we fail, Jesus turns and looks at us with understanding, not anger!","ask":["“Where was Peter sitting when people said he was with Jesus?”","“What did Jesus do when the rooster crowed?”","“What did Willow say to her brother after he broke the teacup?”"],"share":["Share a time the Savior’s understanding, not His anger, met your failure.","Who in your life needs you to turn and look with understanding?","Did a grandparent or ancestor show understanding instead of judgment in a hard moment? Tell about them."]},"b18-w2-n5":{"story":["When morning came, the leaders of the people met together. They brought Jesus in to question Him.","They said, “If You are the Christ, the promised Savior, tell us.” Jesus said, “If I tell you, you will not believe.”","Then Jesus said He would one day sit beside Heavenly Father, in His power.","They all asked, “Are You then the Son of God?” Jesus said, “Ye say that I am.” That was His way of saying yes. He knew saying yes would bring Him trouble. Still, He told the truth, calmly and bravely.","You can be brave like Jesus. If someone asks what you believe, you can say, “Yes, I believe in Jesus.”"],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big heart, brave start!” Jesus stood in front of angry leaders and said, “Ye say that I am,” knowing what it would cost. That is the bravest kind of brave, and Beau wants it. In Beau’s world today: At the park, a group of older kids laughs at a boy wearing a CTR ring. “Do you believe that stuff?” one asks Beau. Beau’s heart pounds. He says, “Yes. I do.” Nobody says anything. Beau walks over and stands next to the boy with the ring. Just like Jesus before the council, Beau learned that being brave means telling the truth about who we are, even when it costs something.","feel":"Stand your back up straight and still, like Jesus before the council. Lift your chin. Say calmly, “Yes, I am.” Can you show me your brave, calm stand?","stones":[["SPOT","The leaders asked Jesus if He was the Son of Heavenly Father. He bravely said yes, knowing it would cost Him."],["NAME","That I’ll-say-the-truth-even-if-it-costs feeling is called brave. Your mind might think, “I won’t hide who I am.” Your body might feel a straight back and a steady voice."],["FIND","When did you say something true about yourself even though someone might laugh?"],["FORK","Jesus could stay quiet to keep safe, or tell the truth. He told the truth."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and stand tall in your heart. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me be brave like Jesus.”"]],"remember":"Jesus bravely said who He was, and we can bravely say we follow Him!","ask":["“What did the leaders ask Jesus?”","“How did Jesus answer?”","“What did Beau say when the older kids asked if he believed?”"],"share":["Share a time you stood up and said, “Yes, I am,” about your faith.","Where does it cost you something to say you follow Jesus, and what helps you say it anyway?","Did a grandparent or ancestor bravely declare their faith when it was costly? Tell their story."]},"b18-w2-n6":{"story":["After His prayer, Jesus crossed a little stream into a garden with His friends. Judas, who had turned against Jesus, knew the place. Jesus often went there.","Judas came with soldiers and helpers sent by the temple leaders. They carried lanterns, torches, and weapons.","Jesus knew what would happen. He did not hide. He stepped forward and asked, “Who are you looking for?”","“Jesus of Nazareth,” they said. “I am He,” said Jesus, and they stepped back and fell to the ground. Jesus said, “If you are looking for Me, let these men go their way.” He kept His friends safe. Not one was lost.","Jesus takes care of His friends. Tonight, think of someone little you can watch over, and be brave like Jesus."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker notices everything, and he noticed this: when the soldiers came, Jesus stepped in front of His friends and said, “Let these go.” Scout feels protected just thinking about it. In Scout’s world today: A big dog gets loose at the park and runs toward Scout and his little sister. Scout’s heart pounds. He steps in front of his sister and spreads his arms wide. The dog’s owner catches it just in time. His sister says, “You stood in front of me.” Scout says, “Like Jesus did.” Just like the disciples in the garden, Scout learned that Jesus steps forward to protect His friends.","feel":"Huddle behind your blanket like the scared disciples. Now step one foot forward and spread your arms wide, like Jesus standing in front of His friends. Can you show me your protecting stand?","stones":[["SPOT","Soldiers came to the garden. Jesus stepped forward and told them to let His friends go. The disciples were protected."],["NAME","That someone-stood-in-front-of-me feeling is called protected. Your mind might think, “He won’t let them hurt me.” Your body might feel safe behind strong shoulders."],["FIND","When did someone step in front of you to keep you safe?"],["FORK","Jesus could run or hide, or step forward and protect His friends. He stepped forward."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel safe behind Jesus. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that Jesus protects me.”"]],"remember":"Jesus stepped forward and said, “Let these go,” and He protects you, too!","ask":["“What did the soldiers carry into the garden?”","“What did Jesus say so His friends could go free?”","“What did Scout do when the big dog ran toward him and his sister?”"],"share":["Share a time you felt the Savior standing between you and harm.","Who is Jesus asking you to step in front of and protect?","Did a grandparent or ancestor protect their family at great cost? Tell their story."]},"b18-w2-n7":{"story":["The leaders took Jesus to Pilate, the ruler of the land. Pilate asked Him, “Are You the King of the Jews?”","Jesus said, “My kingdom is not of this world. If it were, My helpers would fight for Me.” Jesus was not a king with an army.","Pilate asked, “So You are a king?”","Jesus said, “I was born for this. I came into the world to tell the truth. Everyone who loves the truth hears My voice.” Pilate asked, “What is truth?” and walked away. Nothing he said could shake Jesus.","Jesus knew who He was. You can know who you are, too. Tonight, say it out loud: “I am a child of Heavenly Father.”"],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer pauses and ponders until she is sure. Jesus stood before the most powerful man in the land and said, “For this I was born.” Wren wants to be sure like that. In Wren’s world today: A girl at school says, “You’re weird.” Wren thinks about it for a second, then says, “I’m a child of Heavenly Father. I know that for sure.” The girl looks confused and walks off. Wren feels calm and sure inside, like a rock that nobody can push over. Just like Jesus before Pilate, Wren learned that being sure of who you are keeps you steady, no matter who is asking.","feel":"Make a confused, questioning face like Pilate, hmm? Now stand your back straight, put your hand on your heart, and nod slowly, sure. Can you show me your I-know-who-I-am nod?","stones":[["SPOT","Pilate questioned Jesus. Jesus said, “For this I was born.” He was sure of who He was."],["NAME","That I-know-exactly-who-I-am feeling is called sure. Your mind might think, “Nobody can change what’s true about me.” Your body might feel a straight back and a calm, steady heart."],["FIND","When did someone question you, and you knew for sure who you were?"],["FORK","Jesus could let Pilate’s questions shake Him, or stand sure in the truth. He stood sure."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly with your hand on your heart. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, I am Your child, and I am sure of it.”"]],"remember":"Jesus was sure of who He was, and you can be sure that you are a child of Heavenly Father!","ask":["“What did Pilate ask Jesus?”","“What did Jesus say He came into the world to do?”","“What did Wren say when the girl called her weird?”"],"share":["Share what you know for sure about who you are and why you were born.","When has standing in the truth kept you steady before someone powerful or unkind?","Did a grandparent or ancestor know for sure who they were before Heavenly Father? Tell about them."]},"b18-w3-n1":{"story":["Jesus stood before Pilate, the ruler from Rome. The leaders said untrue things about Him. Jesus did not answer.","At that holiday, Pilate let one prisoner go free. “Should I free Barabbas or Jesus?” he asked. He knew the leaders were jealous of Jesus.","Pilate’s wife sent him a message: “Leave that good man alone. I had a hard dream about Him.”","But the leaders got the crowd to choose Barabbas. The crowd grew loud and wild. Pilate washed his hands to say, “Not my fault.” Then he gave in and let them take Jesus.","That was not fair, and it is okay to feel upset. Jesus chose to give His life for us. Like Beau, you can speak up for what is true."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big heart, brave start!” Pilate knew Jesus was good and let the crowd take Him anyway. Beau feels upset when things are unfair, and he would have been the one to speak up. In Beau’s world today: At school, a kid breaks a window and blames Beau’s friend Sam. The teacher believes it. Beau’s face gets hot. “That’s not fair! Sam didn’t do it. I saw.” The teacher listens, and the truth comes out. Beau’s heart is still pounding, but he spoke up. Just like Pilate’s wife, Beau learned that when something is unfair, we feel upset, and that feeling can move us to speak up for what is right.","feel":"Cross your arms and make a frown, that’s not fair! Now uncross them, take a breath, and raise one hand like you are speaking up for someone. Can you show me your speak-up hand?","stones":[["SPOT","The crowd chose Barabbas and Pilate let them take Jesus. It was not fair, and it is okay to feel upset about it."],["NAME","That it’s-not-right, that’s-not-fair feeling is called upset. Your mind might think, “Somebody should say something.” Your body might feel a hot face and a tight chest."],["FIND","When did you see something unfair happen, and you felt upset inside?"],["FORK","Pilate could stand up for the good man, or give in to the crowd. He gave in. Jesus still chose to give His life for us."],["CALM","Breathe out slowly and let your chest loosen. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that Jesus went through what wasn’t fair, for me.”"]],"remember":"Jesus let unfair things happen to Him because He was choosing to save you!","ask":["“What did Pilate’s wife call Jesus in her message?”","“Who did the crowd choose to set free?”","“What did Beau say when Sam was blamed for the window?”"],"share":["Share what it means to you that Jesus accepted injustice willingly for your sake.","Where do you need to stop washing your hands and speak up for what is right?","Did a grandparent or ancestor speak up against something unfair? Tell their story."]},"b18-w3-n2":{"story":["Soldiers led Jesus out of the city toward a hill called Golgotha. They made Him carry a big, heavy wooden cross.","On the road came a man named Simon, from a faraway place called Cyrene. He was walking in from the countryside.","The soldiers stopped Simon. They laid the heavy cross on him and made him carry it.","Simon had not planned to help. But he carried the cross and walked behind Jesus. The Bible even remembers his sons’ names, Alexander and Rufus.","Simon helped carry a heavy load for Jesus. Like Scout, you can help carry heavy things too. Helping makes you feel strong inside. Tomorrow, look for someone who needs a hand."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker looks for ways to help. Simon of Cyrene was just walking by when he got to carry the Savior’s cross, and Scout thinks that is the most important help anyone ever gave. In Scout’s world today: Scout is riding his bike home when he sees old Mrs. Lee struggling to drag her trash can up the driveway. He was not planning to stop. But he hops off, grabs the handle, and pulls it all the way up. “Thank you, young man,” she says. Scout rides home feeling strong. Just like Simon, Scout learned that helping carry a heavy load, even when we did not plan to, is something Jesus never forgets.","feel":"Pretend to walk along, minding your own business. Now stop, lift a heavy pretend cross onto your shoulder, and carry it step by step up a hill. Can you show me your carrying shoulders?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus was too weak to carry the cross. Simon was made to carry it, and he helped carry the Savior’s load."],["NAME","That let-me-carry-that-for-you feeling is called helpful. Your mind might think, “I can take some of the weight.” Your body might feel strong shoulders and steady legs."],["FIND","When did you help carry something heavy for someone who was tired?"],["FORK","Simon could carry the cross grudgingly, or carry it with his whole heart. He carried it for Jesus."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your shoulders rest. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me carry loads for others, like Simon.”"]],"remember":"Simon carried the cross for Jesus, and we can help carry heavy loads, too!","ask":["“Where was Simon coming from when the soldiers stopped him?”","“Who carried the cross for Jesus?”","“Whose trash can did Scout pull up the driveway?”"],"share":["Share a time you were pulled into carrying someone’s burden and it became sacred to you.","Whose cross could you help carry this week?","Did a grandparent or ancestor carry heavy loads for others? Tell about them."]},"b18-w3-n3":{"story":["Jesus was on the cross. It was a very sad day. But Jesus chose it, because He loved us.","At noon the sky went dark for three whole hours. Jesus said, “I am thirsty,” and was given sour wine on a sponge.","Then Jesus said, “It is finished.” He had done all Heavenly Father sent Him to do. He bowed His head and gave His life.","In Heavenly Father’s holy house, a big curtain tore from top to bottom. The soldier in charge said, “Truly this man was the Son of God.”","Jesus did it all for you. Tonight, be still for a quiet minute, like Beau. Think how much Jesus loves you. Easter morning was coming!"],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big heart, brave start!” Even the Roman soldier said, “Truly this was the Son of Heavenly Father.” Beau bows his head, reverent, because this is the bravest thing anyone ever did. In Beau’s world today: On the drive to Grandpa’s, the family stops at a quiet overlook. Dad says, “Let’s have a reverent minute and think about what Jesus did for us.” Nobody talks. Beau bows his head. The wind is the only sound. He feels small and loved at the same time. Just like the centurion, Beau learned that what Jesus finished on the cross is the holiest thing, and we honor it with reverence.","feel":"Fold your arms and bow your head, as still as you can be. Breathe very softly. Now lift your head just a little and look toward the window, because Sunday is coming. Can you show me your reverent stillness?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus said, “It is finished,” and gave His life. The centurion said He was the Son of Heavenly Father. The people felt reverence."],["NAME","That hushed, holy, this-is-sacred feeling is called reverent. Your mind might think, “He did this for me.” Your body might feel a bowed head, folded arms, and a soft breath."],["FIND","When have you felt so quiet and holy inside that you did not want to make a sound?"],["FORK","The centurion could look away, or see who Jesus really was. He saw, and he said it."],["CALM","Breathe very softly with your head bowed. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for Jesus. It is finished.”"]],"remember":"Jesus said, “It is finished,” and that was for you, and Sunday was coming!","ask":["“What did Jesus say right before He gave up His spirit?”","“What did the centurion say about Jesus?”","“What did Beau’s family do at the quiet overlook?”"],"share":["Share, gently, what “It is finished” means for you.","How do you keep reverence for the Savior’s sacrifice in a busy life?","Did a grandparent or ancestor hold the cross and the Atonement especially sacred? Tell about them."]},"b18-w3-n4":{"story":["Two other men were on crosses beside Jesus. They had done wrong things. Jesus had done nothing wrong at all.","One man made fun of Jesus. “If You are the Christ, save Yourself and us!” he said.","But the other man said, “Don’t you fear Heavenly Father? We did wrong things. But this man has done nothing wrong.”","Then he turned to Jesus. “Lord, remember me when You come into Your kingdom.” He had no time to fix things. Jesus answered, “Today you will be with Me in paradise.” Paradise is a peaceful, happy place with Jesus.","Jesus did not turn him away. It is never too late to turn to Jesus. Tonight, if you feel sorry for something, tell Heavenly Father, like Willow."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” The man on the cross had nothing left but one sorry prayer, and Jesus forgave him right then. Willow thinks that is the warmest thing in the whole Bible. In Willow’s world today: Willow was unkind to her cousin all day. At bedtime, way too late to fix it, she feels awful. She prays, “Heavenly Father, I’m sorry. Please forgive me, and help me say sorry tomorrow.” A warm, light feeling fills her chest. It is not too late. It never is. Just like the man on the cross, Willow learned that it is never too late to turn to Jesus and be forgiven.","feel":"Hang your head down low, like you can’t fix anything. Now turn your face toward Jesus and whisper, “Remember me.” Let your face lift and soften. Can you show me your forgiven face?","stones":[["SPOT","One man on the cross asked Jesus to remember him. Jesus forgave him right then. The man was forgiven."],["NAME","That He-said-yes, I’m-not-too-late feeling is called forgiven. Your mind might think, “He still wants me.” Your body might feel a weight lifting and warmth in your chest."],["FIND","When did you say sorry late, and someone forgave you anyway?"],["FORK","The man could mock like the other one, or turn to Jesus. He turned to Jesus."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel the weight lift. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that Jesus forgives me, even late.”"]],"remember":"It is never too late to turn to Jesus, and He forgives right away!","ask":["“What did the second man ask Jesus?”","“What did Jesus say to him?”","“What did Willow pray at bedtime after being unkind to her cousin?”"],"share":["Share a time you turned to Jesus late and found He had not given up on you.","What does the thief on the cross teach you about the reach of the Savior’s mercy?","Did a grandparent or ancestor turn to the Lord late in life? Tell their story."]},"b18-w3-n5":{"story":["After Jesus died, His friends and the women from Galilee stood far off. They watched, and they loved Him.","Then a good man named Joseph asked Pilate for Jesus’s body. Joseph had not agreed with the leaders.","He wrapped the body in cloth. He laid it in a new tomb cut into rock. The women saw where Jesus was laid.","The women went home and made sweet-smelling spices for Jesus. But the next day was the Sabbath, Heavenly Father’s day of rest. So they rested and waited, as He asked.","Waiting is hard, but the women kept doing what was right. Like Wren, you can get ready while you wait. Tonight, think of one kind thing to make for someone."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer knows how to wait and ponder. The women could not fix anything. They could only prepare spices, keep the Sabbath, and wait for morning. Wren admires their patience. In Wren’s world today: Grandpa is in the hospital, and there is nothing Wren can do. So she draws him a picture, picks flowers from the yard, and puts them in a jar for when he comes home. “I’m getting ready,” she tells Mom. Waiting is hard, but getting ready helps. Just like the women who waited, Wren learned that patience means doing what is right while we wait for morning.","feel":"Pretend to mix sweet spices in a bowl, slow and careful. Now set the bowl down, fold your hands in your lap, and wait very still. Can you show me your patient waiting?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus was laid in the tomb. The women prepared spices and rested on the Sabbath, waiting. They were patient."],["NAME","That I’ll-do-what’s-right-while-I-wait feeling is called patient. Your mind might think, “Morning will come.” Your body might feel still hands and a quiet heart."],["FIND","When did you have to wait a long time, and you found something good to do while waiting?"],["FORK","The women could stop keeping the Sabbath in their sadness, or rest and wait as Heavenly Father asked. They rested."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your hands rest in your lap. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me wait with patience.”"]],"remember":"The women did what was right and waited, and Sunday morning was coming!","ask":["“Who asked Pilate for Jesus’s body?”","“What did the women prepare, and why did they wait?”","“What did Wren make while Grandpa was in the hospital?”"],"share":["Share a season of waiting when keeping the commandments carried you to morning.","What are you preparing now, in faith, for a morning you cannot yet see?","Did a grandparent or ancestor wait patiently through a long sorrow? Tell their story."]},"b18-w3-n6":{"story":["Near the cross stood Jesus’s mother, Mary, with her sister and Mary Magdalene. John, the friend Jesus loved, stood there too. They stayed close on that very hard day.","Jesus looked down and saw His mother. Even in His pain, He was thinking of her. Who would take care of her now?","Jesus said to His mother, “Look, here is your son.” He meant John.","Then He said to John, “Look, here is your mother.” He was asking John to care for Mary. From that very hour, John took Mary into his own home.","Jesus was giving His life for the whole world. Still, He made sure His mother was loved. Tonight, do one small thing to care for someone at home, like Willow."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” On the cross, Jesus made sure His mother would be cared for, and Willow’s heart is tender like that, always noticing who needs looking after. In Willow’s world today: Willow’s mom has the flu and lies on the couch. Willow brings her a blanket, a cup of warm tea, and her favorite pillow. Then she sits on the floor beside her, quiet, just in case Mom needs anything. Mom whispers, “You take such good care of me.” Just like Jesus with His mother, Willow learned that a tender heart takes care of the people who care for us.","feel":"Pretend to tuck a blanket gently around someone, smooth it, pat it. Now put your hand on your own cheek, soft and tender. Can you show me your gentle, caring hands?","stones":[["SPOT","On the cross, Jesus made sure John would take care of His mother. Mary and John felt His tender love."],["NAME","That soft, I’ll-take-care-of-you feeling is called tender. Your mind might think, “Who needs looking after?” Your body might feel gentle hands and soft eyes."],["FIND","When did you take care of someone who usually takes care of you?"],["FORK","Jesus could think only of His own pain, or make sure His mother was cared for. He cared for her."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and make your hands gentle. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me have a tender heart like Jesus.”"]],"remember":"Even on the cross, Jesus took care of His mother, and we can take care of ours!","ask":["“Who stood near the cross with Mary?”","“What did Jesus ask John to do for Mary?”","“What did Willow bring her mom when she had the flu?”"],"share":["Share a time you felt the Savior’s tenderness in the way someone cared for you.","Who has cared for you that you could now care for more tenderly?","Did a grandparent or ancestor take someone into their home to care for them? Tell their story."]},"b18-w3-n7":{"story":["After Jesus died, Joseph went to Pilate and asked for Jesus’s body. Joseph followed Jesus, but secretly, because he feared the leaders.","Then Nicodemus came too. Long ago, he had come to see Jesus at night, in the dark. Now he brought about a hundred pounds of sweet-smelling spices.","He was not hiding anymore.","Together the two men wrapped Jesus’s body in cloth with the spices. They laid Him in a new tomb in a garden. They had been secret followers. But when it mattered most, they stood with Jesus for everyone to see.","You can stand with Jesus too, not just in secret. Like Scout, you can pray where others can see. Tomorrow, try one brave way to show you love Jesus."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see!” Nicodemus first came to Jesus in the dark, but at the end, he came in broad daylight with his arms full of spices. Scout loves a story where someone stops hiding. In Scout’s world today: Scout always prays quietly at lunch so nobody notices. Today his friend Eli is sad, and Scout says, out loud, “Want me to pray for you? I pray every day.” Eli nods. Scout bows his head right there at the lunch table. He is not hiding anymore. Just like Nicodemus, Scout learned that being loyal to Jesus means standing with Him where everyone can see.","feel":"Tiptoe in the dark like Nicodemus sneaking at night. Now stand up tall in the daylight, carrying a big armful of spices, walking right down the middle of the road. Can you show me your daylight walk?","stones":[["SPOT","Joseph and Nicodemus had been secret followers. When it mattered most, they came in the daylight. They were loyal."],["NAME","That I’m-with-Him, I-won’t-hide-it feeling is called loyal. Your mind might think, “Everyone can know whose side I’m on.” Your body might feel a tall walk and open arms."],["FIND","When did you stop hiding something good about yourself and let people see it?"],["FORK","Joseph and Nicodemus could stay secret and safe, or stand with Jesus openly. They stood with Him."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and stand tall in your heart. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me be loyal to Jesus in the daylight.”"]],"remember":"Nicodemus came in the daylight, and we can stand with Jesus where everyone can see!","ask":["“When had Nicodemus first come to see Jesus?”","“What did Nicodemus bring?”","“What did Scout do out loud at the lunch table?”"],"share":["Share a time you stepped out of secret discipleship into the daylight.","Where are you still a nighttime follower, and what would daylight look like?","Did a grandparent or ancestor become openly faithful after years of keeping quiet? Tell their story."]},"b18-w4-n1":{"story":["Very early Sunday, as the sky grew light, two women went to Jesus’s tomb. They were Mary Magdalene and the other Mary.","Suddenly the ground shook! An angel came down from heaven and rolled the big stone away. He sat on it, shining like lightning, in clothes white as snow.","The angel said to the women, “Do not be afraid. Jesus is not here. He is risen, just as He said!”","“Go quickly and tell His disciples!” the angel said. So the women ran, a little afraid and full of joy. On the road, Jesus Himself met them! They held His feet and worshipped Him.","Jesus is alive! That is the best news ever. Tomorrow, share it like Scout. Shout with joy, “He is risen!”"],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see!” The angel said, “Come, see the place where He lay,” and then, “Go quickly and tell.” Looking and telling is Scout’s whole life, and this is the best news he has ever had to tell. In Scout’s world today: It is Easter morning, and Scout wakes up before everyone. He runs from room to room, knocking on doors. “Wake up! He is risen! Jesus is alive!” His sleepy sister opens her door and grins. “He is risen indeed,” she says. Scout does a victory lap around the house. Just like the women at the tomb, Scout learned that “He is risen” is news to run and tell with joy.","feel":"Shake like a little earthquake, rumble, rumble! Now see the stone rolled away and throw your arms up high. Run in place to go tell everyone! Can you show me your He-is-risen run?","stones":[["SPOT","The women found the stone rolled away and heard, “He is risen!” Then they met Jesus alive. They felt great joy."],["NAME","That best-news-ever, I-have-to-run-and-tell feeling is called joyful. Your mind might think, “He is alive!” Your body might feel running feet and a laughing heart."],["FIND","When did you get news so good that you ran to tell everyone?"],["FORK","The women could stay frozen in fear, or run with joy to tell. They ran with joy."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your joy settle into a glowing smile. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that Jesus is risen.”"]],"remember":"He is not here, He is risen! Jesus is alive!","ask":["“Who rolled the stone away from the tomb?”","“What did the angel tell the women to do?”","“What did Scout shout as he ran from room to room?”"],"share":["Share your testimony that Jesus is risen, in your own words.","When did the truth of the resurrection first become real joy for you?","How did a grandparent or ancestor’s faith in the risen Christ shape your family?"]},"b18-w4-n2":{"story":["After the day of rest, three women bought sweet spices for Jesus’s body. Very early on Sunday, as the sun came up, they walked to His tomb.","On the way they worried. “Who will roll the stone away for us?” It was much too big for them to move.","But when they looked up, the stone was already rolled away!","Inside sat a young man in a long white robe. “Do not be afraid,” he said. “Jesus is risen! Go tell His disciples, and Peter.” Peter had said three times he did not know Jesus. Still, Peter was named.","Jesus remembers you by name, even when you make mistakes. Tonight, think of someone you can forgive, like Willow’s friend Ava. Then whisper, “Jesus remembers me.”"],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” The angel said, “Tell His disciples, and Peter.” After all Peter’s mistakes, Jesus made sure he was named. Willow thinks that is the kind of remembering that heals a heart. In Willow’s world today: Willow had a fight with her best friend, Ava, and said she didn’t want to play anymore. The next day Ava’s birthday invitation comes, and inside it says, “Willow, I especially want YOU to come.” Willow’s eyes fill up. She was remembered, even after the fight. Just like Peter, Willow learned that Jesus remembers us by name, especially after we have failed.","feel":"Try to push a huge, heavy pretend stone, push, push, can’t move it. Now look up: it’s already rolled away! Put your hand on your heart and say your own name, because Jesus remembers you. Can you show me your remembered heart?","stones":[["SPOT","The women found the stone rolled away and heard the angel say, “Tell His disciples, and Peter.” Peter was remembered."],["NAME","That He-didn’t-forget-me feeling is called remembered. Your mind might think, “He still counts me as His.” Your body might feel a lump in your throat and a warm chest."],["FIND","When did someone remember you by name after you thought you had messed up too badly?"],["FORK","Jesus could leave Peter out after his denial, or name him on purpose. He named him."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and put your hand on your heart. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that Jesus remembers me by name.”"]],"remember":"Jesus said, “Tell the disciples, and Peter,” and He remembers you by name, too!","ask":["“What did the women worry about on the way to the tomb?”","“Whose name did the young man in white make sure to say?”","“What did Ava’s invitation say to Willow?”"],"share":["Share a time the Savior remembered you by name after a failure.","Who needs you to say, “and you,” to make sure they know they are still included?","Did a grandparent or ancestor feel remembered by the Lord after a hard mistake? Tell their story."]},"b18-w4-n3":{"story":["That same Sunday, two of Jesus’s followers walked to a village called Emmaus. They felt sad and talked about all that had happened.","Then Jesus Himself came and walked with them. But they did not know it was Him. “Why are you so sad?” He asked.","Jesus taught them from the scriptures that the Savior had to suffer, then rise.","At supper in the village, He took bread, blessed it, and broke it. Then they knew Him. It was Jesus! Then He was gone. They said, “Didn’t our hearts burn, warm and glowing, while He talked?”","They hurried back to tell the others. A warm heart about Jesus is the Holy Ghost saying it is true. Tonight, think of Jesus and feel that glow."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer pauses and ponders what she feels inside. The two friends felt their hearts burn while Jesus talked, and they knew. Wren has felt that warm burning, too. In Wren’s world today: Grandma reads the Easter story out loud, and when she gets to the empty tomb, something warm spreads through Wren’s chest, like sunshine inside. “Grandma, my heart feels warm.” Grandma smiles. “That’s the Holy Ghost telling you it’s true.” Just like the friends on the road, Wren learned that a burning, warm heart is how the Holy Ghost tells us the truth about Jesus.","feel":"Walk your fingers slowly along a long road, sad and droopy. Now put both hands on your chest and feel it get warm, warmer, warmest. Can you show me your burning-heart hands?","stones":[["SPOT","Two friends walked with Jesus without knowing Him. Their hearts burned as He talked. They felt warm inside."],["NAME","That glowing, burning-inside-my-chest feeling is called warm. Your mind might think, “This is true.” Your body might feel heat spreading in your chest and tingles in your arms."],["FIND","When did you hear about Jesus and feel warm inside?"],["FORK","The friends could ignore the burning in their hearts, or run and tell. They ran seven miles to tell."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel your chest get warm. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for telling my heart that Jesus is real.”"]],"remember":"When your heart burns warm as you hear about Jesus, that is the Holy Ghost saying it is true!","ask":["“Who walked with the two friends on the road to Emmaus?”","“When did the friends finally recognize Jesus?”","“What did Wren feel when Grandma read about the empty tomb?”"],"share":["Share a time your heart burned within you and you knew the Savior was near.","How do you recognize the Lord in the breaking of bread and in ordinary moments?","Did a grandparent or ancestor describe the burning in their heart that gave them their testimony? Tell what they said."]},"b18-w4-n4":{"story":["The risen Jesus met with His friends. He said all the scriptures written about Him had to come true. Then He helped them understand.","“You are My witnesses,” Jesus said. A witness saw something true and tells others about it. They had seen Jesus die and rise again.","He told them to wait in Jerusalem until power came to them from heaven.","Then Jesus led them out to Bethany. He lifted up His hands and blessed them. While He was blessing them, He was carried up into heaven. His friends went back to Jerusalem with great joy, praising Heavenly Father.","Jesus left His friends blessed and happy. Like Beau, you can feel blessed too. Tonight, ask for a hug or a blessing, and feel brave."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big heart, brave start!” Jesus lifted His hands and blessed His friends as He went up to heaven, and they went home full of joy. Beau feels blessed thinking of it. In Beau’s world today: Dad gives Beau a father’s blessing before the first day of school. Dad’s hands rest on his head, and the words are warm and strong. When it is over, Beau opens his eyes and feels brave and blessed and ready for anything. “Thanks, Dad,” he says. Just like the disciples at Bethany, Beau learned that Jesus’s blessing fills us with joy and makes us ready.","feel":"Lift both hands up high like Jesus blessing His friends. Now slowly rise up on your knees, up, up, toward heaven. Then settle down with a big, joyful smile. Can you show me your blessed smile?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus blessed His disciples and was carried up into heaven. They went home with great joy. They felt blessed."],["NAME","That His-hands-are-over-me, I’m-full-of-joy feeling is called blessed. Your mind might think, “He is watching over me from heaven.” Your body might feel a warm head and a light, happy step."],["FIND","When did someone’s blessing or kind words make you feel brave and ready?"],["FORK","The disciples could feel abandoned when Jesus left, or feel blessed and go with joy. They went with great joy."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel a blessing resting on your head. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for Jesus’s blessing on me.”"]],"remember":"Jesus blessed His friends as He went up to heaven, and His blessing is on you!","ask":["“What did Jesus do with His hands before He was carried up?”","“How did the disciples feel when they went back to Jerusalem?”","“What did Beau’s dad give him before the first day of school?”"],"share":["Share a time a blessing left you with great joy and readiness.","What does it mean to you to be a witness of the risen Christ?","Did a grandparent or ancestor give or receive a blessing your family still remembers? Tell about it."]},"b18-w4-n5":{"story":["On the first Easter evening, Jesus’s friends sat together in a room. The doors were shut tight, because they were afraid of the leaders.","Then Jesus came and stood right in the middle of them! “Peace be unto you,” He said. He showed them His hands and His side, and they were so glad.","Then Jesus said it again. “Peace be unto you.”","Next He gave them a job. “My Father sent Me, and now I send you.” He breathed on them and said, “Receive the Holy Ghost.” Jesus was sending them out to share His love. The Holy Ghost would go with them.","Jesus sends you, too. Tomorrow, do one kind thing for a neighbor, like Scout did. Then you can say, “Mission complete!”"],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker is always ready to go somewhere. Jesus told His friends, “As My Father sent Me, so send I you,” and Scout’s bag is already packed. In Scout’s world today: Scout’s Primary teacher says, “This week, I’m sending each of you to do one kind thing for a neighbor and report back.” Scout feels like a secret agent. He rakes Mr. Park’s leaves the very next morning. On Sunday he reports, “Mission complete!” Just like the disciples, Scout learned that Jesus sends us out with the Holy Ghost to do what He did.","feel":"Lock your arms across your chest like a locked door. Now open them wide, Jesus is here! Then point out the door and march: so send I you! Can you show me your sent march?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus came through locked doors, gave His friends peace, and sent them out with the Holy Ghost. They felt sent."],["NAME","That I-have-a-job-from-Jesus feeling is called sent. Your mind might think, “He trusts me to go.” Your body might feel marching feet and a straight back."],["FIND","When did someone give you an important job and send you to do it?"],["FORK","The disciples could stay locked in the room, or go where Jesus sent them. They went."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your marching feet rest. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, send me, with the Holy Ghost.”"]],"remember":"As Heavenly Father sent Jesus, so Jesus sends you, with the Holy Ghost!","ask":["“What did Jesus say when He stood in the middle of His friends?”","“What did Jesus breathe on the disciples?”","“What was Scout’s kindness mission?”"],"share":["Share a time you felt sent by the Savior to do something for someone.","How does the gift of the Holy Ghost change the way you go about your week?","Did a grandparent or ancestor go where the Lord sent them? Tell their story."]},"b18-w4-n6":{"story":["One day Peter and six of Jesus’s friends went fishing. They fished all night and caught nothing.","At sunrise, a man on the shore called, “Children, have you caught any fish?” They did not know it was Jesus. “No,” they said.","“Throw the net on the right side of the boat,” He said. The net was so full they could not pull it in!","John said, “It is the Lord!” Peter grabbed his coat and jumped into the water. He could not wait for the boat. On the shore, Jesus had a fire with fish and bread. “Come and eat,” He said.","Peter hurried to Jesus because he loved Him so much. Tonight, hurry to Jesus in prayer, the way Beau ran to his dad."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big heart, brave start!” Peter did not wait for the boat to row to shore. He jumped straight into the sea to get to Jesus. Beau loves that kind of can’t-wait, all-in eagerness. In Beau’s world today: Dad’s truck pulls into the driveway after a week away. Beau does not walk to the door. He bursts through it, runs across the lawn barefoot, and leaps into Dad’s arms before Dad can even shut the truck door. “I couldn’t wait!” Beau says. Just like Peter, Beau learned that when we know Jesus is near, we go to Him with eager, all-in hearts.","feel":"Pretend to pull a net that is way too heavy, pull, pull! Now hear, “It is the Lord!” and dive off the side of your bed, swim, swim, swim to shore! Can you show me your can’t-wait swim?","stones":[["SPOT","The disciples caught a net full of fish and knew it was Jesus. Peter jumped in the sea to get to Him. He was eager."],["NAME","That I-can’t-wait, I’ll-jump-right-in feeling is called eager. Your mind might think, “Get me to Him now!” Your body might feel like leaping and splashing."],["FIND","When did you see someone you love and could not wait one more second to get to them?"],["FORK","Peter could wait for the boat, or jump in and swim. He jumped in."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your swimming arms rest on the shore. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that Jesus is always waiting for me.”"]],"remember":"When you know it is the Lord, go to Him with your whole eager heart!","ask":["“What did the man on the shore tell the disciples to do with the net?”","“What did Peter do when he heard it was the Lord?”","“What did Beau do when Dad’s truck pulled in?”"],"share":["Share a time you could not wait to get to the Savior, and what you found when you did.","What makes you hesitate in the boat when Jesus is on the shore?","Did a grandparent or ancestor run to the Lord with an eager heart? Tell about them."]},"b18-w4-n7":{"story":["After breakfast by the sea, Jesus turned to Peter. Peter had said three times he did not know Jesus. Now Jesus asked him one question, three times.","“Peter, do you love Me?” “Yes, Lord. You know I love You.” Jesus said, “Feed My lambs.”","Jesus asked again, “Do you love Me?” “Yes, Lord. You know I love You.” “Feed My sheep.”","Jesus asked a third time, and Peter felt sad. “Lord, You know everything. You know I love You.” Jesus said, “Feed My sheep.” Jesus’s people are like sheep who need a kind shepherd. Now Peter would care for them.","Jesus forgave Peter and trusted him again. He trusts you with good things, too. Tomorrow, do one job well, like Willow feeding Peanut."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” Three times Jesus let Peter say, “I love You,” and then He trusted him to feed His sheep. Willow knows being trusted again is the warmest feeling. In Willow’s world today: Last week Willow forgot to feed the class hamster, and it was a big deal. Today the teacher says, “Willow, will you be in charge of Peanut this week?” Willow’s eyes go wide. “You trust me again?” The teacher smiles. “I do.” Willow feeds Peanut right on time, every single day. Just like Peter on the shore, Willow learned that Jesus forgives us and trusts us again with the things that matter most.","feel":"Hold up three fingers, one at a time, and say, “I love You,” each time. Now cradle a pretend little lamb in your arms and feed it gently. Can you show me your feed-My-sheep arms?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus asked Peter three times if he loved Him, then told him to feed His sheep. Peter was forgiven and trusted again."],["NAME","That He-believes-in-me-again feeling is called trusted. Your mind might think, “He is giving me another chance.” Your body might feel careful hands and a proud, warm heart."],["FIND","When did someone trust you again with something important after you had let them down?"],["FORK","Jesus could remind Peter of his failure, or trust him with His sheep. He trusted him."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and hold a pretend lamb close. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that Jesus trusts me. Help me feed His sheep.”"]],"remember":"Jesus forgave Peter and said, “Feed My sheep,” and He trusts you, too!","ask":["“How many times did Jesus ask Peter if he loved Him?”","“What job did Jesus give Peter?”","“What did Willow’s teacher trust her to do?”"],"share":["Share a time the Savior trusted you again after a failure.","Who are the lambs and sheep Jesus is asking you to feed right now?","Did a grandparent or ancestor spend their life feeding the Lord’s sheep? Tell about them."]},"b15-w1-n1":{"story":["A big crowd pushed close around Jesus as He walked. In the crowd was a woman who had been sick for twelve years.","Doctors had not helped her. She only got worse. She thought, “If I can just touch His clothes, I will be well.”","So she reached through the crowd and touched His robe from behind. Right away, she felt it. She was healed!","Jesus stopped and asked, “Who touched My clothes?” The woman came, shaking, and knelt down. She told Him the whole truth. Jesus did not scold her. He said kindly, “Daughter, your faith has made you well. Go in peace.”","Reaching out can feel scary, but she reached anyway. Tonight, reach out to Jesus in prayer. He sees you, too."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm knows how it feels to be tired and sad for a long time. The woman had hoped for twelve years. When she reached for Jesus, He turned and called her daughter. Willow loves that. In Willow’s world today: Willow has wanted to make a friend at her new church for weeks. Today she takes a big breath and reaches out her hand to a girl in her class. “Hi, I’m Willow. Want to color with me?” The girl smiles and takes her hand. Just like the woman in the crowd, Willow learned that when we reach out with hope, Jesus sees us.","feel":"Pretend you are squeezing through a big crowd, wiggle, wiggle. Now stretch your hand out as far as it can go and touch the edge of a robe. Can you show me your reaching hand?","stones":[["SPOT","The woman had been sick for twelve years. She reached out with hope and touched Jesus’s robe, and she was healed."],["NAME","That maybe-today, I’ll-keep-reaching feeling is called hopeful. Your mind might think, “If I can just get to Him.” Your body might feel your hand stretching out."],["FIND","When did you hope for something for a long, long time?"],["FORK","The woman could stay at the back of the crowd, or push through and reach for Jesus. She reached."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your hand rest on your heart. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, I am reaching for Jesus.”"]],"remember":"Reach out to Jesus with hope, and He will turn and see you!","ask":["“How long had the woman been sick?”","“What did Jesus call the woman after she was healed?”","“What did Willow say to the girl at church?”"],"share":["Share a time you reached for Jesus after a long wait and felt Him turn toward you.","What does it mean to you that Jesus called her daughter?","Did a grandparent or ancestor hold onto hope through a long illness or trial? Tell their story."]},"b15-w1-n2":{"story":["Jairus led the place where people met to pray. He fell at Jesus’s feet. “My little daughter is so sick,” he begged. “Please come touch her, so she will live.”","Jesus went with him. On the way, people came from Jairus’s house. “Your daughter has died,” they said. “Don’t trouble the Teacher anymore.”","Jesus looked at Jairus and said, “Be not afraid, only believe.” So Jairus kept walking with Jesus.","At the house, people were crying. Jesus said, “She is not dead, but sleeping.” Jesus took her mother and father to her room. He held her hand and said, “Little girl, arise.” She got up and walked!","When things feel too hard, Jesus says it to you. Say it tonight: “Be not afraid, only believe.”"],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big heart, brave start!” When the sad news came, Jesus told Jairus, “Be not afraid. Only believe.” That is Beau’s kind of brave: keeping faith when it looks too late. In Beau’s world today: Beau’s team is losing, and the big kids say, “Just give up.” Beau remembers, “Be not afraid. Only believe.” He keeps running, keeps trying, and makes a goal. Even when they lose, Beau feels brave inside, because he did not quit. Just like Jairus, Beau learned that faith means believing Jesus even when it looks too late.","feel":"Lie very still like you are sleeping. Now, when I say, “Get up,” sit up tall and stretch! Ready? “Little one, get up!” Can you show me how fast you can sit up?","stones":[["SPOT","People said it was too late for Jairus’s daughter. Jesus said, “Only believe,” and Jairus kept his faith."],["NAME","That I-believe-even-now feeling is called faith. Your mind might think, “Jesus can still do it.” Your body might feel a steady heart and strong feet."],["FIND","When did you keep believing something good could happen, even when others said no?"],["FORK","Jairus could give up when the sad news came, or keep believing. He kept walking with Jesus."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and keep your heart steady. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me not be afraid, and only believe.”"]],"remember":"Be not afraid. Only believe!","ask":["“What did Jesus say to Jairus when people said it was too late?”","“What did Jesus say to the little girl?”","“What did Beau remember when the big kids said to give up?”"],"share":["Share a time you held onto faith when everything looked too late.","What helps you keep believing when others laugh or doubt?","Did a grandparent or ancestor see Heavenly Father’s power after everyone else gave up? Tell their story."]},"b15-w1-n3":{"story":["Two blind men followed Jesus. Their eyes could not see the sky, or faces, or the path.","They called out, “Son of David, have mercy on us!” They were asking for His kind help. They believed He was the Savior promised from King David’s family.","Jesus went into a house, and the two men came in, too. Jesus asked, “Do you believe I can do this?” They said, “Yes, Lord.”","Jesus touched their eyes and said, “As you believe, so it will be.” And their eyes opened! They could see! Then they told people all over that land about Jesus.","Think of the good things your eyes saw today. A face you love, the sky, this book. Tonight, thank Heavenly Father for each one."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see, what could it be?” The two men could not see anything, and then Jesus opened their eyes. Scout can only imagine how grateful that first look felt. In Scout’s world today: Scout gets his first pair of glasses. When he puts them on, he gasps. “The leaves! I can see every leaf on the tree!” He turns in circles looking at everything. “Thank you, Mom,” he says. “I didn’t know I was missing all this.” Just like the two men, Scout learned that when Jesus opens our eyes, the best thing to do is be grateful and tell others.","feel":"Close your eyes tight and reach your hands out like you cannot see. Now open your eyes wide and look all around the room. Can you show me your I-can-see face?","stones":[["SPOT","The two blind men believed Jesus could help. He opened their eyes, and they felt grateful."],["NAME","That thank-you-so-much, overflowing feeling is called grateful. Your mind might think, “I can’t believe He did this for me.” Your body might feel warm, with a big smile."],["FIND","When did someone give you something so good that you had to say thank you over and over?"],["FORK","The men could sit by the road, or follow Jesus and ask for help. They followed Him and believed."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and open your eyes softly. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for everything I can see.”"]],"remember":"Jesus opens our eyes, and grateful hearts tell everyone!","ask":["“What did the two blind men call out to Jesus?”","“What did Jesus ask them before He healed them?”","“What did Scout see when he put on his new glasses?”"],"share":["Share a time Jesus opened your eyes to something you could not see before.","What are you most grateful for that Heavenly Father has let you see?","Did a grandparent or ancestor gain a testimony that opened their eyes? Tell about it."]},"b15-w1-n4":{"story":["Jesus called His twelve apostles, friends He would send to teach. He gave them power to heal the sick.","He sent them to the towns of Israel. “Heal the sick,” He said. “You were given good things for free, so give for free.”","Jesus knew some people would be unkind to them. So He told them not to be afraid.","He talked about sparrows, tiny birds that cost almost nothing. “Two sell for one coin,” He said. “But not one falls without your Father knowing. Even the hairs on your head are all counted. You are worth more than many sparrows.”","Heavenly Father knows every little bird. He knows how many hairs are on your head! Tonight, touch your hair and whisper, “He knows me.”"],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer says, “Pause and ponder, what a wonder!” Heavenly Father knows every little sparrow, and He has counted every hair on Wren’s head. Wren thinks that is the biggest wonder of all. In Wren’s world today: A tiny sparrow lands on the feeder outside Wren’s window. She holds very still and watches. “Heavenly Father knows that exact bird,” she thinks. Then she touches her curls. “And He knows every one of these.” She feels small and precious at the same time. Just like the sparrows, Wren learned that Heavenly Father knows every little thing, and she is precious to Him.","feel":"Make your hands into two little sparrows and let them flutter. Now touch your hair and count, one, two, three... Heavenly Father knows them all! Can you show me your sparrow hands?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus sent His apostles out and told them not to be afraid, because Heavenly Father knows every sparrow. They felt precious to Him."],["NAME","That I-matter-to-Him, He-knows-me feeling is called precious. Your mind might think, “He is watching over me.” Your body might feel safe and warm."],["FIND","When did someone remember a tiny thing about you, and you felt special?"],["FORK","The apostles could be afraid of unkind people, or trust that Heavenly Father was watching. They trusted Him and went."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and touch your hair. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that I am precious to You.”"]],"remember":"Not one sparrow falls without Heavenly Father knowing, and you are worth more than many sparrows!","ask":["“What did Jesus give His apostles power to do?”","“What does Heavenly Father know about the hairs on your head?”","“What landed on the feeder outside Wren’s window?”"],"share":["Share a time you felt known and precious to Heavenly Father.","How have you freely given what Heavenly Father freely gave you?","Did a grandparent or ancestor serve a mission or go out two by two? Tell their story."]},"b15-w1-n5":{"story":["Jesus’s apostles came back from teaching. Jesus took them to a quiet place near Bethsaida. But thousands of people followed.","Jesus welcomed the people, taught them, and healed the sick. At evening, the apostles said, “Send them away to find food. We are far from town.”","Jesus said, “You give them something to eat.” They said, “We only have five loaves and two fish!” About five thousand men were there.","Jesus had the people sit in groups. He looked up to heaven and blessed the food. He broke it, and the apostles passed it out. Everyone ate until they were full! Twelve baskets of food were left over.","Jesus made their little bit enough. Tonight, give Him your little things, like a kind word."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker loves to see what happens next. Five loaves and two fish for thousands of people? Scout watched Jesus bless it, and then he watched the baskets fill up. What a thing to see! In Scout’s world today: Scout’s family invites the new neighbors for dinner, but there is only one pot of soup. Mom adds water, Dad slices more bread, and Scout sets extra bowls. Somehow, everyone eats and is full, and there is still soup left. “Like the loaves and fish!” Scout says. Just like the hungry crowd, Scout learned that when Jesus blesses a little, it becomes enough.","feel":"Hold out your hands like a basket. Now pretend to break bread and pass it, pass it, pass it. Rub your tummy, nice and full. Can you show me your full-tummy face?","stones":[["SPOT","The people were hungry, far from town. Jesus blessed five loaves and two fish, and everyone felt full and satisfied."],["NAME","That full-tummy, I-have-enough feeling is called satisfied. Your mind might think, “There was plenty after all.” Your body might feel relaxed and content."],["FIND","When were you hungry, and then you got to eat until you were full?"],["FORK","The apostles could send the people away, or give Jesus the little they had. They gave it to Jesus."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and put your hands on your tummy. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for giving me enough.”"]],"remember":"Give Jesus your little bit, and He can make it enough for everyone!","ask":["“How much food did the apostles have?”","“How many baskets of leftovers were gathered?”","“What did Scout’s family share with the new neighbors?”"],"share":["Share a time Heavenly Father stretched a little into enough for your family.","What small thing could you give Jesus to bless and multiply?","Did a grandparent or ancestor feed others when they had little? Tell about them."]},"b15-w1-n6":{"story":["One day Jesus asked His disciples, “Who do you say I am?” Peter answered that Jesus was the Christ, the Savior Heavenly Father sent.","Later, Jesus took Peter, James, and John up a mountain to pray. As Jesus prayed, His face changed. His clothes shone bright white, like light.","Moses and Elijah, prophets from long ago, came to talk with Him.","Then a cloud covered them, and the friends felt a little afraid. A voice came from the cloud: “This is My beloved Son. Hear Him.” It was Heavenly Father’s voice! The friends kept it quietly in their hearts.","We hear Jesus when we listen to His words and do them. Be still a moment tonight. Feel how big and kind Heavenly Father is."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer says, “Pause and ponder, what a wonder!” On the mountain, Jesus shone like light and Heavenly Father spoke. Wren would have been very quiet and very full of wonder. In Wren’s world today: Wren’s family hikes to a high lookout at sunrise. The whole valley glows gold, and the sky turns pink and white. Nobody talks. Wren holds Dad’s hand and whispers, “Heavenly Father made this.” She feels tiny and full of awe. Just like Peter, James, and John, Wren learned that when Heavenly Father shows us something holy, we feel awe and we listen.","feel":"Close your eyes and pretend to be sleepy on the mountain. Now open them wide, like you see a bright, shining light, and go very still. Can you show me your still, awe face?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus shone bright on the mountain, and Heavenly Father’s voice spoke. Peter, James, and John felt awe."],["NAME","That so-big, so-holy, I-can’t-even-talk feeling is called awe. Your mind might think, “Heavenly Father is here.” Your body might feel very still, with a quiet breath."],["FIND","When did you see something so beautiful or holy that you went quiet?"],["FORK","The disciples could be scared of the bright cloud, or listen to Heavenly Father’s voice. They listened and kept it in their hearts."],["CALM","Take one slow, quiet breath. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, I will hear Jesus.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father says Jesus is His beloved Son, so we listen to Him!","ask":["“What did Peter say when Jesus asked who He was?”","“What did Heavenly Father’s voice say from the cloud?”","“What did Wren see from the high lookout at sunrise?”"],"share":["Share your own answer to Jesus’s question: “Who do you say I am?”","When have you felt awe in Heavenly Father’s presence, on a mountain or in a quiet moment?","Did a grandparent or ancestor have a sacred experience they kept in their heart? Tell what you know."]},"b15-w1-n7":{"story":["One day Jesus’s disciples started to argue. “Which of us is the greatest?” Each one hoped it was him.","Jesus knew what they were thinking. He did not scold them. He took a little child, a small one just like you.","Jesus stood the child right beside Him, where everyone could see.","Jesus said, “Whoever welcomes this child in My name welcomes Me.” Then He said, “The one who is least among you all will be great.” To Jesus, great does not mean big and important. It means kind and caring for others.","Jesus made room for a little child right beside Him. Who can you make room for tomorrow, in a game or at the table?"],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” Jesus brought a little child close and said that welcoming a child is welcoming Him. Willow loves to make little ones feel included. In Willow’s world today: The big cousins are playing a game, and Willow’s little cousin Sam is too small. He stands at the edge, sad. Willow says, “Sam can be on my team. I’ll help him.” Sam’s face lights up. He is part of the game now. Just like the child Jesus held, Willow learned that Jesus includes the littlest ones, and we can, too.","feel":"Make yourself as small as you can, curled up tight. Now let a big, kind hand (that’s Jesus!) help you stand up tall right beside Him. Can you show me standing tall beside Jesus?","stones":[["SPOT","The disciples argued about who was greatest. Jesus set a little child beside Him, and the child felt included."],["NAME","That I’m-part-of-this, I-belong-right-here feeling is called included. Your mind might think, “He wants me here.” Your body might feel tall and warm."],["FIND","When did someone bring you into a game or a group, and you felt included?"],["FORK","Willow could let little Sam stand at the edge, or bring him onto her team. She brought him in."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and stand tall in your heart. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for welcoming me.”"]],"remember":"Jesus set a little child right beside Him, and that is where He wants you!","ask":["“What were the disciples arguing about?”","“Who did Jesus stand right next to Him?”","“What did Willow do for her little cousin Sam?”"],"share":["Share a time you felt Jesus wanted you close, even when you felt small.","How do you make the littlest and least in your life feel included?","Did a grandparent or ancestor treat children with special kindness? Tell about them."]},"b15-w2-n1":{"story":["John the Baptist was locked in a prison. King Herod put him there because John said the king was doing wrong.","John heard what Jesus was doing. So he sent two of his friends with a question. “Are You the One who was promised, or should we look for another?”","John wanted to be sure. It was okay to ask.","Jesus said, “Go and tell John what you hear and see. Blind people see. People who could not walk are walking. The deaf hear. The poor hear the good news.” Prophets long ago said the Savior would do these things. John’s friends went back to tell him.","Like John, you can ask about Jesus. Tonight, name one kind thing He did."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see, what could it be?” John sent friends to look and see, and what they saw made him sure. That is exactly how Scout finds out what is true. In Scout’s world today: Scout is not sure the new babysitter is nice. So he watches. She helps his sister tie her shoe. She laughs at his joke. She reads the bedtime story with funny voices. Scout relaxes. “She’s nice,” he says. “I saw it.” Just like John, Scout learned that looking at what Jesus does makes us sure He is the One.","feel":"Cup your hand by your ear to hear. Now put your hand over your eyes to look far away. Nod your head slowly: yes, He is the One. Can you show me your sure nod?","stones":[["SPOT","John was in prison and wanted to be sure Jesus was the Savior. Jesus showed him, and John felt reassured."],["NAME","That okay-now-I-know, settled feeling is called reassured. Your mind might think, “It really is true.” Your body might feel your shoulders drop and a slow breath out."],["FIND","When were you not sure about something, and then you saw proof and felt better?"],["FORK","John could sit in prison with his question, or send and ask. He asked, and Jesus answered."],["CALM","Breathe out slowly and let your shoulders drop. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for showing me what is true.”"]],"remember":"Look at what Jesus does, and you will know He is the One!","ask":["“Where was John the Baptist when he sent his friends to Jesus?”","“What did Jesus tell the friends to go and tell John?”","“How did Scout find out the babysitter was nice?”"],"share":["Share a time you had a question about Jesus and the answer came by what you saw.","What has Jesus done in your life that makes you sure He is the Savior?","Did a grandparent or ancestor find their answer about Jesus by watching His works? Tell about it."]},"b15-w2-n2":{"story":["Many people around Jesus were tired. They worked hard all day, and some were sad or sick. The leaders’ many extra rules felt like a heavy load, too.","Jesus said, “Come unto Me, all you who work hard and carry heavy loads. I will give you rest.”","Then He said, “Take My yoke upon you, and learn of Me.”","A yoke is a wooden bar that joins two oxen side by side. Then they pull a heavy cart together, and neither pulls alone. Jesus said, “My yoke is easy, and My load is light.” When we walk with Jesus, hard things feel lighter.","Are you tired tonight? Tell Jesus what feels heavy. Let Him help you carry it."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm knows that tired little ones need rest and someone to help carry the load. Jesus said, “Come unto Me, and I will give you rest,” and Willow thinks that is the warmest invitation ever. In Willow’s world today: Willow carried her heavy backpack all the way home from the park. Her arms ache and her feet drag. Then Dad reaches down and says, “Let me carry that with you.” He takes one strap, she keeps the other, and the walk home feels light. Just like the tired people, Willow learned that Jesus will carry the heavy load with us and give us rest.","feel":"Pretend to carry a big, heavy sack on your shoulders. Ugh, so heavy! Now let Jesus take it. Drop your shoulders, sink into your pillow, and sigh. Can you show me your resting body?","stones":[["SPOT","The people were tired and carrying heavy loads. Jesus said, “Come unto Me, and I will give you rest.”"],["NAME","That worn-out, I-can’t-carry-any-more feeling is called tired. Your mind might think, “I need help.” Your body might feel heavy arms and droopy eyes."],["FIND","When did you feel so tired that you needed someone to help you?"],["FORK","The people could keep carrying their heavy loads alone, or come to Jesus. Jesus says come."],["CALM","Breathe out slowly and sink into your bed. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that Jesus gives me rest.”"]],"remember":"Come unto Jesus, and He will give you rest!","ask":["“What did Jesus say to people who are tired and carrying heavy loads?”","“What is a yoke, and why did Jesus talk about one?”","“How did Willow’s dad help with the heavy backpack?”"],"share":["Share a time you came to Jesus worn out and found rest.","What heavy load could you let Jesus help you carry right now?","Did a grandparent or ancestor find rest in Jesus during a hard, tiring season? Tell their story."]},"b15-w2-n3":{"story":["On the Sabbath, Heavenly Father’s day of rest, Jesus walked through grain fields. His disciples were hungry. They picked some grain and ate it.","Some Pharisees, leaders who cared very much about rules, saw them. “Your disciples are breaking the Sabbath!” they said.","Heavenly Father made the Sabbath for rest. But the Pharisees had added many more rules of their own.","Jesus reminded them that King David once ate holy bread when he was hungry. Then Jesus said Heavenly Father wants mercy, not sacrifice. He wants kind care for people more than extra rules. Jesus said He was Lord of the Sabbath, the One in charge of it.","Tonight, think of someone who needs kind care. How can you show them mercy tomorrow?"],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big heart, brave start!” Jesus stood up for His hungry friends in front of the rule-keepers. Beau knows that mercy, caring about people, takes a brave and big heart. In Beau’s world today: The rule at Beau’s house is no snacks before dinner. But his little sister missed lunch at the park and is crying with hunger. Beau asks Mom, “Can she have some crackers? She’s really hungry.” Mom smiles. “Yes. People matter more than the rule.” Just like Jesus taught, Beau learned that mercy, caring about people, matters more than extra rules.","feel":"Pretend to pick a head of grain and rub it between your hands. Now blow the husks away and eat the kernels. Can you show me your rubbing hands?","stones":[["SPOT","The Pharisees said picking grain broke the Sabbath. Jesus showed them mercy matters more. His disciples felt cared for."],["NAME","That people-matter-most, I’ll-be-kind feeling is called merciful. Your mind might think, “They need help more than I need to be right.” Your body might feel soft hands and a warm heart."],["FIND","When did you choose to be kind to someone instead of just following a rule?"],["FORK","Jesus could let the Pharisees shame His hungry friends, or stand up for mercy. He stood up for mercy."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and open your hands. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me be merciful.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father wants mercy, caring about people most of all!","ask":["“What were the disciples doing in the field on the Sabbath?”","“What did Jesus say Heavenly Father wants instead of sacrifice?”","“What did Beau ask his mom for his hungry sister?”"],"share":["Share a time you chose mercy over a rule, and how it felt.","How does Jesus being Lord of the Sabbath change how you keep that day?","Did a grandparent or ancestor show mercy when others were strict? Tell about them."]},"b15-w2-n4":{"story":["Some leaders were angry and made plans against Jesus. So He quietly went away. But a big crowd followed Him, and He healed them all.","Long before, the prophet Isaiah talked about Jesus. Isaiah said the Savior would not shout or fight in the streets. He would be gentle.","Isaiah said, “A bruised reed He will not break.”","A reed is a thin plant that grows by the water. When it is bent, it snaps easily. Isaiah also spoke of a little lamp wick, almost out. Jesus will not blow it out. He is gentle with all who are weak and hurting.","When you feel bent or small, Jesus is gentle with you. Tomorrow, you can be gentle with someone small, too."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm has the gentlest hands. Jesus does not break a bruised reed, and Willow treats every hurting thing the same way: softly, carefully, so it can get strong again. In Willow’s world today: Willow finds a flower stem bent over in the garden, almost broken. She does not pull it off. She gets a little stick and some string and gently ties the stem straight. A week later, the flower blooms. “You just needed some help,” Willow whispers. Just like Isaiah said about Jesus, Willow learned that tender hands do not break what is bruised; they help it heal.","feel":"Hold up one finger like a thin reed and bend it over gently. Now use your other hand to help it slowly stand up straight. Can you show me your gentle helping hand?","stones":[["SPOT","People were bent and hurting, and Jesus did not break them. He healed them gently. They felt His tenderness."],["NAME","That soft, I’ll-be-careful-with-you feeling is called tender. Your mind might think, “Gently, gently.” Your body might feel light, slow hands."],["FIND","When did someone treat you gently when you felt bent or small?"],["FORK","Jesus could push the weak away, or gently heal them. Jesus gently healed every one."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and make your hands soft. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that Jesus is gentle with me.”"]],"remember":"Jesus will not break a bruised reed; He is gentle with you!","ask":["“What did Jesus do for the sick people who followed Him?”","“What is a bruised reed, and what does Jesus do with one?”","“How did Willow help the bent flower?”"],"share":["Share a time Jesus was tender with you when you felt bruised or nearly out.","Who in your life is a bruised reed right now, and how could you be gentle with them?","Was there a grandparent or ancestor with a gentle, healing way with people? Tell about them."]},"b15-w2-n5":{"story":["Jesus’s friends asked Him to teach them to pray. So He told a story. A man’s friend came at midnight, hungry from a long trip. But the man had no bread.","He knocked at his neighbor’s door. “Friend, lend me three loaves of bread,” he said.","The neighbor called from inside, “Don’t bother me! The door is shut, and my children are in bed.”","But the man kept asking. So the neighbor got up and gave him bread. Jesus said, “Ask, and it will be given to you. Knock, and it will be opened.” Heavenly Father loves to give the Holy Ghost to those who ask.","If a sleepy neighbor helps, Heavenly Father will help even more. Tonight, ask Him in prayer, and keep asking."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker never gives up on finding what he needs. The man at midnight kept knocking until he got the bread. Scout keeps praying the same way, because Heavenly Father always hears. In Scout’s world today: Scout wants to find his lost library book. He looks under the bed. Nothing. He prays and looks again, in the closet, in the car, in his sister’s room. Still nothing. He prays one more time and checks the couch cushions. There it is! “I kept looking,” Scout says. Just like the friend at midnight, Scout learned that when we keep asking, Heavenly Father gives good things.","feel":"Knock on your headboard: knock, knock. Nobody answers. Knock again, knock, knock, knock! Now hold out your hands for three loaves of bread. Can you show me your keep-knocking hand?","stones":[["SPOT","The man needed bread at midnight and kept knocking until he got it. He felt persistent, and it worked."],["NAME","That I-won’t-stop-asking, keep-knocking feeling is called persistent. Your mind might think, “Just one more try.” Your body might feel ready hands and steady feet."],["FIND","When did you keep trying and trying until you finally got what you needed?"],["FORK","The man could go home with no bread, or keep knocking. He kept knocking."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your knocking hand rest. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for always hearing me.”"]],"remember":"Keep asking, keep knocking, and Heavenly Father will give you good things!","ask":["“Why did the man go to his neighbor’s house at midnight?”","“What did the neighbor say at first?”","“Where did Scout finally find his library book?”"],"share":["Share a prayer you kept praying until the answer came.","What good gift has Heavenly Father given you because you kept asking?","Did a grandparent or ancestor keep praying for years about something? Tell about their persistence."]},"b15-w2-n6":{"story":["Jesus was teaching the people. His mother and His brothers came and stood outside. They wanted to talk with Him.","Someone told Jesus, “Your mother and Your brothers are standing outside.”","Jesus looked at His disciples, the friends who followed Him and learned from Him. Then He said something surprising.","He stretched out His hand toward them. “Here are My mother and My brothers!” He said. “Whoever obeys My Father in heaven is My brother, and sister, and mother.” Jesus loved His own family. He wanted everyone to belong to His family, too.","When you do what Heavenly Father asks, you belong to Jesus’s family. Look at the faces you love tonight, and feel your heart grow big."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer pauses and ponders. Jesus stretched out His hand and said that everyone who follows Heavenly Father is His family. Wren wonders at how big that family is, and that she is in it. In Wren’s world today: At the family reunion, Wren meets cousins she has never seen before. Grandma points at each one. “This is your family, and this, and this.” Wren looks around at all the faces. “I belong to all of them,” she thinks, and feels her heart get bigger. Just like the disciples, Wren learned that everyone who follows Heavenly Father belongs to Jesus’s family.","feel":"Stretch out your hand like Jesus pointing at His disciples. Now sweep your arm all the way around the room: all of these are family! Can you show me your family sweep?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus stretched out His hand and called His followers His family. The disciples felt they belonged."],["NAME","That I’m-one-of-them, this-is-my-family feeling is called belonging. Your mind might think, “I have a place here.” Your body might feel warm and settled."],["FIND","When did you feel like you belonged, with family or friends who wanted you there?"],["FORK","The disciples could feel like strangers, or feel like family. Jesus made them family."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and put your hand on your heart. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that I belong to Your family.”"]],"remember":"Whoever does what Heavenly Father asks is Jesus’s brother, sister, and mother!","ask":["“Who was standing outside the house, wanting to talk with Jesus?”","“Who did Jesus say is His brother, sister, and mother?”","“Where did Wren meet cousins she had never seen?”"],"share":["Share a time you felt you belonged to the family of Jesus.","How does being part of Jesus’s family change how you treat others at church?","How did a grandparent or ancestor help your family belong to Heavenly Father’s family?"]},"b15-w2-n7":{"story":["Some leaders came to Jesus. “Teacher, show us a sign,” they said. They wanted a big miracle to prove who He was. They had already seen Him heal many people.","Jesus said they would get only one sign, the sign of the prophet Jonah.","Long ago, Jonah was inside a great fish for three days and three nights. Then he came out alive.","Jesus said He would be like Jonah. He would die and be buried for three days. Then He would live again! The people of Nineveh listened to Jonah, felt sorry, and changed. But these leaders would not change.","When we do something wrong, we can say sorry and change, like Nineveh. Then the twisty feeling inside melts away."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big heart, brave start!” The bravest thing the people of Nineveh did was say, “We were wrong,” and change. Beau knows that saying sorry takes a strong heart. In Beau’s world today: Beau took his sister’s markers without asking and lost the blue one. His tummy feels twisty all afternoon. Finally he goes to her and says, “I took your markers. I’m sorry. I’ll give you mine.” She hugs him. The twisty feeling melts away. Just like the people of Nineveh, Beau learned that saying sorry and changing is the sign of a strong heart.","feel":"Curl up small like Jonah inside the big fish, one, two, three days. Now burst out onto the dry land and stand tall! Can you show me your out-of-the-fish stretch?","stones":[["SPOT","The leaders wanted a sign but would not change. Jesus told about Jonah and Nineveh, who were sorry and changed."],["NAME","That I-did-wrong, I-want-to-fix-it feeling is called sorry. Your mind might think, “I need to make this right.” Your body might feel a twisty tummy and a quiet voice."],["FIND","When did you do something wrong and felt twisty inside until you said sorry?"],["FORK","The leaders could keep asking for signs, or be sorry and change like Nineveh. Jesus asked them to change."],["CALM","Breathe out slowly and let the twisty feeling go. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, I’m sorry. Help me change.”"]],"remember":"Jesus is greater than Jonah, and He rose on the third day, so we can say sorry and change!","ask":["“How many days was Jonah inside the great fish?”","“What did Jesus say would happen to Him for three days?”","“What did Beau say to his sister about the markers?”"],"share":["Share a time you said sorry and changed, and how Jesus helped you.","What does the sign of Jonah, Jesus rising on the third day, mean to you?","Did a grandparent or ancestor make a big change in their life for the gospel? Tell their story."]},"b15-w3-n1":{"story":["One day Jesus’s disciples asked Him, “Why do You teach with parables?”","A parable is a little story about everyday things, like seeds and sheep. Inside hides a big lesson, like treasure in a box.","Jesus said many people heard His stories but did not really listen. Their hearts were shut tight. They heard a story and walked away.","Then Jesus told His disciples, “Your eyes are blessed, for they see. Your ears are blessed, for they hear.” Prophets long ago had wished to see what they saw. The disciples’ hearts were open, so they found the treasure.","Like Scout, you can find treasure too. Tonight, listen with an open heart and ask, “What is the treasure inside?”"],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see, what could it be?” A parable is a story with treasure hidden inside, and nobody loves a treasure hunt like Scout. In Scout’s world today: Grandpa tells Scout a story about a stubborn donkey that would not cross a bridge. Scout laughs, then thinks. “Grandpa, is that story about me and my swimming lessons?” Grandpa winks. Scout found the treasure inside the story. Just like the disciples, Scout learned that when our eyes and ears are open, Jesus’s stories fill us with light.","feel":"Cover your ears with your hands, then uncover them: open ears! Cover your eyes, then uncover them: open eyes! Can you show me your open eyes and open ears?","stones":[["SPOT","The disciples asked why Jesus told stories. They were curious, and their open hearts found the treasure."],["NAME","That what’s-inside, I-want-to-find-out feeling is called curious. Your mind might think, “What does it mean?” Your body might feel leaning in and listening hard."],["FIND","When did you hear a story and figure out what it was really about?"],["FORK","People could hear a parable and walk away, or think about it and find the treasure. The disciples looked for the treasure."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your face be open and soft. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, open my eyes and my ears.”"]],"remember":"Open eyes and open ears find the treasure in Jesus’s stories!","ask":["“What is a parable?”","“What did Jesus say about His disciples’ eyes and ears?”","“What did Scout figure out about Grandpa’s donkey story?”"],"share":["Share a parable of Jesus that opened your eyes to something new.","How do you keep your heart open so Jesus’s words can fill you with light?","Did a grandparent or ancestor teach with stories? Share one they told."]},"b15-w3-n2":{"story":["Jesus told a parable about a farmer. He planted good wheat seeds in his field.","But one night, while everyone slept, an enemy sneaked in. He planted weeds in the wheat.","When the plants grew, the weeds grew too. The farmer’s helpers asked, “Should we pull up the weeds?” The farmer said, “No. You would pull up the wheat too. Let them grow together until harvest.”","Later Jesus explained. He was the farmer, and the field was the world. The weeds were people choosing wrong, planted by Satan. At harvest, angels would sort them out. The good wheat would shine like the sun.","Like Wren, you do not have to fix everyone. Be kind. Tonight, tell Heavenly Father your worries. He will do the rest."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer pauses and ponders. Good and bad grow side by side, and Wren does not have to sort them out, because Heavenly Father is in charge. That makes Wren calm. In Wren’s world today: Some kids at the park are being mean, and some are kind. Wren feels upset. “Why doesn’t somebody make them stop?” Mom kneels down. “You can’t fix everybody, Wren. Just be kind, and let Heavenly Father take care of the rest.” Wren breathes out, calm. Just like the farmer’s helpers, Wren learned to stay calm and grow good, because Heavenly Father will sort it out at harvest time.","feel":"Make your fingers wiggle up like growing wheat. Now add some pokey weed fingers next to them. Let them all grow, and take a calm breath. Can you show me your calm growing hands?","stones":[["SPOT","Weeds grew among the wheat. The helpers wanted to pull them, but the farmer stayed calm and waited."],["NAME","That it’s-okay, Heavenly-Father-is-in-charge feeling is called calm. Your mind might think, “I don’t have to fix everything.” Your body might feel slow breathing and loose shoulders."],["FIND","When did you feel upset about something you could not fix, and then felt calm when you let it go?"],["FORK","The helpers could tear up the field trying to fix it, or trust the farmer and wait. They waited."],["CALM","Breathe out slowly and let your shoulders go loose. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, You are in charge, and I can be calm.”"]],"remember":"Grow strong and good, and let Heavenly Father take care of the harvest!","ask":["“Who sneaked in and planted weeds in the farmer’s field?”","“Why did the farmer say not to pull up the weeds yet?”","“What did Wren’s mom tell her about the kids at the park?”"],"share":["Share a time you learned to let Heavenly Father be in charge instead of fixing everything.","How do you stay good wheat when weeds are growing all around you?","Did a grandparent or ancestor stay faithful and calm among people who were not? Tell about them."]},"b15-w3-n3":{"story":["Jesus told three tiny parables about Heavenly Father’s kingdom. First, He said it was like a little yeast a woman hid in flour. Soon the whole dough grew big.","Next, He said it was like treasure hidden in a field. A man found it and hid it again.","Then, full of joy, he sold everything he had to buy that field!","Last, Jesus told about a merchant looking for beautiful pearls. He found one pearl worth more than all the rest. So he sold everything and bought it. Jesus taught that following Heavenly Father is worth more than anything.","Scout gave his bucket and shovel for one perfect shell. Tonight, thank Heavenly Father for Jesus, the best treasure of all."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker lives for the moment of finding. The man found treasure, the merchant found the perfect pearl, and both were so delighted they gave everything for it. In Scout’s world today: At the beach, Scout digs and digs and finds a perfect spiral shell, shiny and whole. He gasps. He trades his bucket, his shovel, and his snack to his sister so he can carry it home in both hands. “This is the best thing I ever found,” he says. Just like the man who found the treasure, Scout learned that Heavenly Father’s kingdom is worth more than everything else.","feel":"Pretend to dig in the dirt, dig, dig, dig. Suddenly, treasure! Lift it up high and do a happy wiggle. Can you show me your I-found-it wiggle?","stones":[["SPOT","The man found hidden treasure, and the merchant found a perfect pearl. They felt delighted and gave everything for it."],["NAME","That I-found-the-best-thing, bursting-happy feeling is called delighted. Your mind might think, “Nothing is better than this!” Your body might feel a gasp and a big grin."],["FIND","When did you find something so wonderful that you would give up anything else for it?"],["FORK","The man could leave the treasure in the field, or give everything to have it. He joyfully gave everything."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and hold your treasure close to your heart. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, Your kingdom is my treasure.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father’s kingdom is the treasure worth more than everything!","ask":["“What did the man do when he found treasure in the field?”","“What did the merchant give up for the pearl of great price?”","“What did Scout find at the beach?”"],"share":["Share when the gospel became your pearl of great price, and what you gave for it.","What is one thing you are willing to give up to put Heavenly Father’s kingdom first?","Did a grandparent or ancestor give up a great deal to join or keep the gospel? Tell their story."]},"b15-w3-n4":{"story":["Jesus went from town to town, telling the good news about Heavenly Father’s kingdom. His twelve apostles went with Him. Some women came too.","One was Mary Magdalene. Jesus had made her well. Joanna came, too. Her husband worked for King Herod. Susanna and many others came as well.","Jesus had healed these women, and now they helped Him.","They used their own money and things to care for Jesus and His apostles. That gave them what they needed as they walked and taught. The Bible still tells their names, because their help mattered.","Like Willow helping Dad, you can be a helper too. Tomorrow, find one way to help your family."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm loves to help. Mary Magdalene, Joanna, Susanna, and the others used what they had to take care of Jesus and His apostles, and Willow thinks helpers are heroes. In Willow’s world today: Dad is making dinner for the family and for a sick neighbor, and he looks tired. Willow pulls up a stool. She washes the carrots, sets out the napkins, and carries the rolls. “I’m your helper,” she says. Dad says, “You sure are.” Just like the women who followed Jesus, Willow learned that helping with what we have is a way to love Him.","feel":"Pretend to carry a basket of food on your hip. Now hand out bread to one friend, then another, then another. Can you show me your helping hands?","stones":[["SPOT","Mary, Joanna, Susanna, and other women used their own things to help Jesus. They felt helpful and glad."],["NAME","That I-can-do-something, let-me-help feeling is called helpful. Your mind might think, “What do they need?” Your body might feel busy hands and a happy heart."],["FIND","When did you help someone, and it made you feel glad inside?"],["FORK","The women could just follow and watch, or help with what they had. They helped."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your helping hands rest. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me be a helper like them.”"]],"remember":"Helping with what you have is a way to love Jesus!","ask":["“Name one of the women who helped Jesus.”","“What did the women use to help Jesus and the apostles?”","“How did Willow help her dad make dinner?”"],"share":["Share a time you gave of your own things to help the Lord’s work.","Who are the helpers in your ward or family whose names should be remembered?","Did a grandparent or ancestor quietly provide for others? Tell about them."]},"b15-w3-n5":{"story":["Jesus told a parable about a man with a fig tree in his garden.","The man came looking for figs. None! He came the next year. None! He came again the next year. Still none! Three years, and not one fig.","The man told his gardener, “Cut it down. Why should it take up space?”","But the gardener said, “Leave it one more year. I will dig around it and feed its soil. If it grows figs, wonderful. If not, then cut it down.” Jesus was teaching that Heavenly Father is patient. He gives us time and help to grow.","Willow’s mom gave her another chance. Heavenly Father does that for you. Tonight, thank Him for never giving up on you."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm knows that everyone needs another chance sometimes. The gardener said, “Let it alone one more year, and I will help it.” Willow is grateful that Heavenly Father is that kind of gardener. In Willow’s world today: Willow has forgotten to feed the fish two days in a row. Mom could take the job away. Instead she says, “Let’s make a chart, and I’ll remind you this week.” Willow feeds the fish every day after that. “Thanks for not giving up on me,” she says. Just like the fig tree, Willow learned that Heavenly Father patiently gives us another chance and helps us grow.","feel":"Stand your arm up like a little tree with no fruit. Now pretend to dig around it and pat good soil at the bottom. Open your fingers: a fig! Can you show me your fig tree growing?","stones":[["SPOT","The fig tree had no fruit for three years. The gardener asked for one more year to help it, and the tree got another chance."],["NAME","That thank-you-for-not-giving-up-on-me feeling is called grateful. Your mind might think, “I get to try again.” Your body might feel warm and light."],["FIND","When did someone give you another chance instead of giving up on you?"],["FORK","The gardener could cut the tree down, or help it and wait. He chose to help and wait."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel your roots in good soil. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for being patient with me.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father is patient and gives us another chance to grow good fruit!","ask":["“How many years did the man look for figs and find none?”","“What did the gardener ask to do for the tree?”","“How did Willow’s mom help her remember to feed the fish?”"],"share":["Share a time Heavenly Father patiently gave you another chance to grow.","Who needs you to be a patient gardener in their life right now?","Did a grandparent or ancestor get a second chance that changed everything? Tell their story."]},"b15-w3-n6":{"story":["On the Sabbath, Jesus was teaching where people prayed. A woman there had been bent over for eighteen years. She could not stand up straight.","Jesus called her and said, “Woman, you are set free from your sickness.” He put His hands on her. Right away she stood up straight!","But the leader was upset. “Come be healed on the six work days,” he said, “not on the Sabbath.”","Jesus said, “You untie your ox or donkey on the Sabbath. You lead it to water. Shouldn’t this woman be untied too?” If a thirsty animal could be helped, so could she! The people were full of joy.","Like Beau, you may feel stuck sometimes. Tonight, ask Jesus to help you stand tall and free."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big heart, brave start!” For eighteen years the woman could only look at the ground. Then Jesus set her free, and she stood up tall. Beau loves standing tall. In Beau’s world today: Beau has been scared to go down the big slide for a whole year. Today Dad says, “I’ll be at the bottom.” Beau climbs up, takes a breath, and whoosh! He lands laughing. “I’m not scared of it anymore,” he says. He feels free. Just like the woman in the synagogue, Beau learned that Jesus sets us free from what has held us down.","feel":"Curl your back over and look down at the ground, like the bent-over woman. Now slowly, slowly, stand up straight and lift your face to the sky. Can you show me how tall you are?","stones":[["SPOT","The woman had been bent over for eighteen years. Jesus set her free, and she stood up straight."],["NAME","That untied, I-can-stand-tall feeling is called free. Your mind might think, “It doesn’t hold me anymore.” Your body might feel a straight back and a lifted face."],["FIND","When did something that had held you down finally let go, and you felt free?"],["FORK","Jesus could wait until another day to avoid trouble, or set the woman free right then. He set her free."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and lift your chin. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that Jesus sets me free.”"]],"remember":"Jesus unties what holds us down and helps us stand up tall!","ask":["“How many years had the woman been bent over?”","“What did Jesus say about untying an ox or donkey on the Sabbath?”","“What did Beau finally do at the park?”"],"share":["Share a time Jesus set you free from something that had bent you down.","What is Jesus inviting you to be untied from right now?","Did a grandparent or ancestor find freedom through Jesus? Tell their story."]},"b15-w3-n7":{"story":["One day some leaders told Jesus, “Go away. King Herod wants to hurt You.” Jesus was not afraid. He kept on healing people.","Then Jesus thought about Jerusalem, the big city with the temple. Many there had hurt Heavenly Father’s prophets. Jesus’s heart ached for them.","He said, “O Jerusalem! I wanted to gather your children like a hen gathers her chicks. But you would not come!”","Have you seen a mother hen? When a storm comes, she spreads her wings wide. Her chicks run under her wings, warm and safe. Jesus wants to hold us close like that.","Like Wren, you can run to Jesus. His wings are always open. Tonight, say a prayer and feel safe with Him."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer says, “Pause and ponder, what a wonder!” Jesus said He wants to gather us like a hen gathers her chicks under her wings. Wren pictures it, and she feels warm and protected. In Wren’s world today: At Grandma’s farm, a storm rolls in. Wren watches the mother hen spread her wings, and all seven chicks scurry underneath until only their little feet show. “That’s what Jesus does,” Wren whispers. Grandma says, “Exactly right.” Just like the chicks, Wren learned that Jesus wants to gather us close and keep us protected under His wings.","feel":"Spread your arms wide like a hen’s wings. Now tuck yourself under your blanket like a little chick, snug and safe. Can you show me your snug chick?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus wanted to gather the people like a hen gathers her chicks. The ones who came felt protected."],["NAME","That tucked-in, nothing-can-reach-me feeling is called protected. Your mind might think, “I am safe under His wings.” Your body might feel warm, snug, and still."],["FIND","When did someone wrap you up and keep you safe from something scary?"],["FORK","The people could run under Jesus’s wings, or stay out in the storm. Jesus kept His wings open for them."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly under your blanket. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that Jesus gathers me close.”"]],"remember":"Jesus wants to gather you under His wings, warm and safe!","ask":["“What did Jesus say He wanted to do for the children of Jerusalem?”","“Where do chicks run when a hen spreads her wings?”","“What did Wren see the mother hen do at Grandma’s farm?”"],"share":["Share a time you felt gathered and protected by Jesus.","What keeps you from running under His wings, and what helps you come?","Did a grandparent or ancestor gather your family close in a hard time? Tell about them."]},"b15-w4-n1":{"story":["Jesus and His disciples came near Jerusalem. Jesus said to two of them, “You will find a donkey and her colt. Bring them to Me.”","The prophet Zechariah long ago said a gentle King would ride a donkey. Zechariah was talking about Jesus.","The disciples laid their coats on the donkey, and Jesus rode toward the city.","The crowd spread coats on the road, like a carpet for a king. They carried palm branches and laid more on the road. They shouted, “Hosanna! Blessed is He who comes in the name of the Lord!” Hosanna was a happy shout that meant, “Save us!”","Like Scout at the parade, you can cheer for Jesus. Tonight, whisper, “Hosanna!”"],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker loves a parade. When Jesus rode into Jerusalem, the crowd waved palm branches and shouted Hosanna, and Scout would have been right at the front, waving the biggest branch. In Scout’s world today: The town parade is coming down Main Street. Scout hears the drums before he sees them. He climbs on Dad’s shoulders and waves his flag with both hands, shouting, “Here they come!” His whole body buzzes with excitement. Just like the crowd on the road, Scout learned that welcoming Jesus as our King is the happiest thing we can do.","feel":"Wave your arm back and forth like a palm branch, swish, swish! Now lay a pretend coat on the road in front of the King. Can you show me your Hosanna wave?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus rode into Jerusalem on a donkey, and the crowd waved palm branches. They felt excited and shouted Hosanna."],["NAME","That bursting, can’t-stand-still, here-He-comes feeling is called excited. Your mind might think, “The King is here!” Your body might feel bouncy feet and waving arms."],["FIND","When were you so excited that you jumped up and down and shouted?"],["FORK","The people could stand quietly, or welcome Jesus as their King with shouts of joy. They shouted Hosanna."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your waving arm rest. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for sending Jesus, our King.”"]],"remember":"Hosanna! Jesus is our King, humble and gentle, riding on a donkey!","ask":["“What animal did Jesus ride into Jerusalem?”","“What did the people wave and shout?”","“Where did Scout sit to watch the parade?”"],"share":["Share how you welcome Jesus as the King of your life.","What does the word Hosanna, “save us now,” mean for you this Easter week?","Did a grandparent or ancestor have a favorite Easter or Palm Sunday tradition? Share it."]},"b15-w4-n2":{"story":["The night before He died, Jesus ate the Passover supper with His apostles. At Passover, Israel remembered how Heavenly Father freed them.","Jesus tied a towel around Himself and poured water into a bowl. Then He washed His apostles’ dusty feet. That was a servant’s job!","Peter said, “You will never wash my feet!” Jesus said, “If I do not, you are not part of Me.” So Peter let Him.","Jesus said, “Do for each other what I have done for you.” Then He broke bread and said, “This is My body, given for you. Do this to remember Me.” That was the first sacrament, like ours each Sunday.","Like Willow at church, you can remember Jesus. Tonight, whisper, “Thank You, Jesus. I remember You.”"],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” Jesus knelt to wash His friends’ feet, then gave them bread and asked them to remember Him. Willow’s heart fills with thanks. In Willow’s world today: At church, Willow takes the little piece of bread and the tiny cup of water. She closes her eyes and thinks about Jesus kneeling to wash feet. “Thank You, Jesus,” she whispers. “I remember You.” Her heart feels warm and full. Just like the apostles, Willow learned that Jesus serves us with love, and we can remember Him with thankful hearts.","feel":"Pretend to pour water into a basin and gently wash a friend’s feet. Now break a piece of bread and hold it close to your heart. Can you show me your remembering hands?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus washed His apostles’ feet and gave them bread and asked them to remember Him. They felt thankful."],["NAME","That full-heart, thank-You-Jesus feeling is called thankful. Your mind might think, “He did this for me.” Your body might feel warm in your chest and soft hands."],["FIND","When did someone do something kind and humble for you, and your heart filled with thanks?"],["FORK","Peter could refuse to let Jesus wash his feet, or let Jesus serve him. Peter let Him."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and hold your hands over your heart. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for Jesus. I will remember Him.”"]],"remember":"Jesus said, “Do this in remembrance of Me,” and the sacrament helps us remember!","ask":["“What did Jesus do with the towel and the basin of water?”","“What did Jesus say when He gave the apostles the bread?”","“What does Willow think about when she takes the sacrament?”"],"share":["Share what the sacrament means to you and how you remember Jesus during it.","When has someone served you the way Jesus served His apostles?","Did a grandparent or ancestor teach your family to honor the sacrament? Tell about them."]},"b15-w4-n3":{"story":["After supper, Jesus took His apostles to Gethsemane, a garden where He often prayed.","He told His friends, “Pray, so you will not give in to wrong.” Then He knelt a little farther away and prayed alone.","Something very hard was coming. Jesus would take on every person’s sins, sadness, and pain. He did it to save us.","He called the hard thing a cup. He prayed, “Father, if You are willing, take this cup from Me. Still, not My will, but Thine, be done.” He meant, “I will do what You want, even when it is hard.” An angel came to make Him strong.","Like Beau, you can be brave too. Tonight, tell Heavenly Father, “I will do what You want.”"],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big heart, brave start!” In the garden, Jesus said, “Not My will, but Thine.” That is the bravest yes in the whole world, and Beau wants to be brave like that. In Beau’s world today: Beau has to get a shot at the doctor. He is scared. He whispers a prayer, then says, “Heavenly Father, I’ll be brave.” He holds Mom’s hand, squeezes his eyes, and it’s over in a second. The nurse says, “So brave.” Beau thinks, “Jesus was braver.” Just like Jesus in the garden, Beau learned that being brave means doing the hard thing Heavenly Father asks, with His help.","feel":"Kneel in your bed, or fold your arms if you’re lying down, like Jesus praying in the garden. Breathe slowly. Whisper, “Not my will, but Thine.” Can you show me your praying hands?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus knelt in the garden and prayed. It was so hard, and He was so brave. He chose Heavenly Father’s will."],["NAME","That it’s-hard-but-I’ll-do-it feeling is called brave. Your mind might think, “I will do what Heavenly Father asks.” Your body might feel a deep breath and steady hands."],["FIND","When did you do something hard and scary because it was right?"],["FORK","Jesus could choose His own way, or Heavenly Father’s will. He said, “Not My will, but Thine.”"],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your hands rest. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me be brave like Jesus.”"]],"remember":"Jesus was brave in the garden because He loves you!","ask":["“What was the name of the garden where Jesus prayed?”","“What did Jesus say to Heavenly Father in His prayer?”","“What did Beau whisper before his shot at the doctor?”"],"share":["Share what Jesus’s suffering in Gethsemane means to you personally.","When have you said, “Not my will, but Thine,” and what happened?","Did a grandparent or ancestor do something very hard because Heavenly Father asked? Tell their story."]},"b15-w4-n4":{"story":["After the garden, soldiers took Jesus away. Leaders who did not like Him said He had done wrong. But Jesus never did anything wrong.","They put Jesus on a cross on a hill called Calvary. His mother, Mary, and His friends stood near Him, so very sad.","Even then, Jesus was kind. He prayed, “Father, forgive them, for they know not what they do.”","At midday the sky went dark for three hours. Then Jesus said, “Father, into Thy hands I give My spirit.” And He died. Jesus gave His life so we can be forgiven and live again.","It is okay to feel sad about this part, like Wren. Snuggle close tonight, and remember: Sunday is coming!"],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer pauses and ponders, and some things are sad to ponder. Jesus died on the cross, and His friends cried. Wren sits with the sad feeling, because she knows what comes on Sunday. In Wren’s world today: Wren’s family watches an Easter video about the cross. When it gets to the sad part, Wren’s eyes fill with tears. Dad pulls her close. “It’s okay to be sad,” he says. “He did it because He loves you. And Sunday is coming.” Wren nods and leans in. Just like Jesus’s friends, Wren learned that sad feelings are okay, and that Jesus gave His life because He loves us.","feel":"Put your hands over your heart and let your face be soft and sad for a moment. Now take a slow breath and look up, because Sunday is coming. Can you show me your looking-up face?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus died on the cross, and His mother and friends cried. They felt sad, and Jesus still forgave."],["NAME","That heavy, tears-in-my-eyes feeling is called sad. Your mind might think, “I wish it didn’t happen.” Your body might feel heavy shoulders and a lump in your throat."],["FIND","When did you feel sad about something, and someone held you close?"],["FORK","Jesus could be angry at the people who hurt Him, or forgive them. Jesus forgave them."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your tears be okay. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for Jesus. I know Sunday is coming.”"]],"remember":"Jesus gave His life because He loves you, and Sunday is coming!","ask":["“What did Jesus pray for the people who hurt Him?”","“Who stood near Jesus at the cross?”","“What did Wren’s dad say when she felt sad?”"],"share":["Share, gently, what the cross means to you.","How has Jesus’s forgiveness from the cross helped you forgive?","Did a grandparent or ancestor find comfort in Jesus’s sacrifice during a loss? Tell about them."]},"b15-w4-n5":{"story":["A good man named Joseph loved Jesus. He went bravely to Pilate, the ruler, and asked for Jesus’s body. Pilate said yes.","Joseph gently wrapped Jesus’s body in clean cloth. He laid Him in a new tomb cut in the rock. Then he rolled a big stone across the door.","The women who loved Jesus saw where He was laid. They went home and got sweet spices ready for His body.","The next day was the Sabbath, so they rested and waited. It was a quiet, sad day. But Jesus’s spirit went to the spirit world. There He taught the good news to people who had died.","Like Willow, waiting can feel long. Tonight, wait with a hopeful heart. Something wonderful is coming!"],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm knows that waiting can feel sad and long. The women rested and waited for Sunday, and Jesus was busy even then. Willow waits with a hopeful heart. In Willow’s world today: Grandma is coming to visit tomorrow, and today feels so long. Willow helps Mom make Grandma’s favorite cookies and sets out the guest towels. “I’m getting ready while I wait,” she says. Her heart feels hopeful instead of grumpy. Just like the women who waited, Willow learned that we can rest and get ready with hope, because something wonderful is coming.","feel":"Lie very still and quiet, like a resting day. Breathe slowly in and out. Now peek one eye open, because tomorrow is coming. Can you show me your hopeful peek?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus was laid in the tomb, and His friends rested and waited. They felt sad, but hope was coming."],["NAME","That something-good-is-coming, I-can-wait feeling is called hopeful. Your mind might think, “Just wait and see.” Your body might feel quiet and calm, with a little flutter."],["FIND","When did you have to wait for something wonderful, and the waiting felt long?"],["FORK","Joseph could stay hidden and afraid, or bravely care for Jesus’s body. He went to Pilate and asked."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and rest your whole body. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, I will wait with hope.”"]],"remember":"Even on the quietest day, Jesus was working to save everyone, and Sunday was coming!","ask":["“Who asked Pilate for Jesus’s body?”","“Where did Jesus’s spirit go while His body rested in the tomb?”","“How did Willow get ready while she waited for Grandma?”"],"share":["Share a season of waiting in your life when hope carried you through.","What does it mean to you that Jesus taught in the spirit world?","Did a grandparent or ancestor who has passed away teach you something about hope? Share it."]},"b15-w4-n6":{"story":["Very early on Sunday morning, Mary Magdalene and another Mary went to Jesus’s tomb.","An angel from heaven had rolled the big stone away. He sat on it, shining like lightning, his clothes white as snow.","The angel said, “Do not be afraid. You are looking for Jesus. He is not here. He is risen, just as He said!”","The tomb was empty! The women ran to tell the disciples, afraid and full of joy. Later Mary Magdalene stood crying by the tomb. Someone said, “Mary.” She knew that voice! It was Jesus, alive! She went and told the disciples, “I have seen the Lord!”","Like Scout, you can feel that joy! Tonight, tell someone the happiest news ever: Jesus is alive!"],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see, what could it be?” The angel said, “Come, see the place where the Lord lay,” and it was empty! Scout would have run to tell everyone, just like Mary. In Scout’s world today: On Easter morning, Scout runs outside and finds that the bare bush by the fence has burst into white blossoms overnight. “Mom! Dad! Come see!” he yells. “It’s alive!” He runs all the way around the house twice, bursting with joy. Just like Mary Magdalene, Scout learned that Jesus is alive, and that joy is for running and telling.","feel":"Pretend to push a giant stone, push, push... it’s already rolled away! Throw your arms up high and shout in a whisper, “He is risen!” Can you show me your He-is-risen arms?","stones":[["SPOT","The women found the tomb empty, and Mary saw Jesus alive. They felt afraid and then full of joy."],["NAME","That jumping, laughing, best-news-ever feeling is called joy. Your mind might think, “He is alive!” Your body might feel like running and shouting."],["FIND","When did you get such happy news that you had to run and tell someone?"],["FORK","The women could stay afraid at the empty tomb, or run and tell the good news. They ran with joy."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your joy settle into a big smile. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that Jesus is alive.”"]],"remember":"He is not here. He is risen! Jesus is alive!","ask":["“What did the women find when they got to the tomb?”","“What did the angel say about Jesus?”","“What did Scout find had bloomed overnight by the fence?”"],"share":["Share your testimony that Jesus is risen and what it means to you.","When has Jesus called your name, like He called Mary’s, in a hard time?","How did a grandparent or ancestor’s faith in the resurrection bless your family?"]},"b15-w4-n7":{"story":["That Sunday night, Jesus’s disciples were together behind shut doors, afraid. Suddenly Jesus stood with them! He said, “Peace be unto you.”","They thought He was a spirit. Jesus said, “Look at My hands and My feet. It is I. Touch Me and see.”","Then He ate a piece of fish in front of them. Jesus had a real body, alive again.","Thomas was not there. He said, “Unless I see and touch Him, I will not believe.” Eight days later, Jesus came again. “Thomas, touch My hands,” He said. “Believe.” Thomas said, “My Lord and my God!” Now he believed.","Because Jesus lives, everyone will live again, even Beau’s grandpa. Tonight, let that peace wrap around you like a blanket."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big heart, brave start!” The disciples were locked in a room, afraid, and Jesus came and said, “Peace be unto you.” Beau learned that real peace comes from knowing Jesus is alive. In Beau’s world today: Beau’s grandpa died last year, and sometimes Beau still misses him at bedtime. Tonight Mom says, “Because Jesus lives, Grandpa will live again, and you’ll see him.” Beau lets out a long breath. The missing feeling is still there, but now it has peace wrapped around it. Just like Thomas, Beau learned that Jesus is really alive, and that is where peace comes from.","feel":"Lock your arms tight across your chest like a locked door. Now open them wide, because Jesus came in and said, “Peace.” Let your whole body go soft. Can you show me your peace body?","stones":[["SPOT","The disciples were afraid behind locked doors. Jesus came and said, “Peace be unto you,” and they felt peace."],["NAME","That all-is-well, nothing-to-fear feeling is called peace. Your mind might think, “Jesus is alive, so it’s okay.” Your body might feel soft, warm, and still."],["FIND","When did a scared feeling turn into a peaceful one because someone you love was there?"],["FORK","Thomas could keep doubting, or believe when he saw Jesus. He said, “My Lord!” and believed."],["CALM","Breathe out slowly and let your whole body rest. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that Jesus lives. Peace be in my heart.”"]],"remember":"Because Jesus lives, we will all live again, and that is real peace!","ask":["“What did Jesus say when He appeared to the disciples in the locked room?”","“What did Thomas say when he saw Jesus?”","“What did Beau’s mom tell him about Grandpa?”"],"share":["Share your testimony that because Jesus lives, we will live again.","When has the peace of the risen Jesus come into a locked, fearful place in your life?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor you look forward to seeing again because of the resurrection."]},"b17-w1-n1":{"story":["Jesus told His friends, “Keep your lamps burning.” Long ago, there were no light switches. People lit little clay lamps of oil to see at night.","Jesus said, “Be like servants waiting up for their master. He is coming home late from a wedding. When he knocks, open the door right away!”","“Those servants are blessed,” Jesus said, “if he finds them awake and watching.”","Jesus said the master will sit them down at his table and serve them! Jesus was talking about Himself. He will come again one day, and He wants to find us ready.","Getting ready for someone you love feels happy. We keep our lamps burning by praying and being kind. Tonight, say your prayer and plan one kind thing."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer pauses and ponders what it means to be ready. Jesus said to keep your lamp burning and be awake when He comes, and Wren likes to be the one who opens the door right away. In Wren’s world today: Grandma is coming at six. At five-thirty, Wren has already set out the slippers, filled the kettle, and put Grandma’s favorite book on the chair. When the knock comes, Wren is at the door before anyone else. “I was ready,” she says. Just like the watching servants, Wren learned that being ready for Jesus means keeping our lamps burning every day.","feel":"Cup your hands like a little lamp and hold them steady, keep it burning, keep it burning. Now hear a knock and jump to open the door! Can you show me your ready jump?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus said to keep your lamps burning and be ready when He comes. The servants who waited felt ready."],["NAME","That I’m-all-set, waiting-at-the-door feeling is called ready. Your mind might think, “He could come any minute.” Your body might feel awake eyes and quick feet."],["FIND","When did you get everything ready for someone you were waiting for?"],["FORK","The servants could fall asleep and let their lamps go out, or stay awake and ready. The blessed ones stayed ready."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your lamp glow steady. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me be ready for Jesus.”"]],"remember":"Keep your lamp burning, and be ready when Jesus comes!","ask":["“What did Jesus say to keep burning?”","“What will the master do for the servants he finds ready?”","“What did Wren get ready before Grandma came?”"],"share":["Share what keeping your lamp burning looks like in your life.","How does knowing Jesus will serve His faithful servants change how you wait for Him?","Did a grandparent or ancestor live ready for the Lord’s coming? Tell about them."]},"b17-w1-n2":{"story":["Jesus went to dinner at an important leader’s house. He watched the guests come in. Each one hurried to the best seat, the place for the honored guest.","So Jesus told a little story. “At a wedding feast, do not take the best seat. Someone more important may come. Then you must move to the back.”","“Instead, go and sit in the lowest seat, way down at the end.”","“Then the host will say, ‘Friend, come up higher!’” Jesus said, “Whoever lifts himself up will be brought down. Whoever is humble will be lifted up.”","Being humble means you don’t push to be first. Heavenly Father sees the one who waits. Tonight, plan one time you will let someone else go first."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big heart, brave start!” It takes a big, brave heart to take the lowest seat instead of pushing for the best one. Beau knows that real strength is humble. In Beau’s world today: At the pizza party, all the kids rush for the chairs by the birthday boy. Beau hangs back and sits at the end of the table. A minute later the birthday boy’s mom says, “Beau, come sit up here by us, you were so patient.” Beau grins and moves up. Just like Jesus taught, Beau learned that when we humbly take the lowest seat, Heavenly Father lifts us up.","feel":"Pretend to push and rush for the best seat, me first! Now stop, step back, and sit down quietly in the last spot. Then hear, “Friend, come up higher!” and sit up tall. Can you show me your come-up-higher sit?","stones":[["SPOT","The guests pushed for the best seats. Jesus said to take the lowest seat. The humble ones were lifted up."],["NAME","That I’ll-let-others-go-first feeling is called humble. Your mind might think, “They can have the best seat.” Your body might feel calm hands and a quiet smile."],["FIND","When did you let someone else go first, and it turned out well?"],["FORK","We can push to be first, or humbly take the lowest seat. Jesus says take the lowest seat."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your shoulders settle. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me be humble like Jesus.”"]],"remember":"Whoever humbles himself will be lifted up!","ask":["“Which seat did Jesus say to take at a dinner?”","“What will the host say to the humble guest?”","“Where did Beau sit at the pizza party?”"],"share":["Share a time you took the lowest seat and Heavenly Father lifted you up.","Where are you tempted to push for the best seat, and how could you step back?","Did a grandparent or ancestor have a humble way about them? Tell about them."]},"b17-w1-n3":{"story":["Jesus said, “When you make a feast, invite the poor. Invite people who cannot walk or see. They cannot pay you back, but you will be blessed.”","Then Jesus told a story. A man made a great supper and invited many. At supper time, his servant called, “Come, everything is ready!”","But they all made excuses. “I bought a field. I must go see it.” “I bought oxen. I must try them out.” “I just got married. I cannot come.”","The master was not happy. “Go bring in the poor, and those who cannot walk or see!” There was still room. “Bring more, so my house is full!”","Jesus meant Heavenly Father invites everyone to His table. Tonight, tell Him, “Yes, I want to come!”"],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” The master filled his house with people who had never been invited anywhere. Willow loves when everyone gets invited, especially the ones who usually get left out. In Willow’s world today: Willow is making invitations for her birthday party. She writes one for every girl in her class, then stops. “What about Ruby? She’s never been to a party.” Willow makes one more and puts a heart on it. Ruby’s face when she opens it is the best part of the whole party. Just like the master of the feast, Willow learned that Heavenly Father invites everyone, and we can, too.","feel":"Pretend to make excuses, shaking your head: no, no, I’m busy. Now stop, smile big, and wave someone in: come, everything is ready! Can you show me your come-in wave?","stones":[["SPOT","The master invited everyone to his supper, even the ones who were usually left out. They felt invited."],["NAME","That you-want-ME-there, I-get-to-come feeling is called invited. Your mind might think, “There’s a place for me!” Your body might feel a happy skip and a big smile."],["FIND","When did someone invite you to something, and you felt so glad to be wanted?"],["FORK","The first guests made excuses and missed the feast. The others said yes and came. Heavenly Father wants us to say yes."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and smile. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for inviting me to Your feast.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father invites everyone to His feast, so say yes and come!","ask":["“Who did Jesus say to invite to your feast?”","“What did the first guests do when the supper was ready?”","“Who did Willow make one more invitation for?”"],"share":["Share a time you said yes to Heavenly Father’s invitation instead of making excuses.","Who in your life has never been invited, and how could you bring them in?","Did a grandparent or ancestor open their table to everyone? Tell about them."]},"b17-w1-n4":{"story":["People who had done wrong came close to hear Jesus. Some leaders grumbled, “This man welcomes sinners and even eats with them!”","So Jesus told a little story. A woman had ten silver coins, and she lost one!","She lit a lamp and swept the whole house. She looked and looked until she found it.","Then she called her friends and neighbors. “Rejoice with me! I found the coin I lost!” Jesus said heaven’s angels feel that same joy. It happens whenever one person is sorry and comes back to Heavenly Father.","The grumbling leaders wanted Jesus to stay away from people who did wrong. But Jesus came to find those very people. Tonight, thank Heavenly Father that He never stops looking for you."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker loves a good search. The woman lit a lamp, swept every corner, and would not stop until she found her coin. And then she threw a party! Scout would have swept right alongside her. In Scout’s world today: Scout’s sister loses her favorite marble. Scout gets a flashlight and looks under every piece of furniture, in every shoe, in the couch. Finally, under the fridge, there it is! He runs to her shouting, “I found it!” and they both jump around the kitchen. Just like the woman with the coin, Scout learned that heaven rejoices when someone lost is found.","feel":"Pretend to sweep, sweep, sweep with a broom, looking in every corner. Found it! Hold up the coin and do a happy dance. Can you show me your rejoice-with-me dance?","stones":[["SPOT","The woman lost a coin, searched everywhere, found it, and called her friends to rejoice. She felt joyful."],["NAME","That found-it, come-celebrate feeling is called joyful. Your mind might think, “Everybody, rejoice with me!” Your body might feel bouncing feet and a happy shout."],["FIND","When did you find something that was lost, and you had to tell everyone?"],["FORK","The leaders could grumble about who Jesus welcomed, or rejoice that lost people were found. Heaven rejoices."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your happy dance settle. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that You never stop looking for me.”"]],"remember":"Heaven rejoices when one lost person comes home to Heavenly Father!","ask":["“What did the woman do to find her lost coin?”","“What did she say to her friends when she found it?”","“Where did Scout find his sister’s marble?”"],"share":["Share a time you felt heaven rejoice when you or someone you love came back to Heavenly Father.","Who are the grumblers in your heart, and who are the lost ones Jesus is seeking?","Did a grandparent or ancestor come back to the gospel and bring joy to your family? Tell their story."]},"b17-w1-n5":{"story":["Jesus told a story. A father had two sons. The younger son said, “Give me my share of your money now.” He went far away and wasted it all.","Then food ran out in that land. He got a job feeding pigs. He was so hungry, he wanted the pigs’ food!","He thought, “My father’s helpers have plenty to eat. I will go home and say I did wrong.”","While he was still far away, his father saw him. His father ran to him and hugged him! He said, “Bring the best robe and a ring! My son was lost, and now he is found!”","Heavenly Father is like that father. You can always come home to Him. Tonight, tell Him sorry for something."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big heart, brave start!” The son needed a brave heart to turn around and go home. And the father’s heart was even bigger: he ran. Beau wants a heart like that father’s. In Beau’s world today: Beau got mad and said something mean, then ran to his room and hid under the blanket. His tummy hurt. Finally he came out to say sorry. Before he could even finish, Dad was down on his knees with his arms wide. “Come here, buddy.” Beau ran into them. Just like the son who came home, Beau learned that Heavenly Father runs to welcome us when we turn around.","feel":"Walk your fingers slowly down a long road, head down, coming home. Now see the father running! Throw your arms wide open for a huge hug. Can you show me the father’s running hug?","stones":[["SPOT","The son came home ashamed. His father saw him far off, ran, and hugged him. The son felt welcomed."],["NAME","That arms-wide-open, you’re-home feeling is called welcomed. Your mind might think, “He still wants me.” Your body might feel squeezed tight and warm."],["FIND","When did you come back after doing something wrong, and someone hugged you instead of scolding?"],["FORK","The son could stay away ashamed, or go home. He went home, and his father ran to him."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel arms wrapped around you. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for always welcoming me home.”"]],"remember":"When you come home to Heavenly Father, He runs to meet you with open arms!","ask":["“What did the son do with his father’s money?”","“What did the father do when he saw his son coming home?”","“What did Beau’s dad do when Beau came out to say sorry?”"],"share":["Share a time you came home to Heavenly Father and felt Him run to you.","Is there someone you are watching the road for, ready to run and welcome?","Did a grandparent or ancestor welcome someone home with open arms? Tell their story."]},"b17-w1-n6":{"story":["Jesus was walking to Jerusalem. Ten men with a skin sickness called leprosy stood far away. That sickness meant they had to stay away from everyone.","They called out, “Jesus, Master, have mercy on us!” Jesus said, “Go show yourselves to the priests.” The priests could say when someone was well.","So the ten men went. And as they walked, all ten were healed!","But only one turned back. He praised Heavenly Father loudly. He fell at Jesus’s feet to thank Him. Jesus said, “Weren’t ten healed? Where are the other nine?” Then He said, “Go. Your faith has made you well.”","Ten were healed, but only one said thank you. Tonight, be the one. Thank Heavenly Father for something good today."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer pauses to ponder, and that pause is why she remembers to say thank you. Nine men ran off. One stopped, turned around, and came back. Wren wants to be that one. In Wren’s world today: Grandpa drives Wren and her friends to the zoo, buys lunch, and waits all day. When they get home, the other kids run inside. Wren stops at the car, runs back, and hugs Grandpa through the window. “Thank you for today, Grandpa.” His whole face lights up. Just like the one who came back, Wren learned that a thankful heart turns around and says thank you.","feel":"Walk your fingers away quickly, like nine men running off. Now stop, turn around, and walk them back. Fold your hands and bow: thank you! Can you show me your came-back bow?","stones":[["SPOT","Ten men were healed. Only one came back to thank Jesus. He felt thankful, and Jesus blessed him."],["NAME","That I-have-to-go-back-and-say-it feeling is called thankful. Your mind might think, “He did this for me.” Your body might feel a turned-around heart and a warm chest."],["FIND","When did someone do something kind, and you went back to say thank you?"],["FORK","The healed men could run off, or come back and thank Jesus. One came back."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and fold your hands. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You, thank You, thank You.”"]],"remember":"Be the one who comes back to say thank you!","ask":["“How many men did Jesus heal?”","“How many came back to say thank you?”","“What did Wren do before going inside after the zoo?”"],"share":["Share a blessing you have not yet gone back to thank Heavenly Father for.","How do you teach your family to be the one who comes back?","Was there a grandparent or ancestor known for gratitude? Tell about them."]},"b17-w1-n7":{"story":["Jesus loved three friends in Bethany: Mary, Martha, and their brother Lazarus. Then Lazarus got very sick.","By the time Jesus came, Lazarus had died. Martha said, “Lord, if You had been here, my brother would not have died.” Jesus said, “Your brother will rise again.”","Then Mary came to Jesus, crying. And Jesus wept. He cried with her, because He loved them.","Lazarus was buried in a cave with a stone in front. Jesus said, “Take away the stone.” He thanked Heavenly Father for hearing Him. Then He called, “Lazarus, come forth!” And Lazarus walked out, alive!","Jesus feels sad with us when we are sad. And He is stronger than death. When a friend is sad, sit close, like Jesus did."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” When Mary cried, Jesus cried with her before He did anything else, and Willow knows that is sometimes the kindest thing. In Willow’s world today: Willow’s friend Lily’s grandpa died. At school, Lily is crying by the window. Willow does not know any big words to say. She just sits down, holds Lily’s hand, and cries a little, too. Lily squeezes her hand. “Thanks for staying,” she whispers. Just like Mary and Martha, Willow learned that Jesus comforts us by crying with us, and then He brings new life.","feel":"Let your face get sad and wipe away a pretend tear. Now feel a warm hand holding yours, and take a slow, deep breath. Can you show me your comforted breath?","stones":[["SPOT","Mary and Martha were sad because Lazarus died. Jesus wept with them, then He raised Lazarus. They felt comforted."],["NAME","That someone-cries-with-me, I’m-not-alone feeling is called comforted. Your mind might think, “He understands.” Your body might feel a hand holding yours and slower tears."],["FIND","When were you sad, and someone sat with you until you felt better?"],["FORK","Jesus could rush straight to the tomb, or stop and weep with Mary first. He wept with her first."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your tears be okay. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that Jesus cries with me.”"]],"remember":"Jesus wept with His friends, and then He called Lazarus out of the tomb, alive!","ask":["“Who were Jesus’s three friends in Bethany?”","“What did Jesus do when He saw Mary crying?”","“What did Willow do when Lily was crying by the window?”"],"share":["Share a time Jesus wept with you, and what it meant.","What does “I am the resurrection and the life” mean to you?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor you look forward to seeing again because of the resurrection."]},"b17-w2-n1":{"story":["People brought their little children to Jesus. They wanted Him to put His hands on them and bless them. They were little children, just like you.","But Jesus’s disciples, His close friends, told the people to stop. They tried to keep the children away.","Jesus was not pleased. He said, “Let the little children come to Me, and do not stop them.”","“Heaven belongs to people like these.” Little children love and trust so easily. Then Jesus took the children up in His arms. He put His hands on them and blessed them.","Jesus was not too busy for those children. He is never too busy for you, either. Tonight, think of Jesus holding you close, and feel how welcome you are."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” Jesus took the little children in His arms and blessed them. Willow thinks that is the warmest welcome in the whole Bible. In Willow’s world today: The bishop is talking with grown-ups in the hall, and Willow’s little brother toddles right up to him. Willow starts to pull him back. The bishop kneels down. “No, let him come.” He shakes the little boy’s hand, and Willow’s brother beams. Just like the children Jesus blessed, Willow learned that Jesus always welcomes little ones, and so can we.","feel":"Pretend someone is holding you back, no, no. Now hear Jesus say, “Let them come!” and run forward into His arms with a big hug. Can you show me your running-to-Jesus hug?","stones":[["SPOT","The disciples tried to keep the children away. Jesus said, “Let them come,” and blessed them. The children felt welcome."],["NAME","That come-here-I-want-you feeling is called welcome. Your mind might think, “He has time for me.” Your body might feel lifted up and held."],["FIND","When did a grown-up stop what they were doing just to be with you?"],["FORK","The disciples wanted to send the children away. Jesus welcomed them. Jesus always welcomes children."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel Jesus’s arms around you. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that Jesus always lets me come.”"]],"remember":"Jesus said, “Let the little children come to Me,” and He means you!","ask":["“What did the disciples try to do when the parents brought their children?”","“What did Jesus do with the children?”","“What did the bishop say when Willow’s brother toddled up?”"],"share":["Share what it means to you that Jesus is never too busy for a child.","How do you make sure the little ones in your life feel welcome with Jesus?","Did a grandparent or ancestor have a special love for children? Tell about them."]},"b17-w2-n2":{"story":["A young man asked Jesus, “How can I live with Heavenly Father forever?”","Jesus said, “Keep the commandments. Do not hurt anyone. Do not steal or lie. Honor your father and mother. Love your neighbor.”","The young man said, “I have kept all these since I was little. What lack I yet?” He wanted to know what was still missing.","Jesus said, “Sell what you have, and give to the poor. You will have treasure in heaven. Then come and follow Me.” The young man went away sad. He had many things, and he did not want to give them up.","Jesus wanted him to love Him most of all. Sharing can feel hard, then happy. Tonight, think of one thing to share."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big heart, brave start!” The young man wanted to follow Jesus, but he wanted his things, too. Beau knows that torn feeling, and which way a brave heart goes. In Beau’s world today: Beau is saving for a remote-control car. Then he hears that a family in his ward lost everything in a fire. Part of him wants to keep his jar. Part of him wants to give. He stands in front of the jar a long time. Then he picks it up and carries it to Mom. “For the family.” Just like the young man, Beau learned that Jesus asks what we love most, and a brave heart chooses Him.","feel":"Hold out both hands like a scale. Put all your toys in one hand and Jesus in the other. Which way tips? Let the Jesus hand sink low and the other float up. Can you show me your choosing scale?","stones":[["SPOT","The young man wanted to follow Jesus, but he loved his things, too. He felt torn, and he walked away sad."],["NAME","That I-want-both, I-can’t-choose feeling is called torn. Your mind might think, “But I love my stuff.” Your body might feel stuck feet and a sinking tummy."],["FIND","When did you have to choose between something you wanted and something right?"],["FORK","The young man could give his things away and follow Jesus, or keep them. He kept them and went away sad."],["CALM","Breathe out slowly and let your hands rest open. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me love Jesus most of all.”"]],"remember":"Jesus asks us to love Him more than our things, and that is treasure in heaven!","ask":["“What did the young man ask Jesus?”","“Why did the young man go away sad?”","“What did Beau do with his jar of savings?”"],"share":["Share what you have had to let go of to follow Jesus more fully.","When you ask, “What lack I yet?” what does the Spirit whisper?","Did a grandparent or ancestor give up riches or comfort for the gospel? Tell their story."]},"b17-w2-n3":{"story":["The rich young man went away sad. Jesus said, “It is hard for a rich man to get into heaven.”","Then Jesus gave His friends a picture to remember. “It is easier for a camel to go through the eye of a needle.”","A camel is huge, with a big hump! The eye of a needle is the tiny hole for the thread. A camel could never fit!","The disciples were amazed. “Then who can be saved?” Jesus said, “With people, this is impossible. But with Heavenly Father, all things are possible.” No one can get to heaven alone.","Heavenly Father can do what we cannot. When something feels too big, you don’t have to do it alone. Tonight, ask Him for help."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see, what could it be?” A camel through a needle? Scout laughed so hard. Then Jesus said that with Heavenly Father, all things are possible, and Scout was amazed. In Scout’s world today: Scout cannot get the big puzzle piece to fit no matter how he turns it. “It’s impossible!” Dad kneels beside him. “Let’s try it together.” Dad shows him a new way to look at it, and suddenly it clicks. Scout stares. “It was impossible for me. Not for us.” Just like the disciples, Scout learned that what is impossible for us is possible with Heavenly Father.","feel":"Make a tiny circle with your finger and thumb, the eye of a needle. Now make your other arm a big, lumpy camel and try to squeeze it through. No way! Can you show me your amazed face?","stones":[["SPOT","A camel through a needle! The disciples were amazed that with Heavenly Father, all things are possible."],["NAME","That whoa, that’s-impossible-but-He-can feeling is called amazed. Your mind might think, “Only Heavenly Father could do that.” Your body might feel wide eyes and a laugh."],["FIND","When did something seem impossible until someone helped you?"],["FORK","The disciples could give up because it seemed impossible, or trust that Heavenly Father can do anything. They trusted."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your amazed laugh settle. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, with You all things are possible.”"]],"remember":"With people it is impossible, but with Heavenly Father, all things are possible!","ask":["“What did Jesus say is easier than a rich man entering the kingdom?”","“What did Jesus say is possible with Heavenly Father?”","“What happened when Scout and Dad worked on the puzzle piece together?”"],"share":["Share a time Heavenly Father did something in your life that was impossible for you.","What are you trying to squeeze through on your own that you need to hand to Him?","Did a grandparent or ancestor see an impossible thing become possible through faith? Tell their story."]},"b17-w2-n4":{"story":["Jesus told a little story. A man had a vineyard, a big garden of grapes. One morning, he hired workers and promised each a silver coin.","He hired more at nine o’clock, at noon, and at three. At five o’clock, with one hour left, he hired more.","That evening, he paid the last workers first. The five o’clock workers got a whole silver coin!","The all-day workers thought, “We will get more!” But they got one coin too. They grumbled, “We worked all day in the hot sun!” The owner said, “Friend, I paid what I promised. Are you upset because I am kind?”","Heavenly Father’s love is for everyone who comes, early or late. Tonight, be glad for someone who got something good."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer pauses and ponders. The all-day workers grumbled when the owner was kind to the latecomers. Wren has felt that jealous pinch, too, and she thinks about how to let it go. In Wren’s world today: Wren cleaned her whole room, and her little brother only picked up three blocks. Mom gives them both a cookie. Wren’s mouth opens. “But I did way more!” Mom says, “I’m glad you helped so well. Can’t I be kind to him, too?” Wren thinks, then nods. “Yeah. You can.” Just like the all-day workers, Wren learned that Heavenly Father’s kindness to others is not something to be jealous of.","feel":"Pretend to work hard in the hot sun, wipe your forehead. Now get your coin and see the latecomer get the same. Make a grumpy face. Now let it melt into a smile. Can you show me grumpy-to-glad?","stones":[["SPOT","The all-day workers saw the latecomers get the same pay. They felt jealous. The owner said, “Can’t I be kind?”"],["NAME","That it’s-not-fair, they-got-the-same feeling is called jealous. Your mind might think, “But I did more!” Your body might feel crossed arms and a hot face."],["FIND","When did someone else get something, and you felt a jealous pinch inside?"],["FORK","The workers could grumble about the owner’s kindness, or be glad everyone was paid. Jesus asks us to be glad."],["CALM","Breathe out slowly and uncross your arms. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that You are kind to everyone, including me.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father is generous to everyone, so be glad instead of jealous!","ask":["“What did the owner pay the workers who came at five o’clock?”","“What did the owner say to the workers who grumbled?”","“What did Wren’s mom give to both Wren and her brother?”"],"share":["Share a time you felt jealous of Heavenly Father’s kindness to someone, and how you let it go.","What does it mean to you that latecomers receive the same kingdom?","Did a grandparent or ancestor come to the gospel late in life and receive the full blessing? Tell their story."]},"b17-w2-n5":{"story":["The mother of James and John came to Jesus with her sons. She knelt and asked, “Please let my sons sit by You in Your kingdom.” She wanted them to be most important.","Jesus said, “You do not know what you are asking.” Those seats were for Heavenly Father to give.","When the other ten disciples heard, they were upset with James and John.","Jesus called them together. He said, “Rulers like to boss people around. It must not be that way with you. Whoever wants to be great must be your servant.” Jesus came to serve, not to be served.","So the greatest person is the best helper. Helping feels good and makes others smile. Tonight, plan one way to help, just like Jesus."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” Jesus said the greatest is the one who serves. Willow is not interested in the best seat; she is interested in who needs help. In Willow’s world today: At the family picnic, the cousins argue about who gets to be the leader of the game. Willow quietly starts handing out the cups and napkins and helping Grandma carry the watermelon. Grandma says, “Willow is the real leader today. She’s the one serving.” Just like Jesus taught, Willow learned that the greatest person is the one who helps the most.","feel":"Sit up tall and bossy, pointing at everyone: do this, do that! Now get down low and pretend to pour water for a friend, gentle and helpful. Can you show me your serving hands?","stones":[["SPOT","James and John wanted the best seats. Jesus said the greatest is the servant. The ones who listened felt helpful."],["NAME","That what-can-I-do-for-you feeling is called helpful. Your mind might think, “Who needs a hand?” Your body might feel busy, ready hands."],["FIND","When did you help instead of being the boss, and it felt good?"],["FORK","The apostles could fight to be first, or serve each other. Jesus said serve."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your helping hands rest. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me serve like Jesus.”"]],"remember":"Whoever wants to be great must be the servant of all, just like Jesus!","ask":["“What did the mother of James and John ask Jesus?”","“What did Jesus say the greatest person must be?”","“What did Willow do while her cousins argued about being the leader?”"],"share":["Share a time serving made you feel closer to Jesus than being in charge did.","Where do you want the best seat, and how could you serve there instead?","Was there a grandparent or ancestor who led by serving? Tell about them."]},"b17-w2-n6":{"story":["Jesus was leaving the city of Jericho with a big crowd. A blind man named Bartimaeus sat by the road, asking for money.","He heard that Jesus was passing by. So he shouted, “Jesus, Son of David, have mercy on me!” He was calling Jesus the promised Savior.","Many people told him, “Be quiet!” But Bartimaeus shouted even louder.","Jesus stood still. “Call him,” He said. Bartimaeus threw off his coat and came to Jesus. Jesus asked, “What do you want Me to do for you?” “Lord, I want to see.” Jesus said, “Your faith has made you well.” Right away he could see!","Bartimaeus was brave and kept calling to Jesus. You can always call to Jesus, too. Tonight, tell Him what you need."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big heart, brave start!” Everyone told Bartimaeus to be quiet, and he shouted louder. Then he threw off his coat and jumped up. That is Beau’s kind of brave. In Beau’s world today: Beau needs help at the swimming pool, but the big kids are splashing and nobody hears him. He is embarrassed to yell. Then he thinks of Bartimaeus. “Lifeguard!” he shouts, as loud as he can. The lifeguard comes right over. Beau learned to shout when it matters. Just like Bartimaeus, Beau learned that it takes a brave heart to keep calling to Jesus when others say hush.","feel":"Whisper, “Jesus, help me.” Now someone says hush, so say it louder: “Jesus, help me!” Now throw off your pretend coat and jump up! Can you show me your Bartimaeus jump?","stones":[["SPOT","Bartimaeus shouted for Jesus, and people told him to be quiet. He felt brave and shouted louder, and Jesus healed him."],["NAME","That I’ll-shout-anyway, I-won’t-be-hushed feeling is called brave. Your mind might think, “He needs to hear me.” Your body might feel a loud voice and a jumping heart."],["FIND","When did you have to be brave and speak up even though people wanted you quiet?"],["FORK","Bartimaeus could go quiet when people shushed him, or shout louder. He shouted louder."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your voice rest. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that Jesus always stops for me.”"]],"remember":"When people say hush, call to Jesus even louder, and He will stop for you!","ask":["“What did Bartimaeus shout when Jesus passed by?”","“What did Bartimaeus do when Jesus called him?”","“What did Beau shout at the swimming pool?”"],"share":["Share a time you kept calling to Jesus when everything told you to be quiet.","If Jesus asked, “What do you want Me to do for you?” what would you say?","Did a grandparent or ancestor cry out to the Lord in a hard moment and feel Him stop? Tell about it."]},"b17-w2-n7":{"story":["Some people thought they were better than everyone else. So Jesus told a story.","“Two men went to the temple, Heavenly Father’s holy house, to pray. One was a Pharisee, a leader who kept many rules. The other was a tax collector.”","“The Pharisee stood and prayed, ‘Thank You that I am not like others. I am not like this tax collector!’”","“But the tax collector stood far back. He would not even look up. He prayed, ‘Heavenly Father, please forgive me. I have done wrong.’” Jesus said the tax collector went home right with Heavenly Father. “Whoever is humble will be lifted up.”","Heavenly Father loves an honest prayer. Tonight, tell Him about your day, even the hard parts. Feel your heart grow light."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer pauses and ponders, and when she prays she tells the truth about herself. The tax collector did not pretend to be perfect. He said, “Be merciful to me,” and Jesus said he went home right with Heavenly Father. In Wren’s world today: At bedtime, Wren starts her prayer, “Thank You that I was good today.” Then she stops. She wasn’t, not all day. She starts over. “Heavenly Father, I was grumpy and I pushed my brother. I’m sorry. Please help me.” Her heart feels clean and light. Just like the tax collector, Wren learned that Heavenly Father loves an honest, humble prayer.","feel":"Stand up tall and puff out your chest, bragging. Now bow your head, put your hand on your heart, and let your shoulders go soft. Can you show me your honest prayer?","stones":[["SPOT","The Pharisee bragged. The tax collector prayed honestly, and Jesus said he went home right with Heavenly Father."],["NAME","That I’ll-tell-the-truth-about-me feeling is called honest. Your mind might think, “I’m not perfect, and that’s okay to say.” Your body might feel a bowed head and a hand on your heart."],["FIND","When did you tell the truth about something you did, even though it was hard?"],["FORK","We can brag in our prayers like the Pharisee, or be honest like the tax collector. Jesus praised the honest one."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly with your hand on your heart. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, be merciful to me, and help me.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father loves an honest prayer, and He lifts up the humble!","ask":["“Where did the two men go to pray?”","“What did the tax collector say in his prayer?”","“What did Wren say when she started her prayer over?”"],"share":["Share a time an honest, humble prayer brought you peace.","Where do you catch yourself praying like the Pharisee?","Did a grandparent or ancestor pray with real honesty? Tell what you remember."]},"b17-w3-n1":{"story":["Jesus went into the temple, Heavenly Father’s holy house. People were selling things there. Jesus sent them out, because the temple was for prayer.","People who could not see or walk came to Him. He made them well.","Then children in the temple shouted, “Hosanna to the Son of David!” They were cheering for Jesus, the promised King. Hosanna is a happy shout that means, “Save us now!”","The temple leaders frowned. “Do You hear what these children say?” Jesus said, “Yes.” He told them an old scripture says that little children give perfect praise.","Jesus was glad for the children’s happy praise. Tonight, sing to Jesus with your whole voice. He loves to hear you."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big heart, brave start!” The grown-up leaders frowned, but the children kept shouting Hosanna, and Jesus said their praise was perfect. Beau would have shouted the loudest. In Beau’s world today: In Primary, Beau sings the Primary song about being a child of Heavenly Father with his whole voice, way louder than everyone. A big kid snickers. Beau’s cheeks get warm, but he keeps singing loud. Afterward his teacher says, “Beau, that was the best singing I’ve heard all year.” Just like the children in the temple, Beau learned that Jesus loves joyful praise from children, even when grown-ups frown.","feel":"Wave your arms and shout in a whisper, “Hosanna!” Now make a frowning grown-up face. Now go right back to waving and shouting Hosanna, even bigger! Can you show me your perfect praise?","stones":[["SPOT","The children shouted Hosanna in the temple. The leaders frowned, but Jesus said their praise was perfect. The children felt joyful."],["NAME","That I-can’t-help-but-shout feeling is called joyful. Your mind might think, “Jesus is my King!” Your body might feel waving arms and a big voice."],["FIND","When did you sing or shout about something happy, even when someone frowned?"],["FORK","The children could go quiet when the leaders frowned, or keep praising. They kept praising, and Jesus loved it."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your joy settle into a glow. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that Jesus loves my praise.”"]],"remember":"Out of the mouths of children comes perfect praise, so sing to Jesus!","ask":["“What did the children shout in the temple?”","“What did Jesus say about the children’s praise?”","“What did Beau’s teacher say about his singing?”"],"share":["Share a time a child’s simple praise taught you something about Jesus.","How can your family praise Jesus out loud, together, this week?","Did a grandparent or ancestor love to sing or praise? Tell about them."]},"b17-w3-n2":{"story":["Some temple leaders asked Jesus hard questions to trap Him. So Jesus told them a little story.","“A man had two sons. He told the first, ‘Go work in my grape field.’ The son said, ‘I will not!’ But later he was sorry, and he went.”","“Then he asked the second son, who said, ‘Yes, sir, I will go!’ But he never went.”","Jesus asked, “Which son did what his father wanted?” The leaders said, “The first one.” Jesus said some people who did wrong listened to John the Baptist. They were sorry and changed. These leaders heard John too, but never changed.","Saying yes is easy. Doing it is what counts. Tonight, when someone asks you to help, go and do it."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer pauses and ponders which son really obeyed. It was not the one with the nice words. It was the one who went and did it. Wren wants her doing to match her words. In Wren’s world today: Mom asks Wren and her brother to pick up the toys. Her brother says, “Sure, Mom!” and keeps playing. Wren grumbles, “I don’t want to.” Then she thinks about it, sighs, and picks up every toy. Mom hugs her. “You’re the one who did it.” Just like the first son, Wren learned that being obedient means doing what is asked, not just saying yes.","feel":"Shake your head no, cross your arms. Now think, uncross your arms, and march to work. Next, nod yes, big smile, then just sit there doing nothing. Which one obeyed? Can you show me your get-up-and-go march?","stones":[["SPOT","One son said no, then went and worked. The other said yes and never went. The one who did it was obedient."],["NAME","That I’ll-actually-do-it feeling is called obedient. Your mind might think, “Saying it isn’t enough.” Your body might feel feet getting up and hands getting busy."],["FIND","When did you not want to do something, then changed your mind and did it?"],["FORK","The first son said no but went; the second said yes but didn’t. Jesus wants us to go and do."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your feet rest after the work. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help my doing match my words.”"]],"remember":"Don’t just say yes to Heavenly Father; get up and do it!","ask":["“What did the first son say, and what did he do?”","“Which son did what the father wanted?”","“Who picked up the toys at Wren’s house?”"],"share":["Share a time you were the first son, said no, then changed and went.","Where in your life are you saying yes with your mouth but not with your feet?","Did a grandparent or ancestor show faith by what they did more than what they said? Tell about them."]},"b17-w3-n3":{"story":["Some leaders kept trying to trap Jesus with hard questions. One man asked, “Teacher, which commandment is the greatest?”","Heavenly Father had given many rules, called commandments. The man was testing Jesus to see which one He would pick.","Jesus said, “Love Heavenly Father with all your heart, soul, and mind. This is the first and greatest commandment.”","“The second is like it,” He said. “Love your neighbor, the people around you, as yourself.” Jesus said every other rule hangs on these two, like coats on two hooks. Love Heavenly Father and love people, and you will keep the rest.","Two hooks hold it all. Tonight, think of one way to love Heavenly Father. Then think of one way to love your family."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” Jesus said the whole law hangs on two things: love Heavenly Father, love people. That is Willow’s whole heart. In Willow’s world today: Willow’s Primary teacher asks, “What are the most important rules?” Kids shout, “Share! Say please!” Willow raises her hand. “Love Heavenly Father and love everybody. If you do that, you’ll do all the other rules, too.” The teacher says, “That’s exactly what Jesus said.” Just like Jesus taught, Willow learned that loving Heavenly Father and loving people is what all the rules are for.","feel":"Hold up two fingers like two hooks. On the first, hang a big heart for Heavenly Father. On the second, hang a heart for everybody. Now give yourself a hug. Can you show me your two hooks?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus said the greatest commandments are to love Heavenly Father and love your neighbor. Those who listened felt loving."],["NAME","That with-all-my-heart feeling is called loving. Your mind might think, “I want to love Him and everybody.” Your body might feel a full chest and open arms."],["FIND","When did you feel so full of love that you wanted to hug everyone?"],["FORK","We can get lost in hundreds of rules, or hang everything on love. Jesus says hang it on love."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel your heart get full. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, I love You with all my heart.”"]],"remember":"Love Heavenly Father with everything, and love your neighbor as yourself!","ask":["“What did Jesus say is the greatest commandment?”","“What is the second commandment?”","“What did Willow say the most important rules are?”"],"share":["Share how loving Heavenly Father with all your heart has shaped your life.","What rule do you keep carefully while forgetting the love it hangs from?","Did a grandparent or ancestor live the two great commandments? Tell about them."]},"b17-w3-n4":{"story":["One morning, Jesus was hungry. He saw a fig tree full of leaves. But it had no figs at all.","Jesus said to the tree, “No one will eat fruit from you ever again.” His friends heard Him.","The next morning, they walked by again. The tree had dried up, right down to its roots! “Teacher, look!” Peter said.","Jesus said, “Have faith in Heavenly Father.” He said real faith could even tell a big mountain, “Move into the sea!” “When you pray, believe,” He said. “And forgive anyone you are upset with.”","Faith and a forgiving heart go together. Tonight, pray, believing Heavenly Father hears you. Then forgive anyone you need to."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big heart, brave start!” Jesus said that faith can move a mountain. Beau prays with a confident, believing heart, and he keeps it clean by forgiving. In Beau’s world today: Beau has a big spelling test. He is scared. He kneels and prays, “Heavenly Father, I studied. I believe You’ll help me remember.” Then he remembers he is still mad at his friend from yesterday. “And I forgive Max.” He walks into the test with a calm, confident heart. Just like Jesus taught, Beau learned that confident faith and a forgiving heart go together in prayer.","feel":"Point at a pretend mountain and say, “Be moved!” Now push it away with both hands, slowly, into the sea. Then open your hands wide, letting go of anything you’re mad about. Can you show me your mountain push?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus said faith can move mountains, and to forgive when you pray. The disciples felt confident to pray with faith."],["NAME","That I-believe-He-hears-me feeling is called confident. Your mind might think, “Heavenly Father can do it.” Your body might feel a steady voice and open hands."],["FIND","When did you pray believing, and felt sure Heavenly Father heard you?"],["FORK","We can pray while doubting, or believe that we receive. Jesus says believe, and forgive."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and open your hands. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, I believe, and I forgive.”"]],"remember":"Have faith in Heavenly Father, believe when you pray, and forgive!","ask":["“What happened to the fig tree?”","“What did Jesus say faith can move?”","“Who did Beau forgive before his spelling test?”"],"share":["Share a mountain that moved in your life through believing prayer.","Is there someone you need to forgive so your prayers can be full of faith?","Did a grandparent or ancestor pray with mountain-moving faith? Tell their story."]},"b17-w3-n5":{"story":["Jesus came to Jericho. A rich man named Zacchaeus lived there, the boss of the tax collectors.","Zacchaeus wanted to see Jesus. But he was short, and the crowd was big.","So he ran ahead and climbed a sycamore tree. Now he could see!","Jesus stopped and looked up. “Zacchaeus, hurry and come down! Today I must stay at your house.” Zacchaeus hurried down, so happy. The crowd grumbled that he was a sinner. But Zacchaeus said, “I will give half my things to the poor. If I took anything wrongly, I will pay back four times as much.”","Jesus said He came to find and save the lost. He saw Zacchaeus in that tree. He sees you too. Tonight, thank Him for seeing you."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see, what could it be?” Zacchaeus climbed a tree just to see Jesus, and Jesus looked right up and said his name. Scout thinks that is the best. In Scout’s world today: Scout is at the back of the big crowd at the parade, and he cannot see anything. He climbs the fence post. Then the mayor, waving from the float, looks right at him and says, “Hey, buddy on the post, great spot!” Scout nearly falls off. “He saw me!” Just like Zacchaeus, Scout learned that when we go looking for Jesus, He notices us and calls us by name.","feel":"Stand on your tiptoes trying to see over a crowd. Can’t see! Now climb up a pretend tree, hand over hand, and peek down. Hear your name called, and climb down fast with a huge grin. Can you show me your Zacchaeus climb?","stones":[["SPOT","Zacchaeus climbed a tree to see Jesus. Jesus looked up, said his name, and came to his house. Zacchaeus felt noticed."],["NAME","That He-saw-me, He-knows-my-name feeling is called noticed. Your mind might think, “Out of all these people, He picked me!” Your body might feel a jump and a big grin."],["FIND","When did someone see you in a crowd and call your name?"],["FORK","The crowd grumbled about Zacchaeus. Jesus went to his house. Zacchaeus chose to change."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your grin settle. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that Jesus sees me.”"]],"remember":"Jesus looked up and said, “Zacchaeus,” and He knows your name, too!","ask":["“Why did Zacchaeus climb a tree?”","“What did Zacchaeus promise to do after Jesus came to his house?”","“Where did Scout climb to see the parade?”"],"share":["Share a time Jesus noticed you and called you by name.","What has changed in your life since Jesus came to your house?","Did a grandparent or ancestor make things right after Jesus found them? Tell their story."]},"b17-w3-n6":{"story":["Jesus came to Bethany to see His friends Mary, Martha, and Lazarus. They made Him a supper.","Then Mary did something surprising. She took sweet perfume that cost a lot of money. She poured it on Jesus’s feet.","Then she wiped His feet with her hair. The whole house filled with the sweet smell.","Judas, one of Jesus’s helpers, grumbled. “Why wasn’t this sold to help the poor?” But Judas did not really care about the poor. Jesus said, “Leave her alone.” He knew He would soon die, and Mary had saved this for Him.","Mary gave Jesus her very best, with love. Tonight, think of something special you could give to someone you love."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” Mary gave Jesus the most precious thing she owned, without holding back. Willow understands a heart so devoted it gives its very best. In Willow’s world today: Willow has one special sticker, a shiny gold star she has saved for months. Her Primary teacher is moving away. Willow takes the sticker off her mirror and puts it on a card: “For the best teacher.” The teacher’s eyes fill up. Willow is not sad to give it. She is glad. Just like Mary, Willow learned that being devoted to Jesus means giving Him our very best with a glad heart.","feel":"Hold a pretend jar of perfume close, your most precious thing. Now tip it over slowly, slowly, pouring it all out. Take a deep breath in: smell the sweetness! Can you show me your pouring hands?","stones":[["SPOT","Mary poured her most expensive perfume on Jesus’s feet. Judas grumbled. Mary felt devoted, and Jesus praised her."],["NAME","That I’ll-give-You-my-very-best feeling is called devoted. Your mind might think, “Nothing is too good for Him.” Your body might feel open hands and a glad heart."],["FIND","When did you give someone your very best thing because you loved them?"],["FORK","Mary could save her perfume, or pour it all out for Jesus. She poured it all out."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly, like smelling something sweet. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me give Jesus my best.”"]],"remember":"Mary gave Jesus her very best, and so can you!","ask":["“What did Mary pour on Jesus’s feet?”","“What did Jesus say when Judas grumbled?”","“What did Willow give her Primary teacher?”"],"share":["Share a time you gave Jesus your best without holding back.","What is the perfume in your life that you are tempted to keep instead of pour out?","Did a grandparent or ancestor show devotion to the Savior with a costly gift of time or treasure? Tell about them."]},"b17-w3-n7":{"story":["Visitors called Greeks came from far away to Jerusalem for a feast. They told Philip, “Sir, we would like to see Jesus.”","Philip and Andrew told Jesus. Jesus knew the time was near for Him to give His life.","So Jesus taught with a tiny seed of wheat.","He said a seed that stays out of the ground is just one seed. But if it is planted in the dark dirt, it seems gone. Then up comes a plant full of new seeds! Jesus would be buried like that seed, and bring new life to everyone.","Good things can grow even when we cannot see them. Tonight, thank Jesus for giving His life so we can live again."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer pauses and ponders a seed in the dark ground. It looks like nothing is happening, but something wonderful is coming. Wren has learned to wait like a seed. In Wren’s world today: Wren plants a bean and waits. One day, two days, a week. Nothing. She wants to dig it up. Grandpa says, “The seed is busy under there. Be patient.” On day nine, a green loop pushes up through the dirt. Wren whispers, “It was growing the whole time.” Just like the grain of wheat, Wren learned that patience in the dark leads to new life and much fruit.","feel":"Curl up tiny like a seed under your blanket, in the dark, waiting, waiting. Now slowly push up, up, up, and open your arms like a big wheat plant full of seeds! Can you show me your seed growing?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus said a grain of wheat must fall into the ground to bring much fruit. His friends had to wait patiently to understand."],["NAME","That I’ll-wait-in-the-dark, something-is-coming feeling is called patient. Your mind might think, “It is growing even when I can’t see.” Your body might feel still and curled, then slowly stretching."],["FIND","When did you wait a long time for something to grow or happen?"],["FORK","A seed can stay a single seed, or fall into the ground and grow. Jesus chose to give His life so we could live."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly like a seed drinking rain. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me be patient while good things grow.”"]],"remember":"A grain of wheat falls into the ground and grows into much fruit, and so does following Jesus!","ask":["“What did the visitors from Greece want?”","“What happens when a grain of wheat falls into the ground?”","“What came up out of Wren’s dirt on day nine?”"],"share":["Share a time something that felt like an ending became the beginning of much fruit.","What does Jesus’s promise, “Where I am, My servant will be,” mean to you?","Did a grandparent or ancestor plant seeds of faith they never saw grow? Tell their story."]},"b17-w4-n1":{"story":["Jesus sat with His friends on a hill called the Mount of Olives. They asked, “When will You come again?”","Jesus said hard times would come first. Some people would try to trick others. But He said, “See that you are not troubled.”","He said everyone will see Him come in the clouds with great glory. His angels will gather His people.","Then Jesus said, “Heaven and earth will pass away, but My words will not.” Mountains can crumble, but Jesus’s words stay true forever. He said whoever treasures His words will not be tricked.","When things feel wobbly, hold Jesus’s words close, like your most special treasure. They are the steadiest thing there is. Tonight, say softly, “Jesus’s words stay true.”"],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer pauses and ponders what lasts. Jesus said heaven and earth will pass away, but His words never will. When everything else feels wobbly, Wren holds onto that and feels steady. In Wren’s world today: The news is loud, and the grown-ups look worried. Wren’s tummy feels wobbly. Grandma turns off the television, opens the scriptures, and reads, “My words shall not pass away.” She says, “Everything else changes. This doesn’t.” Wren leans on her and feels steady again. Just like the disciples on the mountain, Wren learned that Jesus’s words are the steady thing to hold when the world feels shaky.","feel":"Wobble your whole body like a shaky world, wobble, wobble. Now plant your feet, put your hands on a pretend rock, and go perfectly still. Can you show me your steady rock?","stones":[["SPOT","The disciples asked about hard times to come. Jesus said His words never pass away. They felt steady."],["NAME","That nothing-can-shake-me feeling is called steady. Your mind might think, “His words don’t change.” Your body might feel planted feet and a calm chest."],["FIND","When did something feel wobbly, and holding onto something true made you steady?"],["FORK","The disciples could be troubled by the hard news, or hold onto Jesus’s words. Jesus told them to hold on."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and plant your feet in your heart. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that Jesus’s words last forever.”"]],"remember":"Heaven and earth will pass away, but Jesus’s words will never pass away!","ask":["“Where were Jesus and His disciples sitting when they asked about His coming?”","“What did Jesus say will never pass away?”","“What did Grandma read to Wren when the news was loud?”"],"share":["Share a scripture that has been your steady rock in a shaky time.","How do you treasure up the Savior’s words so you are not deceived or troubled?","Did a grandparent or ancestor hold onto the scriptures through a frightening time? Tell their story."]},"b17-w4-n2":{"story":["Jesus told a little story about ten young women waiting for a groom. He would come at night, so they carried oil lamps.","Five wise ones brought extra oil in jars. Five foolish ones brought none.","The groom took a long time, and they all fell asleep. At midnight someone called, “He’s coming!”","“Our lamps are going out!” said the foolish ones. But the wise ones had no oil to spare. While they went to buy more, the groom came. The ready ones went in to the feast. Jesus said, “Watch, for you do not know the day or the hour.”","The oil is like faith and goodness, gathered a little every day. Tonight, add a drop to your lamp with a prayer."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big heart, brave start!” The wise young women were ready before anyone shouted. Beau likes to get ready early, so when the moment comes, he is not scrambling. In Beau’s world today: The night before the camping trip, Beau packs his bag: flashlight, extra batteries, jacket, water. His cousin says, “I’ll do mine in the morning.” In the morning, Dad says, “Trucks are leaving, now!” Beau grabs his bag and goes. His cousin runs around looking for his shoes. Just like the wise young women, Beau learned that being prepared means filling your lamp before the moment comes.","feel":"Pretend to pour oil into a lamp, glug, glug, nice and full. Now lie down and sleep. Hear the shout, “He’s coming!” and sit up with your lamp shining bright. Can you show me your ready lamp?","stones":[["SPOT","Five women brought extra oil, and five did not. When the bridegroom came, the prepared ones went in. They felt prepared."],["NAME","That I-did-it-ahead-of-time, I’m-all-set feeling is called prepared. Your mind might think, “I filled my lamp already.” Your body might feel calm hands and a steady lamp."],["FIND","When did getting ready early help you when the moment came?"],["FORK","The young women could bring extra oil, or hope they wouldn’t need it. The wise ones brought it."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel your full lamp glowing. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me be ready for Jesus.”"]],"remember":"Fill your lamp a little every day, so you are ready when Jesus comes!","ask":["“What did the five wise young women bring that the foolish ones did not?”","“What did the five ready young women do when the groom came?”","“What did Beau pack the night before the camping trip?”"],"share":["Share how you fill your lamp a little each day.","What oil can no one else carry for you, and how are you building it up?","Did a grandparent or ancestor live prepared, spiritually and practically? Tell about them."]},"b17-w4-n3":{"story":["Jesus told a little story. A man went on a long trip. He gave his helpers big bags of silver, called talents.","One helper got five talents, one got two, and one got one. The first two worked hard and doubled theirs.","But the helper with one talent was afraid. He hid it in a hole in the ground.","When the master came home, he was glad with the first two. “Well done, good and faithful servant!” he said. “Come and share my joy.” But the third had only the talent he buried. The master was not pleased.","Heavenly Father gives each of us gifts. He wants us to use them, not bury them. Tonight, name one gift, and plan to share it."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see, what could it be?” He looks for what he is good at, and then he works at it until it grows. Burying a gift in the ground is the one thing Scout would never do. In Scout’s world today: Scout is good at drawing. He could keep his pictures in a drawer. Instead he draws a picture for every person in the nursing home and hands them out. One lady cries happy tears. “You have a gift,” she says. Scout thinks, “And I’m going to use it.” Just like the good servants, Scout learned that diligently using our gifts makes them grow, and Heavenly Father says, “Well done.”","feel":"Pretend to dig a hole and bury something, no! Fill it back in. Now pretend to work hard with your hands, build, trade, grow. Hold up ten fingers: five became ten! Can you show me your well-done hands?","stones":[["SPOT","Two servants worked hard and doubled their talents. One hid his in the ground. The diligent ones heard, “Well done.”"],["NAME","That I’ll-work-at-it-and-make-it-grow feeling is called diligent. Your mind might think, “Let’s see what I can do with this.” Your body might feel busy hands and a proud chest."],["FIND","When did you work hard at something you are good at, and it got even better?"],["FORK","The servants could use their talents or bury them. The diligent ones used them."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your busy hands rest. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for my gifts. Help me use them.”"]],"remember":"Use the gifts Heavenly Father gave you, and He will say well done!","ask":["“What did the servant with one talent do with it?”","“What did the master say to the servants who made their talents grow?”","“What did Scout do with his drawings?”"],"share":["Share a gift Heavenly Father gave you and how you have tried to make it grow.","What talent have you buried out of fear, and how could you dig it up?","Did a grandparent or ancestor use their gifts to bless others? Tell about them."]},"b17-w4-n4":{"story":["Jesus told His friends about the day He will come again as King.","He will say to the good people, “I was hungry, and you fed Me. I was thirsty, and you gave Me a drink.”","“I was a stranger, and you took Me in. I needed clothes, and you gave them. I was sick and in prison, and you came to Me.”","The good people will wonder, “When did we do that for You?” The King will answer, “When you helped the least of these, you helped Me.” He means even the smallest, most left-out person.","When you care for someone sad or lonely, you care for Jesus. Tonight, think of one person you can help tomorrow."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” Jesus said that when we care for the hungry, the lonely, and the sick, we are caring for Him. Willow has always cared for people, and now she knows who she is really hugging. In Willow’s world today: An old man at the bus stop is shivering. Willow takes off her extra scarf and wraps it around his neck. “You keep it,” she says. On the bus, she thinks about Jesus’s words. “I just gave Jesus a scarf,” she whispers to Mom. Mom squeezes her hand. Just like Jesus taught, Willow learned that caring for the least of these is caring for Jesus Himself.","feel":"Pretend to hand someone bread, then a cup of water, then wrap a coat around them. Now put your hand on your heart: that was for Jesus. Can you show me your caring hands?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus said that when we feed the hungry and visit the sick, we do it for Him. The ones who cared felt caring."],["NAME","That I-see-someone-who-needs-me feeling is called caring. Your mind might think, “This is for Jesus.” Your body might feel warm hands and soft eyes."],["FIND","When did you help someone hungry, sad, or lonely?"],["FORK","We can walk past the least of these, or care for them as if they were Jesus. Jesus says care."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your caring hands rest. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me care for people like they are Jesus.”"]],"remember":"When you help the least of these, you are helping Jesus!","ask":["“What six things did Jesus say the good people did for Him?”","“What did the King say when they asked when they had helped Him?”","“What did Willow give the shivering man at the bus stop?”"],"share":["Share a time you served someone and felt you were serving Jesus.","Who are the least of these in your neighborhood, and what do they need?","Did a grandparent or ancestor care for the poor, the sick, or the stranger? Tell their story."]},"b17-w4-n5":{"story":["Jesus sat in the temple, Heavenly Father’s holy house. He watched people put money in the offering box.","Many rich people dropped in big handfuls of coins. Clink, clink, clink!","Then a poor woman came. Her husband had died, and she had almost nothing. She dropped in two tiny coins.","Jesus called His friends over. “This poor widow has given more than all the others,” He said. “They gave what they had extra. She gave all she had to live on.” Jesus did not count the coins. He counted the heart.","When you give with your whole heart, Heavenly Father sees it. Even a small gift can be very big. Tonight, give someone a hug with your whole heart."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” The widow gave two tiny coins, and Jesus said she gave the most, because she gave everything. Willow knows that generous is about the heart, not the size. In Willow’s world today: It is tithing time. Willow has earned one dollar in dimes. She counts out her tithing, one dime, then looks at the rest. She puts all ten dimes in the envelope. “It’s not very much,” she says. Dad says, “Jesus would say it’s a lot.” Just like the widow, Willow learned that a generous heart gives everything, and Jesus sees it.","feel":"Pretend to drop in a huge handful of coins, clink, clink, clink! Now hold two tiny coins in your fingertips and drop them in, so quiet. Put your hand on your heart. Can you show me your two-coin gift?","stones":[["SPOT","Rich people gave big handfuls. The widow gave two tiny coins, everything she had. She was the most generous."],["NAME","That I’ll-give-all-I-have feeling is called generous. Your mind might think, “It’s small, but it’s everything.” Your body might feel open hands and a full heart."],["FIND","When did you give something small that was everything you had?"],["FORK","The widow could keep her two coins, or give them all. She gave them all."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and open your hands. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, I give You all my heart.”"]],"remember":"Jesus counts the heart, not the coins, so give with your whole heart!","ask":["“How much did the widow put in?”","“Why did Jesus say she gave more than the rich people?”","“How many dimes did Willow put in her envelope?”"],"share":["Share a time you gave from your need, not your leftovers, and what happened.","What are you holding back that Jesus would count as your two coins?","Did a grandparent or ancestor give generously from very little? Tell their story."]},"b17-w4-n6":{"story":["Jesus told His friends that He will come again. But no one knows the day or the hour, not even the angels. Only Heavenly Father knows.","So Jesus said, “Watch and pray.” Then He told a tiny story.","“A man went on a long trip. He gave each helper a job. He told the doorkeeper to watch the door.”","“So watch,” Jesus said, “because you do not know when the master will come. It could be evening, midnight, or morning. Don’t let him find you sleeping. What I say to you, I say to all: Watch!”","Jesus does not mean never sleep. He means be ready every day. Tonight, say your prayer, like a good doorkeeper keeping watch."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big heart, brave start!” A doorkeeper stays awake and alert when everyone else sleeps, and Beau wants to be the one who opens the door for Jesus. In Beau’s world today: Dad puts Beau in charge of the campsite while he fills the water jugs. “Keep an eye on things.” Beau sits on the cooler, watching the tent, the fire, the trail. A raccoon creeps toward the food, and Beau claps loudly and shoos it away. When Dad gets back, Beau says, “I watched.” Just like the doorkeeper, Beau learned that being watchful means doing our jobs with our eyes open, ready for Jesus.","feel":"Make big, wide-open eyes and look left, right, up, down, like a doorkeeper. Now cup your ear to listen. Nod: I’m watching! Can you show me your doorkeeper eyes?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus said no one knows when He will come, so watch. The doorkeeper stayed awake and felt watchful."],["NAME","That eyes-open, paying-attention feeling is called watchful. Your mind might think, “I won’t let Him find me sleeping.” Your body might feel alert eyes and listening ears."],["FIND","When were you put in charge of watching something, and you stayed alert?"],["FORK","The doorkeeper could doze off, or stay awake and watch. Jesus said watch."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your watching eyes rest for the night. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me live ready for Jesus.”"]],"remember":"Watch and pray, and live every day ready for Jesus to come!","ask":["“Who knows the day and hour when Jesus will come again?”","“What did the master tell the doorkeeper to do?”","“What did Beau shoo away from the campsite?”"],"share":["Share what it means to you to watch for the Savior’s coming without fear.","What job has the Master given you to do well while you watch?","Did a grandparent or ancestor live alert and faithful, watching for the Lord? Tell about them."]},"b17-w4-n7":{"story":["Jesus told His friends about signs that will come before He returns. There will be signs in the sun, moon, and stars.","The sea will roar, and many people will be afraid.","But then Jesus told the best part. People will see Him coming in a cloud with great glory!","Jesus said, “When these things begin, look up and lift up your heads. Your redemption is coming close.” Redemption is Jesus saving us and making everything right. He said His words will never pass away.","When the world feels scary, don’t look down. Look up, like seeing blue sky after a storm. Tonight, lift up your head and smile. Jesus is coming."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer pauses and ponders. Jesus said that when scary things happen, we should lift up our heads, because He is coming. Wren looks up, and she feels hopeful instead of afraid. In Wren’s world today: A big storm knocks down a tree in the yard. Wren watches from the window, scared. Then Dad says, “Look up.” Through the clouds, a patch of blue sky and a rainbow. “Storms end,” Dad says. “And Jesus is coming.” Wren lifts her head and smiles. Just like the disciples, Wren learned that when things feel scary, we lift up our heads with hope, because Jesus is coming.","feel":"Let your head droop down low, worried. Now slowly, slowly lift your head up, up, up, and look at the ceiling with a big hopeful smile. Can you show me your lifted-up head?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus told about scary signs, then said to lift up your heads because He is coming. The disciples felt hopeful."],["NAME","That look-up, something-wonderful-is-coming feeling is called hopeful. Your mind might think, “Jesus is coming to make it right.” Your body might feel a lifted head and a lighter chest."],["FIND","When did something scary end, and you saw something beautiful afterward?"],["FORK","We can look down in fear, or lift up our heads in hope. Jesus says lift up your heads."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and lift your chin. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that Jesus is coming, and I can hope.”"]],"remember":"When the world feels scary, lift up your head, because Jesus is coming!","ask":["“What did Jesus say to do when the scary signs begin?”","“What does redemption mean?”","“What did Wren see when Dad told her to look up?”"],"share":["Share a time you lifted your head in hope during a scary season.","How do you help your family look up instead of fear when the news is frightening?","Did a grandparent or ancestor keep hope in Jesus’s coming through hard years? Tell their story."]},"b17-w5-n1":{"story":["Jesus was eating supper at Simon’s house, in a little town called Bethany.","A woman came in with a small white stone jar of costly perfume. She broke it open and poured it all on Jesus’s head.","Some people there grumbled at her. “What a waste!” they said. “That perfume could have been sold to help the poor.”","But Jesus said, “Leave her alone. She has done a good thing for Me. She did what she could.” Jesus knew He would die soon. Her perfume was a loving way to get Him ready. To Jesus, her gift was beautiful.","The woman gave Jesus her whole jar. Tonight, give Him your whole heart. Do one kind thing with all your love."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” The woman did not pour out a little perfume. She broke the jar and gave it all. Willow loves wholeheartedly, with nothing held back, just like that. In Willow’s world today: Willow is making a card for Grandma, who is sick. She could draw one flower. Instead she draws a whole garden, colors every petal, adds glitter, and writes, “I love you,” in her very best letters. It takes all afternoon. “I did what I could,” she says. Just like the woman with the alabaster box, Willow learned that giving Jesus our whole heart is a beautiful thing.","feel":"Hold a pretend jar of perfume with both hands. Now break it open and pour it all out over a pretend head, every last drop. Breathe in the sweet smell. Can you show me your all-of-it pour?","stones":[["SPOT","The woman poured her whole jar of perfume on Jesus. Some grumbled, but Jesus called it beautiful. She felt wholehearted."],["NAME","That all-of-me, nothing-held-back feeling is called wholehearted. Your mind might think, “He gets my best and my all.” Your body might feel open hands and a full chest."],["FIND","When did you give everything you had to something, and it felt beautiful?"],["FORK","The woman could keep her perfume, or pour it all out for Jesus. She poured it all."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly, like smelling sweet perfume. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, I give Jesus my whole heart.”"]],"remember":"She did what she could, and Jesus called it beautiful!","ask":["“What did the woman pour on Jesus’s head?”","“What did Jesus say about what she had done?”","“What did Willow make for her sick grandma?”"],"share":["Share a time you gave the Savior your whole heart and felt it was beautiful.","What does “she did what she could” mean for you in this season of your life?","Did a grandparent or ancestor serve the Lord wholeheartedly? Tell about them."]},"b17-w5-n2":{"story":["It was time for the Passover supper. It helped people remember how Heavenly Father saved them.","Jesus’s friends got a room ready in the city. That evening, Jesus sat down to eat with His twelve friends.","While they ate, Jesus took bread. He blessed it, broke it, and gave it to them. He said, “Take, eat. This is My body.”","Then He took the cup and gave thanks. He said, “Drink, all of you. This is My blood, given so many can be forgiven.” This was the very first sacrament, to help them remember Him. Each Sunday, we take the sacrament to remember Him too.","Tonight, think of Jesus breaking the bread. Whisper, “I remember You.”"],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer pauses and ponders, and that is what the sacrament is for: a pause to remember. Jesus gave His friends bread and a cup so they would never forget Him. In Wren’s world today: During the sacrament, Wren closes her eyes while the bread tray comes. She remembers the last supper, the bread, the cup, Jesus’s friends. She takes her piece and thinks, “I remember You.” The chapel is quiet, and her heart feels close to Him. Just like the disciples at the last supper, Wren learned that the sacrament is how we remember Jesus every week.","feel":"Pretend to break a piece of bread and hold it up. Now lift a small cup. Close your eyes and whisper, “I remember You.” Can you show me your remembering hands?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus gave His disciples bread and a cup to remember Him. They felt the first sacrament, remembering."],["NAME","That I-will-never-forget-You feeling is called remembering. Your mind might think, “This is for Jesus.” Your body might feel still hands and closed eyes."],["FIND","When did you hold something that helped you remember someone you love?"],["FORK","We can let the sacrament go by without thinking, or use it to remember Jesus. Jesus asks us to remember."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly with your eyes closed. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me always remember Jesus.”"]],"remember":"The sacrament is how we remember Jesus, just like His friends did at the last supper!","ask":["“What did Jesus say when He gave His disciples the bread?”","“What do we do each Sunday to remember Jesus?”","“What did Wren think about when the bread tray came?”"],"share":["Share what you remember about Jesus during the sacrament.","What promise do you renew each week, and how does it change your Monday?","Did a grandparent or ancestor hold the sacrament sacred? Tell what you remember of them."]},"b17-w5-n3":{"story":["After supper, Jesus took Peter, James, and John into a garden called Gethsemane.","Jesus’s heart felt heavy. He said, “Stay here and watch with Me.” Then He went a little farther to pray.","He called the hard thing ahead of Him a cup. He prayed, “My Father, if it can be, let this cup pass. But let Your will be done, not Mine.”","Jesus came back and found His friends asleep. He said to Peter, “Could you not watch with Me one hour? The spirit is willing, but the body is weak.” Jesus understood. Their hearts were willing, but they were so tired.","Sometimes your body is tired too. Jesus understands. Tonight, tell Him, “I want to follow You.”"],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see,” and he hates to miss anything. But even Scout gets sleepy. Jesus said, “The spirit is willing, but the flesh is weak,” and Scout knows exactly what that means. In Scout’s world today: Scout promises to stay up and help Dad wait for Mom’s late flight. He really wants to. He sits on the couch with his eyes wide... and wakes up in his bed the next morning. “I wanted to stay awake,” he tells Dad. Dad says, “Your spirit was willing. I know.” Just like Peter, James, and John, Scout learned that Jesus understands when our willing hearts have tired bodies.","feel":"Sit up straight with big, wide eyes, watching. Now let your eyelids get heavy, heavier, and your head droop. Pop back up! Watch and pray! Can you show me your trying-to-stay-awake eyes?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus asked His friends to watch with Him, and they fell asleep. Their spirits were willing, but their bodies were tired."],["NAME","That I-really-want-to, even-if-I’m-tired feeling is called willing. Your mind might think, “I want to stay with Him.” Your body might feel heavy eyes but a reaching heart."],["FIND","When did you really want to do something, but your body was too tired?"],["FORK","Jesus could be angry at His sleeping friends, or understand them. He understood."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your tired body rest. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, my heart is willing. Keep me close to Jesus.”"]],"remember":"The spirit is willing, and Jesus understands when our bodies are tired!","ask":["“What did Jesus ask Peter, James, and John to do?”","“What did Jesus pray in the garden?”","“Where did Scout wake up the next morning?”"],"share":["Share a time your spirit was willing but your flesh was weak, and how Jesus understood.","How do you watch and pray so you do not fall into temptation?","Did a grandparent or ancestor keep a willing heart through weakness or illness? Tell about them."]},"b17-w5-n4":{"story":["A big crowd came with swords and sticks. Judas, one of Jesus’s twelve friends, came with them.","The men grabbed Jesus. One of Jesus’s friends swung his sword and cut off a man’s ear.","He wanted to fight for Jesus. But Jesus said, “Put your sword away. People who fight with swords get hurt by swords.”","Jesus said He could ask Heavenly Father for help. He would send more than twelve armies of angels! Jesus had all that power, but He chose peace. He touched the man’s ear and healed it. Then He went with the men, so He could save us.","When someone is mean, you may want to hit back. Take a big breath and open your hands. Choose peace, like Jesus."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big heart, brave start!” Jesus could have called twelve armies of angels, and He chose not to. Beau knows the bravest strength is the strength to hold back. In Beau’s world today: A boy shoves Beau at the park and laughs. Beau’s fists clench. He could shove him back harder. Instead he takes a breath, unclenches his hands, and says, “I’m not going to fight you,” and walks to the swings. His heart is pounding, but he feels strong. Just like Jesus in the garden, Beau learned that self-control is choosing peace even when you have the power to fight.","feel":"Make tight fists and raise them like you want to fight. Now slowly open your hands, lower them, and let your shoulders drop. Breathe out. Can you show me your put-away-the-sword hands?","stones":[["SPOT","A disciple wanted to fight for Jesus. Jesus said put away your sword and healed the man. Jesus showed self-control."],["NAME","That I-could-but-I-won’t, I’ll-choose-peace feeling is called self-control. Your mind might think, “I’m strong enough not to fight.” Your body might feel fists opening and a slow breath out."],["FIND","When did you want to hit back, and you chose not to?"],["FORK","The disciple could fight with his sword, or put it away like Jesus said. Jesus chose peace."],["CALM","Breathe out slowly and open your hands. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me choose peace like Jesus.”"]],"remember":"Jesus had all the power, and He chose peace, so we can, too!","ask":["“What did one of the disciples do with his sword?”","“What did Jesus say He could ask Heavenly Father to send?”","“What did Beau do when the boy shoved him?”"],"share":["Share a time you put away your sword and chose the Savior’s way of peace.","Where is the Spirit asking you to hold back your power right now?","Did a grandparent or ancestor show self-control in a moment when they could have fought? Tell their story."]},"b17-w5-n5":{"story":["Peter said, “I will never leave You!” Jesus said a rooster would crow two times that night. Before it did, Peter would say three times he did not know Jesus.","Later, men took Jesus away. Peter sat outside, warming himself.","A girl who worked there said, “You were with Jesus!” Peter said, “I don’t know what you mean.” Then a rooster crowed.","Again she said it, and Peter said no. Then others said, “You are one of them!” Peter said, “I don’t know this man!” The rooster crowed a second time. Peter remembered Jesus’s words, and he cried. Later, Jesus forgave him.","When you do wrong, you can be sorry like Peter. Tell Jesus tonight. He forgives you and helps you try again."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm has a tender heart, and tender hearts know how Peter felt when the rooster crowed. Willow also knows that sorry tears are the beginning of being forgiven. In Willow’s world today: At recess, the kids tease a girl for praying before lunch. Someone asks, “Willow, do you pray, too?” Willow says, “Um... no.” Later, in the car, she cries. “I do pray. I was scared.” Mom hugs her. “You can tell them tomorrow. Jesus forgave Peter, and He forgives you.” Just like Peter, Willow learned that when we are sorry, Jesus forgives us and helps us be brave next time.","feel":"Shake your head no, no, no. Now hear a rooster crow, cock-a-doodle-doo, and let your face get sad and sorry. Then feel a hug and let a small smile come. Can you show me your sorry-then-forgiven face?","stones":[["SPOT","Peter said three times that he didn’t know Jesus. The rooster crowed, and Peter cried. He felt so sorry."],["NAME","That I-wish-I-could-take-it-back feeling is called sorry. Your mind might think, “Why did I say that?” Your body might feel tears and a heavy chest."],["FIND","When did you say something you wished you could take back, and you cried?"],["FORK","Peter could stay ashamed forever, or be sorry and let Jesus forgive him. He let Jesus forgive him."],["CALM","Breathe out slowly and let the heavy go. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, I’m sorry. Thank You that Jesus forgives me.”"]],"remember":"Peter was sorry, Jesus forgave him, and He forgives you, too!","ask":["“How many times did Peter say he didn’t know Jesus?”","“What did Peter do when he heard the rooster the second time?”","“What did Willow’s mom say in the car?”"],"share":["Share a time you denied the Savior by silence or fear, and how He forgave you.","What gives you courage to say, “Yes, I know Him,” next time?","Did a grandparent or ancestor fall short and then rise strong in faith like Peter? Tell their story."]},"b17-w5-n6":{"story":["It was almost Passover. Jesus knew He would soon go back to His Father. He loved His friends to the end.","So Jesus got up from supper. He tied a towel around His waist and poured water into a bowl.","Then He washed His friends’ feet, one by one. He dried each foot with the towel.","Jesus sat down again. He said, “I am your Lord, and I washed your feet. So wash each other’s feet. I showed you what to do. If you know these things, you are happy if you do them.”","Jesus showed that helping others brings real happiness. Tonight, find one way to help someone, like Jesus did. Then feel that happy glow inside."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker is always looking for what makes people happy. Jesus told him the secret: “Happy are you if you do them.” Serving like Jesus is where the happy is hiding. In Scout’s world today: Scout’s little sister stomps in with muddy boots and a scraped knee. Scout thinks of Jesus with the towel. He gets a wet cloth, wipes her boots, then gently cleans her knee and puts on a bandage. She stops crying. Scout feels a happy glow he did not expect. Just like the disciples, Scout learned that doing what Jesus did, serving others, is what makes us truly happy.","feel":"Pretend to wrap a towel around your waist and pour water into a basin. Now gently wash a friend’s feet and dry them. Sit up and feel the happy glow. Can you show me your serving-then-happy face?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus washed His disciples’ feet and said, “Happy are you if you do them.” The ones who served felt happy."],["NAME","That warm-glow-from-helping feeling is called happy. Your mind might think, “I did what Jesus did.” Your body might feel a glowing chest and a soft smile."],["FIND","When did helping someone give you a happy glow you did not expect?"],["FORK","The disciples could just know what Jesus taught, or do it. Jesus said happy are you if you do them."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel the happy glow. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me do what Jesus did.”"]],"remember":"Happy are you if you do what Jesus did: serve!","ask":["“What did Jesus do for His disciples at supper?”","“What did Jesus say makes us happy?”","“What did Scout do for his little sister’s muddy boots and scraped knee?”"],"share":["Share a time doing as Jesus did brought you unexpected happiness.","Whose feet is Jesus asking you to wash this week?","Did a grandparent or ancestor find happiness in humble service? Tell about them."]},"b17-w5-n7":{"story":["It was Jesus’s last night with His friends before He died. Judas had gone out into the night. Jesus looked at the friends He loved.","He called them “little children,” because He loved them so tenderly. He said, “I will be with you only a little while longer.”","Then He gave them something very important to remember.","Jesus said, “I give you a new commandment: love one another. Love each other the way I have loved you. This is how everyone will know you are My disciples, My followers.” Not by fancy clothes or big words, but by their love.","Tonight, think of someone who needs a friend. Tomorrow, sit by them or share with them. That is how people will know you follow Jesus."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big heart, brave start!” Jesus gave one new commandment: love one another as I have loved you. Beau’s big heart is for exactly that, and it takes a brave heart to love everyone. In Beau’s world today: The new kid at church sits alone, and some kids whisper about his clothes. Beau walks over, sits down, and shares his crayons. “I’m Beau.” The boy smiles. Later, a leader says, “Beau, that’s how people know you follow Jesus.” Beau’s heart feels big and warm. Just like the disciples, Beau learned that loving one another the way Jesus loves us is how people know we are His.","feel":"Hug yourself tight, that’s how much Jesus loves you. Now open your arms wide and pretend to hug everybody in the room, one at a time. Can you show me your love-one-another arms?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus gave a new commandment: love one another as I have loved you. The disciples who kept it were known by their kindness."],["NAME","That I’ll-love-you-the-way-Jesus-loves-me feeling is called kind. Your mind might think, “This is how they’ll know I’m His.” Your body might feel open arms and a warm heart."],["FIND","When did you show kindness to someone, and they could tell you follow Jesus?"],["FORK","We can love only our friends, or love one another as Jesus loved us. Jesus says love everyone."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your heart fill up. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me love everyone as Jesus loves me.”"]],"remember":"Love one another as Jesus loved you, and everyone will know you are His!","ask":["“What new commandment did Jesus give?”","“How will people know that we are Jesus’s disciples?”","“What did Beau do for the new kid at church?”"],"share":["Share a time someone’s love showed you they were a disciple of Jesus.","How does your family show the world whose disciples you are?","Did a grandparent or ancestor love people in a way that showed they followed Jesus? Tell their story."]},"b23-w1-n1":{"story":["Long ago, Heavenly Father gave His people laws carved on flat stones. But the people did not keep His laws. They kept forgetting.","So He promised something new. Hebrews says Jesus brought a better covenant, a promise with two sides.","Heavenly Father said, “I will put My laws into their mind.” He said, “I will write them in their hearts.” Even the littlest child could know Him.","Why the heart? Words on a stone can be left behind. Words in your heart go everywhere with you. He also promised, “Their sins I will remember no more.” When He forgives, He forgets.","Tonight, think of one good thing you just know to do. That is His law, written on your heart."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” Heavenly Father promised to write His laws on our hearts and forget our sins. Willow thinks a promise written on a heart is the warmest kind. In Willow’s world today: Willow can never remember the rules on the chore chart. But when her baby sister cries, Willow runs to her without checking any chart. Mom says, “That rule is written on your heart.” Willow likes that kind of rule best. Just like the better promise, Willow learned that Heavenly Father writes His laws on hearts, not stone.","feel":"Carve on a stone tablet, chip, chip, hard. Now put your finger on your heart and write there, soft and easy. Say, “I just know it.” Then wipe your hands: sins forgotten! Can you show me your heart-writing?","stones":[["SPOT","Heavenly Father promised to write His laws on our hearts, and remember our sins no more. Willow felt close."],["NAME","That He-is-in-my-heart feeling is called close. Your mind might think, “I just know Him.” Your body might feel a warm chest and a quiet smile."],["FIND","What good thing do you do without needing to be told, because it is written on your heart?"],["FORK","We can keep His laws only on paper, or let Him write them on our hearts. Let Him write."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel His love written on your heart. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for forgetting my sins.”"]],"remember":"I will write My laws in their hearts, and their sins I will remember no more!","ask":["“Where were the laws written long ago?”","“Where does Heavenly Father write His laws now?”","“What does Willow do without checking the chart?”"],"share":["Share something the Lord has written on your heart that you simply know.","Do you believe He remembers your sins no more? What would change if you did?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor whose faith was written on their heart."]},"b23-w1-n2":{"story":["Long ago, Heavenly Father’s people brought lambs to the temple to give to Him. It was a way to say sorry for wrong choices.","The priests gave those gifts again and again, every day. But the gifts could never take sins away for good.","Then Jesus came. He did not bring a lamb. He gave Himself.","The writer of Hebrews said Jesus gave Himself “once for all.” That means one time, for everyone, forever. Then Jesus sat down by Heavenly Father, because the work was finished.","After a wrong choice, Jesus does not have to do it all again. Just say sorry and come back to Him. He loves you."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big faith, brave heart!” Jesus did not bring a lamb. He was the Lamb, once for all. The bravest thing anyone ever did, and for Beau. In Beau’s world today: Beau breaks the same rule twice and worries, “Does Jesus have to do it again?” Dad shakes his head. “Once for all. He sat down because it was finished. You just say sorry and come back.” Beau lets out a big breath. Just like Hebrews teaches, Beau learned that Jesus gave Himself once for all, and the work is finished.","feel":"Carry a pretend lamb to the temple, again, and again, so tired! Now stop. Jesus did it once. Hold up one finger: once for all! Sit down, it is finished. Can you show me your once-for-all sit?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus offered Himself once for all, then sat down because the work was finished. Beau felt free."],["NAME","That it’s-already-done feeling is called free. Your mind might think, “I don’t earn it.” Your body might feel a big breath out, loose shoulders."],["FIND","When did someone finish a hard job for you, so you didn’t have to?"],["FORK","Beau could worry that Jesus has to keep paying, or trust that once was enough. He trusted."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let out the breath you were holding. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that Jesus finished it.”"]],"remember":"Jesus was offered once for all, and then He sat down, finished!","ask":["“What did people bring to the temple long ago?”","“How many times did Jesus give Himself?”","“Why did Jesus sit down?”"],"share":["Share what “once for all” means to you when you fall short again.","Where are you still trying to bring your own lamb instead of trusting His?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who rested in the finished work of Christ."]},"b23-w1-n3":{"story":["Can you see the wind? No. But you can see a kite fly and trees bend. So you know the wind is there.","The writer of Hebrews said, “Faith is the evidence of things not seen.” Faith is being sure of something good, even before you see it.","We cannot see how the world was made. But Hebrews says that by faith, we know Heavenly Father’s word made it.","Hebrews also says we must believe that Heavenly Father is real. And He gives good gifts to those who keep looking for Him.","You cannot see Heavenly Father tonight. But you can see the world He made. Say a prayer, and keep looking for Him."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see!” Scout likes to see things. But Hebrews says faith is evidence of things not seen. Scout is seeking, and he is sure. In Scout’s world today: Scout can’t see the wind, but he watches it fill his kite. “I can’t see it, but I know it’s there,” he tells Grandpa. “That’s faith. You can’t see Heavenly Father, but you see what He does.” Scout holds the string tighter. Just like Hebrews teaches, Scout learned that faith is being sure of what you cannot see yet.","feel":"Cover your eyes, can’t see it. Now hold out your hands and feel the wind blow, whoosh! Hold a kite string tight and feel the tug. Nod: “I know it’s there.” Can you show me your faith tug?","stones":[["SPOT","Faith is the evidence of things not seen, and Heavenly Father rewards those who seek Him. Scout felt sure."],["NAME","That can’t-see-it-but-I-know feeling is called sure. Your mind might think, “He is there.” Your body might feel a steady grip, a certain nod."],["FIND","What is something you are sure of even though you cannot see it?"],["FORK","We can believe only what our eyes see, or reach by faith for what Heavenly Father promised. Reach by faith."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel sure that Heavenly Father is right there, unseen. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, I believe You are.”"]],"remember":"Faith is the substance of things hoped for, the evidence of things not seen!","ask":["“What is faith the evidence of?”","“What must we believe to come to Heavenly Father?”","“What could Scout not see but knew was there?”"],"share":["Share a time faith was the “substance” of something you could not yet see.","What are you diligently seeking right now, and what reward do you hope for?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor whose faith was evidence of things not seen."]},"b23-w1-n4":{"story":["The writer of Hebrews told about people who trusted Heavenly Father. Each of their stories starts with “By faith.”","Heavenly Father warned Noah about a flood no one had seen yet. By faith, Noah built an ark, a giant boat, to save his family.","Heavenly Father called Abraham to go to a new land. By faith, Abraham obeyed. He went out, not knowing where he was going.","By faith, Sarah had a baby when she was very old. She trusted that Heavenly Father keeps His promises. From her family came more people than the stars in the sky.","Going somewhere new can feel scary. But you can go by faith, like Abraham. Heavenly Father always knows the way."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer says, “Wow, look at that!” Noah built a boat on dry land, Abraham walked with no map, old Sarah had a baby, all by faith. Wren wonders what follows her name. In Wren’s world today: Wren’s family prays about moving to a new town, and the answer is yes. Wren is scared. Then she remembers Abraham, who went out not knowing where. She packs her box and writes on it, “By faith, Wren.” Just like the heroes of Hebrews 11, Wren learned that “by faith” ordinary people can do hard, amazing things.","feel":"Hammer an ark like Noah, bang, bang. Pack a bag like Abraham and walk with no map. Rock a surprise baby like Sarah. Say, “By faith!” after each one. Can you show me your by-faith three?","stones":[["SPOT","By faith Noah built an ark, Abraham went out not knowing where, and Sarah received a baby. Wren felt inspired."],["NAME","That if-they-could-I-can feeling is called inspired. Your mind might think, “By faith, me too.” Your body might feel a lifted chin and ready hands."],["FIND","When did you do something scary because you trusted Heavenly Father’s promise?"],["FORK","Noah could stop hammering when people laughed, or keep building by faith. He kept building."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and put “by faith” in front of your own name. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, I will trust Your promises.”"]],"remember":"By faith Noah built, Abraham went, and Sarah believed, and so can you!","ask":["“What did Noah build by faith?”","“Where did Abraham go?”","“What did Wren write on her box?”"],"share":["Share your own “by faith” moment, the one you would want listed in Hebrews 11.","Where is the Lord asking you to go out “not knowing where”?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor whose name belongs on the “by faith” list."]},"b23-w1-n5":{"story":["Long ago in Egypt, the king made a cruel rule about baby boys. But by faith, baby Moses’s parents hid him for three months. They were not afraid of the king.","Moses grew up as the son of the king’s daughter. He could have kept all the treasures of Egypt.","But by faith, Moses said no. He chose to stay with Heavenly Father’s people, even when times were hard.","Why? Moses was looking ahead to the reward Heavenly Father promised. He kept going, as if he could see Heavenly Father beside him. Later, by faith, the people walked through the Red Sea on dry ground.","Sometimes doing right means leaving something fun behind. You can be brave like Moses. Heavenly Father is beside you too."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big faith, brave heart!” Moses gave up a palace and walked through the Red Sea, seeing the invisible Heavenly Father beside him. That is the brave Beau wants. In Beau’s world today: The cool kids invite Beau to their table, but they are making fun of a boy who stutters. Beau looks at the boy eating alone. He picks up his tray and sits with the boy. It feels like leaving a palace. Just like Moses, Beau learned that faith gives you courage to choose Heavenly Father’s people over a palace.","feel":"Sit on a comfy palace throne. Now stand up and walk away, step, step. Hold up your staff and see walls of water rise. March through on dry land! Can you show me your Red Sea march?","stones":[["SPOT","By faith Moses left the palace for Heavenly Father’s people and crossed the Red Sea. Beau felt courageous."],["NAME","That right-over-easy feeling is called courageous. Your mind might think, “He is beside me.” Your body might feel a straight back, marching feet."],["FIND","When did you leave something comfortable to do what was right?"],["FORK","Moses could stay a prince, or join Heavenly Father’s people. He joined them."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and picture the invisible Heavenly Father beside your bed. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me see You.”"]],"remember":"By faith Moses left the palace and walked through the sea, seeing Him who is invisible!","ask":["“Who hid baby Moses by faith?”","“What did Moses give up?”","“Where did Beau sit at lunch?”"],"share":["Share a time you chose Heavenly Father’s people over comfort.","How do you “endure as seeing Him who is invisible” on an ordinary hard day?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who gave up comfort for the gospel."]},"b23-w1-n6":{"story":["The writer of Hebrews had just told about Noah, Abraham, Sarah, and Moses. Then he said we have “a cloud of witnesses” all around us.","He pictured them like a big crowd around a race. They had finished their race of faith. They show us we can finish too.","“Let us lay aside every weight,” he said. Runners do not carry heavy bags. Wrong choices are heavy, and they slow us down.","“Let us run with patience the race,” Hebrews says, “looking unto Jesus.” Jesus started our faith, and He will help us finish. When we get tired, we can think of Him.","Is something heavy slowing you down tonight? Tell Heavenly Father, and set it down. Then keep your eyes on Jesus."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see!” Hebrews says we are surrounded by a cloud of witnesses, Noah, Sarah, and Moses, cheering us on. Scout ran looking at Jesus. In Scout’s world today: Scout runs the last lap of field day with a backpack he forgot to take off. He drops it and hears his family cheering from the bleachers. He fixes his eyes on the finish and runs, light and fast. Just like Hebrews teaches, Scout learned to drop every weight and run his race looking at Jesus, with the heroes cheering.","feel":"Shrug off a heavy pretend backpack, thunk! Look up at a stadium of heroes cheering, wave! Now fix your eyes on Jesus and run in place, run, run, run! Can you show me your cheered-on run?","stones":[["SPOT","We are surrounded by a cloud of witnesses, so lay aside every weight and run looking unto Jesus. Scout felt cheered."],["NAME","That everyone-is-rooting-for-me feeling is called cheered. Your mind might think, “I can too.” Your body might feel light feet, a lifted face."],["FIND","When did someone cheering for you help you keep going?"],["FORK","We can run carrying heavy weights, or lay them aside and look at Jesus. Lay them aside."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and hear the cloud of witnesses cheering softly. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for the heroes.”"]],"remember":"Lay aside every weight, and run looking unto Jesus!","ask":["“Who is in the cloud of witnesses?”","“Who should we look at while we run?”","“What did Scout drop before the last lap?”"],"share":["Share who is in your personal cloud of witnesses, cheering you on.","What weight do you need to lay aside to run your race well?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who is cheering for you from that cloud."]},"b23-w1-n7":{"story":["The letter of Hebrews ends with kind words. “Let brotherly love continue.” Keep loving each other like family.","Be kind to new people, Hebrews says. Some people have been kind to angels without even knowing it!","Hebrews also says, “Be content with such things as ye have.” Be happy with what you already have.","Why can we be happy? Because the Lord promised, “I will never leave thee.” So we can say, “The Lord is my helper, and I will not fear.” Jesus is the same yesterday, today, and forever.","When a room feels too quiet, whisper His promise: “I will never leave thee.” Say it again and again. The Lord is with you."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” Hebrews says, “I will never leave thee, nor forsake thee,” and Jesus is the same yesterday, today, and forever. Willow holds that like a warm hand in the dark. In Willow’s world today: Willow’s first sleepover away from home, and the house is too quiet. She feels alone. Then she remembers, “I will never leave thee.” She whispers it three times. The room does not change, but it feels less empty. She sleeps. Just like Hebrews promises, Willow learned that Jesus never leaves us and never changes.","feel":"Open your door wide to a stranger, come in! Give them a plate. Surprise, an angel! Now hold out your hand and feel Jesus take it: never leaving. Squeeze tight. Can you show me your never-leave squeeze?","stones":[["SPOT","Hebrews says welcome strangers and be content, for He said, “I will never leave thee.” Willow felt accompanied."],["NAME","That someone-is-with-me feeling is called accompanied. Your mind might think, “He never leaves.” Your body might feel a held hand, a settled breath."],["FIND","When did you feel alone, then remember that Jesus was right there?"],["FORK","Willow could lie awake feeling alone, or hold onto “I will never leave thee.” She held on."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel Jesus’s hand holding yours. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that Jesus never leaves me.”"]],"remember":"I will never leave thee, nor forsake thee, Jesus is the same forever!","ask":["“Who might we welcome without knowing it?”","“What did the Lord promise never to do?”","“What did Willow whisper three times at the sleepover?”"],"share":["Share a lonely time when “I will never leave thee” became real for you.","How does an unchanging Christ steady you when everything else changes?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who leaned on “I will never leave thee.”"]},"b23-w2-n1":{"story":["James was a helper of Jesus Christ. He wrote good advice to Heavenly Father’s people, far and wide.","He wrote, “If any of you lack wisdom, let him ask.” Wisdom is knowing the right thing to do.","James promised that Heavenly Father gives to everyone, and gives a lot. He will not scold you for asking.","But ask in faith, James said, without wobbling. Someone who wobbles is like a wave, tossed back and forth by the wind. Long ago, a boy named Joseph Smith read these words. He went into the woods and asked.","When you do not know what to do, ask Heavenly Father. Then wait and listen. He really answers."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer says, “Wow, look at that!” Wren has a million questions, and James says Heavenly Father gives wisdom to all who ask, and never scolds. In Wren’s world today: Wren can’t decide whether to tell on her friend who took a toy. She asks Heavenly Father what to do and waits, not wobbling. A quiet thought comes: “Tell your friend to give it back first.” She does, and her friend does. Wren says, “He really answers.” Just like James promised, Wren learned that Heavenly Father gives wisdom to everyone who asks in faith.","feel":"Scratch your head, I don’t know what to do! Now fold your hands and ask. Stand firm like a rock, no wobbling like a wave. Now tap your head: wisdom came! Can you show me your no-wobble ask?","stones":[["SPOT","James said if you lack wisdom, ask Heavenly Father in faith, without wavering. Wren felt expectant."],["NAME","That the-answer-is-coming feeling is called expectant. Your mind might think, “He will tell me.” Your body might feel still hands, listening ears."],["FIND","When did you ask Heavenly Father a question and get an answer?"],["FORK","We can wobble and wonder, or ask Heavenly Father in faith. Ask in faith."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and ask Heavenly Father for wisdom. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, I ask in faith, nothing wavering.”"]],"remember":"If any of you lack wisdom, let him ask of Heavenly Father!","ask":["“Who was James?”","“How does Heavenly Father give wisdom?”","“What did Wren ask Heavenly Father about?”"],"share":["Share a time James 1:5 was answered in your own life.","What question are you wavering on instead of asking in faith?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who asked Heavenly Father for wisdom and received it."]},"b23-w2-n2":{"story":["James wrote, “Be ye doers of the word, and not hearers only.” A hearer listens. A doer listens and then does it.","Then James drew a funny picture. A man looks in a mirror and sees his face. Then he walks away and forgets what he looked like!","Imagine seeing jam on your face, and then forgetting to wipe it off.","Why a mirror? Heavenly Father’s word shows us what to fix, like a mirror shows our face. A hearer sees it and walks away. A doer does something, and James said the doer will be blessed.","Think of one good thing you learned today. Tomorrow, go and do it. That makes you a doer!"],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big faith, brave heart!” James said be a doer of the word, not a hearer only. Hearing is easy, but doing takes a brave heart. In Beau’s world today: In Primary, Beau hears a lesson about visiting the lonely. On the way home he sees old Brother Hale sitting alone on his porch. Beau asks Mom to stop. He sits with Brother Hale for ten minutes. “Doer,” he whispers on the way home. Just like James taught, Beau learned that hearing is not enough, you have to do the word.","feel":"Look in a pretend mirror, oh no, jam on your face! Walk away and forget, silly. Now look again and wipe it off, scrub, scrub, doer! Point to your feet: go! Can you show me your doer wipe?","stones":[["SPOT","James said be doers of the word, not hearers only, like a man who sees his face in a mirror and forgets. Beau felt active."],["NAME","That I’m-going-to-do-it feeling is called active. Your mind might think, “Hearing isn’t enough.” Your body might feel moving feet, busy hands."],["FIND","When did you hear something good and actually go do it right away?"],["FORK","Beau could think “good lesson” and drive by, or stop and be a doer. He stopped."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and pick one thing you heard today to do tomorrow. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, make me a doer.”"]],"remember":"Be ye doers of the word, and not hearers only!","ask":["“What is the difference between a hearer and a doer?”","“What did James compare a hearer to?”","“What did Beau do after the lesson about the lonely?”"],"share":["Share a time doing the word, not just hearing it, blessed you.","What have you “seen in the mirror” lately and walked away from?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who was a doer of the word."]},"b23-w2-n3":{"story":["Some believers were treating poor people badly at church. So James wrote to help them stop.","Picture a rich man with a gold ring and fine clothes. He gets the good seat. A poor man in old clothes is told, “Stand over there.”","James said that is not fair. He called it “respect of persons,” treating some people as better than others.","James said Heavenly Father has chosen the poor to be “rich in faith.” The man in old clothes might be the richest in faith of all. So do what the scripture says: “Love thy neighbour as thyself.”","Tomorrow, look for someone sitting alone. Pat the seat beside you and say, “Sit by me.”"],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” James said do not give the best seat to the gold ring and make the poor man stand. Willow’s heart has room for everyone, in any clothes. In Willow’s world today: A new girl comes to Primary in a faded, too-small dress, and some kids scoot away. Willow scoots over and pats the chair. “Sit by me.” The girl knows every song by heart. Willow thinks, “Rich in faith.” Just like James taught, Willow learned to treat every person the same, because Heavenly Father does.","feel":"Bow to a man with a gold ring, ooh, fancy! Turn your back on a poor man, no! Stop. Pat the seat next to you for both. Same smile for each! Can you show me your same-seat pat?","stones":[["SPOT","James said do not give the rich the good seat and the poor a bad one, for the poor are rich in faith. Willow felt fair."],["NAME","That everyone-gets-the-same-seat feeling is called fair. Your mind might think, “No gold rings.” Your body might feel a patted chair, an equal smile."],["FIND","When did you make room for someone others were scooting away from?"],["FORK","Willow could scoot away like the others, or pat the chair for the new girl. She patted the chair."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and picture everyone in the same good seat. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me see people as You do.”"]],"remember":"Love your neighbor as yourself, with no respect of persons!","ask":["“What is respect of persons?”","“Who did James say Heavenly Father has chosen, rich in faith?”","“What did the new girl know by heart?”"],"share":["Share a time you learned someone “poor” in the world’s eyes was rich in faith.","Where might respect of persons be hiding in your ward or your heart?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who treated every person the same."]},"b23-w2-n4":{"story":["James asked, “What good is faith with no works?” Works are the good things we do.","Then he gave a picture. A brother or sister is cold and hungry. Someone says, “Go in peace. Stay warm and eat well!”","But he gives them no coat and no food. Did his words help? No!","“Even so faith, if it hath not works, is dead,” James said. Faith that does nothing is like a seed that never grows. James said, “I will shew thee my faith by my works.”","Who can you help tomorrow? Share a snack, help clean up, or give a hug. That is faith people can see."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see!” James said faith without works is dead, and you can see faith by what it does. Scout found faith he could see in a sandwich. In Scout’s world today: A homeless man sits outside the grocery store. Scout’s friend says, “Hope he’s okay,” and walks on. Scout stops, asks Mom for his allowance, and buys the man a sandwich and cocoa. “That’s what faith looks like,” Mom says. Just like James taught, Scout learned that real faith shows itself in what it does.","feel":"Say to a cold person, “Stay warm!” and walk away, uh oh, dead faith. Now go back, take off your pretend coat, and wrap it around them. Hand over a sandwich! Can you show me your faith hands?","stones":[["SPOT","James said faith without works is dead, and you show faith by what you do, like giving a coat and food. Scout felt helpful."],["NAME","That my-faith-does-something feeling is called helpful. Your mind might think, “Words aren’t enough.” Your body might feel giving hands, walking feet."],["FIND","When did you give something instead of just saying kind words?"],["FORK","Scout could say “hope he’s okay” and walk on, or buy the sandwich. He bought it."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and think of one thing your faith can do tomorrow. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, make my faith alive.”"]],"remember":"Faith without works is dead, so give your faith hands!","ask":["“What are works?”","“What is faith without works?”","“What did Scout buy the man outside the store?”"],"share":["Share a time your faith became visible through something you did.","Where are you saying “be warmed and filled” without handing over the coat?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor whose faith you could see in their works."]},"b23-w2-n5":{"story":["James said your tongue, the part of you that talks, is small but strong. He gave three pictures.","A small bit in a horse’s mouth turns the whole big horse. A small rudder under the water turns a great big ship.","And one tiny spark can start a great big fire. Our words can grow big like that too.","James said we use our mouths to praise Heavenly Father. Then sometimes we say mean things to people, who are made like Him. “These things ought not so to be,” James said. Kind words and mean words should not come from the same mouth.","If you said something unkind today, you can put that fire out. Say sorry, and say something kind."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer says, “Wow, look at that!” James said the tongue is tiny like a bit, a rudder, or a spark, but steers a horse, a ship, or burns a forest. In Wren’s world today: Wren says one mean thing about a girl’s drawing, a tiny spark. By lunch the girl is crying and kids are whispering. Wren feels sick. She goes to the girl. “I’m sorry. Your drawing is good.” The fire goes out. Just like James taught, Wren learned that a small tongue can start a big fire, so steer it carefully.","feel":"Steer a giant horse with a tiny bit, pull! Steer a huge ship with a little rudder, turn! Strike a tiny match, a forest catches, whoa! Blow it out, careful. Can you show me your careful tongue?","stones":[["SPOT","James said the tongue is small like a bit, a rudder, or a spark, so bless and do not curse. Wren felt careful."],["NAME","That watch-my-words feeling is called careful. Your mind might think, “Small spark, big fire.” Your body might feel closed lips and a thinking pause."],["FIND","When did one small word of yours cause something bigger than you meant?"],["FORK","Wren could let the spark keep spreading, or go put out the fire with a sorry. She put it out."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your tongue rest, tomorrow it will only bless. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help my mouth bless.”"]],"remember":"The tongue is a little fire, so steer your words with care!","ask":["“What three small things did James compare the tongue to?”","“What should not come out of the same mouth?”","“What was Wren’s tiny spark?”"],"share":["Share a time you learned how much power is in a small word.","Does your mouth bless and curse? What would help it do only one?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who was careful and kind with their words."]},"b23-w2-n6":{"story":["James wrote that Heavenly Father helps the humble. They are people who know they need Him.","Then James gave a plan. “Resist the devil, and he will flee from you.” When you say no to wrong things, the devil runs away.","But there is an even sweeter part.","“Draw nigh to Heavenly Father, and He will draw nigh to you.” Take one step toward Him, and He comes close to you. We step closer when we pray and choose right. Then, James said, “He shall lift you up.”","If something wrong pulls at you tomorrow, say no and walk away. Then say a little prayer, and feel Heavenly Father come close."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big faith, brave heart!” James said resist the devil and he runs, and draw near to Heavenly Father and He draws near. Beau likes a fight he can win. In Beau’s world today: Beau feels a pull to look at something wrong on his cousin’s tablet. “Resist,” he thinks. “No, let’s go outside.” His cousin follows. On the trampoline Beau says a quick prayer and feels Heavenly Father step closer. Just like James taught, Beau learned that when you draw near to Heavenly Father, He draws near to you.","feel":"Stand proud, nose in the air, hmph. Now bow down humble. Push back with both hands, “No!” Watch the enemy run. Step toward Heavenly Father, and feel Him step toward you. Can you show me your draw-near step?","stones":[["SPOT","James said resist the devil and he will flee, and draw near to Heavenly Father and He will draw near. Beau felt humbled."],["NAME","That I-need-Him feeling is called humbled. Your mind might think, “One step closer.” Your body might feel a bowed head and a lifted heart."],["FIND","When did you say no to something wrong and feel Heavenly Father closer afterward?"],["FORK","Beau could give in to the pull, or resist and go outside. He resisted."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and take one step closer to Heavenly Father in your heart. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, I draw near to You.”"]],"remember":"Draw near to Heavenly Father, and He will draw near to you!","ask":["“What happens when you resist the devil?”","“What happens when you draw near to Heavenly Father?”","“What did Beau say when he felt the pull?”"],"share":["Share a time you drew near to the Lord and felt Him draw near to you.","Where is pride keeping you from the grace He gives the humble?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who stayed humble and close to Heavenly Father."]},"b23-w2-n7":{"story":["James ended his letter with prayer. “Is anyone in trouble? Let him pray. Is anyone happy? Let him sing.”","When someone is sick, James said, call the elders who lead the church. They pray and bless the sick person with oil.","James promised, “The prayer of faith shall save the sick.” The Lord will lift him up.","Then James told about Elijah, a prophet long ago. Elijah was a man just like us. He prayed, and no rain came for three and a half years. He prayed again, and the rain came.","Do you know someone who is sick or sad? Pray for them with your whole heart tonight. Your prayers really help."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” James said the prayer of faith helps the sick, and regular Elijah’s prayers moved the rain. Willow believes hers matter too. In Willow’s world today: Grandma is sick, and Dad and Grandpa give her a blessing with oil. Willow kneels in the hallway and prays with her whole heart. In the morning Grandma is sitting up eating toast. “Your prayer availed much,” Grandpa says. Willow knows the feeling. Just like James taught, Willow learned that the fervent prayer of an ordinary person really does something.","feel":"Feel sick, hold your tummy. Bow your head for a blessing. Fold your hands and pray with your whole heart, squeeze! Now look up: rain! Hold out your hands and catch it. Can you show me your rain-catch?","stones":[["SPOT","James said the prayer of faith helps the sick, and Elijah, a man like us, prayed and the rain came. Willow felt believing."],["NAME","That my-prayer-counts feeling is called believing. Your mind might think, “He hears me.” Your body might feel squeezed-tight hands, a hopeful heart."],["FIND","When did you pray with your whole heart and see something happen?"],["FORK","Willow could just hope Grandma got better, or pray fervently. She prayed."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and pray with your whole heart for someone sick or sad. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, my prayer matters.”"]],"remember":"The fervent prayer of a righteous person avails much!","ask":["“What should we do when we are sick?”","“What happened when Elijah prayed?”","“What did Willow do in the hallway?”"],"share":["Share a time a blessing or a fervent prayer availed much in your family.","Do you pray like Elijah, earnestly, or like a wish? What would change?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor whose prayers you believe availed much."]},"b23-w3-n1":{"story":["Peter was one of Jesus’s friends. He wrote to believers far from home who were having a hard time.","Peter wrote that Jesus came alive again after He died. So our hope is alive too, and a home in heaven waits for us.","Peter knew hard times feel heavy. He said they test our faith, the way fire tests gold. Fire shows the gold is real.","Then Peter wrote about people who had never seen Jesus. “You have not seen Him, but you love Him,” he said. Believing filled them with joy unspeakable, joy too big for words.","You have not seen Jesus either, but you can love Him right now. Tonight, whisper, “I love you, Jesus,” and feel that big, warm joy."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see!” Peter wrote to people who had never seen Jesus, like Scout, and said they could love Him anyway with joy too big for words. In Scout’s world today: Scout has a pen pal he has never met. When her letter comes he jumps around the kitchen. “I love her and I’ve never even seen her!” Mom says, “That’s how we can love Jesus.” Scout stops. “Joy unspeakable.” Just like Peter taught, Scout learned that you can love Jesus you have never seen, with joy too big for words.","feel":"Put pretend gold in the fire, watch it glow, real! Cover your eyes, can’t see Jesus. Open your arms wide anyway: I love Him! Jump with joy too big for words! Can you show me your unspeakable jump?","stones":[["SPOT","Peter said though we have not seen Jesus, we love Him and rejoice with joy unspeakable. Scout felt joyful."],["NAME","That too-big-for-words feeling is called joyful. Your mind might think, “I can’t even say it.” Your body might feel jumping legs and a bursting chest."],["FIND","When were you so happy you could not find the words?"],["FORK","We can wait to love Jesus until we see Him, or love Him now by believing. Love Him now."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your joy settle into a quiet glow. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for joy unspeakable.”"]],"remember":"Whom having not seen, you love, with joy unspeakable and full of glory!","ask":["“Who wrote this letter?”","“What tests faith like fire tests gold?”","“Why did Scout jump around the kitchen?”"],"share":["Share a time trials refined your faith like gold.","How do you love Someone you have not seen? What makes it real for you?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who loved Jesus with joy unspeakable."]},"b23-w3-n2":{"story":["Peter wanted the believers to know how much they mattered. Houses then were built of stones. So he used a stone house to teach them.","Peter called Jesus a living stone. Some people did not want Him, but Heavenly Father chose Him. He is precious, like a treasure.","Peter said the believers are living stones too. Fitted together, they are built into a holy house.","Then Peter gave them more names. “You are a chosen generation, a royal priesthood, a holy nation, a peculiar people.” They were Heavenly Father’s very own. He called them out of the dark into His wonderful light.","You are a living stone, chosen and loved. Tonight, stand tall like a royal child and say, “I belong to Him.”"],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer says, “Wow, look at that!” Peter said we are living stones in Heavenly Father’s house, and a chosen, royal, holy people. A stone and a treasure at once! In Wren’s world today: Wren helps Grandpa build a rock wall. “Each rock needs the others,” he says. “Peter said you’re a living stone, and a royal treasure.” Wren pats her rock. “A royal rock,” she says, and Grandpa laughs. Just like Peter taught, Wren learned that she is a living stone in Heavenly Father’s house and part of His chosen, royal people.","feel":"Be a stone, curl up solid. Get fitted into a wall, snug. Stand up and put on a royal crown: chosen! Step out of the dark into the light! Can you show me your royal stone?","stones":[["SPOT","Peter said we are living stones in His house, a chosen generation, a royal priesthood. Wren felt royal."],["NAME","That I’m-His-treasure feeling is called royal. Your mind might think, “I belong to the King.” Your body might feel a straight back, a crowned head."],["FIND","When did you feel like you were a chosen, special part of something bigger?"],["FORK","We can feel like a loose rock, or let ourselves be fitted into His house. Be fitted in."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel yourself set snugly into His house. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that I am Yours.”"]],"remember":"You are a chosen generation, a royal priesthood, called into His marvellous light!","ask":["“What is Jesus called in this story?”","“Name two of the four names Peter gave the believers.”","“What did Wren call her rock?”"],"share":["Share what it means to you to be a living stone in His house.","How are you showing forth His praises for calling you out of darkness?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who was a sturdy living stone in your family’s house of faith."]},"b23-w3-n3":{"story":["Peter walked with Jesus and saw how He treated people. He saw Jesus stay kind, even when people were mean.","Peter wrote, “Christ left us an example, that we should follow His steps.” It is like stepping in footprints in the snow.","So what did Jesus’s steps look like? Jesus never did anything wrong. He never told a lie.","When people called Jesus mean names, He did not call them names back. When He was hurt, He did not say, “You’ll be sorry!” He trusted Heavenly Father, who is always fair.","When someone is mean to you, you can follow Jesus’s steps. Take a deep breath, say nothing mean back, and let Heavenly Father help."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big faith, brave heart!” Peter said when Jesus was called names, He did not call names back. Follow His steps. Not hitting back is braver. In Beau’s world today: A kid at the park calls Beau “baby” over and over. Beau’s fists curl. Then he thinks of Jesus’s steps: reviled, but reviled not again. He unclenches his hands, says nothing mean, and walks to the swings. His heart pounds, but it feels like Jesus’s footprint. Just like Peter taught, Beau learned to follow Jesus’s steps and not give mean words back.","feel":"Step into big footprints in the sand, step, step, following Jesus. Hear someone call you a name, ouch. Zip your lips. Hand it up to Heavenly Father. Keep stepping! Can you show me your footprint walk?","stones":[["SPOT","Peter said Jesus left an example: when reviled, He reviled not again. Beau felt self-controlled."],["NAME","That no-mean-back feeling is called self-controlled. Your mind might think, “Jesus didn’t.” Your body might feel unclenched fists, a steady walk."],["FIND","When did someone say something mean, and you chose not to say something mean back?"],["FORK","Beau could call the kid a name back, or follow Jesus’s steps. He followed Jesus."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your fists unclench. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me walk in Jesus’s steps.”"]],"remember":"Jesus left us an example, so follow His steps!","ask":["“What did Peter say Jesus left for us?”","“What did Jesus do when people called Him names?”","“What did Beau do when the kid called him “baby”?”"],"share":["Share a time you followed the Savior’s steps instead of reviling back.","What would committing your conflict to “Him who judges righteously” look like?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who answered unkindness with the Savior’s silence."]},"b23-w3-n4":{"story":["Peter wrote to believers who were sometimes treated badly for doing what was right. He told them, “Be not afraid, neither be troubled.”","Instead, Peter said, keep the Lord first and holy in your heart. Then you do not need to be afraid.","Then Peter gave a job even a small child can do. “Be ready always to give an answer” when someone asks why you have hope.","People may see that you are happy and hopeful, even on hard days. Someone may ask, “Why?” Peter said to answer with meekness, gently and kindly, not bossy or loud.","Get your answer ready tonight. It can be simple: “Because Jesus loves me.” Practice saying it softly, with a smile."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see!” Peter said be ready to give an answer when someone asks why you have hope. Scout practiced his answer. In Scout’s world today: Scout’s soccer coach asks, “Why are you always so cheerful, even when we lose?” Scout is ready. “Because Heavenly Father loves me no matter what the score is.” Coach is quiet, then says, “Huh. I like that.” Scout grins all the way home. Just like Peter taught, Scout learned to be ready to give a gentle answer for the hope inside him.","feel":"Someone taps your shoulder and asks, “Why are you so hopeful?” Hold up a ready finger, ding! Say your answer gently: “Because Jesus loves me.” Nod politely. Can you show me your ready answer?","stones":[["SPOT","Peter said be ready always to give an answer for the hope in you, with meekness and respect. Scout felt ready."],["NAME","That I-know-what-to-say feeling is called ready. Your mind might think, “Because Jesus loves me.” Your body might feel a lifted finger, a kind voice."],["FIND","When did someone ask you why you are happy or hopeful, and what did you say?"],["FORK","We can be caught with no answer, or have our reason for hope ready. Have it ready."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and practice your answer: why you have hope. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, my hope is because of You.”"]],"remember":"Be ready always to give an answer for the hope that is in you, with meekness!","ask":["“What should we be ready to give?”","“How should we give our answer?”","“What did Scout tell his coach?”"],"share":["Share your own one-sentence reason for the hope that is in you.","Is your answer ready, and is it meek? Practice it tonight.","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who could always explain the reason for their hope."]},"b23-w3-n5":{"story":["Peter said, above all, to love each other with a warm, whole heart. He called that love charity, the pure love of Christ.","Peter said charity covers lots and lots of sins. Love is like a soft blanket over a mistake. When you love someone, you stop pointing at what went wrong.","Peter also said to use hospitality: open your home with a warm welcome. Peter said to do it happily, without grumbling.","Heavenly Father gives each person good gifts. Peter said to use them to help each other.","Tonight, think of someone who made a mistake today. Cover it with love. Then plan one way to welcome a friend tomorrow."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” Peter said charity covers a multitude of sins, and open your home without grumbling. Willow’s blanket of love is big. In Willow’s world today: Willow’s friend spills juice all over Willow’s new rug and starts to cry. Willow could point at the stain. Instead she throws a blanket over it. “Covered! Now let’s have snacks.” Her friend laughs through her tears. Mom says, “Charity covered that one.” Just like Peter taught, Willow learned that love covers mistakes and an open door is a gift to share.","feel":"See a big messy mistake, oh no! Throw a warm blanket of love over it, whoosh, covered! Open your door wide and wave someone in. Hand them a snack, no grumbling! Can you show me your blanket-and-door?","stones":[["SPOT","Peter said charity covers a multitude of sins, and use hospitality without grudging. Willow felt hospitable."],["NAME","That come-in-it’s-covered feeling is called hospitable. Your mind might think, “Covered here.” Your body might feel an open door, a warm hand."],["FIND","When did someone cover your mistake with love instead of pointing at it?"],["FORK","Willow could point at the stain, or cover it with love and open the snacks. She covered it."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and throw a blanket of love over today’s mistakes. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, make my love cover.”"]],"remember":"Charity shall cover the multitude of sins, so love hot and open your door!","ask":["“What does charity cover?”","“What is hospitality?”","“What did Willow throw over the juice stain?”"],"share":["Share a time charity covered a multitude in your home.","Is your hospitality given without grudging? What would make it more glad?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor whose home was always open and whose love covered much."]},"b23-w3-n6":{"story":["Peter wrote to believers who had hard days and heavy worries. He gave them two sentences that go together.","First, Peter said to be humble, bowing low under Heavenly Father’s mighty hand. Then, at the right time, He will lift you up.","Second, Peter wrote, “Cast all your care upon Him, for He cares for you.” To cast is to throw far, like a fisherman throws his net. Peter was a fisherman, so he knew casting.","Peter said not to hold on to your cares, the worries you carry inside. Throw every one to Heavenly Father, because He cares for you.","Tonight, think of one worry. Pretend to throw it far, like a net into the sea. Then let your shoulders drop."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer says, “Wow, look at that!” Peter the fisherman said cast your cares on Heavenly Father like a net, because He cares for you. How far can a worry fly? In Wren’s world today: Wren is worried about her piano recital. Grandpa takes her to the pond. “Pick up a rock for each worry, and cast it.” Splash. Splash. “He’s got them now,” Grandpa says. Wren’s shoulders come down. Just like Peter taught, Wren learned to cast every care onto Heavenly Father, because He cares for her.","feel":"Pick up a heavy worry rock, groan. Wind up like a fisherman and cast it into the sea, splash! Another, cast it! All of them! Shake out your empty arms. Can you show me your worry cast?","stones":[["SPOT","Peter said humble yourself under His mighty hand and cast all your care upon Him, for He cares for you. Wren felt unburdened."],["NAME","That the-heavy-thing-is-gone feeling is called unburdened. Your mind might think, “He’s got it.” Your body might feel dropped shoulders, light arms."],["FIND","When did you give a worry to Heavenly Father and feel lighter?"],["FORK","Wren could carry her worry rocks into the recital, or cast them on Heavenly Father. She cast them."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and cast tonight’s biggest worry onto Heavenly Father, splash. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, here is my care.”"]],"remember":"Cast all your care upon Him, for He cares for you!","ask":["“What does cast mean?”","“Why can we throw our cares on Heavenly Father?”","“What did Wren throw into the pond?”"],"share":["Share a care you cast on Him and how it felt to let go.","What worry are you still holding instead of casting? Why?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who cast their cares on the Lord through hard years."]},"b23-w3-n7":{"story":["Peter wrote another letter to help the believers keep growing. He gave them something like a ladder to climb, one step at a time.","The first step is faith in Jesus. Then add goodness, and then learning what is true. Next comes self-control, and then patience.","Next, live the way Heavenly Father wants. Be kind like a brother or sister. At the very top is charity, the pure love of Christ.","Peter said these steps make your life full of good fruit, like a garden. You do not jump to the top. You add one step to the last.","Tonight, pick one step to work on tomorrow, like patience or kindness. Then climb it, one small try at a time."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big faith, brave heart!” Peter gave a ladder: faith, goodness, knowledge, self-control, patience, godliness, kindness, charity. Beau is climbing. In Beau’s world today: Beau draws Peter’s ladder on a paper and tapes it to his wall. Every night he picks one step to work on tomorrow. Tuesday is patience, and he waits for his little sister without sighing even once. He puts a star on the patience step. “Climbing,” he says. Just like Peter taught, Beau learned to grow by adding one good thing to the last, like climbing a ladder.","feel":"Climb a ladder one step at a time: faith, step! Goodness, step! Knowledge, step! Self-control, step! Patience, step! Godliness, step! Kindness, step! Charity, top! Wave from the top! Can you show me your ladder climb?","stones":[["SPOT","Peter said add to your faith goodness, knowledge, self-control, patience, and more, up to charity. Beau felt growing."],["NAME","That one-step-higher feeling is called growing. Your mind might think, “Add the next one.” Your body might feel climbing legs and reaching hands."],["FIND","Which step of Peter’s ladder did you climb today?"],["FORK","We can stay on one step forever, or keep adding the next one. Keep adding."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and pick one step of the ladder to work on tomorrow. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me keep climbing.”"]],"remember":"Add to your faith, one step at a time, all the way up to charity!","ask":["“What is the first step on Peter’s ladder?”","“What is at the top?”","“Which step did Beau work on Tuesday?”"],"share":["Share which rung of Peter’s ladder the Lord has been teaching you lately.","Which step do you keep skipping, and what would adding it look like?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who kept adding to their faith all their life."]},"b23-w4-n1":{"story":["John was one of Jesus’s friends. He had seen Jesus and heard Him speak. He wrote so others could know Him too.","John wrote, “Heavenly Father is light, and in Him is no darkness at all.” He is all good, without even one shadow.","John said we can walk in the light, like walking in sunshine. Then we stay close to Him and each other.","Everyone does wrong things sometimes. John wrote, “If we confess our sins, He will forgive us.” To confess is to tell the truth about what you did. Then He makes us clean.","Is something hiding in the dark that you feel bad about? Tonight, bring it into the light. Tell Mom or Dad and Heavenly Father, and feel clean."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see!” John said Heavenly Father is light with no darkness, and if we confess, He cleans us. In the light you see everything. In Scout’s world today: Scout hid a broken plate under the couch, a stone in his tummy. Finally he pulls it out and tells Mom. “Thank you for bringing it into the light,” she says. The stone is gone. Just like John taught, Scout learned that confessing brings dark things into the light, where Heavenly Father cleans them.","feel":"Hide in the dark with your hands over your eyes, a secret inside. Step into the light, uncover your eyes, and say the secret out loud. Feel it washed off, clean! Can you show me your into-the-light step?","stones":[["SPOT","John said Heavenly Father is light, and if we confess our sins, He forgives and cleanses us. Scout felt honest."],["NAME","That I-told-the-truth feeling is called honest. Your mind might think, “It’s in the light now.” Your body might feel a lighter tummy, clear eyes."],["FIND","When did you tell the truth about something you had hidden, and feel clean afterward?"],["FORK","Scout could keep the plate in the dark under the couch, or bring it into the light. He brought it out."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and bring anything hidden into His light. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, I confess, please make me clean.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father is light, and if we confess, He forgives and cleanses us!","ask":["“What is Heavenly Father, according to John?”","“What happens when we confess our sins?”","“What did Scout pull out from under the couch?”"],"share":["Share a time confession brought you out of darkness into light.","What are you still keeping under the couch that He is ready to cleanse?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who walked honestly in the light."]},"b23-w4-n2":{"story":["John wrote words that sound like a happy gasp. “Behold, what manner of love the Father has given us!” Behold means, “Look! Look at this!”","John could hardly believe it. Heavenly Father, greater than all the stars, calls us His own children.","Some people do not know this, because they do not know Him yet. John wanted the believers to know it for sure.","John wrote that we are Heavenly Father’s children now. Not someday, but now. And when Jesus comes again, we will be like Him.","You are Heavenly Father’s child tonight, right now. Give yourself a hug and whisper, “I am His, and He loves me.”"],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” John gasped, “What kind of love is this, that we are called His children!” Willow gasps too, every time. In Willow’s world today: Willow finds her baby book. On the first page Mom wrote, “Our daughter. Ours. We can hardly believe it.” Willow reads it three times. Dad says, “Heavenly Father feels that way about you, times a million.” Willow holds the book to her chest. Just like John wrote, Willow learned that Heavenly Father’s love makes us His children right now.","feel":"Gasp with your hand on your heart: “Behold!” Feel love poured out over your head, whoosh! Point to yourself: “His child, right now!” Stand tall, becoming like Jesus. Can you show me your behold gasp?","stones":[["SPOT","John said behold what manner of love, that we should be called the children of Heavenly Father, now. Willow felt adored."],["NAME","That loved-beyond-believing feeling is called adored. Your mind might think, “I am His, right now.” Your body might feel a gasp, a hand on your heart."],["FIND","When did someone’s love for you feel so big it made you gasp?"],["FORK","We can wonder if we belong to Him, or believe we are His children right now. Believe it."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and say, “Right now, I am His child.” Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for what manner of love.”"]],"remember":"Behold what manner of love, that we should be called the children of Heavenly Father!","ask":["“What does behold mean?”","“When are we the children of Heavenly Father?”","“What did Mom write on the first page of Willow’s baby book?”"],"share":["Share when “now are we the sons of Heavenly Father” became real to you.","What does hoping to be like Him make you want to purify this week?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who knew deeply they were a child of Heavenly Father."]},"b23-w4-n3":{"story":["John wanted everyone to know what real love looks like. So he pointed to Jesus. Jesus gave His life for us, and that shows us love.","John said we should help each other that way too, a little every day.","Then John asked a question. Say you have plenty, and someone has a need. If you shut your heart like a door, is love there?","John wrote, “Let us not love in word, but in deed and in truth.” A deed is something you do. Saying “I love you” is sweet, and John said to show it too.","Tonight, think of one thing you can do for someone you love. Help, share, or stay close. That is love in deed."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big faith, brave heart!” John said love not in word only, but in deed and in truth, like Jesus. Love is a verb, and verbs take brave. In Beau’s world today: Beau tells his little sister, “I love you,” every day. Then she gets sick and wants someone to read to her. Beau wants to play outside. He stays and reads six books. Dad says, “Today you loved her in deed.” Just like John taught, Beau learned that real love is something you do, not just something you say.","feel":"Say “I love you” with your mouth. Now show it with your hands: read to someone, open a door you had shut, give up your turn. Love in deed! Can you show me your love-verb hands?","stones":[["SPOT","John said Jesus laid down His life for us, so we should love not in word only, but in deed and in truth. Beau felt loving."],["NAME","That show-it-not-just-say-it feeling is called loving. Your mind might think, “Love is a verb.” Your body might feel helping hands, staying feet."],["FIND","When did you show love by doing something instead of just saying it?"],["FORK","Beau could say “I love you” and run outside, or stay and read. He stayed."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and think of one deed of love you can do tomorrow. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, make my love real.”"]],"remember":"Let us not love in word only, but in deed and in truth!","ask":["“How do we know what love is?”","“What does it mean to love in deed?”","“How many books did Beau read to his sister?”"],"share":["Share a time someone loved you in deed, and it meant more than words.","Where have you shut up your “bowels of compassion” to a brother in need?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who loved in deed and in truth."]},"b23-w4-n4":{"story":["John wrote, “Beloved, let us love one another, for love is of Heavenly Father.” Then he wrote a short, big sentence: “Heavenly Father is love.”","He does not only love. Love is who He is, the way the sun is full of light.","Heavenly Father showed His love by sending His Son, Jesus, so we could live. He loved us first, before we ever loved Him.","John wrote, “Perfect love casts out fear.” Love pushes fear right out the door. When you know how loved you are, fear has no place to stay.","If you feel scared tonight, snuggle up tight and remember His love. Say, “Heavenly Father loves me,” and let that love fill you up."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” John said Heavenly Father is love, and perfect love casts out fear. When Willow is loved, there is no room for scared. In Willow’s world today: Willow is scared of the thunderstorm. Mom crawls in and wraps her up tight. “Feel how much I love you?” Willow nods. “Is there room for scared in here?” There isn’t. The thunder keeps going, but Willow falls asleep. Just like John taught, Willow learned that Heavenly Father is love, and His love throws fear out the door.","feel":"Shake with fear, brrr, scared! Now feel big arms wrap you up tight in love, squeeze. Check inside: is there room for scared? No! Throw the fear out the door, toss! Can you show me your fear toss?","stones":[["SPOT","John said Heavenly Father is love, He loved us first, and perfect love casts out fear. Willow felt unafraid."],["NAME","That no-room-for-scared feeling is called unafraid. Your mind might think, “Too loved to fear.” Your body might feel wrapped-up arms, a slowing heart."],["FIND","When did being loved make a scary thing less scary?"],["FORK","Willow could stay shaking under the blanket, or let love fill up the space. She let love in."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let His love fill every corner. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, Your love casts out my fear.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father is love, and perfect love casts out fear!","ask":["“What is Heavenly Father, in one word?”","“What does perfect love do to fear?”","“Was there room for scared when Mom wrapped Willow up?”"],"share":["Share a time perfect love cast out a fear of yours.","Where is fear still sitting in your heart, and how could His love fill it?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor whose love for Heavenly Father left no room for fear."]},"b23-w4-n5":{"story":["John wrote a very short letter to a lady and her children. He was so happy that he rejoiced greatly. He had found her children walking in truth.","They were living the true way Heavenly Father teaches, step by step, every day. That is what made John so glad.","John reminded them of an old commandment, to love one another. He said love is walking in His commandments.","Later John wrote a letter to his friend Gaius. “I have no greater joy than to hear that my children walk in truth.” Nothing made John happier than that.","When you choose the true way, the people who love you feel that joy. So does Heavenly Father. Tonight, think of one true thing you chose today."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer says, “Wow, look at that!” Old John said there is no greater joy than hearing that children walk in truth. Wren could be someone’s greatest joy. In Wren’s world today: Wren’s grandma lives far away, and Mom calls to tell her Wren chose to tell the truth about a broken vase even when it was hard. Wren hears Grandma’s voice through the phone, cracking a little. “No greater joy,” Grandma says. Just like John taught, Wren learned that walking in truth is the greatest joy she can give those who love her.","feel":"Walk in place, step, step, on a true path. Now a phone rings, and someone far away hears you walked true. See their face light up! Keep walking, true, true. Can you show me your walk-in-truth?","stones":[["SPOT","John said he had no greater joy than to hear that his children walked in truth. Wren felt walking-true."],["NAME","That living-the-true-way feeling is called walking-true. Your mind might think, “Someone’s glad.” Your body might feel steady steps, a lifted face."],["FIND","When did you do the right thing and make someone who loves you very happy?"],["FORK","We can walk any old way, or walk in truth and give joy to those who love us. Walk in truth."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and picture a grandparent’s joy at your true walk. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help my walk be true.”"]],"remember":"I have no greater joy than to hear that my children walk in truth!","ask":["“What made John rejoice greatly?”","“What is John’s greatest joy?”","“What did Grandma say on the phone?”"],"share":["Share the joy of hearing that your children walk in truth.","Who is rejoicing over your walk right now, and who is watching for it?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor whose greatest joy was their children walking in truth."]},"b23-w4-n6":{"story":["John wrote a short letter to his friend Gaius. It was mostly a thank-you note. “You do faithfully whatever you do,” John wrote.","Some believers went from town to town to teach about Jesus, like missionaries. They took nothing from people who did not believe. So they needed friends like Gaius.","Gaius welcomed them, even the ones he did not know. John asked him to help them on their way again.","Not everyone goes out to teach. But John said helping them makes us “fellowhelpers to the truth.” Gaius was part of their work.","You can be a helper like Gaius. Tomorrow, set a place, pour a drink, or say “Welcome!” to someone who serves Jesus."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see!” Gaius did not go on missions, but fed and helped the ones who did, a fellow helper to the truth. You can join from your kitchen. In Scout’s world today: The missionaries come to dinner, and Scout sets the table, pours milk, and packs them cookies for the road. At the door one says, “Thanks, fellow helper.” Scout looks at Dad. “That’s what John called Gaius.” Just like Gaius, Scout learned that helping the people who share the gospel makes you a fellow helper to the truth.","feel":"Open your door to tired travelers, welcome! Set a pretend table, pour milk, pack cookies for the road. Wave them off on their journey. Now point to yourself: fellow helper! Can you show me your fellow-helper wave?","stones":[["SPOT","John thanked Gaius for faithfully helping missionaries, a fellow helper to the truth. Scout felt faithful."],["NAME","That I’ll-keep-doing-my-part feeling is called faithful. Your mind might think, “I’m on the team.” Your body might feel helpful hands, a steady heart."],["FIND","When did you help someone who was doing something good, and feel like part of it?"],["FORK","Scout could sit while the missionaries ate, or help like Gaius. He helped."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and think of one helper you can help tomorrow. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me follow the good.”"]],"remember":"Follow that which is good, and be a fellow helper to the truth!","ask":["“Who was Gaius?”","“What did Gaius do for the missionaries?”","“What did the missionary call Scout at the door?”"],"share":["Share a time you were a fellow helper to someone else’s mission.","Who are the Gaiuses in your ward, and how could you be one?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who faithfully helped missionaries or traveling saints."]},"b23-w4-n7":{"story":["Jude was the brother of James. He wrote a short letter to people who belong to Jesus.","Jude said they were preserved in Jesus Christ. Preserved means kept safe, like jam sealed tight in a jar.","Jude told his friends how to stay strong. Build up your faith like stacking bricks, and pray with the Holy Ghost’s help.","“Keep yourselves in the love of Heavenly Father,” Jude wrote. Stay inside His love, like staying in a warm house. Then Jude praised Him, who “is able to keep you from falling.”","Heavenly Father can hold you up, like a grown-up holding you when you wobble. Tonight, snuggle into His love and say, “He’s got me.”"],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer says, “Wow, look at that!” Jude said Heavenly Father is able to keep us from falling and bring us home with joy. Wren feels safe. In Wren’s world today: Wren is learning to skate and keeps falling. Grandpa skates behind her, hands under her arms. “I’ve got you.” She wobbles, but he keeps her up, all the way around. “That’s Jude’s prayer. Able to keep you from falling.” Just like Jude prayed, Wren learned that Heavenly Father is able to keep her from falling and bring her home with joy.","feel":"Wobble on pretend skates, whoa, about to fall! Now feel strong hands under your arms, holding you up. Glide, glide, all the way around! Stand tall with exceeding joy, arms up! Can you show me your kept-from-falling glide?","stones":[["SPOT","Jude said keep yourselves in His love, for He is able to keep you from falling. Wren felt kept."],["NAME","That He-won’t-let-me-fall feeling is called kept. Your mind might think, “His hands hold me.” Your body might feel steadied shoulders, gliding feet."],["FIND","When did someone hold you up so you would not fall?"],["FORK","We can wander out of His love, or keep ourselves inside it. Stay inside His love."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel His hands holding you up. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, keep me, now and forever. Amen.”"]],"remember":"Unto Him that is able to keep you from falling, be glory, now and forever!","ask":["“What does preserved mean?”","“What is Heavenly Father able to keep us from?”","“Who held Wren up on the ice?”"],"share":["Share a time the Lord kept you from falling.","How do you “keep yourself in the love of Heavenly Father” day to day?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who was kept from falling and finished with exceeding joy."]},"b22-w1-n1":{"story":["Paul wrote a letter to Jesus’s friends in a city called Ephesus. He had lived there and taught them for three years.","Paul wrote, “Heavenly Father chose us before the world was made.” Before there was ground or sky or stars, He already wanted you.","Paul said Heavenly Father planned for us to be adopted as His children. Adopted means chosen on purpose to join a family, and to stay.","Why did Paul tell them this? Many of them had once felt far away from Heavenly Father. Paul said it made Heavenly Father glad to choose them.","You are not an accident. Heavenly Father wanted you first, before anything was made. Tonight, give yourself a hug and say, “I was chosen.”"],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” Paul said Heavenly Father chose us before the world was made, because it made Him happy. Chosen that early is the warmest thing. In Willow’s world today: Willow asks Mom, “Did you want me before I was born?” Mom pulls out a tiny knitted hat. “I made this before I knew if you were a boy or girl. I wanted you first.” Willow holds the hat. “Heavenly Father wanted me first, too.” Just like Paul wrote, Willow learned that she was chosen before the world began.","feel":"Curl up small, before the world was made. Now feel a finger point at you, “That one!” Pop up and say, “Me?” Then hug yourself tight, chosen! Can you show me your chosen pop-up?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul said Heavenly Father chose us before the foundation of the world to be His children. Willow felt chosen."],["NAME","That He-wanted-me-first feeling is called chosen. Your mind might think, “He picked me on purpose.” Your body might feel a warm chest and a shy smile."],["FIND","When did you find out someone wanted you or planned for you before you arrived?"],["FORK","We can feel like an accident, or believe we were chosen on purpose. Believe you were chosen."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and remember that you were chosen before the stars. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for wanting me.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father chose you before the foundation of the world!","ask":["“When did Heavenly Father choose us?”","“What does adopted mean?”","“What did Mom show Willow?”"],"share":["Share how it feels to know you were chosen before the foundation of the world.","How does being chosen on purpose change the way you see your hard days?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who knew they were a chosen child of Heavenly Father."]},"b22-w1-n2":{"story":["Paul wrote to his friends in Ephesus about the best gift of all. Heavenly Father wants to bring us safely home to Him.","We are saved by grace, Paul said, His kind help we can never earn. We trust Jesus, and the gift is ours.","“Not of works,” Paul said, “lest any man should boast.” If we could earn it, we might brag. Nobody brags about a gift.","Then Paul said, “We are His workmanship.” He made us with loving care, like a toy boat carved by hand. He made us to do good things.","A boat is made to float, and you are made to do good. We do good to say thank you. Tonight, thank Heavenly Father for His gift."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see!” Scout wondered if he had to earn Heavenly Father’s love. Paul said no, it is a gift. We do good because we were made for it. In Scout’s world today: Grandpa carves Scout a wooden boat. “Do I have to pay?” Scout asks. “It’s a gift.” Scout floats it. Grandpa calls, “That’s what it was made for!” Scout thinks, “I’m made to do good, too.” Just like Paul wrote, Scout learned that we are saved by grace as a gift, and made for good works.","feel":"Try to earn it, work, work, hammer, hammer, so tired! Now stop and hold out your hands for a gift, ahh. Then float like a little boat, made for it. Can you show me your boat float?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul said we are saved by grace, a gift, not works, and we are His workmanship made for good works. Scout felt grateful."],["NAME","That I-didn’t-earn-it feeling is called grateful. Your mind might think, “It’s a gift.” Your body might feel open hands and a light chest."],["FIND","When did you receive something you could never have earned?"],["FORK","We can try to earn Heavenly Father’s love, or receive His gift and do good out of thanks. Receive the gift."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and rest in the gift of grace. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that I am Your workmanship.”"]],"remember":"By grace are you saved through faith, it is the gift of Heavenly Father!","ask":["“Is being saved a gift or a prize we earn?”","“What does workmanship mean?”","“What did Grandpa carve for Scout?”"],"share":["Share how understanding grace changed the way you do good works.","Where do you still try to earn what has already been given?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor whose good works flowed from gratitude, not fear."]},"b22-w1-n3":{"story":["Most of Paul’s friends in Ephesus were not Jewish. For a long time, Jewish people and everyone else had stayed apart. It was like a wall stood between them.","Paul said, “You who were once far off are made near.” Jesus gave His life for them, so now they were close.","Paul said Jesus “has broken down the middle wall” between us. It was not a wall of stones. Jesus took away what kept people apart.","Then Paul told them, “You are no more strangers.” They were part of Heavenly Father’s own family now. They all belonged together.","Does someone seem different from you? Jesus wants them in His family too. Tonight, think of one new friend you can welcome with a smile."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer says, “Wow, look at that!” Paul said Jesus knocked down the wall in the middle, so nobody is a stranger. Wren wonders at a room that big. In Wren’s world today: Wren’s family hosts a family who speaks no English. Everyone sits on opposite sides of the room. Then Wren builds a block wall and knocks it over, laughing. The other kids laugh too. Soon everyone is on the floor together. Just like Paul taught, Wren learned that Jesus broke down the wall so no one is a stranger.","feel":"Build a tall wall with your hands. Now knock it down, crash! Walk across to the other side and shake hands. Then sit at one big table. Can you show me your wall crash?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul said Jesus broke down the middle wall, so we are no more strangers, but His household. Wren felt welcome."],["NAME","That I’m-inside-now feeling is called welcome. Your mind might think, “I’m in the house.” Your body might feel loose shoulders, a pulled-up chair."],["FIND","When did someone knock down a wall so you felt like you belonged?"],["FORK","We can keep walls between us and people who are different, or let Jesus knock them down. Knock them down."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel yourself inside His house. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that I am no stranger.”"]],"remember":"Jesus broke down the wall, you are no more a stranger, but of His household!","ask":["“What did Jesus break down?”","“What are we instead of strangers?”","“What did Wren build and knock over?”"],"share":["Share a time you went from stranger to family among the saints.","What middle wall still stands in your ward, and how could you take it down?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who welcomed strangers into the household of faith."]},"b22-w1-n4":{"story":["Paul was a prisoner, but he still prayed for his friends in Ephesus. He knelt down and asked Heavenly Father to make them strong inside.","He prayed that Jesus would live in their hearts. He prayed they would be “rooted and grounded in love.”","Rooted is like a big tree with roots deep in the ground. When the wind blows hard, it stays standing. Paul wanted love to hold them steady like that.","Then Paul tried to tell how big Jesus’s love is. It is wide and long, deep and high. It is bigger than anyone can ever understand.","When you feel shaky inside, remember the strong tree. Tonight, stretch your arms out wide and say, “Jesus’s love holds me tight.”"],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” Paul prayed we would be rooted in love wider, longer, deeper, and higher than anything. Willow stretches her arms wide, and it still isn’t enough. In Willow’s world today: A windstorm bends the trees, but Grandpa’s old oak doesn’t move. “Its roots go way down,” Grandpa says. That night a scary dream comes, and Willow remembers the oak. “My roots go way down,” she whispers, and sleeps. Just like Paul prayed, Willow learned that being rooted and grounded in love keeps you steady in any storm.","feel":"Plant your feet like roots going down, down, deep. Now stretch your arms wide for the breadth, step long for the length, bend low for the depth, reach high for the height! Can you show me your wide-long-deep-high?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul prayed we would be rooted and grounded in love, wide, long, deep, and high. Willow felt rooted."],["NAME","That nothing-tips-me-over feeling is called rooted. Your mind might think, “My roots go deep.” Your body might feel planted feet, a steady trunk."],["FIND","When did love hold you steady during a scary or windy time?"],["FORK","We can stand on shaky ground, or get rooted and grounded in love. Get rooted."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel your roots going deep into Jesus’s love. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, root me in Your love.”"]],"remember":"Rooted and grounded in love, wider, longer, deeper, higher than you can imagine!","ask":["“What does it mean to be rooted?”","“How wide, long, deep, and high is Christ’s love?”","“What did Willow remember when the scary dream came?”"],"share":["Share a storm where being rooted in Christ’s love held you.","Which measure of His love do you most need to feel right now?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who was like a deep-rooted oak for your family."]},"b22-w1-n5":{"story":["Paul wrote to his friends in Ephesus about their words. He said, “Let no rotten words come out of your mouth.” He meant mean, hurtful words.","Instead, Paul said, use good words that build people up. Kind words help people grow strong, like blocks stacked into a tower.","Paul said to put away anger, yelling, and mean talk. He said unkind words make the Holy Spirit sad.","Then Paul said, “Be kind one to another, tenderhearted, forgiving one another.” Tenderhearted means having a soft, gentle heart, like holding a baby bird.","Why can we forgive? Heavenly Father forgave us first, because of Jesus. Tonight, think of someone who upset you. Give them a soft heart and forgive them."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big faith, brave heart!” Paul said put away anger and yelling, and be kind and tenderhearted instead. A soft heart takes more courage than a loud voice. In Beau’s world today: Beau’s little brother breaks Beau’s model rocket. Beau feels the yell rising. He stops. He pictures a baby bird in his hands, soft. “It’s okay,” he says quietly. “Let’s fix it together.” His brother bursts into relieved tears, and they glue it back. Just like Paul taught, Beau learned that being tenderhearted and forgiving is braver than being angry.","feel":"Make a loud, angry face and shake, grrr! Now push it away with both hands, put it away! Cup your hands soft like you are holding a baby bird. Gently, gently. Can you show me your tender hands?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul said put away anger and clamour, and be kind, tenderhearted, and forgiving. Beau felt tenderhearted."],["NAME","That my-heart-is-soft feeling is called tenderhearted. Your mind might think, “Gently.” Your body might feel cupped hands and a quiet voice."],["FIND","When did you choose a soft heart instead of a loud yell?"],["FORK","Beau could yell at his brother, or be tenderhearted and forgive. He was tender."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your heart go soft like a baby bird. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for forgiving me first.”"]],"remember":"Be kind, tenderhearted, forgiving, as Heavenly Father forgave you!","ask":["“What kind of words should not come out of our mouths?”","“What does tenderhearted mean?”","“What did Beau picture when he felt like yelling?”"],"share":["Share a time tenderheartedness changed an argument in your home.","What bitterness or clamour do you need to “put away” this week?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who was tenderhearted and quick to forgive."]},"b22-w1-n6":{"story":["Paul wrote one part of his letter just for children. He said, “Children, obey your parents in the Lord, for this is right.”","Then he said, “Honor your father and mother.” To honor someone is to treat them as very special. You listen, speak kindly, and help gladly.","Paul said this rule from Heavenly Father comes with a promise. Things will “be well with you,” and you will “live long on the earth.”","Paul had words for dads too. He said not to make their children angry. Instead, they should raise them with love and teach them about the Lord.","Tomorrow, when Mom or Dad asks you to help, say “Yes” right away. That is how you honor them. Tonight, practice a happy “Yes!”"],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see!” Paul wrote part of the letter just for children, and Scout found it. Honor your parents, and it comes with a promise. Scout likes rules with promises. In Scout’s world today: Mom asks Scout to pick up his trucks, and he wants to say, “In a minute.” Instead he says, “Yes, Mom,” and does it right away. Mom stops. “That honored me, Scout. Thank you.” Scout feels taller. Just like Paul taught, Scout learned that obeying and honoring his parents is right and comes with a promise.","feel":"Point to yourself, children! Now listen with your hand at your ear. Say, “Yes, Mom. Yes, Dad,” and nod. Put a pretend crown on your parents’ heads, honored! Can you show me your honor crown?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul said children, obey and honor your parents, and it will be well with you. Scout felt honoring."],["NAME","That I-treat-you-as-precious feeling is called honoring. Your mind might think, “They matter.” Your body might feel a quick “yes” and helpful hands."],["FIND","When did you obey right away, and feel good about it afterward?"],["FORK","Scout could say, “In a minute,” or honor his mom with a quick yes. He said yes."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and think of one way to honor your parents. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for my mom and dad.”"]],"remember":"Honor your father and mother, the first commandment with a promise!","ask":["“What does it mean to honor?”","“What promise comes with honoring parents?”","“What did Scout say when Mom asked him to pick up his trucks?”"],"share":["Share how honoring your own parents has blessed your life.","How are you doing at “nurture and admonition” without provoking?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who honored their parents in a way that shaped your family."]},"b22-w1-n7":{"story":["Paul wrote this letter while he was in chains. He knew how soldiers dressed to stay safe. So he used armor to teach.","“Put on the whole armor of Heavenly Father,” Paul said. Wear a belt of truth. Wear a breastplate of doing right over your heart.","Wear shoes ready to share the good news of peace. Wear a helmet of salvation, because Jesus saves us.","“Above all,” Paul said, take “the shield of faith.” Faith in Jesus is a shield. It puts out every fiery arrow, every trick to do wrong. Then take the sword, which is Heavenly Father’s word, the scriptures.","Put your armor on each morning. Tonight, tap your head, your heart, and your feet. Say, “I am ready to stand strong.”"],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big faith, brave heart!” Paul named every piece of armor: belt, breastplate, shoes, shield, helmet, sword. Beau puts on every piece every morning. In Beau’s world today: Before school, Beau stands in front of the mirror. “Belt of truth,” he says, tugging his belt. “Breastplate,” tapping his chest. “Shoes of peace. Shield of faith. Helmet of salvation. Sword, the scriptures.” Then he grabs his backpack. “Ready.” Just like Paul taught, Beau learned that the whole armor of Heavenly Father lets him stand strong every day.","feel":"Buckle your belt of truth, click! Strap on your breastplate, pat! Stomp your shoes of peace! Hold up your shield of faith! Helmet on! Lift your sword, the scriptures! Can you show me your full armor stand?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul said put on the whole armor of Heavenly Father: truth, right, peace, faith, salvation, His word. Beau felt protected."],["NAME","That I’m-covered feeling is called protected. Your mind might think, “Nothing gets through.” Your body might feel a strong stance, a lifted shield."],["FIND","When did truth, faith, or the scriptures protect you from something scary?"],["FORK","We can go out with no armor, or put on every piece each morning. Put it all on."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel His armor all around you as you rest. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for Your armor.”"]],"remember":"Put on the whole armor of Heavenly Father, and you can stand!","ask":["“What does the shield of faith do?”","“What is the sword of the Spirit?”","“Where does Beau put on his armor every morning?”"],"share":["Share which piece of the armor has protected you most.","Which piece are you leaving off, and what fiery dart is getting through?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who stood strong in the armor of Heavenly Father."]},"b22-w2-n1":{"story":["Paul loved his friends in the city of Philippi. That is where Lydia opened her heart to Jesus, and a jailer was baptized.","Now Paul was in chains in prison himself. Still, he wrote them a happy letter. “I thank my Heavenly Father every time I remember you,” he said.","He prayed for them with joy. And Paul was confident, very sure, of one happy thing.","Paul said Heavenly Father “has begun a good work in you.” He “will perform it,” and keep working until it is done. He will not stop until Jesus comes again.","Maybe you are still learning to be kind or brave. Heavenly Father is not done with you. Tonight, say, “He will finish His good work in me.”"],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer says, “Wow, look at that!” Paul said Heavenly Father started a good work in us and will finish it. Wren wonders what the finished Wren will look like. In Wren’s world today: Wren’s grandma is knitting her a sweater, and it has only one sleeve. “Will you really finish it?” Wren asks. Grandma laughs. “I never start a sweater I don’t finish.” Wren thinks of Paul. “Heavenly Father is knitting me, too, and He finishes.” Just like Paul promised, Wren learned that Heavenly Father finishes the good work He begins in us.","feel":"Start knitting a pretend sweater, click, click. One sleeve, click. Keep going, don’t quit! Second sleeve, click, click. Hold up the finished sweater, ta-da! Can you show me your finished sweater?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul was confident that He who began a good work in us will perform it until the day of Jesus Christ. Wren felt confident."],["NAME","That He-won’t-quit-on-me feeling is called confident. Your mind might think, “He’ll finish.” Your body might feel a settled chest and a sure nod."],["FIND","When did someone keep working on something for you until it was done?"],["FORK","We can worry that we will never be good enough, or trust that He finishes what He starts. Trust Him."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and picture His hands still working gently on you. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, finish Your good work in me.”"]],"remember":"He who began a good work in you will finish it!","ask":["“Where was Paul when he wrote to Philippi?”","“What was Paul confident about?”","“What was Grandma knitting for Wren?”"],"share":["Share a good work the Lord began in you years ago that He is still performing.","Where do you doubt He will finish, and what does Paul say to that?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor whose faith the Lord carried all the way to the end."]},"b22-w2-n2":{"story":["Paul wanted his friends in Philippi to put others first. He told them to think like Jesus.","Jesus was great and glorious in heaven. Still, He came to earth as a servant, someone who helps others.","Jesus made Himself low and humble. He obeyed Heavenly Father all the way, even to dying on the cross.","So Heavenly Father lifted Jesus up high. He gave Him a name above every name. One day every knee will bow at the name of Jesus. Everyone will say, “Jesus Christ is Lord.”","The One who served the most is now the highest. You can serve like Jesus too. Tomorrow, find one job nobody wants, and do it with a smile."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big faith, brave heart!” Jesus was the King and chose to be a servant, all the way to the cross. Going low on purpose is the bravest thing a king ever did. In Beau’s world today: Beau is captain of the team, and the water bottles need filling. The other boys say, “Make the little kids do it.” Beau picks up the bottles himself. “Captains serve.” He fills every one. The little kids notice. Just like Jesus, Beau learned that the greatest leader is the one who goes low to serve.","feel":"Stand tall like a king with a crown. Take off the crown and kneel low like a servant, washing feet. Bow your head. Then feel Heavenly Father lift you up high! Can you show me your low-then-lifted?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus, the King, took the form of a servant and humbled Himself, so Heavenly Father exalted Him. Beau felt humble."],["NAME","That I’ll-go-low-and-serve feeling is called humble. Your mind might think, “Captains serve.” Your body might feel bent knees and willing hands."],["FIND","When did you do the lowest job on purpose, and feel good about it?"],["FORK","Beau could make the little kids fill the bottles, or serve like Jesus. He served."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and bow your head, letting Jesus’s mind settle in you. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me think like Him.”"]],"remember":"Jesus took the form of a servant, and at His name every knee shall bow!","ask":["“What form did Jesus take upon Himself?”","“What will every knee do at the name of Jesus?”","“What did Beau fill for the team?”"],"share":["Share a time choosing the servant’s place changed you.","Where do you need the mind of Christ instead of the world’s this week?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who led by serving."]},"b22-w2-n3":{"story":["Paul gave his friends in Philippi a hard rule. He said, “Do all things without murmurings and disputings.”","Murmuring is grumbling, like saying, “Ugh, do I have to?” Paul said to do everything with no grumbling and no arguing.","Why? Many people around them were crooked, like a bent stick. They did not follow Heavenly Father’s ways.","Paul said people who do not grumble “shine as lights in the world.” In a dark room, one little lamp is easy to see. People notice a cheerful, helpful heart.","You do not have to be loud to shine. Tonight, think of one thing you might grumble about tomorrow. Plan to say something cheerful instead."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see!” Paul said people who don’t grumble or argue shine like lights in a dark world. Scout looked for the lights at school and found them. In Scout’s world today: The class gets indoor recess because of rain, and everyone groans. Scout decides not to. He pulls out a puzzle. “Who wants to help?” Three kids come over. The teacher says, “Scout, you just turned the lights on in here.” Just like Paul taught, Scout learned that doing things without grumbling makes you shine like a light.","feel":"Grumble, grumble, mumble, with a frowny face. Now zip your lips! Flip on a switch, click, and shine with a bright face. Hold up a lantern for others to see! Can you show me your lantern shine?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul said do all things without murmuring, and you will shine as lights in the world. Scout felt shining."],["NAME","That I’m-the-bright-spot feeling is called shining. Your mind might think, “Just light.” Your body might feel a glowing face and lifted arms."],["FIND","When did you choose not to complain, and notice others felt brighter too?"],["FORK","Scout could groan with everyone, or shine as a light. He shone."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel your light glow steady and calm. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, let me hold up Your light.”"]],"remember":"Do all things without murmuring, and shine as lights in the world!","ask":["“What is murmuring?”","“What do people who don’t grumble shine like?”","“What did Scout do during indoor recess?”"],"share":["Share a time someone’s refusal to complain shone a light for you.","What are you murmuring about, and what would it look like to shine instead?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who shone without complaining through hard times."]},"b22-w2-n4":{"story":["Paul wrote to his friends in Philippi, “Rejoice in the Lord always.” Then he said, “Again I say, rejoice.” He said “rejoice,” be glad, two times, so they would not miss it.","Then he reminded them that the Lord is near.","Next Paul said, “Be careful for nothing.” Long ago, careful meant full of worry. So Paul was saying, do not worry about anything.","What do we do with a worry? Paul said to tell Heavenly Father in prayer, with a thank-you too. Then His peace, too big to explain, will guard your heart and mind.","Is there a worry in your heart tonight? Fold your arms and give it to Heavenly Father. Thank Him for one good thing, and feel His peace."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” Paul said, “Be careful for nothing,” don’t be full of worry, but tell Heavenly Father everything. Hand your worry to His big arms. In Willow’s world today: Willow lies awake worrying about the spelling bee. Mom comes in. “Paul said give the worry to Heavenly Father, with a thank-you.” Willow prays: “Thank You for my brain. Please help me.” The worry floats away, and she sleeps. Just like Paul taught, Willow learned to hand every worry to Heavenly Father for His peace.","feel":"Scrunch up tight with worry, oooh. Now take the worry in your hands and hand it up to Heavenly Father. Say a thank-you. Feel peace stand guard at your heart. Can you show me your worry hand-off?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul said worry about nothing, pray about everything with thanks, and peace will guard your heart. Willow felt peaceful."],["NAME","That the-worry-is-gone feeling is called peaceful. Your mind might think, “He has it now.” Your body might feel an unscrunched tummy and soft hands."],["FIND","When did you tell Heavenly Father a worry, and feel it float away?"],["FORK","Willow could lie awake full of worry, or give it to Heavenly Father with thanks. She gave it."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and hand tonight’s worry up. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You. Keep my heart in peace.”"]],"remember":"Be careful for nothing, pray about everything, and His peace will keep your heart!","ask":["“How many times did Paul say rejoice?”","“What should we do with worry instead of carrying it?”","“What did Willow pray before the spelling bee?”"],"share":["Share a time the peace that passes understanding guarded your heart.","What are you “careful” about that you have not yet told Him with thanks?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who traded worry for prayer."]},"b22-w2-n5":{"story":["Paul had just told his friends in Philippi about Heavenly Father’s peace. Then he told them how to keep their minds peaceful.","He said to think on things that are true and honest. Think on things that are fair, pure, and clean.","Think on things that are lovely, and things people say good things about. “If there be any virtue,” any goodness, “think on these things,” Paul said.","Your mind is a little like a garden. Good thoughts are like good seeds, and they grow into a happy heart. You get to choose what you plant.","If a scary or sad picture keeps coming back, plant a new seed. Tonight, name three lovely things, like a kitten, a hug, or pancakes."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer says, “Wow, look at that!” Paul made a list of what to think about: true, honest, fair, pure, lovely, good. Wren decided to wonder about those. In Wren’s world today: A kid at school tells Wren a scary story, and now it keeps playing in her head. Mom says, “Let’s plant a different seed.” They name lovely things: the kitten, Grandpa’s laugh, the lake, pancakes. The scary story fades. Wren falls asleep thinking about pancakes. Just like Paul taught, Wren learned that she can choose to think on true and lovely things.","feel":"Open the garden gate of your mind, creak. Plant a seed: true! Plant another: lovely! Another: pure! Watch flowers grow up, up, up. Now shut the gate on the weeds, slam! Can you show me your garden gate?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul said think on whatever is true, honest, just, pure, lovely, and of good report. Wren felt thoughtful."],["NAME","That I-choose-my-thoughts feeling is called thoughtful. Your mind might think, “Plant a good seed.” Your body might feel a calm forehead, happy eyes."],["FIND","When did thinking about something lovely push a scary thought out?"],["FORK","Wren could let the scary story keep playing, or think on lovely things instead. She chose lovely things."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and name three lovely things in your mind. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, fill my mind with good things.”"]],"remember":"Whatever is true, honest, just, pure, and lovely, think on these things!","ask":["“What is your mind like, according to the story?”","“Name three things from Paul’s list to think about.”","“What did Wren fall asleep thinking about?”"],"share":["Share how choosing your thoughts has changed your days.","What “weed” keeps getting planted in your mind, and what could replace it?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who kept their mind on good and lovely things."]},"b22-w2-n6":{"story":["Paul was in prison when he wrote to his friends in Philippi. They had sent him gifts to help him. Paul thanked them and said, “I have learned to be content.”","Content means being happy with what you have, without grumbling for more.","Paul had times with plenty of food. He had times when he was hungry. In all of them, he learned to be content.","How could he do it? Paul said, “I can do all things through Christ,” who makes me strong. Paul was not strong all by himself. Jesus gave him strength.","When something hard comes, like a shot, lean on Jesus’s strength. Tonight, whisper, “I can do hard things with Jesus.”"],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big faith, brave heart!” This is Beau’s favorite verse: “I can do all things through Christ which strengtheneth me.” He says it before every hard thing. In Beau’s world today: Beau has to get a shot at the doctor. He is shaking. The nurse says, “Ready?” Beau closes his eyes and whispers, “I can do all things through Christ who strengthens me.” Pinch, done. “That wasn’t my strong,” he tells Mom. “That was His.” Just like Paul, Beau learned that he can do hard things because Christ makes him strong.","feel":"Go weak and wobbly, noodle arms. Now feel Jesus pour strength into you like water into a cup, fill, fill. Flex, strong! Say, “I can do all things through Christ!” Can you show me your Christ-strength flex?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul was content with plenty or nothing, because he could do all things through Christ. Beau felt strengthened."],["NAME","That His-strength-in-me feeling is called strengthened. Your mind might think, “Not my strong, His.” Your body might feel filled arms, a steady heart."],["FIND","When did you do something hard and know the strength came from Jesus?"],["FORK","Beau could shake and cry at the shot, or borrow Christ’s strength. He borrowed it."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let Christ’s strength fill you up. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for His strength.”"]],"remember":"I can do all things through Christ which strengtheneth me!","ask":["“What does content mean?”","“Who makes us strong to do all things?”","“What did Beau whisper before his shot?”"],"share":["Share a time you did something only possible through Christ’s strength.","Where are you trying to be strong on your own instead of through Him?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who did “all things” through Christ."]},"b22-w2-n7":{"story":["Paul wrote to Jesus’s followers in a town called Colosse. He had never even seen their faces. Still, he loved them and wanted to help.","He told them what to put on their hearts, not their bodies. “Put on kindness,” he said, and gentleness, and patience that lasts and lasts.","He said to forgive one another, “even as Christ forgave you.” Jesus forgives us, so we forgive each other too.","Then Paul said, “Above all these things put on charity.” Charity is the pure love of Christ. Like a belt, it holds all the other pieces together.","Paul also said to do each job with your whole heart, for Jesus. Tonight, pick a chore to do that way tomorrow."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see!” Paul said put on kindness like clothes, and do every job heartily, as to the Lord. Scout looked at his chores differently after that. In Scout’s world today: Scout’s chore is scrubbing the bathroom, and he hates it. Then he remembers, “heartily, as to the Lord.” He pretends Jesus is coming over. He scrubs until it sparkles. Dad whistles. “Who did this?” “I did, for Jesus.” Just like Paul taught, Scout learned to put on kindness and do every job with his whole heart, as to the Lord.","feel":"Get dressed: pull on a shirt of kindness, step into pants of patience, button up forgiveness, buckle the belt of charity! Now scrub a pretend floor heartily, scrub, scrub! Can you show me your hearty scrub?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul said put on kindness, patience, forgiveness, and charity, and do all things heartily as to the Lord. Scout felt hearty."],["NAME","That whole-heart feeling is called hearty. Your mind might think, “For the Lord.” Your body might feel busy hands and a glad heart."],["FIND","When did doing a job for Jesus make it feel different?"],["FORK","Scout could scrub grumpily and barely, or heartily as to the Lord. He scrubbed heartily."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and lay out tomorrow’s clothes: kindness, patience, charity. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me wear them.”"]],"remember":"Put on kindness and charity, and do everything heartily, as to the Lord!","ask":["“What did Paul say to put on?”","“What holds all the pieces together, like a belt?”","“How did Scout scrub the bathroom?”"],"share":["Share a time you did an ordinary task “as to the Lord.”","Which piece of Paul’s clothing do you forget to put on?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who did humble work heartily, as to the Lord."]},"b22-w3-n1":{"story":["Paul taught about Jesus in a city called Thessalonica. Then he had to leave quickly. He missed his new friends, so he wrote them a letter.","“We were gentle among you,” Paul wrote. He was like a nurse who cherishes the babies she cares for. To cherish is to hold someone close, like something precious.","His friends were so dear to him. He shared Heavenly Father’s good news and his whole heart, too.","Then Paul gave a second picture. He had cared for them like a good father. He cheered them on and comforted them. He helped them walk in Heavenly Father’s ways.","You can be gentle like Paul. Tonight, hold someone you love close and whisper, “You are precious to me.”"],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” Paul said he was gentle as a nurse with a baby, and would give his own soul for his friends. That is how a teacher of Jesus feels. In Willow’s world today: Willow helps in the nursery at church. A baby cries and cries. Willow holds him close and hums until he sleeps on her shoulder. The leader whispers, “You cherished him.” Willow whispers, “Like Paul.” Just like Paul, Willow learned that teaching about Jesus means cherishing people gently, like a nurse with a baby.","feel":"Rock a pretend baby in your arms, gently, gently, sway, sway. Hum a tiny song. Now be a dad and cheer, “You can do it!” and give a comforting pat. Can you show me your cherish rock?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul was gentle as a nurse cherishing her children, and cared like a father. The Thessalonians felt cherished."],["NAME","That held-close feeling is called cherished. Your mind might think, “I am dear to them.” Your body might feel a warm shoulder, a safe sleepy weight."],["FIND","When did someone take care of you so gently that you felt precious?"],["FORK","Paul could teach with a hard voice, or cherish his friends like a nurse. He cherished them."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel yourself rocked gently in loving arms. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for cherishing me.”"]],"remember":"Paul was gentle as a nurse cherishes her children!","ask":["“What did Paul compare himself to?”","“What does cherish mean?”","“What did Willow do for the crying baby?”"],"share":["Share a teacher or leader who cherished you the way Paul describes.","Whom are you called to cherish gently, and how is it going?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who cherished children like a nurse."]},"b22-w3-n2":{"story":["Paul wrote his friends in Thessalonica happy news. They were already good at loving one another. Heavenly Father had taught them how.","Paul said, “Love more and more.” Then he gave them three quiet things to do.","Try hard to live calmly, without a fuss. Take care of your own jobs. And “work with your own hands,” just as Paul had taught them before.","Why those three things? Then people who did not know Jesus yet would see honest, hard workers. That would show them something good. And workers have what they need.","What can your hands do tomorrow? Maybe pick up toys or help set the table. Hold up your hands tonight and say, “These are good helpers.”"],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see!” Paul said live quietly, mind your own business, and work with your own hands. Scout’s two hands had work to do. In Scout’s world today: Scout wants a new kite. Dad says, “Earn it.” Scout pulls weeds for Mrs. Ortiz, carries recycling, and stacks firewood all week. On Saturday he counts his coins: enough. He flies the kite high. “My own hands did that.” Just like Paul taught, Scout learned that quiet, honest work with his own hands is a way of living the gospel.","feel":"Hold up your two hands. Now put them to work: pull weeds, tug, tug! Stack wood, lift, lift! Sew a tent, in and out! Wipe your brow and smile. Can you show me your working hands?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul said be quiet, mind your own business, and work with your own hands, to walk honestly. Scout felt industrious."],["NAME","That I-like-to-work feeling is called industrious. Your mind might think, “My hands can do this.” Your body might feel busy fingers, a satisfied sigh."],["FIND","When did you earn something with your own hard work, and feel proud?"],["FORK","Scout could beg for the kite, or earn it with his own hands. He earned it."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your working hands rest. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for hands that can work.”"]],"remember":"Study to be quiet, and work with your own hands!","ask":["“What were the Thessalonians already good at?”","“What did Paul say to do with your own hands?”","“How did Scout earn his kite?”"],"share":["Share how working with your own hands has blessed your faith.","Where could you “study to be quiet” and mind your own business more?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who worked hard with their hands and walked honestly."]},"b22-w3-n3":{"story":["Paul’s friends wondered when Jesus would come back. Paul said that day would come as a surprise, “as a thief.”","But they did not need to be caught by surprise. “Ye are all the children of light,” he wrote. “You are children of the day.”","Children of the light keep their hearts awake. Paul said, “Let us not sleep, as do others; but let us watch.” He meant, don’t forget Jesus.","Stay ready, like a soldier dressed for his watch. Wear faith and love, like a strong vest over your heart. Wear hope, like a helmet on your head.","Children of the light will be glad when Jesus comes. Tonight, say a prayer and keep your heart awake for Him."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer says, “Wow, look at that!” Paul said we are children of the light, so Jesus coming back is not scary, because we are awake. Wren wants to be awake for it. In Wren’s world today: Grandpa is coming home from a trip, nobody knows what time. Wren’s brother falls asleep. Wren keeps her lamp on and her shoes by the door. At midnight headlights flash. Wren is first in Grandpa’s arms. “You stayed ready!” Just like Paul taught, Wren learned that children of the light stay awake and ready for Jesus.","feel":"Doze off, snore, snore. Now wake up! Eyes wide, turn on your lamp, click! Shoes by the door. Stand ready, bouncing on your toes, a child of the light! Can you show me your ready bounce?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul said we are children of the light, so watch and be awake, ready for Jesus to come. Wren felt awake."],["NAME","That eyes-open-and-ready feeling is called awake. Your mind might think, “I won’t miss it.” Your body might feel bright eyes and bouncing toes."],["FIND","When did you stay awake and ready for something wonderful?"],["FORK","We can doze off in our faith, or stay awake as children of the light. Stay awake."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your body rest while your heart stays bright. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, keep my light on.”"]],"remember":"You are children of the light, so watch and be ready!","ask":["“How will the day of Jesus’s coming arrive?”","“What are we called instead of children of darkness?”","“What did Wren keep by the door?”"],"share":["Share what keeps you “awake” in your faith in a sleepy season.","If He came tonight, what would you want Him to find you doing?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who lived ready for the Lord’s return."]},"b22-w3-n4":{"story":["Paul’s friends in Thessalonica were having hard days. Near the end of his letter, Paul gave them a list. Cheer up the sad ones. Help the weak. Be patient with everyone.","Don’t pay back mean with mean, Paul said. Always choose what is good.","Then Paul wrote three short rules: “Rejoice evermore. Pray without ceasing. In every thing give thanks.”","Be glad always. Keep a little prayer going in your heart all day long. Find something to thank Heavenly Father for, even on a hard day. Paul said this is what Heavenly Father wants for you.","Even when rain spoils a plan, there is something to be thankful for. Tonight, name three things and say, “Thank You, Heavenly Father.”"],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big faith, brave heart!” Paul said in everything give thanks, even the hard things. That takes a brave heart, and it makes a glad one. In Beau’s world today: Beau’s camping trip gets rained out. He stomps. Then he remembers “in everything give thanks.” “Thank You for the tent in the living room. Thank You for hot cocoa. Thank You for Dad’s flashlight stories.” By bedtime, it is the best camping trip ever. Just like Paul taught, Beau learned that giving thanks in everything turns hard days into glad ones.","feel":"Rejoice: throw your arms up! Pray: fold your hands. Give thanks: count on your fingers, thank You, thank You, thank You! Now do all three at once, big smile! Can you show me your rejoice-pray-thanks?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul said rejoice evermore, pray without ceasing, and in everything give thanks. Beau felt thankful."],["NAME","That I-can-find-good feeling is called thankful. Your mind might think, “Thank You for this.” Your body might feel a lifted face, counting fingers."],["FIND","When did a bad day get better because you started saying thank you?"],["FORK","Beau could stomp about the rain all night, or give thanks in everything. He gave thanks."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and whisper three thank-yous before you sleep. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, in everything I give thanks.”"]],"remember":"Rejoice evermore, pray without ceasing, in everything give thanks!","ask":["“What are Paul’s three short commandments?”","“What does “pray without ceasing” mean?”","“What did Beau thank Heavenly Father for on the rainy night?”"],"share":["Share a time giving thanks “in everything” changed your heart.","What is your way of praying without ceasing through an ordinary day?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who gave thanks in everything, even hard things."]},"b22-w3-n5":{"story":["Some of Paul’s friends were very sad. People they loved had died.","So Paul wrote them a gentle letter. He said the people who died were “asleep.” He chose that soft word because after sleep, you wake up.","Paul did not say, “Never be sad.” He said, don’t be sad like people with no hope. Jesus died and rose again. So the ones who sleep in Jesus will rise, too.","One day Jesus will come down from heaven. Those who sleep in Him will rise first. Then everyone will be together, “and so shall we ever be with the Lord.”","Paul said, “Comfort one another with these words.” Tonight, think of someone sad. Tomorrow, sit close and say, “We will be together with Jesus.”"],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” Paul said we can be sad, but not like people without hope, because those who sleep in Jesus will rise. Willow keeps those words ready. In Willow’s world today: Willow’s friend Ava’s grandpa dies, and Ava cries at recess. Willow sits close. “Paul said people who die in Jesus are only asleep. They’ll rise, and we’ll all be together.” Ava wipes her face. “Forever?” “Forever.” They hold hands until the bell. Just like Paul taught, Willow learned to comfort sad friends with the hope of the resurrection.","feel":"Make a sad face, let a tear fall. Now look up with hope. Lie down asleep, then rise up slowly to meet Jesus. Hug a friend and say, “Together forever!” Can you show me your rise-up?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul said do not sorrow like those with no hope, for those who sleep in Jesus will rise. Willow felt hopeful."],["NAME","That sad-but-not-the-end feeling is called hopeful. Your mind might think, “We’ll be together again.” Your body might feel wet eyes and a lifted chin."],["FIND","When did someone comfort you with the hope that you will see a loved one again?"],["FORK","Willow could leave Ava to cry alone, or comfort her with Paul’s words. She comforted her."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and picture everyone you love with Jesus. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for the resurrection.”"]],"remember":"Those who sleep in Jesus will rise, so comfort one another with these words!","ask":["“What gentle word did Paul use for people who had died?”","“What will happen to those who sleep in Jesus when He comes?”","“What did Willow tell Ava at recess?”"],"share":["Share how the hope of the resurrection comforted you in a loss.","Who needs you to “comfort them with these words” right now?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who sleeps in Jesus and whom you expect to see again."]},"b22-w3-n6":{"story":["Paul heard that some friends in Thessalonica were not working at all. Instead, they were busybodies, busy poking into everyone else’s business but their own.","Paul reminded them how he had lived. He worked hard, night and day.","He paid his own way, so no one had to feed him. He did it so they could copy him.","Paul gave a plain rule. “If any would not work, neither should he eat.” He told them to work quietly and eat their own bread.","Then Paul said to everyone, “Be not weary in well doing.” Don’t get tired of doing good. Tonight, pick one job your hands can finish tomorrow."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see!” Paul said if you will not work, you should not eat, and do not be weary in well doing. Kids who do their part are happiest. In Scout’s world today: At the garden, Scout’s cousin lies in the grass. “I’ll just eat the tomatoes when they’re done.” Scout hands him a trowel. “No work, no eat. Come dig.” His cousin digs. At harvest, they eat tomatoes together, proud. Just like Paul taught, Scout learned that everyone does their own part, and no one gets weary of doing good.","feel":"Lie back lazy in the grass, yawn. Now sit up! Grab a pretend trowel and dig, dig, dig. Plant, water, pull weeds. Then eat a juicy tomato you grew, mmm! Can you show me your dig-and-eat?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul said if anyone will not work, neither should he eat, and be not weary in well doing. Scout felt responsible."],["NAME","That I’ll-do-my-part feeling is called responsible. Your mind might think, “That’s my job.” Your body might feel ready hands and a grown-up nod."],["FIND","When did you do your part of a job and feel like you really belonged at the table?"],["FORK","Scout’s cousin could lie in the grass, or pick up the trowel and dig. He dug."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel good about the part you did today. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me not be weary in doing good.”"]],"remember":"Be not weary in well doing, do your part and keep doing good!","ask":["“What did Paul do so no one had to feed him?”","“What is a busybody?”","“What did Scout hand his cousin?”"],"share":["Share a time doing your part, even when tired, blessed your family.","Where are you weary in well doing, and what would renew you?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who never got tired of doing good."]},"b22-w3-n7":{"story":["Paul’s friends were going through hard times. So Paul prayed that Jesus and Heavenly Father would comfort their hearts.","Then Paul made a promise: “The Lord is faithful.” Being faithful is keeping your word every single time, and the Lord always does.","“He shall keep you from evil,” Paul wrote. The Lord watches over His people, like a shepherd keeping his sheep safe.","Paul also asked the Lord to point their hearts to Heavenly Father’s love. He asked Him to point them to waiting patiently for Jesus. Like a compass, the Lord points us the right way.","When you feel lost, the Lord does not change. Tonight, put a hand on your heart. Pray, “Please point my heart to You.”"],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer says, “Wow, look at that!” Paul said the Lord is faithful and points our hearts to Heavenly Father’s love like a compass. Wren has a compass. In Wren’s world today: On a hike, Wren’s family loses the trail. Dad pulls out a compass. “This always points north, so we’re never lost.” Wren watches the needle settle. “The Lord is like that. He always points the same way.” Just like Paul promised, Wren learned that the Lord is faithful, and He keeps us and points our hearts to His love.","feel":"Spin around dizzy, lost, lost! Now hold out a pretend compass and watch the needle settle, pointing straight at Heavenly Father’s love. Walk that way, steady, steady. Can you show me your compass walk?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul said the Lord is faithful, He will keep you, and direct your heart into Heavenly Father’s love. Wren felt secure."],["NAME","That He-keeps-His-word feeling is called secure. Your mind might think, “He won’t fail.” Your body might feel settled shoulders and a steady heart."],["FIND","When did someone keep their promise to you, every single time, so you felt safe?"],["FORK","We can wander lost, or let the faithful Lord direct our hearts. Let Him direct you."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your heart settle like a compass needle. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, You are faithful. Keep me.”"]],"remember":"The Lord is faithful, and He shall establish you and keep you from evil!","ask":["“What does faithful mean?”","“What two things did Paul want our hearts pointed at?”","“What did Dad pull out when the family lost the trail?”"],"share":["Share a time the Lord proved faithful when everything else was uncertain.","Where is your heart pointed right now, and does it need redirecting?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who trusted the Lord’s faithfulness through loss or change."]},"b22-w4-n1":{"story":["Paul had a young friend named Timothy, who helped him teach about Jesus. Paul loved him so much. He called him “my dearly beloved son.”","Paul wrote Timothy a letter. He remembered that Timothy’s faith in Jesus was real, not pretend. And Paul knew where that faith had started.","It lived first in Timothy’s grandma, Lois. Then it lived in his mother, Eunice. Then it lived in Timothy, too.","Paul wrote, “From a child thou hast known the holy scriptures.” Timothy knew them from the time he was small. Faith passed from grandma to mom to boy, like one candle lighting the next.","Who shares Jesus with you? Tonight, thank Heavenly Father for them by name. Someday, you can light the next candle, too."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” Timothy’s faith came from his grandma Lois and his mom Eunice, like a candle lighting a candle. The Bible wrote down a grandma’s name! In Willow’s world today: Willow asks Grandma, “Who taught you about Jesus?” Grandma says, “My grandma, by the fire, every night.” Willow counts on her fingers. “Your grandma, you, Mom, me. Four candles!” Grandma hugs her. “And you’ll light the fifth.” Just like Timothy, Willow learned that faith is passed down from grandmothers and mothers like a candle lighting a candle.","feel":"Hold up one finger, a candle: Grandma Lois! Light another with it: Mother Eunice! Light another: Timothy! Now light your own candle from your family and hold it high. Can you show me your candle chain?","stones":[["SPOT","Timothy’s faith first lived in his grandma Lois and mother Eunice, who taught him from a child. Willow felt grateful."],["NAME","That passed-down-to-me feeling is called grateful. Your mind might think, “They lit my candle.” Your body might feel a warm glow, a long hug."],["FIND","Who in your family taught you about Jesus when you were little?"],["FORK","Faith can stop with one person, or be passed down like a candle. Pass it down."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and picture candles, one lighting the next. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for my family.”"]],"remember":"Timothy’s faith came from Grandma Lois and Mother Eunice, and yours was passed down too!","ask":["“What was Timothy’s grandmother’s name?”","“When did Timothy start learning the scriptures?”","“How many candles did Willow count?”"],"share":["Share who your Lois or Eunice was, the one who first lit your faith.","Whose candle are you lighting right now, and how?","Tell about the grandparent or ancestor who passed faith down your family line, by name."]},"b22-w4-n2":{"story":["Timothy was young, and he had a big job teaching about Jesus.","Paul wrote to him, “Let no man despise thy youth.” To despise someone is to look down on them, as if they don’t matter.","“Be thou an example,” Paul said, someone others can copy. Be an example in your words and how you live. Be one in love and in faith.","Paul told his helper Titus the same thing. Be “a pattern of good works,” like a shape that others trace. You don’t have to be grown up to show the way.","Little eyes may be watching you, too. Tonight, practice one kind word. Tomorrow, say it, and someone smaller may copy you."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer says, “Wow, look at that!” Paul told young Timothy, “Let no one despise your youth. Be an example.” Wren wonders who is tracing her pattern. In Wren’s world today: Wren’s three-year-old cousin copies everything she does. When Wren says “please,” he says “please.” When Wren folds her arms to pray, he folds his. Wren realizes she is his pattern. The next day she is careful to say kind words, because little eyes are tracing. Just like Timothy, Wren learned that even a young person can be an example others copy.","feel":"Shrink down small, “I’m just a kid.” Now stand up tall, “No one despise my youth!” Trace a pattern in the air, and let a friend trace it too. Can you show me your pattern trace?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul told young Timothy to let no one despise his youth, but be an example in word, love, and faith. Wren felt capable."],["NAME","That I-can-do-this feeling is called capable. Your mind might think, “Someone’s copying me.” Your body might feel a straight back, careful hands."],["FIND","When did someone younger copy something you did, good or bad?"],["FORK","Wren could think she is too young to matter, or be an example for her cousin. She became an example."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and think of one kind thing someone could copy. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, make me a good pattern.”"]],"remember":"Let no one despise your youth, be an example of the believers!","ask":["“What does despise mean?”","“Name two things Paul said to be an example in.”","“Who copied everything Wren did?”"],"share":["Share a young person who was an example to you.","In which of Paul’s six areas is your pattern strongest?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who was an example of the believers from a young age."]},"b22-w4-n3":{"story":["Some people wanted to be rich more than anything. So Paul wrote Timothy a happy secret. “Godliness with contentment is great gain.”","Loving Heavenly Father and being glad with what you have is the real treasure. “We brought nothing into this world,” Paul said.","We cannot carry things out of this world, either. So Paul said, if we have food and clothes, let us be content.","Paul warned about a trap. Wanting more and more catches people like a hidden snare. “The love of money is the root of all evil.” Like a weed’s root, it grows many sad things.","Tonight, name three things you already have and love. Then say, “Thank You, Heavenly Father. I have enough.”"],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see!” Paul said being content with food and clothes is great gain, and loving money is a trap. Scout counted what he had. Enough. In Scout’s world today: Scout’s friend gets a new game system and brags all day. Scout feels the “I want more” tug. At home, he looks at his bike, his dog, and his library books. “Food and clothes and a dog. I’m content.” The tug lets go. Just like Paul taught, Scout learned that being content with what he has is the real treasure.","feel":"Grab, grab for more, more! Uh oh, your foot is caught in a snare! Now stop, sit down, and hold what you have: pat your tummy, pat your shirt. Smile: enough! Can you show me your content smile?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul said godliness with contentment is great gain, and the love of money is a trap. Scout felt content."],["NAME","That what-I-have-is-enough feeling is called content. Your mind might think, “I don’t need more.” Your body might feel still hands, an easy breath."],["FIND","When did you stop wanting more and feel happy with what you had?"],["FORK","Scout could chase the “I want more” tug, or be content with his bike and his dog. He was content."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and count three things you already have. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, I am content.”"]],"remember":"Godliness with contentment is great gain!","ask":["“What two things together are great gain?”","“What is the root of all evil?”","“What three things did Scout count at home?”"],"share":["Share a season when contentment made you richer than money could.","Where is the love of money tugging at you, and what would contentment be?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who was content with little and rich in faith."]},"b22-w4-n4":{"story":["Paul was in prison, and he knew hard days were coming. He wrote to his young friend Timothy to help him be strong.","Paul told Timothy to stir up the gift Heavenly Father gave him. It was like poking a campfire until the coals glow bright again.","Then Paul wrote, “Heavenly Father hath not given us the spirit of fear.” Instead, He gives power, love, and a sound mind.","He gives power to do right and love that thinks of others. He gives a calm, clear mind. Paul wanted Timothy to be brave.","When fear creeps in, you can whisper Paul’s words: “Not fear. Power, love, and a sound mind.” Then take a slow breath and feel brave inside."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big faith, brave heart!” Paul said fear is not from Heavenly Father, but power, love, and a sound mind are. Beau says that verse when his knees shake. In Beau’s world today: Beau has to give a talk in Primary and the fear creeps in. He whispers, “Not fear. Power, love, a sound mind.” He pictures the little kids he wants to help, and his mind clears. His voice does not shake. Just like Timothy, Beau learned that Heavenly Father gives power, love, and a sound mind instead of fear.","feel":"Shiver with fear, brrr, shaky knees! Now push the fear away, not from Heavenly Father! Flex for power. Hug for love. Tap your head for a sound mind. Stand tall! Can you show me your power-love-mind?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul said Heavenly Father has not given us the spirit of fear, but of power, love, and a sound mind. Beau felt brave."],["NAME","That no-need-to-be-scared feeling is called brave. Your mind might think, “Power, love, sound mind.” Your body might feel steady knees, a clear head."],["FIND","When did you push fear away and feel power, love, and a clear mind instead?"],["FORK","Beau could let the fear shake him, or stir up the gift inside him. He stirred it up."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let power, love, and a sound mind settle in. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, fear is not from You.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father has not given us the spirit of fear, but of power, love, and a sound mind!","ask":["“What does “stir up” mean?”","“What three things does Heavenly Father give instead of fear?”","“What did Beau whisper in the hallway?”"],"share":["Share a time this verse replaced fear with power, love, and a sound mind.","What gift in you needs stirring up right now?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who faced fear with power, love, and a sound mind."]},"b22-w4-n5":{"story":["Paul told Timothy to hold on to what he had learned. Timothy had known the holy scriptures since he was a little child.","The scriptures could make even a child wise, Paul said. They teach how Jesus saves us through faith in Him.","Then Paul said, “All scripture is given by inspiration of Heavenly Father.” Inspiration is like a breath. Heavenly Father breathed His words into the people who wrote them.","The scriptures teach what is true and show us when we are wrong. They help us fix it and do right. They are like a toolbox with a tool for every good work.","When you feel mad or stuck, open the toolbox. Tonight, ask a grown-up to read you a verse."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer says, “Wow, look at that!” Paul said all scripture is breathed out by Heavenly Father, a toolbox for every good work. A book with a tool for everything! In Wren’s world today: Wren is mad at her sister. Mom opens the scriptures. “Let’s find the tool.” They read about being kind and tenderhearted. Wren goes and says sorry first. “The toolbox had it,” she says. “It always does.” Just like Timothy, Wren learned that the scriptures are Heavenly Father’s words and a toolbox for every good work.","feel":"Open a big pretend toolbox, creak. Pull out a tool for truth, a tool for fixing mistakes, a tool for doing right! Now open your scriptures and hold them up high. Can you show me your scripture toolbox?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul said all scripture is given by inspiration of Heavenly Father and furnishes us for every good work. Wren felt equipped."],["NAME","That I-have-the-tool feeling is called equipped. Your mind might think, “The scriptures have it.” Your body might feel ready hands, a confident nod."],["FIND","When did a scripture give you exactly the tool you needed?"],["FORK","Wren could stay mad with no plan, or open the toolbox of scripture. She opened it."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and picture your scriptures as a toolbox in reach. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for Your words.”"]],"remember":"All scripture is given by inspiration of Heavenly Father, a toolbox for good works!","ask":["“Who breathed the scriptures into the people who wrote them?”","“What is the scripture toolbox for?”","“What tool did Wren and Mom find for her anger?”"],"share":["Share a time the scriptures corrected or instructed you right on time.","Which of Paul’s four uses do you turn to the scriptures for most?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who treasured the scriptures as Heavenly Father’s own words."]},"b22-w4-n6":{"story":["Paul was in prison, near the end of his life. He wrote some of his last words to Timothy.","His words were brave and full of hope. “The time of my departure is at hand,” he wrote. He was ready, like a ship about to sail.","“I have fought a good fight,” Paul wrote. “I have finished my course, I have kept the faith.” He had run his race to the end.","A crown from the Lord was waiting for him. And it was not just for Paul. It is for everyone who loves Jesus and longs for Him to come.","You are running your race, too, one day at a time. Tonight, tell Heavenly Father, “I will keep the faith.”"],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big faith, brave heart!” Old Paul, chained in prison, said, “I have fought a good fight, I have finished my course, I have kept the faith.” Beau wants to say that someday. In Beau’s world today: Beau visits Great-Grandpa, who is very old. “Are you scared?” Beau asks. Great-Grandpa smiles. “I fought the good fight. I kept the faith. There’s a crown waiting.” He squeezes Beau’s hand. “Run your race, Beau.” Just like Paul, Beau learned that keeping the faith all the way to the end means a crown is waiting.","feel":"Run a long race in place, run, run, run, getting tired, keep going! Fight a good fight, punch, punch! Cross the finish line and put a crown on your head! Can you show me your crown finish?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul said, “I have fought a good fight, finished my course, kept the faith,” and a crown waited. Beau felt finished."],["NAME","That I-made-it-all-the-way feeling is called finished. Your mind might think, “I kept the faith.” Your body might feel tired legs, a crowned head."],["FIND","When did you finish something hard all the way to the end and feel proud?"],["FORK","Paul could be sad and afraid at the end, or look at the crown waiting. He looked at the crown."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly like a runner resting after a race well run. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me keep the faith to the end.”"]],"remember":"I have fought a good fight, I have finished my course, I have kept the faith!","ask":["“Where was Paul when he wrote these words?”","“What three things did Paul say he had done?”","“What did Great-Grandpa tell Beau to do?”"],"share":["Share how you hope to be able to say Paul’s words at the end of your course.","What part of your course needs more endurance right now?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who finished their course and kept the faith."]},"b22-w4-n7":{"story":["Paul wrote a short letter to his friend Philemon. Philemon had a servant named Onesimus. One day Onesimus left Philemon and went away.","Paul was in chains for teaching about Jesus. While Paul was in chains, Onesimus came to believe in Jesus. Paul loved him like his own son.","Now Paul sent Onesimus back home to Philemon, with a letter.","“Once he was no help to you, but now he is,” Paul wrote. Now they both loved Jesus, so they were family. Receive him, Paul asked, “not now as a servant,” but as “a brother beloved.”","When someone comes back, we can open our arms wide. Tonight, think of someone you can welcome home with a hug."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” Paul asked Philemon to take back a runaway servant as a brother. Willow thinks that open door is the warmest moment in the Bible. In Willow’s world today: Willow’s older cousin Drew ran away for a week. When he comes back, some grown-ups are stiff. Willow runs down the driveway and hugs him first. “You’re home!” Drew’s eyes get wet. Grandma says, “You opened the door, Willow.” Just like Paul asked of Philemon, Willow learned to receive someone who comes back as a beloved brother.","feel":"Walk slowly up to a door with your head down, a runaway coming home. Now be Philemon: open the door wide, creak, and throw your arms open! Hug like a brother. Can you show me your welcome-home hug?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul asked Philemon to receive the runaway Onesimus, not as a servant, but as a brother beloved. Willow felt welcoming."],["NAME","That come-in-you’re-family feeling is called welcoming. Your mind might think, “You’re home.” Your body might feel open arms and a running start."],["FIND","When did you welcome someone back after they had done something wrong?"],["FORK","Philemon could punish his runaway, or receive him as a brother. Paul begged him to receive him."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and open your arms wide for someone coming home. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, make my arms a welcome.”"]],"remember":"Receive him, not as a servant, but as a brother beloved!","ask":["“Who was Onesimus?”","“How did Paul ask Philemon to receive Onesimus?”","“What did Willow do when Drew came home?”"],"share":["Share a time you were received back “as a brother” after wronging someone.","Who is the Onesimus at your door, and how will you receive them?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who welcomed a wanderer home as family."]},"b22-w5-n1":{"story":["Long ago, Heavenly Father sent prophets like Moses to tell His people His words. He spoke through them many times, in many ways.","Then a letter called Hebrews told something even better. Now, in these last days, Heavenly Father “has spoken to us by His Son.” He sent Jesus!","The letter says Jesus is “the brightness” shining from Heavenly Father. Jesus is just like His Father. He holds up every star by His word.","Why brightness? Light shines out from the sun. When you see the light, you know the sun is there. When we see Jesus, we see what Heavenly Father is like.","Do you wonder what Heavenly Father is like? Look at Jesus. Tonight, think of one kind thing Jesus did."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see!” Heavenly Father spoke through prophets, then through His Son. Scout loves that Jesus is the exact image of the Father. In Scout’s world today: Scout finds an old photo. “Who’s that?” Grandma says, “Your great-grandpa. Your dad is the spitting image of him.” Scout looks at Dad, then the photo. “So if I know Dad, I kind of know him.” Grandma nods. “Like Jesus and Heavenly Father.” Just like the Hebrews learned, Scout learned that Jesus is the exact image of Heavenly Father.","feel":"Cup your hands like a prophet with a message. Now point up, then down: the Son came Himself! Spread your arms wide and hold up the whole universe. Can you show me your universe hold?","stones":[["SPOT","Heavenly Father spoke by prophets, then by His Son, the exact image of the Father, who holds up all things. Scout felt awed."],["NAME","That too-big-to-take-in feeling is called awed. Your mind might think, “He holds up everything.” Your body might feel a dropped jaw, wide eyes."],["FIND","When did you see something so big or beautiful that you went quiet?"],["FORK","We can wonder what Heavenly Father is like, or look at Jesus and see. Look at Jesus."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and picture Jesus holding up every star with His word. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for Your Son.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father has spoken to us by His Son, the brightness of His glory!","ask":["“How did Heavenly Father speak in the old days?”","“How did He speak in these last days?”","“Who did Scout’s dad look exactly like?”"],"share":["Share what looking at Jesus has taught you about the Father.","Where do you look for Him in messages, when He has shown you His Son?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who saw the Father’s face in the Savior."]},"b22-w5-n2":{"story":["Jesus is the Son of Heavenly Father. Yet He came to earth as a person, just like us.","Jesus looks at us and calls us “brethren.” That is an old word for brothers and sisters. He is “not ashamed” to say we are His family.","Why did Jesus become like us? So He could know just how we feel. He was even tempted, pulled to do wrong, but He never did.","Because He was tempted too, He knows how hard it feels. So the letter to the Hebrews says “He is able to help” us.","When choosing the right feels hard, you are not alone. Jesus understands you. Tonight, say, “Jesus, please help me choose the right.”"],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” Jesus became like us to understand us, and He is not ashamed to call us brothers and sisters. Being understood is the warmest feeling. In Willow’s world today: Willow is sad because she lost the spelling bee. Her big brother says, “I lost it too, in second grade. I cried in the bathroom.” Willow stares. “You did?” “Yep. So I get it.” He sits with her, and the sad gets smaller. Just like Hebrews teaches, Willow learned that Jesus understands us because He felt what we feel.","feel":"Reach up high like an angel, then come down low, like Jesus becoming like us. Feel tired, yawn. Feel hungry, rub your tummy. Now run to help a friend! Can you show me your run-to-help?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus became like us, calls us brethren, and because He was tempted, He runs to help us. Willow felt understood."],["NAME","That somebody-knows-how-this-feels feeling is called understood. Your mind might think, “He’s been here.” Your body might feel a loose chest."],["FIND","When did someone who had been through the same thing make you feel understood?"],["FORK","We can think Jesus is too high up to understand us, or trust that He felt what we feel. Trust Him."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and remember Jesus felt tired and sad too. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that Jesus is my brother.”"]],"remember":"Jesus is not ashamed to call us brothers and sisters, and He understands!","ask":["“What does brethren mean?”","“Why can Jesus help people who are tempted?”","“What did Willow’s brother tell her about second grade?”"],"share":["Share a time knowing Jesus had felt what you felt brought you comfort.","Whom could you “succour” this week because you have been where they are?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who felt deeply understood by the Savior."]},"b22-w5-n3":{"story":["The letter to the Hebrews shares a message from the Holy Ghost. “Today if you will hear His voice, harden not your hearts.”","A hard heart is like dry clay that will not bend. A soft heart is like wet clay that Heavenly Father can shape.","Long ago, His people walked in the desert for forty years. They saw Him do great things. Still, many made their hearts hard.","So we cheer each other on “daily, while it is called Today.” Wrong choices can trick us, little by little, until a heart gets hard. So we listen today, not someday.","Did you feel a tug to do something good? Listen right away. Tonight, ask Heavenly Father to keep your heart soft."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big faith, brave heart!” Hebrews says, “Today, if you hear His voice, don’t harden your heart.” A soft heart is braver than a stubborn one. In Beau’s world today: Beau is stubborn about saying sorry to his sister. His heart feels like a rock. In family prayer, Dad asks Heavenly Father to soften hearts. Beau feels a tug. “Today,” he thinks. Right after “amen” he says sorry, before his heart can dry out. Just like Hebrews teaches, Beau learned to keep his heart soft and listen to Heavenly Father’s voice today.","feel":"Make your heart hard, clench your fists like dry clay, stiff! Now hear Heavenly Father’s voice, cup your ear. Let your heart go soft, melt like wet clay. Say, “Today!” Can you show me your soft-clay heart?","stones":[["SPOT","Hebrews says, “Today, if you hear His voice, harden not your hearts,” and exhort each other daily. Beau felt listening."],["NAME","That my-heart-is-open feeling is called listening. Your mind might think, “Today, not later.” Your body might feel soft hands and a turned head."],["FIND","When did you feel a tug to do right, and do it right away instead of later?"],["FORK","Beau could stay stubborn and hard, or soften his heart today. He softened it."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let any hard place in your heart go soft. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, keep my heart soft.”"]],"remember":"Today, if you hear His voice, harden not your heart!","ask":["“What is a hard heart like?”","“When should we listen to Heavenly Father’s voice?”","“What did Beau do right after “amen”?”"],"share":["Share a “today” moment when you softened your heart instead of waiting.","Who exhorts you daily, and whom do you exhort, to keep hearts from hardening?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who kept a soft, listening heart all their life."]},"b22-w5-n4":{"story":["After Heavenly Father made the world, He “did rest the seventh day.” That is why we have the Sabbath. Each week we stop our busy work and remember Him.","The letter to the Hebrews says “there remains” a rest for Heavenly Father’s people. That rest is still there, waiting, like a gift.","Long ago, some people missed that rest because they did not trust Him.","This rest is even deeper than a nap. It is the peace of trusting Jesus. When we trust Him, our busy, worried insides can feel quiet.","This Sunday, rest with your family and think about Jesus. Tonight, take a slow, deep breath. Then whisper, “I trust You, Jesus.”"],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer says, “Wow, look at that!” Heavenly Father rested after making the world, and a rest is still waiting for us. On Sunday, Wren finds out what it feels like. In Wren’s world today: Saturday is wild: soccer, chores, a party. Wren is frazzled. Sunday, Dad says, “Today we rest like Heavenly Father did.” Church, a nap, stories on the porch. By evening Wren says, “My insides feel quiet.” Dad says, “That’s the rest.” Just like Hebrews teaches, Wren learned that Heavenly Father’s rest is a gift that settles your whole self.","feel":"Work, work, rush, rush, frazzled! Now stop. Sit down. Let your shoulders drop. Fold your hands in your lap and breathe out, ahh. Rest like Heavenly Father did. Can you show me your Sabbath rest?","stones":[["SPOT","Heavenly Father rested on the seventh day, and a rest remains for His people who trust Him. Wren felt restful."],["NAME","That my-insides-are-quiet feeling is called restful. Your mind might think, “I can stop now.” Your body might feel dropped shoulders, slow breath."],["FIND","When did a quiet Sunday make your insides feel calm?"],["FORK","We can rush through every day, or enter the rest Heavenly Father offers. Enter His rest."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your whole body enter His rest tonight. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for rest.”"]],"remember":"There remains a rest for the people of Heavenly Father, so enter in!","ask":["“On what day did Heavenly Father rest?”","“What remains for the people of Heavenly Father?”","“What did Wren’s family do on Sunday?”"],"share":["Share how keeping the Sabbath has given you rest you did not expect.","Where are you still laboring to earn what He offers as rest?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who kept the Sabbath as a true rest."]},"b22-w5-n5":{"story":["The letter to the Hebrews says Heavenly Father’s word is “quick, and powerful.” Long ago, quick meant alive!","His word is “sharper than any two-edged sword,” a sword sharp on both sides. It goes right through, all the way in.","Why a sword? His word, the scriptures, can go deep inside us, right to our hearts. They help us see what we really think and want.","Have you heard a scripture that felt just for you? That is His living word at work. Nothing is hidden from Heavenly Father. He knows the real you, and He loves you.","When a scripture tugs at your heart, listen to it. Tonight, ask, “Heavenly Father, what do You want me to learn?”"],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see!” Hebrews says the word of Heavenly Father is alive and sharper than a sword, and sees what you really think. It searches Scout back. In Scout’s world today: Scout has been sneaking cookies and says it’s fine. Dad reads, “Be ye doers of the word, and not hearers only.” Scout feels it go right in, like a little sword. “That one was for me.” He confesses the cookies before the chapter ends. Just like Hebrews teaches, Scout learned that the scriptures are alive and search the real you.","feel":"Open a pretend scripture and read. Feel the words go zing, right into your heart, like a sharp, gentle sword! Put your hand where it landed. Nod: “That one was for me.” Can you show me your zing?","stones":[["SPOT","The word of Heavenly Father is alive and sharper than a sword, discerning the thoughts of the heart. Scout felt searched."],["NAME","That it-found-the-real-me feeling is called searched. Your mind might think, “That was for me.” Your body might feel a little jolt and honest cheeks."],["FIND","When did a scripture feel like it was written just for you?"],["FORK","Scout could ignore the verse that pierced him, or let it search him and confess. He confessed."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let Heavenly Father see the real you, whom He loves. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, search my heart.”"]],"remember":"The word of Heavenly Father is alive and sharper than any two-edged sword!","ask":["“What does quick mean in this verse?”","“What is the word of Heavenly Father sharper than?”","“Which verse went right into Scout?”"],"share":["Share a scripture that discerned the thoughts and intents of your heart.","What are you telling yourself is fine that His living word might pierce?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who let the scriptures search and change them."]},"b22-w5-n6":{"story":["The letter to the Hebrews calls Jesus our “great high priest.” Long ago, a high priest stood before Heavenly Father for the people. Jesus does that for us.","Jesus came to earth and felt what we feel. He was tempted like we are, but He never did wrong.","Jesus is not far away. He is close and kind.","So the letter says, “Let us come boldly unto the throne of grace.” We can come brave, not hiding, to Heavenly Father, our King. There we find kindness and help.","Did you do something that is hard to tell? You do not have to hide. Tonight, pray and tell Heavenly Father, “I need help.”"],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big faith, brave heart!” Hebrews says come boldly to the throne of grace. Not creeping, not scared. Boldly. The bravest invitation ever written. In Beau’s world today: Beau is scared to tell Dad he broke the window. He creeps toward the office. Then he remembers, “Come boldly.” He stands straight, walks in, and says, “Dad, I broke the window and I need help.” Dad puts down his work. “Thank you for coming to me.” Just like Hebrews teaches, Beau learned that he can come boldly to Heavenly Father and find help.","feel":"Creep, creep, scared, tiptoeing up to a throne. Now stop! Stand straight, shoulders back, and walk boldly right up. Kneel and say, “I need help!” Receive it with both hands. Can you show me your bold walk?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus, our high priest, felt what we feel, so we can come boldly to the throne of grace for help. Beau felt bold."],["NAME","That I-can-walk-right-up feeling is called bold. Your mind might think, “He wants me to come.” Your body might feel straight shoulders, sure steps."],["FIND","When did you ask for help boldly instead of hiding, and find it waiting?"],["FORK","Beau could hide the broken window, or come boldly to Dad. He came boldly."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and walk right up to the throne of grace. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, I come boldly. Give me grace.”"]],"remember":"Come boldly to the throne of grace, and find help in time of need!","ask":["“What is Jesus called in this verse?”","“How should we come to the throne of grace?”","“What did Beau tell Dad?”"],"share":["Share a time you came boldly to Heavenly Father in a time of need.","Where are you creeping toward Him instead of coming boldly?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who prayed boldly and found grace."]},"b22-w5-n7":{"story":["Long ago, Heavenly Father promised to bless Abraham. The letter to the Hebrews says Heavenly Father cannot lie. He always keeps His promises.","So His promises give us strong comfort. We can hold on tight to the hope He gives.","Then the letter paints a picture. This hope is “an anchor of the soul, both sure and steadfast.”","An anchor is a heavy hook a boat drops into the water. When wind blows, the boat may rock, but it stays put. Hope in Jesus holds us steady like that.","When something feels big and stormy, remember your anchor. Tonight, hold your hands tight and say, “Jesus holds me steady.”"],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer says, “Wow, look at that!” Hebrews says hope in Jesus is an anchor of the soul, sure and steadfast. Wren saw one once. In Wren’s world today: On the lake, a sudden wind rocks the boat and Wren grabs the side. Grandpa drops the anchor, splash. The boat rocks but does not drift. “The anchor’s holding. That’s what Jesus is.” Wren lets go of the side. Rocking, but not afraid. Just like Hebrews teaches, Wren learned that hope in Jesus is an anchor that holds her soul in any storm.","feel":"Rock side to side like a boat in the wind, whoa! Now drop a heavy anchor, splash, all the way down. Pull the rope tight. Keep rocking, but you don’t drift! Can you show me your anchor hold?","stones":[["SPOT","Hope in Jesus is an anchor of the soul, sure and steadfast, holding us in every storm. Wren felt anchored."],["NAME","That the-storm-can’t-move-me feeling is called anchored. Your mind might think, “It’s holding.” Your body might feel rocking, but a settled heart."],["FIND","When did hope hold you steady while everything around you was rocking?"],["FORK","In a storm, we can grab the side in fear, or trust the anchor of hope in Jesus. Trust the anchor."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel your anchor of hope dropped deep and holding. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for my anchor.”"]],"remember":"Hope in Jesus is an anchor of the soul, sure and steadfast!","ask":["“What is an anchor for?”","“What is the anchor of the soul?”","“What did Grandpa drop when the wind came?”"],"share":["Share a storm where hope in Christ anchored your soul.","Is your anchor dropped deep right now, or are you grabbing the side?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor whose hope in Christ held through great storms."]},"b11-w1-n1":{"story":["Long ago, Heavenly Father’s people lived in Jerusalem. An army from a far land called Babylon broke the city. Many people had to leave their homes.","Jeremiah, who shared Heavenly Father’s words, was very sad. He wrote sad songs about his city. His heart felt heavy, like a big stone.","Then Jeremiah remembered that Heavenly Father is always kind. He wrote, “The Lord’s kindness never runs out. It is new every morning.”","Why did Jeremiah talk about the morning? The sun comes up every day, after happy days and sad days. Heavenly Father’s kindness comes back new just like that.","If today felt hard, remember what Jeremiah remembered. Tomorrow the sun will come up. Heavenly Father’s kindness will be there, waiting for you."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren fits tonight because she sits still long enough to notice a quiet truth. In Wren’s world today: she spills her juice at breakfast, and the awful feeling stays all morning. At bedtime, Mom says tomorrow will be a brand-new day. Just like the writer of Lamentations remembering that Heavenly Father’s mercies are new every morning, Wren learned that His kindness never runs out.","feel":"Squeeze your eyes shut tight, like the darkest part of the night. Now open them slowly, slowly, the way the sun comes up. Can you show me how a new morning opens?","stones":[["SPOT","Jeremiah's people lost their homes, and his heart felt heavy. Wren spilled her milk and thought her day was ruined."],["NAME","That heavy feeling is called sad. Your mind might think, 'Nothing good is going to happen now.' Your body might feel tight in your chest, or hot behind your eyes."],["FIND","When did you feel like that today? Maybe something spilled, or broke, or a friend went home too soon."],["FORK","Jeremiah could stay sad all day, or he could remember that Heavenly Father is kind every morning. He chose to remember. Wren chose to remember too, and helped wipe up the milk."],["CALM","Put one hand on your chest, take one slow breath in and out, and whisper, 'Heavenly Father, thank You for a new morning.'"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father's kindness comes back brand-new every single morning!","ask":["“What did Jeremiah remember when his heart felt heavy?”","“How often does Heavenly Father's kindness come back new?”","“What did Wren spill at breakfast, and what did Mama say?”"],"share":["When has a new morning given back something a hard night took from you? Share that with someone.","How do you help your child begin again after a hard day? Tell about a time you offered a fresh start.","Did a grandparent or ancestor greet each morning with quiet hope? Describe that quality in them."]},"b11-w1-n2":{"story":["Heavenly Father’s people had been taken far away from home. They felt forgotten and alone.","So Heavenly Father gave Jeremiah a message for them. He said, “I have loved you with an everlasting love.” That is a love that never, ever stops.","He said He had drawn them close with kindness. It was gentle, like a mom pulling her child in for a hug.","Heavenly Father made another promise too. He would build His people up again, like a broken house made new. One day they would play their little drums and dance again!","His love did not stop when His people were far away. It will never stop for you, either. Tonight, hug yourself tight and say, “Heavenly Father loves me!”"],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow fits tonight because she is gentle, and this story shows Heavenly Father being gentle with people who felt forgotten. In Willow’s world today: a new boy sits alone at the edge of the preschool rug. Willow does not tug his arm. She scoots over slowly and shares half her crayons until he scoots closer. Just like Heavenly Father drawing His people close with lovingkindness, Willow learned that real love invites and never pushes.","feel":"Stretch your arms out wide, wider, wider. Now wrap them all the way around yourself in a hug that does not let go. Can you show me a hug that never ends?","stones":[["SPOT","The people were far from home, and they felt like Heavenly Father had forgotten all about them."],["NAME","That far-away, left-out feeling is called lonely. Your mind might think, \"Nobody wants me.\" Your body might feel small and quiet, with a lump in your throat."],["FIND","When did you feel left out, like nobody remembered you? Willow saw that lonely look on the new boy's face."],["FORK","Willow could have walked right past him, or she could share with him and sit by him. She shared. Tomorrow you get to pick too: walk past, or go sit by the lonely one."],["CALM","Hug your own shoulders, slow and tight, and whisper, \"Heavenly Father, thank You for loving me always.\""]],"remember":"Heavenly Father's love for you started before you were born, and it will never stop!","ask":["“What word means a love that never, ever stops?”","“What did Heavenly Father promise to build again for His people?”","“Who can you sit by tomorrow, the way Willow sat by the new boy?”"],"share":["When did you feel drawn back by love instead of pushed by guilt? Share that memory with someone.","How do you invite your child back after they pull away? Tell about a time you chose gentleness.","Did a grandparent or ancestor love people steadily for a very long time? Describe that quality in them."]},"b11-w1-n3":{"story":["Jeremiah told the people what Heavenly Father said. The king did not like those words. So the king shut Jeremiah inside a yard with high walls all around.","Jeremiah could not go home. He could not walk out the gate. But Heavenly Father can reach us anywhere, even behind walls.","Right there inside the walls, Heavenly Father spoke to Jeremiah. He said, “Call to me, and I will answer you.”","Jeremiah could call to Heavenly Father by praying. Heavenly Father promised to answer him. He would show Jeremiah great and wonderful things he did not know yet.","You can call to Heavenly Father too, from anywhere at all. Tonight, fold your arms and tell Him about your day. He hears you, and He will answer."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout fits tonight because asking is how he finds things out, and this story is Heavenly Father saying, “Call unto me.” In Scout’s world today: he wonders why the moon seems to follow the car. He does not just guess and give up. He asks Poppa Allen at dinner, and the answer makes Scout’s mouth fall open. Just like Jeremiah being promised great things if he would call, Scout learned that asking opens the door to wonder.","feel":"Cup your hands around your mouth like a little trumpet and call one quiet word into the dark. Now be very still and listen with your whole self. Can you show me how a listening person sits?","stones":[["SPOT","Jeremiah was shut inside walls and could not go out. He felt stuck."],["NAME","That stuck, can’t-get-out feeling is called trapped. Your mind might think, ‘I can’t do anything.’ Your body might feel tight in your arms and legs."],["FIND","When did you feel stuck and wish you could get out?"],["FORK","Jeremiah could feel stuck and alone, or pray. Jeremiah prayed, and Heavenly Father answered."],["CALM","Cup your hands near your heart and whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, I am calling to You.’"]],"remember":"When you call to Heavenly Father, He hears you and answers!","ask":["“Where was Jeremiah shut in?”","“What did Heavenly Father promise when Jeremiah called?”","“Who did Scout ask about the moon?”"],"share":["When did an answer come only after you finally asked out loud? Share how that answer reached you.","How do you show your child that questions are welcome? Tell about a time you loved their question.","Did a grandparent or ancestor pray as though they truly expected an answer? Describe that quality in them."]},"b11-w1-n4":{"story":["An army from Babylon was all around Jerusalem. Jeremiah was shut inside a yard with high walls.","Heavenly Father told Jeremiah, “Your cousin Hanameel will ask you to buy his field.” Then Hanameel came, just as Heavenly Father said.","Jeremiah paid seventeen pieces of silver for the field. He signed a paper that said the field was his. People watched, so everyone knew it was true.","Jeremiah asked his helper Baruch to keep the paper safe in a clay jar. Why buy a field with an army outside? Heavenly Father promised His people would live in that land again.","Buying the field showed that Jeremiah had hope. He believed good things were coming. Tonight, tell Heavenly Father one good thing you hope for tomorrow."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau fits tonight because doing the brave thing before you can see why is his kind of courage. In Beau’s world today: he plants one tiny seed in a paper cup, and nothing happens for days. He waters it every morning anyway, even when the dirt still looks empty. Just like Jeremiah buying a field in a hard year, Beau learned that faith means doing good things before you see them grow.","feel":"Hold out one hand and count seventeen silver pieces into it, one at a time. Now fold your fingers over them and hold them tight. Can you show me how to hold a promise?","stones":[["SPOT","It was a very hard time, and buying a field seemed to make no sense."],["NAME","That nothing-good-is-coming feeling is called discouraged. Your mind might think, ‘Why even try?’ Your body might feel slow and droopy, like your feet are heavy."],["FIND","When did something feel so hard you did not want to try?"],["FORK","Jeremiah could give up on tomorrow, or trust Heavenly Father’s promise. Jeremiah bought the field."],["CALM","Press your palms together and whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, I trust You with tomorrow.’"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father’s promises are true, so we can do hopeful things even on hard days!","ask":["“Who asked Jeremiah to buy a field?”","“Where did Jeremiah put the paper to keep it safe?”","“What did Beau plant in a paper cup?”"],"share":["When did you act on a promise you could not yet see fulfilled? Share what that choice cost you.","How do you show hope to your child in a hard season? Tell about a time they saw you choose it.","Did a grandparent or ancestor work for a future they never got to see? Describe that quality in them."]},"b11-w1-n5":{"story":["Heavenly Father’s people were very sad. Many had been taken far from home.","So Heavenly Father gave Jeremiah a happy promise to share. One day the people would come home. They would sing together on a high hill in Jerusalem.","He said they would be like a watered garden. A dry garden droops, but a watered garden is green and full of life.","Girls would dance, and young men and old men would be glad together. Heavenly Father said, “I will turn their mourning into joy.” Their big, heavy sadness would turn into great big happiness.","Heavenly Father can help a sad heart feel glad again. If you felt sad today, tell Him about it tonight. He loves to comfort you."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow fits tonight because she stays close until sad feelings soften. In Willow’s world today: her brother drops his ice cream, and his whole face crumples. Willow does not laugh. She sits beside him on the curb and hands him her own spoon, and soon they are both giggling. Just like Heavenly Father turning mourning into joy, Willow learned that comfort helps a sad thing become a glad one.","feel":"Let your face go sad and droop your shoulders way down low. Now lift your shoulders up, let a smile grow slowly, and do one little dance. Can you show me sad turning into glad?","stones":[["SPOT","The people were far from home and had been sad for a long time."],["NAME","That sad-for-a-long-time feeling is called grief. Your mind might think, ‘I miss how things were.’ Your body might feel tired, with tears close to your eyes."],["FIND","When did you feel sad for more than a little while?"],["FORK","They could hide their sadness, or let Heavenly Father comfort them. Heavenly Father promised to turn it into joy."],["CALM","Rest a hand on your tummy and whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, please help my sad turn glad.’"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father can turn our sad times into happy, singing times!","ask":["“What would Heavenly Father turn their mourning into?”","“Their souls would be like a watered what?”","“What did Willow give her brother?”"],"share":["When did Heavenly Father turn a grief of yours into something you could be glad about? Share it.","How do you sit with your child’s sadness before fixing it? Tell about a time you simply stayed.","Did a grandparent or ancestor carry real joy through real sorrow? Describe that quality in them."]},"b11-w1-n6":{"story":["Long before Jeremiah, Heavenly Father gave His people good rules called His law. Moses brought them down, written on flat stones.","But the people did not keep their promises to Him. They knew His rules, but the rules were not in their hearts.","So Heavenly Father gave Jeremiah a new promise. He said, “I will write my law in their hearts.” Then His good ways would live inside them.","He called it a new covenant, a special promise with Heavenly Father. Everyone would know Him, from the littlest child to the biggest grown-up. Many years later, Jesus brought this new covenant.","You can know Heavenly Father too. Tonight, put your hand on your heart. Ask Him to help you keep His good ways inside."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren fits tonight because she keeps quiet truths tucked inside her, right where this promise goes. In Wren’s world today: nobody is watching when she knocks a puzzle piece under the couch. She could walk away. Instead, she lies flat on her tummy, fishes it out, and puts it back. Just like Heavenly Father writing His law on hearts, Wren learned that the best way to do right is from the inside.","feel":"Put your hand flat on your chest and feel your heart tapping. Now trace one slow line over it, like Heavenly Father writing something good inside. Can you show me where your heart is?","stones":[["SPOT","The people had broken Heavenly Father’s first covenant, and they needed a new start."],["NAME","That oops, I-wish-I-had-done-better feeling is called sorry. Your mind might think, ‘I wish I had chosen right.’ Your body might feel a quick drop in your tummy."],["FIND","When did you wish you had made a better choice?"],["FORK","They could do good only when someone watched, or keep His law in their hearts. Heavenly Father promised to write it there."],["CALM","Rest your hand over your heart and whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, please write good things in me.’"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father writes His goodness inside your heart, so it goes wherever you go!","ask":["“Where did Heavenly Father promise to write His law?”","“Who brought the new covenant many years later?”","“What did Wren do with the lost puzzle piece?”"],"share":["When did something you were taught move from your head down into your heart? Share when it changed.","How do you help your child obey from the inside out? Tell about a time they chose right unwatched.","Did a grandparent or ancestor carry their faith quietly on the inside? Describe that quality in them."]},"b11-w1-n7":{"story":["Jeremiah had a friend named Baruch. Jeremiah said Heavenly Father’s words out loud. Baruch wrote them down on a long rolled-up paper called a scroll.","The words were a warning. Heavenly Father wanted the king and the people to stop doing wrong. Then He could forgive them.","But the king did not like the words. He cut the scroll into pieces and burned it in his fire.","Then Heavenly Father told Jeremiah, “Take another scroll, and write all the words again.” So Baruch wrote them all again. They even added more words!","The king could burn a scroll, but he could not stop Heavenly Father’s words. When something you make gets broken, you can try again. Be brave, just like Baruch!"],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau fits tonight because starting over takes courage, and that is his kind of brave. In Beau’s world today: his tall block tower gets bumped over right before show — and-tell. His chin wobbles for a second. Then he kneels down and starts stacking again from the very bottom. Just like Baruch writing every word a second time, Beau learned that something worth making is worth making again.","feel":"Pretend your finger is a pen and write something in the air. Now wipe it away with your hand and write it again, just as carefully. Can you show me how to begin again?","stones":[["SPOT","The king burned the scroll, and all of Baruch’s careful writing was gone."],["NAME","That all-my-work-is-gone feeling is called disappointed. Your mind might think, ‘It’s ruined, so I give up.’ Your body might feel hot cheeks and a wobbly chin."],["FIND","When did something you worked hard on get ruined?"],["FORK","Baruch could quit, or pick up his pen again. Baruch wrote every word once more."],["CALM","Open your two hands and whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, please help me begin again.’"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father’s words can never be lost, and we can always begin again!","ask":["“Who wrote Jeremiah’s words on a scroll?”","“What did Baruch do after the scroll was burned?”","“What happened to Beau’s block tower?”"],"share":["When did you have to rebuild something from nothing? Share what helped you pick it back up.","How do you respond when your child’s work is ruined? Tell about a time you helped them restart.","Did a grandparent or ancestor rebuild after losing nearly everything? Describe that quality in them."]},"b11-w2-n1":{"story":["Ezekiel told the people what Heavenly Father said. An army from Babylon had taken him and his people far from home.","One day Ezekiel was by a river there. Suddenly the sky opened wide! Heavenly Father showed him a vision, a picture from heaven.","Ezekiel saw a king’s chair with Someone shining on it. Bright light was all around Him.","Around the light was a rainbow, like one in the clouds after rain. Ezekiel used a rainbow to help us see how beautiful it was. He did not hurry past. He bowed down low with wonder.","Even far from home, Heavenly Father was still near Ezekiel. The next time you see a rainbow, stop and look with wonder. Say thank you to Heavenly Father."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout fits tonight because a seeker’s whole heart opens wide when he sees something wonderful. In Scout’s world today: the rain stopped and a rainbow bent over the yard. Scout pressed his nose to the cold window and stood so still he forgot to blink. Then he ran and tugged Mom’s hand to come and see. Just like Ezekiel bowing low by the river, Scout learned that wonder is a quiet way of loving Heavenly Father.","feel":"Lie back and open your arms wide, wide, like the sky opening up. Now let warm, soft light settle all over you. Can you show me how big the sky opened for Ezekiel?","stones":[["SPOT","Ezekiel saw bright light and a rainbow, so beautiful he bowed down low."],["NAME","That big, quiet, wonderful feeling is called awe. Your mind might think, ‘Wow, that is so beautiful!’ Your body might feel very still, with wide-open eyes."],["FIND","When did you see something so beautiful you went very still?"],["FORK","Ezekiel could hurry past it, or stop and look with wonder. Ezekiel chose to bow with wonder."],["CALM","Open both hands on your blanket and whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, You are wonderful.’"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father’s light is bright, beautiful, and kind!","ask":["“Where was Ezekiel when the sky opened up?”","“What was all around the bright light?”","“What did Scout see out the window after the rain?”"],"share":["When has something in nature stopped you and felt holy? Share that moment with your child.","How do you make room for wonder on a busy family evening? Tell about a time you stopped to look together.","Did a grandparent or ancestor have a gift for noticing beauty? Describe that quality in them."]},"b11-w2-n2":{"story":["Heavenly Father showed Ezekiel a vision. A hand reached out, holding a long rolled-up paper called a scroll. It was full of writing.","Then He said something surprising: “Eat this scroll. Then go and speak to my people.” His people had stopped listening to Him.","Ezekiel trusted Him. He opened his mouth and ate the scroll. It tasted as sweet as honey!","Why eat a scroll? Heavenly Father wanted His words to go inside Ezekiel. Then Ezekiel would know them by heart when he taught. His words are good, so they tasted sweet.","When someone reads you the scriptures, His words go inside you too. Tonight, ask a grown-up to read you one sweet verse. Keep it in your heart."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren fits tonight because she keeps good words in her heart and thinks about them. In Wren’s world today: Wren asked Dad for the very same story three nights in a row. She said the words along with him, slower each time. In the morning, over her cereal, she was still thinking about it. Just like Ezekiel eating the sweet scroll, Wren learned that Heavenly Father’s words stay sweet long after the book is closed.","feel":"Rub your tummy and smack your lips, like something sweet just went all the way down. Now lay both hands over your heart, where good words stay. Can you show me your sweetest happy face?","stones":[["SPOT","Ezekiel ate the scroll, and it tasted sweet as honey. He was surprised and glad."],["NAME","That happy, surprised, yummy feeling is called delight. Your mind might think, ‘Oh, this is so good!’ Your body might feel a big smile and a warm tummy."],["FIND","When did something turn out much better than you thought?"],["FORK","Ezekiel could keep his mouth shut, or trust Heavenly Father and eat. Ezekiel chose to trust."],["CALM","Take one slow breath and whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, thank You for Your sweet words.’"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father’s words are sweet, and they stay in our hearts!","ask":["“What did Heavenly Father ask Ezekiel to eat?”","“What did the scroll taste like?”","“How many nights did Wren want the same story?”"],"share":["Which scripture has tasted sweet to you when you needed it most? Share that verse with your child.","How do you help good words settle in instead of rushing past? Tell about a time your child asked to hear something again.","Did a grandparent or ancestor love reading the scriptures out loud? Describe that quality in them."]},"b11-w2-n3":{"story":["Long ago, cities had tall stone walls. A watchman stood high on the wall and looked far away. If he saw trouble coming, he blew a loud trumpet to warn everyone.","Heavenly Father told Ezekiel, “I have made you a watchman for my people.” Ezekiel would warn the people to keep them safe.","Many people had wandered away from Heavenly Father. They were choosing wrong things.","Heavenly Father wanted every one of them to come home. So Ezekiel did not stay quiet. He called out kindly: “Turn, turn, and live!” Turning around and coming back to Him is called repenting.","If you choose something wrong, you can turn around too. Say sorry and try again. Heavenly Father is always glad when His children come back."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau fits tonight because calling out when it matters takes a brave voice. In Beau’s world today: Beau saw his little cousin chasing a ball toward the street. His throat went tight, but he shouted “Stop!” loud and clear. His cousin froze at the curb and came running back. Just like Ezekiel the watchman calling out to his people, Beau learned that a brave voice can help someone get safely home.","feel":"Sit up tall like a watchman on the wall and shade your eyes with one hand, looking far, far away. Now wave both arms: come home, come home! Can you show me your watching eyes?","stones":[["SPOT","Heavenly Father’s people had wandered away, and He wanted them to come home."],["NAME","That missing — someone, please-come-back feeling is called longing. Your mind might think, ‘I wish you were here.’ Your body might feel an ache in your chest."],["FIND","When did you miss someone and keep watching for them?"],["FORK","Ezekiel could stay quiet on the wall, or call out to help. Ezekiel chose to call out."],["CALM","Put your hand on your heart and whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, help me turn back to You.’"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father always wants us to turn around and come home to Him!","ask":["“What does a watchman do on the wall?”","“What did Heavenly Father want His people to do?”","“What did Beau shout when his cousin ran toward the street?”"],"share":["When has someone’s warning protected you? Share that story with your child.","How do you correct a child without shaming them? Tell about a time you called your child back gently.","Did a grandparent or ancestor watch over the whole family? Describe that quality in them."]},"b11-w2-n4":{"story":["A good shepherd takes care of his sheep. He finds them grass to eat and keeps them safe.","Israel’s leaders did not care for the people that way. The people were like lost sheep, scattered on a cloudy, dark day.","Heavenly Father told Ezekiel, “I myself will look for my sheep. I will find them.”","He promised to bring the lost ones home to green grass. He would wrap up the hurt ones, like a bandage on a scraped knee. He would make the weak ones strong. Many years later, Jesus said, “I am the good shepherd.”","You are one of Heavenly Father’s little lambs. He knows your name. Snuggle in and feel safe tonight. The Good Shepherd takes care of you."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow fits tonight because going after someone who feels left out is pure tenderness. In Willow’s world today: a boy sat by himself at the edge of the playground with his hood pulled up. Willow left the swings and sat down beside him. After a while she gave him half of her crackers. Just like the Shepherd going to find His lost sheep, Willow learned that Heavenly Father wants nobody left alone.","feel":"Curl up small and round like a little lost lamb. Now feel gentle hands lift you and carry you all the way home to bed. Can you show me how a lamb rests when it is safe?","stones":[["SPOT","The sheep were lost and scattered on a dark day, and they felt alone."],["NAME","That all-alone, far-away feeling is called lonely. Your mind might think, ‘Nobody is with me.’ Your body might feel small, with a lump in your throat."],["FIND","When have you felt left out or all alone?"],["FORK","The Shepherd could leave His sheep scattered, or go find them. He promised to find every one."],["CALM","Hug your knees close, then let them go, and whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, thank You for finding me.’"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father looks for every lost sheep, and He never gives up!","ask":["“Who said He would search for the lost sheep?”","“What did the Shepherd promise to do for the hurt sheep?”","“What did Willow give the boy on the playground?”"],"share":["When were you the one who was found? Share that time with your child.","How do you notice the child who has drifted to the edge? Tell about a time you went after one of your own.","Did a grandparent or ancestor reach out to people others forgot? Describe that quality in them."]},"b11-w2-n5":{"story":["Heavenly Father’s people were far from home. They had stopped listening to Him. Their hearts had grown hard, like a stone. Squeeze a stone all day, and it never gets soft.","Heavenly Father made them a promise through Ezekiel.","He said, “I will give you a new heart. I will take away the heart of stone. I will give you a soft, living heart.”","A soft heart is warm and alive, like the heart beating in you. It can listen, say sorry, and love again. Heavenly Father also promised to put His Spirit inside them. It would help them do good.","When you feel grumpy, He can help. Tonight, put your hand on your heart. Say, “Heavenly Father, please give me a soft, kind heart.”"],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow fits tonight because a soft, warm heart is what she cares about most. In Willow’s world today: Willow stayed mad at her brother all afternoon and would not look at him. Her chest felt tight and cold. At bath time something loosened, and she handed him the good boat without being asked. Just like Heavenly Father’s promise of a new heart, Willow learned that He can turn a hard feeling soft.","feel":"Squeeze both fists tight and hard, like two little stones. Now open them slowly and let your fingers go soft and floppy. Can you show me your stone hands turning into soft hands?","stones":[["SPOT","The people’s hearts had grown hard and cold, and they stopped listening."],["NAME","That tight, grumpy, closed-up feeling is called stubborn. Your mind might think, ‘I won’t, and you can’t make me!’ Your body might feel tight fists and a hard chest."],["FIND","When did your heart feel hard and not want to say sorry?"],["FORK","They could keep hearts of stone, or let Heavenly Father make them new. He promised to help them."],["CALM","Press one hand on your chest, breathe out long, and whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, make my heart soft.’"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father can change a heart of stone into a soft, loving heart!","ask":["“What new thing did Heavenly Father promise to give?”","“What kind of heart did He promise to take away?”","“What did Willow hand her brother at bath time?”"],"share":["When did your own heart grow hard, and what softened it? Share that with your child.","How do you invite softness instead of demanding an apology? Tell about a time you waited for a child’s heart to turn.","Did a grandparent or ancestor forgive when it was very hard? Describe that quality in them."]},"b11-w2-n6":{"story":["In a vision, Ezekiel stood in a quiet valley. It was full of very old, dry bones.","Heavenly Father asked, “Can these bones live?” Ezekiel answered, “Only You know.”","Heavenly Father told Ezekiel to speak His words, and he did. There was a noise and a shaking, and the bones came together. Then breath came into them. So many people stood up, alive!","Why this vision? His people were far from home. They said their hope had dried up, like old bones. Heavenly Father showed He could bring them home and give new life. Because of Jesus, everyone will live again one day too.","Breath is a gift from Heavenly Father. Tonight, take a slow, deep breath in and out. Say thank you for it."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau fits tonight because doing the right thing when it looks impossible takes real faith. In Beau’s world today: Beau planted one seed in a paper cup, and nothing happened for days. He kept watering it, even when his sister said it was done for. On Friday a green curl pushed up through the dirt. Just like Ezekiel speaking Heavenly Father’s words in the still valley, Beau learned that Heavenly Father brings life where we cannot see it yet.","feel":"Lie down very still and quiet, like the still valley before morning. Now take a big breath in and stretch both arms up tall and alive. Can you show me the whole valley waking up?","stones":[["SPOT","The valley was dry and still, and it looked like nothing could ever live there."],["NAME","That nothing-can-change feeling is called hopeless. Your mind might think, ‘It’s too late.’ Your body might feel heavy and droopy, like a wilted flower."],["FIND","When did something feel too broken to ever be fixed?"],["FORK","Ezekiel could stay quiet, or speak Heavenly Father’s words anyway. Ezekiel chose to speak them."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly through your nose and whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, thank You for my breath.’"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father can bring life back, and because of Jesus we will all live again!","ask":["“What did Heavenly Father ask Ezekiel in the valley?”","“What happened when the breath came in?”","“What finally grew in Beau’s paper cup?”"],"share":["What in your life came back after you had given up on it? Share that with your child.","How do you keep hoping out loud for your family? Tell about a time you spoke hope before you felt it.","Did a grandparent or ancestor keep faith through a long, hard season? Describe that quality in them."]},"b11-w2-n7":{"story":["In a vision, Ezekiel saw Heavenly Father’s holy house, the temple. A little stream came out from under its door.","A man walked Ezekiel into the water. First it reached his ankles. Ezekiel kept walking in. Then his knees. Then his waist!","Soon it was a river too deep to walk across. You would have to swim!","Everywhere the river went, things came alive. Fish swam in it. Trees grew beside it, with fruit to eat and leaves to help sick people. That land was a dry desert, so water meant life. Heavenly Father’s goodness brings life like that.","His goodness can grow in you, like that little stream. Tonight, wiggle your toes like you are wading. Think of one good thing He gave you."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout fits tonight because a seeker always wants to take one more step and see what happens. In Scout’s world today: Scout waded into the lake in his boots. Ankles, then knees, then he stopped and looked back at Dad, grinning. Dad held out his hand, and they went deeper together. Just like Ezekiel walking deeper into the river, Scout learned that Heavenly Father’s good gifts grow bigger the farther we walk with Him.","feel":"Lie down and pat your ankles, then your knees, then your tummy, as the water rises higher and higher. Now float still and easy on top. Can you show me how deep the water got?","stones":[["SPOT","The water kept rising higher, and Ezekiel wondered what would come next."],["NAME","That eager, what-will-happen feeling is called curious. Your mind might think, ‘I want to see more!’ Your body might feel bouncy feet and wide eyes."],["FIND","When did you want to keep going just to see what happened?"],["FORK","Ezekiel could stop at the edge, or keep walking in. Ezekiel chose to keep walking in."],["CALM","Wiggle your toes, then hold them still, and whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, lead me close to You.’"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father’s goodness grows deeper the farther we walk with Him!","ask":["“Where did the little stream of water come from?”","“How deep did the water get at the end?”","“Who held out a hand to Scout in the lake?”"],"share":["Where has your faith grown deeper than where you began? Share that part of your journey with your child.","How do you invite a child one step farther without pushing? Tell about a time you held out your hand instead.","Did a grandparent or ancestor keep growing in faith their whole life? Describe that quality in them."]},"b11-w3-n1":{"story":["Daniel and his three friends were taken far away to Babylon, a strange place.","The king sent them his rich food and drink. But Daniel wanted to keep Heavenly Father’s rules about food. So he chose not to eat it.","Daniel asked kindly, “Please give us vegetables to eat and water to drink. Try it for ten days, then look at us.”","For ten days, the four friends ate vegetables and drank water. They looked healthier and stronger than the other young men! Heavenly Father helped them learn and grow wise.","Daniel made a good choice far from home, and he asked kindly. You can make good choices too, even when others do not. Tonight, think of one good choice you made."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren fits tonight because Daniel chose quietly and wisely, and quiet wisdom is Wren’s gift. In Wren’s world today: at snack time everyone reaches for the cookies first. Wren eats her apple slices first, slowly, without telling anybody, and she feels good and strong inside. Just like Daniel asking for vegetables and water for ten days, Wren learned that small, quiet choices make a heart strong.","feel":"Sit up tall like Daniel standing before the king. Take a slow pretend sip of cool water and feel strong all the way down to your toes. Can you show me how tall and strong you can be?","stones":[["SPOT","Daniel was far from home in a new, strange place, and he wanted to obey."],["NAME","That wobbly, far-from-home feeling is called unsettled. Your mind might think, ‘Everything here is different.’ Your body might feel shaky inside, like your tummy can’t sit still."],["FIND","When did you feel unsure in a new place?"],["FORK","Daniel could eat the king’s food to fit in, or ask kindly to obey. Daniel asked kindly and obeyed."],["CALM","Press both feet flat and whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, help me choose what is right.’"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father makes quiet, faithful hearts strong and wise!","ask":["“What did Daniel ask to eat and drink?”","“How many days did the four friends wait?”","“What did Wren eat first at snack time?”"],"share":["When has a small, quiet choice made you stronger than you expected? Tell about it.","How do you help your child choose well without shaming them? Tell about a moment you got it right.","Did a grandparent or ancestor hold quietly to their standards far from home? Describe that quality in them."]},"b11-w3-n2":{"story":["King Nebuchadnezzar had a dream, but he could not remember it. He told his wise men, “Tell me my dream!” No one could.","Daniel asked the king for a little time. He asked his three friends to pray with him.","That night, Heavenly Father showed Daniel the king’s dream. Daniel thanked Him. Heavenly Father knows everything, even things hidden in the dark.","In the dream, a little stone was cut out without any hands. It grew into a mountain that filled the whole earth! Daniel said it was like Heavenly Father’s kingdom, which will never end.","When something is hard, you can pray like Daniel. Your family can pray with you too. Heavenly Father loves to help."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout fits tonight because Daniel went looking for an answer only Heavenly Father could give. In Scout’s world today: Scout cannot find his blue sock anywhere. He stops hunting, sits on the rug, and asks Heavenly Father to help him remember. Then he thinks of the laundry basket, and there it is! Just like Daniel praying with his friends before the answer came, Scout learned that asking Heavenly Father first is the best kind of looking.","feel":"Close your eyes and fold your hands the way Daniel and his friends did. Now open your eyes slowly, like morning light coming into a dark room. Can you show me your quiet asking hands?","stones":[["SPOT","The king forgot his dream and felt upset. Daniel and his friends needed an answer."],["NAME","That stuck, need-to-know feeling is called puzzled. Your mind might think, ‘I can’t figure it out.’ Your body might feel a scrunchy forehead and tight shoulders."],["FIND","When did a question keep spinning in your head?"],["FORK","Daniel could worry all alone, or pray with his friends. Daniel chose to pray with his friends."],["CALM","Fold your hands on your chest and whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, please help me understand.’"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father knows every answer, and He loves to help when we pray!","ask":["“What did the king forget?”","“Who showed Daniel the dream in the night?”","“Where did Scout find his blue sock?”"],"share":["When did an answer come only after you finally stopped and prayed? Tell about it.","How do you teach your child to ask Heavenly Father before solving everything alone? Tell about a time you prayed together.","Did a grandparent or ancestor turn to prayer before big decisions? Describe that quality in them."]},"b11-w3-n3":{"story":["King Nebuchadnezzar made a giant gold statue. He told everyone to bow down to it. But Shadrach, Meshach, and Abed-nego would not bow.","They said, “Heavenly Father is able to save us. But even if He does not, we will not bow down.”","So they were put into a hot furnace, like a giant oven.","The king looked in, surprised. “We put three men in the fire,” he said. “But I see four men walking, and they are not hurt!” Heavenly Father had sent His angel. Not one hair was burned.","The friends were brave together. Heavenly Father was with them in the hard place. He is with you too. Tonight you can whisper, “Heavenly Father is with me.”"],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau fits tonight because the three friends stood brave together, and bravery is Beau’s whole heart. In Beau’s world today: the lights go out in a summer storm, and his little sister starts to cry. Beau holds her hand in the dark and tells her, “We are not alone,” until the lamps hum back on. Just like the friends finding someone walking beside them in the fire, Beau learned that Heavenly Father is with us in the dark.","feel":"Get up on your knees in bed, tall and brave like the three friends. Hold up four fingers and wiggle the fourth one, because someone came to be with them. Can you show me your four walking fingers?","stones":[["SPOT","The three friends would not bow to the statue. They felt brave, standing together."],["NAME","That strong, standing-tall feeling is called courage. Your mind might think, ‘I will do what is right.’ Your body might feel a straight back and steady, planted feet."],["FIND","When did you do the right thing while others watched?"],["FORK","They could bow down to stay safe, or stand true. The three friends chose to stand true."],["CALM","Plant your feet flat and whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, please stay right beside me.’"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father is with you, even in the hardest places!","ask":["“What would the three friends not do?”","“How many men did the king see walking?”","“What did Beau do when the lights went out?”"],"share":["When did you stand alone for something true and find you were not alone? Tell about it.","How do you build courage in your child without shaming their fear? Tell about a moment.","Did a grandparent or ancestor hold their ground at real cost? Describe that quality in them."]},"b11-w3-n4":{"story":["King Nebuchadnezzar dreamed of a tall tree full of fruit. Daniel told him what it meant. He asked the king to be kind to the poor.","But the king forgot. He said, “I built this great city by my own power!” He thought only of himself.","A voice from heaven said he must learn that Heavenly Father rules. For a long time, his mind was not well. He lived outside with the animals and ate grass.","At last the king looked up to heaven, and his good mind came back. He praised Heavenly Father. Now he was humble, remembering every good thing comes from Heavenly Father.","You can remember that too. Tonight, look up and thank Heavenly Father for three good things."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow fits tonight because Heavenly Father gave the proud king another chance, and gentle mercy is Willow’s gift. In Willow’s world today: her big brother says his tower is the best one anybody ever built. Then it topples, and he goes quiet. Willow does not say, “I told you so.” She sits down and hands him blocks. Just like Heavenly Father welcoming the king back, Willow learned that kindness helps a heart after a fall.","feel":"Lie back and look up at the ceiling the way the king lifted his eyes to heaven. Now let both hands rest open and soft on your tummy. Can you show me your looking-up face?","stones":[["SPOT","The king felt big and important, and he forgot who gave him everything."],["NAME","That puffed-up, look-at-me feeling is called pride. Your mind might think, ‘I did it all by myself.’ Your body might feel puffed up, with your chin held high."],["FIND","When did you want everyone to see how big you are?"],["FORK","The king could keep saying, “Look at me,” or look up and give thanks. At last he looked up."],["CALM","Turn your palms up and whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, thank You for everything I have.’"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father helps us when we look up to Him with thankful hearts!","ask":["“What did the king see in his dream?”","“What happened when the king looked up to heaven?”","“What did Willow do when the tower fell?”"],"share":["When did a humbling season turn out to be a mercy? Tell about it.","How do you receive your child after a fall without lecturing? Tell about a moment you managed it.","Did a grandparent or ancestor carry success humbly? Describe that quality in them."]},"b11-w3-n5":{"story":["King Belshazzar had a big party. Then the fingers of a hand wrote words on the wall! The king was afraid.","No wise man could read them. The queen remembered Daniel. “Send for Daniel,” she said. “He can tell you what it means.”","The king promised Daniel gifts for reading it. He would get a red robe, a gold necklace, and a big job.","Daniel said, “Keep your gifts. I will read the writing for you anyway.” Then he told what the words meant. It was not happy news, but Daniel told the truth.","Daniel told the truth because it was right, not to get a prize. You can be truthful too, even when it is hard. Tonight, feel how light an honest heart is."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren fits tonight because Daniel told the plain truth without wanting a prize, and quiet truth is Wren’s way. In Wren’s world today: Wren knocks over the crayon cup, and her friend gets blamed. Wren’s tummy goes tight. She walks over, tells the truth, “It was me,” and helps pick up every crayon. Just like Daniel telling the truth without wanting the king’s gifts, Wren learned that telling the truth is right, even with no reward.","feel":"Put your hands behind your back like Daniel saying, “Keep your gifts.” Now take one big, brave breath. Can you show me your telling-the-truth face?","stones":[["SPOT","A hand wrote on the wall, and the king felt scared and mixed up."],["NAME","That shaky, something’s-wrong feeling is called uneasy. Your mind might think, ‘What is happening?’ Your body might feel a tight tummy and wobbly knees."],["FIND","When did your tummy feel tight because something wasn’t right?"],["FORK","Daniel could take the prize and change the truth, or tell it plainly. Daniel told the truth."],["CALM","Rest a hand on your tummy and whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, help me be honest.’"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father loves a heart that tells the truth, even with no prize!","ask":["“What wrote on the wall at the king’s party?”","“What did Daniel say about the king’s gifts?”","“What did Wren say when her friend got blamed?”"],"share":["When did you tell a hard truth with nothing at all to gain from it? Tell about it.","How do you make honesty safe for your child? Tell about a moment they told you something hard.","Did a grandparent or ancestor refuse a reward to stay honest? Describe that quality in them."]},"b11-w3-n6":{"story":["Some men tricked King Darius into making a new rule. For thirty days, no one could pray to anyone but the king.","But Daniel loved Heavenly Father. He opened his window toward Jerusalem, his faraway home. He knelt and prayed three times a day.","The men told the king. The sad king could not change his rule. Daniel was put down with the lions. The king hoped Heavenly Father would save him.","The king could not sleep. In the morning he hurried back. “Daniel!” he called. Daniel answered, “Heavenly Father sent His angel and shut the lions’ mouths.” Daniel was not hurt!","Daniel kept praying, even when it was hard. Tonight you can kneel and pray, just like Daniel. Heavenly Father watches over you too."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau fits tonight because Daniel kept praying when it was hard, and steady faith is Beau’s strength. In Beau’s world today: some new kids at the park say praying is silly. Beau still bows his head over his crackers, calm and kind, then asks if anybody wants to share. Just like Daniel kneeling to pray three times a day, Beau learned that he never has to hide his love for Heavenly Father.","feel":"Kneel on your bed and bow your head three times, slow and gentle, like Daniel. Now curl up like a big, sleepy lion and let your whole body go soft. Can you show me your sleepiest lion?","stones":[["SPOT","A new rule said not to pray, but Daniel kept praying and trusted Heavenly Father."],["NAME","That steady, trusting feeling is called faith. Your mind might think, ‘Heavenly Father will help me.’ Your body might feel calm and still, like a deep, slow breath."],["FIND","When did you keep doing right, even when it was hard?"],["FORK","Daniel could stop praying to stay safe, or keep praying and trust. Daniel chose to keep praying."],["CALM","Bow your head and whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, thank You for staying near me.’"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father hears our prayers and watches over us all night long!","ask":["“How many times a day did Daniel pray?”","“Who shut the lions’ mouths?”","“What did Beau do when kids said praying was silly?”"],"share":["When did faithfulness cost you something, and you chose it anyway? Tell about it.","How do you keep family prayer steady in busy seasons? Tell about a moment it mattered.","Did a grandparent or ancestor pray with unshakable regularity? Describe that quality in them."]},"b11-w3-n7":{"story":["Daniel had a special dream. He saw royal chairs called thrones set in place. Then a very old ruler called the Ancient of Days sat down.","His clothes were white as snow. His hair was like soft, clean wool. A bright stream of fire flowed before him.","Thousands and thousands stood before him, too many to count.","Next Daniel saw One like the Son of Man. He came with the clouds of heaven. He was given a kingdom that never ends. Jesus called Himself the Son of Man, so Daniel was seeing Jesus, our King!","Daniel wrote his dream down so we could know it too. Jesus is King forever, and He loves you. Tonight, picture soft white clouds and whisper, “Thank You for Jesus.”"],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout fits tonight because Daniel saw something wonderful, and joyful discovery is Scout’s delight. In Scout’s world today: Scout finds a rainbow on the hallway wall, made by sunshine through the window glass. He runs and takes Mom by the hand so she can see it too. They stand there quietly and look. Just like Daniel seeing the bright clouds and sharing his dream, Scout learned that wonder grows bigger when you share it.","feel":"Open both arms as wide as clouds and turn your palms up toward the light. Now let them float down slow, slow, slow onto your blanket. Can you show me how gently clouds come down?","stones":[["SPOT","Daniel saw something so bright and beautiful that his heart felt still."],["NAME","That big, quiet, wow feeling is called awe. Your mind might think, ‘That is so beautiful!’ Your body might feel very still, with wide eyes and an open mouth."],["FIND","When did something beautiful make you stop and stare?"],["FORK","Daniel could forget the dream, or remember it and share it. Daniel wrote it down for us."],["CALM","Open your palms and whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, thank You for Jesus.’"]],"remember":"Jesus is a King forever, and His kingdom will never end!","ask":["“What color were the clothes Daniel saw?”","“Who came with the clouds of heaven?”","“What did Scout find on the hallway wall?”"],"share":["When did one glimpse of glory reset your whole week? Tell about it.","How do you make room for awe in your home? Tell about a moment your child stopped you to look.","Did a grandparent or ancestor speak of the Savior with real reverence? Describe that quality in them."]},"b11-w4-n1":{"story":["Hosea told how Heavenly Father loves His people, like a parent loves a child.","He said, “When Israel was a child, I loved him. I called my son out of Egypt.” He had led His people away from a mean king there. Matthew later said these words were about Jesus too.","His people wandered, but He kept helping them. He called His people Ephraim. He said, “I taught Ephraim to walk, holding them by their arms.”","Think of a baby learning to walk. A parent holds the baby’s arms, so the baby won’t fall. Heavenly Father held His people up like that.","Someone held your hands when you learned to walk. Tonight you can say, “Thank You, Heavenly Father, for holding me up.”"],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow fits tonight because her love shows up in slow, gentle holding. In Willow’s world today: her baby cousin wobbled across the rug and plopped down hard. Willow did not laugh. She knelt, gave him both her hands, and walked slowly backward while he tried again. Just like Heavenly Father held Ephraim by the arms, Willow learned that love helps little steps feel safe.","feel":"Reach up and hold my two hands. Now squeeze softly, the way a baby holds on while learning to walk. Can you show me how gently you can hold on?","stones":[["SPOT","Heavenly Father held His people up like a baby learning to walk, and they felt safe."],["NAME","That safe, held-up, cozy feeling is called loved. Your mind might think, ‘Someone has me.’ Your body might feel warm and soft in your chest."],["FIND","When did someone hold your hand when you felt wobbly?"],["FORK","He could let them wobble alone, or hold them up. Heavenly Father chose to hold their arms."],["CALM","Open your hands on the blanket and whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, please hold me while I sleep.’"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father held His children’s hands, and He holds you close, too!","ask":["“What did Heavenly Father teach Ephraim to do?”","“How did He hold them up?”","“Who did Willow help walk across the rug?”"],"share":["When did Heavenly Father steady you through something hard, though you only noticed later? Share that memory with your child.","How do you slow down to match your child’s small steps? Tell about a time you did.","Did a grandparent or ancestor quietly hold someone up without needing thanks? Tell your child about them."]},"b11-w4-n2":{"story":["Hosea’s people had wandered away from Heavenly Father. So Heavenly Father told them how much He still loved them.","Long ago, farmers led their animals with ropes. But Heavenly Father said, “I drew them with bands of love.” He pulled His people close with soft ropes of love.","Heavenly Father does not yank or push. He gently pulls us close, like a hug.","Working animals wore a heavy wooden bar called a yoke. Heavenly Father said He was like a kind farmer who lifts the yoke off. Then He sets down food for the animal to eat.","Heavenly Father helps when things feel heavy. Tonight you can tell Him what felt heavy today. Then let Him help you feel light and loved."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren fits tonight because she notices how love leads without pushing. In Wren’s world today: a new boy at preschool would not come to circle time. Wren did not tug his sleeve. She sat down nearby, slid the good blue cushion over to him, and waited. He came. Just like Heavenly Father drew His people with bands of love, Wren learned that kindness invites better than pulling.","feel":"Wrap your arms around yourself in a soft hug, gentle and never tight. Now let your shoulders drop, heavy and loose. Can you show me your softest hug?","stones":[["SPOT","Heavenly Father’s people were tired, and He gently lifted their heavy load away."],["NAME","That light, load-lifted-off feeling is called relief. Your mind might think, ‘Ahh, that is better.’ Your body might feel your shoulders drop and loosen."],["FIND","When has someone carried something heavy for you?"],["FORK","He could pull them with hard ropes, or draw them with love. Heavenly Father chose love."],["CALM","Roll your shoulders down slowly and whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, thank You for being gentle.’"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father leads us with soft cords of love, never a hard tug!","ask":["“What did Heavenly Father use to draw His people close?”","“What heavy thing did He lift off?”","“What did Wren slide over to the new boy?”"],"share":["When did someone lift a load off you at just the right moment? Share that story with your child tonight.","Where could you invite instead of tug at home? Tell about a time gentleness worked better than pressure.","Did a grandparent or ancestor lead your family with gentleness instead of force? Describe that to your child."]},"b11-w4-n3":{"story":["Heavenly Father’s people had wandered far away, and they missed Him. In Hosea’s book, they said, “Come, let’s go back to the Lord.”","They said, “He will heal us. He will wrap up our hurts.” Heavenly Father helps our hurts get better, like a bandage on a scraped knee.","They said He would come to them as sure as the morning. Morning never forgets to come.","They said He would come like the rain, too. Farmers waited for rain, because plants need water. Heavenly Father’s help comes just when it is needed.","The sun has gone down tonight, but morning will surely come. Heavenly Father’s love is that sure. If you ever feel far from Him, you can come back with a prayer."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout fits tonight because he loves to go looking and finding. In Scout’s world today: Scout woke before anyone else and pressed his nose to the cold window to watch the sky turn pink. He watched the whole thing. At breakfast he told everyone the sun came back again. Just like the people who learned Heavenly Father comes as sure as the morning, Scout learned that morning always keeps its promise.","feel":"Curl up tight and small like the dark middle of the night. Now slowly open, like the sun coming up, arms stretching wide. Can you show me how morning comes?","stones":[["SPOT","The people were far from Heavenly Father and missed Him. They wanted to come home."],["NAME","That missing, want-to-go-home feeling is called longing. Your mind might think, ‘I want to be close again.’ Your body might feel an ache in your chest."],["FIND","When did you miss someone and want to run to them?"],["FORK","They could stay far away, or come back home. They said, “Come, let us return.”"],["CALM","Breathe in like fresh morning air and whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, I want to stay close to You.’"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father comes to us as sure as the morning and as gentle as the rain!","ask":["“What did the people want to do?”","“They said Heavenly Father comes as sure as what?”","“What did Scout watch through the cold window?”"],"share":["When did Heavenly Father meet you as steadily as sunrise after a long dark time? Share that with your child.","How do you welcome your child back after a hard day? Tell about a time you did.","Did a grandparent or ancestor keep faithful habits day after day? Describe that steadiness to your child."]},"b11-w4-n4":{"story":["Hosea’s people were farmers who had stopped listening to Heavenly Father. So He used a farming picture.","He said, “Break up your fallow ground.” That was dirt nobody had planted for a long time. It got hard and crusty, so seeds could not grow.","A farmer had to dig it soft. Heavenly Father was really talking about their hearts. They had grown hard like that ground.","He said, “Plant good things, and you will gather kindness.” It was time to look for Him. Then He would come and rain goodness on them, like rain on a garden.","Hearts can get hard and grumpy. Saying sorry and being kind makes a heart soft again. Tonight, pick one kind thing to plant tomorrow, like sharing."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau fits tonight because starting over takes steady courage. In Beau’s world today: Beau’s block tower fell over three times, and his chest went tight and hot. He set the blocks down, took a breath, and started the bottom row again, wider this time. Just like the farmer who breaks up the hard ground before planting, Beau learned that the hard, quiet work comes first.","feel":"Make two fists, hard as packed dirt. Now open them slowly, fingers soft and loose like dug-up soil ready for seeds. Can you show me your hands going from hard to soft?","stones":[["SPOT","The ground was hard and crusty, and nothing good could grow until it was dug."],["NAME","That tight, stuck, grumpy feeling is called frustrated. Your mind might think, ‘This is too hard!’ Your body might feel hot and tight in your chest."],["FIND","When did something feel so hard you wanted to stop trying?"],["FORK","They could leave the ground hard, or dig it soft. Heavenly Father asked them to break it up."],["CALM","Unclench your fists on the blanket and whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, please make my heart soft.’"]],"remember":"When our hearts get soft, Heavenly Father sends His goodness like rain!","ask":["“What is fallow ground?”","“What did Heavenly Father promise to send down like rain?”","“What did Beau start over, wider this time?”"],"share":["Where has your own heart gone hard and needed softening? Share honestly with your child what helped.","How do you praise effort, not just results? Tell about a time your child quietly tried again.","Did a grandparent or ancestor do patient work that no one clapped for? Describe that to your child."]},"b11-w4-n5":{"story":["Hosea’s people kept wandering away and stopped following Heavenly Father. But He made them a kind promise.","He said, “I will heal their wandering away. I will love them freely.”","His love was a free gift: nothing to earn, nothing to pay back.","He said, “I will be like the dew to Israel.” In that dry land, tiny morning drops on the grass helped plants live. He said His people would grow like a lily, a pretty flower. Their roots would grow deep, like tall trees.","Heavenly Father loves you freely too, just as you are. Tonight you can whisper, “Thank You, Heavenly Father, for loving me.”"],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow fits tonight because she gives mercy, being kind even after someone makes a mistake. In Willow’s world today: her big brother snapped at her over a spilled cup, then went quiet and pink-eared. Willow brought him a towel and sat beside him until he was ready to talk. Just like Heavenly Father promised to love His people freely, Willow learned that mercy does not wait to be earned.","feel":"Press your palms together, then open them slowly like a lily blooming in the night. Now hold them still and quiet. Can you show me a flower opening without any noise?","stones":[["SPOT","The people had wandered away, and they felt far off. Heavenly Father loved them anyway."],["NAME","That warm, loved-anyway feeling is called forgiven. Your mind might think, ‘I am still loved.’ Your body might feel light, like a big breath out."],["FIND","When did someone love you after you did something wrong?"],["FORK","He could make them earn it, or love them freely. Heavenly Father chose to love them freely."],["CALM","Open both hands like petals and whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, thank You for loving me.’"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father loves you freely, like fresh dew on the morning grass!","ask":["“What did Heavenly Father promise to heal?”","“What flower did He say His people would grow like?”","“What did Willow bring her brother?”"],"share":["When were you loved freely, before you had made anything right? Share that memory with your child.","How do you offer mercy before asking for an apology? Tell about a time you chose that.","Did a grandparent or ancestor forgive without keeping score? Describe that quality to your child."]},"b11-w4-n6":{"story":["Joel told the people what Heavenly Father said. It was a hard, hungry time, and many had turned away.","Heavenly Father said, “Turn to me with all your heart.” He wanted all of them, not just a little bit.","Back then, people tore their clothes to show they were very sorry. But Heavenly Father said, “Tear your heart, and not your clothes.” He wanted them sorry inside, ready to change.","Joel told them why it was safe to come back. Heavenly Father is kind and forgiving. He is slow to get angry. His kindness is very, very big.","When you do something wrong, you can turn back to Him too. You can say sorry and try again. He is happy when you come back."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau fits tonight because telling the truth takes real courage. In Beau’s world today: Beau broke the little clay bird off the shelf and hid the pieces under a cushion. His tummy hurt all afternoon. At bath time he brought the pieces out in both hands and told Mom everything. Just like the people Joel asked to turn back with all their heart, Beau learned that turning back is safe when love is waiting.","feel":"Roll over and face away from me. Now turn all the way back around and look at me. Can you show me your bravest turn-around?","stones":[["SPOT","The people had wandered away and felt sad. Heavenly Father asked them to turn back."],["NAME","That heavy, need-to-tell feeling is called sorry. Your mind might think, ‘I wish I had not done that.’ Your body might feel a tight tummy."],["FIND","When did your tummy feel tight because of something you hid?"],["FORK","They could just look sorry outside, or be sorry inside. Heavenly Father asked for their whole heart."],["CALM","Put one hand on your tummy and whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, I want to come back to You.’"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father is slow to anger and full of kindness, so turning back is safe!","ask":["“What did Heavenly Father ask His people to turn back with?”","“Is Heavenly Father quick or slow to get angry?”","“What did Beau hide under the cushion?”"],"share":["When did you turn back and find kindness instead of the anger you expected? Share that story with your child.","How does your face look when your child tells you the truth? Tell about a time your gentleness helped.","Did a grandparent or ancestor answer with patience instead of temper? Describe that quality to your child."]},"b11-w4-n7":{"story":["Joel’s land had a hard time. Huge crowds of locusts came, bugs like big grasshoppers. They ate the plants, and the people were hungry.","Then Heavenly Father made a happy promise. “Do not be afraid,” He said. “Be glad and rejoice!” He wanted their hearts full of joy.","He promised green grass, fruit on trees, and rain at the right time.","He said, “I will give you back the years the locusts have eaten.” There would be wheat for bread. He also promised to send His Spirit to all people. Later, Jesus’ friend Peter said this came true when the Holy Ghost came.","Heavenly Father can bring good things back. Tonight you can feel glad, and thank Him for the food He gives you."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout fits tonight because he is the first to spot new green after a long gray time. In Scout’s world today: Scout’s little garden pot sat brown and bare all winter, and he almost stopped checking it. One cold morning he found one curled green shoot and shouted so loud that Margo woke up. Just like the green Heavenly Father promised after the hungry time, Scout learned that Heavenly Father gives good things back.","feel":"Lie flat and still like bare winter ground. Now wiggle your fingers up out of the blanket like new green shoots pushing through. Can you show me the grass growing back?","stones":[["SPOT","The people were hungry and sad. Then Heavenly Father promised that green would come back."],["NAME","That bright, it-is-getting-better feeling is called hope. Your mind might think, ‘Good things are coming!’ Your body might feel light and bouncy."],["FIND","What is something you waited a long, long time for?"],["FORK","They could stay afraid, or be glad. Heavenly Father told them to be glad and rejoice."],["CALM","Stretch your fingers wide like new leaves and whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, thank You for giving back.’"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father gives back what was lost and fills our hearts with good things!","ask":["“What ate the plants in Joel’s land?”","“What did Heavenly Father promise to give back?”","“What did Scout find in his garden pot?”"],"share":["What season felt lost to you, and how has Heavenly Father given something back? Share that with your child.","How do you help your child keep hoping through a long wait? Tell about a time it paid off.","Did a grandparent or ancestor rebuild after losing much? Describe that hopefulness to your child."]},"b11-w5-n1":{"story":["Amos was a herdman who took care of sheep. He also picked figs. He was not a prophet.","One day, Amos was walking behind his sheep. Heavenly Father picked him! He said, “Go and tell my people my words.” So Amos went.","The people had stopped looking for Heavenly Father. Some of them were unkind to poor people.","Heavenly Father told them, “Seek me, and you will live.” To seek is to look and look, like in hide-and-seek. Amos said to seek the One who made the stars. He made the seven stars and the big stars of Orion.","The same stars shine over your house tonight. When you pray, you are seeking Him. Tonight you can say, “Thank You for the stars.”"],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout fits tonight because seekers keep looking until they find. In Scout’s world today: Scout lost his little blue marble under the couch. He looked and looked, cheek flat on the rug, until his fingers closed around it, and he sat up glowing. Just like Amos told the people to seek Heavenly Father, Scout learned that the ones who keep looking are the ones who find.","feel":"Lie back and open both hands on your blanket like you are catching starlight. Now curl your fingers closed, holding tight to what you found. Can you show me your seeking hands?","stones":[["SPOT","Amos was just a herdman, yet Heavenly Father picked him. Being picked feels special."],["NAME","That picked-for-something-big feeling is called being wanted. Your mind might think, ‘Me? Really?’ Your body might feel tall and tingly in your chest."],["FIND","When has someone picked you for an important job?"],["FORK","Amos could say, “I am only a herdman,” or he could go. Amos chose to go."],["CALM","Open one hand toward the window and whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, I will look for You.’"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father made the stars, and He is always there when you look for Him!","ask":["“What did Amos take care of before he was a prophet?”","“Who did Heavenly Father tell the people to seek?”","“What did Scout find under the couch?”"],"share":["When did Heavenly Father ask something of you while you felt too ordinary for it? Share that story with your child.","How do you help your child keep looking when something feels lost or hard? Tell about a time they kept trying.","Did a grandparent or ancestor keep seeking Heavenly Father through plain, quiet days? Describe that quality in them for your child."]},"b11-w5-n2":{"story":["Obadiah told what Heavenly Father said about the people of Edom. They were unkind to their own family, the people of Israel.","Edom lived high in the mountains, in homes in the rocky cliffs. Up there, they felt safe. They felt proud, like they needed no one.","They said, “Who can bring me down?”","Heavenly Father answered, “You may fly high like an eagle. You may make your nest among the stars. But I will bring you down.” Eagles build their nests way up high. Edom thought no one could reach them. But Heavenly Father can reach everyone.","He wanted Edom to be humble, to know they needed Him. Tonight you can say, “Heavenly Father, I need You. Please help me be kind.”"],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren fits tonight because a quiet, thinking heart can notice pride before it grows. In Wren’s world today: Wren built the tallest block tower and told her cousin he could not touch it. Then she saw his sad face and felt something turn inside her. She scooted the whole tower to the middle. Just like Obadiah warned the proud people, Wren learned that being close is better than being high.","feel":"Stretch both arms up high, like a nest among the stars. Now bring them down slowly and wrap them around yourself in a warm hug. Can you show me how good close feels?","stones":[["SPOT","Edom lived up high and felt proud, like no one could ever reach them."],["NAME","That I’m-better-than-you feeling is called pride. Your mind might think, ‘Mine is the best.’ Your body might feel puffed up, with your chin lifted high."],["FIND","When have you wanted something all to yourself instead of sharing?"],["FORK","They could stay proud up high, or come down and be humble. Heavenly Father warned them to come down."],["CALM","Lower both hands into your lap and whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, help my heart be humble.’"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father loves a humble heart that stays close to others!","ask":["“Where did the people of Edom build their homes?”","“Where do eagles build their nests?”","“What did Wren do with her tall tower?”"],"share":["When did pride quietly lift you away from someone you loved, and what brought you back? Share that with your child.","How do you help your child come down gently instead of shaming them? Tell about a moment you watched them soften.","Did a grandparent or ancestor stay humble when they could have looked down on others? Describe that quality in them."]},"b11-w5-n3":{"story":["Heavenly Father spoke to Jonah. He said, “Go to the big city of Nineveh.” Jonah was to tell the people to stop doing wrong. But Nineveh’s people were enemies of Jonah’s people.","Jonah did not want to go. So he ran away! He got on a ship sailing the other way.","Out at sea, a great wind began to blow. The waves grew big. The sailors were afraid.","But where was Jonah? He was down inside the ship, fast asleep! The captain woke him up. “Get up,” he said, “and pray!”","Jonah tried to run away, but Heavenly Father still knew right where he was. He always knows where you are too. Tonight you can whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me listen to You.”"],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau fits tonight because it takes courage to walk toward a hard job instead of running from it. In Beau’s world today: Beau knocked over the cracker box, and crumbs went everywhere. His feet wanted to run down the hallway, but he found the little broom instead. Mom came in and found him sweeping. Just like Jonah could not sail away from Heavenly Father, Beau learned that turning around is braver than running.","feel":"Curl up small on your side, like Jonah asleep in the bottom of the ship. Now rock gently side to side, the way a boat rocks on big water. Can you show me how a sleepy sailor rocks?","stones":[["SPOT","Jonah was asked to do a hard thing, and he wanted to run away."],["NAME","That heavy, don’t-make-me feeling is called dread. Your mind might think, ‘I don’t want to!’ Your body might feel slow, heavy feet and a tight tummy."],["FIND","When did you not want to do something hard?"],["FORK","Jonah could go to Nineveh, or sail the other way. This time, Jonah chose the ship."],["CALM","Take one slow breath in and out and whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, help me turn around.’"]],"remember":"No ship can ever sail away from Heavenly Father’s love!","ask":["“Where did Heavenly Father ask Jonah to go?”","“What was Jonah doing while the big wind blew?”","“What did Beau find instead of running away?”"],"share":["When did you go the other way from something Heavenly Father asked of you? Share with your child how you turned around.","How do you invite your child back when they run from a hard task? Tell about a time they turned around.","Did a grandparent or ancestor answer a call they would rather have avoided? Describe that quality in them for your child."]},"b11-w5-n4":{"story":["The wind blew hard. Jonah knew the storm came because he ran away. He told the sailors, “Put me into the sea. Then the sea will be calm.”","The sailors did not want to. They rowed and rowed to get back to land. But the waves were too strong.","At last, they put Jonah into the sea. Then the sea became calm and still.","But Heavenly Father had prepared a great fish, ready and waiting. The fish swallowed Jonah. Jonah stayed safe inside for three days and three nights. Long after, Jesus said Jonah’s three days were a sign pointing to Him.","Heavenly Father kept Jonah safe. He takes care of you too. Tonight you can snuggle in and feel His love."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow fits tonight because a tender heart knows dark places can still be safe. In Willow’s world today: the lights went out at nap time, and her little brother started to cry. She crawled over, found his hand, and held on. They sat together until the lamp blinked back on. Just like Heavenly Father kept Jonah safe inside the great fish, Willow learned that love can keep us safe in the dark.","feel":"Pull your blanket right up over your shoulders, like the great fish’s big, quiet room. Breathe in slow, breathe out slow, all safe inside. Can you show me your calmest, quietest breathing?","stones":[["SPOT","Jonah went down into the deep, dark sea, and he was afraid."],["NAME","That dark, all-alone feeling is called afraid. Your mind might think, ‘Who will help me?’ Your body might feel shivery, with a fast heartbeat."],["FIND","When has the dark felt too big for you?"],["FORK","Jonah could give up, or trust Heavenly Father. Jonah chose to trust Him and pray."],["CALM","Pull the blanket up to your chin and whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, You are here with me.’"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father prepares safe places for the people He loves, and that means you!","ask":["“What did Jonah tell the sailors to do?”","“Who prepared the great fish?”","“What did Willow do when the lights went out?”"],"share":["When did a dark, uncertain season turn out to be a place where you were held? Share that with your child.","How do you sit with your child’s fear instead of hurrying it away? Tell about a moment you simply stayed.","Did a grandparent or ancestor stay steady through a long, dark season? Describe that quality in them for your child."]},"b11-w5-n5":{"story":["Jonah was inside the great fish. It was dark in there. So Jonah talked to Heavenly Father in prayer.","Jonah prayed, “I called to You in trouble, and You heard me.” He remembered the deep sea. Seaweed had wrapped around his head.","Jonah had run away from Heavenly Father. Now he was turning back to Him. “When I felt weak,” Jonah said, “I remembered the Lord.”","Jonah said thank you to Heavenly Father. He promised to do what he had said. Then Heavenly Father spoke to the fish. The fish put Jonah out onto dry land.","No place is too dark or too far for Heavenly Father to hear you. Tonight you can fold your arms and talk to Him, just like Jonah."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren fits tonight because a quiet heart can hear what matters most. In Wren’s world today: Wren had a loud, tangled morning, so she took her blanket behind the big chair and sat down. She stayed there a long, still minute. Then she came out, found Mom, and said, “I’m sorry.” Just like Jonah prayed in the quiet dark, Wren learned that still places help us remember what matters.","feel":"Close your eyes and fold your hands, small and quiet like Jonah in the dark. Keep your hands very, very still. Can you show me your quietest praying hands?","stones":[["SPOT","Jonah was far down in the dark, and he felt far from home."],["NAME","That far-away, need-help feeling is called lost. Your mind might think, ‘How do I get back?’ Your body might feel small and droopy all over."],["FIND","When have you felt far away and wanted someone near?"],["FORK","Jonah could stay quiet in the dark, or pray. Jonah prayed, and Heavenly Father heard him."],["CALM","Fold your hands beneath your chin and whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, thank You for hearing me.’"]],"remember":"No place is too dark or too deep for your prayers to reach Heavenly Father!","ask":["“What did Jonah do inside the great fish?”","“Where did the fish put Jonah?”","“Where did Wren sit with her blanket?”"],"share":["When did a prayer from your lowest place reach Heavenly Father? Share what you remember about that moment.","How do you make room for stillness in your child’s noisy day? Tell about a moment quiet changed their heart.","Did a grandparent or ancestor pray through a long, hard season? Describe that quality in them for your child."]},"b11-w5-n6":{"story":["Heavenly Father spoke to Jonah a second time. “Go to Nineveh,” He said. This time, Jonah went!","Nineveh was so big, it took three days to cross. Jonah called out, “In forty days, Nineveh will be broken down!”","The people believed Heavenly Father. They stopped doing wrong things. They stopped eating for a while. They wore scratchy sackcloth to show they were sorry.","Even the king got down from his throne. He took off his fancy robe. He sat in ashes, a way people showed they were sorry. Heavenly Father saw them change, so He kept their city safe.","Heavenly Father gave Jonah and Nineveh a second chance. He gives you second chances too. Tonight, thank Him for letting you try again."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau fits tonight because obeying the second time takes courage too. In Beau’s world today: Beau was asked to put his boots away, and he did not. Later he thought about it, walked back down the hall all by himself, and set them side by side in the closet. Nobody had to ask again. Just like Jonah went when Heavenly Father asked a second time, Beau learned that it is never too late to turn back.","feel":"Sit up tall and proud, like a king on a high throne. Now climb slowly, slowly down and sit low on your blanket instead. Can you show me how a heart comes down off its throne?","stones":[["SPOT","The people of Nineveh had done wrong things, and they felt very sorry."],["NAME","That I-wish-I-hadn’t feeling is called sorry. Your mind might think, ‘I want to fix it.’ Your body might feel droopy shoulders and a heavy heart."],["FIND","When did you feel sorry and want to make it right?"],["FORK","They could keep doing wrong, or turn back to Heavenly Father. The whole city chose to turn back."],["CALM","Turn your body toward the door and whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, help me turn back to You.’"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father always gives another chance to a heart that turns back to Him!","ask":["“What did Jonah do when he was asked a second time?”","“What did the king do when he heard the message?”","“What did Beau go back to put away?”"],"share":["When did a second chance change the direction of your life? Share that turning point with your child.","How do you offer your child another try without shame? Tell about a moment they turned back on their own.","Did a grandparent or ancestor change course later in life than anyone expected? Describe that quality in them."]},"b11-w5-n7":{"story":["Jonah was angry that Heavenly Father saved Nineveh, his people’s enemies. He had wanted them punished.","Jonah sat outside the city. Heavenly Father made a leafy vine grow over him, shady like an umbrella.","The next morning, Heavenly Father sent a worm. The worm chewed the vine, and it dried up. The hot sun beat down, and Jonah was upset.","Heavenly Father said, “You cared about this vine. You did not make it grow. Should I not care about Nineveh?” Nineveh had more than 120,000 people who could not tell right from left. They were like little children, still learning.","Heavenly Father loves everyone, even people hard to love. Tonight, think of someone hard to be kind to. Ask Him to help you love them."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow fits tonight because a warm heart shows mercy, which means being kind when someone was unkind. In Willow’s world today: a boy knocked over her tower on purpose. Later he tripped and scraped his knee. Willow looked at him a long moment, then went and got the bandage box. Just like Heavenly Father showed mercy to Nineveh, Willow learned that kindness is bigger than being right.","feel":"Hold both arms over your head like a cool green vine making shade. Now open them wide and gather everyone you love into a big hug. Can you show me how big Heavenly Father’s kindness is?","stones":[["SPOT","Jonah wanted Nineveh punished, and he felt angry when Heavenly Father kept it safe."],["NAME","That that’s-not-fair feeling is called angry. Your mind might think, ‘They should not get away with it!’ Your body might feel hot cheeks and tight fists."],["FIND","When did it feel unfair that someone else was forgiven?"],["FORK","Jonah could stay angry, or let mercy in. Heavenly Father chose mercy for the whole city."],["CALM","Unclench both hands on your blanket and whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, help my heart be kind.’"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father loves every person in the whole world, and that means you!","ask":["“What grew up over Jonah to give him shade?”","“What happened to the vine the next morning?”","“What did Willow get for the boy with the scraped knee?”"],"share":["When has Heavenly Father’s mercy toward someone else been hard for you to accept? Share that honestly with your child.","How do you teach mercy when your child has truly been wronged? Tell about a moment they chose kindness anyway.","Did a grandparent or ancestor forgive someone who did not deserve it? Describe that quality in them for your child."]},"b21-w1-n1":{"story":["Paul wrote a letter to the believers in Corinth, a city by the sea. He told them, “Follow after charity,” the pure love of Christ. He told them to want the gifts the Holy Ghost gives.","At church, everyone had something to share, like a song or a teaching. Paul wanted every gift used to help others.","Paul said our words should edify. To edify is to build someone up, like adding bricks to a house.","Paul gave one simple rule for church: “Let all things be done unto edifying.” So before we speak, we can ask, “Will this build someone up?”","Tonight, think of someone who tried something hard today. Tell them one kind thing. That is one brick!"],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” Paul said every gift is for building each other up. Willow loves being a builder, one kind word at a time. In Willow’s world today: In Primary, a boy named Owen gives his first talk and his voice shakes. Afterward Willow finds him. “You were brave, and I learned something.” Owen stands a little taller. Willow thinks, “One brick.” Just like Paul taught, Willow learned that words can build people up like bricks on a house.","feel":"Lay a pretend brick, then another, then another, building a wall. Now say something kind to a friend and add one more brick on top. Stand back and admire your strong house! Can you show me your brick-laying?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul said use your gifts to edify, which means build each other up. Willow felt encouraging."],["NAME","That I-want-to-build-you-up feeling is called encouraging. Your mind might think, “You can do it.” Your body might feel warm hands, a cheering voice."],["FIND","When did someone’s words make you feel taller and stronger?"],["FORK","We can use our words to show off, or to build someone up. Build someone up."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and think of one person to build up tomorrow. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, make my words bricks that build.”"]],"remember":"Let all things be done to build each other up!","ask":["“What does edify mean?”","“What question should we ask before we speak at church?”","“What did Willow tell Owen after his talk?”"],"share":["Share a time someone’s words at church edified you.","Do your gifts tend to build others up, or draw attention to yourself?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor whose words always built people up."]},"b21-w1-n2":{"story":["Paul wrote down the most important things he ever taught. Christ died for our sins, the wrong things we do. Then He was buried.","Then He rose again on the third day, as the scriptures said. But how could people know it was really true?","Paul made a list of people who saw Jesus alive again. Peter saw Him first. Then the twelve, Jesus’s chosen helpers, saw Him.","Then over five hundred people saw Him at once! Paul said most of them were still alive, so anyone could go and ask. Then James saw Him, and last of all, Paul did too.","That is a lot of eyes! Jesus really rose. Tonight, tell someone you love, “Jesus is alive, and many people saw Him.”"],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see!” Paul listed everyone who saw the risen Jesus, five hundred at once, most still alive to ask. Scout loves evidence like that. In Scout’s world today: Scout’s cousin says, “How do you know Jesus came back alive?” Scout opens 1 Corinthians 15. “Peter saw Him. The twelve saw Him. Five hundred people saw Him at once. Paul saw Him. That’s a lot of eyes.” His cousin says, “Huh. Read that again.” Just like Paul, Scout learned that the resurrection is something hundreds of people saw.","feel":"Count: Peter, one finger! The twelve, ten and two more! Five hundred, wave both arms at a huge crowd! Paul, point to yourself. Now point to your eyes: they saw! Can you show me your witness count?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul listed the witnesses: Peter, the twelve, five hundred at once, James, and Paul himself. Scout felt certain."],["NAME","That I-know-it-really-happened feeling is called certain. Your mind might think, “So many saw it.” Your body might feel a firm nod and steady eyes."],["FIND","When did hearing from someone who was there make you sure something was true?"],["FORK","Paul could just say “believe me,” or give the names of people who saw. He gave the names."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel certain that Jesus is alive. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that Jesus rose and was seen.”"]],"remember":"Christ died, was buried, and rose again, and five hundred saw Him at once!","ask":["“What three things did Paul say happened to Christ?”","“How many people saw the risen Jesus at once?”","“What did Scout read to his cousin?”"],"share":["Share why the witnesses of the resurrection matter to your faith.","Who in your life is a living witness whose testimony steadied yours?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who was certain of the resurrection, and how it showed."]},"b21-w1-n3":{"story":["Paul wrote, “Now is Christ risen from the dead.” He called Jesus “the firstfruits.” Firstfruits are the very first ripe fruits picked each year.","Farmers were glad to see them. They knew the whole harvest was coming soon. Jesus rose first, so everyone else will rise too.","Death came into the world through Adam, the first man. Paul wrote, “As in Adam all die.” Then came the happy part: “Even so in Christ shall all be made alive.”","Every grandma and grandpa who has died will live again, because Jesus rose first. Paul said death is the last enemy. One day it will be gone forever.","Tonight, think of someone you miss. Say softly, “Jesus rose first, so they will rise too.”"],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big faith, brave heart!” Paul said that in Christ all will be made alive, every single person. Beau is not scared of death anymore, because the first apple already came. In Beau’s world today: Beau’s great-grandpa dies, and at the funeral Beau feels brave enough to touch his hand. “He’s only sleeping,” Beau whispers. “Jesus rose first, so Great-Grandpa will too.” Grandma hugs him tight. “That’s exactly right, Beau.” Just like Paul wrote, Beau learned that because Jesus rose first, everyone will be made alive.","feel":"Pick the first ripe apple off a tree, crunch! Now look at the whole orchard, so many coming! Lie down asleep, then rise up slowly, alive and stretching to the sky! Can you show me your made-alive stretch?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul said Jesus is the firstfruits, and in Christ all shall be made alive. Beau felt alive."],["NAME","That death-isn’t-the-end feeling is called alive. Your mind might think, “Everyone rises.” Your body might feel a deep breath and wide-awake eyes."],["FIND","When did remembering the resurrection make something sad feel more hopeful?"],["FORK","We can fear death as the end, or trust that in Christ all will be made alive. Trust it."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel how alive you are tonight. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that Jesus rose first.”"]],"remember":"As in Adam all die, even so in Christ shall all be made alive!","ask":["“What are firstfruits?”","“Who will be made alive because of Christ?”","“What did Beau whisper at the funeral?”"],"share":["Share how the promise “all shall be made alive” has comforted you.","Who are you most looking forward to seeing alive again?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor you expect to meet in the resurrection."]},"b21-w1-n4":{"story":["Paul knew someone might ask, “How do people rise from the dead? What kind of body will they have?” Paul answered with a garden.","A seed is small, plain, and dry, like a grain of wheat. You bury it. Then Heavenly Father gives it a new body, as He pleases.","Out comes a tall green plant that looks nothing like the seed! Paul said rising from the dead is like that.","The buried body is like the seed. It gets weak and wears out. The risen body is like the plant. It is strong and full of glory, and never wears out.","Tonight, look at your hands and smile. One day Heavenly Father will make your body new and strong forever."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer says, “Wow, look at that!” Paul said a buried body is a seed, and the risen body is the flower. Wren wonders what her flower will look like. In Wren’s world today: Wren plants a dry, wrinkly seed and asks, “How does something so plain turn into a sunflower?” Grandma says, “Heavenly Father gives it a body as He pleases. Same with us.” Weeks later the sunflower is taller than Wren. “Wow.” Just like Paul taught, Wren learned that the body is sown a seed and raised a glorious flower.","feel":"Curl up tiny like a dry seed in the ground. Now push one arm up like a sprout, then the other. Stand tall and open your hands like a huge sunflower! Can you show me your seed-to-flower?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul said the body is sown like a seed and raised like a flower, in power and glory. Wren felt amazed."],["NAME","That how-does-that-happen feeling is called amazed. Your mind might think, “Nothing like the seed!” Your body might feel wide eyes, an open mouth."],["FIND","When did you watch something small and plain turn into something beautiful?"],["FORK","We can look at a seed and see only a seed, or trust the flower that is coming. Trust the flower."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and imagine the strong body that will rise. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for the flower to come.”"]],"remember":"Sown a seed, raised a flower, that is the resurrection!","ask":["“What did Paul say the buried body is like?”","“What is the risen body like?”","“What grew from Wren’s wrinkly seed?”"],"share":["Share how the seed-and-flower picture shapes the way you think about death.","What “weakness” in your body are you glad will be raised in power?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor whose glorified body you hope to see."]},"b21-w1-n5":{"story":["Paul told the believers a wonderful secret. One day, he said, “we shall all be changed.”","It will happen “in the twinkling of an eye,” as fast as a blink! A trumpet will sound. Those who died will rise, never to wear out again.","Paul wrote words like a winner’s cheer. “Death is swallowed up in victory!” Jesus won, and death lost.","Then Paul asked, “O death, where is thy sting?” A sting hurts, like a bee’s sting. Jesus took death’s sting away, so it cannot hurt us forever.","Paul thanked Heavenly Father for giving us the victory through Jesus Christ. Tonight, blink fast and say, “Thank you, Jesus, for winning!”"],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see!” Paul said the change will happen in the twinkling of an eye. Scout blinked to see how fast that is. Fast! And then death loses its sting forever. In Scout’s world today: Scout gets stung by a bee and howls. Dad pulls out the stinger. “Once the stinger is out, it can’t sting again.” Scout sniffles. “Is that what Paul meant? Jesus pulled out death’s stinger?” Dad grins. “Exactly that.” Just like Paul wrote, Scout learned that Jesus pulled out death’s sting, and the victory is His.","feel":"Blink your eyes fast, twinkle, twinkle, that fast! Now play a loud trumpet, ta-da! Pretend a bee’s stinger is pulled out, phew. Raise both fists: victory! Can you show me your victory fists?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul said in the twinkling of an eye we will be changed, and death’s sting is gone. Scout felt triumphant."],["NAME","That we-already-won feeling is called triumphant. Your mind might think, “Jesus beat it.” Your body might feel raised fists and a big grin."],["FIND","When did you feel like a winner because someone bigger had already won for you?"],["FORK","We can live afraid of death’s sting, or celebrate that Jesus took it away. Celebrate."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let your fists soften into open hands. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that death has no sting.”"]],"remember":"Thanks be to Heavenly Father for the victory through Jesus!","ask":["“How fast will we be changed?”","“What did Paul say was swallowed up in victory?”","“What did Dad pull out of Scout’s arm?”"],"share":["Share how the Savior’s victory over death has taken away a sting for you.","Where do you still feel death’s “sting,” and how does Christ answer it?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who faced death with a victor’s hope."]},"b21-w1-n6":{"story":["Paul had taught that Jesus rose from the dead. Now he told them what to do about it. “Be ye steadfast, unmoveable,” he said.","To be steadfast is to stand firm, like a big rock in a river. The water pushes and pushes, but the rock stays put.","Paul said to keep busy doing the Lord’s good work. Nothing good we do for Him is ever wasted.","At the end, Paul spoke like a coach before a game. “Watch. Stand fast in the faith. Be strong.” Then he said, “Let all your things be done with charity,” Christ’s pure love.","Tonight, stand up tall and plant your feet like a rock. Then say, “I will be strong, and I will be kind.”"],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big faith, brave heart!” Paul said, “Watch, stand fast, be brave, be strong,” and then, “do it all with love.” Beau thinks that is the whole recipe for brave. In Beau’s world today: Beau’s team is losing and the other kids want to quit. Beau plants his feet. “We finish the game.” They lose, but they finish, and Beau shakes every hand on the other team. Coach says, “That’s steadfast, Beau.” Just like Paul taught, Beau learned to stand fast and be strong, and to do it all with love.","feel":"Stand like a rock in a river, feet wide, water rushing past, you don’t move! Watch with sharp eyes. Flex strong arms. Hand on your heart: with love. Can you show me your rock stand?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul said be steadfast, unmoveable, watch, stand fast, be strong, and do all with charity. Beau felt steadfast."],["NAME","That nothing-pushes-me-over feeling is called steadfast. Your mind might think, “I’m not moving.” Your body might feel planted feet and a calm chest."],["FIND","When did you stand firm even though others wanted to give up?"],["FORK","We can wobble when things get hard, or stand steadfast in the Lord. Stand steadfast."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel steady as a rock in a quiet river. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, make me steadfast and loving.”"]],"remember":"Be steadfast, unmoveable, always abounding in the work of the Lord!","ask":["“What does steadfast mean?”","“What did Paul say to do everything with?”","“What did Beau tell his team?”"],"share":["Share a time you stayed steadfast and later saw your labor was not in vain.","Which of Paul’s four orders do you need most right now?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who was unmoveable in the faith."]},"b21-w1-n7":{"story":["Paul was gathering a gift of money for the saints. They were believers in Jerusalem who needed help. Other churches were giving too. Paul wanted the Corinthians to help.","He told them how. On Sunday, the first day of the week, each person should set some aside.","Each one should give as Heavenly Father had blessed them. Then the gift would be ready when Paul came, with no last-minute rush.","Each Sunday’s bit would grow into a big gift, like coins in a jar. Then trusted helpers would carry it all the way to Jerusalem.","Tonight, find a little jar or box. You can put a little in it each week. One day it will be ready to help someone who needs it."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” Paul said set a little aside every Sunday so a gift is ready for people who need it. Willow has a jar for that. In Willow’s world today: Every Sunday Willow drops one coin into her “giving jar.” It takes months. When a family at church loses their house in a fire, Willow carries the whole jar to the bishop. “I saved it for someone who needed it.” Just like Paul taught, Willow learned to set a little aside each week so she is ready to give.","feel":"Drop a pretend coin into a jar, clink. Wait a week, drop another, clink. And another, clink! Now lift the heavy jar and carry it to someone who needs it. Can you show me your giving-jar carry?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul said lay a little aside each Sunday, so a gift is ready for the poor. Willow felt prepared."],["NAME","That I-have-it-ready feeling is called prepared. Your mind might think, “I saved for this.” Your body might feel steady hands and a glad heart."],["FIND","When did you save up a little at a time for something that mattered?"],["FORK","We can wait until someone needs help and have nothing, or save a little each week. Save a little."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and picture your giving jar slowly filling up. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me be ready to give.”"]],"remember":"Lay a little by in store each week, so you are ready to give!","ask":["“Who was Paul collecting money for?”","“On what day did Paul say to set money aside?”","“What did Willow carry to the bishop?”"],"share":["Share how planned, regular giving has changed your heart.","What could you “lay by in store” each week for someone in need?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who saved carefully in order to give generously."]},"b21-w2-n1":{"story":["Paul wrote a second letter to the believers in Corinth. He started it by praising Heavenly Father. He called Him “the Father of mercies,” because He is so kind. He gives all comfort.","Paul said Heavenly Father comforts us in all our hard times. Not some. All.","His comfort helps a sad heart, like a warm blanket on a cold night.","Then Paul told why Heavenly Father comforts us. It is so we can comfort others who are hurting. We give them the same comfort He gave us. It goes from Him, to you, to the next person.","Tonight, think of someone who felt sad today. Tomorrow, you can sit by them, sing, or give a hug. That is passing His comfort on."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer says, “Wow, look at that!” Paul said Heavenly Father comforts us so we can comfort others. Wren pictures a blanket passed down a line of cold people, never running out. In Wren’s world today: When Wren had the flu, Grandma sat by her bed and sang. Now Wren’s little brother has the flu. Wren pulls a chair to his bed and sings Grandma’s song. He stops crying. “I got that from Grandma,” Wren whispers. Just like Paul taught, Wren learned that the comfort we receive is meant to be passed on.","feel":"Shiver like you’re cold and sad. Now feel a warm blanket wrap around you, ahh. Then take the blanket off and wrap it around a friend who is shivering. Can you show me your blanket pass?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul said Heavenly Father is the Father of mercies, who comforts us so we can comfort others. Wren felt comforted."],["NAME","That warm-blanket-on-my-heart feeling is called comforted. Your mind might think, “It’ll be okay.” Your body might feel loose shoulders, a slow sigh."],["FIND","When did someone comfort you, and later you comforted someone the same way?"],["FORK","We can keep comfort for ourselves, or pass it on to the next hurting heart. Pass it on."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel His comfort wrap you like a blanket. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for comforting me.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father comforts us so we can comfort others!","ask":["“What did Paul call Heavenly Father at the start of his letter?”","“Why does Heavenly Father comfort us?”","“What did Wren do for her little brother?”"],"share":["Share a time the Lord comforted you so you could comfort someone else.","Who needs the comfort you have received, this week?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor whose comfort was passed down to you."]},"b21-w2-n2":{"story":["A man in the church at Corinth had done wrong and made people sad. The church gave him a consequence. Now he felt very, very sad.","Paul wrote that the punishment was enough. He told them to forgive the man and comfort him.","Why? Paul did not want the man “swallowed up with overmuch sorrow.” That is being so sad you feel like you are sinking under water.","So Paul begged them to show the man their love. He said, “If you forgive him, I forgive him too.” Not forgiving gives Satan a way in, like an open door. Forgiving shuts the door.","Tonight, think of someone who said sorry to you. Tomorrow, you can tell them, “We’re good. Come play.”"],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big faith, brave heart!” The man had done wrong, and Paul said, “Forgive him and comfort him before he sinks.” It takes a brave heart to open the door again. In Beau’s world today: Beau’s friend Jaxon lied and got Beau in trouble. Jaxon said sorry and sat alone all week. Beau sees him sinking. He walks over. “You said sorry. We’re good. Come play.” Jaxon’s whole face comes back to life. Just like Paul taught, Beau learned to forgive and comfort a sorry person before sorrow swallows them.","feel":"Watch a friend sink down, down, sad, like going under water. Now reach out both hands and pull them up! Pat their back and say, “We’re good.” Can you show me your pull-up hands?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul said forgive and comfort the man who was sorry, so sorrow would not swallow him. Beau felt forgiving."],["NAME","That I-let-you-come-back feeling is called forgiving. Your mind might think, “That’s enough.” Your body might feel open hands and a soft face."],["FIND","When did you forgive someone who was truly sorry, and watch them come back to life?"],["FORK","Beau could keep Jaxon out to teach him a lesson, or forgive and comfort him. He forgave."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and think of someone to welcome back. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me forgive and comfort.”"]],"remember":"Forgive him and comfort him, so he will not be swallowed up with sorrow!","ask":["“What did Paul say the church should do for the man?”","“What does “swallowed up with sorrow” mean?”","“What did Beau say to Jaxon?”"],"share":["Share a time forgiveness rescued someone who was sinking.","Is there someone whose punishment has been “enough” who needs comfort now?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who forgave in a way that healed the family."]},"b21-w2-n3":{"story":["Some teachers came to Corinth carrying letters from important people. The letters said, “You can trust this teacher.” Paul did not need a letter like that.","“Ye are our epistle,” Paul told the believers. An epistle is a letter. The believers themselves were Paul’s letter!","Anyone could look at their kind, changed lives. Then they would see that Paul’s teaching about Jesus was true.","They were a letter from Christ, written “not with ink, but with the Spirit.” Long ago, commandments were carved on stone. Now Jesus writes on hearts, where everyone can read it.","Tonight, think of one kind thing you did today. People read that like a letter. Tomorrow, you can write another page of Jesus’s love."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” Paul said people are letters from Jesus, written on hearts and read by everyone. Willow wants to be a letter that says “love.” In Willow’s world today: A new neighbor asks Mom, “What’s your church like?” Before Mom can answer, the neighbor adds, “Never mind. I watched your Willow share her bike with my boy all week. I already know.” Mom squeezes Willow’s hand. “You’re our letter, sweetheart.” Just like Paul taught, Willow learned that her life is a letter from Jesus that people read.","feel":"Write a letter with a pen, scribble, scribble. Now put down the pen and write with your finger on your own heart. Open your arms so everyone can read it! Can you show me your heart-letter?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul said the believers were a letter from Christ, written on hearts and read by everyone. Willow felt treasured."],["NAME","That Jesus-wrote-on-me feeling is called treasured. Your mind might think, “I carry His message.” Your body might feel a warm chest, a gentle smile."],["FIND","When did someone learn about Jesus just by watching how you acted?"],["FORK","We can tell people about Jesus with words only, or let our lives be the letter. Be the letter."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel Jesus’s love written on your heart. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, make my life a letter.”"]],"remember":"You are a letter from Christ, written on your heart for everyone to read!","ask":["“What is an epistle?”","“Where did Paul say Christ writes His letter?”","“What did the neighbor watch Willow do all week?”"],"share":["Share a person whose life was a letter from Christ to you.","What do people “read” when they watch your life this week?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor whose life preached without a sermon."]},"b21-w2-n4":{"story":["Heavenly Father once made light shine out of the dark. Paul said He has shined a light in our hearts too. It is the light of knowing Jesus.","Paul called that light a treasure. He said we carry it in clay jars, plain and easy to crack. Our bodies are the jars.","Why put treasure in a plain jar? So everyone knows the shine comes from Heavenly Father, not from us.","Clay jars get bumped. Paul said we have troubles all around, but we are not crushed. We get knocked down, but not destroyed. Inside, we are made new day by day.","Tonight, put your hand on your heart. Even on a bumpy day, the light of Jesus shines right there inside you."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see!” Paul said we are clay jars with treasure inside, and when the jar cracks, the light shines out. Best place to hide a light! In Scout’s world today: Scout drops his clay candle holder and it cracks. He lights the candle anyway. Light leaks through the crack in a bright stripe. “Look, Mom! It shines more now!” Mom nods. “The cracks let the light out.” Just like Paul wrote, Scout learned that we are clay jars, and the light of Jesus shines out even through cracks.","feel":"Make a round jar with your arms. Now get knocked, wobble, cracked! But hold your arms up: not destroyed! Open your fingers to let the light shine out the cracks. Can you show me your shining cracks?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul said we carry the light of Jesus in clay jars, troubled but not destroyed. Scout felt resilient."],["NAME","That knocked-but-still-here feeling is called resilient. Your mind might think, “Still shining.” Your body might feel a steady middle, a lifted head."],["FIND","When did you get knocked down but keep going, and even shine a little more?"],["FORK","A cracked jar can give up, or keep carrying the light. Keep carrying the light."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel the light of Jesus glowing inside your jar. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, renew me day by day.”"]],"remember":"We have this treasure in clay jars, cast down but not destroyed!","ask":["“What is the treasure?”","“What is the clay jar?”","“What happened when Scout lit the cracked candle holder?”"],"share":["Share a time your “jar” was cracked and the Lord’s light shone through anyway.","Where is your inward self being renewed, even while the outward wears down?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who was cast down but never destroyed."]},"b21-w2-n5":{"story":["Paul said our bodies are like tents. A tent is good for camping, but it does not last forever.","When our tent is taken down, Heavenly Father has a forever home for us. It is in heaven, a home no hands built.","Then Paul wrote words to carry everywhere: “We walk by faith, not by sight.”","Faith is trusting Heavenly Father, even when our eyes cannot see the way. We keep taking steps, because we trust the One who leads us. And Paul said anyone who belongs to Christ is made brand new.","Tonight, when the lights go off, remember Him. You can hold on, trust, and keep going. That is walking by faith."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big faith, brave heart!” Paul said we walk by faith, not by sight. The bravest steps are the ones you take before you can see the ground. In Beau’s world today: On a cave tour, the guide turns off the lights. Beau can’t see a thing. “Hold the rope and keep walking,” the guide says. Beau holds on and walks. The lights come on, and he is at the exit. Just like Paul wrote, Beau learned to walk by faith, trusting the guide even when he could not see.","feel":"Close your eyes and hold a pretend rope. Now walk forward, step, step, can’t see, but keep going! Trust the guide. Open your eyes: you made it! Can you show me your faith walk?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul said we walk by faith, not by sight, trusting Heavenly Father step by step. Beau felt trusting."],["NAME","That can’t-see-but-keep-going feeling is called trusting. Your mind might think, “He knows the way.” Your body might feel a tight grip, steady feet."],["FIND","When did you keep going even though you could not see how things would turn out?"],["FORK","We can stop when we can’t see the path, or walk by faith. Walk by faith."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and hold the rope of faith in your mind. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, I trust You to lead me.”"]],"remember":"We walk by faith, not by sight!","ask":["“What did Paul compare our bodies to?”","“What do we walk by?”","“What did Beau hold onto in the dark cave?”"],"share":["Share a time you had to walk by faith with no sight of the outcome.","What step is the Lord asking you to take before you can see the ground?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who walked by faith into the unknown."]},"b21-w2-n6":{"story":["Paul wrote that Heavenly Father has reconciled us to Himself by Jesus Christ. To be reconciled is to be friends again, like friends hugging after a fight.","Our wrong choices had put us far away from Heavenly Father. Jesus came to bring us back close to Him.","Paul called believers “ambassadors for Christ,” helpers sent to carry His message.","Their message was, “Be ye reconciled to Heavenly Father.” Come back, and be friends with Him again. He sent His Son to bring you home.","Tonight, if two people you love are upset, carry kind words between them. You can be an ambassador too. And you can always come back to Heavenly Father in prayer."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” Paul said Heavenly Father pleads with us, “Be reconciled, be friends again.” Willow loves making up after a fight, and she loves being the messenger who helps. In Willow’s world today: Willow’s two best friends stop speaking after a fight. Willow carries notes back and forth. “She says she’s sorry.” “She misses you.” By recess they are hugging. Dad says, “You were their ambassador, Willow.” She likes that word. Just like Paul taught, Willow learned that she can be an ambassador who helps people be reconciled.","feel":"Stand far from a friend with your back turned. Now turn around, walk toward each other, and hug: reconciled! Put on a pretend ambassador’s hat and carry a message. Can you show me your ambassador walk?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul said Heavenly Father reconciled us through Jesus, and we are His ambassadors. Willow felt reconciled."],["NAME","That we’re-friends-again feeling is called reconciled. Your mind might think, “The fight is over.” Your body might feel a loose tummy and a long hug."],["FIND","When did you make up with someone after a fight, and feel close again?"],["FORK","We can stay far away from Heavenly Father, or come back and be friends. Come back."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel Heavenly Father close. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for bringing me home.”"]],"remember":"Be ye reconciled to Heavenly Father, He wants to be close to you!","ask":["“What does reconciled mean?”","“What is an ambassador?”","“What did Willow carry between her friends?”"],"share":["Share a time you felt reconciled to Heavenly Father after a distance.","Where could you be an ambassador for Christ this week?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who helped reconcile people in your family."]},"b21-w2-n7":{"story":["Paul had sent the Corinthians a hard letter about their wrong choices. It made them sad. But Paul was glad, because their sadness helped them repent.","To repent is to say sorry and turn around to do what is right.","Paul said there are two kinds of sorrow. One is Heavenly Father’s kind of sorrow, and one is the world’s kind.","The world’s sorrow says, “I got caught,” and stays stuck. Heavenly Father’s kind says, “I hurt someone, and I want to change.” Then it gets up and does better. That kind leads us home to Him.","Tonight, if you did something wrong today, think about how the other person felt. Then say sorry, and do one kind thing to make it right."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer says, “Wow, look at that!” Paul said there are two kinds of sorry: one sinks you, one changes you. Now Wren can tell them apart. In Wren’s world today: Wren hits her brother and gets sent to her room. First she cries because she got caught. Then she thinks about his face, and a different sad comes. She goes out, hugs him, and gives him her swing turn. “The good kind of sorry.” Just like Paul taught, Wren learned that the right kind of sorrow turns you around and changes you.","feel":"Make a sad face and slump, “I got caught,” stuck. Now a different sad face, “I hurt someone,” and stand up, turn around, and walk forward to fix it! Can you show me your turn-around sorry?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul said sorrow Heavenly Father’s way leads to repentance and change, not just feeling bad. Wren felt repentant."],["NAME","That sorry-and-I’ll-change feeling is called repentant. Your mind might think, “I want to do better.” Your body might feel turning feet, a soft heart."],["FIND","When did feeling sorry make you actually do something different?"],["FORK","Wren could stay sad because she got caught, or let sorrow turn her around. She turned around."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let any sorry inside turn into a plan to do better. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, change me.”"]],"remember":"Sorrow Heavenly Father’s way works repentance, it turns you around!","ask":["“What are the two kinds of sorrow?”","“What does Heavenly Father’s kind of sorrow lead to?”","“What did Wren give her brother after she said sorry?”"],"share":["Share a time sorrow turned into real repentance for you.","How do you tell the world’s sorrow from Heavenly Father’s in your own heart?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor whose repentance changed the direction of your family."]},"b21-w3-n1":{"story":["Paul wrote to his friends in Corinth. He told them about the believers in Macedonia, a land to the north.","Those believers were having a very hard time. They were very poor. Yet their hearts were full of joy.","They gave more than anyone expected, even more than they could. Nobody made them do it. They begged Paul to let them help other believers in need.","How could poor people give so much? Paul told their secret. “They first gave their own selves to the Lord.” Before they gave anything else, they gave Jesus their whole hearts.","When your heart belongs to Jesus, your hands open wide. Tonight, tell Him, “My heart is Yours.” Then think of one thing you can share tomorrow."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see!” How did the poor Macedonians give so much? Paul told the secret: they gave themselves to the Lord first. The rest was easy. In Scout’s world today: Scout’s family has a tight month, but Mom still packs a bag of groceries for the neighbor who just lost his job. Scout asks, “How can we give when we don’t have much?” Mom says, “We gave ourselves to Jesus first.” Just like the Macedonians, Scout learned that when you give yourself to the Lord first, your hands open easily.","feel":"Hold out empty pockets, not much here! Now put both hands on your heart and lift it up to Jesus, yourself first. Then open your hands and give, give, give! Can you show me your heart-first give?","stones":[["SPOT","The poor Macedonians gave generously because they first gave themselves to the Lord. Scout felt inspired."],["NAME","That I-want-to-do-that-too feeling is called inspired. Your mind might think, “If they can, I can.” Your body might feel a lifted chest, ready hands."],["FIND","When did someone with very little give a lot, and it made you want to give too?"],["FORK","The Macedonians could keep what little they had, or give themselves first and then give. They gave themselves first."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and give your whole self to Jesus for the night. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, I am Yours first.”"]],"remember":"The Macedonians first gave themselves to the Lord, and then their hands opened!","ask":["“Were the Macedonians rich or poor?”","“What did the Macedonians give first?”","“What did Mom pack for the neighbor?”"],"share":["Share a time giving yourself to the Lord made other giving easy.","What are you holding back from the Lord that makes your hands tight?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who gave generously out of little."]},"b21-w3-n2":{"story":["Paul wanted his friends to know how much Jesus loves them. So he told Jesus’s whole story in one sentence.","Before Jesus was born, He lived in heaven with Heavenly Father. He had glory and every beautiful thing in heaven. That is rich!","But Jesus left it all and came to earth as a little baby. He did not come as a king in a palace. He came to be poor.","Why would Jesus leave heaven? Paul said, “For your sakes.” That means for you! He became poor so you could be rich. Not rich with money, but rich with His love and forgiveness.","Jesus gave up so much because He loves you. Tonight, think of one way to help someone you love."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” Jesus was rich in heaven and became poor on earth, for our sakes. The warmest trade ever made. In Willow’s world today: Willow has the top bunk, the best spot. Her cousin is sleeping over and is scared of heights. Willow gives her the bottom bunk and climbs up, then climbs back down. “I’ll sleep on the floor so you’re not scared.” Her cousin whispers, “Thank you.” Just like Jesus, Willow learned that giving up your best spot for someone’s sake is what love does.","feel":"Stretch up tall and shining, rich in heaven! Now come down low, curled up small in a stable. Give everything away with your hands. Then hug yourself: rich now! Can you show me your rich-to-poor-to-rich?","stones":[["SPOT","Jesus was rich in heaven and became poor for our sakes, so we could be rich. Willow felt humbled."],["NAME","That He-did-that-for-me feeling is called humbled. Your mind might think, “I didn’t deserve this.” Your body might feel a bowed head, a full heart."],["FIND","When did someone give up something good so you could have it?"],["FORK","Jesus could stay rich in heaven, or become poor for our sakes. He became poor for us."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and think how much Jesus gave up. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that Jesus became poor for me.”"]],"remember":"Though He was rich, for our sakes Jesus became poor, so we could be rich!","ask":["“Where was Jesus rich?”","“Why did Jesus become poor?”","“Where did Willow sleep so her cousin wouldn’t be scared?”"],"share":["Share what it means to you that the Savior became poor for your sake.","What “riches” has His poverty given you that money could not?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who gave up comfort for someone else’s sake."]},"b21-w3-n3":{"story":["Paul wanted his friends in Corinth to give to believers who needed help. So he talked about a farmer planting seeds.","If a farmer sows, or plants, a few seeds, a little grows. If he sows lots of seeds, lots will grow. Paul said giving works the same way.","Then Paul told them how to give. Do not grumble about it. Do not give just because someone makes you. Decide in your own heart, and give gladly.","Paul said Heavenly Father loves a cheerful giver, one who gives with a smile. He loves the glad heart behind the gift. And He always gives us enough to keep on giving.","Tonight, think of something you can share tomorrow. Then share it with your very biggest smile."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer says, “Wow, look at that!” Paul said giving is like planting: lots of seeds, lots of flowers. Heavenly Father loves the giver who smiles while planting. In Wren’s world today: Wren has three cookies. Her brother asks for one. She could grumble and give the smallest. Instead she hands him the biggest one with a big smile. “Why are you happy?” he asks. “Giving with a smile is the best kind.” He splits it with her. Just like Paul taught, Wren learned that Heavenly Father loves a cheerful giver.","feel":"Plant one tiny seed with a grumpy face, hmph. Now plant a whole handful of seeds with a giant smile, toss, toss, toss! Watch the flowers pop up everywhere. Can you show me your cheerful planting?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul said sow bountifully and give cheerfully, because Heavenly Father loves a cheerful giver. Wren felt cheerful."],["NAME","That happy-to-give-it feeling is called cheerful. Your mind might think, “I get to share!” Your body might feel a big smile and quick, glad hands."],["FIND","When did giving something make you happier than keeping it?"],["FORK","Wren could give grudgingly, or give with a smile. She gave with a smile."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel a cheerful smile spread across your face. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, I love to give.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father loveth a cheerful giver!","ask":["“What happens if a farmer sows lots of seeds?”","“What kind of giver does Heavenly Father love?”","“Which cookie did Wren give her brother?”"],"share":["Share a time cheerful giving came back to you “bountifully.”","Where do you give grudgingly, and how could you give cheerfully instead?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who was known as a cheerful giver."]},"b21-w3-n4":{"story":["Paul wrote about a different kind of fight. It is not a fight with swords or fists. Heavenly Father gives us mighty help for it.","What are we fighting? Wrong ideas that are not true. Some proud thoughts puff themselves up and try to push Heavenly Father away.","Paul said we can knock those thoughts down. Then he said the part to remember.","Paul said to bring “every thought” to obey Jesus. It is like putting a wild puppy on a leash. If a thought says, “You can’t do it,” catch it! Then say, “That is not what Jesus says.”","You can be the boss of your thoughts, with Jesus’s help. Tonight, if a grumpy thought comes, catch it and give it to Jesus."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big faith, brave heart!” Paul said catch every thought and bring it to Jesus. Beau pictures a puppy thought on a leash, and he holds the leash. In Beau’s world today: Before his spelling test, a thought barks, “You’ll fail!” Beau stops. “Caught you.” He thinks instead, “I studied, and Heavenly Father helps me do hard things.” He walks the wild thought to Jesus on a leash. He gets eight out of ten. Just like Paul taught, Beau learned that he can catch wild thoughts and bring them to Jesus.","feel":"Let a wild puppy thought run in circles, woof, woof! Now reach out and catch it, got you! Clip on a leash and walk it, nice and calm, over to Jesus. Can you show me your thought leash?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul said bring every thought into captivity to Christ, catching the wild ones. Beau felt in-charge."],["NAME","That I’m-the-boss-of-my-thoughts feeling is called in-charge. Your mind might think, “Caught you.” Your body might feel a firm grip, a calm head."],["FIND","When did you catch a mean or scary thought and send it away?"],["FORK","Beau could let the “you’ll fail” thought run wild, or catch it and bring it to Jesus. He caught it."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly, catch any wild thought, and hand the leash to Jesus. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me think like Jesus.”"]],"remember":"Bring every thought into captivity to Christ, catch the wild ones!","ask":["“What are we fighting, according to Paul?”","“What should we do with every thought?”","“What did Beau’s wild thought say before the test?”"],"share":["Share a thought you had to take captive, and what Christ replaced it with.","Which “imagination” or high thing in your mind most needs casting down?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who mastered their thinking with the Lord’s help."]},"b21-w3-n5":{"story":["Paul had a problem he called “a thorn in the flesh.” He did not say what it was. It hurt and kept bothering him, like a thorn stuck in your skin.","Three times Paul begged the Lord to take it away. He prayed and prayed.","But the Lord did not take it away. Instead He said, “My grace is sufficient for thee.” His loving help would be enough.","The Lord said His strength shows best when we are weak. So Paul was glad, even with the thorn. He said, “When I am weak, then am I strong.” Jesus’s strength held him up.","Is something hard for you that will not go away? Tonight, tell Jesus about it. His strength is enough for you too."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” Jesus did not take Paul’s thorn away, but said, “My grace is enough.” Being held while it hurts is warm help. In Willow’s world today: Willow stutters, and she prays every night for it to go away. It doesn’t. One Sunday she stutters through her whole talk, and the room is kind. Dad says, “You were strong up there.” Willow nods. “Jesus held the rest.” Just like Paul, Willow learned that when we are weak, Jesus’s grace is enough and holds us up.","feel":"A thorn is stuck in your hand, ouch, try to pull it out, three times! It stays. Now feel two strong arms hold you up anyway. Stand tall in those arms. Can you show me your held-up stand?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul begged for his thorn to go, but the Lord said, “My grace is sufficient.” Paul felt held."],["NAME","That held-while-it-hurts feeling is called held. Your mind might think, “He fills me.” Your body might feel leaning shoulders, a steady heart."],["FIND","When did something hard stay, but you felt held while you got through it?"],["FORK","Paul could be angry that the thorn stayed, or let Jesus’s strength fill his weakness. He let Jesus fill it."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and let Jesus’s strength hold you up. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, Your grace is enough for me.”"]],"remember":"My grace is sufficient for you, when I am weak, then am I strong!","ask":["“What did Paul call his problem?”","“What did the Lord say to Paul instead of taking it away?”","“What did Willow say after her talk?”"],"share":["Share a thorn the Lord did not remove, and how His grace was enough.","Where does your weakness leave room for Christ’s power right now?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who carried a lifelong thorn with grace."]},"b21-w3-n6":{"story":["Some people in Corinth bragged about how great they were. So Paul made a list too. But his list was full of hard things.","He listed what happened while he told people about Jesus. “Thrice I suffered shipwreck,” he wrote. Three times, his ship broke apart in the sea!","Once Paul was in the deep sea a whole night and day.","Paul faced danger by rivers, by robbers, and in cities. He was often tired, hungry, thirsty and cold. He had every reason to quit, but he never did. Each time, he kept going and told more people about Jesus.","Paul’s list really showed how much he loved the Lord. When something is hard tomorrow, try again. Keep going, just like Paul."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big faith, brave heart!” Paul was shipwrecked three times, floated a night and a day in the sea, and kept going. Bravest list ever. In Beau’s world today: Beau is learning to swim. He sinks and sputters three lessons in a row. On the fourth he wants to quit. He thinks of Paul climbing out of the sea again and gets back in. By the end he floats on his back, grinning at the sky. Just like Paul, Beau learned to climb out of the water and keep going.","feel":"Rock on a ship, then crash, shipwreck! Swim, swim, float on your back all night. Now climb out onto the shore, shake off the water, and march to the next town! Can you show me your climb-out march?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul was shipwrecked, hungry, cold, and in danger many times, and he kept carrying the gospel. He felt persevering."],["NAME","That I’ll-get-up-and-go-again feeling is called persevering. Your mind might think, “One more time.” Your body might feel tired legs that keep moving."],["FIND","When did you fail, try again, and finally make it?"],["FORK","Paul could quit after the third shipwreck, or climb out and keep going. He kept going."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly like floating on calm water after a long swim. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, give me strength to keep going.”"]],"remember":"Three times shipwrecked, and Paul kept going for Jesus!","ask":["“How many times was Paul shipwrecked?”","“What did Paul do after each shipwreck?”","“What did Beau do on the fourth swim lesson?”"],"share":["Share a “shipwreck” you climbed out of to keep serving the Lord.","What is your perseverance list, and what does it say about your love for Him?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who persevered through hardship for the gospel."]},"b21-w3-n7":{"story":["Paul was finishing his letter to his friends in Corinth. He worried that some of them were quarreling. So he gave them a test, but not with paper and pencil.","He said, “Examine yourselves.” He wanted each one to look closely inside their own heart. Each one could ask, “Is Jesus really in me?”","Then Paul said goodbye. “Be of good comfort, be of one mind, live in peace.”","He wanted them to cheer each other up and work together as one. Then came a sweet promise. Heavenly Father, the Father of love and peace, would be with them.","Where there is love and peace, Heavenly Father comes close. Tonight, say something kind to each person in your home."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see!” Paul said, “Examine yourselves,” look inside and check. Scout checked, and found Jesus there, and peace. In Scout’s world today: At family council, everyone is grumpy. Scout says, “Paul said to be of one mind and live in peace, and then Heavenly Father comes.” Dad laughs. “Okay, Scout. Let’s try it.” They talk kindly, agree on chores, and the room feels lighter. “He’s here,” Scout whispers. Just like Paul promised, Scout learned that where there is love and peace, Heavenly Father is with us.","feel":"Look inside with a pretend magnifying glass, examine, examine. Found Jesus! Now wave goodbye like Paul, and put your hands together: one mind. Breathe out, peace. Can you show me your peace breath?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul said examine yourselves, be of one mind, live in peace, and Heavenly Father will be with you. Scout felt peaceful."],["NAME","That calm-and-He’s-here feeling is called peaceful. Your mind might think, “We’re together.” Your body might feel soft shoulders, a slow heart."],["FIND","When did your family stop arguing and feel peace come into the room?"],["FORK","We can stay grumpy and divided, or be of one mind and live in peace. Live in peace."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel the Father of love and peace right here. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You for being with us.”"]],"remember":"Live in peace, and the Heavenly Father of love and peace shall be with you!","ask":["“What did Paul tell the Corinthians to examine?”","“Who will be with us when we live in peace?”","“What did Scout say at family council?”"],"share":["Share a time you examined yourself and found the Lord was truly there.","What would “one mind” look like in your home this week?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor whose home was a place of love and peace."]},"b21-w4-n1":{"story":["Paul wrote to the believers in Galatia, a land of hills and small towns. He had taught them about Jesus. Now some people were teaching them wrong things.","So Paul reminded them where his message came from. No man taught it to him. Jesus Christ Himself showed it to him.","Then Paul wrote something beautiful. “I live; yet not I, but Christ liveth in me.”","Paul’s old self, the one who once hurt Jesus’s followers, was gone. Now Paul did not live by his own power. Jesus lived in him. Paul said Jesus “loved me, and gave himself for me.”","Jesus loves you and gave Himself for you too. Tonight, put your hand on your heart and say, “Jesus, live in me.”"],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer says, “Wow, look at that!” Paul said, “Not I, but Christ lives in me.” Wren thinks having Jesus inside must feel like light. In Wren’s world today: Wren’s balloon glows in the dark when she puts a little light inside it. “It’s the same balloon,” she says, “but now it shines.” Dad says, “That’s what Paul meant. Same Wren, but with Jesus inside, you shine.” Wren carries the balloon to bed. Just like Paul, Wren learned that when Christ lives in us, we are the same person, but shining.","feel":"Make yourself dull and dark, old self, droop. Now feel Jesus come live inside, and light up slowly, glow, glow, glow! Stand tall and shine from your middle. Can you show me your Christ-lives-in-me glow?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul said, “Not I, but Christ lives in me.” Jesus loved him and gave Himself for him. Wren felt filled."],["NAME","That Jesus-is-inside-me feeling is called filled. Your mind might think, “I’m not alone in here.” Your body might feel a warm glow from your middle."],["FIND","When did you feel like Jesus was helping you from the inside?"],["FORK","We can run on our own power, or let Christ live in us. Let Him live in you."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel Jesus’s light glowing in your middle. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, let Jesus live in me.”"]],"remember":"Not I, but Christ lives in me, He loved me and gave Himself for me!","ask":["“Who gave Paul the gospel he preached?”","“Who did Paul say lives in him?”","“What happened when Wren put a light inside her balloon?”"],"share":["Share a moment you knew it was Christ living in you, not your own strength.","What part of your “old self” needs to stay on the cross this week?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor in whom you could see Christ living."]},"b21-w4-n2":{"story":["Paul told the Galatians they were all Heavenly Father’s children by faith in Jesus. Not just some of them. All of them!","He said when people are baptized, they “put on Christ.” It is like putting on a new coat that makes you look like Jesus.","Back then, people built walls between each other. They were not walls of stone, but walls in their hearts. Jews and Greeks stayed apart. Servants and masters stayed apart.","But Paul said those walls do not matter in Jesus’s family. “Ye are all one in Christ Jesus.” Boys and girls, servants and masters, Jews and Greeks: everyone belongs. Nobody is left out.","Tomorrow, look for someone sitting all alone. Smile and say, “You can sit with me.”"],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see!” Paul said no Jew or Greek, no servant or master, nobody ahead of anyone, because we are all one in Christ. Scout looked and found no walls. In Scout’s world today: At the new school, kids sit in groups: the sports kids, the quiet kids, the kids who speak Spanish. Scout sits in the middle of the room. “Anyone can sit here.” By Friday, his table is the fullest. Just like Paul taught, Scout learned that in Christ all the walls come down, and everyone is one.","feel":"Build a pretend wall, stack, stack. Push it over, crash! Build another, crash! Put on a big robe of Christ. Now join hands in a circle with everyone. Can you show me your all-one circle?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul said there is no Jew or Greek, bond or free, male or female, for all are one in Christ Jesus. Scout felt included."],["NAME","That nobody-is-left-out feeling is called included. Your mind might think, “We all belong.” Your body might feel open arms and a settled chest."],["FIND","When did someone make room for you in a group where you felt different?"],["FORK","We can sit with only people like us, or make a table where everyone belongs. Make the table."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel wrapped in the robe of Christ with everyone. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that I belong.”"]],"remember":"You are all one in Christ Jesus, nobody left out!","ask":["“What did Paul say we put on when we are baptized?”","“Name one of the walls Paul said does not matter in Christ.”","“Where did Scout sit in the cafeteria?”"],"share":["Share a time the walls came down and you felt truly one with other believers.","Which “Jew or Greek” wall still stands somewhere in your heart?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who refused to let a wall divide people."]},"b21-w4-n3":{"story":["Paul wrote to the Galatians about two voices inside every person. One voice wants selfish, wrong things. The other is the Spirit, the Holy Ghost, who whispers what is right.","Paul said these two pull opposite ways, like two friends tugging a rope.","So which way should we go? Paul said, “Walk in the Spirit.”","To walk in the Spirit is to let the Holy Ghost lead each step. It is like holding a guide’s hand on a trail. Paul said if we live in the Spirit, we should walk in Him too.","Tomorrow, when you hear two voices, listen for the quiet, kind one. Let the Holy Ghost choose your steps."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau the Brave says, “Big faith, brave heart!” Paul said two voices pull two ways, and the brave thing is to walk with the Spirit, step by step. In Beau’s world today: At the store, a toy falls out of a cart and nobody sees. One voice says, “Keep it.” A quieter voice says, “Give it back.” Beau follows the quiet one and hands the toy to the surprised dad. He walks out feeling light. Just like Paul taught, Beau learned to walk in the Spirit by following the quiet voice step by step.","feel":"Feel a pull to the left, flesh! Feel a gentle pull to the right, Spirit! Now take the Spirit’s hand and walk, step, step, step, the right way. Can you show me your Spirit walk?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul said two voices pull two ways, but if we walk in the Spirit, we are led right. Beau felt led."],["NAME","That the-quiet-voice-is-guiding-me feeling is called led. Your mind might think, “This way.” Your body might feel a gentle tug and steady feet."],["FIND","When did you hear a quiet voice tell you the right way, and follow it?"],["FORK","Beau could follow the “keep it” voice, or walk with the Spirit and give it back. He gave it back."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel the Spirit’s gentle hand leading you. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, lead me by Your Spirit.”"]],"remember":"Walk in the Spirit, step by step, every day!","ask":["“What two voices pull inside every person?”","“What does it mean to walk in the Spirit?”","“What did Beau do with the toy at the store?”"],"share":["Share a time you felt led by the Spirit in a small, everyday choice.","Where does “the flesh” pull hardest, and what helps you walk in the Spirit?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who walked by the Spirit’s leading."]},"b21-w4-n4":{"story":["Paul wanted the Galatians to know if the Holy Ghost was leading them. He said to look at the fruit.","You can tell what kind of tree a tree is by its fruit. Apples grow on an apple tree. Peaches grow on a peach tree.","Paul said the Holy Ghost grows good fruit in us too. “The fruit of the Spirit is love, joy, peace,” he wrote.","There is more: being patient, gentle, good, faithful, humble, and using self-control. No rule says you can’t do these good things! A tree does not push hard to make apples. It stays rooted and drinks, and the fruit comes.","Stay close to the Holy Ghost, and good fruit will grow. Which fruit did you grow today?"],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer says, “Wow, look at that!” Paul listed nine fruits that grow on a person who walks with the Spirit. Wren wondered which one was ripest on her. In Wren’s world today: Wren draws a tree with nine fruits and writes a word on each. Each night she colors in the one she grew that day. By Friday, peace is the brightest. “Peace is my ripest,” she says. Just like Paul taught, Wren learned that when the Spirit lives in us, good fruit grows like on a tree.","feel":"Stand like a tree, feet planted. Drink up the water. Now grow fruit on your branches: love, joy, peace, pop them out one by one on your fingers! Can you show me your fruit tree?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul said the fruit of the Spirit is love, joy, peace, patience, gentleness, and more. Wren felt fruitful."],["NAME","That good-things-grow-on-me feeling is called fruitful. Your mind might think, “The Spirit grows me.” Your body might feel rooted feet, reaching arms."],["FIND","Which fruit of the Spirit grew on you today?"],["FORK","We can try to force ourselves to be good, or stay rooted in the Spirit and let fruit grow. Stay rooted."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly like a tree drinking water through its roots. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, grow love, joy, and peace in me.”"]],"remember":"The fruit of the Spirit is love, joy, peace, and more, growing on you!","ask":["“How do you know what kind of tree a tree is?”","“Name three fruits of the Spirit.”","“Which fruit was brightest on Wren’s tree?”"],"share":["Share which fruit of the Spirit the Lord has grown most in you over the years.","Which fruit is smallest on your tree right now, and what would help it grow?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who was full of one of these fruits."]},"b21-w4-n5":{"story":["Paul taught the Galatians what to do when someone does something wrong. Do not laugh at them or be mean. Gently help them get back up.","Why be gentle? Paul said to remember that you could slip too. Everyone makes mistakes sometimes.","Then Paul said, “Bear ye one another’s burdens.”","A burden is a heavy load, like a big, full backpack. It can be a sad feeling or a hard job. When someone bends under a heavy load, you can help carry it. That is how Jesus wants us to live.","Is someone you know feeling sad or tired? Tomorrow, ask them, “Can I help you carry it?”"],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow the Warm says, “Warm hugs, warm hearts!” Paul said bear one another’s burdens, and that is the whole law of Christ. Willow thinks it is the best law ever. In Willow’s world today: Willow’s friend Ellie’s dog ran away. Willow can’t find the dog, but she can carry part of the sad. She makes signs with Ellie and walks the block. The dog comes home that night. “You carried it with me,” Ellie says. Just like Paul taught, Willow learned that getting under someone’s heavy load is the law of Christ.","feel":"See a friend bent over under a huge heavy backpack. Walk over and get under it with them, lift! Now walk together, sharing the weight, step, step. Can you show me your burden lift?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul said bear one another’s burdens, and so fulfill the law of Christ. Willow felt supportive."],["NAME","That I’ll-carry-it-with-you feeling is called supportive. Your mind might think, “Not alone.” Your body might feel strong shoulders, a steady arm."],["FIND","When did someone help carry something heavy for you, a job or a sad feeling?"],["FORK","We can watch someone struggle under a burden, or get under it with them. Get under it."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and think of one burden you can help carry. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, make my shoulders strong.”"]],"remember":"Bear one another’s burdens, and so fulfill the law of Christ!","ask":["“What is a burden?”","“How should we help someone caught in a fault?”","“What did Willow do when Ellie’s dog ran away?”"],"share":["Share a time someone bore your burden and it felt like Christ Himself.","Whose burden could you get under this week?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who was known for carrying others’ burdens."]},"b21-w4-n6":{"story":["Paul gave the Galatians a farming rule. “Whatsoever a man soweth, that shall he also reap.” Whatever you plant is what will grow. You cannot plant weeds and pick strawberries!","If we plant selfish things, only bad things grow. If we plant good with the Holy Ghost, life that lasts forever grows.","But doing good can make us tired, when nothing seems to happen.","So Paul said, “Let us not be weary in well doing.” Keep doing good! “In due season we shall reap, if we faint not.” The good will grow at the right time, if we do not give up.","A farmer plants in spring and waits for the harvest. Tomorrow, keep doing one good thing, even if nothing seems to happen yet."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout the Seeker says, “Look and see!” Paul said you reap what you sow, and the harvest comes in due season. Scout checks the garden every morning. The strawberries are coming. In Scout’s world today: Scout plants strawberries and checks every day. Nothing. He waters anyway. His sister laughs, “They’ll never grow.” In June, red berries appear. He picks a bowl and shares it with her. “Due season,” he says. She eats one. “Okay, I was wrong.” Just like Paul taught, Scout learned that if you plant good seeds and don’t quit, the harvest comes.","feel":"Plant good seeds in a row, poke, poke, poke. Water them, pour, pour. Wait, yawn, so tired, but don’t quit! Now reap the harvest, pick, pick, pick, fill your basket! Can you show me your harvest pick?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul said we reap what we sow, so don’t be weary in well doing, for the harvest comes in due season. Scout felt hopeful."],["NAME","That the-good-is-coming feeling is called hopeful. Your mind might think, “Keep watering.” Your body might feel patient hands and eyes on the horizon."],["FIND","When did you keep doing something good and finally see it pay off?"],["FORK","Scout could stop watering when nothing grew, or keep going until due season. He kept going."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and picture the good you planted growing in the dark. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me not give up.”"]],"remember":"Whatever you sow, you reap, so don’t be weary in doing good!","ask":["“What does it mean to sow?”","“What happens if we don’t faint, or give up, in doing good?”","“What did Scout plant, and when did it grow?”"],"share":["Share a harvest you reaped long after you sowed in faith.","Where are you weary in well doing, and what reminds you the season is coming?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who sowed good for years before seeing the harvest."]},"b21-w4-n7":{"story":["Some people told the Galatians they must keep many old rules to be saved. Paul said no! Jesus had set them free.","Paul wrote, “Stand fast in the liberty wherewith Christ hath made us free.” Do not let anyone put a heavy yoke back on you. A yoke is the wooden bar oxen wear to pull heavy loads.","But what is freedom for? Paul’s answer is a surprise.","Freedom is not for being selfish. Paul said, “By love serve one another.” All the rules fit inside one: “Thou shalt love thy neighbour as thyself.”","Jesus set you free, and free hands can help. Tomorrow, choose one way to help someone you love."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren the Wonderer says, “Wow, look at that!” Paul said Jesus made us free, and the best thing to do with freedom is serve by love. Now Wren knows. In Wren’s world today: It’s Saturday and Wren has no chores, no school. She is totally free. She sees Grandma pulling weeds alone. Wren runs out and kneels beside her. “I’m free today, so I chose this.” Grandma hands her a trowel and a smile. Just like Paul taught, Wren learned that the best use of freedom is choosing to serve with love.","feel":"Stoop under a heavy yoke, so heavy! Now feel Jesus lift it off, stand up straight, free! Stretch out your free hands, then use them to help a friend. Can you show me your free-to-serve hands?","stones":[["SPOT","Paul said Christ made us free, so we should use our liberty to serve one another by love. Wren felt free."],["NAME","That nothing-holds-me-down feeling is called free. Your mind might think, “I get to choose.” Your body might feel light shoulders and open hands."],["FIND","When did you have a free day and choose to help someone with it?"],["FORK","Wren could use her free day only for herself, or serve Grandma by love. She chose to serve."],["CALM","Breathe in slowly and feel the yoke lifted off your shoulders. Whisper, “Heavenly Father, thank You that Jesus made me free.”"]],"remember":"Christ made you free, so by love serve one another!","ask":["“What is a yoke?”","“What should we do with our freedom?”","“What did Wren choose to do on her free Saturday?”"],"share":["Share how the freedom Christ gives has changed the way you serve.","Is there a “yoke” you keep putting back on that Christ already removed?","Tell about a grandparent or ancestor who used their freedom to serve others."]},"b10-w1-n1":{"story":["Isaiah wrote about how the Lord takes care of His people. He used a picture everyone in his land knew.","Isaiah said, “He shall feed His flock like a shepherd.” A good shepherd finds green grass and cool water for his sheep.","Baby lambs have short, wobbly legs. So the shepherd gathers the lambs in his arms. He carries them close to his heart.","He walks slowly and gently with the mother sheep too. Isaiah was talking about Jesus! Years later, Jesus said, “I am the good shepherd.” He knows each one of His sheep.","You are one of Jesus’s little lambs. When you feel tired or small, He holds you close. Tonight, you can feel safe in His love."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow fits tonight because comfort is her gift, and this story is Heavenly Father comforting His tired people. In Willow’s world today: her cousin cries when her block tower falls over. Willow does not fix it. She sits down close and pats her back until the crying goes quiet. Just like the Shepherd gathering the lambs in His arms, Willow learned that being held is the very first comfort.","feel":"Curl up small like a little lamb in the grass. Now let me scoop you into my arms and hold you close to my heart, the way the Shepherd does. Can you show me how a lamb rests?","stones":[["SPOT","Heavenly Father's people were worn out and sad far from home, and the very first thing He sent them was a soft word: comfort."],["NAME","That tired, I-need-to-be-held feeling is called weary. Your mind might think, \"I can't take one more step.\" Your body might feel heavy and floppy, and your eyes might want to close all by themselves."],["FIND","When did your body feel so tired or so sad that you wanted somebody to pick you up? Willow saw her cousin's block tower fall down and watched his face go sad. When have you seen somebody's face go sad like that?"],["FORK","Willow had two roads. One road: keep playing and let him sit sad by himself. The other road: go sit close and say something soft. Willow took the soft road, and she helped build the tower again. That is what a shepherd does with a tired little lamb."],["CALM","Put your hand flat on your heart and feel it going bump, bump. Take one slow breath. Now whisper, \"Heavenly Father, please hold me close tonight, and help me be soft with someone tomorrow.\""]],"remember":"Jesus is the gentle Shepherd, and He carries His littlest lambs!","ask":["“What soft word did Heavenly Father send first to His tired, sad people?”","“Who is the gentle Shepherd that Isaiah was talking about?”","“What did Willow do when her cousin's block tower fell down?”"],"share":["When has the Lord carried you through a season you could not walk through alone? Tell about it.","How do you comfort your child before you correct them? Tell about a moment you chose holding first.","Did a grandparent or ancestor have a gentle, gathering way with tired people? Describe that quality."]},"b10-w1-n2":{"story":["Long ago, Heavenly Father's people had been taken far away from home, to a land called Babylon. They missed their houses and their hills. All around them were big armies and big kings, and the people felt very small. So Heavenly Father sent a man named Isaiah to help them remember something true.","Isaiah asked them big, happy questions. Who could hold all the water in the whole world in one hand? Who could put a whole mountain on a scale — that is the thing we use to see how heavy something is? Who spread the sky out over us like a big tent? Nobody could do that. Nobody but Heavenly Father.","Some of the people had stopped asking Heavenly Father for help. They had made little statues out of wood and gold, because they wanted a god they could see with their eyes. But a statue cannot hear you. A statue cannot help you. The workers even had to nail it down so it would not tip over! So Isaiah said, 'Look up at the stars!' There are more stars than anyone could ever count, and Heavenly Father made every single one. He brings them out at night, and He calls each one by its own name.","Isaiah was saying: Heavenly Father is so big He can hold the oceans, and so kind He knows the name of every tiny star. Not one star is ever lost or forgotten. And you are far more precious to Him than a star. He knows your name, and He knows your face, and He knows what happened to you today.","Wren learned that out on the driveway after dinner. She lay on her back and looked up, and the sky went on and on and on, and she felt like the smallest thing in the whole world. Then she remembered: the One who names every star is the same One who knows her name. Tonight, if you feel small, you can look up and remember that too."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren fits tonight because she is the one who stops and ponders how big Heavenly Father is. In Wren’s world today: she lies on the driveway after dinner with her coral red jacket zipped up. She counts nine stars, loses count, and starts over again. She asks if the stars have names, and her mom says every one. Just like Isaiah asking the people to lift up their eyes, Wren learned that wondering is a kind of praying.","feel":"Reach both arms up and open your hands wide, like you are holding the whole night sky. Now point one finger at a star only you can see. Can you show me how many stars you can count?","stones":[["SPOT","Wren lay on the driveway after dinner and looked up at all those stars, and the sky felt so big that she felt like the tiniest speck in it. Isaiah's people felt that way too, far from home."],["NAME","That feeling is called feeling small. Your mind might think, 'I am so little — does anybody even see me?' Your body might go very quiet and still, with your eyes wide open, and a tiny squeezy feeling in your tummy."],["FIND","When did something feel so big that it made you feel little? Maybe a tall building, or the ocean, or a room full of grown-ups you did not know?"],["FORK","Wren could feel lost and forgotten because she was so small — or she could remember that Heavenly Father names every star and knows her name too. Wren chose to remember, and the squeezy tummy feeling went soft and warm."],["CALM","Open your hands on top of your blanket, like you are holding something gentle. Take one slow breath in, and one slow breath out. Then whisper, 'Heavenly Father, thank you for knowing my name.'"]],"remember":"The One who names every star already knows your name by heart!","ask":["“Who can hold all the water in the whole world in one hand?”","“What does Heavenly Father give to every single star?”","“What was Wren doing on the driveway after dinner, and how did she feel?”"],"share":["When did the size of creation last make you feel small in a good way? Tell about it.","How do you make room for your child’s questions instead of answering too fast? Tell about a moment.","Did a grandparent or ancestor carry a quiet, pondering kind of faith? Describe that quality."]},"b10-w1-n3":{"story":["Sometimes the Lord’s people felt tired and weak. They wanted to give up. So Isaiah reminded them of something wonderful.","Isaiah said the Lord made the whole earth, and He never gets tired. He gives strength to people who feel weak.","Even strong young people get tired, Isaiah said. But those who wait on the Lord, trusting Him, get new strength.","Isaiah said they “shall mount up with wings as eagles.” An eagle spreads its big wings and lets the wind lift it high. That is how the Lord lifts us. We can run and not get tired.","When your legs or your heart feel tired, say a little prayer. Jesus can lift you up, like wind under an eagle’s wings."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau fits tonight because waiting for strength you do not have yet takes real courage. In Beau’s world today: the walk home from the park is long and his legs ache. He wants to be carried. He takes three slow breaths, holds his dad’s hand, and finds one more step, and then one more. Just like the tired people who waited on the Lord and rose up like eagles, Beau learned that new strength comes to the ones who wait.","feel":"Stretch both arms out wide like eagle wings and lift them up slow, slow, slow. Now let them float back down to the blanket. Can you show me how an eagle rises without flapping?","stones":[["SPOT","Isaiah's people were worn all the way out, far from home, with a long road still in front of them."],["NAME","That empty-tank, legs-too-heavy feeling is called weary. Your mind might think, \"I can't do it anymore.\" Your body might feel slow and droopy, with legs that want to sit right down in the dust."],["FIND","When did your body run all the way out of go, and you still had further to walk? Maybe like Beau on the long walk home."],["FORK","One road is to stay down in the dust and give up. The other road is to wait on the Lord and ask Him for help. Isaiah promised that the ones who wait get new strength, like wings."],["CALM","Put your hand on your tummy, take one slow breath in, and whisper, \"Heavenly Father, please give me strength for one more step.\""]],"remember":"Heavenly Father never runs out of strength, and He shares it with tired little legs!","ask":["“Does Heavenly Father ever get tired?”","“What does an eagle do instead of flapping and flapping?”","“How did Beau find one more step on the long walk home?”"],"share":["When did strength arrive in you that was clearly not your own? Tell about it.","How do you teach a child to wait rather than quit? Tell about a moment you saw it work.","Did a grandparent or ancestor keep going long past their own strength? Describe that quality."]},"b10-w1-n4":{"story":["A long, long time ago, Heavenly Father's people had to leave their homes and live far away in a land called Babylon. They were tired and sad, and they wondered if Heavenly Father had forgotten them. So He sent a prophet named Isaiah to tell them something kind. A prophet is a friend of Heavenly Father who tells people what He wants them to know.","Isaiah told them a word picture. He said: think of people walking in a desert. A desert is a hot, sandy place with no water. Their mouths were dry and they looked and looked and could not find one drink. Isaiah used that picture because that dry, empty feeling was just how their hearts felt, far away from home.","Then Heavenly Father said the happy part: \"When the poor and thirsty people look for water, I will hear them. I will not leave them alone.\" He promised rivers running right over the tops of the hills, and springs — that is water bubbling up out of the ground — in the low places between the hills. He promised to turn the hot sand into a pool of water. In a dry land, water means staying alive, so this was the very best promise there is.","Heavenly Father also promised to plant tall green trees out in the sand. Trees in a desert are like a big sign, so everyone who walked by would stop and say, \"Only Heavenly Father could do that!\" Isaiah said the Holy One of Israel made it happen — and the Holy One of Israel is Jesus. Isaiah was talking about Jesus, who helps Heavenly Father take care of us.","Scout found that out at the park. Scout ran all the way to the drinking fountain, and the fountain was broken. No water came out at all. That was Scout's little desert — and Heavenly Father knew about it."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout fits tonight because he keeps looking, and this story is about seeking and being heard. In Scout’s world today: his water bottle runs empty at the park and the fountain is broken. He does not sit down and wail. He walks the whole long path in his bright green shoes until he finds another fountain, low and cold and perfect. Just like the thirsty people Heavenly Father heard, Scout learned that seeking gets answered.","feel":"Cup both your hands together like a little bowl and hold them up. Now pretend cool water is filling them all the way to the top. Can you show me how you would drink from your own hands?","stones":[["SPOT","Scout pushed the fountain button again and again, and no water came out. The people in Isaiah's story looked and looked for water too, and found none."],["NAME","That is called disappointed — when you want something and it just isn't there. Your mind might think, \"I need this and I can't get it.\" Your body might feel a dry sticky mouth, hot cheeks, and wiggly, grumpy legs."],["FIND","When did you want something so much, and it wasn't there? Maybe the last cracker was gone, or your friend couldn't come over."],["FORK","Scout could sit down and stay grumpy, or Scout could tell Heavenly Father about it and then go ask Mom. Scout chose to say a little prayer and ask — and Mom had a water bottle in her bag. Heavenly Father heard, just like He said He would."],["CALM","Cup your two hands together like a little bowl. Take one slow breath into your hands, and whisper, \"Heavenly Father, I need help. Thank you for hearing me.\""]],"remember":"Heavenly Father hears me, even in my dry places!","ask":["“Why were Heavenly Father's people so sad and far from home?”","“What did Heavenly Father promise to put in the hot, dry sand?”","“What did Scout do when the park fountain was broken?”"],"share":["When did help arrive in a season of your life that felt completely dry? Tell about it.","How do you help your child keep asking instead of giving up? Tell about a moment they tried again.","Did a grandparent or ancestor keep hoping all through a dry season? Describe that quality."]},"b10-w1-n5":{"story":["Isaiah wrote the Lord’s words for His people. Hard times would come, and they might feel all alone.","The Lord said, “Fear not. I have called you by your name. You are Mine.” He knows each person, and He loves them.","The Lord said, “When you pass through the waters, I will be with you. The rivers will not cover you.”","Crossing a deep river can feel scary. So Isaiah used the river as a picture of hard times. The Lord who spoke was Jesus. He promised to be with them in every hard time.","Jesus knows your name too. Tonight, say softly, “I am His, and He loves me.”"],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau fits tonight because walking into deep water takes courage, and courage means knowing Someone is with you. In Beau’s world today: the swim teacher asks him to step off the pool stair. His stomach flips over. He looks up, finds his mom’s eyes, hears her say his name, and lets go of the wall. Just like the people who heard Fear not, I have called you by name, Beau learned that a loving voice is braver than the wall.","feel":"Wiggle your fingers like little waves in front of you. Now let me hold your hand and walk our fingers right through the middle of them. Can you show me how brave hands walk through water?","stones":[["SPOT","The people stood looking at a long, hard road home. Beau stood in the pool holding the wall, looking at the deep part."],["NAME","That tight-tummy, please-not-yet feeling is called afraid. Your mind might think, \"Something bad is going to happen to me.\" Your body might feel tight in your tummy, and your heart might go fast, and your hands might squeeze hard."],["FIND","When did something look too deep or too big for you? Maybe the water, or a big slide, or a new room full of kids you did not know."],["FORK","Two roads. One: stay frozen at the edge and hold the wall. Two: take one small step, knowing Heavenly Father is right there with you. The people heard \"Do not be afraid\" and they went. Beau let go of the wall with Daddy's hands close."],["CALM","Squeeze my hand one time, let your shoulders go soft, and whisper, \"Heavenly Father, please walk with me.\""]],"remember":"Heavenly Father knows my name, and He stays with me in the deep parts!","ask":["“What did Heavenly Father say first, before He talked about the water?”","“Did the people have to walk the long road all by themselves?”","“What helped Beau let go of the pool wall?”"],"share":["When did the Lord meet you inside the hard thing rather than around it? Tell about it.","How do you use your child’s name to steady them? Tell about a moment your voice was enough.","Did a grandparent or ancestor walk through deep water with their faith intact? Describe that quality."]},"b10-w1-n6":{"story":["Once the Lord’s people felt sad and all alone. They said, “The Lord has forgotten me.”","The Lord answered with a question: “Can a mother forget her baby?” Then He said, “Even if she could, I will not forget you.”","He said He had carved them on the palms of His hands. That is the inside of your hand, where you can always see it.","Latter-day prophets teach that this is about Jesus. After He died for us and rose again, His hands still had marks. Those marks show He remembers us always.","Look at your own little hand tonight. Jesus will never forget you. He remembers you always."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow fits tonight because she notices whoever feels left out. In Willow’s world today: a new boy stands alone by the fence during outside time. Willow walks over with a dandelion and sits down beside him without a word. When the bell rings, they walk inside together. Just like Heavenly Father telling His people He could never forget them, Willow learned that nobody stays forgotten once somebody comes and sits down.","feel":"Open both hands wide and look at your palms. Now trace a tiny circle in the middle of one hand with your finger, right where He can see you. Can you show me your open hands?","stones":[["SPOT","The people in Babylon thought Heavenly Father had left them behind and forgotten all about them."],["NAME","That left-behind, does-anybody-see-me feeling is called forgotten. Your mind might think, \"Nobody remembers me.\" Your body might feel small and droopy, with your shoulders down and your chin low."],["FIND","When did you feel like nobody remembered you were even there? Willow saw a boy standing alone at the fence and did not walk past him. Has anyone ever come and stood by you like that?"],["FORK","There were two roads. One road says, \"I am forgotten.\" The other road says, \"My name is carved in His hands.\" The people took the second road, and they sang again."],["CALM","Open both hands flat on your blanket, like Heavenly Father opening His. Take one slow breath, and whisper, \"Heavenly Father, thank you for remembering me.\""]],"remember":"Your name is carved into Heavenly Father's hands, and hands like that never forget!","ask":["“Isaiah said the sky and the mountains should sing.","What was the happy news?”","“What did Heavenly Father say was carved into the palms of His hands?”","“Willow went and stood by the boy at the fence.","Who could you stand by tomorrow?”"],"share":["When have you quietly believed the Lord had forgotten you? Tell about it.","How do you reach a child who feels overlooked at home? Tell about a moment you caught it in time.","Did a grandparent or ancestor have a way of making people feel remembered? Describe that quality."]},"b10-w1-n7":{"story":["Heavenly Father said to His people, “I am the One who teaches you what is good. I show you the way to go.” He also called Himself their Redeemer. A Redeemer is someone who saves you and brings you back home. Isaiah was talking about Jesus. Jesus is our Redeemer.","“If only you had listened to Me,” Heavenly Father said. “Then your peace would be like a river.” A river is not noisy or stuck. It just keeps going, soft and sure, all day long. That is what a listening heart feels like inside. These people had stopped listening, and so they were living far away from home in a land called Babylon, and they felt sad and stuck.","So Isaiah told them a story from long, long ago. Their great-great-grandmas and grandpas walked through the hot, dry desert with Moses, and Heavenly Father led them every single step. Isaiah told that old story on purpose, so the people would think, “He led them. He can lead us home too.”","Out in that dry desert there was no water anywhere. But the people were not thirsty, not once. Heavenly Father opened up a big rock, and water came pouring out for everybody to drink. Nobody knew there was water inside a rock. Heavenly Father knew.","Wren came to a corner and did not know which way to go. One way was fast. One way was the way she had been told. She stopped, and she listened, and she went the told way. Heavenly Father knows the way you should go tonight too. He knows where the water is hiding. When you listen, your heart can be quiet and going-along, like a river."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren fits tonight because she stops and listens before she moves. In Wren’s world today: her big sister says the shortcut goes left, but her grandpa said right. Wren stands still at the corner in her coral red boots and thinks about it. She waits, asks one more time, then takes the way grandpa said. Just like the people who were told to listen so their peace could be like a river, Wren learned that listening comes before walking.","feel":"Put one finger on your lips and listen to the quiet room. Now make the softest river sound you can with your breath, all the way out. Can you show me your listening ears?","stones":[["SPOT","The people stopped listening to Heavenly Father and ran their own way, and then they felt stuck and sad, far from home."],["NAME","That hurry-up, don't-tell-me feeling is called restless. Your mind might think, “I want to go NOW!” Your body might feel wiggly and hot, like your legs cannot hold still."],["FIND","When did you hurry off your own way, and after, wish you had stopped and listened first?"],["FORK","Two roads at the corner: run off fast on your own, or stop and listen for the right way. Wren stopped and listened. Heavenly Father led His people all the way home."],["CALM","Let your breath out long and slow, like a river going by. Then whisper, “Heavenly Father, I am listening.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father knows the way, and He can bring water right out of a rock!","ask":["“What did Heavenly Father say their peace could be like?”","“Where did the water come from out in the dry desert?”","“Which way did Wren choose at the corner?”"],"share":["When did listening change the direction of your life? Tell about it.","How do you invite obedience without demanding it? Tell about a moment your child chose to listen.","Did a grandparent or ancestor carry an unusual kind of peace? Describe that quality."]},"b10-w2-n1":{"story":["Isaiah wrote some words as if Jesus Himself were talking. Long before Jesus was born, Isaiah knew about Him.","In those words, Jesus says the Lord taught Him how to speak. He would know the right word for someone weary.","Weary means so tired that your body and heart need rest. Every morning, the Lord woke Jesus to listen and learn.","Long ago, people walked far on dusty roads and worked hard all day. A kind word helped a tired heart feel strong again. Jesus said, “Come unto Me, and I will give you rest.”","Your words can help a tired heart too. Tonight, give a hug and a kind word to someone who is tired."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau fits tonight because trusting in the dark takes quiet courage. In Beau’s world today: the hallway light burned out right at bedtime. Beau’s chest went tight, but he did not call out. He whispered to Heavenly Father and held his blanket until his breathing slowed. Just like Isaiah, who walked without light and trusted anyway, Beau learned that Heavenly Father stays close when we cannot see.","feel":"Set your face brave and still, the way Isaiah did. Now let your shoulders melt down soft into the pillow. Can you show me your brave face, and then your soft face?","stones":[["SPOT","The hallway light went out, and Beau was standing in the dark."],["NAME","That not-knowing-what-comes-next feeling is called uncertain. Your mind might think, \"What if I can't see the way?\" Your body might feel shaky, and your hands might want to grab on to something."],["FIND","When did you feel unsure because you could not see what was coming next?"],["FORK","Beau could panic in the dark, or he could hold still and trust Heavenly Father. He held still and whispered."],["CALM","Press one hand flat on your chest and whisper, \"Heavenly Father, please help me.\""]],"remember":"Jesus knows a gentle word for a tired heart, right when it needs one!","ask":["“Who is the Servant Isaiah was talking about?”","“What did Heavenly Father teach Jesus to say to people who were tired?”","“What did Beau do when the hallway light went out?”"],"share":["When have you had to keep walking without seeing the end of the path? Share that time with your child.","How do you steady a child when you have no answers yourself? Tell about a moment you offered comfort instead of an explanation.","Did a grandparent or ancestor keep faith through a long dark season? Describe that quality in them."]},"b10-w2-n2":{"story":["Isaiah was a prophet, and he told Heavenly Father's people something funny-sounding: “Look at the rock you were cut from.” Long ago, workers cut big blocks of stone out of one giant rock to build houses. So Isaiah meant: your family is the big rock, and you are a stone that came from it. Look back and remember them!","Then he said their names: Abraham and Sarah, their great-great-great-grandpa and grandma. Heavenly Father called Abraham when he was all by himself, just one man with no children. Then Heavenly Father blessed him, and his family grew and grew and grew. Isaiah said this so the people would remember: Heavenly Father can make something big out of something small.","The people needed to hear that, because they were far away from home in a land called Babylon, and their own city was broken down and empty. They felt small. So Isaiah promised them that the Lord would comfort them — comfort means He would hold them close and make the sad part softer. The Lord Isaiah was talking about is Jesus.","Isaiah said their dry, empty land — a wilderness, where nothing grows and nothing is green — would turn into a garden, as lovely as the Garden of Eden, the very first garden Heavenly Father ever made. There would be happiness, and thank-you songs, and singing.","You came from a family too, with grandmas and grandpas behind you, all the way back. Heavenly Father knows every single one of their names, and He knows yours. Before you sleep, say out loud one name from your family — a grandma, a grandpa, anyone — and say thank you to Heavenly Father for them."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren fits tonight because looking back gently teaches us who we are. In Wren’s world today: Wren found an old photograph of her great-grandma inside a box in the closet. She sat down on the rug and asked questions until dinner was ready. Then she carried the picture to the table to show everyone. Just like the people who looked back to Abraham and Sarah, Wren learned that families are a rock we can stand on.","feel":"Sit up tall and steady like a big rock that will not wobble. Now open your arms wide like a garden growing. Can you show me your strong rock, and then your growing garden?","stones":[["SPOT","Heavenly Father's people were far from home and felt small and dried up, like a wilderness where nothing grows."],["NAME","That empty, nothing-is-growing feeling is called discouraged. Your mind might think, “Nothing I try is working.” Your body might feel slumped and slow and heavy, like a balloon letting its air out."],["FIND","When did you try and try, and it still did not work? Maybe a zipper, or a tower that kept falling down."],["FORK","They could forget where they came from and stay sad, or they could look back and remember what Heavenly Father did for Abraham and Sarah. Isaiah asked them to look back and remember."],["CALM","Trace a slow circle on your palm with your finger and whisper, “Heavenly Father, You know my family, and You know me.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father can turn a dry, empty place into a garden full of singing!","ask":["“Whose names did Isaiah tell the people to look back and remember?”","“What did Heavenly Father promise the dry, empty land would become?”","“What is one name from your family you can thank Heavenly Father for tonight?”"],"share":["What dry season of your life later bloomed into something good? Share that story with your child.","How do you hand family memory down to little ones? Tell about a moment a family story changed how your child saw themselves.","Did a grandparent or ancestor plant something you are still harvesting today? Describe that quality in them."]},"b10-w2-n3":{"story":["Long ago, there were no phones or cars. Important news came with a messenger who ran on foot from town to town.","People waited and waited to hear the news. Would it be happy news or sad news?","Isaiah said the feet that bring good news over the mountains are beautiful! That news tells of peace.","Why feet? Dusty, tired feet carried the happy news to the people. The best good news is about Jesus. The Book of Mormon teaches this is about Jesus and all who share Him.","Your feet can carry good news too. Tonight, tell someone you love, “Jesus loves you!”"],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout fits tonight because good news is meant to be carried, not kept. In Scout’s world today: Scout found out the classroom hamster had babies. He could not hold it in for one second. He ran to every table in the room, and then straight out to the parking lot to tell his mom. Just like the runner on the mountains, Scout learned that happy news grows bigger when we share it.","feel":"March your feet under the blanket like you are running up a mountain. Then cup your hands around your mouth and call out the happy news. Can you show me your running feet?","stones":[["SPOT","The watchmen on the wall saw the good news coming, and they could not stay quiet. And Scout saw the baby hamsters and ran."],["NAME","That bursting, I-have-to-tell-somebody feeling is called joyful. Your mind might think, \"I have to tell someone right now!\" Your body might feel bouncy and light, with a big smile you cannot hold in."],["FIND","When did you have news so happy you ran to tell someone? Who did you run to?"],["FORK","The watchmen could have stayed quiet on the wall, or they could lift their voices and sing. They sang. Scout could have kept the hamsters a secret, or share the happy news. Scout shared."],["CALM","Take one big breath in, then hum it slowly back out. Whisper, \"Thank You, Heavenly Father, for good news.\""]],"remember":"Feet that carry kind news are beautiful to Heavenly Father!","ask":["“What was the runner carrying over the mountains?”","“What did the watchmen do when they saw the good news coming?”","“Who did Scout run outside to tell about the baby hamsters?”"],"share":["When did somebody else’s good news lift a heavy day for you? Share that memory with your child.","How do you make your home a place where news gets shared gladly? Tell about a moment your family celebrated together.","Did a grandparent or ancestor carry peace into every room they entered? Describe that quality in them."]},"b10-w2-n4":{"story":["A long, long time ago, before your grandma's grandma, there was a good man named Isaiah. Heavenly Father showed Isaiah a picture in his mind of Jesus, even though Jesus would not be born for hundreds and hundreds of years. Isaiah told the people about it so that when Jesus came, they would know Him. Isaiah was talking about Jesus.","Isaiah said Jesus would be a man of sorrows. Sorrow is the big, heavy, hurting feeling we get inside when something sad happens. Isaiah was saying that Jesus would know exactly what sad feels like, because He would feel it too. Then Isaiah said something wonderful: Jesus would carry our sad and our hurt, like someone strong lifting a heavy backpack right off your shoulders and putting it on His own.","Isaiah also said we are all like sheep who wander off. Sheep nibble grass and walk and nibble and walk, and then they look up and the shepherd is gone. Isaiah picked sheep because his people lived with sheep every day and knew just how easily one gets lost. He was saying that each of us wanders our own way and does wrong things, and we need Someone to come and find us.","So Jesus picked up our whole heavy load in His own gentle arms, because He loves us. He made everything right again, like a shepherd carrying a lost lamb home.","When your heart feels heavy tonight, you do not have to carry it all by yourself. Jesus knows just how it feels, and He is holding you close."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow fits tonight because carrying someone else’s sadness is the tenderest kind of love. In Willow’s world today: her little brother dropped his ice cream on the sidewalk and could not stop crying. Willow sat down beside him on the warm cement. She did not fix it or hurry him along; she just held his hand. Just like Jesus, who carried our sorrows, Willow learned that love sits with people in the sad.","feel":"Hug your stuffed animal close against your chest, like you are carrying it somewhere safe. Rock it slowly side to side. Can you show me how gently you can carry something?","stones":[["SPOT","Isaiah said Jesus would know sad feelings Himself, and would carry our sad for us so we would never be alone with it."],["NAME","That heavy, hurting-inside feeling is called sorrow. Your mind might think, 'This hurts and I can't make it stop.' Your body might feel heavy in your chest, with a lump in your throat and tears coming up."],["FIND","Remember when Willow's brother dropped his ice cream on the sidewalk and his face crumpled? Willow felt that heavy feeling right along with him. When did your heart feel heavy like that, and who helped you carry it?"],["FORK","Willow could have walked away and left him sad by himself, or she could sit down beside him and share her own ice cream. She sat down beside him. We can hide our sad away, or we can hand it to Jesus. Jesus gladly takes ours."],["CALM","Lay both hands open on the blanket, like you are letting go of something heavy, and whisper, 'Heavenly Father, please hold my sad.'"]],"remember":"Jesus loved you so much that He carried your sadness before you were even born!","ask":["“What did Isaiah say Jesus would carry for us?”","“Why did Isaiah say we are like sheep?”","“What did Willow do when her brother dropped his ice cream?”"],"share":["When did someone carry a sorrow with you instead of trying to solve it? Share that with your child.","How do you sit with your child’s sadness without rushing it away? Tell about a moment you simply stayed beside them.","Did a grandparent or ancestor quietly bear burdens for other people? Describe that quality in them."]},"b10-w2-n5":{"story":["Isaiah wrote the Lord’s words for His sad, hurting people. They worried the Lord might leave them.","Mountains seem like they will stay forever. What could be bigger or stronger than a mountain?","But the Lord said, “The mountains may go away, and the hills be moved. But My kindness will not go away from you.”","Why mountains? Nothing seemed harder to move, yet His love is stronger still. The Lord is Jesus. He promised the children would be taught by Him. Great would be their peace, a calm, safe feeling inside.","That promise is for you! Mountains may move, but Jesus’s kindness will always stay with you."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren fits tonight because some truths are best held quietly and turned over slowly. In Wren’s world today: Wren spilled juice across her mom’s clean floor and froze, waiting. Mom only handed her a towel and hummed while they wiped it up together. Wren thought about it all afternoon. Just like the people who heard that Heavenly Father’s kindness would never depart, Wren learned that real love stays when we make a mess.","feel":"Push your hands out wide like mountains, then let them float slowly away. Now wrap yourself in a hug, because kindness stays. Can you show me the mountains going, and the hug staying?","stones":[["SPOT","Heavenly Father's people were far from home in Babylon, and they worried He had left them behind for good."],["NAME","That worried, are-You-still-there feeling is called afraid. Your mind might think, \"What if they stop loving me?\" Your body might feel tight in your tummy, and your voice might go very quiet."],["FIND","Remember when Wren spilled her juice and went quiet? When was a time you were scared that someone might stop loving you?"],["FORK","When that scared feeling comes, there are two roads. One road believes the scared thought. The other road remembers Heavenly Father's promise. Heavenly Father told His people the promise road was true."],["CALM","Squeeze both hands tight, then let them open slow. Whisper, \"Heavenly Father, thank You for staying.\""]],"remember":"Mountains could pack up and walk away, but Heavenly Father's kindness for you never will!","ask":["“Where were Heavenly Father's people living when they felt forgotten?”","“Which one can go away, the mountains or Heavenly Father's kindness?”","“What did Mom hand to Wren after the juice spilled?”"],"share":["When did you feel far from Heavenly Father and then gathered back? Share that turning point with your child.","How do you show a child that love holds steady after a mistake? Tell about a moment you chose gentleness over correction.","Did a grandparent or ancestor keep loving people who were hard to love? Describe that quality in them."]},"b10-w2-n6":{"story":["Sometimes the Lord’s people did not understand His plans. So the Lord spoke to them through Isaiah.","The Lord said, “My thoughts are not your thoughts.” Look up at the sky. It is so much higher than the ground!","The Lord said His thoughts are higher than ours, just like that. He knows much more than we do. So we can trust Him.","Isaiah said, “You shall go out with joy.” The mountains would sing, and the trees would clap their hands! Trees do not really have hands. Isaiah meant the whole earth would be glad, like leaves rustling in the wind.","You can trust the Lord, even when things feel hard. Tonight, clap your hands softly and say, “I trust You.”"],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout fits tonight because seeking is how joy gets found. In Scout’s world today: Scout hunted all morning for his missing green boot. He looked under the beds, behind the doors, and inside the laundry basket. When it finally turned up in the coat closet, he stomped both boots down the hallway singing. Just like the people promised they would go out with joy, Scout learned that seeking ends in gladness.","feel":"Wiggle your fingers down like rain falling onto your blanket. Then clap your hands soft like the happy trees. Can you show me the rain, and then the trees clapping?","stones":[["SPOT","Heavenly Father promised His people they would go out with joy and be led home in peace."],["NAME","That light, something-good-is-coming feeling is called hopeful. Your mind might think, \"Something good is on its way!\" Your body might feel light and wiggly, like it wants to bounce."],["FIND","What are you waiting for that makes you wiggle? A birthday? A trip? Seeing someone you love?"],["FORK","When we are waiting for something good, there are two roads. One road says, \"I will just wait and worry.\" The other road says, \"I will talk to Heavenly Father while I wait.\" Isaiah told the people to talk to Heavenly Father while He is near — that is the road he pointed to."],["CALM","Wiggle your toes ten times, then hold them very still. Whisper, \"Heavenly Father, I am looking for You tonight.\""]],"remember":"Heavenly Father's words come down like rain, and they always grow something good!","ask":["“What did Isaiah say to do while Heavenly Father is near?”","“Why did Isaiah say the trees would clap their hands?”","“What good thing are you waiting for, and what could you whisper to Heavenly Father while you wait?”"],"share":["When did an answer come only after you kept on seeking? Share that search with your child.","How do you help a child keep looking when nothing has turned up yet? Tell about a moment you searched for something together.","Did a grandparent or ancestor find real joy in ordinary days? Describe that quality in them."]},"b10-w2-n7":{"story":["Long ago, many of Heavenly Father's people had been taken far away from home to a land called Babylon. They felt small and sad. And some other people acted big and proud, like they did not need Heavenly Father at all. So Heavenly Father sent a man named Isaiah to tell them something true.","Isaiah said Heavenly Father is the High and Holy One. \"Holy\" means perfectly good and clean, better than anything. He lives in the highest, best place of all, and He never stops living. He is very, very big.","But then Heavenly Father said something surprising. He said He lives in one more place too: close beside anyone with a humble heart. A humble heart is a heart that says, \"I am little. I need help.\" Heavenly Father does not walk away from a little heart. He comes near, and He lifts it up when it feels low.","Heavenly Father also said, \"Peace, peace, to the one who is far off and to the one who is near. I will heal him.\" The ones far off were His people far from home, and the ones near were the ones still close by. He was saying, \"I have peace for every single one of you.\" Heal means to make the hurt better. Isaiah was talking about Jesus, who came to bring that peace and make sad hearts better again.","Your heart is little and honest, and that is just right. Tonight, when something feels too big, you can say, \"I need help,\" and Heavenly Father will come close, just like He promised."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau fits tonight because telling the truth about yourself is its own kind of bravery. In Beau’s world today: Beau knocked a plant off the windowsill and swept the dirt under the couch. His stomach ached all through lunch. Finally he tugged his dad’s sleeve and told him the whole thing. Just like the humble heart Heavenly Father chose to dwell with, Beau learned that honesty makes room for peace.","feel":"Curl up small and quiet like a little seed. Then let your heart lift as you take one big breath in. Can you show me small and quiet, and then lifted up?","stones":[["SPOT","Beau had something inside he did not want to say, and at last he told his dad about it. He let himself be little and honest instead of pretending he was fine."],["NAME","That small, I-need-some-help feeling is called humble. Your mind might think, \"I can't do this by myself.\" Your body might feel quiet and tight in the tummy, with your head down a little."],["FIND","When did you feel little inside and wish someone would come close and help you?"],["FORK","Beau had two roads: puff up big and say \"I'm fine,\" or be honest and small and tell his dad. He chose honest and small — and that is the heart Heavenly Father comes close to."],["CALM","Fold your hands under your chin, take one slow breath out, and whisper, \"Heavenly Father, here is my whole heart.\""]],"remember":"Heavenly Father lives in the highest place of all, and also right beside your quiet little heart!","ask":["“Where does Heavenly Father live besides the highest place?”","“What word did Heavenly Father say twice to everybody, far away and near?”","“What did Beau finally tell his dad about?”"],"share":["When did humility bring you nearer to Heavenly Father than strength ever did? Share that with your child.","How do you make honesty feel safe in your home? Tell about a moment your child told you something hard to say.","Did a grandparent or ancestor carry a quiet, unshowy humility? Describe that quality in them."]},"b10-w3-n1":{"story":["Long ago, Heavenly Father's people lived in a city called Jerusalem. Life was hard for them, and they wanted Heavenly Father to help. So they had a special day when they did not eat. That is called a fast. Going without food was their way of saying, 'Heavenly Father, we are listening. We need Thee.'","But on that very same day, they were grumpy with each other. They walked past hungry people and did not share. We say their hearts were hard, which only means their hearts had gone stiff and had stopped caring. So the day felt empty, and they asked, 'Why does our fast not feel special?'","Heavenly Father told the prophet Isaiah to teach them a better way. 'Share your bread with the hungry. Open your door to someone who has no bed. Give a coat to someone who is cold. And do not hide from your own family.' Years later, Jesus came and did those very things — He fed people, and healed them, and never hid from anyone. Isaiah was teaching the kind way Jesus would live.","'Then your light will shine out like the morning sun,' Heavenly Father said. When the sun comes up, the dark goes away and everyone can see. That is what kindness does — people see it and feel warm, the way you feel warm in the morning light. He also said, 'You will be like a garden with plenty of water.' A garden with water grows and grows and never dries up. A kind heart is like that: the more you give, the more love keeps growing inside you.","You have bread on your table, and you have two arms for hugging. Tomorrow you can give away a little of both — half your snack, one big hug for someone sad — and that morning light will shine right here in your house too."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow fits tonight because tenderness is the fast Heavenly Father chose. In Willow’s world today: Willow opens her lunch and finds two clementines. The girl beside her has none. Willow rolls one across the table without saying a word. Just like the people who shared their bread and watched their light rise, Willow learned that kindness is how a heart turns bright.","feel":"Hold your hands together like a little bowl, then open them slowly, as if you were handing warm bread to someone hungry. Can you show me how gently you would give it away?","stones":[["SPOT","Isaiah's people did their fasting day, but they were not kind to anyone, and when the day was over they still felt empty inside."],["NAME","That nothing-filled-me feeling is called emptiness. Your mind might think, 'Something is missing.' Your body might feel hollow and quiet in your tummy, even right after dinner."],["FIND","When did your heart feel empty today — maybe when you had something lovely but nobody to share it with?"],["FORK","The people came to a fork with two roads. One road: keep the food to themselves and stay empty. The other road: hand some bread to a hungry neighbour. Heavenly Father asked them to take the sharing road, because that is the road that fills a heart back up."],["CALM","Rest one hand on your tummy, breathe out slow and long, and whisper, 'Heavenly Father, please make my heart soft and kind.'"]],"remember":"When you share what you have, your own light comes up like the morning!","ask":["“What did Heavenly Father ask the people to give to someone who was hungry?”","“What happens to your light when you share something?”","“What could you share tomorrow with someone in your house?”"],"share":["When has giving something away lit something up inside you? Share that memory with your little one.","How do you teach small hands to give without turning it into a rule? Tell about a moment your child gave on their own.","Did a grandparent or ancestor keep a table where hungry people were always welcome? Describe that quality in them."]},"b10-w3-n2":{"story":["Long ago, Isaiah told the people what Heavenly Father said. He taught them about the Sabbath, the Lord’s holy day of rest each week.","Some people were using His special day just to do whatever they wanted.","So the Lord said, “Call the Sabbath a delight.” A delight is something that makes your heart smile.","The Lord asked them to follow His ways on that day, not their own. Then He promised, “You will delight in the Lord.” His day would bring them joy.","Now our Sabbath is Sunday, the day Jesus rose again. We go to church and remember Him. What could you do on Sunday to feel close to Jesus?"],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren fits tonight because a quiet day teaches what a loud day cannot. In Wren’s world today: Sunday morning, Wren’s shoes are on and the toy bin is calling. Wren sits down by the window instead and watches the light move across the floor. By the time the family sings, Wren is already still inside. Just like the people who learned to call the Sabbath a delight, Wren learned that quiet is not empty, it is full.","feel":"Let your arms go soft and heavy on the blanket, like a day with nothing at all to hurry for. Can you show me your quietest, restingest body?","stones":[["SPOT","The people's rest day got crowded with their own running and selling, so Wren's Sunday morning almost got crowded with running too."],["NAME","That too-full, no-room-left feeling is called hurry. Your mind might think, \"Go, go, go, there is no time to stop!\" Your body might feel tight and wiggly, with fast feet and a fast heart."],["FIND","When did your feet want to run away from something quiet, like a prayer or a story or a hug?"],["FORK","Wren had two roads: run outside with her toys, or sit close with her family. She sat close, and the quiet turned into a delight."],["CALM","Let your shoulders drop all the way down, slow as a feather, and whisper, \"Heavenly Father, help my heart be still with Thee.\""]],"remember":"Heavenly Father saved one whole day just for being near Him, and He called it a delight!","ask":["“What does the word delight mean?”","“Why did Heavenly Father give His people a rest day?”","“Where did Wren sit instead of running outside on Sunday morning?”"],"share":["What does Sabbath delight, rather than Sabbath duty, feel like for you? Share a Sunday that felt that way.","How do you protect stillness for a busy little one? Tell about a moment your child chose quiet all on their own.","Did a grandparent or ancestor keep the Sabbath in a way you can still feel? Describe that quality in them."]},"b10-w3-n3":{"story":["Long ago, a prophet named Isaiah lived in a city called Jerusalem. The people there had stopped listening to Heavenly Father. They were doing unkind things, and little by little they stopped feeling Him close. They said, \"Maybe Heavenly Father cannot hear us anymore.\" Their prayers felt like smoke floating away.","Isaiah told them the true thing. He said Heavenly Father's arm is not too short to reach them, and His ear is not too tired to hear them. A short arm cannot reach very far. But Heavenly Father's arm can reach all the way to anybody, anywhere.","Then Isaiah said something kind and honest: \"It is you who turned away.\" The wrong things they chose were like a cloud sliding in front of the sun. The sun is still shining the whole time. You just cannot feel it warm on your face until the cloud moves.","And clouds do move! Isaiah promised a Redeemer would come. A Redeemer is someone who buys you back and brings you home. Isaiah was talking about Jesus. Jesus comes to every person who turns back to Heavenly Father, even when they turned away first.","That is just like Beau behind the couch. He hid there feeling far away, and when he came out he said he was sorry. Tonight, if you ever feel far away, you do not have to crawl anywhere. You just turn back. Heavenly Father's arm is long enough to reach the very corner where you are."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau fits tonight because turning back takes more courage than running. In Beau’s world today: Beau knocks a cup off the counter and hides behind the couch. His chest thumps for a long, long minute. Then he walks out, small and honest, and says, ‘That was me.’ Just like the people Isaiah asked to turn back to Heavenly Father, Beau learned that coming back is always the brave way home.","feel":"Stretch one arm out as far as it will go, then reach a little farther still. Heavenly Father’s reach never runs out. Can you show me your longest, farthest reach?","stones":[["SPOT","Beau stayed behind the couch, and Isaiah's people stayed turned away. Both of them felt like nobody was hearing them."],["NAME","That far-away, is-anybody-listening feeling is called lonely. Your mind might think, \"Nobody hears me.\" Your body might feel cold and small, and you might want to hug your knees up tight."],["FIND","When have you hidden somewhere, like Beau behind the couch, after you did something you wished you had not done?"],["FORK","Beau could have stayed hidden, or he could come out. Isaiah's people could stay turned away, or turn back. Beau came out and said sorry, and Isaiah asked the people to turn back — and promised Jesus would come."],["CALM","Press both hands flat over your heart and whisper, \"Heavenly Father, I am turning back to You.\""]],"remember":"Heavenly Father's hand is never too short to reach you!","ask":["“Was Heavenly Father's arm too short to reach His people?”","“Who is the Redeemer Isaiah promised would come?”","“What did Beau say when he came out from behind the couch?”"],"share":["When did prayer feel like it went nowhere, and what finally brought you back? Share that stretch of the road.","How do you make turning back easy for a child who has hidden? Tell about a moment your child owned something small.","Did a grandparent or ancestor keep reaching for someone who had wandered off? Describe that quality in them."]},"b10-w3-n4":{"story":["Long ago, Isaiah told the people what Heavenly Father said. He gave them happy words: “Arise, shine; for your light has come!”","Think of a dark night. Then the sun comes up. People wake up and get up!","That is the picture Isaiah used. He told them to get up and shine. The Lord’s bright goodness was shining on them!","Isaiah said darkness covered the earth, but the Lord would shine on His people. People from far lands, even kings, would come to that light. Jesus is that light! He said, “I am the light of the world.”","When you are kind, others can see Jesus’s light in you. Tomorrow when you wake, get up and shine!"],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout fits tonight because a seeker is first up to meet the light. In Scout’s world today: Scout wakes before anyone and finds the hallway grey and still. Instead of calling out, Scout tiptoes to the window to watch the sky turn pink at the bottom. When Mama comes, Scout whispers, ‘It came back!’ Just like the people told to arise and shine because their light had come, Scout learned that morning always keeps its promise.","feel":"Curl up small like the night, then rise slowly with your arms opening wide like the sun coming over a hill. Can you show me how a morning wakes up?","stones":[["SPOT","Isaiah's people sat in the dark feeling sad and far from home, and all the while a great light was already rising for them, like Scout at the window seeing the first pink sky before anyone else woke up."],["NAME","That watching-in-the-dark feeling is called hoping. Your mind might think, \"Is it coming yet? Is it almost here?\" Your body might feel very still, with your eyes wide open, looking and looking."],["FIND","When have you waited in the dark? Maybe you woke up early and waited for the sky to turn light. Maybe you waited for Mom or Dad to come turn the lamp on. That waiting is hoping too."],["FORK","Isaiah's people had two roads. One road: keep sitting down in the dark and stay sad. The other road: stand up and let the light shine on them. Isaiah called them to stand up, and that is the road he wanted them to take."],["CALM","Open your hands slow, like the sun coming up. Breathe in, breathe out. Whisper, \"Heavenly Father, thank You for morning.\""]],"remember":"Jesus is the light that never, ever goes out!","ask":["“Why were Isaiah's people feeling dark and sad?”","“Who is the light Isaiah was really talking about?”","“What did Scout see out the window before anyone else woke up?”"],"share":["When did light finally arrive after a long dark season for you? Share what that first morning felt like.","How do you help a child rise toward good instead of being pushed? Tell about a moment they got up on their own.","Did a grandparent or ancestor carry light into hard rooms? Describe that quality in them."]},"b10-w3-n5":{"story":["Long ago, Isaiah wrote about Someone Heavenly Father would send. That Someone would “bind up the brokenhearted.” He would gently wrap very sad hearts, like a bandage on a knee.","He would also give “beauty for ashes.” Long ago, very sad people put gray ashes from a fire on their heads. So ashes meant sadness. He would give beauty and joy instead.","Many years later, Jesus went to the meeting house in His hometown, Nazareth. He stood up and read Isaiah’s words out loud.","Then Jesus sat down, and every eye was on Him. He said, “Today these words have come true.” Isaiah was talking about Jesus!","When your heart feels sad, Jesus can help it heal. He is gentle, and He loves you."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow fits tonight because comfort is her gift and her calling. In Willow’s world today: Willow’s block tower falls and her little brother sits down in the wreckage and cries. Willow does not rebuild it yet. She sits down in the pile beside him and pats his knee until his breathing slows. Just like Isaiah, sent to comfort everyone who mourned, Willow learned that sitting close is the first gift a sad heart needs.","feel":"Cup your hands as if you were holding grey ashes, then turn them over and shake them out into flower petals. Can you show me the trade Heavenly Father makes?","stones":[["SPOT","Heavenly Father's people were far from home, and their hearts felt broken and heavy and sad."],["NAME","That heavy, achy, hard-to-carry feeling is called sadness. Your mind might think, \"This will never get better.\" Your body might feel slow and droopy, with a lump in your throat and tears in your eyes."],["FIND","Can you think of a day when your heart felt heavy like that? Who came and sat beside you?"],["FORK","The people could keep sitting in their ashes all alone, or they could let Jesus make the trade. Isaiah said Jesus would bring them something beautiful, happy oil, and a glad song."],["CALM","Cross your arms into a hug, rock slowly, and whisper, \"Heavenly Father, please carry my heavy.\""]],"remember":"Heavenly Father sent Isaiah to tell us about Jesus, who trades our sad ashes for something beautiful!","ask":["“Who sent Isaiah to tell the people the good news?”","“Who was Isaiah really talking about?”","“What does Jesus give us instead of ashes?”"],"share":["Where has Heavenly Father traded you beauty for ashes? Share that exchange with your child.","How do you sit with a child’s sadness before fixing it? Tell about a moment you chose presence over repair.","Did a grandparent or ancestor turn hard years into something beautiful? Describe that quality in them."]},"b10-w3-n6":{"story":["Long ago, Isaiah’s people had turned away from the Lord. Isaiah wanted them to come close to Him again. So he said a prayer.","Isaiah prayed, “O Lord, You are our Father. We are the clay, and You are our potter.”","In Isaiah’s day, everyone used clay bowls and jars. A potter made them, shaping soft, wet clay with his hands.","Isaiah prayed, “We all are the work of Your hand.” A potter shapes clay slowly and carefully into something good. The Lord gently shapes us too, as we listen and learn.","You are His work, made with love. Bit by bit, Heavenly Father helps you grow kind and good like Jesus."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren fits tonight because a wonderer notices whose hands are holding her. In Wren’s world today: Wren’s playdough snake keeps breaking, and her hands go still with frustration. She watches Grandpa roll his own slowly instead of fast. Wren tries again, gentler this time, and it holds together. Just like Isaiah, who called Heavenly Father the potter and His people the clay, Wren learned that good things are made by patient hands.","feel":"Squeeze your hands into one soft ball, then let them open slowly, like clay resting in kind hands. Can you show me your softest, most restful hands?","stones":[["SPOT","Isaiah felt very small next to Heavenly Father, and safe in His hands anyway. And Wren felt it too, when big hands helped her roll the playdough slowly instead of squishing it fast."],["NAME","That small-but-held, someone-has-me feeling is called trust. Your mind might think, “Somebody bigger has me.” Your body might feel soft, with your shoulders down low and your breathing slow."],["FIND","When did someone bigger help you with something your own hands could not do yet — like Wren and the playdough, or a zipper, or a heavy door?"],["FORK","Isaiah could have said, “Heavenly Father, do it my way,” or he could rest and let Heavenly Father shape him. Isaiah chose to rest. He prayed, “We are the clay, and You are the potter.”"],["CALM","Cup your two hands together like a little bowl, breathe all the way out, and whisper, “Heavenly Father, I am the work of Your hands.”"]],"remember":"You are the work of Heavenly Father’s hands, and He is not finished with you yet!","ask":["“In Isaiah’s prayer, who was the potter and who was the clay?”","“Why were Isaiah’s people sad?”","“Who showed Wren how to roll the playdough slowly?”"],"share":["Where is Heavenly Father still shaping you? Share the place you can feel His thumbprint.","How do you stay patient with a child mid- shaping? Tell about a moment you slowed down instead of pressing harder.","Did a grandparent or ancestor have patient, making hands? Describe that quality in them."]},"b10-w3-n7":{"story":["Long ago, Heavenly Father's people had to live far away from their homes, in a land called Babylon. They were sad. They missed their houses and their gardens. So Heavenly Father gave a helper named Isaiah a happy picture to tell them about. Isaiah was a prophet, which means Heavenly Father told him things and he told the people.","Here is the picture: one day Heavenly Father will make the whole world new and good. Isaiah was talking about the time after Jesus comes again. In that new world, nobody will cry. Not one tear. Only happy days.","People will build their own houses and get to live in them. They will plant little seeds and eat the fruit that grows. Nobody will ever take their good things away. That was a very happy thing for sad people far from home to hear.","Isaiah said even the animals will be friends. Today a wolf and a lamb could never nap together, because a wolf is a hunter and a lamb is small. But in the new world they will eat side by side, safe. The big lion will eat straw, like a gentle cow in a barn. Nothing will hurt anybody on Heavenly Father's holy mountain. 'Holy mountain' is a picture-word for the place where Heavenly Father lives with His people, and Isaiah said 'mountain' because a mountain is high and strong and safe.","Heavenly Father even said, 'Before you finish asking, I will answer.' He is listening that close. Tonight your bed is warm and your door is shut and someone who loves you is right here. That is a tiny taste of the safe world Heavenly Father is making. You can close your eyes and let yourself feel safe."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout fits tonight because wondering what comes next is how a seeker walks. In Scout’s world today: The neighbor’s big dog barks and Scout’s feet freeze on the sidewalk. Scout crouches down slowly, holds out one careful hand, and waits. The dog leans in, and Scout’s whole face opens up. Just like the lamb who will one day rest beside the wolf, Scout learned that peace can grow where you thought it never could.","feel":"Lay your hands side by side on the blanket, one the wolf and one the lamb, resting quietly together. Can you show me two hands that are not afraid of each other?","stones":[["SPOT","Isaiah told sad people about a new world where nobody gets hurt and nobody cries."],["NAME","That safe-at-last feeling is called peace. Your mind might think, 'Nothing can hurt me here.' Your body might feel warm and floppy, with slow breaths and sleepy eyes."],["FIND","When did you feel safe today? Maybe on someone's lap, or in your bed, or holding a hand?"],["FORK","When something feels scary, you can keep thinking about the scary thing, or you can think about Heavenly Father's promise that you are safe. Isaiah chose the promise. Which road will you take tonight?"],["CALM","Put one hand on your heart, breathe out slow and long, and whisper, 'Heavenly Father, thank You for keeping me safe.'"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father is making a world where nothing hurts and nobody cries!","ask":["“Which two animals will eat side by side in the new world?”","“What will the big lion eat there instead?”","“What is one place where you feel safe?”"],"share":["What would you gladly set down in a world with no more weeping? Share what you long to put away.","How do you speak hope to a child who is afraid? Tell about a moment your child chose courage over fear.","Did a grandparent or ancestor live as though that peace were already coming? Describe that quality in them."]},"b10-w4-n1":{"story":["Long ago, Jeremiah grew up in a family of priests who served the Lord.","One day the Lord spoke to Jeremiah. The Lord was Jesus, speaking for Heavenly Father.","He said, “Before I made you in your mother’s tummy, I knew you. Before you were born, I chose you to be a prophet.” Jeremiah would tell people Heavenly Father’s words.","How could the Lord know Jeremiah before he was born? Latter-day prophets teach that we lived with Heavenly Father before coming to earth. We lived there as spirits, the real us inside.","Heavenly Father knew you there, and He knows you now. He has loved you from the very start."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau fits tonight because it takes courage to trust you are enough when you feel very small. In Beau’s world today: Beau is the smallest one in his class, and today it was his turn to hold the heavy door. His hands shook a little. He held it anyway, and every friend walked through. Just like Jeremiah, who was young and went anyway, Beau learned that Heavenly Father sends small people to do good things.","feel":"Hold up both hands and look at them in the dark. Heavenly Father knew those hands before you were born. Now press them softly over your heart, where His warm words go. Can you show me your quiet, brave hands?","stones":[["SPOT","Heavenly Father asked Jeremiah to do something big, and Jeremiah felt far too small for it."],["NAME","That small-and-not-ready feeling is called unsure. Your mind might think, \"I am too little for this.\" Your body might feel wobbly, with shaky hands and a very quiet voice."],["FIND","When did you feel too little today? Maybe someone asked you to try something new, like Beau standing by that heavy door."],["FORK","Jeremiah could hide and say nothing. Or he could go, because Heavenly Father said, \"I am with you.\" Jeremiah went."],["CALM","Press one hand on your heart, take one slow breath, and whisper, \"Heavenly Father, please walk with me.\""]],"remember":"Heavenly Father knew you and loved you before you ever took your first breath!","ask":["“What did Jeremiah say when Heavenly Father asked him to go?”","“What did Heavenly Father promise Jeremiah when he felt afraid?”","“What did Beau hold open for all his friends today?”"],"share":["When did you feel far too small for what you were asked to do, and Heavenly Father carried you through? Tell your child about that time.","How do you answer your child when they say ‘I can’t’? Tell about a moment you watched courage grow in them.","Did a grandparent or ancestor step forward before they felt ready? Describe that quiet courage to your child."]},"b10-w4-n2":{"story":["The Lord chose young Jeremiah to tell people Heavenly Father’s words. Jeremiah felt too little. He said, “Oh, Lord, I cannot speak. I am a child.”","The Lord said, “Do not say, ‘I am a child.’ Go where I send you. Say what I tell you.”","He said, “Do not be afraid, for I am with you.” Then the Lord reached out His hand and touched Jeremiah’s mouth.","The Lord said, “I have put My words in your mouth.” Jeremiah did not have to find the words by himself. The Lord was Jesus, and He helps the ones He calls.","You are never too little to help Jesus. When you feel shy, He can help you find kind and true words."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow fits tonight because tender words are a gift we are given, not something we must make alone. In Willow’s world today: a friend at the sand table was crying, and Willow had no idea what to say. She sat down close and stayed. Then the words came: ‘You can build with me.’ Just like Jeremiah, whose words were placed in his mouth, Willow learned that Heavenly Father sends kind words right when they are needed.","feel":"Touch your own lips with one fingertip, softly, the way morning light touches a window. Now whisper one kind word out into the dark room. Can you show me your very gentlest whisper?","stones":[["SPOT","Jeremiah felt empty, with no words to give at all."],["NAME","That nothing-to-say feeling is called speechless. Your mind might think, “I don’t know what to say.” Your body might feel stuck, with your mouth closed and your hands still."],["FIND","When did you want to help somebody but not know what to say?"],["FORK","He could stay silent and turn away. Or open his mouth and trust. Jeremiah opened it."],["CALM","Breathe out slowly and whisper, ‘Heavenly Father, please put kind words in me.’"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father hands us the words we need at the very moment we need them!","ask":["“What did Heavenly Father touch when Jeremiah had no words?”","“What did Heavenly Father send Jeremiah out to do?”","“What did Willow finally say to her friend at the sand table?”"],"share":["When did the right words come to you that you know were not your own? Share that moment with your child.","How do you help your child find words for big feelings? Tell about a time their kindness surprised you.","Did a grandparent or ancestor have a gift for saying the right thing? Describe that gentleness to your child."]},"b10-w4-n3":{"story":["The Lord had just chosen Jeremiah to share His words. Then the Lord asked him, “Jeremiah, what do you see?”","Jeremiah looked closely. He said, “I see a branch from an almond tree.” In spring, the almond tree wakes up first, with early blossoms.","The Lord said, “You have seen well.” Jeremiah had looked carefully and seen it right.","Then the Lord said, “I will hurry to make My word come true.” Like the early almond, His word would never be late. The Lord was Jesus, and what He says really happens. Jeremiah could feel sure, even when people would not listen.","The Lord keeps His promises to you too. You can trust Him, every single day."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout fits tonight because looking closely is how we find what Heavenly Father is quietly showing us. In Scout’s world today: Scout kept a bare twig in a jar on the windowsill and checked it every morning. Most mornings, nothing. Then one morning, one tiny green bud, and he ran to show everybody. Just like Jeremiah, who saw the waking branch, Scout learned that Heavenly Father keeps every promise, even slow-looking ones.","feel":"Peek out from under your blanket with just your eyes, like a bud peeking out at spring. Now open your hands slowly, slowly, like a blossom in the morning sun. Can you show me how a flower wakes up?","stones":[["SPOT","Heavenly Father asked, \"What do you see?\" and Jeremiah stopped and looked closely at one little branch."],["NAME","That wide-awake, look-again feeling is called wonder. Your mind might think, \"Wait - what is that?\" Your body might lean in close, with big eyes and a quiet, held breath."],["FIND","Think of a time today when you stopped and looked hard at something little - a bug, a cloud, a crack in the sidewalk. What did you see?"],["FORK","Jeremiah had two roads. He could look away fast and see nothing. Or he could look carefully. Jeremiah looked carefully, and he saw - and Heavenly Father taught him something lovely."],["CALM","Blink slowly three times, nice and soft, and whisper, \"Heavenly Father, help me see the good things You made.\""]],"remember":"Heavenly Father is wide awake, watching over every promise He makes, and He has never once forgotten one!","ask":["“What kind of little branch did Jeremiah see?”","“Which tree wakes up first when spring comes?”","“What did Heavenly Father say He would keep?”"],"share":["When did a small sign remind you Heavenly Father was still working on something? Share that sign with your child.","How do you teach a small child to wait on a promise? Tell about a time your family waited together and it came.","Did a grandparent or ancestor notice small beauties others walked past? Describe that noticing to your child."]},"b10-w4-n4":{"story":["The Lord told Jeremiah, “Go down to the potter’s house.” So Jeremiah went to see a man who made pots and bowls from clay.","The potter sat at his wheel. It spun around and around while his hands shaped the soft, wet clay.","But the pot got spoiled in his hands. So the potter shaped that clay into a new pot.","The Lord said, “Like clay in the potter’s hand, you are in My hand.” His people had made wrong choices. If they came back to Him, the Lord could make them new.","When you make a mistake, you are still safe in Heavenly Father’s hands. He gently helps you start new."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren fits tonight because quiet truths often hide inside ordinary work, waiting for someone to sit still and watch. In Wren’s world today: Wren’s playdough snowman flopped sideways, and her lip began to wobble. Her grandma only smiled and rolled it back into a smooth round ball. They started again, and it turned out better. Just like the potter who made the clay again, Wren learned that Heavenly Father remakes what bends.","feel":"Hold both hands together like a bowl and turn them round and round, slow as a potter’s wheel. Now let them settle open and soft on your tummy. Can you show me your gentle potter hands?","stones":[["SPOT","The clay went crooked and flopped over, and nobody got angry about it."],["NAME","That safe, not-thrown-away feeling is called hopeful. Your mind might think, \"I can try again.\" Your body might feel lighter, with your shoulders coming back up and your tummy soft."],["FIND","When did something you made go floppy, or when did you do something wrong, and someone helped you begin again?"],["FORK","The potter could have thrown the clay out. Or he could shape it again. He shaped it again. Grandma chose that road too."],["CALM","Squeeze your fists tight, open them slowly like soft clay, and whisper, \"Heavenly Father, shape me.\""]],"remember":"You are never a mistake to throw away, you are soft clay resting in the kindest hands there are!","ask":["“Where did Heavenly Father send Jeremiah to watch and learn?”","“What did the potter do when the clay went crooked?”","“What did Heavenly Father say we are like?”"],"share":["When did Heavenly Father reshape something in you instead of setting you aside? Share that remaking with your child.","How do you respond when your child’s work collapses? Tell about a moment you helped them start over.","Did a grandparent or ancestor rebuild after something fell apart? Describe that patience to your child."]},"b10-w4-n5":{"story":["A long, long time ago, Heavenly Father's children stopped listening to Him. They walked away from Him a little at a time, until they were very far away. Then soldiers took many of them to a land far up north, a long way from their homes.","Heavenly Father did not forget them. He gave a man named Jeremiah the words to call out to them. A prophet is someone Heavenly Father tells His words to, so the people can hear them. Jeremiah called, \"Come back! Heavenly Father is merciful. Merciful means He is kind to you even when you have done something wrong. He will not stay angry forever.\"","Heavenly Father promised to go and find His children, even one child here and two children there, and bring them home to Zion. Zion is Heavenly Father's own city, where His people live close to Him and love each other. He said \"come home\" in a soft voice, not a shouting one, because He wanted them to know He still loved them.","He also promised to send them shepherds. A shepherd is someone who watches sheep all day and keeps every single one safe. Heavenly Father meant good leaders who would watch over His children the same gentle way He does, and teach them what is true. Jesus is called the Good Shepherd, and He watches over us just like that.","Heavenly Father counts His children one by one, like a shepherd counting sheep, and He knows right where you are tonight, in your own bed. If you ever walk away, even a little, you can always turn around. \"Come home\" is His softest word."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow fits tonight because mercy is the tenderness that leaves the door standing open. In Willow’s world today: Willow’s little brother knocked her block tower flat and ran to hide behind the couch. Willow looked at the mess for a long, quiet minute. Then she called out softly, ‘You can come out. We can build it again.’ Just like Heavenly Father calling His children home, Willow learned that love does not stay angry forever.","feel":"Cup your hands around your mouth and call very softly across the dark room: ‘Come home.’ Now open both arms as wide as they will go for a welcoming hug. Can you show me your come- home arms?","stones":[["SPOT","Heavenly Father's children had walked far away from Him. He did not shout at them. He called, \"Come home.\""],["NAME","Missing the people who love you is called homesick. Your mind might think, \"I want to go back where I belong.\" Your body might feel achy right in your chest, and your arms might want a hug."],["FIND","When did you feel far away from someone you love and want to go back? Maybe you hid, like Willow's brother behind the couch, and waited for someone to call you."],["FORK","When Willow found her brother behind the couch, she could have walked away and left him there. Or she could say, \"Come out, I want you.\" Willow called him. You can call someone too, or go when someone calls you."],["CALM","Open your arms wide, then hug yourself tight and whisper, \"Heavenly Father, I am coming home.\""]],"remember":"Heavenly Father will not stay angry forever, and He comes looking for every single one of us!","ask":["“Why were Heavenly Father's children so far from home?”","“What is a shepherd, and who did Heavenly Father promise to send?”","“What did Willow say to her brother hiding behind the couch?”"],"share":["When did someone welcome you back without a lecture? Share how that mercy felt with your child.","How quickly do you set anger down with your children? Tell about a moment you chose to reopen the door.","Did a grandparent or ancestor keep loving someone who wandered? Describe that steady welcome to your child."]},"b10-w4-n6":{"story":["Long, long ago there was a prophet named Jeremiah. A prophet is someone Heavenly Father lets talk to His children for Him. In Jeremiah's time, many people had stopped listening to Heavenly Father. When something hard happened, they ran to other people for help, and they felt worried all the time.","So Heavenly Father gave Jeremiah a word picture to help them understand. Jeremiah said it out loud so everyone could hear: \"Happy is the person who trusts in the Lord.\" The Lord means Jesus. Jeremiah was talking about Jesus, and Jesus and Heavenly Father love us and want to help us.","Then Jeremiah said that person is like a tree planted right beside a river. He picked a tree because those people grew trees and knew them well. Roots are the long arms a tree grows down under the ground. A tree by a river pushes its roots way, way down until they touch the water. Down where nobody can see, that tree is drinking all day long.","So the tree is not scared when the hot, dry days come. Its leaves stay green, and it keeps growing fruit, because the water is still down there under the ground.","Trusting Heavenly Father is like that. Tonight, when you say your prayer and listen in the quiet, your roots are drinking too, from a river that never dries up. Even on your hardest day, something quiet inside you stays green."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau fits tonight because trusting quietly, when nothing looks easy, is its own kind of brave. In Beau’s world today: it was a hard day, and rain shut down the whole playground. His best friend stayed home sick. Beau still fed the class fish and still sang at circle time, doing the good small things anyway. Just like the tree with roots down in the river, Beau learned that trusting Heavenly Father keeps us green on dry days.","feel":"Stretch both arms up tall like branches, then wiggle your toes down deep like roots reaching for water. Hold still and breathe slow, the way a tree stands in the sun. Can you show me your strongest roots?","stones":[["SPOT","The tree stayed green even when the hot, dry days came, because its roots were drinking from the river."],["NAME","That steady, not-worried feeling is called trust. Your mind might think, \"It will be okay. I am not alone.\" Your body might feel calm and slow, with soft breaths and still feet."],["FIND","When did you feel that today? Maybe when you held a grown-up's hand, or when someone said, \"I am right here.\""],["FORK","When a day gets hard, we can worry all by ourselves and feel dry inside, or we can tell Heavenly Father and let our roots drink. Jeremiah chose to trust Him, and Beau did too."],["CALM","Press your feet flat on the bed like roots going down, and whisper, \"Heavenly Father, I am trusting You.\""]],"remember":"Roots that reach all the way down to the river are never afraid of a hot day!","ask":["“What is the person who trusts Heavenly Father like?”","“What did the tree's roots find way down under the ground?”","“What did Beau keep doing on his hard, rainy day?”"],"share":["What keeps your roots in the water during a dry season? Share one of those quiet practices with your child.","How do you stay steady for your children on hard days? Tell about a day you kept going anyway.","Did a grandparent or ancestor stay green through a long dry season? Describe that endurance to your child."]},"b10-w4-n7":{"story":["The Lord sent Jeremiah to stand at the gate of His house, the temple. Jeremiah gave the people the Lord’s words. “Obey My voice, and you will be My people.”","Many people did not want to listen. Some laughed at Jeremiah every day. One leader even locked him up for a night.","Jeremiah felt sad. He thought, “I will not speak about the Lord anymore.”","But Jeremiah said the Lord’s word was in his heart. It was “like a burning fire shut up in my bones.” It was a warm, strong feeling he could not hold in. So Jeremiah kept on sharing.","Jesus’s words can feel warm in your heart too. Tonight, tell your family one thing you love about Him."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout fits tonight because a true thing you have found is almost impossible to keep to yourself. In Scout’s world today: Scout learned a brand new song at preschool and decided to save it as a surprise. He lasted until the car door shut. Then out it came bubbling, all the verses at once, and then again. Just like Jeremiah with the fire in his bones, Scout learned that good news from Heavenly Father wants to be sung.","feel":"Put one hand flat on your chest and feel it go warm and up-and-down. Now hum one little tune, quiet as a candle flame in the dark. Can you show me the song hiding inside you?","stones":[["SPOT","Jeremiah was tired and wanted to stay quiet. But something warm inside him would not sit still."],["NAME","That warm, can't-keep-it-in feeling is called eager. Your mind might think, \"I have to say it RIGHT NOW!\" Your body might feel warm in your chest, with wiggly feet and a big breath all ready to go."],["FIND","When did you feel that? Maybe you had good news, or made something you loved, and you ran to find Mom or Dad to tell them. What was it? Tell me now."],["FORK","Jeremiah stood at a fork, like two roads. One road: keep the words in and say nothing. The other road: let them out. Jeremiah took the second road. He sang to Heavenly Father. You have two roads too: keep your good thing inside, or share it."],["CALM","Put your hand on your chest and hum one soft note, long and slow, until it runs out. Then whisper, \"Heavenly Father, thank you for the warm little light in me.\""]],"remember":"Heavenly Father tucked a warm little light inside you that no tired day can ever put out!","ask":["“Why did Jeremiah say the words felt like a fire inside him?”","“Jeremiah was tired and sad.","What did he do instead of staying quiet?”","“In our Traveler story, where did Scout's brand new song finally burst out?”"],"share":["When did something true burn in you until you finally said it out loud? Share that moment with your child.","How do you make room for your child’s excitement at the end of a long day? Tell about a time you listened anyway.","Did a grandparent or ancestor carry a testimony they could not keep quiet? Describe that fire to your child."]},"b9-w1-n1":{"story":["King David wrote a happy song. He sang, “Bless the Lord, O my soul.” He was telling the real him, deep inside, to say thank you.","David sang, “Forget not all His good gifts.” He did not want to forget one good thing the Lord had done.","Then David thought of a loving dad. He sang that the Lord loves His people “like a father” loves his children.","Picture a little child who falls down. A good dad hurries to hug and help. The Lord’s love is just like that. Jesus taught us to pray to “Our Father.”","Heavenly Father loves you even more. When you feel sad, His love is near. Tonight, thank Him for one good gift."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow fits tonight because she feels how gentle and forgiving love can be. In Willow’s world today: Willow spilled her juice all over the floor and felt scared she was in trouble. But Mom just gave her a hug and said, “It’s okay — let’s clean it up together.” Willow’s worried heart melted into a happy one. Just like David sang about Heavenly Father’s kind, forgiving love, Willow felt how big and gentle that love can be.","feel":"Let’s bless Heavenly Father like David! Wrap your arms around yourself in a big hug, then stretch them wa-a-ay up high to the sky. His love is bigger than we can even reach! Can you show me how high His love goes?","stones":[["SPOT","David felt thankful and full of love for Heavenly Father."],["NAME","That warm, cared-for feeling is called loved."],["FIND","When do you feel loved and safe, like someone is taking good care of you?"],["FORK","We can forget the good things — or remember them and thank Heavenly Father. David chose to remember every one."],["CALM","Give yourself a hug and whisper, “Thank you for loving me, Heavenly Father.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father loves you higher than the sky and takes your mistakes far away when you’re sorry! You are so loved, sweet one.","ask":["“What did David want to remember about Heavenly Father?”","“How big is Heavenly Father’s love for us?”","“What is one good thing Heavenly Father has done for you that you can thank Him for?”"],"share":["When have you felt Heavenly Father’s forgiveness lift a weight off your heart? Share how it felt to be free.","How do you remind yourself to “count the good things” when life feels heavy? Tell your child your practice.","Did a grandparent or ancestor have a thankful, praising heart? Describe how their gratitude blessed the family."]},"b9-w1-n2":{"story":["There once was a person who felt sad and afraid. Big troubles were all around, and he didn’t know what to do. So he did the best thing of all — he called out to Heavenly Father.","“Please help me, Heavenly Father!” he prayed. And guess what? Heavenly Father heard every single word.","Heavenly Father listened close and helped him. His sadness turned to gladness, and his heart felt calm again. “I love Heavenly Father,” he sang, “because He hears me when I call!”","The person was so thankful. He decided he would keep praying and keep loving Heavenly Father for the rest of his life.","Whenever you talk to Heavenly Father, He hears you too — every whisper, every prayer. You are never, ever alone."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau fits tonight because he trusts Heavenly Father to hear him when things feel hard. In Beau’s world today: Beau got lost from Dad for a scary minute at the busy store. His heart pounded, but he remembered to whisper, “Heavenly Father, help me,” and stay put. Soon Dad came around the corner! “Heavenly Father heard me,” Beau said. Just like the person who called out and was heard, Beau learned that Heavenly Father listens the moment we call.","feel":"Let’s call out to Heavenly Father! Cup your hands around your mouth and softly call, “I love You, Heavenly Father!” Now put your hand on your ear — He always hears. Can you show me your best listening ears?","stones":[["SPOT","The person felt sad and scared, then heard and helped."],["NAME","That someone-is-listening feeling is called heard."],["FIND","When has telling someone your worry helped you feel better?"],["FORK","A worried feeling can stay stuck inside — or we can call out to Heavenly Father, who hears. The person chose to pray, and help came."],["CALM","Take a slow breath and whisper, “Heavenly Father, You hear me.”"]],"remember":"Whenever you talk to Heavenly Father, He hears every word — even a tiny whisper! He loves to listen to you.","ask":["“What did the person do when he felt sad and afraid?”","“What did Heavenly Father do when he called?”","“What would you like to tell Heavenly Father tonight?”"],"share":["When did you cry out to Heavenly Father and feel truly heard? Share that answered prayer.","How do you teach your child that no prayer is too small for Heavenly Father? Tell about a time He heard you.","Did a grandparent or ancestor have a story of a prayer that was answered? Describe how Heavenly Father heard them."]},"b9-w1-n3":{"story":["Long ago, the Lord’s people sang a happy song. It began, “O give thanks to the Lord, for He is good.”","Then they sang, “This is the day which the Lord has made. We will be happy and glad in it.”","The Lord who made each day is Jesus. He made the world for Heavenly Father. So every morning is a gift from Him.","The song does not say, “a perfect day.” It says “this day,” the one we have right now. Even on a rainy day, we can find something to be glad about.","Think back on today. What made you smile? Tell Heavenly Father thank you for that happy thing."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout fits tonight because Scout finds something wonderful in every new day. In Scout’s world today: it was a plain, rainy morning with no big plans. But Scout said, “I wonder what good things today will bring?” and went looking — and found a puddle to splash, a rainbow after lunch, and a cozy story with Grandma. Just like the song-writer who was glad for the day Heavenly Father made, Scout discovered that every day holds a gift when we look for it.","feel":"Let’s welcome the new day! Stretch your arms up in a big yawn, then open them wide like the morning sun coming up. “This is the day Heavenly Father made!” Can you show me your happiest good-morning stretch?","stones":[["SPOT","The song-writer felt happy and glad for the new day."],["NAME","That bright, morning-happy feeling is called glad."],["FIND","What makes you feel glad when you wake up in the morning?"],["FORK","We can grumble that a day is boring — or find the gift Heavenly Father put in it. The song- writer chose to be glad."],["CALM","Take a happy breath and whisper, “Thank You for today, Heavenly Father.”"]],"remember":"Every new day is a gift made just for you by Heavenly Father! You can wake up glad and look for the good in it.","ask":["“Who made this brand-new day?”","“What did the song-writer say he would be?”","“What is one thing you’re excited to do tomorrow?”"],"share":["How do you choose gladness on ordinary or hard days? Share a morning that turned around.","What small daily gift do you want your child to learn to notice? Tell why it matters to you.","Did a grandparent or ancestor greet each day with joy and gratitude? Describe their sunny spirit."]},"b9-w1-n4":{"story":["The longest song in all the Psalms is about something very special: Heavenly Father’s words, the scriptures. The song-writer loved them so much!","“Your words are like a lamp for my feet,” he sang, “and a bright light for my path.” A lamp helps us see where to walk in the dark.","When we don’t know what to do, Heavenly Father’s words show us the way — just like a little light helps us take the next safe step.","The song-writer even said, “I keep Your words safe inside my heart.” He learned them and remembered them, like hiding a treasure where it can never be lost.","You can keep Heavenly Father’s words in your heart too. When you listen to scripture stories, you’re filling your heart with a light that helps you always know the way."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren fits tonight because she treasures Heavenly Father’s words in her heart. In Wren’s world today: Wren couldn’t decide whether to tattle or be kind when her friend made a little mistake. Then she remembered a scripture story about being gentle, and it lit up the right thing to do — like a little lamp. She chose kindness. Just like the song-writer whose path was lit by Heavenly Father’s words, Wren found that the scriptures show us the way.","feel":"Let’s light our little lamp! Hold your hands like you’re carrying a lantern out in front of you, and take slow, careful steps. The light shows the way! Can you walk your safe little path with your lamp?","stones":[["SPOT","The song-writer felt safe and sure, because Heavenly Father’s words showed the way."],["NAME","That I-know-the-way feeling is called guided."],["FIND","When has knowing what to do helped you feel safe and calm?"],["FORK","In the dark we can stumble and guess — or follow Heavenly Father’s light. The song-writer chose to let His words guide him."],["CALM","Hold your hands like a lamp, breathe slow, and think of a favorite scripture story."]],"remember":"Heavenly Father’s words are like a little lamp that shows you the way — and you can keep them safe in your heart!","ask":["“What did the song-writer say Heavenly Father’s words were like?”","“Where did he keep Heavenly Father’s words safe?”","“What is one thing scripture stories have taught you to do?”"],"share":["When did a scripture light your path in a moment of not-knowing? Share the verse and the choice.","How do you “hide His word in your heart” as a family? Tell about a passage you love.","Did a grandparent or ancestor treasure the scriptures? Describe how their love of Heavenly Father’s word guided them."]},"b9-w1-n5":{"story":["King David wrote a song about the Lord being near. He sang, “If I take the wings of the morning.” He pictured flying far across the sea.","Morning light spreads across the whole sky so fast. It is like the light has wings!","Even there, David said, “Your hand shall lead me. Your right hand shall hold me.”","David also sang, “You made me inside my mother. I am fearfully and wonderfully made.” Here, fearfully does not mean scared. It means made with great care and wonder.","Wherever you go, the Lord is with you. Jesus promised, “I am with you always.” Wiggle your fingers and toes. Heavenly Father made you with great care!"],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren fits tonight because she loves to wonder at how amazing she’s made. In Wren’s world today: Wren looked at her own hand and wondered, “How did Heavenly Father make my fingers wiggle and my heart go thump?” She felt so special knowing He made every part of her on purpose. Just like the song-writer who marveled that he was wonderfully made, Wren discovered that Heavenly Father made her just right.","feel":"Let’s show how well Heavenly Father knows us! Wiggle your fingers (He made those!), pat your head (He knows your thoughts!), and point to your smile (He made that too!). Can you show me all the wonderful parts He made?","stones":[["SPOT","The song-writer felt amazed and special, knowing Heavenly Father made and knew him."],["NAME","That treasured, one-of-a-kind feeling is called special."],["FIND","When do you feel really special and loved just for being you?"],["FORK","We can think we’re just ordinary — or remember Heavenly Father made us wonderfully. The song-writer chose to feel how special he was."],["CALM","Put a hand on your heart, breathe slow, and whisper, “Heavenly Father made me special.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father knows everything about you and made you wonderfully — there is no one else exactly like you!","ask":["“What does Heavenly Father know about us?”","“How did Heavenly Father make each of us?”","“How does it feel to know Heavenly Father made you just right?”"],"share":["When did you feel deeply known and loved by Heavenly Father? Share that assurance.","How do you help your child feel treasured exactly as they are? Tell about a moment you felt His notice.","Did a grandparent or ancestor carry a quiet confidence that Heavenly Father knew and loved them? Describe it."]},"b9-w1-n6":{"story":["Long ago in Israel, parents passed their land down to their children. That land was a precious gift kept in the family.","A song in the Bible says children are a gift like that. It sings, “Children are a heritage of the Lord.” Each child is a precious gift from Him.","Heavenly Father gave you to your family. You are their precious gift, and you are His child too.","The song also says the Lord gives sleep to the ones He dearly loves. Jesus loved children too. He took them up in His arms and blessed them.","You are a precious gift, and you are dearly loved. Snuggle down now, and let sweet sleep come."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow fits tonight because she cherishes the people in her home. In Willow’s world today: at dinner, Willow looked around at everyone laughing together and felt her heart go warm. “I’m so glad you’re my family,” she said, and gave her little brother a hug — even though he’d been silly all day. Just like the song about children being a gift from Heavenly Father, Willow felt how precious her family is.","feel":"Let’s make a cozy family circle! Reach your arms out wide like they’re wrapping all around your family, then pull them into a big squeeze-hug. Home is full of love! Can you give a great big family hug?","stones":[["SPOT","The family in the song felt happy and blessed to be together."],["NAME","That safe, glad-to-belong feeling is called treasured."],["FIND","When does your family make you feel happy and loved?"],["FORK","We can take our family for granted — or thank Heavenly Father for the gift of them. The song chose to see family as a blessing."],["CALM","Give a family member a hug and whisper, “Thank You for my family, Heavenly Father.”"]],"remember":"Your family is a gift from Heavenly Father — and so are you! He helps your home be full of love.","ask":["“Who helps build a happy home?”","“What does the song say children are?”","“Who in your family are you thankful for tonight?”"],"share":["When have you felt your family was a sacred gift from Heavenly Father? Share that tender awareness.","How do you invite Heavenly Father to help “build your house”? Tell about a way He’s strengthened your home.","Did a grandparent or ancestor build a home of faith and love? Describe the family blessing they began."]},"b9-w1-n7":{"story":["The very last psalm is a big, happy song. It says, “Praise Him with the sound of the trumpet. Praise Him with the harp.”","It says to praise Him with dancing and a jingly drum called a timbrel. Then come strings, pipes and loud, clashing cymbals!","The song asks everyone to praise the Lord with all they have.","It ends, “Let every thing that has breath praise the Lord.” If you can breathe, you can praise Him! Once, children in the temple shouted “Hosanna” to praise Jesus. Jesus said their praise was perfect.","Take a big breath and let it out slowly. Every breath is a gift, and every breath can praise Him."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout fits tonight because Scout loves to notice everything praising Heavenly Father. In Scout’s world today: on a walk, Scout listened to the birds chirping, the wind whooshing the trees, and a puppy’s happy bark. “It’s like the whole world is singing to Heavenly Father!” Scout said, and started humming a thank-You song too. Just like the psalm that calls everything to praise Him, Scout discovered that all creation — and Scout — can join the joyful song.","feel":"Let’s join the whole world’s song! Clap your hands, stomp your feet, and cheer, “Praise Heavenly Father!” Now take a big breath — you have breath, so you can praise Him too! Can you make your most joyful praising sound?","stones":[["SPOT","All of creation feels full of joy and praise for Heavenly Father."],["NAME","That bursting, happy-hearted feeling is called joyful."],["FIND","When do you feel so happy you just want to sing or shout?"],["FORK","Big joy can stay quiet inside — or bubble out in praise. All creation lets its joy sing out to Heavenly Father."],["CALM","Take a happy breath and hum one joyful note to Heavenly Father."]],"remember":"Everything that breathes can praise Heavenly Father — and that means YOU! You can praise Him with singing, clapping, and a thankful heart.","ask":["“Who does the song say should praise Heavenly Father?”","“Since you can breathe, what can you do?”","“What is your favorite happy way to praise Heavenly Father?”"],"share":["When has all creation lifted your heart to praise Heavenly Father? Share a moment of wonder.","How does your family make joyful praise a habit? Tell about a time of singing or celebrating together.","Did a grandparent or ancestor overflow with praise for Heavenly Father? Describe the joy of their worship."]},"b9-w2-n1":{"story":["Long ago, people walked everywhere on dusty, rocky paths. A traveler needed someone who knew the way.","King Solomon wrote, “Trust in the Lord with all your heart. Do not lean on your own understanding.” The Lord knows so much more than we do.","Solomon said, “In all your ways remember Him. He will show you your paths.”","The Lord is like a guide who knows every path. He will never get lost. The Lord is Jesus, and He said, “I am the way.”","When you are not sure what to do, you can pray. Jesus will show you the way. You can trust Him with all your heart."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau fits tonight because he trusts Heavenly Father even when he can’t see the whole way. In Beau’s world today: Beau’s family went on a hike, and the trail wound up a hill where Beau couldn’t see the top. His legs felt unsure. But Dad said, “Just take the next step — I know the way.” Beau trusted and kept going, and the view at the top was amazing! Just like Solomon said to trust Heavenly Father to direct our paths, Beau learned to trust the One who knows the way.","feel":"Let’s follow the leader! Hold out your hand like you’re holding Heavenly Father’s, and take slow, trusting steps forward. He knows the way! Can you walk your path, trusting your guide?","stones":[["SPOT","When we don’t know the way, we might feel unsure — but we can choose to trust."],["NAME","That I-don’t-know-but-I’ll-trust feeling is called trusting."],["FIND","When have you had to trust a grown-up who knew the way, even when you didn’t?"],["FORK","Not knowing can make us worry and freeze — or we can trust Heavenly Father and take the next step. Solomon taught us to trust Him."],["CALM","Take a slow breath and whisper, “I trust You to lead me, Heavenly Father.”"]],"remember":"When you don’t know which way to go, you can trust Heavenly Father with your whole heart — He will lead you, step by step!","ask":["“How much should we trust Heavenly Father?”","“What does Heavenly Father do when we trust Him?”","“What is something you can trust Heavenly Father to help you with?”"],"share":["When did trusting Heavenly Father — over your own understanding — lead you rightly? Share the story.","How do you model “leaning not on your own understanding” for your child? Tell about a step of faith.","Did a grandparent or ancestor follow Heavenly Father’s direction into the unknown? Describe their trusting heart."]},"b9-w2-n2":{"story":["Wise Solomon wanted everyone to have something wonderful called wisdom. Wisdom means knowing the good and right thing to do.","“Look for wisdom like you’re searching for hidden treasure!” Solomon said. “Dig for it, hunt for it — it’s more precious than gold and shiny jewels!”","How do we find this treasure? Solomon said to listen to Heavenly Father, and to listen to the wise grown-ups who love us, like our mom and dad.","“Pay attention, my child,” he wrote. “Keep my good words close, and you will grow wise and happy.”","Every time you listen and learn, you’re finding a little more treasure. Heavenly Father loves to help you grow wise, one good lesson at a time."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout fits tonight because Scout loves to search out good and wonderful things. In Scout’s world today: Scout wanted to build the tallest sandcastle but didn’t know how. Instead of giving up, Scout asked Grandpa, who knew a clever trick — wet sand sticks better! Scout listened, tried it, and built a mighty castle. Just like Solomon said to hunt for wisdom like treasure, Scout discovered that listening to wise helpers is like finding gold.","feel":"Let’s hunt for treasure! Put your hand above your eyes and “search” all around, then dig-dig-dig with your hands and — “I found it!” — hold up the sparkly treasure of wisdom. Can you show me your excited treasure-hunting face?","stones":[["SPOT","Looking for wisdom, we can feel curious and eager to learn."],["NAME","That want-to-know-more feeling is called curious."],["FIND","When have you felt curious and excited to learn something new?"],["FORK","We can shrug and not bother — or hunt for wisdom like treasure. Solomon taught us to search for it eagerly."],["CALM","Take a slow breath and whisper, “Help me grow wise, Heavenly Father.”"]],"remember":"Wisdom is a treasure worth hunting for — and you find it by listening to Heavenly Father and the wise people who love you!","ask":["“What did Solomon say to look for like treasure?”","“How do we find the treasure of wisdom?”","“What is something wise you learned from a grown-up you love?”"],"share":["When did seeking wisdom — over quick or easy answers — bless your life? Share it.","How do you nurture a love of learning and listening in your child? Tell about a treasure you’ve found.","Did a grandparent or ancestor pass down wisdom you still treasure? Describe the gift of their counsel."]},"b9-w2-n3":{"story":["King Solomon wrote, “A soft answer turns away anger. But hurting words stir up anger.”","When someone feels hot and mad, gentle words help cool them down. Hurting words make the mad feeling grow bigger.","Solomon also wrote, “Kind words are like a honeycomb, sweet to the soul.”","Bees keep their honey in a honeycomb. Long ago, honey was one of the sweetest treats people had. So kind words taste sweet to the heart. Jesus said He is “meek and lowly in heart,” gentle and kind.","Your words can be sweet like honey too. Tomorrow, when someone is upset, try a soft, gentle answer."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow fits tonight because gentle words are her superpower. In Willow’s world today: Willow’s brother knocked over her block tower and she felt her face go hot and mad. She almost yelled — but instead she took a breath and said softly, “That’s okay, will you help me build it again?” Her brother smiled and helped, and the mad feeling floated away. Just like Solomon taught that a soft answer turns anger away, Willow found that gentle words make peace.","feel":"Let’s practice soft words! First, make a loud grumpy face and shake your fists — grr! Now take a slow breath, soften your face, and whisper, “It’s okay.” Can you feel how a soft answer calms us down?","stones":[["SPOT","When something goes wrong, we might feel angry — but a soft answer helps us feel calm."],["NAME","That grumpy, hot-inside feeling is called angry."],["FIND","When have you felt angry and wanted to say a not-nice word?"],["FORK","An angry feeling can burst out in mean words — or we can breathe and choose a soft, kind answer. Solomon taught us to choose gentle words."],["CALM","Take a slow breath, soften your voice, and say, “I can use kind words.”"]],"remember":"When you feel grumpy, you can choose soft, kind words — and watch the angry feelings melt away! Gentle words make hearts happy.","ask":["“What does a soft, kind answer do to angry feelings?”","“What do mean, grumpy words do?”","“When could you use a soft answer this week?”"],"share":["When did a soft answer defuse anger and bless a relationship? Share that moment.","How do you coach your child (and yourself) toward gentle words in hot moments? Tell what helps.","Did a grandparent or ancestor have a gift for peacemaking words? Describe their gentle way."]},"b9-w2-n4":{"story":["The last part of Proverbs has words King Lemuel’s mother taught him. It ends with a poem about a good, wise mother.","She gets up while it is still dark to make food for her family. She spins soft wool, twisting it into thread for clothes. She helps the poor.","The poem says, “She opens her mouth with wisdom. On her tongue is the law of kindness.”","Her words were wise and kind. “Her children rise up and call her blessed.” They were saying, “We are so thankful for you!” Jesus loved His mother, Mary, and made sure she was cared for.","Moms and grown-ups who care for you are a blessing. Tomorrow, give them a big hug and say thank you."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow fits tonight because she loves to care for others like the kind woman in the song. In Willow’s world today: Willow noticed Mom was tired after a long day. So Willow used her helping hands — she put her toys away without being asked and brought Mom a pretend cup of tea. Mom’s face lit up. “Thank you, my helper!” Just like the loving woman whose family called her blessed, Willow learned that caring hands make a home warm.","feel":"Let’s use loving, helping hands! Pretend to stir a pot, fold a blanket, and give a pat on the back. Now clap for the people who care for you! Can you show me how YOU could help with your hands tomorrow?","stones":[["SPOT","The family in the song felt thankful and full of love for the kind woman."],["NAME","That warm, thank-you feeling is called grateful."],["FIND","Who works hard to take care of you, and how do they help?"],["FORK","We can forget to say thank you — or notice and love the people who care for us. The children chose to call her blessed."],["CALM","Put a hand on your heart and whisper, “Thank You for the people who love me, Heavenly Father.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father gives you loving people who work hard to care for you — and you can thank them AND help with your own hands!","ask":["“How did the woman use her hands?”","“What did her children call her?”","“Who could you thank and help tomorrow?”"],"share":["Who has been a “Proverbs 31” presence of loving strength in your life? Share their example.","How do you help your child notice and honor those who serve them? Tell about a way you say thanks.","Did a grandparent or ancestor pour out love through hard, quiet work? Describe their blessed hands."]},"b9-w2-n5":{"story":["The Preacher was a son of King David and a king in Jerusalem. He wrote a book of wise sayings called Ecclesiastes.","He wrote, “To every thing there is a season.” There is a right time for everything.","Farmers knew this well. There was a time to plant seeds, and a time to pick the food.","There is “a time to laugh” and “a time to dance.” There is a time to hug and a time to be quiet. The Preacher said Heavenly Father “made every thing beautiful in his time.”","Now it is a time to rest. Lie still and cozy. Heavenly Father made this sleepy time beautiful too."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren fits tonight because she thinks about the right time for things. In Wren’s world today: Wren wanted to play outside, but it was pouring rain. Instead of fussing, she wondered, “Maybe this is a time to stay cozy inside.” So she read books and built a fort, and when the sun came out later, it was the perfect time to splash in puddles. Just like the wise teacher said there’s a time for everything, Wren learned to trust each season’s time.","feel":"Let’s move through the times! Pretend to plant a tiny seed, then reach up like it’s growing tall. Now give a big laugh — “ha ha!” — then a slow, calm breath. Can you show me a time to jump and a time to be still?","stones":[["SPOT","The wise teacher felt calm, knowing everything has its right time."],["NAME","That settled, it’s-all-okay feeling is called peaceful."],["FIND","When did waiting for the right time make something even better?"],["FORK","We can fuss when it’s not the time we want — or trust that Heavenly Father knows the right time. The teacher chose peace with the seasons."],["CALM","Take three slow breaths and whisper, “Heavenly Father holds all my times.”"]],"remember":"There’s a right time for everything — a time to play and a time to rest — and Heavenly Father is with you in every one!","ask":["“What are some of the times the teacher talked about?”","“Who is with us in every season and time?”","“What is something this is the right time for tonight?”"],"share":["When did trusting Heavenly Father’s timing bring you peace? Share a season you’re grateful you waited through.","How do you help your child accept that feelings and seasons pass? Tell what steadies you.","Did a grandparent or ancestor live with patient trust in Heavenly Father’s timing? Describe their steadiness."]},"b9-w2-n6":{"story":["The Preacher loved the morning sun. He wrote, “Truly the light is sweet. It is a happy thing to see the sun.”","Then he wrote, “Remember now your Creator in the days of your youth.” Remember the One who made you while you are young, like right now!","Heavenly Father is the Father of your spirit, the you that thinks and loves. Jesus made this world for Him.","Last, the Preacher said the most important thing. Love and honor Heavenly Father. Keep His commandments, His loving rules that keep us safe and happy.","You do not have to wait to grow up. Tonight, thank Heavenly Father for the sun, and for making you."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout fits tonight because Scout loves to start the adventure of knowing Heavenly Father early. In Scout’s world today: Scout’s big cousin said, “You’re too little to say prayers by yourself.” But Scout said, “No I’m not — I can start now!” and folded little arms and prayed a sweet, simple prayer all alone. Just like the wise teacher said to remember Heavenly Father while young, Scout discovered you’re never too little to know Him.","feel":"Let’s start now! Point to yourself and say, “I can love Heavenly Father today!” Now plant a pretend seed in your heart and watch it grow tall. Can you show me your heart growing love for Him, starting right now?","stones":[["SPOT","Learning to love Heavenly Father young, we can feel eager and close to Him."],["NAME","That excited-to-begin feeling is called eager."],["FIND","What is something you’re eager to do because you’re growing up?"],["FORK","We can think “I’ll wait till I’m big” — or start knowing Heavenly Father right now. The teacher said to start while young."],["CALM","Fold your arms, breathe slow, and whisper, “I love You now, Heavenly Father.”"]],"remember":"You don’t have to wait until you’re grown up to know Heavenly Father — you can start today, while you’re little!","ask":["“When did the wise teacher say to remember Heavenly Father?”","“Are you ever too little to love Heavenly Father?”","“What is one way you can grow closer to Heavenly Father right now?”"],"share":["What helped you begin to know Heavenly Father, and when did it take root? Share your beginning.","What early habit of faith do you most hope your child plants now? Tell why it matters.","Did a grandparent or ancestor come to Heavenly Father young and stay close a lifetime? Describe their early faith."]},"b9-w2-n7":{"story":["Wise Solomon shared one more treasure: he said a good name is better than piles of gold and silver.","What is a “good name”? It means being someone people can trust — someone honest, kind, and true. When you do good, your name becomes a good name.","Solomon knew that money and toys come and go. But being a good, honest person is a treasure that lasts forever.","Every time you tell the truth, keep a promise, or do the right thing, you’re building your good name — like adding a shiny brick to a strong, tall wall.","Heavenly Father is so happy when we choose to be honest and good. A good name is one of the best treasures of all, and you can build yours every day."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau fits tonight because he chooses to be honest, even when it’s hard. In Beau’s world today: Beau accidentally broke a little toy while playing. No one saw. He could have stayed quiet — but his tummy felt yucky. So he took a brave breath and told the truth: “I broke it, and I’m sorry.” His mom hugged him and said, “Thank you for being honest.” Just like Solomon said a good name is a treasure, Beau built his good name by telling the truth.","feel":"Let’s build a good name! Stand up tall and proud, put your hand on your heart, and say, “I will be honest and good!” Now stack pretend bricks, one on top of another. Can you build your strong, good name nice and tall?","stones":[["SPOT","Choosing to be truthful, Beau felt a little nervous but good inside."],["NAME","That did-the-right-thing feeling is called honest."],["FIND","When have you told the truth, even though it felt a little hard?"],["FORK","We can fib to feel safe — or tell the truth and build a good name. Solomon taught that being honest is worth more than gold."],["CALM","Hand on your heart, take a strong breath, and say, “I will be honest and good.”"]],"remember":"A good name — being honest, kind, and true — is a treasure worth more than gold, and you build it every time you do right!","ask":["“What did Solomon say is better than gold and silver?”","“How do we build a good name?”","“When could it be brave to tell the truth this week?”"],"share":["When did integrity cost you something but prove more valuable than gold? Share it.","How do you help your child treasure honesty over convenience? Tell about a moment truth mattered.","Did a grandparent or ancestor leave a legacy of a “good name”? Describe the integrity they handed down."]},"b9-w3-n1":{"story":["Long ago in Jerusalem, many of Heavenly Father’s people had stopped doing good.","So the Lord gave Isaiah a message for them: “Wash, and make yourselves clean.” He did not mean a bath! He wanted to wash the wrong things out of their hearts.","“Stop doing wrong,” the Lord said. “Learn to do good.” Then He said, “Come now,” and made a happy promise.","“Your sins may be bright red, but they will be white as snow.” Why red? A red stain is very hard to wash out. But the Lord can make any heart clean, white as snow. The Lord is Jesus.","Do you feel sorry for something? Tell Jesus about it tonight. He can make your heart clean and new."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow fits tonight because she knows the gift of being made clean and new. In Willow’s world today: Willow said an unkind word to her friend and felt a heavy, yucky feeling inside. She went back, gave a hug, and said, “I’m sorry — that wasn’t kind.” Right away her heart felt light and clean, like fresh snow. Just like Heavenly Father’s promise to make our hearts white as snow, Willow found that saying sorry brings a clean, happy heart.","feel":"Let’s think of clean, white snow! Wiggle your fingers down slowly like gentle snowflakes falling, then brush your hands over your heart — all clean! Can you show me your fresh, snowy-clean heart?","stones":[["SPOT","Before, the heart felt heavy — after Heavenly Father’s washing, it feels clean."],["NAME","That fresh, all-better feeling is called clean."],["FIND","When have you felt fresh and happy after making something right?"],["FORK","A heavy heart can stay stuck and hide — or come to Heavenly Father to be made clean. Isaiah shared His promise to wash us white as snow."],["CALM","Brush your hands over your heart, breathe out, and whisper, “Make me clean, Heavenly Father.”"]],"remember":"No mistake is too big — when you’re sorry, Heavenly Father washes your heart white as snow, clean and new!","ask":["“What did Heavenly Father promise to make our hearts like?”","“Is any mistake too big for Heavenly Father’s love?”","“What helps you feel better after you’ve made a mistake?”"],"share":["When did Heavenly Father’s cleansing love give you a true fresh start? Share that relief.","How do you teach your child that repentance is gentle and hopeful, not scary? Tell of His mercy to you.","Did a grandparent or ancestor find peace in being made clean by Heavenly Father? Describe their “white as snow” story."]},"b9-w3-n2":{"story":["In the year King Uzziah died, Isaiah saw the Lord in the temple. The Lord sat high up on a king’s throne.","Heavenly helpers named seraphim called out, “Holy, holy, holy is the Lord! The whole earth is full of His glory.”","Isaiah felt very small. “My lips are not clean,” he said. “I have seen the King, the Lord!”","Then a seraph touched Isaiah’s lips and said his sin was taken away. Now Isaiah was clean and ready. The Lord asked, “Whom shall I send?” Isaiah said, “Here am I; send me.” The Lord Isaiah saw was Jesus.","You can be the Lord’s helper too. When someone needs help, raise your hand high. Say, “Here am I!”"],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"","feel":"Let’s be a willing helper like Isaiah! Raise your hand up high like you’re volunteering, and say big and brave, “Here am I — send me!” Can you show me how ready you are to help?","stones":[["SPOT","Isaiah felt small at first, then brave and willing to help."],["NAME","That ready-to-help feeling is called willing."],["FIND","When have you felt happy and ready to be a helper?"],["FORK","We can feel too shy and stay quiet — or bravely say, “Send me!” Isaiah chose to be a willing helper for Heavenly Father."],["CALM","Take a strong breath and whisper, “I’m ready to help, Heavenly Father.”"]],"remember":"When there’s good work to do, you can be brave and willing like Isaiah and say, “Here am I — send me!”","ask":["“What did the angels sing in Isaiah’s vision?”","“What did Isaiah say when Heavenly Father needed a helper?”","“What is a good way you could be a helper tomorrow?”"],"share":["When did you say “here am I” to Heavenly Father, even feeling unready? Share what happened.","How do you encourage your child to volunteer and serve willingly? Tell about a time you stepped up.","Did a grandparent or ancestor answer a call to serve Heavenly Father? Describe their willing heart."]},"b9-w3-n3":{"story":["Isaiah lived long ago in Jerusalem. The Lord showed him the last days, before Jesus comes again.","Isaiah saw the Lord’s holy house, the temple, standing on top of the mountains. It stood higher than all the hills.","Why up high? Long ago, people often climbed mountains to be near the Lord.","Isaiah saw people from every land coming to it. They called, “Come, let us go up to the mountain of the Lord! He will teach us His ways, and we will walk in His paths.” Today there are temples all over the world!","Tonight, picture a temple shining on a hill. You can walk on the Lord’s path too. Jesus will teach you His ways."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout fits tonight because Scout loves the journey to special places. In Scout’s world today: Scout’s family drove to see the temple, all lit up and beautiful. Scout pressed against the window and gasped, “It’s like a castle of light!” On the peaceful grounds, Scout felt happy and calm and close to Heavenly Father. Just like Isaiah’s message about going up to Heavenly Father’s house, Scout discovered how special it feels to be near His holy home.","feel":"Let’s go up to Heavenly Father’s house! March your feet up a pretend hill — up, up, up — then fold your arms reverently and whisper, “It’s so peaceful here.” Can you show me your quiet, reverent face inside His house?","stones":[["SPOT","The people in Isaiah’s message felt eager and glad to go to Heavenly Father’s house."],["NAME","That excited-and-peaceful feeling is called eager."],["FIND","When have you felt excited to go somewhere special and good?"],["FORK","We can drag our feet about holy places — or be eager to go and feel Heavenly Father near. The people chose, “Let’s go up!”"],["CALM","Fold your arms, breathe slow, and whisper, “I love being near You, Heavenly Father.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father’s house is a special, peaceful place where you can feel Him close — and you can be eager to go!","ask":["“Where did the people want to go up to?”","“What did they say Heavenly Father would teach them?”","“How do you feel when you’re in a place that feels close to Heavenly Father?”"],"share":["When has the temple or a house of worship drawn your heart to Heavenly Father? Share it.","How do you help your child love sacred places and reverence? Tell about a meaningful visit.","Did a grandparent or ancestor cherish the temple or Heavenly Father’s house? Describe their devotion."]},"b9-w3-n4":{"story":["Isaiah shared the happiest secret of all — a promise about a very special baby who would come someday.","“Unto us a child will be born!” Isaiah said. “And He will be wonderful. He will be called the Prince of Peace!”","This special baby was Jesus, Heavenly Father’s own Son. Isaiah told about Him long, long before He was born, because Heavenly Father wanted His people to have hope.","Jesus would be a mighty helper, full of love and peace. He would show everyone the way back to Heavenly Father.","Even a long time ago, Heavenly Father was planning something wonderful for His children. He always keeps His promises — and the promise of Jesus was the most wonderful of all."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren fits tonight because she loves to wonder about Heavenly Father’s beautiful plans. In Wren’s world today: Wren saw her aunt getting ready for a new baby and wondered, “Isn’t it amazing how Heavenly Father plans good things before they even happen?” She thought about how He promised baby Jesus long ago too. Just like Isaiah wondered at the promise of the Prince of Peace, Wren marveled at Heavenly Father’s wonderful, hope-filled plans.","feel":"Let’s think about the happy promise! Rock your arms gently like you’re holding a sweet baby, and whisper softly, “The Prince of Peace is coming.” Can you show me how gently we hold something precious?","stones":[["SPOT","Isaiah felt full of hope about the special baby who was promised."],["NAME","That good-things-are-coming feeling is called hopeful."],["FIND","When have you felt hopeful and excited about something good coming?"],["FORK","We can forget Heavenly Father’s promises — or hold onto them with hope. Isaiah held onto the hope of the Prince of Peace."],["CALM","Rock your arms gently, breathe slow, and whisper, “Thank You for Jesus, Heavenly Father.”"]],"remember":"Long ago, Heavenly Father promised a special baby — Jesus, the Prince of Peace — because He loves you and wanted you to have hope!","ask":["“What did Isaiah say the special baby would be called?”","“Who was the special baby that was promised?”","“What good thing are you feeling hopeful about?”"],"share":["When has the hope of the Savior carried you through a hard time? Share that hope.","How do you help your child anticipate and treasure Jesus? Tell about a way you point to Him.","Did a grandparent or ancestor testify of Jesus with great hope? Describe the faith they shared."]},"b9-w3-n5":{"story":["Isaiah said a new green branch would grow from the roots of Jesse’s family. Jesse was King David’s father.","Jesus was born into that family. Isaiah was talking about Jesus!","Isaiah saw a time of peace Jesus will bring. Prophets teach it will come after He comes again. Even the animals will be gentle.","Today a lamb must stay far from a wolf. But Isaiah said the wolf will live with the lamb. The leopard will lie down with the baby goat. The calf and the young lion will be together. “A little child shall lead them.” No one will hurt anyone.","You can be gentle like that little child. Pat a soft toy, slowly and kindly. Jesus helps you be gentle like Him."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow fits tonight because she loves to make peace and help everyone feel safe. In Willow’s world today: at the park, a big kid and a little kid were both grabbing the same swing and starting to fuss. Willow gently said, “You can both have a turn — I’ll help count!” Soon they were laughing together. Just like Isaiah’s peaceful world where the lamb and lion are friends, Willow helped make a little pocket of peace.","feel":"Let’s make a peaceful world! Pretend to pet a soft, gentle lamb, then a big friendly lion, right next to each other. Now give a calm, happy sigh — “aahh.” Can you show me your most peaceful, gentle face?","stones":[["SPOT","In Isaiah’s peaceful world, everyone feels safe and calm."],["NAME","That safe, gentle, nothing-scary feeling is called peaceful."],["FIND","When do you feel most peaceful and safe?"],["FORK","We can be rough and grabby — or gentle and kind like Heavenly Father’s peaceful world. Isaiah showed us the way of peace."],["CALM","Take a slow, gentle breath and whisper, “Help me be peaceful, Heavenly Father.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father is preparing a peaceful world where everyone is safe — and you can bring a little of that peace today by being kind and gentle!","ask":["“Who lies down together as friends in Isaiah’s peaceful world?”","“Who leads them all?”","“How could you help someone feel peaceful and safe tomorrow?”"],"share":["When have you glimpsed Heavenly Father’s peace in the middle of an unpeaceful world? Share it.","How do you cultivate a home of gentleness and safety? Tell about a peacemaking moment.","Did a grandparent or ancestor carry a deep, calming peace? Describe the gentleness they modeled."]},"b9-w3-n6":{"story":["Isaiah wrote a happy song for a day still to come. It is a song about trusting the Lord.","The song says, “I will trust, and not be afraid.” It says, “The Lord Jehovah is my strength and my song.” Jehovah is Jesus’s name from long ago. He makes us strong, and He makes us sing!","Isaiah sang, “With joy shall ye draw water out of the wells of salvation.”","In Isaiah’s land, families got their water from deep wells. They pulled it up in a bucket on a rope. Jesus saves us and gives our hearts what they need, like cool water.","When you feel tired or small, sing a song about Jesus. He is your strength and your song too."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau fits tonight because he trusts Heavenly Father instead of being afraid. In Beau’s world today: there was a big thunderstorm, and the loud booms made Beau’s heart jump. But he took a deep breath and remembered Isaiah’s song. “Heavenly Father is with me — I will trust and not be afraid,” he whispered, and he even hummed a little tune until the storm passed. Just like Isaiah sang about trusting and not fearing, Beau turned his scared feeling into a brave, trusting one.","feel":"Let’s sing a brave, trusting song! Stand up tall, put your hands on your heart, and say, “I will trust and not be afraid!” Now hum a happy, brave little tune. Can you show me your bravest, most trusting face?","stones":[["SPOT","When something felt scary, Isaiah chose to trust instead of being afraid."],["NAME","That Heavenly-Father-is-with-me feeling is called trusting."],["FIND","When has something felt scary, and you wanted to feel brave?"],["FORK","A scary feeling can grow bigger and bigger — or we can trust Heavenly Father and feel brave. Isaiah chose to trust and not be afraid."],["CALM","Hand on your heart, take a brave breath, and say, “I will trust and not be afraid.”"]],"remember":"When something feels scary, you can sing Isaiah’s song — “Heavenly Father is with me, so I will trust and not be afraid!”","ask":["“What did Isaiah say he would NOT be?”","“What did Isaiah say Heavenly Father was to him?”","“What could you remember the next time you feel scared?”"],"share":["When did trusting Heavenly Father dissolve your fear? Share the before and after.","How do you help your child move from fear to trust? Tell about a fear He helped you face.","Did a grandparent or ancestor face frightening times with trust in Heavenly Father? Describe their courage."]},"b9-w3-n7":{"story":["Isaiah’s happy song had one more joyful part. He sang about drawing up joy like cool water from a deep, wonderful well.","“With joy you will draw water from the wells of Heavenly Father’s saving love!” Isaiah sang. Imagine a bucket coming up full — not of water, but of happiness!","When we remember all the good things Heavenly Father does, our hearts fill up with joy, splashing over like a fresh, cool drink on a hot day.","And Isaiah didn’t want to keep the joy to himself. “Tell everyone!” he sang. “Sing about the wonderful things Heavenly Father has done!”","You can draw up joy too, just by remembering Heavenly Father’s love — and then share it with everyone, so the happiness spreads and spreads."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout fits tonight because Scout loves to find joy and share it with everyone. In Scout’s world today: Scout’s grandma got better after being sick, and Scout was so happy and grateful. Scout couldn’t hold the joy in — running to tell everyone, “Heavenly Father helped Grandma feel better!” Soon the whole family was smiling and thankful. Just like Isaiah sang about drawing up joy and telling everyone, Scout discovered that shared joy grows even bigger.","feel":"Let’s draw up joy from the well! Pretend to pull a rope hand over hand — up, up — then lift the happy bucket high and cheer, “Joy!” Now splash the joy to someone you love. Can you draw up your happiest bucket of joy?","stones":[["SPOT","Remembering Heavenly Father’s love, Isaiah felt his heart fill up with joy."],["NAME","That overflowing, happy-hearted feeling is called joyful."],["FIND","When has remembering something good filled you up with happiness?"],["FORK","Joy can stay small and quiet inside — or we can draw it up and share it. Isaiah chose to fill up with joy and tell everyone."],["CALM","Take a happy breath and think of one good thing Heavenly Father has done for you."]],"remember":"You can fill your heart with joy just by remembering Heavenly Father’s love — and then share it so the happiness grows!","ask":["“What did Isaiah say we would draw up with joy?”","“What did Isaiah want everyone to do with the good news?”","“What good thing about Heavenly Father could you share with someone tomorrow?”"],"share":["When did drawing on Heavenly Father’s goodness fill an empty place with joy? Share it.","How do you help your child both feel and share joy in Heavenly Father? Tell about a time you testified.","Did a grandparent or ancestor overflow with joyful gratitude to Heavenly Father? Describe their well of joy."]},"b9-w4-n1":{"story":["Isaiah told about a happy day that will come. On that day, no one will need to be sad anymore.","Isaiah said, “The Lord will wipe away tears from off all faces.” Every tear, on every sad face!","Think of a mom gently wiping a crying child’s cheek. Isaiah used that picture so we would know how kind the Lord is.","People will say, “We have waited for Him, and He will save us.” They will be so glad! The Lord they waited for is Jesus.","Jesus sees your tears too. When you are sad, He helps you. Tonight, gently wipe a teddy’s cheek, the kind way Jesus cares for you."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow fits tonight because she loves to comfort and dry tears. In Willow’s world today: Willow’s little cousin was crying because she missed her mommy. Willow didn’t have a way to fix it, but she gently wiped her cousin’s tears and gave her a hug and sat close until she felt better. Just like Heavenly Father’s promise to wipe away every tear, Willow brought comfort with her gentle, loving care.","feel":"Let’s feel Heavenly Father’s comfort! Pretend to gently wipe a tear from your cheek, then give yourself a soft, warm hug. “It’s going to be okay.” Can you show me a sad face turning into a comforted, peaceful one?","stones":[["SPOT","When we’re sad, Heavenly Father promises to comfort us and dry our tears."],["NAME","That better-now, cared-for feeling is called comforted."],["FIND","When has a gentle hug or kind touch helped you feel better after crying?"],["FORK","A sad feeling can stay heavy — or we can let Heavenly Father comfort us and dry our tears. Isaiah shared His promise of comfort."],["CALM","Wipe a pretend tear, breathe out slow, and whisper, “Thank You for comforting me, Heavenly Father.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father sees every tear and promises to wipe them all away — He loves to comfort you!","ask":["“What did Isaiah say Heavenly Father would wipe away?”","“What does Heavenly Father do when we feel sad?”","“Who can give you a comforting hug when you feel sad?”"],"share":["When did Heavenly Father comfort you in a season of tears? Share how He met you.","How do you help your child bring their sadness to Heavenly Father? Tell about a comfort He gave you.","Did a grandparent or ancestor find deep comfort in Heavenly Father through sorrow? Describe their solace."]},"b9-w4-n2":{"story":["Isaiah wrote a song for the people to sing someday. It was a song about trusting the Lord.","The song says the Lord will keep you “in perfect peace.” That is a calm, quiet feeling, deep in your heart.","That peace comes when your mind is “stayed” on the Lord. Your thoughts stay close to Him, because you trust Him.","Isaiah said, “Trust ye in the Lord for ever.” He called Him the Lord Jehovah, Jesus’s name long ago. In Him is “everlasting strength,” strength that never, ever runs out.","When your mind feels busy or jumbled, think about Jesus. Say softly, “I trust You.” Feel your heart grow calm."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren fits tonight because she knows that thinking about Heavenly Father brings peace. In Wren’s world today: Wren felt worried about her first day at a new class — her thoughts were spinning fast. So she closed her eyes and thought about how Heavenly Father loved her and was with her. Slowly, her spinning thoughts grew calm and peaceful. Just like Isaiah said keeping our minds on Heavenly Father brings perfect peace, Wren found calm by thinking of Him.","feel":"Let’s find perfect peace! Put your hands on the sides of your head, then slowly move them down as you take a deep breath, thinking one calm thought about Heavenly Father. Can you show me your peaceful, settled face?","stones":[["SPOT","When Isaiah kept his mind on Heavenly Father, he felt perfect peace."],["NAME","That calm, settled, quiet feeling is called peaceful."],["FIND","When do your thoughts feel busy or worried, and you need to feel calm?"],["FORK","Worried thoughts can spin faster and faster — or we can think of Heavenly Father and find peace. Isaiah kept his mind on Him."],["CALM","Take three slow breaths and think one gentle thought about Heavenly Father."]],"remember":"When your thoughts feel busy or worried, you can think about Heavenly Father — and He fills you with perfect peace!","ask":["“How do we get Heavenly Father’s perfect peace?”","“What happens when we keep our minds on Heavenly Father?”","“What happy thought about Heavenly Father could you think when you feel worried?”"],"share":["When did fixing your mind on Heavenly Father bring peace amid chaos? Share your practice.","How do you teach your child to redirect anxious thoughts toward Heavenly Father? Tell what helps you.","Did a grandparent or ancestor carry a settled, peaceful mind rooted in Heavenly Father? Describe their calm."]},"b9-w4-n3":{"story":["Isaiah taught that Heavenly Father is like the strongest, most reliable rock you could ever build on.","“Trust in Heavenly Father forever,” Isaiah said, “for He is like a Rock that lasts always and always.”","He also said Heavenly Father is like a precious, sure cornerstone — the special, strong stone builders use to hold a whole house together, so it never falls.","When we build our lives on Heavenly Father, we’re standing on something that will never wobble, crack, or crumble. He is steady and strong forever.","No matter what happens around us, Heavenly Father holds us firm. When we trust in Him, we are safe and steady, standing on the strongest Rock of all."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau fits tonight because he stands strong on Heavenly Father. In Beau’s world today: Beau was learning to balance on the low wall at the park, and his legs felt wobbly and unsure. He remembered that Heavenly Father was his strong Rock, holding him steady. He took a brave breath, steadied himself, and walked the whole way! Just like Isaiah said to trust Heavenly Father, the Rock that lasts forever, Beau found his steady footing by leaning on Him.","feel":"Let’s stand on the strong Rock! Plant your feet wide, make your body big and firm, and don’t wobble — steady as a rock! Now say, “Heavenly Father is my strong Rock!” Can you stand as steady and sure as stone?","stones":[["SPOT","Trusting Heavenly Father, the Rock, we can feel steady and safe."],["NAME","That firm, won’t-wobble feeling is called steady."],["FIND","When have you felt wobbly and needed something strong to hold onto?"],["FORK","A wobbly feeling can tip us over — or we can stand firm on Heavenly Father, our Rock. Isaiah taught us to trust Him forever."],["CALM","Plant your feet firm, breathe in, and stand steady like a strong rock."]],"remember":"Heavenly Father is your sure, strong Rock — when you trust in Him, you can stand steady and safe forever!","ask":["“What strong, steady thing did Isaiah say Heavenly Father is like?”","“What happens when we build our lives on Heavenly Father?”","“When could you lean on Heavenly Father to feel steady?”"],"share":["When did Heavenly Father prove a steady “Rock” while everything shook? Share it.","How do you help your child find footing on Heavenly Father in unsteady moments? Tell about a time you did.","Did a grandparent or ancestor stand firm on Heavenly Father as their rock? Describe their steadiness."]},"b9-w4-n4":{"story":["The Lord showed Isaiah things far in the future. Isaiah told about a book that was sealed, closed up tight.","Someone asked a wise man to read it. He said, “I cannot; for it is sealed.”","Some people praised the Lord with words, but not with their hearts. So the Lord said, “I will do a marvelous work and a wonder.” It would be something amazing!","Prophets in our day teach that Isaiah was talking about the Book of Mormon. It is a book about Jesus. Even people who could not hear would “hear the words of the book.”","You can hear those words too. Tonight, touch a Book of Mormon if you can. Its words help you know Jesus."],"traveler":"Scout the Seeker","why":"Scout fits tonight because Scout loves to discover Heavenly Father’s marvelous wonders. In Scout’s world today: after a rainstorm, Scout spotted a huge, bright rainbow arching across the whole sky. “How does Heavenly Father DO that?” Scout gasped, staring in wonder. It felt like a marvelous surprise made just for that moment. Just like Isaiah said Heavenly Father does a marvellous work and a wonder, Scout discovered that His amazing works are everywhere.","feel":"Let’s be amazed at Heavenly Father’s wonders! Open your eyes and mouth w-i-d-e in a big “WOW!” and throw your hands up in surprise. Heavenly Father does marvelous things! Can you show me your most amazed, wonder-filled face?","stones":[["SPOT","Seeing Heavenly Father’s marvelous works, we feel amazed and full of wonder."],["NAME","That big-eyed, “wow!” feeling is called amazed."],["FIND","What is something amazing that made you say “wow”?"],["FORK","We can rush past amazing things — or stop and wonder at Heavenly Father’s marvelous works. Isaiah invites us to be amazed by Him."],["CALM","Take a slow breath, look up, and whisper, “You do marvelous things, Heavenly Father.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father does marvelous works and wonders — amazing, surprising, wonderful things — today and always!","ask":["“What did Isaiah say Heavenly Father would do?”","“What do marvelous works make us want to say?”","“What marvelous thing that Heavenly Father made amazes you most?”"],"share":["When did you witness a “marvellous work and a wonder” from Heavenly Father? Share the awe.","How do you help your child stay wonder-filled at Heavenly Father’s works? Tell about a favorite marvel.","Did a grandparent or ancestor witness a wonder or miracle from Heavenly Father? Describe their amazement."]},"b9-w4-n5":{"story":["Many people in Isaiah’s day did not want to listen. They told the prophets, “Do not tell us what is right.”","But the Lord still loved them. Isaiah said the Lord was waiting, ready to be kind to them.","When you cry out to the Lord, Isaiah said, “He will answer you.”","Isaiah said, “Your ears shall hear a word behind you.” It says, “This is the way, walk ye in it.” Picture a kind grown-up just behind you, saying, “Go this way!”","The Lord helps you know the way through prophets and the Holy Ghost. Listen for that quiet voice inside you. Then walk His way, one step at a time."],"traveler":"Wren the Wonderer","why":"Wren fits tonight because she gets quiet and listens with her heart. In Wren’s world today: Wren wasn’t sure whether to share her new crayons or keep them all to herself. Instead of rushing, she got quiet and thought about what was kind. In the calm, she felt a gentle nudge in her heart: sharing is the good way. She shared, and it felt right. Just like Isaiah said we hear “this is the way” when we’re quiet and trusting, Wren listened and found the good path.","feel":"Let’s listen for the way! Cup your hand to your ear and get very quiet… then point ahead and whisper, “This is the way!” Now take a peaceful step forward. Can you show me your best quiet- listening ears?","stones":[["SPOT","Listening quietly, we can feel guided and sure of the right way."],["NAME","That I-know-the-good-way feeling is called guided."],["FIND","When have you gotten quiet and figured out the right thing to do?"],["FORK","We can rush ahead and guess — or get quiet and listen for Heavenly Father’s gentle guidance. Isaiah taught us to listen in quietness."],["CALM","Get very still, take a slow breath, and listen with your heart for the good way."]],"remember":"When you’re not sure what’s right, you can get quiet and listen — Heavenly Father will gently guide you, whispering, “This is the way!”","ask":["“What gentle words does Heavenly Father help us hear?”","“What helps us hear Heavenly Father’s guidance?”","“When could you get quiet and listen for the good way?”"],"share":["When did a quiet prompting from Heavenly Father show you the way? Share how you heard it.","How do you help your child slow down to sense Heavenly Father’s guidance? Tell about a time you did.","Did a grandparent or ancestor follow Heavenly Father’s quiet guidance? Describe their listening faith."]},"b9-w4-n6":{"story":["Much of Isaiah’s land was desert, dry and sandy, with very few plants.","Isaiah said, “The desert shall rejoice, and blossom as the rose.” Picture dry sand bursting with roses! Water would break out, and thirsty ground would become a pool.","Isaiah’s picture showed that the Lord can make dry, sad places new.","Isaiah said the blind would see and the deaf would hear. People who could not walk would leap like a deer! Many years later, Jesus did these things. He said, “The blind receive their sight, and the lame walk.”","Open your hands like a flower. Jesus heals hearts and bodies. He can make sad places bloom with joy."],"traveler":"Willow the Warm","why":"Willow fits tonight because she believes good things can bloom again. In Willow’s world today: Willow’s favorite little plant looked droopy and brown, and she felt sad. But Mom showed her how to water it and set it in the sun. Day by day, it grew green and even sprouted a tiny flower! Willow clapped with joy. Just like Isaiah’s desert that blossomed like a rose, Willow learned that with gentle care and hope, beautiful things can bloom.","feel":"Let’s watch the desert bloom! Crouch down small like dry, cracked ground, then slowly grow up and open your arms wide like a flower bursting into bloom. Can you show me the empty desert blossoming into a beautiful garden?","stones":[["SPOT","Watching the desert bloom, we feel hopeful that good things can grow."],["NAME","That good-is-coming feeling is called hopeful."],["FIND","When has something sad or empty gotten better and happy again?"],["FORK","We can believe dry places stay dry forever — or hope in Heavenly Father, who makes things bloom. Isaiah showed us the desert blossoming."],["CALM","Take a slow breath, open your arms like a flower, and whisper, “You make things bloom, Heavenly Father.”"]],"remember":"Heavenly Father can make good things bloom, even in the driest, saddest places — so there’s always hope for something beautiful!","ask":["“What did Isaiah say the dry desert would do?”","“What can Heavenly Father do to dry, sad places?”","“What is something you’re hopeful will get better and bloom?”"],"share":["When did Heavenly Father bring “bloom” to a dry season of your life? Share that renewal.","How do you nurture hope in your child when things feel bleak or bare? Tell about a bloom you’ve seen.","Did a grandparent or ancestor see Heavenly Father bring new life after a barren time? Describe their hope."]},"b9-w4-n7":{"story":["Isaiah’s beautiful message ended with words of courage and joy — a wonderful way to finish the whole month!","“Be strong, and don’t be afraid,” Isaiah said. “Heavenly Father is coming to help you and save you!”","In Isaiah’s happy picture, wonderful things happen: streams splash through the desert, and everyone who was sad leaps and sings for joy. “Sorrow and sighing fly away!”","Isaiah even told of a special, safe road — a happy path that leads home to Heavenly Father, where His people walk and sing with joy forever.","So be strong and brave, little one! Heavenly Father is always helping you and leading you home. And on the way, He fills our hearts with singing, and turns all our sorrow into joy."],"traveler":"Beau the Brave","why":"Beau fits tonight because his courage carries him all the way to joy. In Beau’s world today: Beau felt nervous about riding his bike without training wheels — it seemed so hard and a little scary. But he remembered to be strong and not afraid, took a deep breath, and pushed off. Wobble, wobble — then he was riding! He leaped off, cheering with pure joy. Just like Isaiah said, “Be strong, don’t be afraid,” Beau’s brave heart turned into a happy, joyful one.","feel":"Let’s be strong and joyful! Flex your muscles strong — “I am brave!” — then leap up high and cheer, “Joy!” Sorrow flies away! Can you show me strong and brave, then leaping with joy?","stones":[["SPOT","Isaiah said to be strong and brave, and then joy comes leaping in."],["NAME","That strong, I-can-do-it feeling is called brave."],["FIND","When have you felt brave and strong doing something hard?"],["FORK","Fear can make us give up — or we can be strong, trust Heavenly Father, and find joy. Isaiah said, “Be strong, don’t be afraid.”"],["CALM","Take a strong, brave breath and whisper, “Heavenly Father makes me strong.”"]],"remember":"Be strong and don’t be afraid — Heavenly Father is always helping you and leading you home, and He turns sorrow into joy!","ask":["“What did Isaiah say to be, instead of afraid?”","“What happens to sorrow and sighing in Isaiah’s happy message?”","“What is something brave and strong Heavenly Father can help you do?”"],"share":["When did Heavenly Father give you strength and turn sorrow into joy? Share that journey.","How do you help your child be strong and unafraid with Heavenly Father’s help? Tell about a brave moment.","Did a grandparent or ancestor walk a hard road with courage and find joy? Describe their strong, singing faith."]}};
